,"url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"GitHub credentials for a workspace"},"81":{"id":"/coder-templates-44","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"Where the token comes from"},"82":{"id":"/coder-templates-45","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"The credential ladder, in git's consult order"},"83":{"id":"/coder-templates-46","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#the-four-commands","content":"The four commands"},"84":{"id":"/coder-templates-47","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"**What's on this page**"},"85":{"id":"/coder-templates-48","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"What the two templates make, and when the `devcontainer` toggle matters"},"86":{"id":"/coder-templates-49","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"How `push-template.sh` ships the source, and the same push by hand"},"87":{"id":"/coder-templates-50","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Every create-form field and template variable, with its default"},"88":{"id":"/coder-templates-51","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Autostop, TTLs, Docker-in-Docker, and the DinD sidecar failure modes"},"89":{"id":"/coder-templates-52","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Terminal and editor defaults inside the container, and workspace GitHub credentials"},"90":{"id":"/coder-templates-53","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"**What this enables**"},"91":{"id":"/coder-templates-54","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Choosing between the plain container and the devcontainer before you click create"},"92":{"id":"/coder-templates-55","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Reading a failed push or a stuck workspace against what the template actually does"},"93":{"id":"/coder-templates-56","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Changing a template variable without guessing which file owns it"},"94":{"id":"/coder-templates-57","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Deep reference for `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/`: what they build,\nhow they get pushed, what each template variable means, and how the create form and the\nin-editor terminal and editor behave."},"95":{"id":"/coder-templates-58","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Related: project-conventions.md (shell and payload conventions),\nSECURITY.md, and\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md\n(the long prose on the tmux profiles and the Grok Build session)."},"96":{"id":"/coder-templates-59","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"Two templates live under `templates/`, and a push covers both."},"97":{"id":"/coder-templates-60","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`workspace` (`templates/workspace/`) is the plain dev container."},"98":{"id":"/coder-templates-61","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`devcontainer` (`templates/devcontainer/`) is the DinD-backed one."},"99":{"id":"/coder-templates-62","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`workspace` goes first, `devcontainer` last: a dependent template pushes last so a\npartial failure leaves the base template usable."},"100":{"id":"/coder-templates-63","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"`devcontainer` reuses `workspace`'s variables. `repo_url` exists in both; `dind` does\nnot exist in `devcontainer`."},"101":{"id":"/coder-templates-64","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-two-templates-make","content":"Each template dir keeps its own independent copy of the `.tftpl` payloads\n(`startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). Change one twin,\nchange the other."},"102":{"id":"/coder-templates-65","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`templates/common/` exists once instead of twice, and both templates consume it.\n`template_push_one` stages every file in this list beside the staged `main.tf`; a\nmissing one fails the push before the CLI runs."},"103":{"id":"/coder-templates-66","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` is the orchestration-protocol seed text the startup scripts\ninline into every workspace's `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` (read with `file()`, chomped)."},"104":{"id":"/coder-templates-67","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`vscode-alt.svg` is the editor icon (read with `filebase64()`). It stays small\nenough that the resulting `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` URL fits Coder\nv2.36's `workspace_apps.icon` column (`varchar(256)`)."},"105":{"id":"/coder-templates-68","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`notes-setup.sh` is the library behind each clone's private `notes.md`. The\nstartup script inlines it with `file()` the same way, writes it into\n`~/.grok/`, sources it, and calls `notes_materialize` per clone. That call also\nseeds the ignore entries that keep the file uncommitted; see \"What is shared\" in\n`docs/grok-build-and-agents.md` for what it writes into git config.\nOnce every clone has been through it the same script calls `run_notes_watch`, which\nreaches `notes_sync_start` on the home owner's uid and leaves the container with one\nbackground watcher (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL` seconds, 30 by default; one instance per\ncontainer behind a `flock` on `$HOME/.notes-sync.lock`) copying each clone's notes back\nto the owner's profile volume between boots. The workspace twin watches\n`$HOME/workspace`; the devcontainer twin passes its `$dc_dir`, because those clones sit\non the `/srv/coder-devcontainers` bind."},"106":{"id":"/coder-templates-69","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`, and `vscode-terminal-tmux.sh` are the\nterminal payloads the startup script writes. Helpers use `$folder` (not\n`$${folder}`) because `file()` contents are not re-interpolated."},"107":{"id":"/coder-templates-70","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` and `coder-agents.md` are the Coder Agents\nskill and instruction seeds (chomped `file()`)."},"108":{"id":"/coder-templates-71","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and `ez-create-skill.SKILL.md` are the\nstack's three skill commands: `/ez-learn` writes memory files, reproducing the upstream\nmemory family `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`; `/ez-skillify` captures the\nsession that just worked into a skill; `/ez-create-skill` scaffolds one from what the\noperator describes. Skill authoring is those last two - `/ez-learn` stores facts, not\nprocedures. They ride `coder_script.stack_skills` rather than the startup argument (see\n\"The one-argument ceiling\"), which writes each absent skill into\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and twin-copies it to `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`,\neach only while absent. Upstream there is no `/learn` and no `/skillify` to shadow: no\npublished source names either one. `/create-skill` is real, but only as a *bundled*\nplatform skill whose catalog is fetched per identity/team behind an x.ai sign-in this\nstack does not have; and the memory family `/ez-learn` copies is no skill at all but the\nCLI's own `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` registry entries plus the pager's `/remember`,\nwith the registry three gated on a memory store, which is off by default and never\nconfigured here. So these payloads are what make the three commands exist, and the `ez-`\nprefix is what keeps them clear of those upstream names."},"109":{"id":"/coder-templates-72","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`stack-skills.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_skills\"` body that seeds that trio,\nrendered with `templatefile()` from the three `chomp(file())` entries `main.tf` passes it.\nIt is absent-only and silent on a healthy workspace, because its log is what an operator\nreads when a skill is missing."},"110":{"id":"/coder-templates-73","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`mcp-cache-proxy.json` is the context7 MCP cache-proxy blob the startup script\nwrites, into `~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json` and only while that file is absent\n(so a shared profile volume reuses one SQLite cache across that owner's workspaces). It\nnames no secret: the empty `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` value is what tells `mcp-cache-proxy` to\ninherit `process.env`."},"111":{"id":"/coder-templates-74","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl` is the shared GitHub-token refresh `coder_script`,\nrendered with `templatefile()` so `${github_auth_id}` and\n`${coder_github_app_install_url}` interpolate."},"112":{"id":"/coder-templates-75","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-templatescommon-payloads","content":"Editing the common copy changes both templates; the staged copy overwrites any\nsame-named file the template dir still carries."},"113":{"id":"/coder-templates-76","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"Presets collapse the create form to one click."},"114":{"id":"/coder-templates-77","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"`workspace`: **AI workspace** (golden image, the selected default), **Minimal shell**\n(bare `ubuntu:24.04`, for when the golden image is mid-rebuild), **AI workspace + DinD**\n(golden image plus a privileged Docker daemon)."},"115":{"id":"/coder-templates-78","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"`devcontainer`: **Devcontainer default** (the template default) and **Plain clone**."},"116":{"id":"/coder-templates-79","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#presets","content":"Preset keys are parameter *names*; `display_name` is UI-only and would be ignored."},"117":{"id":"/coder-templates-80","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` runs `scripts/push-template.sh`."},"118":{"id":"/coder-templates-81","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It resolves the repo root from `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout\n`bazelisk run` names) ahead of the runfiles copy, so `.env` and the template\nfiles are regular files rather than execroot symlinks."},"119":{"id":"/coder-templates-82","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It resolves the running stack on the Docker **daemon** by container label, never\nthrough a cwd-resolved compose project."},"120":{"id":"/coder-templates-83","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"Per template it checks `main.tf` exists (otherwise \"run this from the repo root\") and\nnotes a missing `.terraform.lock.hcl`."},"121":{"id":"/coder-templates-84","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It rejects a `coder_workspace_preset` description over Coder's 128-**byte** ceiling."},"122":{"id":"/coder-templates-85","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It rejects a `vscode-alt.svg` whose inlined data URL would exceed Coder's\n256-character `workspace_apps.icon` column."},"123":{"id":"/coder-templates-86","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It streams the source as a tar on stdin, with symlinks dereferenced into regular\nfiles. Coder's provisioner unpacks that archive into an empty workdir and does\nnot follow host paths, so a stored runfiles symlink plans as \"No configuration\nfiles\"."},"124":{"id":"/coder-templates-87","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It confirms an **active** version."},"125":{"id":"/coder-templates-88","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It re-sends the template's own description."},"126":{"id":"/coder-templates-89","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#how-push-template-works","content":"It re-pins **Autostop** to *Disabled*."},"127":{"id":"/coder-templates-90","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"No `.terraform.lock.hcl` is committed under `templates/`. Each `main.tf` pins\n`coder/coder` `~> 2.18` and `kreuzwerker/docker` `~> 4.6` in `required_providers`; Coder\npushes with a warning when no lockfile sits beside them. `coder_devcontainer` needs a\nserver `>= 2.21`, which this stack runs (v2.36.6)."},"128":{"id":"/coder-templates-91","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"`terraform init` on the server still talks to `registry.terraform.io` (and\n`registry.coder.com` for `devcontainer`'s `devcontainers_cli` module), even\nwhen a previous provider version is cached. The `coder` compose service\nforwards those lookups to `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` so a host whose only\nnameserver is Tailscale MagicDNS does not SERVFAIL the push. Recreate\n`coder` after pulling that compose change (`prod-stack up`) before pushing."},"129":{"id":"/coder-templates-92","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"`templates push` carries no description (`push` has no `--description` flag), so\n`templates edit --description` is the write. That text is the routing hint the Coder\nAgents chat reads when it picks a template to provision."},"130":{"id":"/coder-templates-93","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"Both the preset descriptions and the two routing hints fit the same 128-byte ceiling.\n`coder_parameter` descriptions are **not** capped (some run past 450 characters) and are\nnot checked."},"131":{"id":"/coder-templates-94","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"A failed description edit and a failed autostop pin only add a note; neither blocks the\ntemplate."},"132":{"id":"/coder-templates-95","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-does-and-does-not-do","content":"A template whose push or verification failed is counted in the\n`N of M template(s) did not reach an active version` line and makes the run exit\nnon-zero, while every other template still gets its push attempt."},"133":{"id":"/coder-templates-96","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"The agent runs `coder_agent.startup_script`, and every `coder_script.script`, as\n`bash -c <body>`. The whole body therefore travels as **one** argument to `execve(2)`,\nand Linux caps a single argument at `MAX_ARG_STRLEN`, 32 pages = 131072 bytes. Measured\non this host: an argument of 131071 bytes execs and one of 131072 fails with `E2BIG`.\nPast the wall the agent logs `fork/exec /bin/bash: argument list too long` with\n`exit_code=255` a few milliseconds in, the home directory never seeds, and the workspace\nbanner reads \"A startup script has failed\". Coder puts no limit there and the plan\nreports no error, so the wall only announces itself on a live recreate."},"134":{"id":"/coder-templates-97","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"The size that matters is not `startup.sh.tftpl`. `main.tf` composes the body from that\ntemplate plus every `templates/common/` payload it inlines, so any edit to the rulebook,\nthe skills, the tmux config, the notes seeder, or the Grok config moves the number. That\nis how six commits crept from 104 KB to 130 KB and one documentation commit crossed the\nwall at 135 KB."},"135":{"id":"/coder-templates-98","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"Two things follow from the mechanism:"},"136":{"id":"/coder-templates-99","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"**Each body is its own `execve`, so each gets its own ceiling.** Splitting a body into\ntwo `coder_script` resources relocates the wall; it does not raise it. On this server\n(v2.36) the start scripts fan out concurrently in an errgroup and `ready` is set only\nafter all of them return, so splitting does not reduce a peak either.\n`coder_script_order` needs Coder `>= v2.39`. This is the escape the stack now uses: the\nthree skill payloads ride `coder_script.stack_skills` rather than the startup argument,\nbecause the devcontainer twin had 491 bytes of headroom left and three more inlined\npayloads could not fit there. `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` still rides the startup\nargument - it predates the split, it fits, and its own tests pin it there."},"137":{"id":"/coder-templates-100","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"**A `#!` line does not help.** The body still becomes\n`execve(env, [env, bash, -c, body])`; the interpreter line is content inside the\nargument."},"138":{"id":"/coder-templates-101","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"`startup_size_guard` in `scripts/push-template.sh` enforces this before the CLI runs,\nbeside the icon and preset-description guards. It sums each body's composed bytes and\nrefuses the push past `STARTUP_SIZE_BOUND` (122880, which leaves 8 KiB, about two\ndocumentation-sized commits, under the kernel's own limit). Its reader is\n`scripts/lib/startup_size.awk`; `//tests:bats_startup_script_size_test` re-derives the\nsame totals with an independent parser and pins the two together, so a guard that starts\nmeasuring something else goes red in review."},"139":{"id":"/coder-templates-102","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"The guard also says the number while it is still a warning: a body within\n`STARTUP_SIZE_WARN` (4096) of the bound prints its composed total and the bytes left,\nbecause the distance to the budget is what a reviewer needs and a hard failure only\never reports the crossing. Measured on 2026-11-06 with the byte algorithm in\n`tests/bats/startup-script-size.bats`: the widest body is now the devcontainer startup\nscript at 115967 bytes, 6913 clear of the bound, with the workspace twin at 113767 and\n9113 clear. Before the trio moved out that same devcontainer body measured 122389 with\nonly 491 clear, and `ez-learn.SKILL.md` plus its heredoc accounted for roughly 7 KB of\nit. The three payloads now travel in their own body, `coder_script.stack_skills`, which\ncomposes to 36847 bytes."},"140":{"id":"/coder-templates-103","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"When a body really does need to grow past the budget, the options are:"},"141":{"id":"/coder-templates-104","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"**Move the prose out.** Rationale belongs in this file, not in `startup.sh.tftpl`.\nComments in the template are bytes in everybody's argument; here they cost nothing."},"142":{"id":"/coder-templates-105","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"**Stage the payload as a file, not an argument.** The obvious resource does not\nexist: the OSS provider's `ResourcesMap` (`provider/provider.go`) registers\n`coder_agent`, `coder_agent_instance`, `coder_ai_task`, `coder_app`,\n`coder_metadata`, `coder_script`, `coder_env`, `coder_devcontainer` and\n`coder_external_agent`, and nothing else. `coder_file` was requested in\n`coder/coder#3055` and closed `not_planned` on 2022-09-23; a maintainer was still\nasking for it in 2025. Do not plan around it."},"143":{"id":"/coder-templates-106","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"**`coder_env` is the only provably-earlier file write.** The agent's\n`writeSecretFiles` (`agent.go`) runs before any start script, `MkdirAll 0700` then\n`WriteFile 0600`. It takes a string value, so it can carry a payload, and it writes\ninto the agent's secret directory rather than anywhere you name."},"144":{"id":"/coder-templates-107","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"**`CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` is the per-script scratch the agent already hands you.**\n`agent/agentscripts/agentscripts.go` appends `CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` and\n`CODER_SCRIPT_BIN_DIR` to every script's environment, which is a writable place to\nassemble something across scripts."},"145":{"id":"/coder-templates-108","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-one-argument-ceiling","content":"What is deliberately not done: bind-mounting `templates/` into the workspace so the\nscript can `cat` a payload off the host, and fetching one over HTTP at boot. The first\ncouples boot to a host path that a template push rewrites, and the second puts a\nnetwork dependency on the path that creates the home directory."},"146":{"id":"/coder-templates-109","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"No arguments pushes every template in `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT` order."},"147":{"id":"/coder-templates-110","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"Arguments are template *names*, and they select which names to push. A name that is not\none of the known names is an error that lists the known names, so a typo cannot quietly\ndo nothing."},"148":{"id":"/coder-templates-111","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"A repeated name is one push, not two, and the push order stays `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT`\norder whatever the argument order is. Naming `devcontainer` first cannot orphan it."},"149":{"id":"/coder-templates-112","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#argument-handling","content":"It is also the manual path after you register an account, and the retry path for one\ntemplate."},"150":{"id":"/coder-templates-113","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"Everything Coder-facing runs **inside the coder container**, against\n`http://127.0.0.1:3000`."},"151":{"id":"/coder-templates-114","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"That is the server's own plain listener (`CODER_HTTP_ADDRESS` binds `0.0.0.0:3000`\ninside the container), reached without crossing the reverse proxy, Authelia, or the WAN."},"152":{"id":"/coder-templates-115","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"`coder login` on the host must reach the API *before* it has a credential, and in\npublic mode the only URL it will use is `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, which Authelia gates."},"153":{"id":"/coder-templates-116","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"Its first unauthenticated call (the first-user check) gets the HTML sign-in page back,\nand the CLI reports that as `unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"`."},"154":{"id":"/coder-templates-117","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"Loopback inside the container cannot be gated, so the push cannot fail that way."},"155":{"id":"/coder-templates-118","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"It runs the image's own `coder` binary, so client and server are the same build."},"156":{"id":"/coder-templates-119","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-it-runs-and-why","content":"No host `coder` CLI and no `coder login` are involved, and a stale host-side session\ncannot aim the push at some other deployment."},"157":{"id":"/coder-templates-120","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"The script mints a short-lived token by inserting a row into the stack's own Postgres."},"158":{"id":"/coder-templates-121","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Same trust model as `coder reset-password`, which also bypasses the API and talks to\nthe database directly."},"159":{"id":"/coder-templates-122","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"The token is scoped to minutes (`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, default 10), deleted on the\nway out including on a failed or interrupted run, never printed, and minted **once** for\nthe whole run however many templates it covers."},"160":{"id":"/coder-templates-123","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Its scope has to be `coder:all` with `allow_list` `{*:*}`: a narrowed token fails the\nCLI's opening organization lookups, so least privilege is not available on v2.36."},"161":{"id":"/coder-templates-124","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Template source travels as a tar on stdin (`coder templates push --directory -`), so\nnothing is copied into the container or its volumes."},"162":{"id":"/coder-templates-125","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"A push is also the only thing that *registers* a template: Coder stores the source as a\nFilestore row, not as files it scans on disk."},"163":{"id":"/coder-templates-126","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"The tar is built from a throwaway staging copy, not from the template dir itself.\n`.terraform/` is excluded (pushing it would put the plan's provider binaries into the\nversion's Filestore row), symlinks are dumped as their targets (`tar -h`), and the\n`templates/common/` payloads are copied in beside `main.tf`. A staged copy that still\nholds a symlink is refused before the CLI runs."},"164":{"id":"/coder-templates-127","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#credentials","content":"Then, per template, it confirms an **active** template version. A push can exit 0 and\nstill leave the template inactive if its Terraform run failed, which shows up as the\ntemplate missing from the create dropdown."},"165":{"id":"/coder-templates-128","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"Nothing else about GitHub travels in a `--var`."},"166":{"id":"/coder-templates-129","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as      | Condition                           |\n| ---------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------- |\n| `DEV_IMAGE`      | `--var image=` | always; default `evo-t1-dev:latest` |","table":"table-0"},"167":{"id":"/coder-templates-130","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`     | Passed as            | Condition                                                     |\n| -------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` | `--var litellm_key=` | only when the key is real, never the `change-me*` placeholder |","table":"table-0"},"168":{"id":"/coder-templates-131","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`  | Passed as                | Condition                           |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------ | ----------------------------------- |\n| `CODER_AGENT_URL` | `--var coder_agent_url=` | only when the key is present at all |","table":"table-0"},"169":{"id":"/coder-templates-132","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`    | Passed as                  | Condition                                                                                        |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `GROK_PROFILE_MODE` | `--var grok_profile_mode=` | when non-empty; the retired `GROK_PROFILE_SHARE` maps (`true` to `shared`, `false` to `private`) |","table":"table-0"},"170":{"id":"/coder-templates-133","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as         | Condition                       |\n| ---------------- | ----------------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `REPO_URL`       | `--var repo_url=` | only when present and non-empty |","table":"table-0"},"171":{"id":"/coder-templates-134","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as                | Condition                                                                                                                               |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `GITHUB_MCP`     | `--var github_mcp=false` | only when the value is `false` (the github MCP server is on by default; any other value pushes nothing and the template default stands) |","table":"table-0"},"172":{"id":"/coder-templates-135","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as                  | Condition                                                                                                                                        |\n| ---------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `CONTEXT7_MCP`   | `--var context7_mcp=false` | only when the value is `false` (the cached context7 MCP server is on by default; any other value pushes nothing and the template default stands) |","table":"table-0"},"173":{"id":"/coder-templates-136","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`   | Passed as                    | Condition                                      |\n| ------------------ | ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` | `--var context7_api_key=...` | only when non-empty; the value is never logged |","table":"table-0"},"174":{"id":"/coder-templates-137","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env` | Passed as                | Condition                      |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------ |\n| `SEARCH_MCP`     | `--var search_mcp=false` | only when the value is `false` |","table":"table-0"},"175":{"id":"/coder-templates-138","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"| Read from `.env`    | Passed as                                              | Condition                                                 |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `STACK_SEARCH_PORT` | `--var searxng_url=http://host.docker.internal:<port>` | always; a blank or non-numeric value falls back to `8181` |","table":"table-0"},"176":{"id":"/coder-templates-139","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"`GITHUB_MCP`, `CONTEXT7_MCP` and `SEARCH_MCP` share one polarity: all three MCP blocks\nship from the template default (`true`), so the only value worth setting is `false`,\nand `true`/unset/empty pushes nothing at all."},"177":{"id":"/coder-templates-140","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"The two research lanes split by question type: a library, framework, CLI or setup\nquestion goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then query docs on that\nid), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool, and a URL already\nin hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`. Grok's native `web_search` is parked\nhere, and native `web_fetch` is unavailable because that tool needs an egress proxy\nendpoint this stack does not run, so `SEARCH_MCP=false` leaves a session with library\ndocs and no way to reach the web."},"178":{"id":"/coder-templates-141","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"A dev stack that moved the search port needs its own template push: `searxng_url`\nis derived from `STACK_SEARCH_PORT` as read from `.env`, so a push made while\n`dev-stack.env` carries `18181` still names `8181` unless the key is set in `.env`\ntoo. A workspace dials the host gateway, where the compose network does not help,\nso the port in the rendered `SEARXNG_URL` has to be the one actually published."},"179":{"id":"/coder-templates-142","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"`repo_url` prefills the Git repository field on both create forms. The Terraform\ndefault is still no clone (`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`).\n`.env.sample` sets `REPO_URL` to this stack's GitHub URL, so a normal push prefills\n`https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git` and **VS Code Web** opens\n`~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. A blank `REPO_URL=` line stays unpassed."},"180":{"id":"/coder-templates-143","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"There is no GitHub-token template variable, so no push ever carries a personal access\ntoken. See GitHub credentials for a workspace."},"181":{"id":"/coder-templates-144","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#env-keys-it-reads","content":"`coder_agent_url` exists so agents keep dialing the local plain listener when\n`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is a public name."},"182":{"id":"/coder-templates-145","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"`CODER_CONTAINER_SERVICE` (default `coder`), `CODER_DB_SERVICE` (default `db`),\n`CODER_CONTAINER_API_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:3000`), `CODER_PUSH_USER`,\n`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, `DOCKER_BIN`, `PROBE_TIMEOUT` (20s) and `VERIFY_TIMEOUT`\n(60s) are the overrides."},"183":{"id":"/coder-templates-146","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"A missing `docker`, `timeout`, `tar` or `openssl`, a stack that is not running, or no\nactive Coder account is a *skip*: a one-line reason and exit 0. `bootstrap.sh` calls\nthis script, and a fresh clone has none of those prerequisites yet."},"184":{"id":"/coder-templates-147","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"`SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` skips the step."},"185":{"id":"/coder-templates-148","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#knobs-and-skips","content":"A `DEV_IMAGE` that is not in the local image store is a note, not a stop: build it\nfirst with `bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`."},"186":{"id":"/coder-templates-149","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"The supported push is `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: no arguments pushes every\ntemplate, a template name retries just that one, and the image tag comes from `DEV_IMAGE`\nin `.env`. The raw equivalent below is what that script does internally — it streams the\nsame tar and makes the same CLI call the script makes for each template, execing into the\ndaemon's `coder` container by name."},"187":{"id":"/coder-templates-150","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"That form addresses the stack through `docker compose` instead, so it names the\nproject with `-p`. From the checkout root the folder name would have said the same thing."},"188":{"id":"/coder-templates-151","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"The service appears twice by necessity: the first `coder` selects the compose service, the\nsecond is the CLI binary inside it."},"189":{"id":"/coder-templates-152","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"Set a token first. `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN` is any token from the Coder **Tokens** page or\nfrom `coder tokens create`."},"190":{"id":"/coder-templates-153","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"Then push `workspace` (the repo is not mounted into the container, so the directory has to\ntravel as a tar):"},"191":{"id":"/coder-templates-154","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"Use this shape for reference, or for pushing a tag `.env` does not carry."},"192":{"id":"/coder-templates-155","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"`coder templates` is the canonical name; `template` is its alias."},"193":{"id":"/coder-templates-156","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-same-push-by-hand","content":"The docker provider resolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from the local image store and never\nattempts a registry pull, which is what keeps workspace creation working with no\ninternet."},"194":{"id":"/coder-templates-157","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Autostop is metadata, not Terraform. Re-pin it with `templates edit` after a push made\nfrom the checkout root, or on the dev stack swap `-p evo-t1-coder-stack` for\n`-p evo-t1-dev --env-file dev-stack.env`."},"195":{"id":"/coder-templates-158","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Point the CLI at the in-container plain listener, with a token by name:"},"196":{"id":"/coder-templates-159","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Then re-pin both values:"},"197":{"id":"/coder-templates-160","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#pinning-autostop-by-hand","content":"Check it in the Coder UI afterwards: **Default autostop** must read *Disabled* (0 h)."},"198":{"id":"/coder-templates-161","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"What a create form shows, in `workspace` order."},"199":{"id":"/coder-templates-162","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Workspace image** (`workspace_image`, dropdown, immutable). `--var image=` seeds its\ndefault and is also injected as its own option, so a custom tag still reaches the\ncontainer while the form stays honest about what it is building."},"200":{"id":"/coder-templates-163","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Docker-in-docker** (`dind`, Yes/No radio, immutable)."},"201":{"id":"/coder-templates-164","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Git repository** (`repo_url`, input, mutable). The Terraform default is empty\n(`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`); after a push that forwards\n`.env` `REPO_URL` (this stack's own repo in the sample) the form prefills that URL.\nAnything you type is cloned into the workspace on the next start."},"202":{"id":"/coder-templates-165","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Git repositories (additional)** (`additional_repos`, textarea, mutable, default\nempty)."},"203":{"id":"/coder-templates-166","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Grok profile** (`grok_profile_mode`, dropdown, immutable, default `shared`).\n`private` gives that one workspace a private `~/.grok` on its own home volume, so one\nworkspace can sit outside the shared profile while its siblings stay inside it.\nSwitching needs a rebuild."},"204":{"id":"/coder-templates-167","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Grok default model** (`grok_default_model`, dropdown, immutable, default `agent`)."},"205":{"id":"/coder-templates-168","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"**Grok config extras** (`grok_config_extra`, textarea, mutable, default empty).\nWhatever you type is appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is only observably per-workspace while the\nprofile mode is `private`: one shared profile is one file for every workspace that\nowner owns, and the last boot to re-render it wins."},"206":{"id":"/coder-templates-169","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#create-form-fields","content":"`devcontainer` asks two questions instead: **Git repository** and **Build repo's dev\ncontainer** (`use_devcontainer`, Yes/No, immutable), plus the same three Grok fields."},"207":{"id":"/coder-templates-170","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"Template variables are pushed defaults; the create form can still override them per\nworkspace."},"208":{"id":"/coder-templates-171","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable | Default             | Notes                                                                                                                       |\n| -------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `image`  | `evo-t1-dev:latest` | Seeds the **Workspace image** dropdown default. In `devcontainer` it decides nothing about the toolchain inside the editor. |","table":"table-1"},"209":{"id":"/coder-templates-172","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable      | Default                               | Notes                                    |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` | Reached through the Docker host gateway. |","table":"table-1"},"210":{"id":"/coder-templates-173","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable      | Default           | Notes                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `LITELLM_API_KEY` |","table":"table-1"},"211":{"id":"/coder-templates-174","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable             | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_default_model` | `agent` | `agent` is the tool-capable Spark lane (thinking `xhigh` by default, picker `low` / `medium` / `xhigh`); `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` bake the level into the alias. |","table":"table-1"},"212":{"id":"/coder-templates-175","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable        | Default | Notes                                                         |\n| --------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `docker_socket` | `\"\"`    | Optional Docker socket URI; empty means the provider default. |","table":"table-1"},"213":{"id":"/coder-templates-176","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable          | Default                            | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `coder_agent_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:3002` | The URL the agent dials. Kept on the local plain listener so a public `CODER_ACCESS_URL` does not hairpin agent traffic through the reverse proxy. Empty keeps the provider-rendered access URL. |","table":"table-1"},"214":{"id":"/coder-templates-177","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable | Default | Notes                                                                                           |\n| -------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dind`   | `false` | Turns on the per-workspace privileged daemon described below. Does not exist in `devcontainer`. |","table":"table-1"},"215":{"id":"/coder-templates-178","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable   | Default                                                                                                                                                 | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repo_url` | `\"\"` in `workspace`, `https://github.com/coder/coder` in `devcontainer` (Terraform); `.env.sample` `REPO_URL` overrides both to this stack's GitHub URL | Prefills the **Git repository** field. Both templates clone that URL on the first start; a later start leaves a folder that already holds a `.git` alone. `REPO_URL` in `.env` overrides the default. The sample value is this stack's own repo so **VS Code Web** opens `~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. |","table":"table-1"},"216":{"id":"/coder-templates-179","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable           | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `additional_repos` | `\"\"`    | The **Git repositories (additional)** textarea under the repository field. Extra URLs cloned into their own folder beside `repo_url`. |","table":"table-1"},"217":{"id":"/coder-templates-180","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable            | Default  | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_profile_mode` | `shared` | `shared` mounts the owner's shared `~/.grok` profile volume; `private` gives that workspace a private `~/.grok` on its own home volume. Also the default behind the per-workspace **Grok profile** dropdown; switching a live workspace between the two modes needs a rebuild. |","table":"table-1"},"218":{"id":"/coder-templates-181","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable     | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `github_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the `github` MCP HTTP server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. Its `Authorization` header names `$GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, so no token value is ever part of a template variable. Set `GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |","table":"table-1"},"219":{"id":"/coder-templates-182","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable       | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `context7_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the cached `context7` MCP stdio server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. The key is optional, so it ships without one. Set `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |","table":"table-1"},"220":{"id":"/coder-templates-183","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable           | Default           | Notes                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `context7_api_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `CONTEXT7_API_KEY`. The rendered file names the variable. |","table":"table-1"},"221":{"id":"/coder-templates-184","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"| Variable            | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_config_extra` | `\"\"`    | The **Grok config extras** textarea: free-form TOML appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. |","table":"table-1"},"222":{"id":"/coder-templates-185","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"Notes on the two dropdowns that take a custom value:"},"223":{"id":"/coder-templates-186","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"The `grok_default_model` aliases are `agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`,\n`agent-xhigh`, `coder`, `coder-fast` and `chat`, plus a **Custom** entry when the\nvariable names another one."},"224":{"id":"/coder-templates-187","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"The provider requires a parameter default to be one of its options, which is why a\ncustom `--var` value is injected as its own option instead of being dropped."},"225":{"id":"/coder-templates-188","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#template-variables","content":"A `github_mcp` of `true` with no GitHub account connected gives that workspace a server\nthat fails its handshake once per session, because `$GH_TOKEN` is empty. A\n`context7_mcp` of `true` with an empty `context7_api_key` is *not* the same shape:\nthe key is optional, the block renders either way, and the server handshakes and\nanswers without one - no `Authorization` header goes out, so the lane runs at the\npublic rate limit and private libraries are the only thing out of reach. That keyless\nblock is the pushed default and what a fresh deploy gets, since nothing in git\ncarries a key, and `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` only warns about it. Park a\nlane deliberately with `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed\nby `push-template.sh`) or `--var github_mcp=false` / `--var context7_mcp=false` on\nthe push; a block missing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a\npark. Fill the context7 key with `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in `.env` plus a re-push when a\nsession needs private libraries or a higher rate limit."},"226":{"id":"/coder-templates-189","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Autostart, autostop and TTLs are template *metadata*, so they stay out of the Terraform\n(where they would fight the UI) and travel on the CLI after pushing."},"227":{"id":"/coder-templates-190","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"**Autostop is disabled by default.**"},"228":{"id":"/coder-templates-191","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Coder reads the template's **Default autostop** (its default TTL) when it seeds a\nworkspace. That field also decides whether the create form's **Enable Autostop**\narrives ticked, and it ships at 24 h - which is how a workspace would otherwise start\nwith a shutdown deadline nobody chose."},"229":{"id":"/coder-templates-192","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"A 0 default TTL leaves the checkbox unchecked and leaves `coder create` with nothing to\ninherit, so a workspace runs until its owner stops it."},"230":{"id":"/coder-templates-193","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"`--activity-bump` goes to `0` with it: a bump only extends a deadline, and with no\ndeadline there is nothing to extend."},"231":{"id":"/coder-templates-194","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"`push-template.sh` re-sends both after every push, and `new-workspace.sh` names no\n`--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline either."},"232":{"id":"/coder-templates-195","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"A workspace owner can still tick the box for one workspace: `allow_user_autostop`\nstays on, because CE cannot turn it off -- `--allow-user-autostop=false` is marked\n\"can only be disabled in enterprise\"."},"233":{"id":"/coder-templates-196","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Keep the other licensed flags out of the CE stack too. `--failure-ttl` (Failure\ncleanup), `--dormancy-threshold` and `--dormancy-auto-deletion` are all marked \"This\nlicensed feature's default is 0h (off)\" in `templates edit --help` on v2.36.6, and\n`--allow-user-autostart` works the same way. Older notes also list `--inactivityTTL`\namong the rejected names; v2.36.6 has no flag of that name, and `--default-ttl` and\n`--activity-bump` are the closest."},"234":{"id":"/coder-templates-197","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#autostop-and-ttls","content":"Check it in the Coder UI: the template editor's **Default autostop** reads *Disabled*\n(0 h), and a workspace's **Schedule** tab shows **Enable Autostop** unchecked."},"235":{"id":"/coder-templates-198","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Opt-in in `workspace` (the **Docker-in-docker** Yes/No question, preset *AI workspace +\nDinD*, template variable `dind`). With the toggle off, `docker` in that workspace fails\nthe normal way rather than against a daemon that does not exist."},"236":{"id":"/coder-templates-199","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"With it on, per workspace:"},"237":{"id":"/coder-templates-200","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"One privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on a bridge network private to that\nworkspace (`coder-<wsid>-dind`, network alias `dind`)."},"238":{"id":"/coder-templates-201","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The workspace container attaches to **both** `bridge` and that private network; once a\n`networks_advanced` block appears the provider stops letting `network_mode` do the\nattaching, so `bridge` has to be listed explicitly."},"239":{"id":"/coder-templates-202","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` is injected into the agent environment - absent when the\ntoggle is off."},"240":{"id":"/coder-templates-203","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`docker build`, `docker run` and `docker compose` then work inside the workspace. The\n*AI workspace + DinD* preset is the usual way to get this shape, since the client\nbinaries ship only in the golden image."},"241":{"id":"/coder-templates-204","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Details that matter:"},"242":{"id":"/coder-templates-205","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The toggle is `mutable = false`: turning it on or off means a workspace **rebuild**,\nbecause a network, a volume and a second container appear or vanish. The preset makes\nthat one click."},"243":{"id":"/coder-templates-206","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Only the literal string `\"true\"` enables it; anything else reads as off."},"244":{"id":"/coder-templates-207","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The daemon's `/var/lib/docker` lives on its own volume (`coder-<wsid>-dind-lib`), so a\nstop/start does not re-pull every image the builds used. It is not gated on\n`start_count`, so it survives a stop and is reused by the next start."},"245":{"id":"/coder-templates-208","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` is deliberately **empty**, so the daemon serves plain\n`tcp://0.0.0.0:2375` with no TLS and no auth. (`DOCKER_TLS_CERTSIZE=0` does not do\nthis: the daemon would listen on 2376 with TLS and the client fails against 2375.)"},"246":{"id":"/coder-templates-209","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"Nothing is published to the host, and 2375 is reachable only from that workspace's own\nnetwork - which is also why the network is not `internal`. An internal network gets no\ngateway route or NAT, so `host.docker.internal` would resolve and never answer, and the\nagent would strand. See SECURITY.md."},"247":{"id":"/coder-templates-210","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"The daemon image is resolved with `data \"docker_image\"`, which inspects the host store\nand **never pulls**, so `docker:29.8.1-dind` must already be there. `bootstrap.sh`\npre-arms it; to do it manually:"},"248":{"id":"/coder-templates-211","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"A missing image fails the workspace build at **plan** time, before anything is\ncreated, with `did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` - see\ntroubleshooting.md."},"249":{"id":"/coder-templates-212","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`cgroupns_mode = \"private\"` and `shm_size = 1024` (MB to the provider, so 1 GiB) are\nboth load-bearing; the sidecar is torn down with the workspace because it is gated on\n`start_count` as well as on the toggle."},"250":{"id":"/coder-templates-213","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#docker-in-docker","content":"`DOCKER_CLI_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and the `docker:29.8.1-dind` tag\nin `templates/workspace/main.tf` and `templates/devcontainer/main.tf` are the same\nengine line (29.8.1). Bump all three together."},"251":{"id":"/coder-templates-214","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-devcontainer-template","content":"That is the *AI workspace + DinD* shape, always on, plus a repository that brings its own\ntoolchain."},"252":{"id":"/coder-templates-215","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"One workspace container from the golden image: launcher and clone target only. It still\nhas to be the golden image, because that is what ships the Docker client the\ndevcontainer flow talks through. The editor's real environment is whatever the repo\nbuilds, so `image` decides nothing about the toolchain inside it."},"253":{"id":"/coder-templates-216","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"An always-on privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on the per-workspace bridge\nnetwork, with `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` injected **unconditionally** and\n`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` empty for the reasons above. `internal` stays false for the same\none reason."},"254":{"id":"/coder-templates-217","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"`dind` is not a variable here. A devcontainer workspace without a daemon is nothing,\nand the plain-clone fallback still wants a daemon its `docker` commands can reach."},"255":{"id":"/coder-templates-218","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#shape","content":"One host bind, `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>`, mounted at that **identical**\npath in both containers."},"256":{"id":"/coder-templates-219","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"The devcontainer CLI always bind-mounts the workspace folder into the dev container it\ncreates, and a bind source is resolved by the daemon that *runs* that container - here\nthe sidecar, not the workspace's."},"257":{"id":"/coder-templates-220","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"So the clone must exist at the same path inside the sidecar too, or it dies with\n`bind source path does not exist`."},"258":{"id":"/coder-templates-221","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"Named volumes cannot substitute: a volume is a daemon-local object and does not cross\nto the sidecar's daemon."},"259":{"id":"/coder-templates-222","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"Docker creates the missing host directory itself (root-owned) and `startup.sh.tftpl`\nchowns it."},"260":{"id":"/coder-templates-223","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"Inside a built dev container the same folder appears under `/workspaces/<repo-name>`,\nthe devcontainer CLI's default mount target and where the dev-container editor opens."},"261":{"id":"/coder-templates-224","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"`main.tf` therefore spells the editor's `folder` per hosting environment\n(`local.editor_folder`): `/workspaces/<repo-name>` inside the dev container,\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>` on the plain container, and\n`/home/coder` when no repository was named."},"262":{"id":"/coder-templates-225","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-shared-bind-path-is-load-bearing","content":"One host-side path for all three would open the editor on a folder that exists nowhere\nthe editor runs, and VS Code answers that with\n`Workspace does not exist. Please select another workspace to open.`"},"263":{"id":"/coder-templates-226","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-two-questions","content":"**Git repository** (`repo_url`) is mutable: switching repository is a re-clone plus a\ndevcontainer build, not a new topology the way `workspace`'s DinD toggle is."},"264":{"id":"/coder-templates-227","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-two-questions","content":"**Build repo's dev container** (`use_devcontainer`, default `true`) is immutable:\nbuilding a dev container or not is a different topology. With Yes the editor lives in\nthe dev container; with No, in the plain container."},"265":{"id":"/coder-templates-228","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-two-questions","content":"A repository that ships no `devcontainer.json` gets no dev container and hence no\neditor with the toggle on. The toggle - or\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url>`, which reads the repository and\npicks for you - is how that case is meant to be handled."},"266":{"id":"/coder-templates-229","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"`startup.sh.tftpl` clones the repository onto that bind in **both** toggle modes."},"267":{"id":"/coder-templates-230","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"With the toggle on, `coder_devcontainer.repo` then builds the repo's own\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` on the sidecar through the Coder agent\n(`@devcontainers/cli` is installed into the parent workspace container by a\n`run_on_start` module, and the golden image does not ship it)."},"268":{"id":"/coder-templates-231","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"That exposes the dev container as a sub-agent with its own terminal and apps, and the\nin-browser editor attaches to *that* sub-agent, so the editor runs inside the dev\ncontainer where the cloned repo's toolchain lives."},"269":{"id":"/coder-templates-232","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"With the toggle off - or on a repo that carries no `devcontainer.json`, where the build\nhas nothing to build - the editor attaches to the plain workspace agent instead, opened\non the same clone path."},"270":{"id":"/coder-templates-233","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"Either way `folder` is the clone, so code-server and its terminal profiles land on the\nrepository, and the **Web Terminal** button `cd`s there too."},"271":{"id":"/coder-templates-234","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"No `config_path` is set, so the CLI discovers the file the way VS Code does, and a repo\nusing the other legal locations behaves the same."},"272":{"id":"/coder-templates-235","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#clone-and-build","content":"`coder_script.dind_bootstrap` is an ordering guard, not a starter: it waits up to 120s\n(180s cap) for the sidecar to answer `docker version`, because the agent runs its\n`run_on_start` scripts before it creates the dev containers."},"273":{"id":"/coder-templates-236","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-reuses-and-the-trust-warning","content":"The mount covers the workspace container only; a dev container built on the sidecar\nkeeps its own home either way."},"274":{"id":"/coder-templates-237","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-reuses-and-the-trust-warning","content":"It carries its own copies of `startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl` and\n`grok-config.toml.tftpl`, so the tmux-default terminal and LiteLLM wiring above apply\nto it too."},"275":{"id":"/coder-templates-238","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-reuses-and-the-trust-warning","content":"**Treat the repository as untrusted code.** Everything it builds runs on a privileged\ndaemon on this box, so a Dockerfile in that repo is root-equivalent on the stack host.\nThe create form's own description says so, and only repositories you trust belong in\nthe field."},"276":{"id":"/coder-templates-239","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url> [name]` answers the two questions\nabove for you: which template, and whether to build the dev container."},"277":{"id":"/coder-templates-240","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"The `--` ends Bazel's own flags, so a URL that begins with a dash still reaches the\nscript. Every fence below is one complete command."},"278":{"id":"/coder-templates-241","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"Dry run (decision only, creates nothing):"},"279":{"id":"/coder-templates-242","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"Create the workspace it picks:"},"280":{"id":"/coder-templates-243","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#from-a-git-url-bazelisk-run-scriptsnew-workspace","content":"Force the `workspace` template:"},"281":{"id":"/coder-templates-244","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It shallow-clones (`--depth 1 --no-checkout --single-branch`) into a temporary\ndirectory, from the host, and lists the tracked files."},"282":{"id":"/coder-templates-245","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It looks for a dev container in `.devcontainer/`, `.github/devcontainers/`, or a\n`devcontainer.json`/`devcontainer.yml` anywhere in the tree."},"283":{"id":"/coder-templates-246","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"A tracked `.devcontainer/contributor-env` outranks every other signal: the probe is a\n**miss** even when the same clone also tracks `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`. That is\nhow this repo keeps its own Coder workspaces on `workspace` — the `.devcontainer/` it\nships is the contributor one for a laptop and CI, not a nested toolchain to rebuild\ninside the workspace's own DinD sidecar (see\ncontribute/devcontainer.md). Application repositories do\nnot carry the sentinel, so their dev containers stay a hit."},"284":{"id":"/coder-templates-247","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"Found means `devcontainer`; not found means `workspace`. A probe that fails **entirely**\nis also a `workspace` answer, with a warning."},"285":{"id":"/coder-templates-248","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"The clone is a plain `git clone` from the host, so it consults the host's own git\ncredentials. A private repository therefore needs a grant covering it **on the machine\nyou run this from**."},"286":{"id":"/coder-templates-249","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It then assembles `coder create` with the template's parameters: `repo_url=<url>`\nalways, `use_devcontainer=false` whenever `devcontainer` was chosen but no dev\ncontainer was found (forced with `--devcontainer` onto a repo that has none, or a probe\nthat could not check the repo - the cases where leaving the toggle at its default would\nbuild nothing anyway), `dind=true` on `--dind`, and `additional_repos=<list>` whenever\n`--additional-repos` named at least one extra URL."},"287":{"id":"/coder-templates-250","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It names no `--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline."},"288":{"id":"/coder-templates-251","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It either runs the command (`--no-wait`; watch with `coder ls` / `coder logs <name>`)\nor, with no usable `coder` login, prints the same command to run by hand and exits 0."},"289":{"id":"/coder-templates-252","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"`--devcontainer` forces the devcontainer template and `--template <name>` overrides the\ntemplate choice outright."},"290":{"id":"/coder-templates-253","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"A workspace name can follow the URL. Coder names start with a letter (40 characters\nmax); the derived default is the repository leaf."},"291":{"id":"/coder-templates-254","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-it-decides","content":"It never mints, reads, or stores a token - unlike `push-template.sh`, which mints its\nown because a template push has no other credential path. With `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN`\nset but `CODER_URL` unset it refuses to guess, and an empty token is a *not yet* state\nrather than an error."},"292":{"id":"/coder-templates-255","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"Each template creates one Docker container per workspace. Extra named volumes\n(`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` at `~/.shared/vscode`, and `grok-profile-<owner-id>`\nat `~/.grok` in shared mode) are chowned to the home owner at the start of\n`startup.sh.tftpl`, because Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root`\n755 and the agent runs as `coder`."},"293":{"id":"/coder-templates-256","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"Image from `images/dev/Dockerfile` (overridable with the `image` variable) carrying:\nNode 22 + 24, Python 3.12 + 3.11, Go 1.24, Bazelisk/buildifier,\nTerraform/tflint/terragrunt, protoc and its Go plugins, kubectl/helm/kubeconform,\nshfmt/shellcheck/bats/ruff/mypy/prettier, the Coder CLI, and the pinned Grok Build CLI\nat `/usr/local/bin/grok`."},"294":{"id":"/coder-templates-257","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"The Docker **client** toolchain only (`docker`, `docker buildx`, `docker compose` as\nCLI plugins under `/usr/local/lib/docker/cli-plugins`). No daemon: the build asserts\n`dockerd`, `containerd`, `runc` and `docker-proxy` are absent."},"295":{"id":"/coder-templates-258","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"The five offline MCP servers ship in the image, but the rendered config declares none\nof them by default, so a workspace needs no network to start a session."},"296":{"id":"/coder-templates-259","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"A persistent per-workspace home volume (`coder-<wsid>-home`)."},"297":{"id":"/coder-templates-260","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"`host.docker.internal` mapped to `host-gateway`, so workspaces reach the Coder server\nand LiteLLM."},"298":{"id":"/coder-templates-261","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"`startup_script_behavior = \"blocking\"`, so a workspace only reports *ready* once the\ntoolchain is genuinely staged - no opening an editor mid-install."},"299":{"id":"/coder-templates-262","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#inside-the-workspace-container","content":"`connection_timeout = 300`."},"300":{"id":"/coder-templates-263","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Installs `tmux` if it is missing, then seeds `~/.tmux.conf` (only while that file is\nabsent or still carries the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker)."},"301":{"id":"/coder-templates-264","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Ensures the **Grok Build CLI** is on PATH. The golden image ships it, so the\n`https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` download runs only where `grok` is missing from PATH -\nthe `ubuntu:24.04` image option, or a home volume that never received the CLI."},"302":{"id":"/coder-templates-265","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Puts `~/.grok/bin` on PATH (login shells and that process)."},"303":{"id":"/coder-templates-266","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Writes the two terminal-profile tmux helpers (see below). The default one gives each\nvisible terminal its own pane: it re-joins a folder's session only while nothing is\nattached to it, and otherwise opens a numbered sibling."},"304":{"id":"/coder-templates-267","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Seeds the Coder Agents context files, then the shared code-server User settings, only\nfor files that are absent."},"305":{"id":"/coder-templates-268","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"Renders `~/.grok/config.toml` (the three gated MCP servers ship by default; the five\noffline stdio ones are not declared)."},"306":{"id":"/coder-templates-269","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"**Clones a non-empty Git repository field** into `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`\n(in `devcontainer`, onto the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>` bind). The\nscript creates that folder first (`mkdir -p` plus a chown to the workspace user) so\nthe editor's wait loop can see it, then clones into it. This is idempotent, runs as\nthe workspace user so their git credentials are consulted, and refuses to touch an\nexisting non-empty non-git folder there. An empty pre-created target still clones."},"307":{"id":"/coder-templates-270","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#what-the-startup-script-does-in-order","content":"The repository leaf is derived the same way in `main.tf` and in `startup.sh.tftpl`:\nsplit on `/`, drop empty segments, take the last, drop a trailing `.git`, map every\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` to `-`, and fall back to `repo` when that leaves no\nleading alphanumeric. Both derivations must agree, or the editor and the terminal\nhelpers point at a folder that was never cloned."},"308":{"id":"/coder-templates-271","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"The order above is not incidental; each line is a constraint the script has to keep."},"309":{"id":"/coder-templates-272","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"`~/.tmux.conf` is seeded *before* the two terminal helpers. `tmux` reads that file once\nat server start and every terminal (plus an SSH client that reattaches) shares that one\nserver, so seeding after the first `tmux new-session -A` could run would leave that\nsession unconfigured for the whole boot. `mouse off` is what keeps every click with\nthe terminal client: tmux requests no mouse reporting, so a drag is the client's own\nhighlight, the settings seed's `copyOnSelection` puts it on the machine clipboard,\nand right-click and middle-click paste it back. Measured on the shipped tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient and an attach with it off writes none of them. tmux keeps no clipboard of its\nown next to that: the copy-mode copies, the prefix paste `]`, the buffer picker `=`\nand the root-table middle-click paste are unbound, and `Prefix m` is only the switch\nthat hands the wheel to tmux for a long scroll and hands it back.\n`set-clipboard on` and `allow-passthrough on` stay for the one copy route that is\nnot tmux's own - an app inside a pane (vim's `+` register, anything that emits\nOSC 52) drives the machine clipboard through them, which is what a native terminal\nhands that app - and a client that blocks OSC 52 (iTerm2 by default) gets nothing at\nall, because there is no tmux buffer left to fall back to. The browser build of VS\nCode drops that escape as well\n(microsoft/vscode#318254), so a\npane app's OSC 52 copy is silent in the tab and lands over ssh from a real terminal\napp, while the operator's own copy in the tab is the plain drag."},"310":{"id":"/coder-templates-273","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"The clone folder is created before git runs, and before the `coder_script.code_server`\nscript's own `mkdir`: the editor starts in parallel with the startup script, and an\nempty tree is a folder VS Code can open while the clone is still writing. An empty\ndirectory is also what `git clone` accepts, so pre-creating it does not change what gets\ncloned - the emptiness guard still refuses a folder holding anything."},"311":{"id":"/coder-templates-274","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"`notes_materialize` runs *before* the recursive chown of a fresh clone: the notes file\nand the `.git/info/exclude` write are the stack's to make, and the chown afterwards\nsettles their ownership."},"312":{"id":"/coder-templates-275","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"The chown of `~/.grok` and `~/.shared` runs a second time at the end of the script,\nbecause the CLI installer and the seeds above may have created files as root when the\nagent ran as root."},"313":{"id":"/coder-templates-276","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"`run_notes` and `run_notes_watch` follow the clone's identity rule rather than the\nagent's uid, because that stage writes into the workspace home and runs\n`git config --global`: run as root it would rename a root-owned `~/.gitconfig` over the\nworkspace user's and leave `notes.md` unreadable on an already-cloned folder."},"314":{"id":"/coder-templates-277","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"The watcher left at the end is what makes notes survive a recreate that lands *between*\nedits. The boot-time sync runs once, so anything written later lived solely inside that\ncontainer, which is how a whole workspace's notes went missing on a recreate."},"315":{"id":"/coder-templates-278","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#why-the-stages-sit-where-they-do","content":"Every stage from the Coder Agents seeds onward is best-effort: an offline first boot has\nto leave a working terminal, and a repository that will not clone has to leave a\nworkspace someone can fix, so nothing there may abort the script. In `devcontainer` a\nmissing or half-cloned folder is not silent either: `coder_devcontainer` fails on its own\nwith a visible error naming the folder it looked in, and the clone stage warns first when\na clone arrives with no devcontainer configuration in it."},"316":{"id":"/coder-templates-279","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-grok-build-cli-probe-the-download-and-the-installer-stub","content":"The install check is `command -v grok`, not `[ -x ~/.grok/bin/grok ]`: the golden image\nbakes the CLI into `/usr/local/bin`, so on a golden-image boot the probe answers yes and\nnothing downloads. The download exists for the **Minimal shell** (`ubuntu:24.04`) preset\nand for a home volume that never received a CLI."},"317":{"id":"/coder-templates-280","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-grok-build-cli-probe-the-download-and-the-installer-stub","content":"It is bounded and retried on purpose: one unbounded attempt burned a whole start on a\nsingle stalled connection to x.ai (`curl: (28) SSL connection timeout`) and still booted\nthe workspace without a CLI."},"318":{"id":"/coder-templates-281","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-grok-build-cli-probe-the-download-and-the-installer-stub","content":"`GROK_FRESH_INSTALL` records that an install *completed* this boot - the second\n`command -v` probe inside the install branch is what sets it, so a failed download leaves\nthe flag at `0`. The installer writes its own `~/.grok/config.toml` (a\n`[cli] installer = ...` stub) that is indistinguishable from a user's config, so the\nconfig stage may treat that stub as stack-owned only on a completed install: a download\nthat failed must never wipe a user-owned config in its place. Without that flag a\nbrand-new workspace would be left with an unconfigured CLI, because the stub carries\nneither stack marker and the file already exists."},"319":{"id":"/coder-templates-282","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"Most seeded files are written only while absent, so a hand-edited copy is user-owned and\nthe stage re-runs harmlessly. Three of them are re-written on every start, and ownership is\ndecided by a marker - never by mtime and never by first boot:"},"320":{"id":"/coder-templates-283","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"`~/.tmux.conf` and `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` are rewritten while the\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` line is present; editing the file away from the marker\nhands it to the user for good, and each prints one warning line when it is marker-free."},"321":{"id":"/coder-templates-284","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"`~/.grok/config.toml` is re-rendered when it is absent, when\n`GROK_FRESH_INSTALL` is set, or while *either* marker is present: the `managed-by`\ncomment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key."},"322":{"id":"/coder-templates-285","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"The key is what makes the rule hold. The CLI's own settings writer (`grok mcp add` and\nfriends) deletes every comment line, so a comment-only marker can vanish with nobody\ntouching the file."},"323":{"id":"/coder-templates-286","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"On a shared profile volume the config file usually already exists, because another of the\nowner's workspaces wrote it, so the marker rather than first boot is what decides."},"324":{"id":"/coder-templates-287","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"Re-rendering carries over the tables the CLI owns instead of wiping them: `[cli]` holds\n`cli.installer`, which picks the CLI's update path; `[ui]` holds `permission_mode`, so\nre-adopting a file must never flip an operator back to prompting; and `[marketplace]` is\nCLI settings the stack never owns."},"325":{"id":"/coder-templates-288","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"The unmanaged case is announced rather than silent, because the render is the only place\na workspace's model catalog and MCP lanes are ever written."},"326":{"id":"/coder-templates-289","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#marker-ownership-of-the-seeded-files","content":"`~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` is the exception to the absent-only rule: it is stack-owned code\nand the copy on the shared profile volume has to be the one this template shipped, so it\nis rewritten every start. It is written beside its target and `mv`'d in - and `chmod\n0644`'d first, because `mktemp` makes 0600 and `mv` keeps that mode - so the rename is the\nfirst moment the file exists for anyone to source, and a sibling workspace booting at the\nsame time against the one shared volume never sources a half-written library."},"327":{"id":"/coder-templates-290","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-shared-grok-profile-volume","content":"`~/.grok` is not necessarily a workspace's own directory, and that decides several of the\nrules above."},"328":{"id":"/coder-templates-291","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-shared-grok-profile-volume","content":"In `shared` mode (the default `grok_profile_mode`) the container mounts the owner's\nshared per-user Docker volume read-write at `/home/coder/.grok`, nested inside that\nworkspace's own `/home/coder` volume. The terminal helpers, `config.toml`,\n`notes-setup.sh` and the `memory-v2/` tree the CLI keeps there are then the same files\nevery other workspace this owner has reads and writes, and they survive a rebuild."},"329":{"id":"/coder-templates-292","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-shared-grok-profile-volume","content":"That is also why `config.toml` cannot be per-workspace in that mode: it is one file, and\nthe last workspace to boot re-renders it for all of them, so this workspace's default\nmodel (or, in `devcontainer`, its model alias) and config extras overwrite whatever the\nprevious boot wrote - for every sibling."},"330":{"id":"/coder-templates-293","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-shared-grok-profile-volume","content":"`private` mounts nothing extra: the tree then lives on this workspace's own home volume,\nso its `config.toml`, defaults and extras included, is its own and dies with it."},"331":{"id":"/coder-templates-294","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-shared-grok-profile-volume","content":"In `devcontainer` the mount is on the workspace container only. A dev container the repo\nbuilds on the sidecar keeps its own home and its own `~/.grok`, which is why the live\n`notes-setup.sh` there is a regular file reached through the clone bind rather than a\nlink into `~/.grok`, and why a built dev container sees neither the settings volume nor\nthe editor symlink."},"332":{"id":"/coder-templates-295","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-shared-grok-profile-volume","content":"The Coder Agents seeds under `~/.coder` and `~/.agents` are not on that mount: they live\non the workspace's own home volume, and that stage neither needs nor disturbs it."},"333":{"id":"/coder-templates-296","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-coder-agents-seeds-and-their-agents-tree-twins","content":"The chat agent discovers its context through its *default* paths (verified against the\nv2.36.6 agent binary and its `agentcontextconfig` source): the instruction file\n`AGENTS.md` under `~/.coder`, skills under `~/.coder/skills`, and a working-dir-relative\n`.agents/skills`, which here resolves to `/home/coder/.agents/skills` - the same folder\nthe Grok Build CLI looks in."},"334":{"id":"/coder-templates-297","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-coder-agents-seeds-and-their-agents-tree-twins","content":"Neither `main.tf` sets `CODER_AGENT_EXP_SKILLS_DIRS` or the\n`CODER_AGENT_EXP_INSTRUCTIONS_*` keys: those *replace* the default lists rather than\nappending, and the seeded copies already sit where the unmodified defaults point. Chat\nitself is on by default in 2.36.6 - no experiment name and no entitlement gates it."},"335":{"id":"/coder-templates-298","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-coder-agents-seeds-and-their-agents-tree-twins","content":"Each file also gets a twin in the `~/.agents` tree, because the `execute`-lane delegation\nthis stack teaches starts `grok -p` inside the workspace and that CLI reads the\n`~/.agents` convention. The chat agent would only ever take the `~/.coder` copies - skill\ndedupe is first-match-wins, home scope before working-dir paths - so one agent never\nloads a skill twice; the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."},"336":{"id":"/coder-templates-299","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-coder-agents-seeds-and-their-agents-tree-twins","content":"A twin is copied only while absent, and only from the managed `~/.coder` copy, so a\nhand-placed file in `~/.agents` is never overwritten and the dedupe keeps exactly one\nlive source. The CLI-side spelling of the instruction file keeps the `INSTRUCTIONS.md`\nname because that is the name the `~/.agents` convention was seeded under."},"337":{"id":"/coder-templates-300","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-coder-agents-seeds-and-their-agents-tree-twins","content":"In `devcontainer`, working-dir resolution has a second case: with the dev-container\ntoggle off the agent's working dir is `/home/coder`, exactly as in `workspace`; with it\non, the dev container's own working dir is the cloned folder under\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, and that folder ships no skill tree, so the `~/.coder`\ncopies remain the live source either way."},"338":{"id":"/coder-templates-301","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-coder-agents-seeds-and-their-agents-tree-twins","content":"In a `SKILL.md` the marker sits *after* the `---` frontmatter: the skill parser needs\n`---` on line 1 and silently drops a skill whose first line is anything else. In an\ninjected instruction file the marker is pure provenance, because injected content has its\nHTML comments stripped anyway. The stage is best-effort: a read-only or missing `$HOME`\nchanges nothing observable."},"339":{"id":"/coder-templates-302","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-coder-agents-seeds-and-their-agents-tree-twins","content":"`/ez-learn` is seeded on the same absent-only contract because nothing here supplies the\ncommand: no published source ships an upstream `/learn` at all, and the built-in memory\nfamily it copies is gated behind a memory store that is off by default, so those names\nstay hidden. The bundle is a separate thing, and a real one: the CLI extracts\nplatform skills such as `create-skill` into `~/.grok/bundled/`, read back with\n`SkillScope::Bundled`, and that catalog is fetched per identity/team behind an x.ai\nsign-in this stack deliberately does not have - LiteLLM is the only transport - so the\ndirectory is never created and none of those skills ever lands. Writing the file is what\nmakes each command exist, and it is seeded under the `ez-` name, which exists to stay\nclear of the upstream ones. The same stage deletes a leftover `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from\nbefore the rename, but only while it still carries the stack marker; a marker-free one is\nuser-owned and stays. Deleting a seeded copy never keeps your own - the boot writes it\nback - so only dropping that marker does."},"340":{"id":"/coder-templates-303","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#startup-script-templatefile-rules","content":"`startup.sh.tftpl` is rendered by `templatefile()`, so every braced shell expansion has\nto be written `$${name}`; the one-dollar braced form is swallowed as an interpolation.\nThe unbraced form (`$name`) needs no escape."},"341":{"id":"/coder-templates-304","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#startup-script-templatefile-rules","content":"Only the variables `main.tf` passes interpolate. The `workspace` startup script gets\ntwelve: `grok_config`, `grok_build_agents`, `notes_setup`, `tmux_conf`,\n`grok_build_terminal`, `vscode_terminal_tmux`, `grok_build_delegation_skill`,\n`coder_agents`, `mcp_cache_proxy`, `repo_url`, `additional_repos`, `vscode_settings`.\nThe `devcontainer` twin adds `dc_dir` and `build_devcontainer`. The three skill payloads\nare not among them: `ez_learn_skill`, `ez_skillify_skill` and `ez_create_skill_skill`\nbelong to the `coder_script.stack_skills` map, and their `chomp(file())` calls sit inside\nthat block rather than behind a `local.`, because both size readers credit a body by the\n`path.module}` names in its own block."},"342":{"id":"/coder-templates-305","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#startup-script-templatefile-rules","content":"That is why the `.git` suffix is stripped with `sed` rather than the shorter\n`${leaf%.git}`: a braced expansion would add an interpolation `main.tf` does not pass."},"343":{"id":"/coder-templates-306","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#startup-script-templatefile-rules","content":"The repository URLs arrive as rendered literals and are read through a *quoted* heredoc,\nso no shell quoting has to survive and the value is only ever expanded as data. An empty\nGit repository field means a home-directory workspace, so the whole clone stage is\nskipped rather than attempted with an empty URL."},"344":{"id":"/coder-templates-307","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#startup-script-templatefile-rules","content":"`~/.grok/config.toml` is emitted from a quoted heredoc for the same reason: a\n`Bearer $${GH_TOKEN}` line the config template emits travels through as literal text\ninstead of being expanded there, and the CLI expands it from the agent environment. The\nsame holds for the `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` reference."},"345":{"id":"/coder-templates-308","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#startup-script-templatefile-rules","content":"`grok_config` arrives already rendered from `grok-config.toml.tftpl`. Its boolean keys\n`github_mcp`, `search_mcp` and `context7_mcp` only decide whether their\n`[mcp_servers.*]` block appears, and no credential byte is part of that decision;\n`search_url` supplies the URL the searxng block dials, and the workspace affinity key is\na per-workspace routing hint (a workspace UUID, not a secret)."},"346":{"id":"/coder-templates-309","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-as-the-workspace-user","content":"`clone_owner` is whichever user the workspace really belongs to: the owner of `$HOME`\nwhen the agent runs as root, otherwise whoever is running the script. It decides both the\nchown target and whose git credentials the clone consults."},"347":{"id":"/coder-templates-310","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-as-the-workspace-user","content":"The parent folder (`/home/coder/workspace`, or the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<wsid>` bind\nin `devcontainer`) is chowned before git runs, and *non-recursively*: other clones live\nalongside this one and are not ours to take over. The clone folder itself is chowned too,\nor a root agent leaves it root-owned and the clone dies writing `.git` inside it."},"348":{"id":"/coder-templates-311","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-as-the-workspace-user","content":"git never runs as root, so the credentials it consults - a helper, `~/.git-credentials`,\nssh keys - are the workspace user's. `HOME` is passed explicitly through `env` because\n`runuser` keeps the invoker's environment; a root agent would otherwise hand git `/root`\nand miss those files entirely. An empty prefix means \"run git directly\", which is correct\nfor a non-root agent and for a root agent with no derivable workspace user."},"349":{"id":"/coder-templates-312","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-as-the-workspace-user","content":"A folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone: re-fetching is the user's call,\nbecause a pull there could conflict mid-edit or fail on a dirty tree, and either way the\nworkspace would start on code nobody chose."},"350":{"id":"/coder-templates-313","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-as-the-workspace-user","content":"A failed clone is loud and non-fatal. The workspace still boots, an empty editor folder\nis fixable from a terminal, git removes its own partial clone on failure, and nothing in\nthe script deletes a folder that may hold someone's work."},"351":{"id":"/coder-templates-314","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#mcp-command-reachability-advisory","content":"Every `[mcp_servers.*]` block the render writes names its binary by the absolute path the\ngolden image bakes (`/opt/npm-global/bin/...`), so the config and the image are two\nartifacts that have to agree - and they do not always. A workspace running an image built\nbefore that binary was added boots with a lane that can never start, and the CLI only\nsays so in its own MCP log."},"352":{"id":"/coder-templates-315","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#mcp-command-reachability-advisory","content":"The stage reads the *rendered* file, pulls every `command = \"...\"` value out of it, and\nnames the ones that are not executable files, in the same startup log that wrote the\nconfig. `sed` keeps only a double-quoted value and a TOML path carries no spaces, so an\nempty entry there is an odd line rather than a missing binary."},"353":{"id":"/coder-templates-316","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#mcp-command-reachability-advisory","content":"It is advisory only: one warning line per missing path, never a failed start, so the call\nswallows whatever status the function returns."},"354":{"id":"/coder-templates-317","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#dev-container-preflight","content":"It runs after the clone because it is about what the clone holds, and it also runs on a\nre-start that cloned nothing, because the devcontainer build re-runs then too."},"355":{"id":"/coder-templates-318","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#dev-container-preflight","content":"The six paths it probes are the ones `@devcontainers/cli` looks in: `devcontainer.json`\nand `devcontainer.yml` at the root, under `.devcontainer/`, and under\n`.github/devcontainers/`."},"356":{"id":"/coder-templates-319","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#dev-container-preflight","content":"Nothing here changes what the build does. With the toggle on, a clone carrying no\ndevcontainer configuration fails the build either way, and the editor hangs off the dev\ncontainer, so no dev container also means no editor and no in-container terminal; the\nwarning is only early notice in the same log the clone warnings use. With the toggle off\nthe same miss is expected and the editor opens the clone directly."},"357":{"id":"/coder-templates-320","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"`display_apps` gives: the **VS Code Desktop** helper (a locally installed VS Code /\nCursor / Windsurf plus the `coder.coder-remote` extension), port forwarding, and the\nSSH helper."},"358":{"id":"/coder-templates-321","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"A separate in-browser **code-server** editor is Coder's VS Code fork (no local VS\nCode), labelled **VS Code Web** with `order = 5` so it sorts before GitHub reauth (7)\nand Install GitHub App (8) and reads apart from the **VS Code Desktop** helper. It is\nthe only app in the bar the agent still proxies."},"359":{"id":"/coder-templates-322","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"A custom **Web Terminal** command-app at `order = 6` takes the terminal slot. Custom\napps are ordered among themselves; static helper buttons have no `order` or `icon` knob\nin this Coder release and are not reorderable."},"360":{"id":"/coder-templates-323","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"A **GitHub reauth** external app (`order = 7`, `/icon/github.svg`) opens Coder's\n`/external-auth/<id>` page in a slim window on the user's Coder session: connect, or\n**Reauthenticate** when the account is already linked. It stays on the main agent so\na failed clone (or a `devcontainer` subagent that never starts) still leaves the\nbutton on the workspace page. Device-flow login is not the App install: with the\ninjected App (id `github`) that page prints \"You must install the GitHub App to\nclone private repositories\" as unlinked plaintext, because Coder omits\n`AppInstallURL` on the default provider."},"361":{"id":"/coder-templates-324","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"An **Install GitHub App** external app (`order = 8`, same icon, opens in a tab)\nis the clickable install path for that default App:\n`https://github.com/apps/coder/installations/new`. It is declared only while\n`github_auth_id` is `github`. After installing, grant the private repos the\nworkspace clones, then restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints."},"362":{"id":"/coder-templates-325","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"Memory (90 percent) and disk (85 percent, `/home/coder`) usage gauges, six resource\nmetadata tiles sampled every 30s - four `coder stat` calls (`cpu --host`,\n`mem --host`, `disk --path /home/coder`, `cpu --host`) and two awk one-liners for swap\nand load average."},"363":{"id":"/coder-templates-326","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"Those four are the whole bar. The proxy address reaches the operator as the `litellm`\nitem on the `workspace_info` tile below (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out\nrather than a proxied app, so health-checking the proxy is\n`curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack host."},"364":{"id":"/coder-templates-327","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-terminal-and-the-metadata-tiles","content":"`coder_metadata.workspace_info` shows the image, the cloned repository (in `workspace`,\nor `none` when the Git repository field is empty), the LiteLLM URL, the default Grok\nmodel, whether DinD is `on (tcp://dind:2375)` or `off`, and - redacted - which key is\nwired in. A misconfigured workspace is therefore visible in the UI without shell\naccess. `devcontainer` adds a `devcontainer folder` tile and always reads\n`on (tcp://dind:2375)` for Docker-in-docker."},"365":{"id":"/coder-templates-328","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries an\ninlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`) because that\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. The script body\nlives in a sibling `code-server.sh.tftpl` and is rendered with `templatefile()`\n(workspace: `{ editor_folder, code_server_version }`; devcontainer: that plus\n`settings_b64`). Do not wrap that `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."},"366":{"id":"/coder-templates-329","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"Both twins pin the editor release in one `code_server_version` local in their own\n`main.tf`, and the script hands that value to the upstream installer as\n`--version`. Without the flag `https://code-server.dev/install.sh` follows\n`latest`, so a release nobody reviewed would reach every new workspace with no\ncommit in this repo - the same reason the Registry module was forked out. The two\nlocals carry the same value and `//tests:bats_coder_apps_test` fails a drift. To\nbump: read `https://github.com/coder/code-server/releases`, edit both locals,\n`bazelisk test //tests:bats_coder_apps_test`, `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`,\nthen restart the workspaces. The pin is a template value and deliberately stays out\nof `images/dev/tool-versions.env`, where every key has to be an `images/dev/Dockerfile`\n`ARG` and this tool ships in no image at all."},"367":{"id":"/coder-templates-330","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The `workspace` fork installs code-server **only when the pinned release is\nmissing** from `/home/coder/.local/share/code-server`, and starts it with\n`--auth none --port 13337 --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`,\npassing no settings payload. The guard tests\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server/lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`,\nthe directory the installer unpacks, because `bin/code-server` beside it is a\nsymlink to whatever release was installed last: a guard on that symlink would keep\na warm volume running its first release after a bump. A bump therefore re-downloads\non the next start and leaves the previous release under `lib/`; removing that old\ndirectory by hand is what reclaims the space. Before that launch it `mkdir -p`s the\nfolder and, when the folder is not `/home/coder`, waits up to 60 s for\n`<folder>/.git` so a first-start\nclone and the editor no longer race. That wait loop increments with\n`waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar) inside the tftpl: `templatefile()` interpolates\n`${…}` and leaves `$((` alone. `$$((` would expand `$$` to the PID. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the agent would\nchdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail the agent. The\neditor's settings live on the per-owner `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume seed, not\nin a per-start merge."},"368":{"id":"/coder-templates-331","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The `devcontainer` fork has no persistent prefix to reuse - both toggle modes rebuild\nthe container that hosts the editor - so it installs the pinned release fresh under\n`/tmp/code-server` on every start, and bakes the settings seed as base64 into `settings_b64` (empty when the\ntoggle is off, so the shell skips the merge). The DinD wait lives in\n`templates/devcontainer/dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`."},"369":{"id":"/coder-templates-332","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"Both twins' `coder_app` opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`. The\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL, in the Web Terminal's `command`, and as a positional\nargument to the code-server binary with `--ignore-last-opened` (path-based apps\ndrop the query string, and without that flag the last restored workspace wins)."},"370":{"id":"/coder-templates-333","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"`coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count`. `coder/preview` warns\n`Unexpanded count argument on block coder_app.*` when they are: that value is\nnot expanded at workspace-create preview. Scripts, metadata and the container\nstay gated."},"371":{"id":"/coder-templates-334","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The `devcontainer` editor cannot set `agent_id` with\n`local.use_dc ? coder_devcontainer.repo[0].subagent_id : coder_agent.main.id`.\n`coder_devcontainer.repo` is also gated on `start_count`, so the stop /\ntemplate-import ephemeral plan has an empty tuple even with the toggle on\n(the default), and that ternary then fails with `Invalid index` / `empty\ntuple`. `local.code_server_agent_id` is\n`concat(coder_devcontainer.repo[*].subagent_id, [coder_agent.main.id])[0]`:\nthe sub-agent when that resource exists, the parent agent on the stopped\npage. The start-gated `coder_script.code_server` uses the same local."},"372":{"id":"/coder-templates-335","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-inlined-code-server-fork","content":"The custom icon `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` travels as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from `templates/common/`. Coder serves icons\nonly from its bundled `/icon/*` set or from a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is\nnot bundled. `workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a\ncompact ribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`. `push-template` rejects\nthat locally, before the CLI runs."},"373":{"id":"/coder-templates-336","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"`settings.json.tftpl` is copied once, the first time `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`\nis missing, onto the per-owner volume `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`. Startup chowns\nthat volume (and the shared `~/.grok` profile volume) to the home owner before\nthe write: an empty Docker named volume is `root:root` 755, and the agent runs\nas `coder`. A failed write logs a warning instead of running `jq`/`python`\nagainst a file that was never created."},"374":{"id":"/coder-templates-337","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"code-server's User settings file is a symlink to that path, so every workspace that\nowner has opens the same file, and an edit - in any workspace, or from the host by\nmounting the volume - is not overwritten on the next start. Delete the file to reseed\nfrom the template."},"375":{"id":"/coder-templates-338","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"A built dev container sees neither the volume nor that symlink, so it is the one place\nthe seed is merged instead of linked."},"376":{"id":"/coder-templates-339","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"The code-server module is deliberately given no settings map in plain mode: its merge\nruns on every start and `mv`s over that symlink, which would fork a private copy and\ndiscard the sharing. (`devcontainer` passes the seed to the module only in the mode where\nthe merge is wanted, and bakes it as `settings_b64` for the built container.)"},"377":{"id":"/coder-templates-340","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"The seed in git carries no API key. Cline and Roo read their key out of `settings.json`\nand have no `env_key` field, so when `LITELLM_API_KEY` is set the startup script injects\nit into the seed once (`jq`, falling back to `python3`) and nowhere else."},"378":{"id":"/coder-templates-341","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"A *regular* file at the User settings path is the old per-workspace copy: the startup\nscript keeps it beside the link once, as `<path>.pre-shared`, then points the editor at\nthe shared file on every start so a workspace cannot drift off it. GNU `ln -sfn` replaces\na symlink and does not follow it."},"379":{"id":"/coder-templates-342","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#editor-settings-seed","content":"`subdomain = false` everywhere: there is no `CODER_WILDCARD_ACCESS_URL` on this box, so\nthe editor is proxied on the path-based access URL."},"380":{"id":"/coder-templates-343","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Template-side facts only; the long prose lives in\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md."},"381":{"id":"/coder-templates-344","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `\"tmux\"`. The automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build/tasks commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."},"382":{"id":"/coder-templates-345","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Two helpers are seeded into `~/.grok/bin` on every start, both `chmod +x`:\n`vscode-terminal-tmux` (the default profile) and `grok-build-terminal`."},"383":{"id":"/coder-templates-346","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`vscode-terminal-tmux` keeps `vscode-<folder>-<hash>` as its base session name. It\nre-joins one of that folder's sessions while no client is attached to it, oldest\nfirst (the closed-tab rejoin: an unattached session is reused even while sibling\nterminals are live); it takes the next numbered sibling, `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`,\n`-3`, ... only when every session of the folder already has a client. With no\n`tmux` on PATH it falls back to a plain login shell.\nBecause the profile runs inside a session rather than in the tab's own process,\nclosing a tab detaches the client and leaves whatever was running alive."},"384":{"id":"/coder-templates-347","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`grok-build-terminal` keeps its own session, `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`, keyed off\nthe repository root found with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or the terminal's own\n`$PWD` outside a clone) and runs `grok --cwd <that root>` inside it. It is the\nalways-reattach case on purpose: every tab of the clone watches the one agent run."},"385":{"id":"/coder-templates-348","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The seed also pins `terminal.integrated.cwd` and `terminal.integrated.splitCwd` to the\nopened folder, adds a `terminal.integrated.env.linux` block, and carries a\n`commandsToSkipShell` list - all described in the sibling doc."},"386":{"id":"/coder-templates-349","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"One repository per workspace is the default, and that is what `repo_url` and the\n**Git repository** field mean: one URL, cloned into one folder."},"387":{"id":"/coder-templates-350","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"That folder is what code-server, the Web Terminal button and the Grok Build session\nopen on. Inside a dev container, code-server sees that same folder under its mount\ntarget, `/workspaces/<repo-name>`."},"388":{"id":"/coder-templates-351","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"Keep it that way unless a workspace genuinely needs two trees open at once."},"389":{"id":"/coder-templates-352","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"`--additional-repos` is the opt-in escape hatch for the service-and-the-library-it-uses\ncase. It takes a comma-separated list (`\"url1,url2\"`), one URL per line works too, and\nthe spaces around each entry are trimmed. It joins the list into one `additional_repos`\nparameter, so `\"a , b\"` and `\"a\\nb\"` both arrive in the template as `a,b`."},"390":{"id":"/coder-templates-353","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"Without the flag the script passes exactly the parameters it used to pass, and the create\nform's **Git repositories (additional)** textarea stays empty."},"391":{"id":"/coder-templates-354","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"The extras are cloned **beside** the primary repository, into sibling folders, by the\nsame clone-once rule (a folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone on later\nstarts, never pulled on)."},"392":{"id":"/coder-templates-355","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"`workspace` keeps the clone tree under `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`;\n`devcontainer` keeps it under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>`."},"393":{"id":"/coder-templates-356","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"An extra folder never becomes the folder the tools open on: the editor, the Web\nTerminal button and the tmux helpers all stay on the primary clone."},"394":{"id":"/coder-templates-357","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"A private extra repository still has to be GRANTED to the connected GitHub account, or\nits clone is refused, just like the primary one."},"395":{"id":"/coder-templates-358","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#cloning-more-than-one-repository","content":"`--dry-run` prints the list under `also:` before it creates anything, so the decision\nis readable first."},"396":{"id":"/coder-templates-359","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#github-credentials-for-a-workspace","content":"GitHub credentials arrive by one path only: opt-in, and HTTPS-only. This is the canonical\npage for that path: where the token comes from, the order git consults it in, and the four\ncommands that read or repair it. The troubleshooting entries link here rather than\nrepeating it."},"397":{"id":"/coder-templates-360","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"An `ssh://` or `git@` URL reaches nothing here - this stack provisions no SSH keys."},"398":{"id":"/coder-templates-361","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"The path is Coder's external-auth broker. With no `coder.env`, Coder CE injects\na GitHub App whose id is `github`, and both templates declare that id by\ndefault. A custom OAuth app lives in `coder.env` (beside `docker-compose.yml`);\n`push-template.sh` forwards `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` so the templates declare\nthe same id. Link that provider to **both** templates."},"399":{"id":"/coder-templates-362","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"Fill every `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*` key or omit them all. A defined-but-empty key\ncrash-loops the server, which is why these keys live in `coder.env` and not in `.env`."},"400":{"id":"/coder-templates-363","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"Then connect your GitHub account. On a running workspace, click **GitHub reauth** on\nthe agent bar (Coder v2.36's `/external-auth/<id>` page; use **Reauthenticate** if the\npage says you are already authenticated). First-time connect also works from the\nworkspace create/update form and from Settings -> Tokens / external auth. Device-flow\nlogin is not enough for private repos: install Coder's GitHub App with **Install\nGitHub App** on the same bar (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant those repositories, then restart. An empty\ngithub.com/settings/apps page is not a misconfiguration:\nwith `auth_type=client_secret` Coder has no App to install, and Coder's built-in GitHub\nApp only earns a page there once it is installed on the account. Until something is\ninstalled and granted, that page simply has nothing to **Configure**, and the credential\nthe provider mints still authenticates - it just cannot write to a private repository."},"401":{"id":"/coder-templates-364","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"Each workspace's agent injects a `GIT_ASKPASS` helper pointing at the `coder` binary it\nstages, alongside the `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair in the agent environment. Some\nbuilds of that binary answer every prompt with `unrecognized subcommand`, so the stack\ndepends on the helper below rather than on askpass, and `check` prints a note line when\nthat askpass answers nothing."},"402":{"id":"/coder-templates-365","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"Nothing long-lived lives in any file, env or template: the rendered `config.toml`\nnames `GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, and the pair stops at the workspace container's edge\n(a dev container built on the sidecar is not handed either key)."},"403":{"id":"/coder-templates-366","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#where-the-token-comes-from","content":"One connection covers every workspace, and `gh auth status` inside a workspace is how\nyou check it landed. Ask it with the pair cleared - `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh\nauth status` - because gh reports the env pair in preference to the store."},"404":{"id":"/coder-templates-367","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"git never reads `GH_TOKEN` itself: it asks its credential helpers in order and sends the\n**first** answer. That order is what decides what github.com sees, and the first slot that\nanswers wins outright - everything under it exists but a real push never reaches it, which\nis the whole difference between a credential that works and a push that works."},"405":{"id":"/coder-templates-368","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"The credential helper configured for `https://github.com`. Both template twins seed\n`~/.gitconfig` at startup, for `github.com` and `gist.github.com` alike:"},"406":{"id":"/coder-templates-369","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"The blank first value is git's reset marker: it drops any helper inherited from a\nlower-precedence config file, and git takes the **last** value it finds for that host.\nThe startup seed rewrites both keys every start (idempotent), and `github-auth fix`\ninstalls the same form, leaving a helper that already ignores the pair alone. This\nhelper is the slot that wins, and the clearing is the subject of the paragraph below."},"407":{"id":"/coder-templates-370","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"`GIT_ASKPASS`, when no helper answers. Where the staged binary is alive it mints on\ndemand; where it answers `unrecognized subcommand` git gets no answer at all and falls\nthrough to prompting, which is where `could not read Username` comes from."},"408":{"id":"/coder-templates-371","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"The `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair - reached through the helper, because\n`gh auth git-credential` echoes either name whenever one is set. A helper that does not\nclear the pair therefore answers with the injected copy and never asks the store: that\nis the shadow, and it is why a hand-edited helper or a hand-rolled askpass script is the\nwrong instrument (see\nthe push-denied entry)."},"409":{"id":"/coder-templates-372","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"The gh auth store (`~/.config/gh`, 0600): the one copy on disk, maintained by\n`gh_token_refresh`. Reachable only past a helper that clears the pair."},"410":{"id":"/coder-templates-373","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"A live `coder external-auth access-token <id>` mint, on demand. Not a helper: it is\nwhat `github-auth fix` calls, and what a workspace shell can call, whenever a token has\nto be certainly fresh."},"411":{"id":"/coder-templates-374","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"**Why the env pair goes stale.** Coder injects `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` into the agent\nenvironment when the workspace starts. That is a GitHub App user-to-server token, it\nexpires about an hour later, and the injected copy is never refreshed for the life of the\nworkspace: `gh_token_refresh` re-mints on start and every 20 minutes into the gh auth store\nand the gitconfig helper, never into the environment, and no process can rewrite another\nprocess's environment anyway. So for most of a workspace's life the helper echoes a dead\ntoken, github.com answers `remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is\nnot supported for Git operations.`, and the healthy store beside it is never asked. `gh`\nconsults the same pair before its own store, so inside that workspace `gh` needs\n`env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh <args>` for the whole of its life; `git` needs no\nprefix once the helper is the env-proof form above, which is what `fix` installs."},"412":{"id":"/coder-templates-375","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"**Refresh, and reconnect.** `coder external-auth access-token` is the live path when the\nstore goes stale: it re-mints on demand and answers with a live token, or with the\nre-authentication URL and exit 1; with no connected account the refresh script prints its\none note (pointing at **GitHub reauth**, and at **Install GitHub App** when the injected\nApp is in use) and exits 0, and mints nothing. After a reconnect, restart the workspace (or\nwait for the next `gh_token_refresh`) so the injected pair and the store match the new\ngrant. The refresh script unsets `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before\n`gh auth login --with-token`, because gh refuses stdin while those names are set."},"413":{"id":"/coder-templates-376","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-credential-ladder-in-gits-consult-order","content":"**A token that authenticates still has to be granted.** A credential that was never\nGRANTED the repository fails clone and push, and reads prove nothing about write: a private\nrepository hidden from the credential answers 403 or 404 on both lanes, and a public one\nanswers a read probe with 200 for any authenticated caller, so a read-only credential\npasses a read test while every write to that repo is denied. `check` therefore asks the\nwrite question directly: one GET of `<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`, the\nendpoint `git push` itself negotiates through."},"414":{"id":"/coder-templates-377","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-four-commands","content":"Every one prints slot names, credential kinds and verdicts, and none of them prints a\ntoken. Repo convention: `bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- <subcommand> ...`."},"415":{"id":"/coder-templates-378","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-four-commands","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check [container] [repo-url] [--source=<slot>]` -\nread-only. Inside a workspace or the contributor devcontainer it runs SELF mode with no\ndocker and prints `self:` lines for this environment's own chain; on the stack host it\nenumerates the labeled workspace containers, or probes a named container alone.\n`--source=chain|env|gh|mint` names one slot (chain is the default), and flags may go\nbefore or after the positionals. The last line is `push would WORK` or\n`push would FAIL`, keyed to the credential git would really send, and check exits\nnon-zero on FAIL. The verdicts are PUSH-READY (200 advertising refs), REFUSED-GRANT\n(403, it authenticates and every write is denied), NOT VISIBLE (404, hidden or absent),\nREJECTED (401, the credential itself is refused, as an expired mint is), or no useful\nanswer (offline)."},"416":{"id":"/coder-templates-379","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-four-commands","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix [container] [repo-url]` - THE ONE COMMAND,\nand the answer to every `push would FAIL` line. Inside a workspace it repairs that\nworkspace; on the host a named container repairs that one workspace, and no container is\nrefused there because it names the blast radius. It mints a live token through Coder\nexternal-auth, writes the gh auth store, points git's `github.com` and `gist.github.com`\ncredential helper at that store through a form that clears the env pair first, then\nre-runs the write probe and prints the verdict again, so it proves itself. Idempotent.\nWhen nothing can be minted AND the store is empty its line begins `HANDOFF` and names the\nthree clicks the human owes it."},"417":{"id":"/coder-templates-380","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-four-commands","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> [-]` - STACK HOST ONLY (it\ndrives `docker exec`). Installs a pasted PAT on stdin into that one workspace, then\nre-installs the env-proof helper that `gh auth setup-git` would otherwise overwrite.\nLegitimate only once `check` has already answered REFUSED-GRANT, NOT VISIBLE or REJECTED:\nthe repo rule is that a workspace's credential comes from the External Auth provider, so\nno verdict short of those justifies a pasted token. It REPLACES the per-start minted\ncredential, the next `gh_token_refresh` rewrites the store while an account is connected,\nand `gh auth logout` or a rebuild clears it - treat it as a stand-in and rotate or remove\nit in GitHub -> Settings -> Applications when the work ends. The PAT arrives on stdin only\n(the `-` form, or the hidden prompt): argv is readable through `ps` and keeps the token in\nshell history."},"418":{"id":"/coder-templates-381","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-four-commands","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- -h` - full usage: the verdict kinds, both argument\norders, both modes and the fix contract."},"419":{"id":"/coder-templates-382","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates#the-four-commands","content":"The lines `check` prints are documented with the failures they explain, in\ntroubleshooting.md:\n`git would send this credential (source: ...)`, `NO GitHub credential ...`,\n`[shadowed: ...]`, `push would WORK` / `push would FAIL`, and the dead-`GIT_ASKPASS` note.\n`gh` refusing a healthy store is its own entry there, at\n`gh` answers `Bad credentials`,\nand a workspace with no `gh` binary at\n`gh: command not found`."},"420":{"id":"/config-updates","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"page","content":"Keeping a checkout's config current","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates"},"421":{"id":"/config-updates-0","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates","content":"What goes stale after a `git pull`, what `update-configs` refreshes, and what it deliberately will not touch."},"422":{"id":"/config-updates-1","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"Keeping a checkout's config current"},"423":{"id":"/config-updates-2","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#what-goes-stale","content":"What goes stale"},"424":{"id":"/config-updates-3","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"The two passes"},"425":{"id":"/config-updates-4","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"Flags"},"426":{"id":"/config-updates-5","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#where-to-run-it","content":"Where to run it"},"427":{"id":"/config-updates-6","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"What it will not do"},"428":{"id":"/config-updates-7","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"GitHub external auth and coder.env"},"429":{"id":"/config-updates-8","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"Grok profile mode"},"430":{"id":"/config-updates-9","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Public hosts and the dashboard"},"431":{"id":"/config-updates-10","page_id":"/config-updates","type":"heading","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"Spark agent model group"},"432":{"id":"/config-updates-11","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"**What's on this page**"},"433":{"id":"/config-updates-12","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"Which files go stale and why `git pull` cannot fix them"},"434":{"id":"/config-updates-13","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"The two passes of `update-configs`, and the flags that select one"},"435":{"id":"/config-updates-14","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"That env comments always refresh from the twin, even when values stay"},"436":{"id":"/config-updates-15","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"Where the command may run, and where it refuses to"},"437":{"id":"/config-updates-16","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"The long form of the `.env.sample` comments: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, the Spark `agent` group"},"438":{"id":"/config-updates-17","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"**What this enables**"},"439":{"id":"/config-updates-18","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"Bringing an old checkout back to a runnable state with one command"},"440":{"id":"/config-updates-19","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"Telling a ported env key apart from a value you set yourself and must keep"},"441":{"id":"/config-updates-20","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"Two kinds of file go stale in this repo, and because neither of them is\nchecked in, `git pull` cannot fix either one for you. `update-configs` is the\none command that refreshes both, and it is written to be safe to run when you\nare not looking closely at the diff."},"442":{"id":"/config-updates-21","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"These four sections are also the long form of the `.env.sample` comments that\npoint here: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, and the\nSpark `agent` model group. Where a topic page already owns a subject, this page\npoints at it instead of restating it."},"443":{"id":"/config-updates-22","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#keeping-a-checkouts-config-current","content":"Run it after you pull, not before: a pull is what changes the templates these\ntools copy from. `bazelisk run` feeds the real checkout (`BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`),\nnot the runfiles copy, so the env pass rewrites the live `.env` beside\n`docker-compose.yml`."},"444":{"id":"/config-updates-23","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-goes-stale","content":"**The env files.** `docker-compose.yml` builds its variable references in two\nsteps: `${VAR}` inside `expose` expands during the shell phase, so compose\nnever sees those variables and a changed value needs a rebuild, and an unset\nvariable collapses to `127.0.0.1:57620:57620/tcp`, which fails to bind. So the\nhost ports and addresses live in a key=value env file - `.env` for the stable\nstack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and each has a checked-in twin beside\nit that carries the keys and explains them in the comment lines above. A pull\nthat renames, adds or retires a key silently changes what the stack runs on."},"445":{"id":"/config-updates-24","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-goes-stale","content":"**The workspace config.** `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.grok/config.toml`,\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`, `~/.tmux.conf` and the `~/.coder` and\n`~/.agents` seeds are materialised inside each workspace when it first boots and\nare never revisited after that. A re-pushed template only reaches workspaces\nthat have been **start**ed or **restart**ed since the push - **stop** and\n**start** is what re-runs the provisioning script and therefore re-runs\n`before_config`."},"446":{"id":"/config-updates-25","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"`env` reconciles one env file with its twin. It keeps every value you have set,\nfills a key the twin adds with the twin's default (or a reusable value already\nset in a sibling live file - `.env` filling `dev-stack.env`), and moves a key\nthe twin no longer declares into a commented trailer at the end of the file -\nparked, with its value and its explanation still readable, rather than deleted.\nComment lines always come from the twin, even when every value stays as you\nset it, so a pull that only rewrote an explanation still lands in `.env`. The\nrewrite lands in a staging file and replaces the live file only at the end,\nwith mode `600` kept. On a TTY the pass then walks every key that is new this\npull or still a placeholder (`change-me…`, `NOTSET`, `YOUR_LAN_IP`). An empty\nvalue whose example default is also empty is the intended value (LAN-only\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`, a parked MCP flag) and is not asked again. Enter keeps a\nsibling's value or the example default. A missing live file is created from\nthe twin only after a `Y/n` confirm. `--non-interactive` (and `--dry-run`)\nlist those keys and write no answers. A `summary:` line closes the pass."},"447":{"id":"/config-updates-26","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"`workspace` re-materialises the stack-owned config inside the targets you name.\nThe reseed set is the thirteen home-relative targets the templates seed -\n`~/.tmux.conf`, `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.coder/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`, the two `grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md` copies, the six\n`ez-learn`, `ez-skillify` and `ez-create-skill` `SKILL.md` copies (two homes each) and\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` - and the same rule\napplies as in `refresh-configs`: a file is replaced only while it still carries its\nstack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment, the\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` JSON key (settings files) or the `__evoT1Stack = true` TOML\nkey (`~/.grok/config.toml`) - so a config you hand-edit in a terminal is your\nfile and this tool will not move it. `--force` overrides that, per run, and says\nso. The six skill copies are the stack's three skill commands, each seeded at\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and twin-copied to its `~/.agents` twin:\n`/ez-learn`, which turns a session into durable memory (this stack's copy of the\nupstream `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory` - the CLI's own commands, not\nskills: flush the conversation into a session log, dream those logs into organized\nmemory, save one note, browse or manage the store. Three of the four are gated on a\nmemory store that is off by default, so they stay hidden until one is configured);\n`/ez-skillify`, which captures the session that just worked; and\n`/ez-create-skill`, which scaffolds one from a description. To take a seeded copy\nover, edit it and\ndelete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a marker-free file is\nuser-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass `--force`. A deletion is\nundone - the workspace boot writes the file again whenever it is absent, and\n`refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores the stack's\ncopy. To hide one of the slash commands, set `disabled = [\"ez-learn\"]` (or\n`ez-skillify`, or `ez-create-skill`) under `[skills]` in\n`~/.grok/config.toml`. An edited copy which keeps its marker is still rewritten\nback to its `templates/common/ez-<name>.SKILL.md` by this tool. The pass also retires the two\n`skills/learn/SKILL.md` paths this skill occupied before the rename, and only while\neach still carries the stack marker, so a leftover copy cannot keep offering a `/learn` the\nstack no longer maintains, and a file someone took over stays."},"448":{"id":"/config-updates-27","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#the-two-passes","content":"`all` runs `env` and then `workspace`."},"449":{"id":"/config-updates-28","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag        | Effect                        |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `--dry-run` | print the plan, write nothing |","table":"table-0"},"450":{"id":"/config-updates-29","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag                  | Effect                                                                       |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `-i`, `--interactive` | stop and ask about every key that still needs a value (the default on a TTY) |","table":"table-0"},"451":{"id":"/config-updates-30","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag                | Effect                                 |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------------- |\n| `--non-interactive` | never prompt; list what would be asked |","table":"table-0"},"452":{"id":"/config-updates-31","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag      | Effect                                                      |\n| --------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--force` | reseed workspace files that carry none of the three markers |","table":"table-0"},"453":{"id":"/config-updates-32","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag           | Effect                                                   |\n| -------------- | -------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--from <dir>` | read the payloads from that checkout instead of this one |","table":"table-0"},"454":{"id":"/config-updates-33","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag           | Effect                                          |\n| -------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| `--home <dir>` | treat that host directory as a workspace `HOME` |","table":"table-0"},"455":{"id":"/config-updates-34","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"| Flag                 | Effect                        |\n| -------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `--container <name>` | address one container by name |","table":"table-0"},"456":{"id":"/config-updates-35","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"Every flag goes **after** the subcommand, so `update-configs --dry-run env` is a\nusage error and prints the usage rather than guessing."},"457":{"id":"/config-updates-36","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"A target named without a flag is a container, the same reading `refresh-configs`\ngives a bare word, so these two lines address the same container:"},"458":{"id":"/config-updates-37","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"Anything that carries a prefix other than `c:` (container) or `h:` (host\ndirectory) is reported as unrecognized and counted in the closing summary's\nskipped tally. A target never leaves the pass unheard."},"459":{"id":"/config-updates-38","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#flags","content":"On a TTY the env pass asks by default (`-i`/`--interactive` is then a no-op;\n`--non-interactive` turns it off). For every key that is new this pull or still\n`change-me…`/`NOTSET`/`YOUR_LAN_IP` it prints the explanation the twin carries\nabove that key and prompts for a value. Enter keeps a reusable value already\nset in a sibling env file, or the example default. A key whose\nname carries `KEY`, `SECRET`, `TOKEN` or `PASSWORD` is echoed nowhere - the\nreport says `set` or `empty` and the prompt does not echo what you type - so a\nscreenful of this output is safe to paste into an issue. With no terminal to\nprompt through the pass warns and leaves those keys alone, which is why\n`--dry-run` / `--non-interactive` is the shape that belongs in a script or CI."},"460":{"id":"/config-updates-39","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#where-to-run-it","content":"`workspace` addresses docker, so run it from a **host** shell. A terminal inside a\nworkspace is not a host shell: the `docker` CLI there is only installed so you can\ndrive that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so `docker ps` answers empty and an empty\n`docker ps` means \"wrong vantage\", not \"stack down\". The pass now prints which\ndaemon it addressed and tells you to use a host shell, or to name the target:"},"461":{"id":"/config-updates-40","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#where-to-run-it","content":"`--home` takes a host directory shaped like a workspace `HOME`, which is also how\nyou try a change on a scratch tree before pointing a live stack at it. See\ntroubleshooting.md for the docker-vantage rules."},"462":{"id":"/config-updates-41","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"It never prints an env value that looks like a credential."},"463":{"id":"/config-updates-42","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"It never drops a key your env file still sets; retired keys are commented out\nin place."},"464":{"id":"/config-updates-43","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"It does not touch `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is rendered *inside* the\nworkspace, and `~/.grok` is one profile volume per owner, so a copy from the\nhost would move the model list, the session-affinity key and the editor keys\nof every workspace sharing the profile. A **start/restart** re-renders it from\nthis checkout, which is the only way a per-workspace value gets in, and the\npass says so after it reseeds."},"465":{"id":"/config-updates-44","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` is the one rendered target it does reseed, and\nit re-*renders* rather than copies: `${litellm_url}` and the `$${...}`\ndollar-brace unfoldings are rewritten, and that workspace's own `cline.apiKey`\nand `roo.openaiApiKey` are carried forward."},"466":{"id":"/config-updates-45","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#what-it-will-not-do","content":"Inside a running container the same thirteen targets are `refresh-configs`'s job\ntoo: same reseed set, same marker rule, `--json` for a machine-readable\nanswer, and `--litellm-url <url>` for a stack environment that publishes\nLiteLLM on a port other than 4000 (the reseeded `settings.json` then still\nnames that stack's own model transport). grok-build-and-agents.md covers the profile\nvolume and what seeds on first boot."},"467":{"id":"/config-updates-46","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"`.env.sample` deliberately carries no GitHub credential of any kind, so the env\npass has nothing to reconcile here: the twin declares no `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\nkey and `update-configs` will never add one. Those keys belong in `coder.env`\nbeside `docker-compose.yml` (gitignored), which is not a file this tool touches."},"468":{"id":"/config-updates-47","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**A defined-but-empty key crash-loops the server.** Any `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\ncounts as a configured provider even when its value is empty, and an empty key\nmakes `coder server` exit at boot with `external auth provider \"\" doesn't have a\nvalid id` - which the compose restart policy turns into a crash loop. Do not set\nonly `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_APP_INSTALL_URL` either: it suppresses the injected\nApp like any other key in the set. A custom GitHub App belongs in `coder.env`\nfully filled, including\n`APP_INSTALL_URL=https://github.com/apps/<slug>/installations/new`."},"469":{"id":"/config-updates-48","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Leave `coder.env` absent for the usual case.** Coder CE injects its own GitHub\nApp (provider id `github`, device flow) and both templates declare that id by\ndefault, so `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` works with no extra file.\nWorkspace owners connect GitHub under Settings -> Tokens / external auth."},"470":{"id":"/config-updates-49","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Create `coder.env` only for a *custom* GitHub OAuth app**, and fill EVERY key -\n`ID` names the provider and `TYPE` says what kind it is. Restart coder to apply\nthe file."},"471":{"id":"/config-updates-50","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"`CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REGEX` is only for telling providers or hosts apart;\nomitted, it matches every `github.com` remote."},"472":{"id":"/config-updates-51","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"`push-template.sh` reads `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` from `coder.env` and\nforwards it as `--var github_auth_id=` so the templates declare the same id.\nChanging the id orphans existing GitHub account links."},"473":{"id":"/config-updates-52","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"Then connect your GitHub account in Coder (Settings -> Tokens / external auth)\nand link the same provider to **both** workspace templates (Templates -> edit\n-> External Auth)."},"474":{"id":"/config-updates-53","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"The callback URL on the GitHub side is\n`<CODER_ACCESS_URL>/external-auth/<id>/callback` - `github` for the injected\nApp, `primary-github` for the sample app above."},"475":{"id":"/config-updates-54","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Device-flow login is not the App install.** Private repos need the Coder GitHub\nApp installed from\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new\n(the workspace \"Install GitHub App\" button), then a restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints.\nThe grant must include the repos you clone, or a connected credential authenticates\nand is still 403."},"476":{"id":"/config-updates-55","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**How the token reaches a workspace.** It arrives as `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN`\nin the agent environment, and that pair is a snapshot: Coder mints it when the\nworkspace starts and never refreshes it, so it expires about an hour later while\nthe workspace keeps running. The template's `gh_token_refresh` coder_script\nre-mints on start and every 20 minutes (`0 */20 * * * *`) into the **gh auth store**,\nwhich is the copy that stays live. git asks its credential helper, and the\nstack's helper reads the store through a form that clears the stale pair first,\nso push follows the store and not the frozen environment. Check it with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check <repo-url>` - pass the repository,\nbecause without it the doctor lists credentials and never asks the write\nquestion. `bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix <repo-url>` repairs a\nworkspace that cannot push.\ncoder-templates.md owns\nthe template-side detail."},"477":{"id":"/config-updates-56","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"**Never a static PAT in `.env`.** `.env.sample` used to carry a `GITHUB_TOKEN=`\nline holding a long-lived fine-grained PAT, which `push-template.sh` forwarded to\nboth templates as a template variable, into every workspace's environment. That\npath is gone: there is no such template variable and no such key in the twin. A\nworkspace's GitHub credential comes from the External Auth provider alone, so an\nowner who connected nothing has no GitHub credential anywhere - and the only way\nto add one by hand is per workspace, after `check` has answered `REFUSED-GRANT`,\n`NOT VISIBLE` or `REJECTED` (a passing read is not that answer):"},"478":{"id":"/config-updates-57","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#github-external-auth-and-coderenv","content":"That pipes a PAT straight into that container's `gh` credential store - no `.env`\nedit, no template push - and `gh auth logout` inside the container, or a rebuild,\nremoves it. The repositories a token can reach are whatever that GitHub app\ninstallation was granted."},"479":{"id":"/config-updates-58","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"`GROK_PROFILE_MODE` in the twin is the default for the template variable\n`grok_profile_mode`, which the create form also shows per workspace as the **Grok\nprofile** dropdown. Read once per push, so changing the default here needs\n`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`. Overriding it for one workspace happens on\nthat dropdown and needs a rebuild, like `dind`."},"480":{"id":"/config-updates-59","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"`shared` (the default): every workspace of a user mounts that user's shared\ngrok-profile volume nested inside `/home/coder`, so `config.toml`, the MCP warm\ncaches, `memory-v2`, skills and a self-installed CLI are one profile shared by\nthat account's workspaces. It is re-rendered on every start and the\n**last-started workspace wins** for all of them."},"481":{"id":"/config-updates-60","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"`private`: no nested mount. Each workspace gets a private `~/.grok` on its own\nhome volume, so its `config.toml` - plus memory, skills and logs - differs per\nworkspace and dies with the workspace."},"482":{"id":"/config-updates-61","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"A workspace moving `shared` -> `private` starts on an empty profile: nothing is\ncopied and the shared volume is left alone."},"483":{"id":"/config-updates-62","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#grok-profile-mode","content":"Switching either way is a **rebuild**, not a stop/start, because the nested mount\nis decided when the workspace is built.\ngrok-build-and-agents.md covers what the shared\nprofile holds and what seeds on first boot; the `workspace` pass above never\nreseeds `~/.grok/config.toml` for exactly that reason."},"484":{"id":"/config-updates-63","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is the one switch, and it has three states. Empty keeps everything\nbelow LAN-only and every derived `STACK_*_URL` stays empty. `auto` composes the five\nnames from the naming parts - `STACK_DOMAIN_BASE`, `STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX`,\n`STACK_ENV_LABEL` and the per-service `STACK_*_NAME` prefixes, all documented in\n`.env.sample`. A comma-separated list is the hand-pin: those names are used verbatim."},"485":{"id":"/config-updates-64","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Entries are DNS names, not URLs: no scheme, no port, no path. The entry with no\nprefix is the dashboard."},"486":{"id":"/config-updates-65","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Each service name is **one label** under the zone and carries its prefix with a\n**HYPHEN**: a wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so the dotted\ntwo-label form has no certificate at the public edge."},"487":{"id":"/config-updates-66","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Under `auto` the parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output\nonly: bootstrap writes the resolved list back and records the intent in\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, so a later run re-composes and a list edited by hand\nin `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is ignored. Bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear when that happens."},"488":{"id":"/config-updates-67","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"`bootstrap.sh` derives `STACK_DASHBOARD_URL` / `STACK_CODER_URL` /\n`STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` from the prefix on\neach entry (`coder-` / `kasm-` / `litellm-` / `hermes-`, dotted form still\nrecognised) and upgrades `CODER_ACCESS_URL` to the coder name, so Coder builds\nworkspace links on a secure context with a real certificate. Compose sets\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the Chat tab's Host and\nOrigin)."},"489":{"id":"/config-updates-68","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"The names also go into `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` (compose appends this raw value)\nand into the Coder certificate's subjectAltName."},"490":{"id":"/config-updates-69","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"The Traefik on the other box owns TLS for those names and must forward plain\nHTTP to this box's router container **with the original Host header intact** -\n`STACK_ROUTER_CONF` names the conf that container mounts (`proxy/router.gen.conf`,\nrendered by bootstrap from `proxy/router.conf.template`, or the checked-in\n`proxy/router.conf` while the key is empty), and it picks the service from\n`$http_host` on `STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` (default 8080). A rewritten Host comes back\nas `421`.\nThat box needs one file, and the paste-ready version of it - one `loadBalancer`\nat `:8080`, a router per name, Authelia on the four interactive names, the\nlong-lived timeouts - is\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."},"491":{"id":"/config-updates-70","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#public-hosts-and-the-dashboard","content":"Set the parts in `.env`, and in `dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside\nthe stable one, then re-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` and recreate the\nstack you switched. That one command is the whole rename: it re-renders the router's\nvhost table and rewrites the derived URLs, so a hostname is no longer an edit split\nacross `proxy/router.conf`, `scripts/bootstrap.sh` and `docker-compose.yml`. A\n`hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is part of that list:\nbootstrap writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`, and the LAN `:9119` listener stays either\nway. Leave the hermes name out and the Homepage card keeps the LAN URL.\npublic-access.md owns the naming rule, the measured\n`ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` on the dotted form, the request path and the\nchecks to run on the Traefik box."},"492":{"id":"/config-updates-71","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"The `agent` model group is three deployments behind one LiteLLM alias, one per\nSpark box, and these are the keys that fill it:"},"493":{"id":"/config-updates-72","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` are the Ollama endpoints behind the `spark-<n>` aliases. The\nplaceholder hostnames are on purpose: replace them with real Ollama URLs or\nleave them as-is, and those aliases simply fail when used."},"494":{"id":"/config-updates-73","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` are the OpenAI-compatible `/v1` endpoints. A workspace pins\nits session to one healthy box; fresh session-id-less traffic fans out by\n`least-busy`, shortest in-flight queue first."},"495":{"id":"/config-updates-74","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARK_n_API_KEY` is the bearer token for each endpoint. TensorFold is\nvLLM-lineage, so treat it like vLLM/SGLang: any non-empty string is accepted\nunless the server was started with `--api-key`, in which case set the REAL key\nper box. Empty is not a working value - LiteLLM v1.102.1 fails every request at\nclient construction (`AuthenticationError`, before reaching the box) and `agent`\nfalls back to `coder`. The placeholder `local-spark` is not a secret and is\nignored by a keyless backend."},"496":{"id":"/config-updates-75","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` (kept commented in the twin) feed the dashboard's per-box\nSpark cards - one widget per box, so no card assumes one \"usual\" box."},"497":{"id":"/config-updates-76","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"`AGENT_MODEL_URL` is the single `/v1/models` URL the \"Agent backend\" card uses\nfor both its browser link and its status probe. All three boxes serve the same\nmodel id, so point it at any reachable one; the per-box health keys are what tell\nyou which box answered."},"498":{"id":"/config-updates-77","page_id":"/config-updates","tags":["env","config","update-configs","maintenance"],"type":"text","url":"/config-updates#spark-agent-model-group","content":"Leaving all three `SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` keys empty keeps `agent` defined but\nunusable: LiteLLM then falls back to `coder`, the Arc alias. That fallback is what\nthe \"Agent backend\" card exists to catch - Grok Build, Cline and Roo need\nstructured tool calls, which the Arc IPEX-LLM Ollama returns as plain text for the\nQwen2.5-Coder weights. litellm-routing.md owns the alias\ntable, the picker, the pin, and the measured tool-call limitation (including which\naliases do emit real calls)."},"499":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"page","content":"Building with Bazel","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel"},"500":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-0","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","content":"Bazelisk as the ingress for tests, lint and the operator CLIs, and what the runfiles layout means for a script you just added."},"501":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-1","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"Building with Bazel"},"502":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-2","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"The contributor path"},"503":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-3","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"Stack-host bootstrap"},"504":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-4","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"Daily commands"},"505":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-5","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"What Bazel brings"},"506":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-6","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"Script targets and runfiles"},"507":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-7","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"The dependency maps and their gate"},"508":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-8","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"**What's on this page**"},"509":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-9","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"Stack-host bootstrap: the one script run straight from a shell"},"510":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-10","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"The contributor path — the dev container, or `test-in-image` — and where it fits"},"511":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-11","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"The daily commands, and the Makefile shim over them"},"512":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-12","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"What Bazel brings over running the scripts directly"},"513":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-13","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"Script targets, runfiles, and why a new `scripts/*.sh` is a target automatically"},"514":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-14","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"**What this enables**"},"515":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-15","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"Running any operator CLI from a foreign working directory with pinned inputs"},"516":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-16","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"Adding a script without editing a BUILD file"},"517":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-17","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"Getting the same Bazel, Bats, shellcheck and Terraform that CI uses without installing\nany of them"},"518":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-18","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#building-with-bazel","content":"Bazelisk is how this repo runs tests, lint, and the operator CLIs. The Makefile targets call\nthe same commands. test-with-bazel.md covers the Bats harness;\n../branching.md the branch rules that CI guards;\ndevcontainer.md the container that carries the toolchain."},"519":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-19","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"The toolchain Bazel drives comes from one Linux golden image, `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and\nthe contributor path is that image rather than a set of tools installed on your machine:"},"520":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-20","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"Open the checkout in the contributor dev container (`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nbuilds it) and run the commands below unchanged."},"521":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-21","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"Or, from a machine that is not in that container, `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image --`\ndocker-runs `evo-t1-dev:latest` and runs the gate inside it."},"522":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-22","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-contributor-path","content":"Both give you the GNU userland the gate runs on, which a macOS laptop cannot promise\nnatively. Neither replaces the bootstrap below — that one is for the stack host."},"523":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-23","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"`scripts/setup-host.sh` is the only script run straight from a shell, and it is the\n**stack host's** bootstrap: the box that runs Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and\nHermes, where Compose and Bazel have to live on the host itself. It installs what Bazel\nneeds (Bazelisk, shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier, Terraform), so it necessarily runs before\nBazel exists — that is its whole exemption, not a convenience:"},"524":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-24","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"`--repair` is the recovery when `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` dies creating\nthe runfiles tree (`FileAccessException ... Permission denied` on\n`homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`). Bazel never reaches the stack script, so the\nrepair has to live in this shell entry point. On Linux it also installs public\nDNS so `github.com` still resolves when Tailscale MagicDNS is the only\nnameserver (`Could not resolve host: github.com` after a compose `up`). `--check`\nreports that lookup and writes nothing."},"525":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-25","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#stack-host-bootstrap","content":"Everything else is a Bazel target, including every `scripts/*.sh`. CI repeats the gate from\na clean runner inside the same golden image: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds\n`images/dev` with buildx and the Actions layer cache and docker-runs the two suites below in\nit, so the bazel job does not install the pins onto the runner at all — the image already\ncarries them. The persistent cache on that path is the buildx GHA layer cache, so a pull\nrequest that leaves `images/dev/**` alone replays the image layers."},"526":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-26","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target         | What it runs                                                                             |\n| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:test-fast` | One `sh_test` per `tests/bats/*.bats` file, plus the shell inventory and docstring gates |","table":"table-0"},"527":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-27","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target    | What it runs                                       |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:lint` | ShellCheck, shfmt, buildifier (manual: host tools) |","table":"table-0"},"528":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-28","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target        | What it runs                                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:validate` | test-fast, lint, `docker compose config -q`, `.env.sample` vs `.env.example`, terraform fmt when terraform is installed |","table":"table-0"},"529":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-29","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target   | What it runs                    |\n| -------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `//:fix` | buildifier and shfmt write mode |","table":"table-0"},"530":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-30","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target             | What it runs                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:<stem>` | One operator CLI: `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`, e.g. `//scripts:dev-stack -- up`, `//scripts:new-workspace -- --dry-run <url>` |","table":"table-0"},"531":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-31","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target                    | What it runs                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:test-in-image` | The merge gate inside `evo-t1-dev:latest`, for a machine that is not the contributor container |","table":"table-0"},"532":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-32","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"| Target                   | What it runs                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:seed-dev-env` | Materialize a contributor home (Grok config, editor settings, notes, tmux, helpers) from the workspace templates |","table":"table-0"},"533":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-33","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"`make test`/`bats`/`lint`/`fix`/`validate` and the `stack-*`/`prod-stack-*` shims are the\nsame commands;\n`make help` lists them. `//:test-fast` does not start Compose, pull models, or SSH to a Spark\nbox: the suites exercise those scripts against `tests/bats/tool_stubs`. `bazelisk run\n//scripts:<stem> -- <args>` is how a human runs one of them for real."},"534":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-34","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#daily-commands","content":"Those operator runs are bounded: a script's non-interactive `docker exec` closes stdin\n(`</dev/null`), and the docker wrappers go through `timeout(1)` with `--kill-after`, so a\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem>` cannot sit forever the way `docker exec -i` did under\n`bazelisk run`, waiting for an EOF that never came. `logs --follow` is the intentional\nlong-running exception, and you stop it with Ctrl-C."},"535":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-35","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"**Hermetic sandbox.** `test --spawn_strategy=local,sandboxed` and `build\n--spawn_strategy=sandboxed`: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not\nthe whole checkout. That is why a suite declares its stubs and payload files as `data` and\nwhy a script resolves its repo root with `resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`) instead of\ntrusting the cwd."},"536":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-36","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"**Action cache.** An action's outputs are keyed on its inputs and command line, so an\nunchanged suite replays its cached PASS instead of re-running Bats."},"537":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-37","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"**Local disk and repository cache.** `.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` at\n`~/.cache/bazel-disk` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache/bazel-repo`. A contributor\ncontainer and a Coder workspace reuse those directories across runs. In CI the gate\ncontainer is `--rm` and runs with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)`, so a plain run keeps\nneither cache between jobs, and Bazel can name a uid the image passwd file does not\nlist; the cache that survives there is the image's buildx GHA cache. No remote cache is\nconfigured — the same two flags would take a `--remote_cache=<uri>` endpoint later."},"538":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-38","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#what-bazel-brings","content":"The repository cache is the one worth persisting in CI, and `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE=<host dir>`\nis the seam: `//scripts:test-in-image` bind-mounts that directory into the gate container and\npoints `--repository_cache` at it, so each external archive downloads once per key. Each CI\ngate step exports it as `${RUNNER_TEMP}/bazel-repo-cache` and is wrapped in an `actions/cache`\npair keyed on `MODULE.bazel`, `MODULE.bazel.lock` and `.bazelrc`, with the save under\n`if: always()` — the archives fetched\nbefore a red gate are exactly what the next run would otherwise pay for again. That endpoint\nmatters: `github.com/.../releases/download/...` answers 504 for a slice of requests, one such\n504 aborts Bazel's analysis, and a refetch-on-every-job job decides its colour by luck. The\nin-container path is passed as an explicit option because the `~` in `.bazelrc` expands from\nthe Bazel server's own `user.home`, which this container's uid does not appear in: a mount\nplaced from that `~` could sit where Bazel never writes and look like a working cache for\nexactly one run. With the variable unset the script mounts nothing, which is what a\ncontributor laptop gets."},"539":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-39","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"`scripts/BUILD.bazel` declares one `exec_shell` launcher plus one `sh_binary` wrapper per\n`scripts/*.sh`, generated from the directory, so a new CLI is a Bazel target without a second\nedit there. `//:validate` is an alias for `//scripts:validate`; `//:fix` keeps the same\nlauncher pattern at the repo root."},"540":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-40","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"`rules_shell` only symlinks an `sh_test` or `sh_binary` source when that file is executable.\n`//tools:exec_shell.bzl` writes a generated launcher and runs the real `.sh` from runfiles\nwith bash, so a checkout whose scripts are mode `100644` still builds."},"541":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-41","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#script-targets-and-runfiles","content":"Each CLI target also declares the repo files that script reads by path: the libs it\nsources (`_SCRIPT_LIBS`) and the payload groups whose files it opens (`_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD`),\nboth keyed by script stem in `scripts/BUILD.bazel`. Those payload groups live one per\npayload directory (`//litellm:config`, `//searxng:settings`, `//homepage:config`,\n`//homepage:icons`, `//hermes:seed`, `//hermes:skills`, `//proxy:confs`,\n`//images/dev:dockerfile`, `//images/dev:tool_versions`, and a `:payload` in each of\n`templates/common`, `templates/workspace`, `templates/devcontainer`), with the root\nBUILD.bazel holding the groups that have no directory of their own (`//:compose_file`,\n`//:env_sample`, `//:env_example`, `//:license`, `//:devcontainer`, `//:githooks`, and the\n`//:ci_*` groups). `//:stack_data` is the explicit aggregate of all of them, and\n`//scripts:doc_sources` (every `scripts/**.sh`, so a sourced `scripts/lib/*.sh` resolves\ntoo) rides along on every CLI. `proxy/certs/` is `.bazelignored` (bootstrap issues those on the host), so a\n`bazelisk run` of a stack runner, of `push-template`, or of the six CLIs that read a file the\ncheckout alone holds (`dashboard-password`, `pull-models`, `coder-agents`, `spark-verify`,\n`spark-configure`, `build-dev-image`) prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout)\nover the runfiles copy as the compose project directory, the live `.env`, and the template tree (runfiles\n`templates/**` are symlinks that Coder cannot follow after unpack). Without the committed payloads a\nsandboxed run would read a partial copy of the repo and fail for the wrong reason. A new\ntop-level payload directory gets its own package with its own `filegroup` targets, joins\n`//:stack_data` by label, and is named in the map of every target that reads it: `test-fast`\nand `lint` only see repo files that travel in runfiles, so an unlisted directory is invisible\nto the harness rather than checked loosely."},"542":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-42","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"The two maps in `scripts/BUILD.bazel` and the one in `tests/BUILD.bazel` are the\nmachine-readable record of which repo file each target reads:"},"543":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-43","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"| Map            | Says                                                                           |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `_SCRIPT_LIBS` | the `scripts/lib` closure one CLI sources, executes, or ships to a remote host |","table":"table-1"},"544":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-44","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"| Map               | Says                                                                |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD` | the payload groups whose files that CLI (or a lib it sources) opens |","table":"table-1"},"545":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-45","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"| Map              | Says |\n| ---------------- | ---- |\n| `_SUITE_PAYLOAD` |      |","table":"table-1"},"546":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-46","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"| Map               | Says                                                                                |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `*_PAYLOAD_ALLOW` | a pairing the source scan cannot see from either side; each entry states its reason |","table":"table-1"},"547":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-47","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"`tests/bazel_deps.py` re-derives all of them by scanning the shell and Bats sources and\nreports the drift both ways: a script that starts reading a payload its target does not\ndeclare, and a target that still ships a payload nothing opens. `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test`\nruns it, so the BUILD files stay something an automated reader can trust, and a\n`bazelisk query` of any target names the files a change to which should be reviewed with it."},"548":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-48","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"The scan treats a comment, a here-doc body, a log message, and a scratch file a suite writes\nas prose rather than a dependency, so the maps record reads and not mentions. When the scan\ngenuinely cannot see a pairing (a lib that builds a path and returns it as a string, a\n`case` glob over `git diff` output, help text), the entry goes in the `_ALLOW` map with the\nreason as a comment above the key; the Bats gate fails an `_ALLOW` key that has no reason."},"549":{"id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel-49","page_id":"/contribute/building-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","build","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/building-with-bazel#the-dependency-maps-and-their-gate","content":"A script that needs the checkout reaches it the same way: source its libs by absolute path from\n`SELF_DIR`, resolve the root with `resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`), then `cd` into\nwhat it prints. Never `cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\"` - under `bazelisk run` the launcher execs the\nrunfiles copy of the script, so that is the runfiles tree, which holds only `//:stack_data` and is\nrecreated on the next build."},"550":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"page","content":"Contributor environment","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer"},"551":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-0","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer","content":"The one Linux golden image behind laptop work, the Coder workspaces and CI: what the contributor dev container builds, how it is seeded, and which path is the merge gate."},"552":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-1","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"Contributor environment"},"553":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-2","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"The one image, the one pin file"},"554":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-3","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"Open it on a laptop"},"555":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-4","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"Seeding the contributor home"},"556":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-5","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"GitHub auth in the container"},"557":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-6","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"Running the gate here"},"558":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-7","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"What CI does"},"559":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-8","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"Why this repo's own workspaces stay on `workspace`"},"560":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-9","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","type":"heading","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"What this is not"},"561":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-10","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"**What's on this page**"},"562":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-11","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"The single golden image, and the file that pins it"},"563":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-12","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"Opening the repo as a contributor container in VS Code or a compatible editor"},"564":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-13","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"What `seed-dev-env` materializes into that home, and what it refuses to touch"},"565":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-14","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"GitHub auth inside the container, and why `github-auth check` runs SELF there"},"566":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-15","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"The merge gate (Linux image) versus the convenience path (native Darwin)"},"567":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-16","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"What CI does, and why this repo's own Coder workspaces stay on `workspace`"},"568":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-17","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"**What this enables**"},"569":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-18","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"Reproducing CI's failure on your own machine with the same image CI runs"},"570":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-19","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"Running the Bazel gate on macOS without maintaining a second, BSD-flavoured toolchain"},"571":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-20","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"Telling the contributor container, the Coder workspace and the stack host apart in one look"},"572":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-21","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#contributor-environment","content":"One image — `images/dev/Dockerfile`, built as `evo-t1-dev:latest` — is the contributor and\ntest runtime on macOS, on Linux, and on GitHub. The same Dockerfile serves the Coder\nworkspaces the stack provisions, the contributor dev container on a laptop, and the\ncontainer CI docker-runs its checks inside. Every version in it comes from\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`; ../coder-templates.md covers the\nworkspace side, and CONTRIBUTING.md\nis the checklist the PR follows."},"573":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-22","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"`images/dev/tool-versions.env` is the single source of truth for every tool version:\nBazelisk, buildifier, shfmt, shellcheck (apt), bats, Terraform/tflint/terragrunt, the\nCoder CLI, the Docker *client* trio, Node, Python, Go, protoc and the pinned Grok CLI."},"574":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-23","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"Each `KEY` there is also an `ARG` in `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and the `ARG` default\nmirrors the pin, so a bare `docker build` matches a scripted one."},"575":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-24","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` refuses to build when a pin has no matching\n`ARG`, or when an `ARG` default drifted from the pin. Add both sides of a new pin in the\nsame change."},"576":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-25","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"The image ships the Docker client only. The build ends with `test ! -x\n/usr/local/bin/dockerd` (plus `containerd`, `runc`, `docker-proxy`), so the merge-gate\ncontainer drives a daemon it is given rather than owning one."},"577":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-26","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#the-one-image-the-one-pin-file","content":"Build it locally when you want the tag in the local store (the Coder docker provider\nresolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from there and never pulls):"},"578":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-27","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"Use VS Code, Cursor, or any editor with the Dev Containers extension, and reopen this\nrepository **in a container**."},"579":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-28","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` is what that reads:"},"580":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-29","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                                  | Value                          | Why                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `build.context` / `build.dockerfile` | `../images/dev` / `Dockerfile` | The same Dockerfile the workspace and CI use; `ARG` defaults are the pins, so no build args are needed. |","table":"table-0"},"581":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-30","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key          | Value   | Why |\n| ------------ | ------- | --- |\n| `remoteUser` | `coder` |     |","table":"table-0"},"582":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-31","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                          | Value                   | Why                                                                                            |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `containerEnv` / `remoteEnv` | `EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER=1` | The marker that says *contributor container*, not Coder workspace. Also carries `SEARXNG_URL`. |","table":"table-0"},"583":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-32","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key      | Value                  | Why                                                                                                       |\n| -------- | ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `mounts` | `/var/run/docker.sock` | The container talks to the workstation's own daemon, which is how a `docker run` inside it is a real one. |","table":"table-0"},"584":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-33","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key       | Value                                          | Why                                           |\n| --------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| `runArgs` | `--add-host=host.docker.internal:host-gateway` | `:4000` and SearXNG on `:8181` on the laptop. |","table":"table-0"},"585":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-34","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                 | Value                                                        | Why                                                                                         |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `postCreateCommand` | `bash scripts/seed-dev-env.sh --devcontainer --context7-mcp` | Seeds the contributor home the way a workspace start seeds a workspace, docs lane included. |","table":"table-0"},"586":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-35","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"| Key                                | Value | Why                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------------------------------- | ----- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `customizations.vscode.extensions` |       | shellcheck and shfmt are the two `//:lint` runners, wired into the editor beside the Terraform, Docker and YAML helpers. |","table":"table-0"},"587":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-36","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#open-it-on-a-laptop","content":"`updateRemoteUserUID` is on, so the container's `coder` uid matches the host user and the\nbind-mounted checkout stays writable without a `chmod` dance."},"588":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-37","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh` materializes, into a laptop or CI home, the same stack-owned\nfiles a Coder workspace writes at startup — from the same template payloads, with no\nTerraform and no docker:"},"589":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-38","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag           | Effect                                                                                            |\n| -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--home <dir>` | Home tree to shape (default `$HOME`; created when absent; an existing non-directory is an error). |","table":"table-1"},"590":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-39","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag                  | Effect                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--litellm-url <url>` | Model-transport address rendered into `settings.json` and `~/.grok/config.toml` (default `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`). |","table":"table-1"},"591":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-40","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag             | Effect                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ---------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--context7-mcp` | Keep the `[mcp_servers.context7]` docs lane in the rendered `~/.grok/config.toml` even with no `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in the environment. Without it a keyless home renders the context7-free file, so the contributor has no docs lane while their workspaces do. The key is optional: with no value the server sends no `Authorization` header, still handshakes, and answers at the public rate limit (private libraries are the only thing out of reach), so a missing block is the defect this flag prevents, not a key problem. |","table":"table-1"},"592":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-41","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag             | Effect                                                                                                                                                        |\n| ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--devcontainer` | After a successful write, run `gh auth setup-git` when `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` is set and `gh` is on PATH. Best effort, and the token is never an argument. |","table":"table-1"},"593":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-42","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag      | Effect                            |\n| --------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `--force` | Re-materialize files a user owns. |","table":"table-1"},"594":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-43","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"| Flag        | Effect                                                            |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--dry-run` | Print the plan; write nothing, create no directory, call no `gh`. |","table":"table-1"},"595":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-44","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"The two research lanes that config can render are split by question type: a library,\nframework, CLI or setup question goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then\nquery docs on that id), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool,\nand a URL already in hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content` - Grok's native\n`web_search` is parked here and native `web_fetch` needs an egress proxy endpoint this\nstack does not run, so neither is a lane. Prove what this home declares with:"},"596":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-45","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"A missing `[mcp_servers.context7]` block in that file means the seed ran keyless and\nwithout `--context7-mcp`: re-run the seeder with the flag, adding `--force` when the\nexisting file carries no stack marker and the seeder left it alone. On a Coder\nworkspace the same repair runs from the stack host:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>`."},"597":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-46","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"What it writes: `~/.tmux.conf`, the shared `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` (with the\ncode-server and desktop VS Code User settings symlinked to it), `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`, the rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, the two terminal helpers in\n`~/.grok/bin`, `~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` plus this clone's `notes.md`, and the\n`~/.grok/bin` line in `~/.bashrc`."},"598":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-47","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"That `notes.md` is `notes_materialize` from the same library: it restores the canonical copy\nunder `~/.grok/notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/` over a live file that is empty or still the\nseeded stub, and copies a real live file back there, so a stub never walks over written\nnotes. Here that runs once, at seed time - the background watcher that repeats the back-copy\nabout every 30 seconds is a workspace-startup thing (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`; see\n../grok-build-and-agents.md) - so re-run the\nseeder to push a fresh edit to the canonical copy in this container."},"599":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-48","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"Two rules decide every write, and they are the same rules the workspace startup applies:"},"600":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-49","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"A file carrying the stack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment, the\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` JSON key, or the `__evoT1Stack = true` TOML key - is\nstack-owned and is reseeded when the seed in this repo drifted. Taking\n`~/.grok/config.toml` over means deleting both its marker lines, since the Grok CLI\ndeletes comments when it rewrites that file:\n../grok-build-and-agents.md."},"601":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-50","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"A marker-free file is user-owned and stays untouched unless `--force` says otherwise.\nThe terminal helpers and `notes-setup.sh` are the exception: startup rewrites those on\nevery boot, so this script does too."},"602":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-51","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#seeding-the-contributor-home","content":"It never writes a secret, never writes a GitHub token or a PAT, and never installs the\nGrok CLI — the golden image already carries `/usr/local/bin/grok`. Exit `5` means no\nproven checkout was found (it wants `docker-compose.yml` where it is looking), which is the\nsame repo-root probe every other script in the repo makes."},"603":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-52","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"The contributor container is its own credential chain, so the doctor runs in SELF mode\nthere — exactly as it does inside a Coder workspace:"},"604":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-53","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"SELF mode applies when `CODER_WORKSPACE_ID` is set **or** `EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER=1` is. The\ncontainer sets the latter; a Coder workspace sets the former."},"605":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-54","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"SELF means no docker at all and no `DOCKER_HOST`: one probe of this environment's own\ncredential chain, printed as one `self:` line per credential. `docker` in here addresses\nthe workstation daemon mounted in, never the stack host's workspace fleet."},"606":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-55","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"Pass only the repository URL. A named container argument is refused with a pointer to the\nhost shell, because that container lives on a daemon this environment cannot enumerate."},"607":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-56","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"Credentials still arrive by the one supported path: Coder External Auth mints them, and\nthe gh auth store is what git actually reads. That store is the target because\n`GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` are the copy injected when the container started and expire\nabout an hour later, and the plain `!gh auth git-credential` helper echoes that pair, so\ngit would keep sending a dead token. The helper this stack installs clears the pair first,\nwhich is why `gh auth setup-git` alone is not enough. There is no static token and no PAT\nin this repo's tooling."},"608":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-57","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#github-auth-in-the-container","content":"`push would FAIL` from the check above means one command:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git`.\nIt mints a live token, writes the store, installs that helper, and re-proves the verdict.\nFor `gh` itself, prefix `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN`: no process can rewrite another\nprocess's environment."},"609":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-58","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"The merge gate is the Linux image, so run it in the contributor container:"},"610":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-59","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"From a machine that is **not** inside the container, ask the image to run them instead of\ninstalling the toolchain onto that machine:"},"611":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-60","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"That is the host ingress: it `docker run`s `evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid\n(`$(id -u):$(id -g)`), with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)` so Bazel has a writable home\nand a name for a uid the image passwd file does not list, and runs the same suites CI\nruns. A macOS laptop gets the Linux result without a Linux box. Build the tag first with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` if the daemon does not have it."},"612":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-61","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#running-the-gate-here","content":"Native `bazelisk test` on macOS is a convenience, not a merge path. The suites run there,\nbut the BSD userland differs from the GNU one the image ships (`sed`, `date`, `find`,\n`sort`), so a green Darwin run is not the run that gates the merge and a red one is not\nproof of a defect. The container, or `test-in-image`, is the answer\ntest-with-bazel.md and ../branching.md gate on."},"613":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-62","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"`.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds `images/dev` with `docker buildx build` and the GitHub\nActions layer cache (`type=gha`), then `docker run`s that image and runs the checks inside\nit: `//:test-fast`, `//:lint`, `//:validate`, and the `docs-site/` Node checks."},"614":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-63","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"No Actions `container:` key is involved. The job runs on a plain runner and reaches the\ntoolchain through `docker run --user \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\"` with `HOME=/tmp` and\n`USER=$(id -un)`, so what gates the merge is an image tag, the bind-mounted checkout\nstays writable, permission tests see a non-root identity, and Bazel can name a uid the\nimage passwd file does not list."},"615":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-64","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"The Bazel job no longer installs the pins onto the runner: the image already has them at\nthe pinned versions."},"616":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-65","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"A pull request that does not touch `images/dev/**` replays the cached layers instead of\nrebuilding them, which is what keeps the gate's wall time flat."},"617":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-66","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-ci-does","content":"The equivalent run on your own machine is the host ingress above, so a CI-only failure is\nreproducible without a Linux box or a workflow rerun."},"618":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-67","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"`.devcontainer/contributor-env` is a sentinel, not a config file. It is tracked in this\nrepository, and `probe_repo` (in `scripts/lib/workspace.sh`) treats a clone that tracks it\nas a **miss** — exit `3` — even when the same clone also tracks\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`."},"619":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-68","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"The sentinel outranks every other signal, so `new-workspace` answers `workspace` for this\nrepo and a Coder workspace of it never builds the contributor container on its DinD\nsidecar."},"620":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-69","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"That container is the *laptop and CI* environment: it mounts the host docker socket and\nassumes a workstation daemon, neither of which belongs inside a workspace's nested dev\ncontainer."},"621":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-70","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#why-this-repos-own-workspaces-stay-on-workspace","content":"Do not copy the sentinel into another repository. An application repo's\n`.devcontainer/` is exactly what `new-workspace` is supposed to find, and the sentinel in\nfront of it silently downgrades that workspace to the plain template."},"622":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-71","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not the stack host.** The compose host (gmktec) is bootstrapped with\n`bash scripts/setup-host.sh`, and it cannot be this container: it owns the daemon that\nruns Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes. See\n../project-conventions.md for the ingress rule that keeps\nthat script the single documented `bash scripts/…`."},"623":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-72","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not a template change.** `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/startup.sh.tftpl` are untouched by any of this; the contributor\nseed reads them as payloads, it does not rewrite the twins."},"624":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-73","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not a second toolchain.** Nothing in the contributor path installs pins onto a laptop\nor a runner — that is what the image is for."},"625":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-74","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"**Not a credential store.** GitHub auth stays Coder External Auth; no static token and no\nPAT appears in any file this page describes."},"626":{"id":"/contribute/devcontainer-75","page_id":"/contribute/devcontainer","tags":["contributing","devcontainer","bazel","ci","docker"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/devcontainer#what-this-is-not","content":"When the container itself is the suspect, `bazelisk run //:validate` from inside it is the\nfastest answer, and ../troubleshooting.md covers the stack-side\nfailures it rules in or out."},"627":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"page","content":"Writing documentation","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style"},"628":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-0","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style","content":"The contract every page in docs/ has to satisfy before the site will render it: frontmatter, the authored H1, the opening scan pair, and the navigation file that decides whether a page is reachable at all."},"629":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-1","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"heading","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"Writing documentation"},"630":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-2","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"heading","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style#frontmatter","content":"Frontmatter"},"631":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-3","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"heading","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style#the-page-title","content":"The page title"},"632":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-4","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"heading","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style#the-opening-scan-pair","content":"The opening scan pair"},"633":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-5","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"heading","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style#chrome-that-arrives-on-its-own","content":"Chrome that arrives on its own"},"634":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-6","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"heading","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style#navigation","content":"Navigation"},"635":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-7","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","type":"heading","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"url":"/contribute/docs-style#checks","content":"Checks"},"636":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-8","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"**What's on this page**"},"637":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-9","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"What a page's frontmatter has to carry, and what each field is used for"},"638":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-10","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"Why every page writes its own `# H1` and what the layout prints beside it"},"639":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-11","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"The two opening lists that become the scan card, and the order they have to sit in"},"640":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-12","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"How abbreviations, tables and diagrams pick up their chrome without a component call"},"641":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-13","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"Where the sidebar comes from, and which check refuses a page it cannot reach"},"642":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-14","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"**What this enables**"},"643":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-15","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"Adding a page that shows up in the menu, the search index and the prev/next footer"},"644":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-16","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"Predicting what a reader sees from what you wrote, without opening a browser to find out"},"645":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-17","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"Fixing a docs failure from the assertion that named it"},"646":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-18","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#writing-documentation","content":"The site is a Fumadocs app that reads `docs/` straight out of the repository, so the markdown\nhere is the source of record: nothing is copied into `docs-site/`, and the README's links keep\npointing at these files. Most of the chrome is derived from the prose rather than authored in\nit, which is what keeps 20-odd pages consistent."},"647":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-19","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#frontmatter","content":"Every page declares three fields, and `docs-site/tests/unit/docs-content.test.ts` refuses a\npage that is missing any of them:"},"648":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-20","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#frontmatter","content":"`title` is the sidebar label, the search result title, and the string your `# H1` has to\nmatch character for character."},"649":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-21","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#frontmatter","content":"`description` is printed as the lead paragraph under the title and indexed for search. Quote\nit when it contains a colon, or YAML reads the line as a mapping."},"650":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-22","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#frontmatter","content":"`tags` are indexed, so a reader who searches for the word you filed the page under finds it.\n`pageSchema` strips unknown keys, which is why `tags` is declared explicitly in\n`docs-site/lib/content.ts` — a new field needs the same treatment or it silently never\nreaches the index."},"651":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-23","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#the-page-title","content":"Each page writes its own `# H1`, once, as the first heading of the body, spelled exactly like\nthe frontmatter `title`. That heading is the one the table of contents and every `#anchor`\npoint at, so `DocsH1` prints it and then the description beneath it as the lead paragraph. A\npage whose first heading is not its title keeps a plain heading and gets no lead paragraph,\nwhich is the visible sign that the two strings drifted apart."},"652":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-24","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#the-page-title","content":"The reason the authored heading is the only title: the page header used to print the frontmatter\ntitle and the body printed its own, so every page opened with the same string twice."},"653":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-25","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#the-opening-scan-pair","content":"Immediately after the title, two bold labels each followed by a bullet list:"},"654":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-26","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#the-opening-scan-pair","content":"`docs-site/lib/remark-page-brief.ts` recognises that four-node run and rewrites it into one\n`<PageBrief>` — the two-column card with the list and tick icons. The match is exact: the label\nhas to be the whole paragraph, bold, and spelled `What's on this page` and `What this enables`;\nthe lists have to be lists; and no heading may sit between the title and the pair. Get any of\nthat wrong and the page renders the lists as plain bullets, which is the only symptom."},"655":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-27","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#chrome-that-arrives-on-its-own","content":"Nothing below needs a component call in the page."},"656":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-28","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#chrome-that-arrives-on-its-own","content":"**Abbreviations.** A term listed in `docs/includes/abbreviations.md` as `*[TERM]: definition`\nis wrapped wherever it appears in prose. Hover or focus shows the definition; click or Enter\nopens it in a dialog. Text inside a link, a heading, inline code, or an existing term is left\nalone. Keep each definition tooltip-sized."},"657":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-29","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#chrome-that-arrives-on-its-own","content":"**Tables.** A content table gets its head pinned under the navbar while the table is on\nscreen, and a mirrored scroll bar at the bottom of the viewport when its columns are wider\nthan the page. Both come from `docs-site/components/table-chrome.tsx`; a table that already\ncarries a class is left as authored."},"658":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-30","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#chrome-that-arrives-on-its-own","content":"**Diagrams.** A ` ```mermaid ` fence renders through `Mermaid`, follows the light/dark switch,\nand shows the chart text beside a parse error rather than leaving a blank box."},"659":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-31","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#chrome-that-arrives-on-its-own","content":"**Fenced code.** Every fence needs a language, checked by the same contract test, because the\nhighlighter and the copy button both key off it."},"660":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-32","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#navigation","content":"The sidebar is not read from the filesystem: most pages sit flat in `docs/` while belonging to\ndifferent sections, and the reading order a prev/next footer needs is an editorial decision. The\ntree is declared once in `NAV` inside `docs-site/scripts/gen_nav.py` and generated into\n`docs-site/lib/nav.json`, which the page-tree builder imports and a Zod schema validates."},"661":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-33","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#navigation","content":"A section holds an ordered mix of leaves and collapsible folders. A leaf is `(label, path)` and a\nfolder is `(label, [child, ...])`. Folders stay ordinary directories: a section whose first entry\nis an index page uses that page as its clickable label."},"662":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-34","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#navigation","content":"Regenerate after you change either the tree or the file layout:"},"663":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-35","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#navigation","content":"`//docs-site:nav_test` and the contract test together refuse a page that exists but is not\ndeclared, and a declaration whose file is gone, so an unlinked page cannot be merged."},"664":{"id":"/contribute/docs-style-36","page_id":"/contribute/docs-style","tags":["docs","fumadocs","writing","nav"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/docs-style#checks","content":"`docs/manage-docs.sh` is the same set as a single operator CLI (`serve`, `build`, `preview`,\n`verify`, `typecheck`, `test`, `nav`, `status`, `clean`) for a host that has Node but not Bazel.\nThe gates run through Bazel; see Testing with Bazel for how the npm-backed\ntargets are wired and why they stay out of `//:test-fast`."},"665":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"page","content":"Navigating the repo with the Bazel graph","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel"},"666":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-0","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","content":"Answer what-depends-on-this with one bazelisk query instead of a grep sweep: the bazel-map CLI, the raw query predicates behind it, and the empty-result trap."},"667":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-1","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Navigating the repo with the Bazel graph"},"668":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-2","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"Why a query, not a grep"},"669":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-3","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-cli-bazel-map","content":"The CLI: bazel-map"},"670":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-4","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#before-a-large-edit-what-moves-with-this-file","content":"Before a large edit: what moves with this file?"},"671":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-5","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-suites-that-read-a-payload","content":"The suites that read a payload"},"672":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-6","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-twin-template-copies","content":"The twin template copies"},"673":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-7","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-filegroup-that-must-claim-a-new-payload-file","content":"The filegroup that must claim a new payload file"},"674":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-8","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-map-key-a-new-scriptssh-needs-and-the-test-a-new-function-needs","content":"The map key a new `scripts/*.sh` needs, and the test a new function needs"},"675":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-9","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#while-you-iterate-which-tests-and-when-you-still-owe-a-full-pass","content":"While you iterate: which tests, and when you still owe a full pass"},"676":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-10","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#enumerate-targets-instead-of-guessing-them","content":"Enumerate targets instead of guessing them"},"677":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-11","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#what-a-target-reads-and-why-an-unlisted-directory-is-invisible","content":"What a target reads, and why an unlisted directory is invisible"},"678":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-12","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"Raw query recipes when a verb does not fit"},"679":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-13","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-empty-result-is-not-an-answer","content":"The empty result is not an answer"},"680":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-14","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"**What's on this page**"},"681":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-15","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Why a structural question is a query here, not a grep sweep"},"682":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-16","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"The `bazel-map` CLI, its verbs, and the `--json` split that feeds `bazelisk test`"},"683":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-17","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Before a large edit: the twin copies, the filegroup, the map key, the test a new function owes"},"684":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-18","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"While you iterate: the derived test list, and when you still owe one uncached pass"},"685":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-19","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Enumerating real target names instead of guessing them"},"686":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-20","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"What a target actually reads, and why an unlisted payload directory stays invisible"},"687":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-21","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"The raw `bazelisk query` predicates for when a verb does not fit"},"688":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-22","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"**What this enables**"},"689":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-23","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Naming every suite that must move with a payload file in one query, in about 60 ms"},"690":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-24","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Running the suites that cover the files you touched instead of the whole fast gate"},"691":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-25","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Catching a payload directory no filegroup claims before the gates stay green by accident"},"692":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-26","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Telling a rule class from a target name without opening a BUILD file"},"693":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-27","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"Stopping the late-gate discovery of a coupling rule the graph already recorded"},"694":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-28","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"This repo keeps its coupling in BUILD files: `scripts/BUILD.bazel` names the libs and payload\ngroups each CLI reads, `tests/BUILD.bazel` names what each Bats suite opens, and the root\n`BUILD.bazel` aggregates every payload group into `//:stack_data`. `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test`\nfails when those maps drift from the sources, so the graph is a record an automated reader can\ntrust, and \"which suites cover this file\" has a ground truth sitting in it. Answering that from\n`grep` re-derives what one `bazelisk query` prints."},"695":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-29","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#navigating-the-repo-with-the-bazel-graph","content":"building-with-bazel.md explains the maps, runfiles, and the daily\ncommands; test-with-bazel.md explains the suites those queries name and the\ntwo inventory gates; ../project-conventions.md the CLI vocabulary this\ntool prints in."},"696":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-30","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"A sweep of this repo's own agent session logs measured the habits the graph is meant to\nreplace. Corpus: the 199 parent sessions recorded for this checkout under\n`~/.grok/sessions/` before 2026-11-01, counted with `jq` and `grep -c` over each session's\n`chat_history.jsonl`. Every count is a floor, because a truncated tool result hides a call."},"697":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-31","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"| Habit                   | Measured             |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------- |\n| `bazelisk query` at all | 8 sessions, about 4% |","table":"table-0"},"698":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-32","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"| Habit                      | Measured                  |\n| -------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `rdeps()` and `somepath()` | never used in that corpus |","table":"table-0"},"699":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-33","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"| Habit                                    | Measured                                                                                 |\n| ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Recursive grep for a structural question | the default instrument: `grep -rn` outnumbered `bazelisk query` by an order of magnitude |","table":"table-0"},"700":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-34","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"| Habit                                         | Measured                                          |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:test-fast` re-run to learn one dependency | 48 sessions ran it; 16 ran it three or more times |","table":"table-0"},"701":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-35","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"| Habit                                                       | Measured    |\n| ----------------------------------------------------------- | ----------- |\n| Reading the checker's own source to learn why a gate failed | 19 sessions |","table":"table-0"},"702":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-36","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"| Habit                                               | Measured                                                      |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Guessing a target name into `ERROR: no such target` | 4 to 7 sessions, depending on how strictly a match is counted |","table":"table-0"},"703":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-37","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"The cost of not querying shows up late. In the sessions that hit a coupling rule, the first\n`FAIL: //` line appeared only after a hundred or more messages of editing: the rule was\ndiscoverable in the graph on message 1, and was instead discovered by running the gate. One agent\naudited `//:stack_data` by listing the repo's top-level directories rather than diffing the graph\nagainst `git ls-files`, which is the same question asked in a way that cannot answer it."},"704":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-38","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#why-a-query-not-a-grep","content":"The verbs below are the shapes of those questions."},"705":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-39","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-cli-bazel-map","content":"`bazel-map` is a thin shell over `bazelisk query`. It reads nothing and writes nothing; each verb\nis one query, run from the real checkout, with Bazel's stderr kept so a miss is reported instead of\nswallowed. Run it through Bazel like every other CLI:"},"706":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-40","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-cli-bazel-map","content":"Flags, accepted anywhere in argv: `--staged`, `--untracked`, `--ref <ref>`, `--all`,\n`--label-kind`, `--json`, `-h|--help`. Exit codes: 0 when every named thing resolved, 1 on a query\nfailure, 2 on a usage error (an unknown verb or flag, or a verb invoked without the argument it\nneeds)."},"707":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-41","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-cli-bazel-map","content":"**Use `--json` when you want to act on the answer.** Prose (`== section ==`, `ok:`, `note:`,\n`summary:`) rides stderr and stdout carries only labels, one per line, which is the shape\n`bazelisk test` consumes:"},"708":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-42","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-cli-bazel-map","content":"Without `--json` the same labels are interleaved with the report lines, so piping them straight into\nanother command feeds it `ok: tests-for` as an argument. Paths are repo-relative, and a batch costs\none query: when a batch fails, the verb re-runs it per path and names the path the graph does not\nknow instead of hiding every other answer."},"709":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-43","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#before-a-large-edit-what-moves-with-this-file","content":"This is the question that costs the most sessions, because the answer is spread over four different\nmechanisms and each one is normally discovered by a failing gate."},"710":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-44","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-suites-that-read-a-payload","content":"Seven suites, printed in about 60 ms. Change that file and those are the suites that go red; edit one\nof them and the same verb in the other direction tells you what it covers."},"711":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-45","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-twin-template-copies","content":"`templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` keep independent payload copies that must move\ntogether, and `templates/common/` holds the files both consume as one shared copy. The verb matches\nbasenames over the union of the three payload groups, so the twin set is the graph's answer rather\nthan a convention you have to remember:"},"712":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-46","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-twin-template-copies","content":"A per-tree file whose sibling tree has no counterpart is a `warning:` naming the group that claims\nit: that asymmetry is the drift worth knowing about before you edit, not after."},"713":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-47","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-filegroup-that-must-claim-a-new-payload-file","content":"A file the graph does not know is a warning naming that path, and the usual cause is a file you just\ncreated that no filegroup claims yet. `owners` answers \"is this visible to the harness at all\":"},"714":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-48","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-filegroup-that-must-claim-a-new-payload-file","content":"An empty list is the finding: no filegroup carries the file, so `test-fast` and `lint` never open it.\nThe pair verb, `unlisted`, asks it per directory by diffing `git ls-files` against the graph's own\nsource list. It skips Bazel's own declaration files (`BUILD.bazel`, `*.bzl`, `MODULE.bazel`), which\nBazel reads implicitly and which only appear in the graph when a package deliberately exports them,\nso a directory holding nothing but a BUILD file reads as clean."},"715":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-49","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-filegroup-that-must-claim-a-new-payload-file","content":"A new top-level payload directory belongs in its own package with its own `filegroup` targets, joins\n`//:stack_data` by label, and is named in the map of every target that reads it, as\nbuilding-with-bazel.md spells out. `unlisted` is how you find out you have\nnot done that, rather than finding out that the gate never noticed."},"716":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-50","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-map-key-a-new-scriptssh-needs-and-the-test-a-new-function-needs","content":"A new `scripts/*.sh` is a Bazel target automatically (the package is generated from the directory),\nbut it is not wired automatically: it needs a `_SCRIPT_LIBS` key (the `scripts/lib` closure it\nsources) and a `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD` key (the payload groups it opens), both in `scripts/BUILD.bazel`.\nUntil those keys land the graph does not know the file at all, which is worth saying out loud:\n`rdeps` reads the graph, not the filesystem."},"717":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-51","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-map-key-a-new-scriptssh-needs-and-the-test-a-new-function-needs","content":"That warning is the honest version of \"nothing depends on this\", and it is the reason to keep\nstderr with a query. Go to the gate that owns the map instead of guessing its label:"},"718":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-52","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-map-key-a-new-scriptssh-needs-and-the-test-a-new-function-needs","content":"A script missing its key outright fails `//scripts` analysis before any test runs, and a script\nwhose declared map disagrees with its sources is what that checker reports, naming the pairing and\nthe direction. Once the keys are in place the same verb resolves the file, and\n`tests-for scripts/bazel-map.sh` lists `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` among its consumers. Every new function additionally owes one `# @function` block and one asserting test in exactly\none suite, which test-with-bazel.md covers; `//tests:shell_inventory` and\n`//tests:doc_coverage` are the two gates, and both are test targets the graph can point you at\nbefore the gate does."},"719":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-53","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#while-you-iterate-which-tests-and-when-you-still-owe-a-full-pass","content":"`scope` derives the test list from what this checkout changes, which is the loop that replaces\nre-running the whole fast gate after every edit:"},"720":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-54","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#while-you-iterate-which-tests-and-when-you-still-owe-a-full-pass","content":"Default scope reads `git diff HEAD`; add `--staged` for the index, `--untracked` to count files git\ndoes not track yet (a file you just created is exactly the case worth covering), or `--ref <ref>` for\nwhat this branch adds over a base. `--all` also lists the aggregate suites that reach everything\n(`//:test`, `//:test-fast`, `//:test-all` and `//tests:bats`), which is useful when you want the full\nset named explicitly rather than the leaf suites you can invoke. The default already includes the\nlint targets: every test rule here, `//lints:shell` and `//lints:shfmt` among them, is an `sh_test`."},"721":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-55","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#while-you-iterate-which-tests-and-when-you-still-owe-a-full-pass","content":"Two things scope tells you that a habit of `bazelisk test //:test-fast` hides:"},"722":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-56","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#while-you-iterate-which-tests-and-when-you-still-owe-a-full-pass","content":"**A Bats file is cheap; a script is not.** `tests-for tests/bats/common.bats` returns five targets.\n`tests-for scripts/lib/common.sh` returns 60 of the 67 test targets on this checkout, because every\nsuite declares `//scripts:doc_sources`, which is every `scripts/**.sh` (66 targets reach that\ngroup). That is the honest graph answer: editing a broadly sourced script genuinely re-runs nearly\nthe whole suite set, and knowing it before you start is what decides whether to run the derived\nlist or the aggregate."},"723":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-57","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#while-you-iterate-which-tests-and-when-you-still-owe-a-full-pass","content":"**An empty result means run everything.** Nothing changed against HEAD, or the change touches no\nfile the graph tracks, and `scope` says so and points at `//:test-fast`."},"724":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-58","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#while-you-iterate-which-tests-and-when-you-still-owe-a-full-pass","content":"The uncached pass is not replaced. `bazelisk test` replays a cached PASS for an action whose inputs\ndid not change, so the derived list proves your change against whatever actually rebuilds, and one\n`bazelisk test //:test-fast --nocache_test_results` remains the pass you owe before the PR, per\nbuilding-with-bazel.md. Use the derived list for the loop and keep the full\nuncached pass for the end, instead of paying for sixty suites to learn one thing per edit."},"725":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-59","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#enumerate-targets-instead-of-guessing-them","content":"Seven logged sessions guessed a target name and got `ERROR: no such target`. A target is never\nguessed, it is listed:"},"726":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-60","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#enumerate-targets-instead-of-guessing-them","content":"The rule class is the part that saves the round trip. `//docs-site:app` looks like the site's test\ntarget and is a `filegroup`, so `bazelisk test` on it fails to start; the real test targets are the\n`sh_test` rows. The same listing filtered by a name fragment is how you find a Bats suite's target\nwithout knowing the stem convention:"},"727":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-61","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#what-a-target-reads-and-why-an-unlisted-directory-is-invisible","content":"`//:test-fast` runs in a sandbox: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not the\ncheckout. So \"what does this target read\" is a real question with a printed answer, and the answer has\ntwo depths worth keeping separate."},"728":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-62","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#what-a-target-reads-and-why-an-unlisted-directory-is-invisible","content":"The direct inputs are the `data` the dependency maps write, which is what you edit when a target\nneeds one more payload group:"},"729":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-63","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#what-a-target-reads-and-why-an-unlisted-directory-is-invisible","content":"The files it can actually open are the transitive closure of those groups, which is what the verb\nprints:"},"730":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-64","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#what-a-target-reads-and-why-an-unlisted-directory-is-invisible","content":"That target resolves to 198 source files through 25 declarations: the gap is the `filegroup` and\n`scripts/lib` expansion under each label. `labels(\"data\", ...)` is the narrower question (\"what does\nthe BUILD file say\"), `files` is the wider one (\"what is in the sandbox\"), and the two disagreeing is\nusually the bug."},"731":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-65","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#what-a-target-reads-and-why-an-unlisted-directory-is-invisible","content":"This is why an unlisted payload directory stays invisible rather than being checked loosely: a file\nthat travels in no runfiles tree is a file no sandboxed action can open, so `test-fast` and `lint`\nnever read it and never fail. Nothing prints a warning about that. `owners <path>` and\n`unlisted <dir>` are the two ways to ask, and they are the difference between a payload the harness\nguards and a payload nobody has ever executed."},"732":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-66","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"Every verb is one of these, so a question outside the verb set needs one predicate, not a grep plan.\nAll of these run warm in about 50 to 70 ms."},"733":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-67","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"| Predicate              | Question it answers       |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `rdeps(//..., \"path\")` | what depends on this file |","table":"table-1"},"734":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-68","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"| Predicate        | Question it answers                       |\n| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------- |\n| `deps(//target)` | what this target depends on, transitively |","table":"table-1"},"735":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-69","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"| Predicate              | Question it answers                |\n| ---------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `kind(\"sh_test\", ...)` | narrow to the rules you can act on |","table":"table-1"},"736":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-70","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"| Predicate                   | Question it answers              |\n| --------------------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| `attr(\"name\", \"re\", pkg:*)` | find a target by a name fragment |","table":"table-1"},"737":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-71","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"| Predicate                  | Question it answers                        |\n| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| `labels(\"data\", //target)` | the groups a target declares as its inputs |","table":"table-1"},"738":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-72","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"| Predicate        | Question it answers                            |\n| ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |\n| `somepath(a, b)` | is there any path from this suite to that file |","table":"table-1"},"739":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-73","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"| Predicate                     | Question it answers     |\n| ----------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `A intersect B`, `A except B` | combine two result sets |","table":"table-1"},"740":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-74","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"The `kind` filter is what makes an `rdeps` usable. A bare `rdeps(//..., \"litellm/config.yaml\")`\nincludes `//:test-fast`, which reaches every file and therefore tells you nothing about this one:"},"741":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-75","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"Two exclusions earn their place. `except kind(\"test_suite\", //...)` drops the aggregates, which is\nwhat the CLI filters on so `//:test-fast` cannot answer a per-file question, and\n`except kind(\"sh_test\", //lints:*)` drops the two manual lint targets, `//lints:shell` and\n`//lints:shfmt`, that ride on every script:"},"742":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-76","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"A reachability proof, for when you must know that a gate really does sit on top of a file:"},"743":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-77","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"The whole-graph enumeration `unlisted` is built from, which is also the answer to \"how much of the\nrepo do the gates actually see\":"},"744":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-78","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"The `//` half is the honest measure of what the gates can see, and it moves as files are added, so\ntreat any absolute total in a document as a snapshot rather than a fact to assert."},"745":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-79","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#raw-query-recipes-when-a-verb-does-not-fit","content":"And set algebra, for \"is this payload covered by the suite I trust with it\":"},"746":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-80","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-empty-result-is-not-an-answer","content":"This is the trap that makes a hand-check unreliable, and it is the reason the CLI keeps Bazel's\nstderr instead of discarding it. A query can print `ERROR: no such target` and leave stdout empty,\nwhich looks exactly like \"nothing depends on this\":"},"747":{"id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel-81","page_id":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel","tags":["bazel","contributing","tooling","graph"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/navigate-with-bazel#the-empty-result-is-not-an-answer","content":"One mistyped character, an empty stdout, and a shell that tests only `-z \"$out\"` reports a clean\nresult. So before you believe an empty list: check the exit status (Bazel exits non-zero here), read\nstderr, and confirm the operand resolved at all with `bazelisk query '\"litellm/config.yaml\"'`, which\nprints its label back. A miss you can see is a finding; a miss you cannot see is a green gate over a\nfile nobody tests."},"748":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"page","content":"Strong types","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types"},"749":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-0","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types","content":"The typing rule for every language in this repo: Python under mypy with no Any, TypeScript under the strictest flags it compiles, Terraform variables with a declared constraint, and what bash and awk owe instead."},"750":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-1","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"heading","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Strong types"},"751":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-2","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"heading","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types#the-rule","content":"The rule"},"752":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-3","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"heading","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types#python","content":"Python"},"753":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-4","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"heading","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types#typescript","content":"TypeScript"},"754":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-5","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"heading","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types#terraform","content":"Terraform"},"755":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-6","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"heading","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types#bash-awk-and-bats","content":"Bash, awk, and Bats"},"756":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-7","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","type":"heading","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/strong-types#adding-a-file","content":"Adding a file"},"757":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-8","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"**What's on this page**"},"758":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-9","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"The rule in one sentence, and the two gates that hold it"},"759":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-10","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Python: the mypy profile, the aliases that replace `Any`, and how to add a file"},"760":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-11","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"TypeScript: the compiler switches, and how the `.mjs` scripts get checked"},"761":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-12","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Terraform: why a `variable` without a `type` is a runtime bug"},"762":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-13","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Bash, awk, and Bats: what an untyped language owes instead of annotations"},"763":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-14","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Adding a new language, or a new file to an existing one"},"764":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-15","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"**What this enables**"},"765":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-16","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Making an annotation that a reviewer cannot argue about, because a tool decides it"},"766":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-17","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Catching a wrong-shaped input at plan time or type-check time rather than in a container"},"767":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-18","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Adding a `.py`, `.ts`, or `.tf` file without guessing which list it belongs on"},"768":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-19","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#strong-types","content":"Telling a contributor why `Any` came back from CI"},"769":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-20","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#the-rule","content":"Every value has a declared shape at the boundary where it enters a module, and a\nbroad type is a design smell rather than a shortcut. Where a language has a type\nsystem, the strictest setting that compiles is the setting the repo uses. Where it\nhas none, the convention is the gate."},"770":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-21","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#the-rule","content":"Two gates carry most of it, and both are members of `//lints:all`, so `//:lint`,\n`//:test-all`, and the `bazel` job of `.github/workflows/ci.yml` run them:"},"771":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-22","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#the-rule","content":"| Gate           | Language | What it refuses                                         |\n| -------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//lints:mypy` | Python   | a missing annotation, an explicit `Any`, a bare generic |","table":"table-0"},"772":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-23","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#the-rule","content":"| Gate                    | Language   | What it refuses                                         |\n| ----------------------- | ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//docs-site:typecheck` | TypeScript | the same, plus every index access that can be undefined |","table":"table-0"},"773":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-24","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#the-rule","content":"`//:validate` adds the Terraform half, and `//tests:bats_mypy_lint_test` and\n`//tests:bats_tf_types_test` are the asserting sides of the two newest gates."},"774":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-25","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#python","content":"`mypy.ini` at the repo root is the whole policy. It is `--strict` written out option\nby option, plus the two that make this a rule rather than a preference:"},"775":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-26","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#python","content":"`disallow_any_explicit` refuses `Any` typed into a signature by a person.\n`disallow_any_generics` refuses `list` and `dict` written without their element\ntype, which are the same hole reached by a different spelling. The binary is the\n`MYPY_VERSION` pin from `images/dev/tool-versions.env`, baked into the golden image\nby `images/dev/Dockerfile`, so the gate needs no network and no `pip install`."},"776":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-27","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#python","content":"Run it directly when you want the error text without Bazel in the way:"},"777":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-28","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#python","content":"The pattern the profile pushes you toward is a named alias for anything unstructured.\n`scripts/lib/mcp_lane_report.py` reads TOML and JSON, so rather than widen a\nsignature it declares the two document shapes and narrows with `isinstance`:"},"778":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-29","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#python","content":"The same file writes its two fixed vocabularies as `Literal`s, which is what makes a\ntypo in a check name a gate failure rather than a line an operator never sees. A\nrecord that travels between functions gets a `NamedTuple`, a `@dataclass`, or a\n`TypedDict` — `tests/bazel_deps.py` names its Starlark map `LabelMap` and its\n`drift()` result `Drift`, and `tests/coverage/py_report.py` types the slice of\n`coverage report --json` it reads with three `total=False` TypedDicts."},"779":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-30","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#python","content":"`# type: ignore` needs a specific error code and a reason that names the real cause.\n`warn_unused_ignores` makes a stale one an error, so an ignore cannot quietly\noutlive the problem it papered over. Where the value is unstructured but an\n`isinstance` guard already decides what it is, a `cast` is the honest instrument:\nnarrowing there would change what happens to the malformed input."},"780":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-31","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#typescript","content":"`docs-site/tsconfig.json` starts at `strict: true` and adds the switches that find\nreal defects at no churn cost: `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `noImplicitOverride`,\n`noImplicitReturns`, `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch`, `noUnusedLocals`, and\n`noUnusedParameters`. Each carries a comment naming the class of bug it catches."},"781":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-32","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#typescript","content":"Two well-known switches are deliberately off. `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` collides\nwith React's optional DOM props and with fumadocs' `StaticOptions`;\n`noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature` collides with how the unit tests read the\nfumadocs source config. Both would be paid for in casts, which is the wrong\ndirection."},"782":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-33","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#typescript","content":"`allowJs` and `checkJs` are on, which is how the two operator CLIs\n`docs-site/scripts/serve_static.mjs` and `verify_site.mjs` are type-checked: each\nopens with `// @ts-check` and carries JSDoc `@param`/`@returns` on every function,\nwith `@typedef`s for the shapes it reads out of `lib/nav.json`. `zod` already\nvalidates the frontmatter at the content boundary in `docs-site/lib/content.ts`, so\nparsed pages arrive as typed `PageNode`s rather than as `unknown`."},"783":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-34","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#typescript","content":"`any`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, and `@ts-nocheck` are absent from every\ntracked file. The one exclusion is `docs-site/.source/`, the code fumadocs-mdx\ngenerates, which is gitignored. `docs-site/tests/unit/helpers/fumadocs-stub.ts` is\nwhere a dependency's shape gets stated once when a test needs one."},"784":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-35","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#terraform","content":"A `variable` block is the only place a Coder template can declare what an input is,\nand an undeclared one takes whatever HCL guessed. `search_mcp = \"flase\"` then reaches\na `== \"true\"` comparison and reads as false, so the operator gets a workspace that\ncame up wrong and a plan that said nothing. A declared constraint turns that into a\nplan-time error."},"785":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-36","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#terraform","content":"`scripts/lib/tf_types.awk` is run by `//:validate` over both template twins and\nreports three findings: `missing-type`, `broad-any`, and `bare-container` (a `list`,\n`set`, or `map` with no element type, which is `any` wearing a different hat). Only\nthe `type` attribute of a top-level `variable` block is read, so a `type` key inside\na `default` body is never mistaken for the constraint, and `any` is matched as a word\nso `company_mcp` is not a finding."},"786":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-37","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#terraform","content":"Both twins conform today: every `variable` carries a `type` and a `description`, and\nneither uses `any`, `list(any)`, or a bare container. `//tests:bats_tf_types_test`\nholds that, with fixtures that prove the gate still reports each finding rather than\nhaving quietly stopped looking."},"787":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-38","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#bash-awk-and-bats","content":"Bash has no type system, and no annotation comment buys one. What the repo does\ninstead is put the checking where the language can actually be checked:"},"788":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-39","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#bash-awk-and-bats","content":"`//lints:shell` runs ShellCheck at `--severity=warning` over every first-party\n`.sh`, and the tree is clean at that level today."},"789":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-40","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#bash-awk-and-bats","content":"Variables are declared before they are used and carry their shape in the\ndeclaration: `local -a` for an indexed array, `local -A` for an associative one,\n`local -r` or `declare -r` for a constant. Naming the kind is what makes an array\nthat was meant to be empty distinguishable from one that was never set."},"790":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-41","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#bash-awk-and-bats","content":"Every function in `scripts/` carries a `# @function` block with `Globals:`,\n`Arguments:`, `Outputs:`, and `Returns:`, and each `Arguments:` line states what\nthe parameter is. `//tests:doc_coverage` enforces the block and\n`//tests:shell_inventory` enforces that a test names the function."},"791":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-42","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#bash-awk-and-bats","content":"`set -euo pipefail` is the floor, so an unset parameter is a failure rather than\nan empty string."},"792":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-43","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#bash-awk-and-bats","content":"awk is POSIX-subset and single-purpose: a program in `scripts/lib/*.awk` takes its\ninput in `ARGV`, its configuration in `ENVIRON`, and prints tab-separated fields the\ncaller reads line by line. The types are that line shape, and they are documented in\nthe program's header block — see `scripts/lib/tf_types.awk` for the shape to copy.\nBats suites are bash and follow the same rules as their shell."},"793":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-44","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#adding-a-file","content":"| Adding                  | You also edit                                                                                                                               |\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `scripts/lib/<name>.py` | nothing: `//scripts:py_programs` globs it and `lints/run_mypy.sh` scans the directory; if a CLI runs it, it joins that CLI's `_SCRIPT_LIBS` |","table":"table-1"},"794":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-45","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#adding-a-file","content":"| Adding                | You also edit                                                                                                                                           |\n| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| another `.py` package | `lints/run_mypy.sh` `targets` **and** the data edge of `:mypy` in `lints/BUILD.bazel` — the two are kept equal by hand, and the runner's header says so |","table":"table-1"},"795":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-46","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#adding-a-file","content":"| Adding              | You also edit                                    |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `docs-site/**/*.ts` | nothing: it is already in the `tsconfig` program |","table":"table-1"},"796":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-47","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#adding-a-file","content":"| Adding       | You also edit                                                     |\n| ------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| a `.mjs` CLI | `// @ts-check` and JSDoc on every function, since `checkJs` is on |","table":"table-1"},"797":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-48","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#adding-a-file","content":"| Adding                     | You also edit                                                                  |\n| -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| a `variable` in a template | its `type` and its `description`, in both twins where the template keeps twins |","table":"table-1"},"798":{"id":"/contribute/strong-types-49","page_id":"/contribute/strong-types","tags":["types","mypy","typescript","terraform","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/strong-types#adding-a-file","content":"A Python file that lands outside the runner's list is unchecked code that looks\ngated, which is the failure `//tests:bats_mypy_lint_test` exists to catch: it\nenumerates the tree and fails on any `.py` the target list does not cover, with\n`tests/bats/fixtures/` the one sanctioned exclusion because that tree is scaffold\npayload typed in the repository it is copied into."},"799":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"page","content":"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Contribute"],"tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel"},"800":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-0","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","content":"Hermetic Bats: how a suite is discovered, what the stub PATH gives you, and the two inventory gates a new function has to satisfy."},"801":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-1","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)"},"802":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-2","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"Layout"},"803":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-3","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"Two gates, one rule"},"804":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-4","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#running-a-suite","content":"Running a suite"},"805":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-5","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"Which runtime gates"},"806":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-6","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"Measured coverage"},"807":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-7","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-three-paths-a-suite-may-use","content":"The three paths a suite may use"},"808":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-8","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"The stub-PATH recipe"},"809":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-9","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","type":"heading","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"Sandbox-safe: probe for the repo root, then say so"},"810":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-10","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"**What's on this page**"},"811":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-11","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"The layout: suites, helpers, stubs and fixtures"},"812":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-12","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"The two inventory gates that tie a production function to a test"},"813":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-13","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Running one suite, from any directory"},"814":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-14","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Which runtime actually gates a merge, and which one is a convenience"},"815":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-15","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"The stub-PATH recipe, and the two paths a suite may take"},"816":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-16","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Sandbox-safe repo-root probing"},"817":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-17","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"**What this enables**"},"818":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-18","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Adding a test that passes in the Bazel sandbox and on a laptop"},"819":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-19","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Making a new function satisfy `//tests:shell_inventory` without guessing"},"820":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-20","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Reproducing a CI failure locally by running the suites in the image CI runs them in"},"821":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-21","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#testing-with-bazel-the-bats-harness","content":"Every suite under `tests/` is hermetic Bats: it runs against stubs and a throwaway tree,\nnever against a daemon, a network, or the operator's real `.env`."},"822":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-22","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats/*.bats` — one suite per module. `tests/bats.bzl` turns each file into one\n`bats_<stem>_test` sh_test (dashes become underscores: `bats_spark_configure_test` for\n`spark-configure.bats`) and adds it to the `bats` suite that `//:test-fast` runs."},"823":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-23","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats/test_helper.bash` — the shared helpers, pulled in with `load 'test_helper'`."},"824":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-24","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/test_helpers.sh` — the assertions, shared with the bash gates."},"825":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-25","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats/tool_stubs/` — the fake external tools (`docker`, `ssh`, `curl`, `git`,\n`coder`, `psql`, `timeout`, …); `tests/bats/fixtures/` holds a fixture that has to answer\nlike the real client, such as the `psql-token` copies that echo `returning id`."},"826":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-26","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#layout","content":"`tests/bats_runner.sh` — what Bazel executes: it locates bats-core and this checkout in\nrunfiles, exports `REPO_ROOT`, and execs bats."},"827":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-27","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a function under `scripts/` is not named under\n`tests/`; `tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails when a `# @function` block (or the file header) is\nmissing or incomplete. So every new function needs, in the same change as the function:"},"828":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-28","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"one asserting test in the **one** suite for its module — a `scripts/lib/<mod>.sh` helper\nbelongs to `tests/bats/<mod>.bats`, and a helper that only an entry CLI defines belongs to\n`tests/bats/entry-fns.bats`, which loads single functions with `load_shell_function`;"},"829":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-29","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"the `# @function` block above the definition, with Globals/Arguments/Outputs/Returns."},"830":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-30","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"Name it in exactly one suite. A chain test that happens to mention the name also satisfies\nthe inventory, but the owning suite is the one that asserts its behaviour."},"831":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-31","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#two-gates-one-rule","content":"The inventory strips comment lines before it looks for a name, so a name that only ever\nappears in a `# @function` block or in prose is still untested: it has to sit in a code line\nsomewhere under `tests/`. The usual way to get one there is a unit case that calls the\nhelper directly:"},"832":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-32","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#running-a-suite","content":"A suite is one cached action. Its `.bats` file, `test_helper.bash`, `test_helpers.sh`,\n`bats_runner.sh` and the `scripts/**.sh` plus stub/fixture files it declares as `data` are\nits inputs, so editing one script re-runs only the suites that read it, and an unchanged\nsuite replays its cached PASS. Beyond that common set each suite names only the payload\ngroups it opens, in `_SUITE_PAYLOAD` in `tests/BUILD.bazel`; a suite whose target does not\ndeclare a payload it reads finds that payload missing under its own target, and\n`//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` is the gate that keeps the map honest."},"833":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-33","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"The merge gate is the Linux golden image — the same `images/dev/Dockerfile` the Coder\nworkspaces boot and CI builds:"},"834":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-34","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"Inside the contributor dev container (devcontainer.md) the commands\nabove are exactly what CI runs."},"835":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-35","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"On a host that is not that container, `//scripts:test-in-image` docker-runs\n`evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid, with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER` set so\nBazel can name that uid, and runs them there. `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE=<dir>` keeps the\narchives Bazel downloads between runs; both CI jobs set it and wrap it in\n`actions/cache` (building-with-bazel.md)."},"836":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-36","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"A native `bazelisk test` on macOS is convenience only: the BSD userland differs from the\nGNU one in the image (`sed`, `date`, `find`, `sort`), so a red run there is not proof of a\ndefect and a green one is not the run that unlocks the merge button. CI and branch\nprotection gate on the image result either way."},"837":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-37","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#which-runtime-gates","content":"The npm-backed gates (`//docs-site:typecheck`, `:unit`, `:unit_coverage`, `:verify`) are\n`manual` for the same reason, and they all run out of `docs-site/node_modules` in the\ncheckout: `run_npm.sh` resolves its own runfiles copy back into it, so a cold checkout\ninstalls once for the whole family, under `docs-site/.npm-ci.lock`. Without that lock\nevery test action ran its own `npm ci`, and a gate that started while another was still\nwriting the tree died on a SIGBUS reading a short esbuild binary. A box with no `flock`\n(a macOS laptop) installs unlocked, since nothing else is running there."},"838":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-38","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"Three targets answer \"is this code actually executed by a test\" instead of\nassuming it. They are `manual` like the npm gates, so `//:test-fast` stays fast, and\nthey are members of `//:test-all`, which pre-push and CI run:"},"839":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-39","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"`//tests:shell_coverage` is the one to understand before you write a shell test.\nIt builds a mirror of the checkout with `tests/coverage/instrument.py`: every\nproduction `.sh` is copied with one extra statement at the top of each function\nbody that appends `<path>\\t<function>` to `$EVO_COV_LEDGER`, and every other path\nstays a symlink into the real tree. `tests/bats_runner.sh` points `REPO_ROOT` at\nthat mirror when `EVO_COV_MIRROR` is set, so the suites execute the instrumented\ncopies through whichever access pattern they already use — run the CLI, source the\nlib, or `load_shell_function`. Then `tests/coverage/aggregate.py` names every\nfunction whose body never ran."},"840":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-40","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"Why a ledger and not `bash -x`: xtrace traces bats-core itself, wrote 3.4 GB of log\nfor one suite, and leaked trace lines into the stderr bats captures per test, which\nturned output-asserting suites red. The ledger measures the narrower thing the gate\nasks and breaks nothing."},"841":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-41","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"The consequence for a test author: a function counts when something calls it. A\nname in a comment, a stub, a fixture or a quoted grep pattern satisfies\n`//tests:shell_inventory` and satisfies nothing here."},"842":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-42","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"`//tests:py_coverage` discovers `tests/python/test_*.py` and\n`docs-site/test_gen_nav.py` under coverage.py 7.17, pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image; without it the\ntarget fails with a pointer to `//scripts:test-in-image` rather than passing while\nmeasuring nothing. `//docs-site:unit_coverage` is `npm run unit:coverage`\n(vitest v8 provider, thresholds in `docs-site/vitest.config.ts`)."},"843":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-43","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"Three runfiles traps bit this gate, and all fail *silently*, so they are worth\nnaming:"},"844":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-44","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"Under `bazelisk test` every source arrives as a **symlink**. A `find -type f`\nover the tree then sees no regular file and reports an empty suite, which reads\nas \"nothing to test\". `py_cov.sh` uses `find -L`."},"845":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-45","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"coverage.py resolves the symlink and records the **checkout** path, while the\nprocess cwd is the runfiles tree. A relative `--include=scripts/lib/*.py` is\nmatched against the cwd, matches nothing, prints `No data to report`, and exits\n`0`. The gate therefore decides from `tests/coverage/py_report.py`, which lists\nthe `.py` files the tree ships and requires each one to appear in the machine-\nreadable report above the floor. A module nobody imported is a named failure."},"846":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-46","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"The mirror pads every `.sh` it instruments, so a suite that measures a payload's\n**byte size** through `REPO_ROOT` prices the harness instead of the product. This is\nhow `//tests:shell_coverage` failed an execve budget the checkout had already passed:\n`templates/common/notes-setup.sh` weighed 1182 bytes more in the mirror than it\nships. A size gate resolves its tree through `bats_source_repo_root`, and\n`startup-script-size.bats` builds a real instrumented mirror to prove it still\nmeasures what ships."},"847":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-47","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#measured-coverage","content":"The same \"empty looks like green\" rule applies to the vitest gate: it must measure\nfiles no test imported. Vitest 4 made that the default and removed the `all` key\nfrom `CoverageOptions`. To prove the default still holds after a vitest bump, drop\na `lib/__cov_probe.ts` exporting one uncalled function and confirm the run reports\nit at 0% and fails; then delete the file."},"848":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-48","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-three-paths-a-suite-may-use","content":"`REPO_ROOT` — where the checkout is *for this run*. `tests/bats_runner.sh` exports it;\noutside Bazel it is whatever `bats_canonical_repo_root` resolves. Derive it, never\nhardcode a machine path, and treat \"cannot resolve\" as a `skip`, not a failure."},"849":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-49","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-three-paths-a-suite-may-use","content":"`BATS_TEST_TMPDIR` — per-test scratch, removed after each test. `make_scratch` prints a\nfresh directory under it; `fake_root`-style helpers build a throwaway checkout there."},"850":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-50","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-three-paths-a-suite-may-use","content":"`bats_source_repo_root` — the checkout whose **bytes** are what the stack ships, which\nunder the coverage mirror is the one the mirror was built from (`EVO_COV_SOURCE_ROOT`)\nwhile `REPO_ROOT` is the mirror. A gate that counts the size of a shipped payload reads\nit there; the production functions it loads still come from `REPO_ROOT`, so measuring\nthe shipping tree costs no coverage mark."},"851":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-51","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"Copy this shape; it is the convention every suite already uses (`use_stubs` prints nothing\nand shadows PATH for the current test only, and fails when a stub is missing):"},"852":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-52","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`stub_bin <tool…>` copies each stub out of `STUB_BIN_DIR` into `${BATS_TEST_TMPDIR}/bin`\nand `chmod 755`s it; `use_stubs` = `stub_bin` + a fresh `STUB_LOG` + the PATH shadow.\nThe copy-plus-chmod is load-bearing: the Bazel sandbox and some tar extractions strip the\nexec bit, and a per-test copy keeps one test's argv log out of the next test."},"853":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-53","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`with_path <dir…>` prepends directories to PATH when a test needs a hand-written fake\ninstead of a shared stub."},"854":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-54","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"**Prepend; never replace PATH.** `use_stubs` and `with_path` put the stub directory *in\nfront of* the host PATH. `PATH=\"${BIN}\"` takes `bash`, `grep`, `cut`, `sort` and `wc` away\nfrom the script under test as well, and a script that pipes its answer through\n`grep | wc | tr` then fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the contract the\nsuite means to check."},"855":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-55","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"**`run` keeps stdout in `$output`.** With `run --separate-stderr` there is no `$stdout`:\n`$output` is stdout and `$stderr` is stderr. An assertion written against `$stdout`\ncompares against an empty string and fails, which is how a whole suite of real\nassertions can look like a broken harness."},"856":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-56","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"**`run` can call a helper, and a helper that `exit`s is safe.** `run` forks, so\n`run --separate-stderr usage_error '…'` followed by `[ \"${status}\" -eq 2 ]` works even\nthough `usage_error` ends in `exit 2`. Pass a real command (`bash \"${SCRIPT}\" …`) when\nthe script is what is under test."},"857":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-57","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`stub_log <tool>`, `stub_log_last`, `stub_log_count` read back what the stub recorded from\n`$STUB_LOG/<tool>.log`; assert on the argv, not on prose."},"858":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-58","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"`real_bin <tool>` resolves a tool with the stub directory removed from PATH. That is the\nintegration-tier gate: `if [ -z \"$(real_bin terraform)\" ]; then skip \"terraform not\ninstalled\"; fi` — a test that could talk to a real daemon skips when the tool is absent\ninstead of failing."},"859":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-59","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"The `docker` stub passes `exec`/`compose exec` through by default (`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=1`),\nso a binary the script execs inside a container lands on its own stub; set\n`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=0` with `STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RC`/`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_OUT` for the\n\"the daemon answered, and it said No\" case."},"860":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-60","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#the-stub-path-recipe","content":"Assertions come from `tests/test_helpers.sh`: `fail`, `assert_eq`, `assert_match`,\n`assert_not_match`, `assert_file_exists`, `assert_file_not_exists`, `load_shell_function`.\n`load_shell_function` is the one harness place that expands a function body (it is how a\nsingle entry-CLI function gets unit-tested); production scripts still never use `eval`."},"861":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-61","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"A script must work when the cwd is **not** the checkout. Under `bazel test` the working\ndirectory is an exec root or a runfiles tree that holds *copies* of whatever the target\ndeclares as `data`; under `bazel run` it is wherever the operator typed `bazelisk`, and\n`bazel run` alone sets `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`. Neither guarantees a working Docker\ndaemon, an `ssh`, or a reachable Spark box — a sandboxed action usually has none of them on\nPATH."},"862":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-62","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"So resolve the root from the environment first, then prove it, and fail loudly when the\nproof fails. `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` is that resolver and every CLI that\nreads a file the checkout alone holds calls it, so do not write a second one. It takes the calling\nscript's own directory as its last candidate and the marker to probe for (`docker-compose.yml` by\ndefault); the call site in every one of those CLIs is this:"},"863":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-63","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"It tries `${REPO_ROOT:-}`, then `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}`, then `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}`,\nthen `${PWD}`, then `${SELF_DIR}/..`, and prints the first candidate that proves out."},"864":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-64","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"The `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` default is not decoration: these scripts run\nunder `set -euo pipefail`, and a bare `${REPO_ROOT?}` aborts with `REPO_ROOT: parameter not\nset` the moment a sandbox or CI runner leaves the variable unset."},"865":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-65","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"The file probe (`-f \"${candidate}/${marker}\"`) says \"this is a repo root\"; refusing a derived\ncandidate whose `pwd -P` lands under `bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or a\n`*.runfiles` tree says \"and it is the real checkout, not a copy\", because runfiles carries its\nown copy of the marker. Two probes, because either one alone passes in the wrong case: writing a\nrotated `.env` or a `sparks/<host>.env` through the runfiles copy reports success while the\ncheckout keeps the old bytes. An explicit `${REPO_ROOT}` is exempt from the second probe — it is\na statement about where to work, and this harness points it at the copy of the repo under test."},"866":{"id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel-66","page_id":"/contribute/test-with-bazel","tags":["bats","tests","bazel","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/contribute/test-with-bazel#sandbox-safe-probe-for-the-repo-root-then-say-so","content":"Exit `5` with one `unresolvable repo root` line beats guessing a cwd and half-running a\nfleet check against a copy of the repo."},"867":{"id":"/dashboard","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"page","content":"Dashboard and desktops","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard"},"868":{"id":"/dashboard-0","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard","content":"The Homepage dashboard and Kasm Workspaces: what each card reads, how the password is issued, and how a desktop gets streamed."},"869":{"id":"/dashboard-1","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"Dashboard and desktops"},"870":{"id":"/dashboard-2","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#homepage-dashboard","content":"Homepage dashboard"},"871":{"id":"/dashboard-3","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#what-it-shows","content":"What it shows"},"872":{"id":"/dashboard-4","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#login","content":"Login"},"873":{"id":"/dashboard-5","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#config-files","content":"Config files"},"874":{"id":"/dashboard-6","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#after-a-recreate-revalidate-once","content":"After a recreate: revalidate once"},"875":{"id":"/dashboard-7","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#host-stats-and-the-docker-socket","content":"Host stats, and the docker socket"},"876":{"id":"/dashboard-8","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#look","content":"Look"},"877":{"id":"/dashboard-9","page_id":"/dashboard","type":"heading","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"Kasm Workspaces"},"878":{"id":"/dashboard-10","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"**What's on this page**"},"879":{"id":"/dashboard-11","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"What each dashboard card shows and which API it reads"},"880":{"id":"/dashboard-12","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"The header tiles: CPU, memory, temperature, disk, interface throughput"},"881":{"id":"/dashboard-13","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"The password gate, the proxy in front of it, and how to rotate the password"},"882":{"id":"/dashboard-14","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"Kasm's wizard-and-UI port pair, and what resetting it destroys"},"883":{"id":"/dashboard-15","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"**What this enables**"},"884":{"id":"/dashboard-16","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"Reading the box's health from one page without a shell"},"885":{"id":"/dashboard-17","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"Fixing a card that reports down when the service itself is up"},"886":{"id":"/dashboard-18","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#dashboard-and-desktops","content":"Two services that read the box rather than serve code: the Homepage dashboard (the\nlanding page) and Kasm Workspaces (streamed desktops)."},"887":{"id":"/dashboard-19","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#what-it-shows","content":"A card per service, with status pulled from each service's own API over the compose\nnetwork - `http://coder:3000`, `http://litellm:4000`, `http://ollama:11434`,\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status` - so nothing here needs a daemon credential. The\nheader carries boxed tiles for host CPU, memory, CPU temperature, disk and `eth0`\nthroughput, beside the clock, local weather (open-meteo, keyless) and a DuckDuckGo\nsearch."},"888":{"id":"/dashboard-20","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#what-it-shows","content":"Card `href`s come from `STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` /\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`, which default to LAN URLs built from `STACK_LAN_HOST` and the\nport variables. Change a port or re-detect the LAN IP and the links follow; set `auto`\nin `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` and the cards follow the names bootstrap composes from the\nnaming parts (public-access.md). The Hermes card\nfollows `STACK_HERMES_URL` when bootstrap sets it, and stays on\n`http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` when that key is empty. See hermes.md."},"889":{"id":"/dashboard-21","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#login","content":"`https://<host>/` serves the dashboard behind a self-signed certificate that\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` issues, so expect a one-time browser warning. Port 80 redirects\nto HTTPS."},"890":{"id":"/dashboard-22","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#login","content":"The password gate is homepage v2's built-in one, and it is worth keeping: the page\nlists every service on the box. `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` holds the plaintext, because\nhomepage hashes that env var itself but still needs the value."},"891":{"id":"/dashboard-23","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#login","content":"Either form rewrites `.env`, recreates the container and verifies the new value landed.\nThe script's job is keeping the secret out of shell history, `ps` and git, and leaving\n`.env` at mode 600."},"892":{"id":"/dashboard-24","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#config-files","content":"`homepage/config/*.yaml` plus `custom.css` are bind-mounted read-only, so `git diff`\nstays the record of dashboard changes, and the directory itself has to stay writable\nbecause the app creates `logs/` inside it. A new config file (`kubernetes.yaml`, ...)\nneeds its own mount line in `docker-compose.yml` before the app sees it."},"893":{"id":"/dashboard-25","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#config-files","content":"`custom.css` lives in `/app/config` and is served by the allow-listed\n`/api/config/custom.css` route. It styles the sign-in page too, so keep internal\nhostnames out of it."},"894":{"id":"/dashboard-26","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#after-a-recreate-revalidate-once","content":"v2.4.0 serves a prerendered page with the settings baked in, so a freshly created\ncontainer shows an empty default layout (`initialSettings {}`) until the config is\nre-read. Click the refresh button in the header, or:"},"895":{"id":"/dashboard-27","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#after-a-recreate-revalidate-once","content":"A plain `restart` keeps the generated page, so day-to-day YAML edits need nothing.\nNew asset files do need a recreate (`up -d`), since `restart` keeps the old mounts."},"896":{"id":"/dashboard-28","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#host-stats-and-the-docker-socket","content":"CPU, memory, CPU temperature and disk are host-wide through `/proc`. A network widget\nonly sees the container's own netns (`lo`/`eth0`) - `/sys/class/net` is namespaced, so\nthe host Wi-Fi NIC stays invisible even with `/sys` mounted. That tile reads `eth0`."},"897":{"id":"/dashboard-29","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#host-stats-and-the-docker-socket","content":"The docker socket stays unmounted: service status comes from each service's API, which\navoids handing the dashboard a root-equivalent host credential. The Hermes card in the\n**Host** group is the same pattern (`siteMonitor` of `http://hermes:9119/api/status`);\nthe Hermes container itself *does* mount the socket — that credential lives there, not\nhere. See hermes.md."},"898":{"id":"/dashboard-30","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#look","content":"The layout follows the upstream README example - logo plus greeting top-left, boxed\nresource tiles, clock, weather and search top-right, cards on a wallpaper. The\nwallpaper (`background.webp`) and the logo (`evo-t1.svg`) live in\n`homepage/assets/icons/`, mounted at `/app/public/icons` and served as `/icons/...`.\nHomepage's auth middleware exempts only that path from the sign-in redirect, which is\nwhat lets the same wallpaper render on the login page."},"899":{"id":"/dashboard-31","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"**First boot:** `https://<host>:3000` - run the install wizard once."},"900":{"id":"/dashboard-32","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"**After install:** the UI is on `https://<host>:4443`. Both ports serve TLS with the\nself-signed certificate Kasm's own installer generates, and plain `http://` reaches\nneither port."},"901":{"id":"/dashboard-33","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"`KASM_UI_PORT` has to match on **both** sides of the port mapping. The daemon-side\nlistener listens on that number, so the mapping is `4443:4443`."},"902":{"id":"/dashboard-34","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"The image ships default users (`admin@kasm.local` / `user@kasm.local`); change them\nduring the wizard."},"903":{"id":"/dashboard-35","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"Kasm is a privileged Docker-in-Docker container and the only **always-on** privileged\nservice in this stack - the other privileged container is the opt-in workspace DinD\nsidecar. Keep Kasm LAN-only unless you have decided who may reach the public name\n(see SECURITY.md)."},"904":{"id":"/dashboard-36","page_id":"/dashboard","tags":["homepage","kasm","dashboard","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/dashboard#kasm-workspaces","content":"Losing the wizard after install is expected: use `:4443`. Resetting Kasm means\nremoving the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config."},"905":{"id":"/getting-started","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"page","content":"Getting started","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started"},"906":{"id":"/getting-started-0","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started","content":"First boot on a GMKtec EVO-T1, top to bottom — bootstrap the host, bring the stable stack up, register Coder, push the templates, open a workspace."},"907":{"id":"/getting-started-1","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"Getting started"},"908":{"id":"/getting-started-2","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#before-you-start","content":"Before you start"},"909":{"id":"/getting-started-3","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"First boot"},"910":{"id":"/getting-started-4","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Once Coder answers"},"911":{"id":"/getting-started-5","page_id":"/getting-started","type":"heading","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"What to read next"},"912":{"id":"/getting-started-6","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"**What's on this page**"},"913":{"id":"/getting-started-7","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"The one-time host bootstrap, and why it is the only step run straight from a shell"},"914":{"id":"/getting-started-8","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"Bringing the stable stack up and pulling the models"},"915":{"id":"/getting-started-9","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"Registering the first Coder account, pushing both templates, wiring the chat agent"},"916":{"id":"/getting-started-10","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"Creating the first workspace from a git URL"},"917":{"id":"/getting-started-11","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"**What this enables**"},"918":{"id":"/getting-started-12","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"A working stack on a fresh box without reading the whole README first"},"919":{"id":"/getting-started-13","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"Knowing which step to re-run when a later one says a prerequisite is missing"},"920":{"id":"/getting-started-14","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#getting-started","content":"The commands are the README's spin-up section\nwith the reasoning attached. Run them on the stack host, not inside a workspace: a\nworkspace's `docker` addresses its own DinD sidecar, and the whole-stack targets reach the\ndaemon that runs Coder."},"921":{"id":"/getting-started-15","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#before-you-start","content":"A GMKtec EVO-T1 (or any box with the Arc 140T and enough RAM for the model in\n`OLLAMA_MODELS`) with Docker and Compose v2 installed."},"922":{"id":"/getting-started-16","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#before-you-start","content":"The LAN address you will browse to. `CODER_ACCESS_URL` has to be an `https://` URL that\nis not `localhost`, or a workspace cannot reach LiteLLM."},"923":{"id":"/getting-started-17","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Create the one file that holds real secrets, and lock it down before anything else reads\nit:"},"924":{"id":"/getting-started-18","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Bootstrap the toolchain — the only step that runs before Bazel exists, because it is what\ninstalls Bazelisk:"},"925":{"id":"/getting-started-19","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Then look before you change anything. `--check` is read-only: missing tools, placeholder\nsecrets, certificates, images."},"926":{"id":"/getting-started-20","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Prepare the box: rotate the secrets, set `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, issue the self-signed\ncertificates, build `evo-t1-dev:latest`, pull the DinD image. It is idempotent, so re-run\nit after changing `docker-compose.yml`, `litellm/config.yaml`, `searxng/settings.yml` or\n`proxy/*.conf`."},"927":{"id":"/getting-started-21","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#first-boot","content":"Start the stable stack, and pull the models `OLLAMA_MODELS` names (a large download):"},"928":{"id":"/getting-started-22","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Register the first account — that account is the site admin. Browsers only expose\n`crypto.randomUUID()` in a secure context, so this points at the TLS listener:"},"929":{"id":"/getting-started-23","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Push both templates (`workspace` first, `devcontainer` last). The source travels as a tar\non stdin and the credential is minted, used and deleted in the same run, so there is no\nhost `coder` CLI and no login here:"},"930":{"id":"/getting-started-24","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Wire the control-plane chat agent — print the intended end state, then apply it:"},"931":{"id":"/getting-started-25","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Create the first workspace. One repository per workspace is the default, and the template\npicks itself from the URL:"},"932":{"id":"/getting-started-26","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#once-coder-answers","content":"Then finish the two browser-only steps: run the Kasm wizard once at `https://<host>:3000`\nand use `https://<host>:4443` after it, and open the dashboard at `https://<host>/` with\nthe `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` that `bootstrap` printed."},"933":{"id":"/getting-started-27","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Coder workspace templates — what the two templates build and which\nvariable does what."},"934":{"id":"/getting-started-28","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"LiteLLM model routing — why the answer you got came from the box it\ncame from."},"935":{"id":"/getting-started-29","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Troubleshooting — start here when a step above says no."},"936":{"id":"/getting-started-30","page_id":"/getting-started","tags":["start","bootstrap","install","coder"],"type":"text","url":"/getting-started#what-to-read-next","content":"Develop and\nBranching — the gates a change passes on its way back to `main`."},"937":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"page","content":"Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents"},"938":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-0","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents","content":"What runs inside a workspace and in the Coder control plane: terminal profiles, the shared `~/.grok` profile, and where models come from."},"939":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-1","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace