ading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"The three stack-seeded skill commands"},"957":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-19","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","type":"heading","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-this-is-not","content":"What this is not"},"958":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-20","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"**What's on this page**"},"959":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-21","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Terminal and editor defaults, and the profile the templates seed"},"960":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-22","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"What `grok doctor` reports inside a workspace, and which findings are expected"},"961":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-23","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image, and how it is pinned"},"962":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-24","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"The rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`: model aliases and role fallbacks"},"963":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-25","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"One shared profile volume per user, and what last-booted rewrites"},"964":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-26","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"The search lane and the MCP servers behind it"},"965":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-27","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Coder Agents on the control plane and the model config it needs"},"966":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-28","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"**What this enables**"},"967":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-29","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Changing a model alias in one place and knowing which workspaces see it"},"968":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-30","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Explaining why one workspace's `~/.grok` disagrees with another's"},"969":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-31","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Knowing why the native `web_search` tool is parked and the SearXNG lane answers"},"970":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-32","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Deep reference for what runs *inside* a workspace and in the Coder control plane: the\nterminal profiles, the per-user `~/.grok` profile, where the CLI and the chat agent get\ntheir models, and what the stack seeds for the agent."},"971":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-33","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Related: coder-templates.md (the template files themselves),\nlitellm-routing.md (what the aliases route to), and\ntroubleshooting.md."},"972":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-34","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `tmux`; the automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build and task commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."},"973":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-35","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The `tmux` profile gives each terminal of a folder its own pane. The base session\nname is unchanged: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>`. A new terminal walks into one of\nthat folder's sessions only while no client is attached to it, oldest first -\nthat is the closed-tab rejoin, so a tab you closed or reloaded leaves its\nsession unattached and the next terminal finds it with its history intact, and\n`ssh` + `tmux attach -t <session>` lands there too. A terminal opened when\nevery session of the folder already has a client takes the next numbered\nsibling instead: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ... Sessions persist\nunattached until the container restarts, so a reattached terminal still\nreaches the output a closed tab produced. `tmux ls` lists the names and\n`tmux list-clients` says who is looking at each. Every target the helper\ngives tmux carries the `=` exact-match prefix (a bare name is answered by any\nsession starting with it, so a closed `foo` could be answered by a live\n`foo-2` and re-create the mirror), and a reopen whose session vanished\nmid-attach raises a fresh session rather than exiting along with the tab.\nTwo tmux behaviors shape that path: a session raised by a tab's own\nforeground `new-session` has its first pane closed when that client exits\n(superuser#575909),\nwhich is why the helper creates detached and attaches separately; and with\nthe default `exit-empty` the server itself leaves when its last session does\n(tmux#2081), which is why a\nreopen probes the server before raising anything - a tab whose server is gone\nhas ended normally, and re-raising there would boot a server for a terminal\nnobody is looking at (tmux has no server probe: `has-session` answers the same\nfor a missing session and a missing server, which is why upstream declined to\nadd `check-server`, tmux#2523).\nNo upstream issue accounts for the session loss: `destroy-unattached` is off,\nso a detached session is meant to survive, and tmux gives a session no grace\nperiod after it loses its last client\n(tmux#5821 is an open request for\none)."},"974":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-36","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"One pane per visible terminal is what that rule buys. Clients attached to one\ntmux session share that session's current window, so a design that always\nreattaches makes every extra browser tab a mirror of the same pane: another\nfull xterm.js in the Chrome tab re-parsing the same stream (each with a\nscrollback buffer preallocated from `terminal.integrated.scrollback`), another\nper-client encode in the tmux server, and a re-wrap repaint for every client\neach time any client activates or resizes. Measured on this workspace: 14\nattached clients against 2 real panes. Two terminals opening in the same\ninstant can still land on one session - that same mirror, and rarer than a\nkeystroke."},"975":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-37","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"**Grok Build** keeps its own session, keyed off the repository: the helper walks up\nfrom the terminal's folder with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or uses the\nterminal's `$PWD` outside a clone), names the session `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`\nafter that root, and runs `grok --cwd <repo root>` inside it. A terminal opened\nanywhere inside the clone rejoins the one session the repository shares, and starts\nGrok Build at the repository root. That profile is deliberately the old\nalways-reattach shape: watching the same agent run from several tabs is the\nfeature, and the mirrors pay what mirrors pay. What the seed gives them is\n`focus-events on`, so a TUI in the pane can idle its animations while its tab\nis blurred, and `escape-time 10`, which keeps typing responsive on each of\nthem."},"976":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-38","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"With no tmux (offline first boot) the helper execs a plain login shell, so the\ndefault terminal still opens."},"977":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-39","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`display_apps.web_terminal` stays **off**: it would open a shell on the agent's\nworking dir (`/home/coder`). A custom command-type `coder_app` named **Web\nTerminal** (`order = 6`, after **VS Code Web** at `order = 5`, before **GitHub\nreauth** at `order = 7` and **Install GitHub App** at `order = 8`) takes that slot,\n`cd`s to the clone folder - an empty `repo_url` keeps it on `/home/coder` - and\nexecs a login shell. Like the built-in, it reads no `terminal.integrated.*`\nsettings."},"978":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-40","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries\nan inlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`), because the\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. Both twins pin the\nrelease in one `code_server_version` local and pass it to the upstream installer as\n`--version`: without that flag `https://code-server.dev/install.sh` follows `latest`,\nand the editor would move under every workspace with no commit in this repo.\n`workspace` installs code-server only while that pinned release is missing from\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server` (the guard names\n`lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`, the directory the installer unpacks, not\nthe `bin/code-server` symlink, which points at the last release installed and would\nhold a warm volume on its first one) and starts it with `--auth none --port 13337\n--app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`, passing no settings payload.\n`devcontainer` has no persistent prefix to reuse, so it installs the pinned release\nfresh under `/tmp/code-server` on every start."},"979":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-41","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The editor opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`: the\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL and as a positional argument to the code-server binary\nwith `--ignore-last-opened`. `workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and\n`/home/coder` with no repository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a\nbuilt dev container and `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the\nplain container. On first start the inlined `coder_script.code_server` creates that\nfolder and waits up to 60 s for `.git` (skipped when the folder is `/home/coder`) so\nthe clone can land before code-server launches. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the\nagent would chdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail\nthe agent. With the sample `REPO_URL` that folder is\n`/home/coder/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`."},"980":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-42","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"The icon travels as a `data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from\n`templates/common/vscode-alt.svg`: Coder serves icons only from its bundled\n`/icon/*` set or a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is not bundled.\n`workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a compact\nribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`."},"981":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-43","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"One clipboard is in play: the one on the computer you are typing on. The seed runs\n`set -g mouse off` and unbinds every key and every click that could move text into\nor out of tmux's own buffer, so the terminal client keeps each button and does the\ncopying itself, the way it does in a native terminal on that machine. OSC 52 is the\none route that still depends on what sits at the other end of the terminal, and it\nbelongs to an app inside the pane rather than to the operator. Where each claim\nbelow comes from: the tmux ones were read off the shipped tmux 3.4 on this box (what\nan attach writes to the client, and what `list-keys` still holds once the payload is\nsourced), and the setting names, values and defaults were read out of the shipped\ncode-server 4.145.0's own config registry (VS Code base 1.145.0). The two\nClipboard-API observations -- that a client selection fires\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText`, and that `document.execCommand('copy')` is the\nfallback that still lands on an insecure origin -- come from the earlier\nheadless-Chromium pass over that build with the rendered `settings.json` twin and the\ntmux payload loaded, when `mouse` was still on and the copy out was a Shift+drag.\nThat path is the client's own selection, which is what a plain drag drives now; the\nplain drag is due the same pass before it is restated as measured under `mouse off`."},"982":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-44","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Out of a pane: drag, and it is already on the clipboard.** tmux asks the outer\nterminal for mouse reporting only while `mouse` is on: measured on tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient, and an attach with it off writes none of them. With the seed's\n`mouse off` tmux never sees a button, so a plain drag is the client's own\nhighlight and the seed's `terminal.integrated.copyOnSelection: true` puts that\nhighlight on the machine clipboard with no chord (the harness recorded\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText` firing on a client selection, before this switch)."},"983":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-45","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Right-click and middle-click paste that clipboard.** The seed sets\n`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` - copy while there is a\nselection, paste when there is none - and pins\n`terminal.integrated.middleClickBehavior: \"paste\"`, because the config registry in\nthe shipped 4.145.0 gives that setting the default `\"default\"`, which pastes only\non the Linux build. tmux has no middle-click paste of its own to answer first:\n`MouseDown2Pane` is unbound in the root table."},"984":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-46","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**tmux keeps no buffer, so there is nothing to paste out of one.** `Prefix-[`\nstill enters vi copy-mode and it is a viewer: the cursor keys, `PPage`/`NPage`,\n`Ctrl-u`/`Ctrl-d` and `k`/`j` walk history, `q` leaves, and `Escape` clears a\nselection. Every copy that used to end a selection is unbound - Enter, `C-j`, `D`,\n`A`, the word and line copies a double- or triple-click fires, the copy a drag\nrelease ran, and the `y` the earlier seed bound itself - along with the prefix\npaste `]` and the buffer picker `=`. Selecting inside copy-mode with `Space`, `V`\nor the cursor keys stays bound and is inert, with no copy command left for it to\nend on. Proof: sourcing the payload on a scratch server leaves `list-keys` with no\ncopy command in `copy-mode-vi`, no `paste-buffer` or `choose-buffer` in `prefix`,\nand no `MouseDown2Pane` in `root`. The pointer's other jobs keep their prefix\nkeys: `Prefix-o` or `Prefix-q` to select a pane, and `Prefix-Alt-arrow` to resize\n(plain `Prefix-arrow` selects a pane in the shipped 3.4's own default table)."},"985":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-47","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**`Prefix m` is the wheel, not a copy route.** One press hands the pointer to\ntmux so the wheel walks pane history, the next hands it back. It is the escape\nhatch for a long scroll, because tmux paints in the alternate screen - it sends\n`ESC[?1049h` either way - so the client holds no scrollback of its own and a wheel\nover a pane has nothing to walk. Output that has scrolled away is copied by\nbringing it on screen first: `Prefix-[`, scroll it into view, select it with a\nclient drag, `q`. A drag while `Prefix m` has the pointer on selects nothing,\nsince the copy bindings stay unbound either way."},"986":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-48","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**An app inside the pane drives the machine clipboard over OSC 52; the browser\nbuild drops it.** `set -g set-clipboard on` with `set -g allow-passthrough on`\nstay in the seed for that one route: vim's `+` register, and anything else that\nemits the escape, reach the machine clipboard the way a native terminal hands them\nto it. Over ssh from Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm or iTerm2 that copy lands.\ncode-server's browser build drops the escape before any clipboard call: a\nhand-written `printf '\\033]52;c;<base64>\\a'` was just as silent with\n`set-clipboard off` as with it on, which puts the drop in the browser and not in\ntmux. Upstream\nmicrosoft/vscode#318254 is\nopen with two unmerged fixes, and the shipped 4.145.0 registers no OSC 52 setting\nat all, so there is nothing here to turn on. Nothing on the tmux side can spoil\nthat clipboard either: tmux has no copy left to send, so an accidental selection\nin the pane cannot overwrite what the client just put there."},"987":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-49","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Into the pane: `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click, on a secure context.** Both\narrive bracketed (`ESC[200~ ... ESC[201~`) in a bash terminal and in a tmux\npane alike, and a three-line paste arrives whole without a warning dialog. Both\nchords ask the browser's clipboard API, which the browser only exposes to a\nsecure context, so the URL the tab was opened on decides it. On the TLS public\nname they work; on `http://<LAN-address>` `navigator.clipboard` is absent,\nright-click stops with VS Code's own toast (\"This browser doesn't support the\nclipboard.readText API needed to trigger a paste, try Ctrl+V instead.\"), and\nthe browser's native paste event is what still delivers. A Clipboard site\npermission that was once denied breaks both API chords the same way, with\n`NotAllowedError: Read permission denied`. Neither is a tmux route: the prefix\npaste `]` and the buffer picker `=` are unbound, so the client's own clipboard is\nwhat arrives in the pane."},"988":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-50","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**`Ctrl+V` on a secure origin is not a paste.** VS Code binds terminal paste to\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` on Linux and `Cmd+V` on macOS, so `Ctrl+V` reaches the pane as\nthe `\\x16` byte (readline's quoted-insert, vim's visual-block). The seed\nre-binds nothing there on purpose: a TUI in the pane owns that chord."},"989":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-51","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Two scroll-back stores, two different budgets.** `terminal.integrated.scrollback`\nin the settings seed is the xterm.js buffer, which is what a plain `bash`\nprofile scrolls through; tmux's `history-limit` is what `Prefix-[` walks\ninside a pane. They read 2000 and 100000 lines here, and the two numbers\ndiffer on purpose because the two stores are billed differently. VS Code\npreallocates the xterm.js buffer from the setting's value - its own\ndescription warns that \"as the value increases, so will the amount of\nmemory\", against a VS Code default of 1000\n(terminalConfiguration.ts) -\nand xterm.js keeps 3 `Uint32` words per column per line\n(BufferLine.ts),\nso that cost is paid per terminal in the tab, and again per view of a pane. tmux\nholds its history once per pane, in the server, and every client of that pane\nshares the one buffer. Hence the split: the deep store is tmux's and the\nbrowser keeps a short viewport. Inside a tmux pane the client's wheel has nothing\nto walk - tmux paints in the alternate screen, so that buffer holds no pane\nhistory - and the 100000 lines live behind `Prefix-[`, paid for in the server and\nnot in the tab. Measured on tmux 3.4 with one pane pushed to its limit, 100000\nlines of 120-column output holds the tmux server at 381 MB RSS; the same 100000\nlines of plain 80-column output costs 60 MB. tmux reads `history-limit` when a\npane is created, so an existing pane keeps the number it was born with."},"990":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-52","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"`grok doctor` checks terminal, clipboard and microphone support without starting a\nsession, and `/doctor` inside a session adds the checks that need a live one. Each\nitem in the **Findings** list carries its own fix, and `--json` prints the same\nreport for a script. What the report should read here:"},"991":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-53","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**tmux options.** `templates/common/tmux.conf` seeds the four options Doctor's\nown fixes write, `set -g set-clipboard on`, `set -g allow-passthrough on`,\n`set -g extended-keys on` and the `*:RGB` `terminal-features` entry, plus the\n`xterm*:extkeys` entry Doctor leaves to the operator. Those last two decide\nwhat survives the multiplexer: tmux rewrites 24-bit color down to the palette\nan attached client advertises, which is what makes a theme look washed out in\nthe browser, and extkeys is what makes Shift+Enter and Ctrl+. arrive distinct\nfrom Enter. A client picks up `terminal-features` when it attaches, so after a\nreseed run `tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf` and then detach and reattach\n(a `refresh-client` keeps the client on its old features). Proof:\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'` lists `RGB` and `extkeys`.\n`tests/bats/tmux-fidelity.bats` boots a scratch server on the seed and reads\nall of it back."},"992":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-54","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`terminal.limited-color` during an agent turn.** A tool command launched by a\nrunning Grok session inherits `NO_COLOR=1`, `FORCE_COLOR=0`, `CLICOLOR=0` and\n`CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never` from that session, so a `grok doctor` run from inside\nan agent turn reads color as off. The `grok` process itself and the tmux server\ncarry none of those variables, so run it from a plain terminal for the real\ncapability: the line reads `color truecolor` and `themes all`."},"993":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-55","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`voice.no-input-device`.** Expected in a workspace. The container has no\ncapture device at all (`/dev/snd` and `/proc/asound` are absent) and no\nrecorder on PATH. The probe only looks for a recorder binary, so installing\n`alsa-utils` moves the line to `available` while capture still has nowhere to\nread from, and `features.voice_mode = false` leaves the finding in place.\nNeither would be a fix, so the stack ships neither and the finding stands as an\naccurate report of a headless container. Dictation belongs on a laptop-side\n`grok`. To give a workspace dictation instead: run PulseAudio on the workstation\nwith its TCP module enabled, export `PULSE_SERVER=tcp=<workstation>:4713` into\nthe workspace, and install `pulseaudio-utils` there for `parec`."},"994":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-56","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`terminal.ssh-wrap`** is a recommendation about the computer you type `ssh`\non, since the wrapper has to sit in front of that client: run\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there, or `grok wrap ssh <host>` per connection. It\nforwards copies to the local clipboard and restores terminal modes when the\nlink drops. `/settings` -> **Show contextual hints** -> **SSH wrap** silences\nthe tip."},"995":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-57","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`unavailable` tmux and runtime probes.** `tmux.version`,\n`tmux.client-features` and the `runtime.*` lines come back `unavailable`\nwhenever Doctor has no interactive client of its own, which covers a piped run\nand a run from an agent tool call. Read them by hand (`tmux -V`,\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`) or from `/doctor` in a live\nsession."},"996":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-58","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"Baked in at `/usr/local/bin/grok`, with an `agent` link beside it, **outside** the\n`/home/coder` home volume, so a rebuilt volume cannot lose it."},"997":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-59","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"Pinned as `GROK_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` (1.0.77 at the time of\nwriting, mirrored as the Dockerfile `ARG` default). The image build unpacks that\npinned `.zst` and runs `grok --version` in two PATH contexts as a build-time smoke\ncheck."},"998":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-60","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"Each template's startup script probes `command -v grok` on every start and\nre-downloads from `https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` - bounded, with retries - only\nwhile `grok` is missing from PATH. That installer follows the `stable` pointer, not\nthis pin. It deliberately does **not** fetch on every boot: the bake-in under\n`/usr/local/bin` is the upgrade path, so shipping a new CLI means bumping the pin,\nrebuilding the image (`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`) and re-pushing the templates."},"999":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-61","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"A self-installed CLI under `~/.grok/bin` stays first on the login-shell PATH, so it\nwins over the baked copy."},"1000":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-62","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"**No published source names an upstream `/learn`, and the bundled skill set never\nlands here**: the `xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of\ndocs.x.ai, every first-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog carry zero occurrences of it. The bundled\nplatform skills are a separate set, enumerated by\n`FORMER_PLATFORM_SKILL_HASHES` in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/builtin.rs`\n(help, imagine, create-skill, check-work, check, xlsx, pptx, docx, code-review,\nbest-of-n, create-workflow), and neither `learn` nor `skillify` is among them. That\nset is downloaded at runtime through the CLI's proxy endpoints config\n(`cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`, gated by `GROK_SKILLS_BASE_URL`) and needs an x.ai\nsign-in: the upstream `product_skills*` fetch in\n`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/slash_commands.rs` is gated on\n`xai_grok_login::GrokAuth`. This stack has no x.ai credential:\nLiteLLM is the sole model transport, with `env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"` and\n`base_url` `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, so `~/.grok/bundled/` is never\ncreated and there is no bundled copy for `grok inspect` to label. The one surface\nnobody can read anonymously is the authenticated skills feed (`x.ai/skills/*` answers\n403), so the honest claim is that no published source names `/learn`, not that it\ncannot exist behind that feed. Nothing writes `~/.grok/skills/` either, but\nthat is a stack choice, not a CLI limit: the upstream user guide\n(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/docs/user-guide/08-skills.md`) documents both tiers -\n`~/.grok/skills/` is the User scope and `<repo_root>/.grok/skills/` the Repo one, and\n`grok inspect` labels each definition by its actual source (`user`, `project`,\n`bundled`) - and startup discovery skips project skills and commands in untrusted\nfolders, so a project-tier folder has to be trusted first or its skill is withheld.\nWhat separates the two consumers is that the **Coder Agents** chat agent reads\n`~/.coder/skills/` only, so reaching both means shipping both homes."},"1001":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-63","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"**The stack ships its own three skill commands as `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify` and\n`/ez-create-skill`**, so they are stack payloads like the delegation skill, not something\nto install, and the `ez-` prefix keeps them clear of the upstream names they sit\nbeside: `/create-skill` is one of the CLI's bundled platform skills and\n`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory` are its built-in memory commands, while no\npublished source names a `/learn` or a `/skillify` at all. A user-scope skill overrides\na same-named bundled one, so a seed named `create-skill` would have hidden the bundled\ncopy.\n`/ez-learn` writes memory files - it reproduces the upstream `/flush`, `/dream`,\n`/remember` and `/memory`; the other two write skill files.\nThe three payloads - `templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md` - are seeded at workspace start by\n`coder_script.stack_skills`: each is written into `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and\ntwin-copied to `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, each only while that file is absent.\nTo take a\ncopy over, edit it and delete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a\nmarker-free file is user-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass\n`--force`. A deletion is undone - the boot writes the file again whenever it is\nabsent, and `refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores\nthe stack's copy. To hide the slash command, set `disabled = [\"ez-learn\"]` under\n`[skills]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`. A seeded `SKILL.md` shows up in\n`grok inspect --json` as `userInvocable: true`, and a live session advertises it in\nboth `slash_commands` and `skills`, so it works on whichever CLI the image baked at\n`/usr/local/bin/grok` and this needs no image rebuild; the **Grok Build** terminal\nprofile gets it because that profile execs the same binary."},"1002":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-64","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-cli-in-the-golden-image","content":"**A leftover `/learn` seed retires itself.** This skill was seeded under the name\n`learn` until it moved, and a copy left standing keeps offering that old name beside\n`/ez-learn` for the life of the volume; there is no bundled `learn` for it to hide,\nbecause no published source names one. Both startup scripts delete\n`~/.coder/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and `~/.agents/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and prune the\ndirectory each leaves, but only while the file still carries the\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker; a marker-free `learn/SKILL.md` is user-owned\nand stays. `refresh-configs`, `storage-clean` and `seed-dev-env` retire the same two\npaths on reseed. That removal only finishes the rename: there is no upstream `/learn`\nwaiting on the name, because no published source names one."},"1003":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-65","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"Rendered per workspace from `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` (and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy of the same payload), and re-rendered idempotently\nwhile the managed block is intact."},"1004":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-66","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"At top level the render sets `disable_web_search = true`, which parks Grok's native\n`web_search` tool: no transport this box ships serves that tool type, so the\nself-hosted `searxng` MCP server is the search lane. The search lane section below\ncarries the measurement and how to flip the key back."},"1005":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-67","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`[models] default` points at the `grok_default_model` alias - `agent` unless the\ntemplate variable or the create form's **Grok default model** dropdown names\nanother. `[models] default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`. The Spark lane answers\n`low` / `medium` / `xhigh`; `high` is never advertised."},"1006":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-68","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"The seven aliases (`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast`, `chat`) are defined with `base_url` set to `litellm_url` and\n`env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"`. The key is injected as an environment variable, so\nit never appears in a file. `agent` carries three\n`[[model.\"agent\".reasoning_efforts]]` tables (`xhigh` is the default). The\n`agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` aliases set\n`supports_reasoning_effort = false` because LiteLLM bakes the level for callers\nthat have no reasoning-effort field."},"1007":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-69","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`[models]` also carries `extra_headers` with\n`x-litellm-session-id: ws-<workspace-id>` - the key LiteLLM uses to pin a\nworkspace's traffic to one healthy Spark box. Under a shared profile the\nlast-booted workspace's key wins for the whole fleet."},"1008":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-70","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`github_mcp` (default `true`, so the block renders unless you park it with\n`GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push) adds the `github` HTTP MCP server\n(`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`), whose `Authorization` header references\n`${GH_TOKEN}` **by name**, so no token value is ever part of a template variable.\nThe agent environment fills it from the Coder External Auth provider (id `github` by default); a\nworkspace whose owner connected no GitHub account renders the block anyway and pays\none failed handshake per session."},"1009":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-71","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`context7_mcp` (default `true`, parked the same way with `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`) adds\nthe cached `context7` stdio MCP server. Grok launches\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/mcp-cache-proxy` with\n`--config /home/coder/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json`, and that proxy fronts the\nofficial `/opt/npm-global/bin/context7-mcp`; both are pre-installed in the golden\nimage, so nothing is fetched with `npx` at launch. The proxy config names\n`$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` and nothing else: the key reaches the workspace environment from\nthe sensitive template variable `context7_api_key`, filled by the push from\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in the host `.env` - not from Coder External Auth, which is what\nfills `GH_TOKEN` above."},"1010":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-72","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`grok_config_extra` appends whatever the create form's **Grok config extras** field\nholds, verbatim under its own comment header."},"1011":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-73","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`[permission]` holds the guardrails the stack owns; the approval mode itself stays\na per-user call (`[ui] permission_mode`). `Bash(rm -rf *)` blocks the destructive\nform, and the dotenv family is split by what a file actually holds: the live\n`.env` and the populated suffixed shapes (`.env.local`, `.env.*.local`,\n`.env.production` / `.env.prod`, `.env.staging` / `.env.stage`,\n`.env.development` / `.env.dev`, `.env.test` / `.env.testing`, `.env.ci`,\n`.env.override`) are denied for `Read` and `Edit`, while the secret-free\ntemplates a repo ships (`.env.example`, `.env.sample`, `.env.template`,\n`.env.dist`, `.env.defaults`, plus the `.env.*.example` / `.env.*.sample`\nspellings) are named in `allow`, so an agent maintains the env key list without a\nprompt in any mode. Neither list is the old blanket `**/.env.*`: deny outranks\nallow, so the blanket form blocked the templates along with the live files and\nleft the key list -- the file that keeps a real `.env` honest -- to drift.\n`.gitignore` carries the matching half on the git side: the whole family is\nignored except the names `allow` lists."},"1012":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-74","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`Bash(rm -rf *)` is a prefix glob, so it catches a benign per-path scratch cleanup\nalong with the destruction it is there to prevent. Agents run into that constantly:\nacross 271 workspace sessions, 86 recorded at least one denied tool call and 56\nattempted an `rm -rf`, and 55 sessions did both. The shapes that are never denied\nare per-path deletions - `find <dir> -type f -delete`, or `rm -f` naming each path -\nso clean scratch that way. An `allow` entry cannot rescue `rm -rf`, because deny\noutranks allow. Narrowing the pattern is an operator call and a deliberate one: it\nis the only thing between an agent and a recursive delete on a path it got wrong."},"1013":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-75","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"Stack ownership of the file rests on either marker spelling: the line-1\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key.\nBoth twins emit both, because the comment alone does not last - `grok mcp add`\npersists its own settings by rewriting the file and deletes every comment line, so a\ncomment-only marker handed the config to the user with no human in the loop, and the\ndefault lanes silently stopped arriving. A hand-edited config is left alone only\nwhile it carries one of them, so takeover means deleting **both** lines; delete the\nfile and restart to hand it back. When the guard does re-render, the CLI-owned tables\n`[cli]`, `[ui]` and `[marketplace]` are carried over rather than wiped, because the\nstack owns no approval mode - `[ui] permission_mode` / yolo stays a per-user call."},"1014":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-76","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-state-one-shared-profile-per-user","content":"Every workspace gets its own `/home/coder` volume, and `~/.grok` is a **second,\nnested** mount inside it: a Docker volume named `grok-profile-<owner-id>`,\nreferenced by name rather than declared as a Terraform resource, which every\nworkspace of that owner mounts read-write at the same path **while that workspace is\nin `shared` mode**."},"1015":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-77","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"`shared` (the default here, so a workspace gets the profile unless you turn the\nmode off): configure the CLI once and the rest of that account's workspaces\ninherit it."},"1016":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-78","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"`private`: that workspace's `~/.grok` - `config.toml` included - lives on its own\nhome volume, differs from its siblings, and dies with the workspace (or survives a\nrebuild on the same volume)."},"1017":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-79","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"Set in two places: the template variable `grok_profile_mode`\n(`GROK_PROFILE_MODE=shared|private` in `.env`, forwarded by `push-template.sh` as\n`--var grok_profile_mode`), and the create form's per-workspace **Grok profile**\nfield, which defaults to the variable. One workspace can therefore sit outside the\nshared profile while its siblings stay inside it."},"1018":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-80","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"Changing the mode needs a **rebuild** - the nested mount either exists or it does\nnot. A workspace that goes from `shared` to `private` starts on an empty profile:\nnothing is copied, and the shared volume is left alone."},"1019":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-81","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"In `shared` mode: `config.toml`, `skills/`, `memory-v2/`, `sessions/`, `notes/` (the\ncanonical copy of each repository's private scratch `notes.md`), and a CLI you\ninstalled yourself under `~/.grok/bin`. The CLI's own skill directories, `skills/` and\n`bundled/`, are never created here without an x.ai sign-in; the stack's skill seeds\nlive under `~/.coder/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`, outside this volume."},"1020":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-82","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/notes.md` is keyed per git remote, so every clone a\nworkspace holds — the primary and each additional repository — has its own file. What you\nedit is the regular file at the clone root: that is what the editor, the terminal and a\nnested dev container all see, since the inner container mounts the clone bind and has\nits own `~/.grok`. The copy runs both ways, and not only at boot. Startup copies the live\nfile here and restores it when a recreate leaves the clone with nothing or with only the\nseeded stub line, and then leaves **one background watcher per container** that repeats\nthe back-copy about every 30 seconds (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`, in seconds) over every clone\nunder `$HOME/workspace`; the devcontainer template watches its `/srv/coder-devcontainers`\nbind root instead, since those clones sit there rather than under the workspace home. The\nwatcher is single-instance behind a non-blocking `flock` on `$HOME/.notes-sync.lock`, in\nthe container's own home rather than the shared volume, because each of the owner's\nworkspaces needs its own."},"1021":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-83","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"That is what makes \"survives workspace recreate\" true rather than a hope: an edit is on the\nvolume within roughly one interval, so a recreate no longer waits on someone restarting the\nworkspace to pick up what was typed since the last boot. What is not promised is the tail"},"1022":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-84","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"a file written in the last two seconds is left for the next tick, because copying it\nwhole could archive a half-saved file. Going the other way, two guards stop a recreate from\nerasing notes: a live file that is empty or still holds only the seeded stub never replaces\na canonical copy carrying real notes, and a real canonical copy restores over such a stub\nin a clone. Before any substantive canonical copy is replaced it is archived as\n`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/backups/notes-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS.md`, keeping the newest\n`NOTES_BACKUP_KEEP` (10 by default), and that directory is where an operator recovers notes\nafter an accident. `grok_profile_mode=private` mounts no shared volume, so those notes die\nwith that workspace; `shared` is the default. Like `config.toml`, the last workspace\nstarted for a given user and repository wins."},"1023":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-85","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"Two things about that start are worth knowing before you go looking for a vanished\nsetting. Boot **writes `git config --global core.excludesFile`** — but only when you\nhave not set it yourself. Left unset it points at `~/.grok/notes/exclude`, which lists\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, so from then on *every* git repository in that\nworkspace ignores both names at any depth; if you already set the option, those two\nlines are appended to the file it names and nothing else about it is touched. Boot also\ndrops the same two lines into each clone's own `.git/info/exclude`, which is the layer\nthat works even with no global config at all. And when a repository tracks a `notes.md`\nof its own, the scratch file is written as `.scratch-notes.md` beside it instead — your\nclone is never asked to delete somebody else's committed file."},"1024":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-86","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"Keyed by **owner id**, never host-wide and never cross-user: another user's\nworkspace mounts a differently named volume, and a stop or start of a *different\nuser's* workspace never sees the volume at all."},"1025":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-87","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"The memory and index files are SQLite, opened live by every workspace holding the\nmount, so two workspaces writing memory at once can race. *Roughly in sync* is the\nbar, not transactional sharing."},"1026":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-88","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"Two workspaces of one owner that both hold a clone of the *same* repository each run a\nnotes watcher over the same canonical path, so there too the last write wins, tick by\ntick. The stub and empty guards and the `backups/` archive are what bound the damage a\nlosing tick can do; they do not merge two diverging files."},"1027":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-89","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"One shared profile is one `config.toml` for every workspace that owner owns, and\nthe last boot to re-render it wins - for the default model, the affinity key and\nthe config extras alike."},"1028":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-90","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"`searxng` (SearXNG metasearch, host port 8181) is the search backend the stack runs\nfor itself: keyless engines only, no key, no account, no request logging. A\nworkspace reaches it through the host gateway, so the workspace networks stay\nuntouched."},"1029":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-91","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"It is **not** a model transport, and the native tool is parked: both twins render\n`disable_web_search = true` at top level, so a session starts with no `web_search`\ntool at all. Measured 2026-10-25: no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type. The `agent` group is LiteLLM in front of the Spark boxes\n(`openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next`), and it refuses the request before anything is\nfetched - `tools of type 'web_search' are not supported: this server runs function\ntools only`, HTTP 400. `supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it,\nbecause the client-side fallback still hands the model alias a `web_search`-typed\ntool, which is the exact shape that endpoint rejects. The fallback lane fails too:\nit is the `coder` alias, Ollama `qwen2.5-coder:32b`, and that model is not pulled on\nthe host. So `web_search = \"agent\"` and `supports_backend_search = false` stay as\nthey are - the role stays filled so re-enabling is a one-key flip - but while the\nkey reads `true` nothing dials it."},"1030":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-92","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"The `searxng` MCP stdio server is what answers instead, and it is the search lane\nrather than a fallback: both templates render that block gated on `search_mcp`,\nwhich defaults to true. It dials `$SEARXNG_URL` (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:8181`), the same key `main.tf` puts in the agent\nenvironment. It carries nine tools - `search`, `image_search`, `news_search`,\n`video_search`, `music_search`, `paper_search`, `fetch_content`, `autocomplete`,\n`list_engines` - and an agent's two workhorses are the first and the seventh:\n`search` for anything that is not a library question, `fetch_content` for a URL\nalready in hand. Measure the self-hosted lane with:"},"1031":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-93","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"An empty result page and \"no answer\" are different answers, which is why the probe\nreports which one it got. It also names every engine the instance has parked and\nthe reason its backend gave (`parked by its backend: google: access denied`),\nread out of the reply body whether or not that reply found results - an instance\nrunning on half its engines is visible there before it starts answering empty. A\npark is temporary: SearXNG holds an engine out for `search.suspended_times`\nseconds after it refuses, so re-run before writing one off. `--json` adds\n`engines_parked`. It checks the service hop; `grok mcp doctor searxng`\ninside the workspace checks the MCP hop. The binary it launches\n(`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`) is baked into the golden image, so a\nprobe that answers with results plus a failed handshake is a server, URL or port\nproblem, not a missing package."},"1032":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-94","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"To put native search back on a transport that really serves the tool: flip the key\nto `false` in the seed - both `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy - and re-push, which applies it fleet-wide. For one\nworkspace without a push, the create form's **Grok config extras** field appends\nfree-form TOML verbatim to that workspace's file. Either way start a new session,\nand expect the same 400 back if the endpoint still answers function tools only."},"1033":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-95","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"**MCP servers: the default render declares three - `github`, `context7` and the\ngated `searxng` server, which is the search lane itself.** Each is a boolean gate in\nthe template, so one env flag parks exactly one block and a template re-push applies\nit fleet-wide: `GITHUB_MCP=false`, `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`, `SEARCH_MCP=false`.\nThe `searxng` block names the pre-installed `searxng-mcp-server` binary (pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image), is gated on\n`search_mcp` (default true) and dials the service above through `SEARXNG_URL`.\nThat URL is one of six keys the render sets in `[mcp_servers.searxng.env]`; the\nothers pin the bridge's own tuning rather than taking its compiled-in defaults:\n`SEARXNG_TIMEOUT_MS` (20s, above the instance's per-engine budget and equal to the\nprobe's `--timeout`), `SEARXNG_CACHE_TTL_MS` (15 minutes, which switches on the\nbounded 128-entry response cache that ships disabled, so a repeated query stops\nre-dialling backends that already refused this instance), `SEARXNG_MAX_RESULTS`\n(a ceiling that keeps one search inside the MCP output cap - on the pinned 1.0.77\nCLI that cap rides `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`, because `grok inspect` still answers\n`[mcp] — unrecognized config key` and never applies it),\n`SEARXNG_DEFAULT_SAFESEARCH` (mirrors `search.safe_search` in\n`searxng/settings.yml`) and `SEARXNG_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE` (`all`, so an image bump\ncannot quietly start geo-filtering every agent search).\nThe five offline stdio servers (`filesystem`, `memory`, `sequential-thinking`,\n`git` and `time`) stay pre-installed in the image (`npm -g` for the first\nthree, `uv tool` for the Python two), but the rendered `config.toml` names\nnone of them: the session traces collected on this machine called none of\ntheir tools, and a declared-but-unused server still costs every session its\nhandshake time and tool schemas. Declare one by hand and check a stubborn\nserver with `grok mcp doctor <name>` inside a workspace."},"1034":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-96","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"The `github` server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) renders by default even\nthough it needs a token and outbound network: with GitHub tools on, the agent can\ndrive the repository it is working in, and the cheaper failure is the block that\nrenders with no credential and pays one failed handshake per session. `github_mcp`\n(default true) is the gate; `$GH_TOKEN` comes from Coder External Auth, never from a\nPAT."},"1035":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-97","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"The `context7` server (library and API docs) also renders by default, and is the\nonly cached one. `context7_mcp` (default true) is its gate, and the block it\nrenders runs `mcp-cache-proxy` in front of the official `context7-mcp` - two packages\npinned in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image, so parking\nor restoring it is a flag plus a template push, while changing the packages\nthemselves takes an image rebuild. The proxy keeps its SQLite cache under\n`~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/`, which rides the owner's shared\nprofile volume in `shared` mode, and the cache TTL is 12 h: the first lookup of a\nlibrary reaches the Context7 API and needs network, while an identical lookup inside\nthe TTL is answered locally. `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is optional and the lane does not\ndepend on it: an empty value sends no `Authorization` header at all, so the server\nhandshakes and answers at the public rate limit, with private libraries the only\nthing out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env. What *parks* the\nlane on purpose is `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var context7_mcp=false` on a template push; a `[mcp_servers.context7]` block\nmissing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a park. The lookup\norder is fixed - `resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on the id it returns"},"1036":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-98","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"and `search_tool` lists the live tool names rather than a hardcoded list. Prove it\n  with `grok mcp doctor context7` inside a workspace."},"1037":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-99","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"Prove all three at once with `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check`: it reads the\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, reports whether each lane is declared, whether its\n`command=` binary is present, whether the lane's credential and cache config are\nthere, and what each server's handshake says. A binary the box cannot find is image\ndrift (the running `evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake), and the check prints the\npinned `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command that restores it."},"1038":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-100","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"A lane an agent needs fails in exactly three shapes, and that check tells them\napart, so run it before editing anything:"},"1039":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-101","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"**The block is missing from the rendered config.** That is a failure, not a\nwarning: the default render declares the lane, so something took it away - a push\npassed its gate `false`, the workspace runs an older template, or the file lost\nits stack marker and nothing re-renders it. Remedy: clear the gate and re-push,\nthen re-materialize the file with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>` from\nthe stack host, or `bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` for a\nhost home."},"1040":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-102","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"**The binary is missing from the golden image.** A warning: the running\n`evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake. Remedy: rebuild the image and recreate the\nworkspace, or run the `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command the check prints\nto install the pinned package into the running box (pins in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."},"1041":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-103","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"**The credential is missing.** Also a warning, and per lane it means something\ndifferent. `github` with `$GH_TOKEN` empty is a real gap: connect GitHub External\nAuth (the **GitHub reauth** app) and start a new session. An empty\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is not a gap - see the key's paragraph above, the lane answers\nkeyless at the public rate limit."},"1042":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-104","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"What the agent is told about all this lives in `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, inlined from\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`. Two sections cover the lanes. **Research\nlanes** makes a lookup part of the normal loop rather than a last resort, with the\nrouting fixed by question type: a library, framework, CLI or setup question goes to\n`context7` (resolve the library id first, then query docs on that id); anything else\non the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool; a URL already in hand goes to\n`searxng`'s `fetch_content`, because Grok's native `web_fetch` is unavailable here\ntoo - `features.web_fetch` is on, but on a non-xAI endpoint that tool only runs\nthrough `toolset.web_fetch.proxy_endpoint`, and this stack sets none. It also states\nthat native `web_search` is parked so nobody spends a turn rediscovering the 400.\n**Tool output stays out of the parent context** makes that\ntool use delegated work: the child returns a digest - numbered findings, each one\nclaim, one source URL and at most two lines of support, no raw result dumps,\nconfidence stated - and keeps the raw pages to itself."},"1043":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-105","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"Cline and Roo/Kilo are pre-wired to the LiteLLM proxy (endpoint from `litellm_url`,\ndefault `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`; model alias `agent`, with\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`/`coder`/`coder-fast`/`chat` listed in\n`__evoT1StackModels`). The seed is\n`settings.json.tftpl`, copied onto the per-owner volume\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` (mounted at `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`) only\nwhile that file is absent, and symlinked from\n`~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json`. Startup chowns that volume to\nthe home owner before the first write (empty named volumes are `root:root`\n755). Edits stick, and every workspace\nthat owner has shares the file; delete it to copy the template in again. From the\nhost it is the volume's `settings.json`:"},"1044":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-106","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"A built dev container mounts neither the volume nor the symlink, so it is the one\nplace the seed is **merged** instead of linked: the inlined\n`coder_script.code_server` fork bakes a base64 copy of it and writes it over the\ndev container's private `User/settings.json` (or merges over a copy that survived a\nrebuild, when `jq` is on PATH; without `jq` the existing file is kept). Edits\ninside a built dev container therefore survive neither a rebuild nor a look at the\nshared file. Everywhere else no settings map is passed at all, because a merge\nwould replace the symlink."},"1045":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-107","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"Installing Cline, Roo, the Red Hat YAML extension and Material Icon Theme is a\nmanual step in the Extensions panel (Open VSX by default). If an extension build\nnames its settings differently, set the OpenAI-compatible endpoint and key in its\nUI."},"1046":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-108","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#coder-agents-control-plane","content":"Coder v2.36.6 ships *Coder Agents*: a coding agent that lives in the Coder **server** -\na chat UI and an API beside your workspaces, not inside one. A chat runs its tools\n(`read_file`, `edit_files`, `execute`, `spawn_agent`) against a workspace over that\nworkspace's agent connection, and consults two pieces of per-workspace context first:\nan **instructions** file and any **skills** it discovers. The agent itself is upstream\nCoder, not a home-grown agent."},"1047":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-109","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"The chat agent is compiled into coderd unconditionally."},"1048":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-110","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"`CODER_EXPERIMENTS` in `.env` (empty by default since 2026-10-08) has never gated\nanything about it: the old `agents` default was not a recognized experiment name,\nand the server logged `ignoring unknown experiment` and ignored it on every boot."},"1049":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-111","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"`workspace_external_agent`, the entitlement the docs name, gates *external* agents\n(Claude Code, Codex, ...), not this one, and nothing here needs it."},"1050":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-112","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"If you know a real experiment you want (`chat-advisor`, `chat-virtual-desktop`,\n...), list it in `.env` and restart the coder service. Empty means no experiments\non."},"1051":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-113","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Scripted, idempotent, re-runnable, and already started from `prod-stack` /\n`dev-stack` `up` (`--apply --wait`, default 1800 s). Registering the first account\nis the only operator step: the seeder continues once an owner exists, and the Agents\npage is usable with no extra Coder UI setup. Print the plan:"},"1052":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-114","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Apply it (or re-run after a skip):"},"1053":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-115","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"`--help` prints the same two commands with every flag and every `CODER_AGENTS_*`\noverride, which is the list to reach for from a shell."},"1054":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-116","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"The deployment default follows the health gate. `agent` is registered as the default,\nand when LiteLLM reports no healthy deployment behind the Spark alias the Arc `chat`\nconfig takes the default in its place. That matters because a chat opened without an\nexplicit model pick has only the default to resolve to, so a stack that comes up while\nthe Spark lane is dark still gets an Agents chat that answers. An existing config is\nleft as it stands, since this API surface has no PATCH: the script names the Admin\nscreen to correct it when a stored row drifted."},"1055":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-117","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"The closing summary counts how many of the four auxiliary lanes carry a pin, and a lane\nleft without one prints a `warning:` that names the lane."},"1056":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-118","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"`--wait` (or `--wait=SECONDS`) polls for that first owner instead of skip-0. The\nwindow is counted from the database's first \"no owner\" answer, so a slow buildinfo\nprobe or a slow first query cannot spend it. `SKIP_CODER_AGENTS=1` skips bootstrap\nand stack-up seeding. `CODER_AGENTS_WAIT_SECS`\nis what stack `up` forwards as `--wait=`."},"1057":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-119","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Stack `up` starts that seeder in the background and keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (`CODER_AGENTS_SEED_LOG` moves it, and the\n`note:` line stack `up` prints names the path it chose). Read that file first when\nthe Agents chat is dead after a boot: the seeder's most common outcome is a `skipped:`\nline that exits 0 - no coder container yet, no db container, nobody registered - and\nan unwritable log path degrades to the old discard with a `warning:` instead of\nfailing the boot."},"1058":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-120","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"A chat turn needs an enabled chat model config - the chat's own pick, else the\ndeployment default, else it errors before the first tool call - and that wiring is\nwhat the script writes. It mints a short-lived admin API token from the stack's own\nPostgres (`token_name 'stack-coder-agents'`, deleted by the trap, never printed) and\ndrives the server's experimental chat API:"},"1059":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-121","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"an `openai-compat` AI provider named **`litellm`** (not `litellm-auto`), base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1` - LiteLLM over the compose network, *server-side*,\nauthenticated with `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`. The agent dials the in-process AI Gateway\n(aibridge), which forwards to the provider's `base_url` + `/chat/completions`, so\n`/v1` has to be part of the base URL and the key has to match the live proxy: with\n`.env` leaving the key empty, compose still boots the proxy on its\n`change-me-litellm` interpolation default, and that default is what gets sent.\nCreated when absent, drift-patched when an existing provider carries a drifted\n`base_url` or key."},"1060":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-122","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"chat model configs `agent` (context limit 262144, set as the default; LiteLLM bakes\n`reasoning_effort` `xhigh`), `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` (same\nlane with a baked thinking level, not default), and `chat` (32768), for models not\nalready registered. `coder` and `coder-fast` stay out on purpose: on this stack's\nIPEX-LLM runtime those weights return tool calls as plain text with no\n`tool_calls`, and an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or edit, so\nregistering them would only add a silently broken model to the pickers.\n`context_limit` is the agent's own compaction budget (a 70 per cent default\ncompaction threshold applies), which is why it is pinned per alias.\nBefore that sweep the script asks the proxy `GET /v1/model/info` and `GET /health`\nand joins them on `model_id`, so it knows which aliases still have a healthy\ndeployment. An alias with none is not registered: `chat` goes unhealthy the moment\n`qwen2.5:7b` is not pulled on the Arc, and a dead entry in the model picker is a\nchat that errors on the first send. `/health` is read without\n`?check_deployments=true` because that form fires a live completion per deployment\nand evicts the Spark prompt caches the health-check notes in `litellm/config.yaml`\nwarn about. The gate fails open: a proxy that will not answer leaves every alias\nregisterable, and `CODER_AGENTS_LIVE_ALIASES=\"agent chat\"` overrides it outright."},"1061":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-123","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"pins for the four auxiliary lanes - `general` and `explore` (the two subagent\ntypes) prefer `agent`, `compaction` and `title_generation` (the helper calls)\nprefer `chat` - chosen only from the aliases the health gate left standing, and\nwritten when a lane carries no pin or is pinned to one of these configs whose alias\nhas since gone unhealthy. A pin aimed at a config the script did not create is the\nadmin's and is left alone. Both helper lanes landing on a dead `chat` is what made\nconversations lose their titles and die at the compaction threshold, which is why a\nstale pin now moves instead of being kept forever."},"1062":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-124","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"a read-only drift check on the stored configs: a config that is disabled, is not\nthe deployment default, or carries a `context_limit` that does not match the alias's\nserving window is reported with a `warning:` and the screen to fix it on. The\nexperimental chat API has no PATCH for model configs, so these are named rather than\nwritten."},"1063":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-125","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"a three-line system-prompt addendum, appended after Coder's default prompt text\n(`include_default_system_prompt: true`), that keeps the delegation and\nsecret-hygiene rules which must hold even when a chat has no workspace attached."},"1064":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-126","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#model-wiring","content":"Coder's optional AI Gateway proxy stays off - LiteLLM remains the model transport,\nwhich is why no AI-Gateway keys exist in `docker-compose.yml`."},"1065":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-127","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"The `compaction` pin above belongs to Coder's chat agent and to nothing else. It is one\nof the four auxiliary lanes the seeder writes into Coder's own model-config store, so it\ndecides which alias serves *that* agent's summariser and title call. It never reaches a\nGrok Build CLI: the CLI reads no Coder table, and its compaction summary is an\n**in-session call on the session model** - the `[models] default` alias, `agent` here -\nwhich is why the summary inherits that lane's output ceiling (measured on the wire at\n`max_tokens: 32768`) instead of a cheap helper lane's. `[models] session_summary` is not\nthat call either; the key serves titles and the session recap."},"1066":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-128","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"So the two compactions fail for unrelated reasons, and the fix for one is not the fix\nfor the other:"},"1067":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-129","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"Coder's chat dies at its context budget when `compaction` and `title_generation` both\nsit on a dead `chat` alias. `bazelisk run //scripts:coder-agents -- --apply` moves the\nstale pin, as above."},"1068":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-130","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"A Grok Build session logging `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s\n(runaway generation)` is the session-model summary hanging before its first token on\nthe lane that also serves the whole agent loop. Its only live bounds are\n`GROK_COMPACTION_WALL_CLOCK_SECS`, rendered into the agent `env` because no TOML table\ntakes it, and the proxy's `request_timeout`; troubleshooting.md\nowns the measurement and the retry split that stops one hang being bought three times."},"1069":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-131","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"The thresholds are separate as well. `context_limit` above is Coder's own compaction\nbudget with its 70 per cent default, while the CLI fires at\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` (70) of its 262144-token window."},"1070":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-132","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-delegation-sketch","content":"Chat on the Agents page -> if the ask needs more tooling than chat has, Coder Agents\nprovisions or uses a workspace through `execute` -> big multi-file tasks are\n*delegated inside* that workspace: a headless Grok Build CLI run, started backgrounded\nand polled:"},"1071":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-133","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-delegation-sketch","content":"Backgrounded and polled because `execute` waits only a short window per tool call\n(about a minute), so a synchronous foreground `grok -p` would die mid-run on any big\ntask. Coder Agents then reviews the delegated diff, runs the project's tests, and\ncommits. Subagents spawned by Coder Agents cannot create workspaces: provisioning\ndecisions stop at the top-level chat."},"1072":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-134","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"Ten files seed into every new workspace, on first boot and only while the file is\nabsent:"},"1073":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-135","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`"},"1074":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-136","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"},"1075":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-137","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`"},"1076":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-138","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"},"1077":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-139","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"},"1078":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-140","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"},"1079":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-141","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"},"1080":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-142","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"},"1081":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-143","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"},"1082":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-144","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"},"1083":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-145","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"1 and 2 are the Coder agent's *default* discovery paths."},"1084":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-146","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"3 and 4 are the same text under the Grok Build CLI's convention, and the CLI is\nwhat `execute`-lane delegation drives. The chat agent dedupes skills by name,\nfirst match wins, home scope before a working-dir path, so one agent never loads a\nskill twice: the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."},"1085":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-147","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"1-4 are written by `startup.sh.tftpl`. 5-10 are the stack's three skill commands and are\nwritten by `coder_script.stack_skills`, whose own `execve` carries the three payloads;\nthey ride their own script body rather than the startup argument because that argument\nhas a single-argument ceiling of its own\n(coder-templates.md). Each `.agents`\ncopy is a `cp` of the managed `.coder` copy, never a second inline body, so the pair\ncannot drift."},"1086":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-148","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"5 and 6 are `/ez-learn`: this stack's copy of the upstream memory commands `/flush`,\n`/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`. It turns the current session into durable facts -\nflush the conversation into a session log under the workspace's `~/.grok/memory/`\ndirectory, dream those logs into an organized `MEMORY.md`, save one note, or browse and\nclear what was stored. Reaching past those logs into the raw transcripts under\n`~/.grok/sessions/` is opt-in depth, done only when the operator asks for it. Its\ndefault mode is report-only, and it writes memory files, never a skill. 7 and 8 are\n`/ez-skillify`, which turns the session that just worked into a skill from its own\ntranscript, and 9 and 10 are `/ez-create-skill`, which scaffolds one from what the\noperator describes. The three payloads are\n`templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, on the same absent-only contract. `/ez-create-skill` stands\nin for the bundled platform skill `create-skill`, which arrives only in the skills\nbundle behind an x.ai sign-in this stack does not have; `/ez-skillify` has no published\nupstream counterpart at all; and the memory family `/ez-learn` copies is upstream\n*built-ins*: `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` are gated behind cross-session memory,\nwhich is off by default, while `/remember` is a pager command with no such gate. Either\nway the seed is what makes the three commands exist. Each ships under its own `ez-`\nname to stay clear of those upstream names, since a user-scope skill overrides a\nsame-named bundled one and a seed named `create-skill` would hide the bundled copy on a\nworkspace that ever does sign in. The boot and the reseed delete a leftover\nmarker-carrying `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from before the rename. See the CLI section\nabove."},"1087":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-149","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"The text of 1-4 is the orchestration protocol (fan-out / fan-in, the edit-conflict gate,\nprobe-one-worker-first, verify-what-a-worker-actually-produced). It travels as\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, staged beside the staged `main.tf` and\nread back with `file()` / `filebase64()`; `templates/common/` keeps one copy for\nboth templates, and the push stages it into each template's pushed copy."},"1088":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-150","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`devcontainer` and `workspace` both seed it, so the seed is *not* fetched from\nGitHub any more: no `web_search`/`web_fetch` call is needed to inspect this repo,\nand the instruction text tells the agent so."},"1089":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-151","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"Both `main.tf` files deliberately set **no** `CODER_AGENT_EXP_*` env keys on\n`coder_agent.main`: those keys *replace* the agent's discovery defaults, and the\ndefaults already cover exactly the seeded paths - the `~/.coder` roots plus a\nworking-dir-relative `.agents/skills`, which resolves inside the agent's working\ndir (`/home/coder` in `workspace`; in `devcontainer` with the toggle on, the\ncloned folder under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, where a dev container built on\nthe sidecar carries its own home and no seeded path is visible from inside it\nanyway)."},"1090":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-152","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"Each seeded file carries the stack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack`\ncomment, or the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` / `__evoT1Stack = true` key in a JSON or TOML\nfile - in `SKILL.md` directly under the `---` frontmatter, since the agent's skill\nparser requires frontmatter on line 1 and silently drops anything else. Delete a file\nto take it over; edit it and the stack leaves it alone. `~/.grok/config.toml` is the\none seed where deleting a file is not the way: it needs both marker lines gone, see\nThe rendered `~/.grok` config. Either way the marker\nnever reaches the model: the agent strips HTML comments from injected instruction\ntext."},"1091":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-153","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"The seeds live per workspace, on that workspace's own home volume. They are *not*\non the shared `~/.grok` profile volume above, and seeding does not care whether\nthat mount exists."},"1092":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-154","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"One line each: `/ez-learn` turns a session into durable memory - flush it into a session\nlog, dream the logs into organized memory, remember one note, or browse and clear the\nstore; `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked, working from its own\ntranscript; `/ez-create-skill` builds a skill from what the operator describes. The last\ntwo write skill files; `/ez-learn` writes memory files."},"1093":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-155","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"Three things about skills matter in practice here:"},"1094":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-156","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"Write a specific `description` and `when-to-use`. That text is what the model matches\non, so a vague one leaves the skill reachable only by its slash name."},"1095":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-157","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"Set `disable-model-invocation: true` when only an explicit `/name` should run it.\nAnything that deploys, pushes or resets belongs behind that key."},"1096":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-158","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"After creating or editing anything, check discovery and provenance with `grok inspect`,\nand `grok inspect --json` for each skill's `source` path and `userInvocable`. `/skills`\nopens the same set as a menu, where a toggle takes a skill out of the slash menu\nwithout deleting the file. Discovery reloads from disk, so an edit lands next turn\nwith no restart."},"1097":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-159","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"This is workflow curation, separate from the memory system, which stores durable facts\n(upstream `/remember`, `/memory`, `/dream`, shipped here as `/ez-learn`), and from\n`AGENTS.md`, which is the always-loaded rules file. A skill loads when its `description`\nmatches, and holds a procedure."},"1098":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-160","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-this-is-not","content":"Coder Agents is control-plane-native. This stack does not wrap Grok Build: the wiring\nabove *teaches* (skill + instructions) and *equips* (the workspace-side CLI with its\nLiteLLM alias) the native Coder agent to drive Grok Build when work is too big to\nhand-drive. Delegation is a taught habit, not the product."},"1099":{"id":"/hermes","page_id":"/hermes","type":"page","content":"Hermes host assistant","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes"},"1100":{"id":"/hermes-0","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"The on-host assistant with two doors - the Web Dashboard on :9119 and the host terminal via hermes-shell: how to reach it, what it may touch, and how approvals gate a mutating command from either."},"1101":{"id":"/hermes-1","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Hermes host assistant"},"1102":{"id":"/hermes-2","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#access","content":"Access"},"1103":{"id":"/hermes-3","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"From the host terminal"},"1104":{"id":"/hermes-4","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"Audit and drift"},"1105":{"id":"/hermes-5","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#auth","content":"Auth"},"1106":{"id":"/hermes-6","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#models","content":"Models"},"1107":{"id":"/hermes-7","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#web-research","content":"Web research"},"1108":{"id":"/hermes-8","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"Mounts and \"be the host\""},"1109":{"id":"/hermes-9","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"Approvals"},"1110":{"id":"/hermes-10","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"Bootstrap and skills"},"1111":{"id":"/hermes-11","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"Security notes"},"1112":{"id":"/hermes-12","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Commands"},"1113":{"id":"/hermes-13","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1114":{"id":"/hermes-14","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"How to reach the dashboard, on the LAN and on the public name"},"1115":{"id":"/hermes-15","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Opening the same assistant in your own terminal with `hermes-shell`"},"1116":{"id":"/hermes-16","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Sign-in: HTTP basic auth, with Authelia in front of the public name"},"1117":{"id":"/hermes-17","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"The models it calls through LiteLLM"},"1118":{"id":"/hermes-18","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Web research through the stack's own SearXNG, with the third-party fallback off"},"1119":{"id":"/hermes-19","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"What the container mounts, and what \"be the host\" costs"},"1120":{"id":"/hermes-20","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Approval prompts, bootstrap seeding, and the skills directory"},"1121":{"id":"/hermes-21","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"**What this enables**"},"1122":{"id":"/hermes-22","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Running stack operations by conversation with a plan and an approval first"},"1123":{"id":"/hermes-23","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Reaching the assistant on the LAN, and on the public name `auto` composes from the parts"},"1124":{"id":"/hermes-24","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Asking from a terminal you already have - the right Linux command, a triage, a\nGitHub project deployed into the deploy workspace - with the same skills, memory\nand approval gate behind the answer"},"1125":{"id":"/hermes-25","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Hermes is the Nous Research Hermes Agent running as the interactive assistant **on the\nstack host itself**: not inside a workspace, not in a streamed desktop, but with the\ncheckout, the compose project and the docker socket in reach. It adds an official Web\nDashboard with a Chat tab that carries approval prompts, so the assistant can plan a\nchange, show the plan, and wait for you to approve it before it touches anything."},"1126":{"id":"/hermes-26","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"That is the whole point of the service: stack ops by conversation. \"Recreate the dev\nstack and tell me which container restarts\", \"why is the coder agent offline\", \"run the\ngate and fix what it reports\" - each one runs with a plan first and an approval card\nbefore the mutating command."},"1127":{"id":"/hermes-27","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Related: dashboard.md (the card that links here),\nlitellm-routing.md (the aliases Hermes calls),\npublic-access.md (the public name, which `auto` composes),\nSECURITY.md."},"1128":{"id":"/hermes-28","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"Dashboard on host port **9119**, from `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PORT`. The dev sandbox shifts\nit to **19119** the same way it shifts every other published port."},"1129":{"id":"/hermes-29","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"**LAN:** `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` - plain HTTP, exactly like LiteLLM on `:4000`.\nThere is no self-signed cert for this port and no redirect to HTTPS. This URL stays\nwhen the public name is on. Tailscale reaches it the same way, on that port."},"1130":{"id":"/hermes-30","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"**Public name, opt-in:** `hermes-<suffix>.<base>`, so\n`https://hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` under the shipped defaults, when\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` composes it or an explicit list names it. Bootstrap\nclassifies `hermes-*` and `hermes.*` and writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`. The path is the\nsame as the other public names: Cloudflare\n(orange cloud, WebSockets on by default), Traefik on the other LAN server (TLS\n`*.overeazy.io`, Authelia), the `router` on this box `:8080`, then\n`http://hermes:9119`, with the original Host preserved. Traefik's backend is\n`:8080`, not `:9119`. See public-access.md."},"1131":{"id":"/hermes-31","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"Sign-in is HTTP basic auth from `.env`: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` (default `admin`) and\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD`. Bootstrap generates the password; it is **not** the\nHomepage password, and `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` does not rotate it.\nThat basic auth stays on every door. Hermes v0.22.5 refuses to bind `0.0.0.0` with no\nprovider. On the public URL Authelia challenges first and basic auth challenges\nsecond; two logins are expected. LAN `:9119`, and LAN `:8080` with this name's Host\nheader, never see Authelia, so basic auth is the credential on those paths."},"1132":{"id":"/hermes-32","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"The **Chat tab** is the primary UX. It embeds the Hermes TUI - a PTY over WebSocket\non `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` - so the terminal agent and the browser are the same\nprocess, and approval prompts render as cards in the chat rather than vanishing into\na detached tty. Hermes sends a keepalive every 20 seconds. Traefik and Authelia have\nto pass the `Upgrade` hop. A close code **4403** is the Host / Origin / peer guard.\nCompose sets `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the seed file\ndoes not carry `public_url`). The seeded `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is\n`[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]`, which is what makes Hermes honour `X-Forwarded-Proto` from the\nrouter (the Secure cookie and `wss`). `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is environment\nand applies when the container is recreated. An existing `hermes-data` volume does\nnot pick up `trusted_proxies` until a reseed or `hermes config set` - the volume\nseed is copy-once. The reseed command is below."},"1133":{"id":"/hermes-33","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#access","content":"The Homepage card in the **Host** group links to `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the public\norigin when set, otherwise the LAN URL). Its `siteMonitor` is\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status`, which resolves over compose DNS, so the card stays\ncorrect whatever the browser link is. See dashboard.md."},"1134":{"id":"/hermes-34","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"The Chat tab is the door for someone at a browser. The terminal door is for someone\nalready on the box, and it opens the **same** assistant - same `SOUL.md`, same seeded\n`config.yaml`, same git-tracked skills, same LiteLLM lane, same memory, same manual\napproval gate:"},"1135":{"id":"/hermes-35","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**Subcommands:** `chat` (the default), `ask TEXT` for one question and exit, `shell`\nfor a shell with the container's mounts and socket, `check` for the readiness report,\n`commands` for the assistant's own help. Every word after the subcommand is handed to\nthe assistant verbatim, so a flag this script has never heard of still works."},"1136":{"id":"/hermes-36","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**Options** (before the subcommand): `-p/--project-name` (`evo-t1-prod`; the dev\nsandbox is `evo-t1-dev`), `-f/--file`, `-e/--env-file`, `-m/--model <alias>` for one\nsession, `-w/--writable <dir>`, `--one-shot`, `--dry-run`, `-h/--help`."},"1137":{"id":"/hermes-37","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**It enters the running container rather than starting a second one.** Hermes keeps\nits sessions and memory in a SQLite store under `/opt/data`, and two containers on one\ndata directory is the documented way to corrupt it - the WAL sidecars and the lock\nonly mean something inside one container. So while the gateway is up this is\n`docker compose exec`, and the terminal agent and the dashboard agent are literally\nthe same process on the same store. Only when nothing is running does it\n`docker compose run --rm --no-deps` a lone container. `--one-shot` forces the second\ncontainer anyway and says out loud what it risks."},"1138":{"id":"/hermes-38","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**`check` is the first thing to run when an answer looks impossible.** It reports the\ndaemon, the compose CLI, the project, the container state, whether the service\nresolves, the image, whether the data volume is seeded, whether the model key is set\nor still a shipped placeholder, whether LiteLLM answers `/health/liveliness`, and the\nwork directory. It prints key names and never a value, and it launches nothing."},"1139":{"id":"/hermes-39","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**Approvals are the same gate, inline.** A dangerous command stops in your tty with\n`[o]nce | [s]ession | [a]lways | [d]eny`, deny by default. There is no browser card\nhere and nothing to approve elsewhere; the terminal door does not loosen\n`approvals.mode: manual`, and `--yolo` / `HERMES_YOLO_MODE` have no place in this\nstack. See Approvals."},"1140":{"id":"/hermes-40","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**An interactive session needs a real tty** (the TUI draws on a pty compose allocates\nfrom your terminal), so `chat` and `shell` refuse from a pipe or a cron line - that is\nwhat `ask` is for."},"1141":{"id":"/hermes-41","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**What it can see:** the checkout at `$STACK_HOST_PATH`, the deploy workspace at\n`${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}`, `/opt/stack-skills`, the data volume, and\nthe docker socket. **What it cannot:** `/home` is not mounted, so no dotfiles, SSH\nkeys or shell history - by design. The image carries git and the Docker CLI but not\nbazelisk, so inside the container the same operations go through\n`docker compose -p evo-t1-prod -f \"$STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml\"`."},"1142":{"id":"/hermes-42","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"**`--writable` matters more than it looks.** The official image sets\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT=/opt/data`, so a file-tool write anywhere else is refused\noutright and no approval card even appears. To have the assistant edit files on the\nhost, name the directory: `--writable \"${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}\"` or\nthe checkout path."},"1143":{"id":"/hermes-43","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"`hermes-shell -- check` answers \"can I open the assistant\". It cannot answer \"is this\nstill the assistant this repo describes\", and the two come apart on a long-lived box:\nan operator edits the config inside the volume, a recreate reads a stale `.env`, a\nmerge changes the seed and a seed-only-when-absent copy never takes it. `hermes-doctor`\nanswers the second question. It is read-only and prints key names with no value."},"1144":{"id":"/hermes-44","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"**What it reads:** the config the container actually loads, the environment it was\ncreated with, the mounts it was given, and the two seed files beside the checkout.\nThe created environment is the fact and the `.env` beside the compose file is the\nintention: compose interpolated the former at create time, so a key can be right in\nthe file and stale in the container until the next recreate. Every container-level\nfact comes from `docker inspect` or `docker compose ps`; nothing writes, restarts,\nrecreates, or execs."},"1145":{"id":"/hermes-45","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"**The twelve checks:** `approvals` (manual, with cron and unattended both deny),\n`terminal-cwd`, `write-root`, `web-lane`, `keyless-egress`, `mcp-servers`,\n`seed-drift`, `mount-identity`, `image-pin`, `model-key`, `dashboard`, `memories`."},"1146":{"id":"/hermes-46","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"**It reports, it does not gate.** Exit 0 on a clean bill (warnings allowed), 1 when a\ncheck failed, 2 on a bad invocation, 5 when it cannot find a checkout.\n`hermes-shell -- check` stays the thing that decides whether to launch a session."},"1147":{"id":"/hermes-47","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"**Three findings it exists to catch**, each one measured on a live box:\n`terminal.cwd` still carrying the `${STACK_HOST_PATH}` token (Hermes validates that\nvalue with `expanduser()` alone, so the token reads as a missing directory while\n`hermes config get` prints it looking healthy), `STACK_HOST_PATH` naming a directory\nwith no checkout (compose mounts an empty directory over the repo and every\ncontainer-level check still reads healthy), and a `web` block that stopped pinning\n`searxng` or flipped a keyless switch back on, which routes a search to a vendor's\nfree tier while the config still looks local."},"1148":{"id":"/hermes-48","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"**`--data DIR`** points the two volume checks at a directory instead of asking the\ndaemon where the volume is mounted, so the audit can be run against a copy of one."},"1149":{"id":"/hermes-49","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"**Run it before a `--force` re-seed and after a merge.** The drift check names the\nfile that differs and prints the `hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force` line that\nwould overwrite it, which is the pair an operator wants side by side."},"1150":{"id":"/hermes-50","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"Hermes since the June 2026 hardening refuses a non-loopback bind without a\n`DashboardAuthProvider`. Binding `0.0.0.0` with no provider is a boot error, not a\nwarning."},"1151":{"id":"/hermes-51","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a deprecated no-op. Setting it changes nothing and buys\nnothing, and it is not how the public name is opened."},"1152":{"id":"/hermes-52","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"Compose supplies the provider: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_USERNAME`,\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD` and `HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_SECRET`,\ninterpolated from `.env` with the same non-empty fallbacks `.env.sample`\ncarries (`admin` / `change-me-hermes` / `change-me-hermes-secret`). An empty\ninterpolation default is a boot error on `0.0.0.0`: the plugin skips\nregistration and the gateway refuses to bind."},"1153":{"id":"/hermes-53","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#auth","content":"The result fails closed: no credentials, no dashboard. There is no unauthenticated\nread-only mode to fall back to."},"1154":{"id":"/hermes-54","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Hermes talks to the LiteLLM proxy the stack already runs: base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1`, model alias **`agent`**. That is the tool-capable Spark lane,\nwhich is what an agent loop needs, and it stays the default."},"1155":{"id":"/hermes-55","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"`agent` also thinks hard by default: it bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`.\n**`agent-low`**, **`agent-medium`** and **`agent-xhigh`** are the thinking variants of\nit - the same Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on the same three Spark boxes, with real\n`tool_calls` and that level baked into the alias instead. Use one when a run needs a\ndifferent level; Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and `xhigh` and nothing else."},"1156":{"id":"/hermes-56","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"The key is `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`, substituted from the environment into the seeded\n`config.yaml`. Do not copy the key into a Hermes file - one secret, one owner."},"1157":{"id":"/hermes-57","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Fallbacks, in the order worth trying them:"},"1158":{"id":"/hermes-58","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"`chat` - `qwen2.5:7b` on the Arc, and it returns real `tool_calls`. The reliable\ndegrade path when every Spark box is down."},"1159":{"id":"/hermes-59","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"`coder` / `coder-fast` - the Qwen2.5-Coder weights on the Arc. Chat is good, but\nthose weights write the tool call into the message body as plain text, so agent\nturns silently lose their tools. See\nlitellm-routing.md."},"1160":{"id":"/hermes-60","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Switch it inside the container with `hermes config set model.model chat` (or\n`agent-medium` for a thinking level), which edits the live config under `/opt/data`."},"1161":{"id":"/hermes-61","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#models","content":"Nous Portal is an optional upstream, not a default, and this page does not document it.\nNothing here needs an account."},"1162":{"id":"/hermes-62","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#web-research","content":"The assistant can look things up, and the query leaves through your own SearXNG."},"1163":{"id":"/hermes-63","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#web-research","content":"`web_search` goes to the stack's SearXNG container: the seeded config sets\n`web.search_backend: searxng`, and compose gives the container\n`SEARXNG_URL=${STACK_HERMES_SEARCH_URL:-http://searxng:8181}`. The variable's\npresence is what registers the provider (`plugins/web/searxng` reads\n`SEARXNG_URL` as its key env), and it is the only bundled web provider that\nneeds no API key. The name resolves on the compose network, so this adds no\npublished port."},"1164":{"id":"/hermes-64","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#web-research","content":"**Both keyless switches stay off, and that is the part that keeps the promise.**\n`agent/web_search_registry.py` resolves a backend in five steps, and its last\none is an anonymous free-tier ring - `exa`, `parallel`, `firecrawl`,\n`keenable` - reached with no key at all. A seed that named no backend and left\n`web.keyless_fallback` and `web.keyless_rescue` at their shipped `true` would\nanswer a search from a vendor's public endpoint while looking local. With both\nfalse, a query either goes to your instance or fails on the box."},"1165":{"id":"/hermes-65","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#web-research","content":"`web_extract` is deliberately unconfigured. SearXNG aggregates engine results\nand does not fetch pages, so the seed leaves `web.backend` and\n`web.extract_backend` empty and page extraction fails with \"no extract provider\nconfigured\". Set `web.extract_backend` yourself when you decide a fetch is\nworth a third-party extractor; nothing reaches one by default."},"1166":{"id":"/hermes-66","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#web-research","content":"Query the lane the same way the agent does:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` from a host shell counts results per\nengine, which is how an empty answer stays distinguishable from no answer.\nInside the container,\n`curl -s -H 'Accept: application/json' \"$SEARXNG_URL/search?q=hermes&format=json\"`\nis the exact request the provider makes. The instance only answers JSON\nbecause `searxng/settings.yml` adds `json` to `search.formats`; without it the\nrequest is refused with 403 before the query is parsed."},"1167":{"id":"/hermes-67","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#web-research","content":"Re-point the lane with `STACK_HERMES_SEARCH_URL` in `.env` (a sibling box, say)\nand recreate. An operator who wants a keyed provider sets\n`web.search_backend` plus that vendor's key env in the container themselves -\nthe seed carries neither, and adding one is a decision with a privacy cost."},"1168":{"id":"/hermes-68","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount                        | Inside      | Why                              |\n| ---------------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------- |\n| `hermes-data` (named volume) | `/opt/data` | config, sessions, memory, SQLite |","table":"table-0"},"1169":{"id":"/hermes-69","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount                   | Inside                           | Why                     |\n| ----------------------- | -------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `${STACK_HOST_PATH:-.}` | `${STACK_HOST_PATH:-/opt/stack}` | identical-path checkout |","table":"table-0"},"1170":{"id":"/hermes-70","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount                                     | Inside                 | Why                  |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |\n| `${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}` | the same absolute path | the deploy workspace |","table":"table-0"},"1171":{"id":"/hermes-71","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount             | Inside                   | Why                |\n| ----------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------ |\n| `./hermes/skills` | `/opt/stack-skills` (ro) | git-tracked skills |","table":"table-0"},"1172":{"id":"/hermes-72","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"| Mount                  | Inside                 | Why                           |\n| ---------------------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `/var/run/docker.sock` | `/var/run/docker.sock` | compose and container control |","table":"table-0"},"1173":{"id":"/hermes-73","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`/opt/data` is a **named volume on purpose**. Hermes keeps a SQLite database there in\nWAL mode, and WAL on a virtiofs bind mount is not safe. Bind-mounting that path is how\nyou corrupt the assistant's own memory."},"1174":{"id":"/hermes-74","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`STACK_HOST_PATH` is the checkout's `pwd -P`, or, from a subdirectory or a linked\nworktree, its git top-level. Compose bind-mounts that host path at\nthe same path inside the container, so `docker compose -f $STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml`\ntalking through the socket names bind sources the daemon can find. Empty (CI\n`compose config`) falls back to `.:/opt/stack`. `bootstrap` refreshes the key only\nafter checking the path holds this `docker-compose.yml`: compose mounts whatever the\nkey names, so a stale value leaves the assistant working an empty directory while\nevery container-level check still reads healthy."},"1175":{"id":"/hermes-75","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"The working directory the agent runs in is `terminal.cwd`, and it has to be a literal\npath. Hermes validates it in `agent/runtime_cwd.py` with `expanduser()` alone, so a\n`${VAR}` left in the file is reported as `does not exist` and the agent works somewhere\nelse, while `hermes config get terminal.cwd` still prints the token because the config\n*loader* does expand `${VAR}` - the two paths disagree, and only the runtime one runs\ncommands. `hermes-bootstrap` renders the placeholder in the seeded `config.yaml` at\nseed time (`--cwd DIR` overrides what it renders), which is the part that fixes a\nlong-lived box. compose also sets `TERMINAL_CWD` from the same key, as the backstop for\na config that names no `terminal.cwd`: `apply_terminal_config_to_env` lets an explicit\n`terminal.cwd` in the file own that variable, so the file decides wherever it carries\none. Re-seed a volume that predates the render with\n`hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force`."},"1176":{"id":"/hermes-76","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`hermes/skills/` is tracked in git. Add a skill by editing the repo, never by editing\nthe volume; the volume copy is not the record."},"1177":{"id":"/hermes-77","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`STACK_DEPLOY_PATH` (default `/opt/stack-deploy`) is the **deploy workspace**: the one\ndirectory meant for clones, builds, and the compose project of a third-party project\nthe assistant brings up. It is mounted at its own absolute path for the same reason\n`STACK_HOST_PATH` is - a bind source inside a project file the assistant writes has to\nresolve on the host daemon - and bootstrap fills it in without overwriting a path you\nset yourself. It is deliberately outside `/home`, so giving the assistant somewhere to\nwork did not mean giving it your dotfiles. An existing container gains the mount on\nrecreate, since `restart` keeps the old mounts."},"1178":{"id":"/hermes-78","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`group_add: ${DOCKER_GID}` plus the socket is the same credential the `coder` service\nalready holds: root-equivalent on the host. Nothing new is exposed here that the Coder\ncontainer does not already have - which is precisely why approvals, below, are the\ncontrol rather than the mount list. A public Hermes name has that same standing as\npublic Coder."},"1179":{"id":"/hermes-79","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`HERMES_UID` / `HERMES_GID` are set to the host operator, so a file Hermes writes into\n`$STACK_HOST_PATH` is yours to commit and not owned by uid 10000."},"1180":{"id":"/hermes-80","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#mounts-and-be-the-host","content":"`/home` is **not** mounted in v1. Hermes cannot see your dotfiles, your SSH keys or\nyour shell history, and it should not need to."},"1181":{"id":"/hermes-81","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"The seeded config sets `approvals.mode: manual`. `cron_mode` and `unattended_mode` are\nboth `deny`, so a scheduled or unattended run cannot approve anything either."},"1182":{"id":"/hermes-82","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"Leave the mode on `manual`. `off` is `--yolo`: every mutating command executes on the\nhost with no card."},"1183":{"id":"/hermes-83","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"`terminal.backend: local` keeps the dangerous-command checker active. The `docker`\nbackend skips that checker, because it treats the container as the safety boundary -\nand here the container is mounted onto the host, so that boundary is not real."},"1184":{"id":"/hermes-84","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"Working agreement with the assistant: it explains the plan in chat, then waits for the\napproval card in the Chat tab before running anything mutating. If a run looks like it\nis acting without one, stop it and re-read the mode."},"1185":{"id":"/hermes-85","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#approvals","content":"From the terminal door the same gate is inline in your tty: a dangerous command stops\nwith `[o]nce | [s]ession | [a]lways | [d]eny` and does not proceed until you answer,\nwith deny the default. An approval is scoped to the session you answer in, so a yes\ngiven over there does not cover a fresh session opened at this door.\n`--yolo` and `HERMES_YOLO_MODE` bypass the gate entirely; they have no place here."},"1186":{"id":"/hermes-86","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"The first `compose up` runs `hermes-init`, which is the in-container equivalent of\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap`: it runs\n`scripts/hermes-bootstrap.sh --in-container` and copies `hermes/config.yaml` and\n`hermes/SOUL.md` into `/opt/data` **only when those files are missing**. Editing the\ncopy in the volume is how an operator ends up with a config no one can reproduce."},"1187":{"id":"/hermes-87","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"The seed is a template for one key: `${STACK_HOST_PATH}` in `terminal.cwd` is rendered\nto a literal path on the way into the volume, from `--cwd`, then `STACK_HOST_PATH`,\nthen `/opt/stack` (announced when it falls back). `${LITELLM_MASTER_KEY}` stays a token\non purpose - Hermes expands it at boot, and a byte of the key in a volume copy is what\nMounts and \"be the host\" argues against."},"1188":{"id":"/hermes-88","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"To reseed deliberately:"},"1189":{"id":"/hermes-89","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"`<mount>` is the host directory of the Hermes data volume (what compose mounts at\n`/opt/data`). `--force` is how an existing volume picks up the seeded\n`dashboard.trusted_proxies`, and `--cwd /absolute/path/to/checkout` is how a host re-seed\nnames the working directory when the shell running it carries no `STACK_HOST_PATH`.\nSetting that key with `hermes config set` is the\nin-container alternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is not in the volume;\nrecreate the container and the env applies."},"1190":{"id":"/hermes-90","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"A skill is one directory: `hermes/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, with\nagentskills.io frontmatter for the name and description.\nCommit it, and the next `up` has it."},"1191":{"id":"/hermes-91","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"What ships, and which question each one answers: `evo-stack-ops` (pull, status,\nrestart, logs for this stack), `coder-workspace` (workspaces and their GitHub auth),\n`model-routing` (which LiteLLM alias, and how to switch it), `host-linux-ops` (the\nright Linux command, what each flag does, and the blast radius before it runs),\n`host-diagnostics` (read-only triage for a slow, hot, full or failing box),\n`web-research` (what is worth looking up, how to read an empty answer from the\nlane above, and what that lane refuses to do), and\n`deploy-from-github` (a named repository to running, with the read-first, pin-the-ref,\nisolate, preflight, health-check, keep-a-rollback order). The last four are what make\nthe terminal door worth opening: they carry the mentoring stance - inspect, explain,\npropose, then act on the yes."},"1192":{"id":"/hermes-92","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"A bind-mounted skill needs no recreate when the agent reloads skills from disk; start a\n**new session** - a fresh Chat tab, or a new `hermes-shell` run - and the new skill is\noffered. Recreate only if it still does not show up, since `restart` keeps the old\nmounts."},"1193":{"id":"/hermes-93","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#bootstrap-and-skills","content":"`SOUL.md` is the identity - voice and stance, a short persona. It is not a fork of\nAGENTS.md: the repo's shell conventions stay in that file, where the\nwriters and the linters read them."},"1194":{"id":"/hermes-94","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"The public name is opt-in (`hermes-*`, which `auto` composes from the naming parts or\nan explicit list pins). It does not change what the container can do: local terminal,\nhost mounts and the docker socket still mean this agent can affect the real host.\nTreat its approvals the way you treat a root shell. That is the same standing as\npublic Coder."},"1195":{"id":"/hermes-95","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"HTTP basic auth stays, including on the public URL. Authelia is the gate in front of\nthat name only. LAN `:9119` and a matching Host on `:8080` bypass Authelia, so the\nbasic-auth provider is load-bearing. It is a provider, not a toggle."},"1196":{"id":"/hermes-96","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` does not remove that requirement. Leave\n`approvals.mode` on `manual`."},"1197":{"id":"/hermes-97","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"Secrets live in gitignored `.env` and in the `hermes-data` volume. Never commit them,\nand never paste `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` into a chat to prove a route works."},"1198":{"id":"/hermes-98","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"Prefer structured commands - the existing Bazel scripts and `docker compose` - over\nad-hoc shell. A script the repo owns is reviewable and test-covered; a one-liner the\nmodel invented is neither. See AGENTS.md."},"1199":{"id":"/hermes-99","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"No GitHub PAT in Hermes. GitHub auth in this stack is Coder External Auth only, and a\ntoken here would sit on a container that can both write to the checkout and drive the\nhost daemon. See SECURITY.md."},"1200":{"id":"/hermes-100","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"The terminal door adds a way in for someone who already has a shell on this host, and\nopens no new listener, no new port and no new credential. `hermes-shell` authenticates\nnothing of its own: `docker` on that host is the credential, and it was always\nroot-equivalent here. Its one guard is that the assistant's approvals stay `manual` on\nboth doors."},"1201":{"id":"/hermes-101","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#security-notes","content":"The deploy workspace is writable by the assistant's file tools only when you say so\nwith `--writable`, and the file tools refuse anything outside the image's\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT` (the official default is `/opt/data`) with no prompt at all.\nName the narrowest directory that finishes the job."},"1202":{"id":"/hermes-102","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Open the assistant in your own terminal (subcommands `chat`, `ask`, `shell`, `check`,\n`commands`; `--help` for the flags):"},"1203":{"id":"/hermes-103","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Read-only audit of what the assistant was created with, and how far it has drifted from\nthis checkout (`report`, `posture`, `web`, `diff`; `--check` narrows to one):"},"1204":{"id":"/hermes-104","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Stable stack - `hermes` comes up with the rest:"},"1205":{"id":"/hermes-105","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Bootstrap options, including `--data` and `--force`:"},"1206":{"id":"/hermes-106","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"Dev sandbox, which puts the dashboard on 19119:"},"1207":{"id":"/hermes-107","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes#commands","content":"The stack runners take `up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`; `status` and `logs` are\nwhere a Hermes that will not bind its port is diagnosed first. Status across the whole\nbox, including this card, is in dashboard.md; the LAN and public\nrouting rules are in public-access.md, and the service inventory is\nin README.md."},"1208":{"id":"/","page_id":"/","type":"page","content":"evo-t1-coder-stack","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["overview","start","stack"],"url":"/"},"1209":{"id":"/-0","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/","content":"One compose stack that turns a GMKtec EVO-T1 into a self-hosted dev-workspace server — Coder, LiteLLM, IPEX-LLM Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes."},"1210":{"id":"/-1","page_id":"/","type":"heading","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"evo-t1-coder-stack"},"1211":{"id":"/-2","page_id":"/","type":"heading","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"url":"/#start-here","content":"Start here"},"1212":{"id":"/-3","page_id":"/","type":"heading","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"What runs here"},"1213":{"id":"/-4","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1214":{"id":"/-5","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"What runs on the box and which door each service answers on"},"1215":{"id":"/-6","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"Where to start depending on what you came to do"},"1216":{"id":"/-7","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"How the sidebar tabs divide the subjects"},"1217":{"id":"/-8","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"**What this enables**"},"1218":{"id":"/-9","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"Getting oriented in one screen before opening a terminal"},"1219":{"id":"/-10","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"Reaching the page that owns a subject from one card, instead of reading four that touch it"},"1220":{"id":"/-11","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"This repository brings up one Compose project on a GMKtec EVO-T1 (Intel Core Ultra 9\n285H, Arc 140T iGPU, 96 GB DDR5) and keeps it runnable by conversation, by dashboard, or\nby `bazelisk`. Everything is a Bazel target addressed as\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`. On the stack host, `scripts/setup-host.sh` is\nthe one script meant to run straight from a shell. On a laptop, reopen the repo in the\ncontributor environment or run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image`."},"1221":{"id":"/-12","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#evo-t1-coder-stack","content":"The box deliberately runs **two isolated copies** of the stack: a stable one you work in\nand a dev one you break. Two stacks on one box\nin the README is the short version; docs/config-updates.md covers\nwhat a `git pull` leaves behind."},"1222":{"id":"/-13","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Getting started\" href=\"/getting-started/\" description=\"First boot, the order the services come up in, and what to check once Coder answers.\" />"},"1223":{"id":"/-14","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Learn the stack\" href=\"/learn/\" description=\"Templates, model routing, the workspace agent, Hermes, conventions, branching.\" />"},"1224":{"id":"/-15","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Operate the box\" href=\"/operate/\" description=\"Dashboard and desktops, config refresh, public access, troubleshooting.\" />"},"1225":{"id":"/-16","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#start-here","content":"<Card title=\"Contribute\" href=\"/contribute/devcontainer/\" description=\"The golden image, the contributor container, and the Bazel gates a change runs through.\" />"},"1226":{"id":"/-17","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                     |\n| ------- | --------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 80 / 443  | The landing page, and the only thing publishing it |","table":"table-0"},"1227":{"id":"/-18","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                             |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n|         | 3001      | Control plane: one container per workspace |","table":"table-0"},"1228":{"id":"/-19","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                                        |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 3002      | The plain listener a workspace agent dials via `host.docker.internal` |","table":"table-0"},"1229":{"id":"/-20","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                            |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 4000      | The single OpenAI-compatible door in front of every model |","table":"table-0"},"1230":{"id":"/-21","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                          |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | —         | The Arc 140T model runtime, never published to the host |","table":"table-0"},"1231":{"id":"/-22","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port   | What it is for                                   |\n| ------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n|         | 3000 → 4443 | Streamed desktops; the wizard only on first boot |","table":"table-0"},"1232":{"id":"/-23","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                                                                 |\n| ------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 9119      | The host assistant. LAN `:9119` stays; the public name is opt-in via `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` |","table":"table-0"},"1233":{"id":"/-24","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"| Service | Host port | What it is for                              |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 8181      | Keyless metasearch, the agent's search lane |","table":"table-0"},"1234":{"id":"/-25","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"Ollama is reachable only from inside the compose network: LiteLLM is the gateway, and it\nis the thing that carries the master key."},"1235":{"id":"/-26","page_id":"/","tags":["overview","start","stack"],"type":"text","url":"/#what-runs-here","content":"The sidebar tabs divide the rest: **Learn** covers how the pieces work, **Operate** covers\nrunning the box day to day, and **Contribute** covers the gates a change runs through.\nEach subject has exactly one page, and the README links to that page rather than\nrepeating it."},"1236":{"id":"/learn","page_id":"/learn","type":"page","content":"Learn the stack","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Learn the stack"],"tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn"},"1237":{"id":"/learn-0","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn","content":"The concepts behind the box — what the templates build, how a model request is routed, what the agents file seeds, and the rules a change follows."},"1238":{"id":"/learn-1","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"Learn the stack"},"1239":{"id":"/learn-2","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#how-the-pieces-fit","content":"How the pieces fit"},"1240":{"id":"/learn-3","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#the-model-lane","content":"The model lane"},"1241":{"id":"/learn-4","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#the-assistant-on-the-host","content":"The assistant on the host"},"1242":{"id":"/learn-5","page_id":"/learn","type":"heading","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"url":"/learn#the-rules-a-change-follows","content":"The rules a change follows"},"1243":{"id":"/learn-6","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1244":{"id":"/learn-7","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"Which page explains which part of the stack"},"1245":{"id":"/learn-8","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"The order that makes the pieces click, if you are reading them all"},"1246":{"id":"/learn-9","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"How this tab differs from the `/ez-learn` slash command in Grok Build"},"1247":{"id":"/learn-10","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"**What this enables**"},"1248":{"id":"/learn-11","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"Understanding a behaviour before trying to change it"},"1249":{"id":"/learn-12","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"Knowing which page owns a concept when two of them seem to"},"1250":{"id":"/learn-13","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#learn-the-stack","content":"> **Not the same as the `/ez-learn` command.** This tab is the concepts index for people\n> reading the docs. `/ez-learn` typed at the Grok Build prompt turns the session you are in\n> into durable memory - flush it into a session log, dream those logs into organized memory,\n> remember one note, or browse and clear the store; it is this stack's copy of the upstream\n> `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`. Skill authoring is its siblings:\n> `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked and `/ez-create-skill` scaffolds one\n> from a description. `grok inspect` lists the three once their seed has landed. A missing\n> one is the skill-seed entry in troubleshooting.md."},"1251":{"id":"/learn-14","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#how-the-pieces-fit","content":"Two templates turn a git URL into a running container: the plain `workspace` template, and\n`devcontainer`, which builds the clone's own `.devcontainer` on a DinD sidecar. Both are\npushed from this checkout, and both seed the agent config the workspace starts with."},"1252":{"id":"/learn-15","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#how-the-pieces-fit","content":"Coder workspace templates owns the templates, the create form and\nthe variables; Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal\nowns what runs inside the container once it is up."},"1253":{"id":"/learn-16","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#the-model-lane","content":"One OpenAI-compatible door, several backends behind it: the Arc 140T through IPEX-LLM, and\noptional Spark boxes. Which one answers is a routing decision made in\nlitellm-routing.md, not in the client — and that page also states the\none thing the local model cannot do."},"1254":{"id":"/learn-17","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#the-assistant-on-the-host","content":"Hermes is the interactive assistant with the checkout, the compose project and\nthe docker socket in reach, gated by approval prompts. Its public name is opt-in with\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto`, which composes it from the naming parts; with that key empty\nthe dashboard stays on the LAN.\npublic-access.md is the page that decides what the box exposes."},"1255":{"id":"/learn-18","page_id":"/learn","tags":["learn","concepts","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/learn#the-rules-a-change-follows","content":"project-conventions.md is what a script has to look like to be\nmergeable, and branching.md is how it gets to `main`. The Bazel harness they\nboth assume is in the Contribute tab."},"1256":{"id":"/litellm-routing","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"page","content":"LiteLLM model routing","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing"},"1257":{"id":"/litellm-routing-0","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing","content":"The model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request picks a backend, and what happens when a Spark box goes down."},"1258":{"id":"/litellm-routing-1","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"LiteLLM model routing"},"1259":{"id":"/litellm-routing-2","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#one-front-door","content":"One front door"},"1260":{"id":"/litellm-routing-3","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Two doors, one key"},"1261":{"id":"/litellm-routing-4","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"What an alias is"},"1262":{"id":"/litellm-routing-5","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"Config keys that gate all of this"},"1263":{"id":"/litellm-routing-6","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"When a box answers `NoRoom`"},"1264":{"id":"/litellm-routing-7","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"The alias table"},"1265":{"id":"/litellm-routing-8","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"Reading the table"},"1266":{"id":"/litellm-routing-9","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#tool-calls-the-measured-limitation","content":"Tool calls: the measured limitation"},"1267":{"id":"/litellm-routing-10","page_id":"/litellm-routing","type