,"page_id":"/litellm-routing","type":"heading","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"The one-front-door rule"},"1287":{"id":"/litellm-routing-30","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1288":{"id":"/litellm-routing-31","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"The one front door every client dials, and why nothing else is published"},"1289":{"id":"/litellm-routing-32","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"The alias table, and which backend each alias resolves to"},"1290":{"id":"/litellm-routing-33","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Tool calls: the measured limitation and the path that supports them"},"1291":{"id":"/litellm-routing-34","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Thinking levels: what `agent` bakes, and what the baked aliases change"},"1292":{"id":"/litellm-routing-35","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Routing inside the `agent` group, and the `agent` to `coder` fallback"},"1293":{"id":"/litellm-routing-36","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Reading the routing back, and editing an alias safely"},"1294":{"id":"/litellm-routing-37","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"**What this enables**"},"1295":{"id":"/litellm-routing-38","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Moving a workload between the Arc iGPU and a Spark box without touching a workspace"},"1296":{"id":"/litellm-routing-39","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Diagnosing a wrong-backend answer from the routing rather than from the client"},"1297":{"id":"/litellm-routing-40","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Deep reference for the model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request\npicks a backend, what a pinned conversation does, and what happens when a Spark box goes\ndown."},"1298":{"id":"/litellm-routing-41","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Short version lives in README.md (\"LiteLLM model aliases\"); this file is\nthe long version behind it. Symptom-first checks live in\ntroubleshooting.md."},"1299":{"id":"/litellm-routing-42","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#litellm-model-routing","content":"Related: coder-templates.md (the templates that render\n`~/.grok/config.toml`), grok-build-and-agents.md (the CLI and\nthe control-plane chat), project-conventions.md."},"1300":{"id":"/litellm-routing-43","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Workspaces and desktops always talk to one base URL,\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, and pick a model alias. That is the\nhost-gateway door."},"1301":{"id":"/litellm-routing-44","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"The control-plane chat agent dials the same proxy at `http://litellm:4000/v1`, over the\ncompose network, from inside the `coder` container. That is the server-side door."},"1302":{"id":"/litellm-routing-45","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Both doors authenticate with the same `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` value."},"1303":{"id":"/litellm-routing-46","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#two-doors-one-key","content":"Inside a workspace the key travels as the `LITELLM_API_KEY` environment variable, which\n`~/.grok/config.toml` references by `env_key`, so no key byte lands in a file."},"1304":{"id":"/litellm-routing-47","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"An alias is a `model_name:` entry in `litellm/config.yaml`, not a model."},"1305":{"id":"/litellm-routing-48","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"Every alias is a stable name. A workspace setting never has to change when a backend\nmoves behind one."},"1306":{"id":"/litellm-routing-49","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-an-alias-is","content":"A rename or deletion is the edit that can reach workspaces: both\n`templates/*/grok-config.toml.tftpl` twins render the alias list into\n`~/.grok/config.toml`, a running workspace keeps the copy it rendered at start, and\nthe next start re-renders it."},"1307":{"id":"/litellm-routing-50","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`model_list:` is the alias list; `router_settings:` is the routing."},"1308":{"id":"/litellm-routing-51","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`routing_strategy: least-busy` is the picker;\n`optional_pre_call_checks: [\"session_affinity\"]` is the pinning layer above it."},"1309":{"id":"/litellm-routing-52","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`fallbacks:` carries the degrade path `agent: [\"coder\"]`, repeated for each thinking\nalias, because those groups go down with exactly the same boxes."},"1310":{"id":"/litellm-routing-53","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`allowed_fails: 40` and `cooldown_time: 5` decide when a box stops being a candidate."},"1311":{"id":"/litellm-routing-54","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`background_health_checks: true`, with `health_check_interval: 300` and\n`health_check_concurrency: 2`, re-probe a box on their own, so recovery does not\nwait for user traffic to rediscover it. Those two keys have to sit in\n`general_settings:`; under `router_settings:` the proxy raises `ValueError` at boot.\nThe interval is LiteLLM's own default and the concurrency is deliberately small:\na probe is a real generation that carries the lane's baked `reasoning_effort`, and\non a box whose prompt cache sits full it evicts a warm conversation to fit itself.\nSee the TensorFold log section of troubleshooting.md."},"1312":{"id":"/litellm-routing-55","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`num_retries: 2` covers a transient blip; moving off a busy box is routing's job, not\nthe retry chain's. `request_timeout: 1200` is now the tightest deadline on the chain -\nthe CLI's streaming idle allowance (`inference_idle_timeout_secs`) sits at 1800 s - so\nthe proxy is the end that gives up first, at a full twenty minutes per attempt."},"1313":{"id":"/litellm-routing-56","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#config-keys-that-gate-all-of-this","content":"`model_group_retry_policy:` gives the four agent lanes their own `RetryPolicy`. The\nreason it exists is narrow enough to be worth its own section below."},"1314":{"id":"/litellm-routing-57","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"TensorFold on the Spark boxes refuses an over-admitted stream with the Zig error value\n`NoRoom`, delivered as an HTTP 500 body. The OpenAI SDK hands that to LiteLLM with no\nint `status_code`, which at the pinned `v1.102.1` puts it on the status-less branch of\n`exception_mapping_utils`, so the router sees `APIConnectionError`."},"1315":{"id":"/litellm-routing-58","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"`RetryPolicy` in that tag has no `APIConnectionErrorRetries` field. `DefaultRetries` is\nthe only field that can reach the error, which is why each agent lane declares one:"},"1316":{"id":"/litellm-routing-59","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"Read these three facts before changing the block:"},"1317":{"id":"/litellm-routing-60","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The four groups are independent and none inherits `agent`, so each needs its own six\nkeys. The zeros pin the exceptions `DefaultRetries` would otherwise reach; at this tag\n`NotFoundError` has no field of its own, so without a policy a typo'd model id is\nretried on `DefaultRetries`."},"1318":{"id":"/litellm-routing-61","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The policy is scoped to the Spark lanes. `coder` (Arc) and `chat` keep the plain\n`num_retries` path."},"1319":{"id":"/litellm-routing-62","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"These retries are instant and stay on the same box. A status-less exception means\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` excludes nothing and `_time_to_sleep_before_retry`\nreturns 0 while any deployment is healthy, and the workspace-wide\n`x-litellm-session-id` pin sends every subagent of a workspace to one box anyway. The\nblock makes a refusal less likely to surface. Relocation comes from the box and the\nfan-out."},"1320":{"id":"/litellm-routing-63","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The two levers that actually move load off a shedding box:"},"1321":{"id":"/litellm-routing-64","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"The box-side reclaim switch. The recipe measured the same refusal going from\n\"2 of 5 streams refused\" to \"5 of 5 admitted\" once patch 0029 is live with\n`TF_FLASHNEXT_ADMIT_RECLAIM=1`. `//scripts:spark-configure --apply` owns the per-host\noverride file those settings belong in, and `//scripts:spark-verify` already fails a\nbox whose `streams.max` drifts from the fleet's `PARALLEL`. Asserting the\nadmit-reclaim setting itself is still open work: neither script names that key today."},"1322":{"id":"/litellm-routing-65","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"Keep in-flight fan-out at or under a box's `PARALLEL` (5) per workspace. The\norchestrator cap of 4 is inside that budget."},"1323":{"id":"/litellm-routing-66","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-box-answers-noroom","content":"Two knobs that look right and do nothing here: `retry_after` is only a `min_timeout` and\nis never consulted while the group has healthy deployments, and `fallbacks_by_model`\ndoes not exist in this tag (the proxy warns and ignores an unknown `router_settings`\nkey, so a config that looks correct silently does nothing, which is why\n`//tests:bats_litellm_config_test` rejects it by name)."},"1324":{"id":"/litellm-routing-67","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias   | Model                | Backend                                                                                | Structured tool calls |\n| ------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `agent` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` (TensorFold on :8888 - one deployment per Spark box, optional) | yes (measured)        |","table":"table-0"},"1325":{"id":"/litellm-routing-68","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias       | Model                | Backend                                                                          | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ----------- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-low` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `low` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1326":{"id":"/litellm-routing-69","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias          | Model                | Backend                                                                             | Structured tool calls                 |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-medium` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `medium` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1327":{"id":"/litellm-routing-70","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias         | Model                | Backend                                                                            | Structured tool calls                 |\n| ------------- | -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `agent-xhigh` | `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` | the same three Spark boxes as `agent`, as its own group; thinking baked to `xhigh` | yes (same model and boxes as `agent`) |","table":"table-0"},"1328":{"id":"/litellm-routing-71","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias   | Model               | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------- | ------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `coder` | `qwen2.5-coder:32b` |         | no (measured)         |","table":"table-0"},"1329":{"id":"/litellm-routing-72","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias        | Model               | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------ | ------------------- | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `coder-fast` | `qwen2.5-coder:14b` |         | no (measured)         |","table":"table-0"},"1330":{"id":"/litellm-routing-73","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias  | Model        | Backend | Structured tool calls |\n| ------ | ------------ | ------- | --------------------- |\n| `chat` | `qwen2.5:7b` |         | yes (measured)        |","table":"table-0"},"1331":{"id":"/litellm-routing-74","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias         | Model               | Backend                                      | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------- | ------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `spark-coder` | `qwen2.5-coder:32b` | `SPARK1_OLLAMA_URL` (NVIDIA Spark, optional) | unverified            |","table":"table-0"},"1332":{"id":"/litellm-routing-75","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-alias-table","content":"| Alias        | Model        | Backend                                      | Structured tool calls |\n| ------------ | ------------ | -------------------------------------------- | --------------------- |\n| `spark-chat` | `qwen2.5:7b` | `SPARK2_OLLAMA_URL` (NVIDIA Spark, optional) | unverified            |","table":"table-0"},"1333":{"id":"/litellm-routing-76","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `agent` row is ONE model group with three deployments, one per Spark box,\nall equal."},"1334":{"id":"/litellm-routing-77","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The three `agent-*` rows are that same lane with a thinking level baked into\n`litellm_params.reasoning_effort`. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and\n`xhigh` and nothing else - there is no `high` - so the level is a name to pick\nrather than a knob to turn, and `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` are\nhow the level reaches Coder Agents: its model dropdown chooses an alias, not a\nrequest parameter."},"1335":{"id":"/litellm-routing-78","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`agent` is not the unthinking default. It bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`, so a\nCoder Agents chat on the default alias thinks hard, and `agent-xhigh` asks the\nbox for the same level by name. A `reasoning_effort` the client sends in the\nrequest still wins over the baked one."},"1336":{"id":"/litellm-routing-79","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"Those three groups reuse the same `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` / `SPARK_n_API_KEY`\npairs as `agent`. They are separate model groups on the same hardware, not three\nmore boxes: routing state is per group, so least-busy's counters and the session\npin are computed inside a group and two chats on different aliases can land on the\nsame box at once. What does not change is the box - 5 full 262144-token streams in\ntotal, whichever aliases arrive on it. This is not the \"two deployments on one\nbox\" mistake below, which is about a single group counting two URLs on one machine\nas two machines."},"1337":{"id":"/litellm-routing-80","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `spark-*` rows are a different thing: the same Qwen2.5-Coder and Qwen2.5\nweights, served over Ollama on the Spark boxes instead of on the Arc."},"1338":{"id":"/litellm-routing-81","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`unverified` on the `spark-*` rows means exactly that: this box never ran them\nagainst a live Spark, so the column records the absence of a measurement, not a\n\"no\"."},"1339":{"id":"/litellm-routing-82","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"`model:` is sent to the backend verbatim, so `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` has to match the\nserved id byte-for-byte, case and hyphens included."},"1340":{"id":"/litellm-routing-83","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#reading-the-table","content":"The `agent` lane deliberately carries the `openai/` provider prefix. `ollama_chat/`\nwould route those boxes through the Ollama-specific code path, and `api_base` has to\ninclude `/v1`."},"1341":{"id":"/litellm-routing-84","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"The Qwen2.5-Coder weights do not emit structured tool calls on this runtime."},"1342":{"id":"/litellm-routing-85","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"Measured against this box's IPEX-LLM Ollama, whose bundled `ollama` is 0.9.3."},"1343":{"id":"/litellm-routing-86","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"A request carrying `tools` for `qwen2.5-coder:32b` or `:14b` comes back as plain text,\n`finish_reason: \"stop\"`, and no `tool_calls` field at all."},"1344":{"id":"/litellm-routing-87","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"It behaves that way on both `/api/chat` and `/v1/chat/completions`."},"1345":{"id":"/litellm-routing-88","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"The model writes the JSON call into the message body, which no agent driver can consume."},"1346":{"id":"/litellm-routing-89","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"`qwen2.5:7b` on the same runtime does return a real `tool_calls` array."},"1347":{"id":"/litellm-routing-90","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-was-measured","content":"So the limitation tracks the model, not the runtime."},"1348":{"id":"/litellm-routing-91","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"Agent mode therefore needs either the Spark `agent` lane or the small `chat` model."},"1349":{"id":"/litellm-routing-92","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"Grok Build, Cline, Roo and Kilo all default to `agent`."},"1350":{"id":"/litellm-routing-93","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-agent-mode-needs","content":"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, for the same reason: an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or\nedit, so registering `coder` or `coder-fast` would only add a visibly broken model to\nthe pickers. The four Spark aliases all drive tools, so all four are registerable - the\nthinking aliases are there to make the level selectable from that picker."},"1351":{"id":"/litellm-routing-94","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"Three deployments, one per Spark box, and no `order:` tier. A tier would make the\nlower boxes unreachable while any tier-1 box stayed healthy."},"1352":{"id":"/litellm-routing-95","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"Spreading across every reachable box is the point of running three of them, five full\n262144-token streams each. Pinning only narrows the candidate list first."},"1353":{"id":"/litellm-routing-96","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#one-group-three-deployments","content":"One endpoint per box, one box per endpoint. Never point two deployments, or two\naliases, at the same physical machine: the proxy counts deployments, not machines."},"1354":{"id":"/litellm-routing-97","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"`least-busy` picks the healthy deployment carrying the fewest in-flight requests."},"1355":{"id":"/litellm-routing-98","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"Its counters are per deployment and expire after a hardcoded 3600 s; there is no\n`routing_strategy_args` block to set, and `ttl` there would be a paste from newer docs."},"1356":{"id":"/litellm-routing-99","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"It decides only for unpinned traffic: the affinity pin below resolves first, and\n`least-busy` runs when nothing is pinned, when the pinned deployment is unhealthy or in\ncooldown, and for any request with no session id."},"1357":{"id":"/litellm-routing-100","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"Health checks, cooldowns and those counters all attribute per deployment through\n`model_info.id`, which is why each box carries a unique id: `agent-spark-1`,\n`agent-spark-2`, `agent-spark-3`. Each thinking group has its own triple -\n`agent-low-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-medium-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-xhigh-spark-1/2/3` - so the\nsame physical box is a separate deployment, with its own health state and its own\nin-flight counter, inside every group it belongs to."},"1358":{"id":"/litellm-routing-101","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-picker-least-busy","content":"A cooled box therefore stays visibly out of `healthy_deployments` instead of blending\ninto the group's aggregate stats."},"1359":{"id":"/litellm-routing-102","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"`session_affinity` sits above the picker, in `router_settings.optional_pre_call_checks`,\nand it arms the router's `DeploymentAffinityCheck`. Checked on LiteLLM 1.102.1."},"1360":{"id":"/litellm-routing-103","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"One conversation from one client sticks to the box that answered its first turn, for as\nlong as that box stays healthy."},"1361":{"id":"/litellm-routing-104","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"The pin idles out after 1 h (3600 s) without a request, and every request refreshes it.\nIt bounds idle time between requests, not conversation length."},"1362":{"id":"/litellm-routing-105","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"A conversation that goes quiet for longer than the TTL may move."},"1363":{"id":"/litellm-routing-106","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"A busy pinned box sheds its own overflow in its own scheduler instead of queueing\ninside the proxy."},"1364":{"id":"/litellm-routing-107","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-pin-session_affinity","content":"The trade is deliberate: stickiness buys prefix-cache locality and one predictable box\nfor the whole conversation, so one workspace's agent loop can sit on a busy box."},"1365":{"id":"/litellm-routing-108","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"A value has to look like an id to count: alphanumerics, hyphens or underscores, 8\ncharacters or more. The sources, in order, are `x-litellm-trace-id`, then\n`x-litellm-session-id`, then any `x-<vendor>-session-id`, then\n`session-id`/`session_id`/`thread-id`/`conversation_id` (Codex style), then W3C\ntraceparent and baggage, plus `metadata.session_id` in the body."},"1366":{"id":"/litellm-routing-109","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"The Grok CLI forwards `extra_headers` verbatim."},"1367":{"id":"/litellm-routing-110","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"Both workspace templates render `x-litellm-session-id = \"ws-<workspace-uuid>\"` under\n`[models]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`, so one key covers every conversation of a\nworkspace, subagent calls included."},"1368":{"id":"/litellm-routing-111","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-carries-a-session-id","content":"In `shared` profile mode that is one file per user, re-rendered by the last-booted\nworkspace, which then shares that key across that user's workspaces."},"1369":{"id":"/litellm-routing-112","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Cline and Roo editor traffic carries no session id: the extension settings have no\ncustom-header field."},"1370":{"id":"/litellm-routing-113","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Coder Agents chats carry none either. Coder's aibridge forwards model calls with the\nprovider key only and adds no per-conversation session id."},"1371":{"id":"/litellm-routing-114","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#what-floats-freely","content":"Both float across the healthy boxes by load."},"1372":{"id":"/litellm-routing-115","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"That request routes elsewhere, to whichever box `least-busy` picks."},"1373":{"id":"/litellm-routing-116","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"The pin itself is kept."},"1374":{"id":"/litellm-routing-117","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#when-a-pinned-box-cools-or-goes-unhealthy","content":"The conversation goes back to the box once it is healthy again."},"1375":{"id":"/litellm-routing-118","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"When every Spark box is down, `router_settings.fallbacks` degrades `agent` straight to\n`coder`, which is text-only."},"1376":{"id":"/litellm-routing-119","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"Chat stays usable while agent mode quietly loses its tools. That is the point of the\nhop, not an accident."},"1377":{"id":"/litellm-routing-120","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"Each thinking alias carries the same hop of its own - `agent-low: [\"coder\"]`, and the\nsame for `agent-medium` and `agent-xhigh`. Groups are independent, so none of them\ninherits `agent`'s entry, and all four go down with exactly the same boxes."},"1378":{"id":"/litellm-routing-121","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"It used to be a chain, `agent -> agent-fast -> coder`. `agent-fast` was a second alias\nfor the same model on a second box with a lower output ceiling; one group of three equal\ndeployments already covers every reachable box, so the chain collapsed to one acyclic\nhop."},"1379":{"id":"/litellm-routing-122","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"If a concurrency cap is ever reintroduced, its pairing is `fallbacks` plus\n`context_window_fallbacks`. Both, not one."},"1380":{"id":"/litellm-routing-123","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#fallback-agent-to-coder","content":"There are no rpm, tpm or `max_parallel_requests` caps anywhere, on purpose. A cap parks\nrequests behind an in-memory semaphore inside the proxy; without one, a fully offered\nbox sheds load in its own scheduler."},"1381":{"id":"/litellm-routing-124","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"The dashboard's **Agent backend** card exists to surface exactly that failure."},"1382":{"id":"/litellm-routing-125","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"Its per-box widgets go to error state while `agent` still answers, because `agent`\nanswers from the Arc while every Spark is down."},"1383":{"id":"/litellm-routing-126","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-agent-backend-card","content":"The card and its per-box widgets read `AGENT_MODEL_URL` and `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` from\n`.env`, so they follow the LAN, and they answer without a key."},"1384":{"id":"/litellm-routing-127","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"The `x-litellm-model-id` response header names the deployment that answered."},"1385":{"id":"/litellm-routing-128","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"With a pin, it names the pinned box; without one, whichever box `least-busy` picked."},"1386":{"id":"/litellm-routing-129","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"The proxy's own `/health` lists every deployment, so trust it over the absence of\nboot errors."},"1387":{"id":"/litellm-routing-130","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#which-box-answered","content":"A wrong model id does not crash the proxy: 1.102.1 builds the Router with\n`ignore_invalid_deployments` forced to True, so a bad id drops that deployment at boot\nwith one log line and later surfaces as \"No deployments available\"."},"1388":{"id":"/litellm-routing-131","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"Measured on a harness boot of this config against the live Spark boxes, before\n`session_affinity` existed."},"1389":{"id":"/litellm-routing-132","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"That 30-request burst was multi-session, fan-out-style traffic."},"1390":{"id":"/litellm-routing-133","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"It spread 12/10/8 across the three deployments and returned 30x 200."},"1391":{"id":"/litellm-routing-134","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"With no concurrency cap configured, the proxy neither queued nor shed inside itself,\nand a fully offered box absorbed the overflow in its own scheduler."},"1392":{"id":"/litellm-routing-135","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-measured-burst","content":"A single conversation pinned to one box now keeps its streams on that box while it\nstays healthy - that is the behaviour the burst predates."},"1393":{"id":"/litellm-routing-136","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Edit `litellm/config.yaml`."},"1394":{"id":"/litellm-routing-137","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Restart the proxy on the stack you changed, so it re-reads that file."},"1395":{"id":"/litellm-routing-138","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Check that `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY` and `SPARK_3_API_KEY` are still\nnon-empty. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy, before it ever reaches\nthe box."},"1396":{"id":"/litellm-routing-139","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"Neither stack runner has a `restart` subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`\nare the whole set), so the proxy restart stays a direct compose call. Stable stack:"},"1397":{"id":"/litellm-routing-140","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#editing-an-alias","content":"The dev sandbox takes the same restart with its own project name and env file:"},"1398":{"id":"/litellm-routing-141","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as the Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, through `CODER_AGENTS_PROVIDER_URL`\n(default `http://litellm:4000/v1`)."},"1399":{"id":"/litellm-routing-142","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#where-an-alias-edit-lands","content":"So an alias edited or deleted in `litellm/config.yaml` moves in the Agents model\ndropdowns exactly as it does in the CLI."},"1400":{"id":"/litellm-routing-143","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"The IPEX-LLM runtime is Intel's XPU path (oneAPI / Level Zero) for the Arc stack."},"1401":{"id":"/litellm-routing-144","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"`OLLAMA_NUM_GPU=999` offloads every layer to the 140T, and the iGPU pulls its weights\nstraight out of the 96 GB DDR5."},"1402":{"id":"/litellm-routing-145","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"Vanilla Ollama on an Intel iGPU is effectively CPU-bound, and several times slower on a\n32B coder model."},"1403":{"id":"/litellm-routing-146","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#ipex-llm-over-vanilla-ollama","content":"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in the ollama logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."},"1404":{"id":"/litellm-routing-147","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#moe-over-dense","content":"`qwen2.5-coder:32b` is treated here as a Mixture-of-Experts model: 32B-class quality\nwith far fewer active parameters per token."},"1405":{"id":"/litellm-routing-148","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#moe-over-dense","content":"On a memory-rich but bandwidth- and latency-constrained iGPU that is the sweet spot,\nbecause an equivalent dense model would move every parameter per token and run much\nslower at the same footprint."},"1406":{"id":"/litellm-routing-149","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"Workspaces see a stable OpenAI-compatible URL and stable alias names."},"1407":{"id":"/litellm-routing-150","page_id":"/litellm-routing","tags":["litellm","models","routing","spark"],"type":"text","url":"/litellm-routing#the-one-front-door-rule","content":"So you can flip `coder` from the local Arc to a Spark box, or add a new alias, and no\nworkspace setting has to change."},"1408":{"id":"/luks-unlock","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"page","content":"Unlock an encrypted host remotely","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock"},"1409":{"id":"/luks-unlock-0","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock","content":"Configure the stack host so a reboot with an encrypted root can be answered from a laptop, and prove the boot image can do it before you reboot."},"1410":{"id":"/luks-unlock-1","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Unlock an encrypted host remotely"},"1411":{"id":"/luks-unlock-2","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#the-command-that-matters","content":"The command that matters"},"1412":{"id":"/luks-unlock-3","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"What install writes"},"1413":{"id":"/luks-unlock-4","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Unlocking from a laptop"},"1414":{"id":"/luks-unlock-5","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"What the check catches"},"1415":{"id":"/luks-unlock-6","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#static-networks","content":"Static networks"},"1416":{"id":"/luks-unlock-7","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#two-keys-two-purposes","content":"Two keys, two purposes"},"1417":{"id":"/luks-unlock-8","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"What this deliberately does not do"},"1418":{"id":"/luks-unlock-9","page_id":"/luks-unlock","type":"heading","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"Where to look next"},"1419":{"id":"/luks-unlock-10","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1420":{"id":"/luks-unlock-11","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Why a reboot is the dangerous moment on an encrypted headless box"},"1421":{"id":"/luks-unlock-12","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"The one command that answers \"can I unlock this from a laptop\""},"1422":{"id":"/luks-unlock-13","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"What `install` writes, and which file each line belongs to"},"1423":{"id":"/luks-unlock-14","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"The unlock itself, step by step, from the operator's own machine"},"1424":{"id":"/luks-unlock-15","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"What the check catches, and what it deliberately leaves alone"},"1425":{"id":"/luks-unlock-16","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"**What this enables**"},"1426":{"id":"/luks-unlock-17","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Rebooting a server nobody is standing next to"},"1427":{"id":"/luks-unlock-18","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Catching a config that looks right and boots dead, before the reboot"},"1428":{"id":"/luks-unlock-19","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"Reading the exact ssh command and host key fingerprint to expect"},"1429":{"id":"/luks-unlock-20","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"An Ubuntu server with a LUKS root stops in the initramfs on every reboot and\nwaits for a passphrase. With no keyboard attached, that wait is the outage: the\nmachine is up, the disks are unreadable, and the only fix is a person at the\nconsole. The distro answer is to move an SSH server and cryptsetup's unlock\nprompt into the initramfs, so the passphrase is typed over SSH from anywhere."},"1430":{"id":"/luks-unlock-21","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlock-an-encrypted-host-remotely","content":"`//scripts:luks-unlock` writes that config and then proves the generated\ninitramfs really contains the unlock path. The proof is the point: every one of\nthe six settings involved fails by staying silent until the boot that needs it."},"1431":{"id":"/luks-unlock-22","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#the-command-that-matters","content":"That is `check`, the default verb, and it writes nothing. It answers one\nquestion - if this box rebooted right now, could you decrypt it from a laptop -\nand closes with either `ok: this box can be unlocked from a laptop after a\nreboot` or `error: do not reboot yet - N blocker(s)`."},"1432":{"id":"/luks-unlock-23","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#the-command-that-matters","content":"Run it before every reboot of an encrypted box, and after any kernel or\n`initramfs-tools` update. It exits `1` when something blocks the unlock, so it\nfits in front of a maintenance script as a gate."},"1433":{"id":"/luks-unlock-24","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"`install` installs the two packages, writes the config, regenerates the\ninitramfs, and then runs the same check over what it produced. Add `--dry-run`\nfirst and every write and command is printed as `would: ...` instead of\nexecuted. Nothing reboots."},"1434":{"id":"/luks-unlock-25","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                  | Line it writes | Why it matters                                                                                              |\n| ------------------------------------- | -------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/cryptsetup-initramfs/conf-hook` | `CRYPTSETUP=y` | Without it the cryptroot scripts never enter the boot image, so there is nothing to type a passphrase into. |","table":"table-0"},"1435":{"id":"/luks-unlock-26","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                         | Line it writes                                                          | Why it matters                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| -------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/initramfs-tools/conf.d/zz-luks-unlock` | `DROPBEAR=y`, `IP=`, `UMASK=0077`, and `HOOKS=` when a list is in force | One file this CLI owns, named to sort last so it wins over every other drop-in. `IP=` is what gives the boot image an address; `UMASK=0077` keeps the packed key out of unprivileged hands. |","table":"table-0"},"1436":{"id":"/luks-unlock-27","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                      | Line it writes               | Why it matters                                                                                               |\n| ----------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `/etc/dropbear/initramfs/authorized_keys` | the operator's public key(s) | Password logins are off in the initramfs. No key there means the SSH server refuses everyone, including you. |","table":"table-0"},"1437":{"id":"/luks-unlock-28","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                    | Line it writes                              | Why it matters                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/dropbear/initramfs/dropbear.conf` | `DROPBEAR_OPTIONS=\"-p 2222 -s -I 60 -j -k\"` | `-s` keeps password logins off, `-I` bounds a session someone walked away from, and the port stays off the installed sshd's so the different host keys do not look like an attack. An existing `DROPBEAR_OPTIONS` is left alone. |","table":"table-0"},"1438":{"id":"/luks-unlock-29","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"| File                                          | Line it writes                               | Why it matters                                                                                 |\n| --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `/etc/dropbear/initramfs/dropbear_*_host_key` | generated with `dropbearkey` when none exist | dropbear refuses to start with no host key, and the package only creates them at install time. |","table":"table-0"},"1439":{"id":"/luks-unlock-30","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-install-writes","content":"The order is deliberate: the hook packs the authorized key at generation time,\nso a key written after `update-initramfs` is not in the image. That is why\n`install` ends by reading the image back."},"1440":{"id":"/luks-unlock-31","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Pin the identity while the box is still up, so a stranger's key cannot be\nmistaken for it later:"},"1441":{"id":"/luks-unlock-32","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Reboot the box."},"1442":{"id":"/luks-unlock-33","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Wait a minute, then connect on the initramfs port. The check prints the\ncommand; it keeps its own `known_hosts` on purpose:"},"1443":{"id":"/luks-unlock-34","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"Type the passphrase:"},"1444":{"id":"/luks-unlock-35","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#unlocking-from-a-laptop","content":"The boot continues on its own once the mapping appears - no second reboot. If\nthe SSH connection is refused, the box is still in the initramfs or before it:\nthe boot does not proceed past an unmapped root, so waiting is safe and the\nconsole (out-of-band console, IPMI, or the provider's serial console) still\nshows where it stopped."},"1445":{"id":"/luks-unlock-36","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"Every row below is a `check` line you can get instead of an `ok`, and each one\nhas booted dead in the field."},"1446":{"id":"/luks-unlock-37","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                             | Cause                  | Fix                                                                                               |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `no initramfs address (IP is unset, none, or off)` | nothing ever set `IP=` | `install --ip dhcp`, or `--iface ens3` to pin one NIC, or a raw netboot spec for a static network |","table":"table-1"},"1447":{"id":"/luks-unlock-38","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                          | Cause                                                   | Fix                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HOOKS leaves out net dropbear` | a hand-written `HOOKS=` list that predates the packages | `install` merges the four names it needs into whatever list is in force |","table":"table-1"},"1448":{"id":"/luks-unlock-39","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                 | Cause                                                       | Fix                                                            |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `the boot image holds no bin/dropbear` | the package was installed after the last `update-initramfs` | re-run `install` so the image is regenerated after the install |","table":"table-1"},"1449":{"id":"/luks-unlock-40","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                             | Cause                                                                              | Fix                                                                                        |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `no authorized key was packed into the boot image` | the key was written after the image, or in a path the installed hook does not read | re-run `install`; the CLI asks the installed hook which path it reads rather than assuming |","table":"table-1"},"1450":{"id":"/luks-unlock-41","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                              | Cause                         | Fix                                          |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `the key body holds characters that are not base64` | a pasted key wrapped mid-body | re-paste on one line; `check` names the line |","table":"table-1"},"1451":{"id":"/luks-unlock-42","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                                          | Cause                                                          | Fix                                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `key file /etc/keys/root.key is not covered by KEYFILE_PATTERN` | the crypttab key lives on disk and never reaches the initramfs | add a `KEYFILE_PATTERN` to `conf-hook`, or move that volume to a passphrase |","table":"table-1"},"1452":{"id":"/luks-unlock-43","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                            | Cause                                                              | Fix                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `MODULES=dep packs only drivers loaded right now` | the NIC driver is not loaded today, so it will be missing tomorrow | name the driver in `/etc/initramfs-tools/modules`, or go back to `MODULES=most` |","table":"table-1"},"1453":{"id":"/luks-unlock-44","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                                                 | Cause                                                 | Fix                                                                     |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `the initramfs listens on 2222, the same port the installed sshd uses` | the two daemons share a port with different host keys | pick another `--port`, and keep using the separate `UserKnownHostsFile` |","table":"table-1"},"1454":{"id":"/luks-unlock-45","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"| Report                                             | Cause                                  | Fix                                              |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `an encrypted /boot needs GRUB to read the initrd` | `/boot` is inside the encrypted volume | verify GRUB can read it before trusting a reboot |","table":"table-1"},"1455":{"id":"/luks-unlock-46","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-the-check-catches","content":"The last one is worth dwelling on: `check` can read every config file and still\nbe looking at an image GRUB cannot load, so an encrypted `/boot` is reported as\na warning to verify by hand rather than cleared."},"1456":{"id":"/luks-unlock-47","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#static-networks","content":"A box with no DHCP gets the raw initramfs form, which is passed through\nuntouched because `initramfs-tools` parses it, not this CLI:"},"1457":{"id":"/luks-unlock-48","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#static-networks","content":"`--iface ens3` with `--ip dhcp` produces the same pinned-device shape with\nDHCP kept. A name the kernel never assigned is a blocker, not a typo you\ndiscover after the reboot: `check` compares it against `/sys/class/net`."},"1458":{"id":"/luks-unlock-49","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#two-keys-two-purposes","content":"The unlock key authorizes a root shell on a machine whose disks are still\nsealed, and the initramfs image holding it sits on an unencrypted `/boot`.\nAnyone who can read `/boot` can read that public key, so treat it as a\nseparate credential with its own purpose: a dedicated key whose private half\nlives on the laptop you bring to a reboot, not the key you push to every\nrepository. `UMASK=0077` keeps the image itself out of unprivileged hands, and\nthe initramfs host keys stay separate from `/etc/ssh` on purpose - they are\ngenerated per boot image, and sharing them would leak which key the sealed\nmachine answers to."},"1459":{"id":"/luks-unlock-50","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Reboot anything.** The reboot is the operator's decision, made after a\ngreen `check`."},"1460":{"id":"/luks-unlock-51","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Edit `/etc/crypttab` or the LUKS headers.** The volumes are read to decide\nwhether the boot can open them. Key slots, passphrases and headers are never\nwritten, so this CLI cannot be the thing that destroys a key."},"1461":{"id":"/luks-unlock-52","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Enroll a TPM.** `systemd-cryptenroll` is a different design with a\ndifferent failure mode - a firmware update that changes the PCR policy seals\nthe box with nobody in the loop - and it belongs in its own change."},"1462":{"id":"/luks-unlock-53","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#what-this-deliberately-does-not-do","content":"**Add an auto-reboot watchdog.** A box sitting in the initramfs with dropbear\nlistening is the recoverable state; rebooting on a timer destroys the\nrecovery window and can turn one bad boot into a loop. `-I` bounds the\nsession, and the listener stays."},"1463":{"id":"/luks-unlock-54","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"troubleshooting.md for the symptom-first list once the\nbox is back up."},"1464":{"id":"/luks-unlock-55","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"public-access.md for what the box exposes on the network\nafter it boots - the initramfs listener is gone by then."},"1465":{"id":"/luks-unlock-56","page_id":"/luks-unlock","tags":["operate","luks","disk-encryption","boot","headless","ssh"],"type":"text","url":"/luks-unlock#where-to-look-next","content":"operate/index.md for the rest of the day-to-day runbooks."},"1466":{"id":"/new-repo","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"page","content":"New projects from the template","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo"},"1467":{"id":"/new-repo-0","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo","content":"How scripts/new-repo.sh fetches the ezrepo scaffold from GitHub, renders it into a new repository, and what the Coder side adds."},"1468":{"id":"/new-repo-1","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"New projects from the template"},"1469":{"id":"/new-repo-2","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"Two kinds of template"},"1470":{"id":"/new-repo-3","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"What the scaffold ships"},"1471":{"id":"/new-repo-4","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"Lanes"},"1472":{"id":"/new-repo-5","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Rendering a repository"},"1473":{"id":"/new-repo-6","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"What each GitHub step needs"},"1474":{"id":"/new-repo-7","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"Coder side"},"1475":{"id":"/new-repo-8","page_id":"/new-repo","type":"heading","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"Where this is enforced"},"1476":{"id":"/new-repo-9","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1477":{"id":"/new-repo-10","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Two different things called \"template\", and which one this page is about"},"1478":{"id":"/new-repo-11","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"What the scaffold repository ships, and the account-wide gaps it closes"},"1479":{"id":"/new-repo-12","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Where the scaffold is fetched from, and how to pin or replace it"},"1480":{"id":"/new-repo-13","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Rendering a repository with `new-repo`, its flags, and its lanes"},"1481":{"id":"/new-repo-14","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Which GitHub steps need which App permission, and what the CLI does when one is refused"},"1482":{"id":"/new-repo-15","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"The `New project` workspace preset and the optional Coder Task"},"1483":{"id":"/new-repo-16","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Where all of this is enforced"},"1484":{"id":"/new-repo-17","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"**What this enables**"},"1485":{"id":"/new-repo-18","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Starting a new repository with the quality gate already in place, in one command"},"1486":{"id":"/new-repo-19","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Getting the same scaffold on a laptop, in CI, and from a workspace"},"1487":{"id":"/new-repo-20","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Pinning a scaffold revision, or rendering a local tree with no network at all"},"1488":{"id":"/new-repo-21","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#new-projects-from-the-template","content":"Telling a refused GitHub permission apart from a broken render"},"1489":{"id":"/new-repo-22","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"This repository already uses the word *template* for a **Coder template**: the Terraform tree\nunder `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` that `push-template.sh` registers with\nthe Coder server."},"1490":{"id":"/new-repo-23","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"The other *template* is a **project scaffold**: a tree of files that `scripts/new-repo.sh` copies,\nsubstitutes, and hands to `git init` as the first commit of a brand new repository. It is not\nvendored here. It lives in its own repository -- by default `https://github.com/toxicoder/ezrepo`\n-- and this repo's whole job is to fetch that tree and render it. The scaffold is a product with\nits own CI and its own release rhythm, and a vendored copy drifts from the day it is committed, so\nnothing here is kept in step with it."},"1491":{"id":"/new-repo-24","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#two-kinds-of-template","content":"The coupling runs one way: this repo may name the scaffold repository; the scaffold repository\nnever names this one. And the scaffold's quality gate is its own -- the `.self-check/` suite and\nthe CI that runs it in that repository -- so a scaffold change is tested where it is made, and a\nlane or a file the scaffold adds needs no change here."},"1492":{"id":"/new-repo-25","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"These files belong to the scaffold repository. This repo does not ship them, lint them, or keep a\ncopy: it fetches them at run time and renders them. The scaffold is language-neutral at the core,\nwith optional per-language lanes. Everything below was chosen from what is measurably missing\nacross the account, not from taste: across a sample of eleven ordinary projects, `SECURITY.md`,\n`CONTRIBUTING.md`, issue templates, a pull-request template, `dependabot.yml`, `CODEOWNERS` and\n`.editorconfig` were each present in none of them, and no repository in the surveyed set had a\nsingle release or tag."},"1493":{"id":"/new-repo-26","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area      | What ships                                                                                                                      |\n| --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Community | `SECURITY.md` with a supported-versions table and private-report route, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, `CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` |","table":"table-0"},"1494":{"id":"/new-repo-27","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area   | What ships                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| GitHub | `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `release.yml`, `security.yml`, `dependabot.yml`, `CODEOWNERS`, issue templates with blank issues disabled, a PR template with a Safety/ops-impact section |","table":"table-0"},"1495":{"id":"/new-repo-28","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area    | What ships                                                                                                                                    |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Hygiene | `.editorconfig`, `.gitattributes`, `.gitignore`, `env.example` (the render writes it out as `.env.example`), `.yamllint.yml`, `.shellcheckrc` |","table":"table-0"},"1496":{"id":"/new-repo-29","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area  | What ships                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ----- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Gates | `.pre-commit-config.yaml` whose local hooks **fail** when a tool is missing, plus `tests/run.sh`, a dispatcher that errors rather than skipping when a runner is absent |","table":"table-0"},"1497":{"id":"/new-repo-30","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area | What ships                                                                                       |\n| ---- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Pins | `tool-versions.env`, read by the dev container, `make doctor`, and CI, so the three cannot drift |","table":"table-0"},"1498":{"id":"/new-repo-31","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area           | What ships                                                                              |\n| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Agent contract | `AGENTS.md` with the cost-tiered \"which check first\" table and the path-to-test mapping |","table":"table-0"},"1499":{"id":"/new-repo-32","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area | What ships                                                                                                 |\n| ---- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Docs | `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/project-conventions.md`, and `docs/adr/` with a template plus one worked ADR |","table":"table-0"},"1500":{"id":"/new-repo-33","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"| Area      | What ships                                                           |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Packaging | a multi-stage non-root `Dockerfile` and a separate `Dockerfile.test` |","table":"table-0"},"1501":{"id":"/new-repo-34","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-the-scaffold-ships","content":"The rule that ties it together: **a check that silently skips is a bug**. Two existing\nrepositories print `tool not found ... Skipping` and then exit zero, and a pre-commit config in\nanother wraps every hook in `command -v ... || exit 0`. Nothing in the scaffold may do that, and\nthe scaffold's own `.self-check/` suite is what holds the line."},"1502":{"id":"/new-repo-35","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"Lane ids are not declared here. `scripts/new-repo.sh` discovers them from the fetched tree's\n`.template-lanes/` directory at run time, so a lane the scaffold repository adds needs no change\nin this repo. The four below are what it ships today:"},"1503":{"id":"/new-repo-36","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane              | Adds                                                                                |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `shell` (default) | `src/main.sh`, a pure helper under `src/lib/`, Bats suites, `scripts/lint-shell.sh` |","table":"table-1"},"1504":{"id":"/new-repo-37","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane     | Adds                                                                                                    |\n| -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `python` | `pyproject.toml` with Ruff and `fail_under = 100`, `mypy.ini` at strict, a package under `src/`, pytest |","table":"table-1"},"1505":{"id":"/new-repo-38","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane         | Adds                                                                        |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `typescript` | ESLint 9 flat config, Prettier, Vitest with 100% thresholds, `tsc --noEmit` |","table":"table-1"},"1506":{"id":"/new-repo-39","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"| Lane | Adds                                                                               |\n| ---- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `go` | `go.mod`, `.golangci.yml`, table-driven tests, `go vet`, `gosec`, a coverage floor |","table":"table-1"},"1507":{"id":"/new-repo-40","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#lanes","content":"Each lane lives at `<scaffold>/.template-lanes/<lane>/`. A file there is copied over the rendered\ntree at the same path, so a lane may add files or replace a core one. `LANE.md` and\n`gitignore.fragment` are lane metadata: the renderer appends the fragment to `.gitignore` and drops\nboth from the output. Ask for a lane the fetched tree does not have and the run dies naming the\nlanes it does offer."},"1508":{"id":"/new-repo-41","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"The scaffold is fetched before anything is rendered: a shallow (`--depth 1`), single-branch clone\nof `--template-ref` into a scratch directory an EXIT trap removes. `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` and an\nempty credential helper keep a scaffold that turned private from hanging on a prompt nobody can\nanswer inside a workspace. A fetched tree with no `template.yml` at its root is a hard failure --\nthat manifest belongs to the scaffold repository, so pick a ref that has it. The new repository's\nfirst commit records what it was built from: `- Scaffold: <url>@<sha>.`, or\n`- Scaffold: local tree <dir>.` for a `--template-dir` render."},"1509":{"id":"/new-repo-42","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag        | Effect                                   |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `--dry-run` | render, report, create nothing on GitHub |","table":"table-2"},"1510":{"id":"/new-repo-43","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                                                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--out <dir>` | parent directory for the rendered repository (default: the current directory) |","table":"table-2"},"1511":{"id":"/new-repo-44","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag        | Effect                         |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------ |\n| `--no-push` | render and commit locally only |","table":"table-2"},"1512":{"id":"/new-repo-45","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                 |\n| ------------- | ---------------------- |\n| `--no-remote` | skip every GitHub step |","table":"table-2"},"1513":{"id":"/new-repo-46","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag        | Effect                        |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `--private` | create the repository private |","table":"table-2"},"1514":{"id":"/new-repo-47","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag            | Effect                           |\n| --------------- | -------------------------------- |\n| `--as-template` | mark it as a template repository |","table":"table-2"},"1515":{"id":"/new-repo-48","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                                             | Effect                                                        |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--description`, `--owner`, `--holder`, `--year` | token values; the owner defaults to the authenticated account |","table":"table-2"},"1516":{"id":"/new-repo-49","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--lane <id>` | add a lane; repeatable, default `shell`. Ids are discovered from the fetched scaffold, so a lane the scaffold adds needs no change here. `--lane none` clears the lanes named so far and renders the bare core, whose `make test` asks for a lane |","table":"table-2"},"1517":{"id":"/new-repo-50","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag          | Effect                  |\n| ------------- | ----------------------- |\n| `--topic <t>` | extra topic; repeatable |","table":"table-2"},"1518":{"id":"/new-repo-51","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                   | Effect                                                                                                |\n| ---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--template-url <url>` | scaffold repository to clone (default: `$EZREPO_URL`, else `https://github.com/toxicoder/ezrepo.git`) |","table":"table-2"},"1519":{"id":"/new-repo-52","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                   | Effect                                                                                            |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--template-ref <ref>` | branch or tag to fetch (default: `$EZREPO_REF`, else `main`). Pin a tag for a reproducible render |","table":"table-2"},"1520":{"id":"/new-repo-53","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                   | Effect                                                                                                  |\n| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `--template-dir <dir>` | render this local tree and fetch nothing (default: `$EZREPO_DIR`); `--template-ref` has no effect on it |","table":"table-2"},"1521":{"id":"/new-repo-54","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag                            | Effect |\n| ------------------------------- | ------ |\n| `--task`, `--task-template <n>` |        |","table":"table-2"},"1522":{"id":"/new-repo-55","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"| Flag              | Effect                            |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| `--branch <name>` | default branch, `main` by default |","table":"table-2"},"1523":{"id":"/new-repo-56","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"The three scaffold flags exist so the source can be pointed elsewhere without editing anything:\nthe env pair covers a mirror or a fork for a whole deployment, and `--template-dir` renders a tree\nyou already hold, which is the offline route and the one the hermetic tests use."},"1524":{"id":"/new-repo-57","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"The run exits 0 when the render succeeded -- a refused GitHub step is a `warning:`, not a failure --\nand 1 on a render failure, a bad argument, or a scaffold that cannot be fetched."},"1525":{"id":"/new-repo-58","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Placeholders are substituted in file contents *and* in file and directory names:\n`__PROJECT_NAME__`, `__PROJECT_SLUG__`, `__PROJECT_MODULE__`, `__PROJECT_DESCRIPTION__`,\n`__PROJECT_OWNER__`, `__PROJECT_LICENSE_HOLDER__`, `__PROJECT_YEAR__`. Those seven are named once,\nin `NEW_REPO_TOKENS`, and they are the whole contract between this script and the scaffold -- a\nceiling rather than an equality, so the template may use a subset of them. A token that survives is\na hard failure, not a warning -- a repository that ships with its placeholders showing is worse\nthan no template at all."},"1526":{"id":"/new-repo-59","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Markdown carries a token in a second spelling. Prettier reads a bare `__PROJECT_NAME__` as strong\nemphasis and rewrites it to `**PROJECT_NAME**`, so a markdown formatter run over the template turns\nevery prose placeholder into that form; the renderer substitutes both spellings. Which of the seven\nthe scaffold actually uses, in either spelling, is the scaffold's own invariant and its own\n`.self-check/` suite checks it."},"1527":{"id":"/new-repo-60","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#rendering-a-repository","content":"Two kinds of path are removed on the way out: a lane's reserved metadata (`LANE.md`,\n`gitignore.fragment`, whose contents are consumed rather than copied) and the generator-owned paths\nin `NEW_REPO_TEMPLATE_META`, stripped from every render: `.template-lanes/` (the overlay source this\nscript reads), `.self-check/` (the scaffold's conformance suite, which goes stale the moment a real\nproject diverges from the template it was checked against), and `.git` (which would hand the new\nrepository the template's history and origin remote)."},"1528":{"id":"/new-repo-61","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"The credential here is the Coder External Auth GitHub App **user-to-server token**; this stack\nnever introduces a static token or PAT. An App can be granted one endpoint and refused another, so\nthe CLI runs the publish side as independent steps, each reporting `ok:` or `warning:` on its own.\nNo step is fatal, because aborting on the first refusal would abandon the steps that were allowed."},"1529":{"id":"/new-repo-62","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step   | Endpoint           | App permission                                            |\n| ------ | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Create | `POST /user/repos` | `Administration: write` (or `Repository creation: write`) |","table":"table-3"},"1530":{"id":"/new-repo-63","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step     | Endpoint               | App permission          |\n| -------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Settings | `PATCH /repos/{o}/{r}` | `Administration: write` |","table":"table-3"},"1531":{"id":"/new-repo-64","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step   | Endpoint                    | App permission          |\n| ------ | --------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Topics | `PUT /repos/{o}/{r}/topics` | `Administration: write` |","table":"table-3"},"1532":{"id":"/new-repo-65","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step   | Endpoint                                  | App permission          |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Alerts | `PUT /repos/{o}/{r}/vulnerability-alerts` | `Administration: write` |","table":"table-3"},"1533":{"id":"/new-repo-66","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"| Step | Endpoint       | App permission       |\n| ---- | -------------- | -------------------- |\n| Push | git over HTTPS | the credential above |","table":"table-3"},"1534":{"id":"/new-repo-67","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"Dependabot **secrets** are a different permission that this App does not currently hold, which is\nwhy the CLI does not attempt them. When a step is refused the CLI prints the endpoint, GitHub's\nmessage, and the permission to ask for, then re-mints nothing and continues."},"1535":{"id":"/new-repo-68","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#what-each-github-step-needs","content":"Because git and `gh` consult `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before the stored login, an expired token\npair from an old refresh can shadow a working credential. Every call tries the environment first\nand, on refusal, retries once with the pair cleared and says so in a `warning:`. Re-mint with\n`coder external-auth access-token github`."},"1536":{"id":"/new-repo-69","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"Two things, and neither replaces the CLI."},"1537":{"id":"/new-repo-70","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"The **`New project (scaffold ready)` preset** in both templates is a form shortcut. It pins the\ngolden image, turns on the DinD sidecar in the workspace template, and passes this stack as\n`additional_repos`, which is what puts `scripts/new-repo.sh` inside a fresh workspace beside the\nproject being started. The scaffold does not ride along: the CLI fetches it from GitHub when it\nruns. It is a preset, so it only sets parameter values."},"1538":{"id":"/new-repo-71","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"**`--task`** calls `coder task create` once the repository exists. The Coder server here is v2.36.6\nand does ship `coder task`, but the Terraform provider is pinned to `~> 2.18`, so a task cannot be\ndeclared inside a template. The CLI therefore creates it at run time and, when it cannot reach the\nCoder API -- the usual case outside a logged-in host shell -- prints the exact command and continues\nwith exit 0. That follows `new-workspace.sh`, which treats \"cannot create right now\" as a not-yet\nrather than an error."},"1539":{"id":"/new-repo-72","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"The CLI never creates the workspace itself. It prints\n`next: bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <url>` and stops, because `new-workspace.sh` owns the\ndev-container probe and the template choice, and a second copy of that decision would eventually\ndisagree with the first."},"1540":{"id":"/new-repo-73","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#coder-side","content":"That preset/CLI pair is the whole hand-off. It is where this repo hands a new project over, and the\nonly coupling between the two repositories: this repo names the scaffold it clones, the scaffold\nnever names this one."},"1541":{"id":"/new-repo-74","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"The split follows the ownership: the scaffold's own invariants are checked in the scaffold\nrepository, and this repo checks only the renderer."},"1542":{"id":"/new-repo-75","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"The scaffold's `.self-check/` suite, in that repository and run by its own CI, owns the template's\ninternal invariants: every token the template uses is one this script declares, every lane ships\nits `LANE.md` plus `gitignore.fragment` pair, every tool is pinned rather than `@latest`, the\nworkflows declare `permissions`, the docs links resolve, and the pins cover the lanes present."},"1543":{"id":"/new-repo-76","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"`//tests:bats_new_repo_test` owns the renderer: token substitution in file contents and in file\nand directory names, the markdown `**NAME**` alias, the lane overlay and its `gitignore`\nfragments, the generator-owned paths stripped from every render, the argv of the scaffold clone,\nthe credential ladder that retries once with `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` cleared, the GitHub ladder\nstaying advisory (a refused step warns and never fails the run), and the Coder Task being a\nconvenience that cannot fail a render."},"1544":{"id":"/new-repo-77","page_id":"/new-repo","tags":["template","new-repo","scaffold","coder-task","ezrepo"],"type":"text","url":"/new-repo#where-this-is-enforced","content":"`//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` keeps `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD[\"new-repo\"]` honest about what the CLI\nactually reads."},"1545":{"id":"/operate","page_id":"/operate","type":"page","content":"Operate the box","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Operate the box"],"tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate"},"1546":{"id":"/operate-0","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate","content":"Day-to-day operation — the dashboard and desktops, what to refresh after a git pull, the public shape, and the symptom-first fix list."},"1547":{"id":"/operate-1","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Operate the box"},"1548":{"id":"/operate-2","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"The two commands"},"1549":{"id":"/operate-3","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"Where to look"},"1550":{"id":"/operate-4","page_id":"/operate","type":"heading","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"url":"/operate#before-you-reach-for-a-hammer","content":"Before you reach for a hammer"},"1551":{"id":"/operate-5","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1552":{"id":"/operate-6","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Which page answers which operational question"},"1553":{"id":"/operate-7","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"The two commands an operator runs most"},"1554":{"id":"/operate-8","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"**What this enables**"},"1555":{"id":"/operate-9","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Getting to the right runbook in one click instead of searching the README"},"1556":{"id":"/operate-10","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#operate-the-box","content":"Refreshing a stale checkout before diagnosing what it looks like"},"1557":{"id":"/operate-11","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"Everything else is a target those two feed:"},"1558":{"id":"/operate-12","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#the-two-commands","content":"`prod-stack` addresses the stable project by name from any directory; `dev-stack` does the\nsame for the dev one. `update-configs` is what a `git pull` cannot do for you, because the\nfiles that go stale are not tracked — see\nconfig-updates.md."},"1559":{"id":"/operate-13","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                               | Page         |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------ |\n| A card is red, or the dashboard will not load | dashboard.md |","table":"table-0"},"1560":{"id":"/operate-14","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                               | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Something fails, and you have an error string | troubleshooting.md |","table":"table-0"},"1561":{"id":"/operate-15","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                         | Page              |\n| --------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| A `git pull` landed and the ports moved | config-updates.md |","table":"table-0"},"1562":{"id":"/operate-16","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                         | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A model answered from the wrong backend | litellm-routing.md |","table":"table-0"},"1563":{"id":"/operate-17","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                    | Page               |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A workspace is stuck, or its editor will not start | coder-templates.md |","table":"table-0"},"1564":{"id":"/operate-18","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                   | Page             |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| A public name does not resolve or redirects wrong | public-access.md |","table":"table-0"},"1565":{"id":"/operate-19","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                                              | Page           |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------- |\n| The box will not finish booting and nobody is at the console | luks-unlock.md |","table":"table-0"},"1566":{"id":"/operate-20","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#where-to-look","content":"| Symptom or task                | Page      |\n| ------------------------------ | --------- |\n| The assistant will not sign in | hermes.md |","table":"table-0"},"1567":{"id":"/operate-21","page_id":"/operate","tags":["operate","dashboard","maintain","overview"],"type":"text","url":"/operate#before-you-reach-for-a-hammer","content":"`storage-clean` is the janitor for the persistent layer and asks before it deletes; the\ndev-stack `reset` and a Kasm reset both destroy state that has no backup. The cost of each\nis spelled out on the entry in troubleshooting.md — read that line\nfirst."},"1568":{"id":"/project-conventions","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"page","content":"Project conventions","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions"},"1569":{"id":"/project-conventions-0","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."},"1570":{"id":"/project-conventions-1","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Project conventions"},"1571":{"id":"/project-conventions-2","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"File header"},"1572":{"id":"/project-conventions-3","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Functions"},"1573":{"id":"/project-conventions-4","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Tests"},"1574":{"id":"/project-conventions-5","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Ingress"},"1575":{"id":"/project-conventions-6","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"Scripts"},"1576":{"id":"/project-conventions-7","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"Templates"},"1577":{"id":"/project-conventions-8","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"Names and flags"},"1578":{"id":"/project-conventions-9","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1579":{"id":"/project-conventions-10","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"},"1580":{"id":"/project-conventions-11","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Ingress rules: what runs from a shell and what is a Bazel target"},"1581":{"id":"/project-conventions-12","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"One language per file, the shared log contract, and the payload copies the templates share"},"1582":{"id":"/project-conventions-13","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Names, flags, and the style guide shell follows"},"1583":{"id":"/project-conventions-14","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"**What this enables**"},"1584":{"id":"/project-conventions-15","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Writing a script that passes `//:lint` and `//tests:doc_coverage` first try"},"1585":{"id":"/project-conventions-16","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Matching the house style instead of inventing a local one"},"1586":{"id":"/project-conventions-17","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Shell follows the Google Shell Style Guide.\nBranch rules live in branching.md; the Bazel harness lives in\ncontribute/building-with-bazel.md and\ncontribute/test-with-bazel.md."},"1587":{"id":"/project-conventions-18","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"Every `scripts/**/*.sh` file starts with a `# ## ` header that names the file and says whether it is an entry point or a sourced library."},"1588":{"id":"/project-conventions-19","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Every function carries this block immediately above the definition:"},"1589":{"id":"/project-conventions-20","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."},"1590":{"id":"/project-conventions-21","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Every function under `scripts/` is named by an asserting test under `tests/`: one suite per\nmodule, plus one test per entry-level function that drives the whole script (`tests/bats/\nentry-fns.bats` is where those live). `tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a name is\nmissing, and `tests/doc_coverage.sh` when a `# @function` block is missing or incomplete.\nTests are hermetic: docker, ssh, curl, git and the other external tools resolve through\n`tests/bats/tool_stubs`."},"1591":{"id":"/project-conventions-22","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Bazelisk is the only ingress, with one exception: `scripts/setup-host.sh`. That script is\nthe **stack host's** bootstrap — it installs Bazelisk, the shell/Starlark lint toolchain\n(shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier) and Terraform — so it has to run before Bazel exists, and\nstays the single documented `bash scripts/setup-host.sh`. On a laptop you do not run it:\nthe contributor container or `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image` brings the same pinned\ntoolchain in from `images/dev/` instead\n(contribute/devcontainer.md). `--repair` is the other half of\nthat exemption: leftover Docker bind-mounts in Bazel's runfiles tree make\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` die creating `prod-stack.runfiles` (`Permission\ndenied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`) before any stack script can run.\nOn Linux it also writes a systemd-resolved drop-in (`1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8`) and\nruns `tailscale set --accept-dns=false` so host `git pull` still resolves\n`github.com` when MagicDNS is the only nameserver."},"1592":{"id":"/project-conventions-23","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Everything else is a Bazel target:"},"1593":{"id":"/project-conventions-24","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"`make` only delegates. An operator CLI runs as `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`\n(`<stem>` = the file name under `scripts/`); `make help` lists the shims."},"1594":{"id":"/project-conventions-25","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"No `eval`. Prefer `\"${var}\"`, `[[ ]]`, and `$(...)`."},"1595":{"id":"/project-conventions-26","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"One language per file where reasonable. A bash, python, or awk program that lives\ninside another file is its own tested file, imported with `source`, `cat` + `sh -c`\n/ `ssh`, `python3 file.py`, `awk -f`, or Terraform `file()` / `templatefile()`. Keep\n1–3 line awk, usage heredocs, Dockerfiles, YAML data, docs fences, and\n`python3 -m webbrowser`. Remote probes are POSIX and self-contained under\n`scripts/lib/probes/`; the host `cat`s them and tests run the file with `sh`."},"1596":{"id":"/project-conventions-27","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"Operator CLIs source `scripts/lib/common.sh` and do not redefine `log` / `die` /\n`skip`. Grouping: `section` prints `== name ==`, `phase` prints `-> text`, `ok`\nprints `ok: text`, `summary` prints `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed` plus\n`  failed: …` and optional `next: cmd`. `JSON_MODE` set sends prose to stderr.\nCLIs without `--json` unset `JSON_MODE` after source. Keep prefixes `warning:` /\n`note:` / `error:`."},"1597":{"id":"/project-conventions-28","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"A script never runs another repo script by relative path, and never assumes the cwd is the\ncheckout: it resolves the repo root with `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` and then\n`cd`s there (see the harness doc), because a Bazel sandbox may only hold a copy. That helper tries\n`${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` / `${PWD}` /\n`${SELF_DIR}/..` in order, and a candidate counts only when it holds the marker file\n(`docker-compose.yml` unless the caller names another) and its `pwd -P` is not under a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree — an explicit `REPO_ROOT` is\nthe one candidate trusted as given, which is how the Bats runner names the copy under test.\nNothing proving out is one\n`error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line on stderr and exit `5`, never a guessed cwd."},"1598":{"id":"/project-conventions-29","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"External tools are probed with `command -v` and reported as \"not on PATH\", not assumed.\n`require_tool` skips locally and fails under `CI=true`."},"1599":{"id":"/project-conventions-30","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"A probe-style CLI takes its target as a flag (`--url`) rather than a hard-coded host, and reports\n\"nothing answered\" as a result rather than a crash, so one run works from the host and from inside\na workspace and still answers the question the operator asked. Its suite stubs the transport."},"1600":{"id":"/project-conventions-31","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload. `templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and\nholds the single-copy payloads both templates consume: `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`,\n`vscode-alt.svg`, `notes-setup.sh`, `tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`,\n`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`, `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md`, `ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, `stack-skills.sh.tftpl`,\n`coder-agents.md`, `mcp-cache-proxy.json`, and `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`. Markdown\nand JSON are read with `file()` (chomp the markdown); shell helpers that Terraform must\nnot re-interpolate are also `file()` (so `$folder` stays `$folder`); `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`\nis `templatefile()` because it interpolates `${github_auth_id}`. `coder_script`\nbodies are sibling `.tftpl` files next to each `main.tf` (`code-server.sh.tftpl`,\nand on `devcontainer` also `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`) rendered with `templatefile()`;\n`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl` and `stack-skills.sh.tftpl` (the body that seeds the\n`ez-learn` / `ez-skillify` / `ez-create-skill` trio) are the two that live in\n`templates/common/` instead, so both twins share one copy of them.\nThe SVG stays small enough that the resulting data URL fits Coder v2.36's 256-character\n`workspace_apps.icon` column. The push\n(`scripts/push-template.sh` through `template_push_one` in `scripts/lib/template-push.sh`)\nstages every common payload beside each template's staged `main.tf` copy, which is the only place the\nserver-side Terraform can read them from, so editing the common copy changes both\ntemplates; a staged copy that lacks any one of them fails the push early by design."},"1601":{"id":"/project-conventions-32","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"Dollar escaping differs across those two layers. A `templatefile()` `.tftpl` treats `$$`\nas an escaped `$` everywhere (`$name` or `$${name}`), and leaves `$((` arithmetic\nalone. An HCL heredoc in `main.tf` (`<<-EOT`) only escapes `$${name}` to `${name}`;\na lone `$` is already literal, so shell arithmetic is `$((...))` with one dollar.\n`$$((` is two dollars, and Coder runs `coder_script` bodies as `bash -c`, which\nexpands `$$` to the PID. Do not wrap `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."},"1602":{"id":"/project-conventions-33","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`devcontainer` — the dev-container template (directory `templates/devcontainer`, Coder\ntemplate name `devcontainer`), always backed by its own DinD sidecar. Its sibling is\n`workspace`: directory `templates/workspace`, template name `workspace`, the plain\ndev container with the optional `dind` parameter."},"1603":{"id":"/project-conventions-34","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`devcontainer.json` — a file the *application repository* carries, and the one thing the\n`devcontainer` template builds. `coder_devcontainer.repo` in\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf` sets no `config_path`, so `@devcontainers/cli` on the\nDinD sidecar searches the clone for `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` (or a\n`devcontainer.json` anywhere inside it). This repo ships a `.devcontainer/` of its own —\nthe **contributor** one for a laptop and CI, built from `images/dev/Dockerfile` — and it\nis not that nested kind: `.devcontainer/contributor-env` alongside it makes\n`probe_repo` answer a miss, so a Coder workspace of this repo stays on the `workspace`\ntemplate instead of building its own container inside its own DinD sidecar. Other\nrepositories keep carrying their own nested dev-container files, and those are still a\nprobe hit. See contribute/devcontainer.md."},"1604":{"id":"/project-conventions-35","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`additional_repos` — the `--additional-repos` flag on the new-workspace CLI, and the\n`coder_parameter` of the same name that receives its value in both templates\n(`mutable = true`, default empty): extra git URLs for the workspace's clone folders,\ngiven as `url1,url2` or one URL per line. The flag exists on\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` only - the stack runners (`dev-stack`,\n`prod-stack`) have no `--additional-repos` (their flags are `-p`, `-f`, `-e`,\n`--tail`, `--follow`, `--json`, `-y`, `-h`)."},"1605":{"id":"/project-conventions-36","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name). The env file follows the runner: the dev project takes `dev-stack.env`\nbeside the compose file when one exists, and any other project gets none, so a run aimed at\nthe stable stack never picks up the dev host ports; `prod-stack` names no env file unless\n`-e` does, and compose then reads the `.env` beside the compose file itself."},"1606":{"id":"/project-conventions-37","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough). `bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."},"1607":{"id":"/project-conventions-38","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"GitHub auth is Coder External Auth, id `github` by default (Coder's injected\nGitHub App when coder.env is absent). A custom OAuth app in coder.env keeps\nits `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` (commonly `primary-github`); push-template.sh\nforwards that id. There is no GitHub-token template variable and no PAT in\nany agent env map."},"1608":{"id":"/project-conventions-39","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"Runtime stays `docker compose`: Bazel runs the CLIs that drive Compose and never talks to a\ndaemon, a Spark box, or a model registry by itself. No Kubernetes layer."},"1609":{"id":"/public-access","page_id":"/public-access","type":"page","content":"Public subdomains","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access"},"1610":{"id":"/public-access-0","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Serving the same five services on public names over real TLS, additively, with the LAN shape left intact."},"1611":{"id":"/public-access-1","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Public subdomains"},"1612":{"id":"/public-access-2","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The path a public request takes"},"1613":{"id":"/public-access-3","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"The composed names"},"1614":{"id":"/public-access-4","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"Getting the naming right"},"1615":{"id":"/public-access-5","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"What `bootstrap` changes when the names are set"},"1616":{"id":"/public-access-6","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"Traefik configuration"},"1617":{"id":"/public-access-7","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Checking the other machine"},"1618":{"id":"/public-access-8","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Authelia"},"1619":{"id":"/public-access-9","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Two follow-ups, both by hand"},"1620":{"id":"/public-access-10","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1621":{"id":"/public-access-11","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"},"1622":{"id":"/public-access-12","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The naming rule, and what `auto` composes from the parts you set"},"1623":{"id":"/public-access-13","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"What `bootstrap.sh` changes when the names are set"},"1624":{"id":"/public-access-14","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"},"1625":{"id":"/public-access-15","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Checking the other machine (including the Hermes WebSocket), Authelia, and the two follow-ups left by hand"},"1626":{"id":"/public-access-16","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"**What this enables**"},"1627":{"id":"/public-access-17","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Exposing the stack without breaking the LAN routes anyone at home uses"},"1628":{"id":"/public-access-18","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"},"1629":{"id":"/public-access-19","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Putting the five routers on the Traefik box without guessing its timeouts"},"1630":{"id":"/public-access-20","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The default shape is LAN-only. `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` additionally serves the same\nfive services on public names over real TLS, each name composed from the parts you set\nonce. It is purely additive: every LAN route keeps working."},"1631":{"id":"/public-access-21","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."},"1632":{"id":"/public-access-22","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Cloudflare proxies the name (orange cloud) in front of Traefik. WebSockets are on\nthere by default, which is what the Hermes Chat tab needs."},"1633":{"id":"/public-access-23","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."},"1634":{"id":"/public-access-24","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Kasm is the only hop that speaks TLS to its origin. `proxy_ssl_verify` is off there\nbecause the certificate is the one Kasm's installer generates and reissues. Hermes,\nlike Homepage, Coder and LiteLLM, is plain HTTP at the origin (`http://hermes:9119`)."},"1635":{"id":"/public-access-25","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Nothing in this path replaces the LAN routes: `https://<lan-ip>/` still serves the\ndashboard on the self-signed cert, `https://<lan-ip>:3001` still serves Coder's\nown TLS listener, and `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` still serves Hermes."},"1636":{"id":"/public-access-26","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"A workspace **app** link (the **VS Code Web** button is the only app this stack still\nproxies) rides the coder row of that table. `coder_app.code_server` sets\n`subdomain = false`, so its URL keeps the `/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` path\nform and goes to `coder:3000` on its way to the editor on `localhost:13337` inside the\nworkspace. A 502 on that link is an app-lane failure and `litellm-gmktecbeast.` took\nno part in it."},"1637":{"id":"/public-access-27","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Every `location` adds `X-Evo-Proxy` with the upstream name, and `always` is what\nmakes it survive a 502. Read it beside `X-Coder-Request-Id`, which Coder's own proxy\nsets on an answered request: neither header means the request stopped at Traefik or\nCloudflare, and `421` means the Host header matched no server block here."},"1638":{"id":"/public-access-28","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"These are what the shipped defaults compose. The operator sets the parts, not these\nstrings: the grammar is `<service>-<environment>-<suffix>.<base>`, always ONE label\nunder the zone."},"1639":{"id":"/public-access-29","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                      | Router upstream |\n| ------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `homepage:3000` |","table":"table-0"},"1640":{"id":"/public-access-30","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                            | Router upstream               |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `coder:3000` - plain listener |","table":"table-0"},"1641":{"id":"/public-access-31","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                                | Router upstream                         |\n| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `coder:3000` - the dev stack's own name |","table":"table-0"},"1642":{"id":"/public-access-32","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                           | Router upstream                              |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `https://kasm:4443` (`proxy_ssl_verify off`) |","table":"table-0"},"1643":{"id":"/public-access-33","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                              | Router upstream |\n| --------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `litellm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `litellm:4000`  |","table":"table-0"},"1644":{"id":"/public-access-34","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                             | Router upstream |\n| -------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `hermes:9119`   |","table":"table-0"},"1645":{"id":"/public-access-35","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"The dev row is what `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up` composes for the\n`evo-t1-dev` project: that command applies `STACK_ENV_LABEL=dev` when neither the shell\nnor the env file already sets a label, and only for that project, so\n`--project-name evo-t1-review` gets none. A label goes on every name, so that stack's\ndashboard is `dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` too, and all five need their own Traefik\nrouters (see below). `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` never invents a label."},"1646":{"id":"/public-access-36","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"Hermes is the same additive hop as the other four. Traefik forwards\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with the original Host, and the router sends that\nHost to `http://hermes:9119`. The LAN URL `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` stays.\nHTTP basic auth on the dashboard stays too; Authelia is the extra public gate.\nSee hermes.md."},"1647":{"id":"/public-access-37","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`scripts/lib/domain.sh` owns the grammar and every part is normalized before it is\njoined, so a value copied out of a browser bar cannot leak into an origin or a\n`server_name`:"},"1648":{"id":"/public-access-38","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_DOMAIN_BASE` (default `overeazy.io`) is the zone the edge terminates TLS for;\n`STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX` (default `gmktecbeast`) is this box's label, shared by every\nname."},"1649":{"id":"/public-access-39","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_CODER_NAME` / `STACK_KASM_NAME` / `STACK_LITELLM_NAME` / `STACK_HERMES_NAME`\n(defaults `coder` / `kasm` / `litellm` / `hermes`) are the service prefixes.\n`STACK_DASHBOARD_NAME` is empty by default, which puts the dashboard on the zone apex."},"1650":{"id":"/public-access-40","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_ENV_LABEL` is empty for the stable stack and sits **after** the service prefix.\nTwo reasons: the one-label rule below rules the dotted form out anyway, and\n`classify_public_entry` in `scripts/lib/bootstrap.sh` routes an entry by its service\nprefix, so `coder-dev-box.example.com` is Coder while `dev-coder-box.example.com`\nfalls through to the dashboard."},"1651":{"id":"/public-access-41","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"The shape is load-bearing: each name is **one label under the zone**, and each service\nprefix joins it with a **hyphen**. A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so\nthe dotted two-label form (`coder.gmktecbeast.`) has no certificate at the public edge:\nthe browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` and Traefik never sees the\nrequest. Measured on this box - `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` completes the handshake\nwith the zone's `*.overeazy.io` cert, while the dotted form fails with a TLS alert 40\nand presents no certificate at all."},"1652":{"id":"/public-access-42","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` has three states, and only the middle one composes:"},"1653":{"id":"/public-access-43","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"empty is LAN-only;"},"1654":{"id":"/public-access-44","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"`auto` composes the five names from the parts above;"},"1655":{"id":"/public-access-45","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"a comma-separated list is used verbatim, which is how you pin a name by hand (leave\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE` empty, or that key keeps re-deriving)."},"1656":{"id":"/public-access-46","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"A part that cannot be a DNS label composes nothing: that service is left out of the list\nrather than sent as an empty entry, and a missing zone keeps the whole stack LAN-only."},"1657":{"id":"/public-access-47","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"},"1658":{"id":"/public-access-48","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"},"1659":{"id":"/public-access-49","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL`,\nthe card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills `STACK_HERMES_URL`.\nCompose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the Host and Origin\nthe Chat tab checks;"},"1660":{"id":"/public-access-50","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an upgraded `CODER_ACCESS_URL` (the public coder name);"},"1661":{"id":"/public-access-51","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an extended `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` - homepage validates the incoming Host against\nthat exact-match list, so an unlisted name fails its host check;"},"1662":{"id":"/public-access-52","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"a Coder certificate whose DNS SANs now carry the public names alongside the existing\nones. The LAN cert keeps working; in public mode the browser never sees it, since\nTraefik presents real ones;"},"1663":{"id":"/public-access-53","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, which records that the names are derived. Under `auto`\nthe parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output only, so a list\nedited by hand there is ignored and bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear. It clears itself on the LAN-only and explicit-list paths;"},"1664":{"id":"/public-access-54","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"the router's vhost table: `proxy/router.conf.template` is rendered into\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` (gitignored) and `STACK_ROUTER_CONF=router.gen.conf` is\nwritten, because compose mounts `./proxy/${STACK_ROUTER_CONF:-router.conf}`. The\n`warn_missing_router_hosts` check runs against that rendered file, so a name the\ntable does not answer is reported before the recreate rather than as a `421` after\nit."},"1665":{"id":"/public-access-55","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"Set the naming parts in `.env` and put `auto` in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` (`.env.sample`\ndocuments every part and shows the explicit-list shape beside it), and in\n`dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside the stable one. Then re-run `bootstrap`\nand recreate the stack you switched. The commands are in the README."},"1666":{"id":"/public-access-56","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"Nothing in this repo configures Traefik. This is the file the other box needs: drop it\nin the directory that box's `providers.file.directory` watches and Traefik picks it up\nwithout a restart. The shape below is the one Traefik v2 and v3 both read; the\nentryPoint name, the certificate and the middleware name have to match what that box\nalready defines, so treat those three as the values to align, not as new requirements."},"1667":{"id":"/public-access-57","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`passHostHeader: true` is the line that makes this work at all. Without it Traefik\nsends the upstream URL's host, no `server_name` matches, and this box answers `421`."},"1668":{"id":"/public-access-58","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**A published dev stack needs its own routers here.** The `dev-stack.env` behind\n`bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack` moves every published port by 10000 (the recipe is\nin the\nREADME),\nso that stack's `router` answers on `18080` (`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` default 8080) and\nits names carry the environment label\n(`coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`). A dev name is a different `Host` rule and a\ndifferent backend port: add a router per dev name to the file above aimed at\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:18080`, and the DNS records for those names have to exist\ntoo, or the browser fails before this box is ever asked."},"1669":{"id":"/public-access-59","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`authelia@file` is a placeholder for the Authelia middleware that box already\ndeclares. Name it exactly as that box names it - a middleware created by labels is\n`authelia@docker`, and a name Traefik cannot resolve fails the router rather than\nserving it unauthenticated. The four interactive names carry it; LiteLLM must not."},"1670":{"id":"/public-access-60","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."},"1671":{"id":"/public-access-61","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."},"1672":{"id":"/public-access-62","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder and hermes 300s, LiteLLM 600s, Kasm 3600s - so\nTraefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all five at `3600s` is the\nsimple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway. To cap each tier separately,\ndeclare one `loadBalancer` service per tier, each pointing at the same\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with `passHostHeader: true` and its own transport\n(`evo-t1-coder` / `evo-t1-hermes` at `300s`, `evo-t1-litellm` at `600s`,\n`evo-t1-kasm` at `3600s`), and point each router at its own service. If a session\ndies at about a minute with the page still loaded, check the client-facing side\ntoo: an entryPoint's `transport.respondingTimeouts.readTimeout` (default 60s) and\n`idleTimeout` (default 180s) apply to the browser-to-Traefik hop."},"1673":{"id":"/public-access-63","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"SearXNG is deliberately absent. It stays LAN-only on `:8181`, which is where the\nworkspaces reach it through `host.docker.internal`; putting it behind Traefik would\nexpose a search box that has no authentication of its own."},"1674":{"id":"/public-access-64","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."},"1675":{"id":"/public-access-65","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"A router per name pointing at `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080`, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`. That backend is `:8080` with the original Host,\nthe same as the other four - not `:9119`. A router aimed at `:9119` skips this\nbox's vhost. The file in Traefik configuration is that\nconfig; diff what the box runs against it."},"1676":{"id":"/public-access-66","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."},"1677":{"id":"/public-access-67","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Any authentication as the Traefik router's middleware. Authelia already fronts\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` (the name 302s), the same interactive policy as\nthe homepage and Coder names. The sidecar forwards what it receives and\nauthenticates nothing, so a name whose router skips the auth middleware reaches\nits container the same way the LAN does. The app path is behind that policy\nlike the rest of its name, so an expired session surfaces there too."},"1678":{"id":"/public-access-68","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"DNS records for all five names, each one label deep and Cloudflare-proxied (orange\ncloud). WebSockets are on there by default. A missing record shows up as a DNS\nerror, a rewritten Host as `421`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and LiteLLM resolve\nthat way and complete the TLS handshake, checked from this box; the Hermes\nname is the same zone and the same proxy."},"1679":{"id":"/public-access-69","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"WebSocket `Upgrade` forwarded by Traefik and Authelia on the Hermes router. The\nChat tab uses `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`, and Hermes sends a keepalive every 20\nseconds. A proxy that drops `Upgrade` loads the page and then fails the tab."},"1680":{"id":"/public-access-70","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"`https://gmktecbeast.overeazy.io/`, `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` 302 to\n`https://authelia.overeazy.io/`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and Hermes are interactive\nsessions, and the Hermes policy is already in place. Covering a newly named host\nis an Authelia-side config choice; nothing here adds or requires a policy. Hermes\nis not an API bypass: it stays behind that interactive policy, and after Authelia\nit asks for its own HTTP basic auth. Both prompts on the public URL are expected."},"1681":{"id":"/public-access-71","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Put LiteLLM behind it and every client breaks: LiteLLM authenticates from the\n`Authorization` header, and header-carried API traffic cannot pass an interactive\nsign-in. Either leave `litellm-gmktecbeast` outside any policy or add an explicit\nbypass - and then `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` is the only credential in front of an\nendpoint that went from LAN-only to reachable WAN-wide (see\nSECURITY.md)."},"1682":{"id":"/public-access-72","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Workspace agents never reach Authelia either way. The template rewrites the agent URL\nto `http://host.docker.internal:3002`, the plain listener published through\n`CODER_AGENT_TUNNEL_PORT`, so agent traffic stays on the box."},"1683":{"id":"/public-access-73","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Workspaces created before public mode existed need a template re-push plus a\nrestart, to pick up the template's `coder_agent_url` variable (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:3002`). That variable is what keeps agents dialing the\nlocal plain listener whatever the access URL says."},"1684":{"id":"/public-access-74","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Kasm stores its connection endpoints in its own setup database (first-boot wizard or\nadmin UI), so the endpoint has to change there too or streams keep dialing the LAN\naddress."},"1685":{"id":"/troubleshooting","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting"},"1686":{"id":"/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."},"1687":{"id":"/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Troubleshooting"},"1688":{"id":"/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#stack-bring-up","content":"Stack bring-up"},"1689":{"id":"/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"`not a directory: Are you trying to mount a directory onto a file` -> missing compose bind-mount source"},"1690":{"id":"/troubleshooting-4","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"`FileAccessException` / `Permission denied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` -> leftover runfiles bind-mount"},"1691":{"id":"/troubleshooting-5","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#error-env-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptsdashboard-password---the-runfiles-copy-has-no-working-env","content":"`error: .../.env not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` -> the runfiles copy has no working `.env`"},"1692":{"id":"/troubleshooting-6","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"`Could not resolve host: github.com` -> Tailscale MagicDNS is the only nameserver"},"1693":{"id":"/troubleshooting-7","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#model-lane-spark-litellm-the-agent-backends","content":"Model lane: Spark, LiteLLM, the agent backends"},"1694":{"id":"/troubleshooting-8","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#env-line-188-qwen25-coder14b-command-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptspull-models---the-model-list-was-being-parsed-as-shell","content":"`./.env: line 188: qwen2.5-coder:14b: command not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:pull-models` -> the model list was being parsed as shell"},"1695":{"id":"/troubleshooting-9","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"How do I know the IPEX-LLM server came up? -> look for `runners=[ipex_llm]`"},"1696":{"id":"/troubleshooting-10","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Grok agent mode makes no tool calls (it narrates and stops) -> an Arc alias served it"},"1697":{"id":"/troubleshooting-11","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"The TensorFold log is full of warnings, or `resumed at 0` -> read the line shapes first"},"1698":{"id":"/troubleshooting-12","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Sessions log `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s (runaway generation)` -> a compaction stream that never sent a token, bought three times"},"1699":{"id":"/troubleshooting-13","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"Coder Agents chat refuses every prompt, or its model dropdown is empty -> no model config"},"1700":{"id":"/troubleshooting-14","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-offers-a-model-that-errors-or-a-chat-never-gets-a-title---an-alias-with-no-healthy-deployment","content":"Coder Agents offers a model that errors, or a chat never gets a title -> an alias with no healthy deployment"},"1701":{"id":"/troubleshooting-15","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"Workspace cannot reach LiteLLM -> `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, or `host.docker.internal`"},"1702":{"id":"/troubleshooting-16","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"Search never answers (or answers empty) -> prove the search lane before blaming the MCP hop"},"1703":{"id":"/troubleshooting-17","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"Context7 or GitHub tools missing in Grok Build -> parked MCP block, or a handshake with nothing to authenticate"},"1704":{"id":"/troubleshooting-18","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"A workspace app link answers `Bad Gateway` -> find who answered"},"1705":{"id":"/troubleshooting-19","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#workspaces-startup-images-the-toolchain-inside-them","content":"Workspaces: startup, images, the toolchain inside them"},"1706":{"id":"/troubleshooting-20","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-stuck-in-starting---startup-is-blocking","content":"Workspace stuck in *starting* -> startup is blocking"},"1707":{"id":"/troubleshooting-21","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#image-missing-tools-or-the-build-aborts-mid-layer---curl-22","content":"Image missing tools, or the build aborts mid-layer -> `curl: (22)`"},"1708":{"id":"/troubleshooting-22","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"`grok` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> the CLI was never installed"},"1709":{"id":"/troubleshooting-23","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"A stack skill command missing from Grok Build on code-server web -> the stack's own seed is what provides it"},"1710":{"id":"/troubleshooting-24","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#grok-doctor-flags-voiceno-input-device-or-terminallimited-color---a-headless-container-and-where-you-ran-the-command","content":"`grok doctor` flags `voice.no-input-device` or `terminal.limited-color` -> a headless container, and where you ran the command"},"1711":{"id":"/troubleshooting-25","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#docker---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---an-older-image-or-the-wrong-preset","content":"`docker` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> an older image, or the wrong preset"},"1712":{"id":"/troubleshooting-26","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#permission-denied--jq-error-could-not-open-file-on-sharedvscodesettingsjson---empty-named-volume-is-root-owned","content":"`Permission denied` / `jq: error: Could not open file` on `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` -> empty named volume is root-owned"},"1713":{"id":"/troubleshooting-27","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#grok-profile-missing-in-a-fresh-workspace---startup-already-finished-so-read-the-log","content":"Grok profile missing in a fresh workspace -> startup already finished, so read the log"},"1714":{"id":"/troubleshooting-28","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#docker-in-docker","content":"Docker-in-docker"},"1715":{"id":"/troubleshooting-29","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"`Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at tcp://dind:2375` -> the sidecar, or DinD off"},"1716":{"id":"/troubleshooting-30","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#did-not-find-docker-image-docker2981-dind---plan-time-failure-not-runtime","content":"`did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` -> plan-time failure, not runtime"},"1717":{"id":"/troubleshooting-31","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#terraform-and-the-host-coder-cli","content":"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI"},"1718":{"id":"/troubleshooting-32","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"`lookup registry.terraform.io on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving` from `push-template` -> coder cannot reach the Terraform registry"},"1719":{"id":"/troubleshooting-33","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#pq-value-too-long-for-type-character-varying256-from-push-template---editor-icon-data-url","content":"`pq: value too long for type character varying(256)` from `push-template` -> editor icon data URL"},"1720":{"id":"/troubleshooting-34","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#error-no-configuration-files-from-push-template---runfiles-symlinks-in-the-tar","content":"`Error: No configuration files` from `push-template` -> runfiles symlinks in the tar"},"1721":{"id":"/troubleshooting-35","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#repo_root-unbound-variable-from-push-template---the-size-guard-read-an-ambient-root","content":"`REPO_ROOT: unbound variable` from `push-template` -> the size guard read an ambient root"},"1722":{"id":"/troubleshooti