:"/troubleshooting-48","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"gh_token_refresh says gh would not store the minted token"},"1735":{"id":"/troubleshooting-49","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Private-repo clone or push denied while a credential is present -> the grant, not the format"},"1736":{"id":"/troubleshooting-50","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#gh-answers-bad-credentials---the-stale-env-pair-not-the-store","content":"`gh` answers `Bad credentials` -> the stale env pair, not the store"},"1737":{"id":"/troubleshooting-51","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#gh-command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-helper-needs-gh","content":"`gh: command not found` inside a workspace -> the helper needs gh"},"1738":{"id":"/troubleshooting-52","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#ui-and-logins","content":"UI and logins"},"1739":{"id":"/troubleshooting-53","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"`Refusing to bind dashboard to 0.0.0.0` -> empty Hermes basic-auth env"},"1740":{"id":"/troubleshooting-54","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"Public `hermes-gmktecbeast` answers `421` -> no vhost, a rewritten Host, or Traefik aimed past the router"},"1741":{"id":"/troubleshooting-55","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"Chat tab never connects, or the socket closes `4403` -> public URL, trusted proxies, or a dropped Upgrade"},"1742":{"id":"/troubleshooting-56","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#kasm-wizard-gone-after-install---expected","content":"Kasm wizard gone after install -> expected"},"1743":{"id":"/troubleshooting-57","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#coder-login-problems---the-admin-account-and-coder_access_url","content":"Coder login problems -> the admin account, and `CODER_ACCESS_URL`"},"1744":{"id":"/troubleshooting-58","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"**What's on this page**"},"1745":{"id":"/troubleshooting-59","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Stack bring-up: bootstrap, ports, containers that refuse to start"},"1746":{"id":"/troubleshooting-60","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"The model lane: Spark boxes, LiteLLM, and the agent backends"},"1747":{"id":"/troubleshooting-61","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Workspaces: startup, the golden image, Grok Build (`/ez-learn`, MCP), the toolchain"},"1748":{"id":"/troubleshooting-62","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Docker-in-Docker, Terraform, and the host `coder` CLI"},"1749":{"id":"/troubleshooting-63","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Editor, credentials, the clone, UI logins, and the public names (`421`, Chat `4403`, Traefik)"},"1750":{"id":"/troubleshooting-64","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"**What this enables**"},"1751":{"id":"/troubleshooting-65","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Starting from the error text you actually saw and landing on one command"},"1752":{"id":"/troubleshooting-66","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Knowing the cost of a fix before running it, including what it destroys"},"1753":{"id":"/troubleshooting-67","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Deep, symptom-first list. The operational `dev-stack`/`prod-stack`, `search-check` and\n`spark-verify` targets are described in README.md and\nproject-conventions.md."},"1754":{"id":"/troubleshooting-68","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Each fence below is one copy-paste action. A heading quotes the error string where there\nis one, so start with `grep -n` on the text you saw."},"1755":{"id":"/troubleshooting-69","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Placeholders: `<wsid>` is a workspace id and `<ws>` a workspace name. The workspace\ncontainer is `coder-<owner>-<ws>` (so `coder-alx-self-coder`); the `<wsid>` form names\nthe DinD pieces around it - the sidecar `coder-<wsid>-dind-sidecar`, on the network\n`coder-<wsid>-dind`, with its own `coder-<wsid>-dind-lib` volume. What runs \"from the\nstack host\" needs a shell there: inside a workspace `docker` addresses that workspace's\nown private daemon through `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375`, so a `docker inspect` aimed at\nthe stack's `coder` or `router` container and run from inside a workspace queries the\nwrong daemon and answers `no such container`. `bazelisk run //scripts:...` also runs\nfrom the checkout."},"1756":{"id":"/troubleshooting-70","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"Cause: `docker compose` resolved `./proxy/homepage.conf` (or another file bind\nmount) next to a compose file that did not have that file. Under `bazelisk run`\nthat file used to be the runfiles copy, which only holds `//:stack_data`. Docker\nthen created a directory at the source path and failed mounting it onto nginx's\n`/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf`, which is a file in the image."},"1757":{"id":"/troubleshooting-71","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"Fix: `homepage/**` and `proxy/*.conf` belong in `//:stack_data`, and the stack\nrunners prefer `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout) so relative\nmounts and bootstrap-issued `proxy/certs/` resolve there. Re-run from the stack\nhost:"},"1758":{"id":"/troubleshooting-72","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"The same command with `dev-stack` brings the dev project up. A leftover\n`homepage-proxy` container from the failed create is recreated on that `up`."},"1759":{"id":"/troubleshooting-73","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"A failed bind from an older runfiles compose file can also leave a **directory**\n(or a root-owned file) at `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` inside\n`bazel-out/.../prod-stack.runfiles`. The next `bazelisk run` then dies before\nthe stack script starts; that spelling is the next heading."},"1760":{"id":"/troubleshooting-74","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"Cause: an older `bazelisk run` used the runfiles copy of `docker-compose.yml`, so\ncompose mounted `./homepage/assets/icons` from `bazel-out`. Docker then created a\ndirectory where `evo-t1.svg` belongs, or the homepage container chowned those\nfiles to root. Bazel cannot recreate `prod-stack.runfiles` over that leftover,\nand the stack script never starts, so a repair inside `prod-stack` cannot help."},"1761":{"id":"/troubleshooting-75","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"Fix: recover from a shell, then re-run. `--repair` uses sudo when chmod is not\nenough:"},"1762":{"id":"/troubleshooting-76","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#fileaccessexception--permission-denied-on-homepageassetsiconsevo-t1svg---leftover-runfiles-bind-mount","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` is the command that pair recovers with; the\n`make prod-stack-up` (and `make stack-up`) shims run `--repair` before calling that same\ntarget. Bare `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` repairs leftovers and then installs any missing\nhost tools. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --check` lists leftover paths without writing."},"1763":{"id":"/troubleshooting-77","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-env-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptsdashboard-password---the-runfiles-copy-has-no-working-env","content":"Cause: an older target ran the runfiles copy of the script, so `$(dirname \"$0\")/..`\nwas the runfiles tree, and that tree ships only `//:stack_data`. The working `.env`\nis untracked, so it exists only in the checkout, and the rotation reported a missing\nfile on a host that had one. The path in the message tells the two apart: a\n`bazel-out/.../dashboard-password.runfiles/...` path is this entry. The same bug had\n`pull-models` pulling its default list instead of the checkout's `OLLAMA_MODELS`,\n`coder-agents` provisioning the compose placeholder `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` instead of the\ncheckout's, and `spark-configure` / `spark-verify` writing `sparks/` output into a tree\nBazel recreates."},"1764":{"id":"/troubleshooting-78","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-env-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptsdashboard-password---the-runfiles-copy-has-no-working-env","content":"Fix: those six CLIs (`dashboard-password`, `pull-models`, `coder-agents`,\n`spark-verify`, `spark-configure`, `build-dev-image`) resolve the checkout with\n`resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`) before they read or write anything, so\nthey honour `REPO_ROOT` and the `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` that `bazel run` names.\nWhen nothing proves out - no candidate holds `docker-compose.yml` outside a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree - the run exits `5` with one\n`error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line instead of half-running against a copy. Re-run\nfrom the stack host:"},"1765":{"id":"/troubleshooting-79","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-env-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptsdashboard-password---the-runfiles-copy-has-no-working-env","content":"The `first boot? run bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap to create it` hint alongside it\nmeans the checkout has no `.env` at all: that is the bootstrap path. `DASHBOARD_ENV_FILE`\npoints the rotation at another file and exists for test harnesses; there is deliberately\nno flag for it, because a flag that names a file this CLI overwrites is one mistype away."},"1766":{"id":"/troubleshooting-80","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"Cause: `/etc/resolv.conf` names only Tailscale MagicDNS (`100.100.100.100`).\nDocker's iptables, and Kasm's privileged nested dockerd, can make that stub\nunreachable. When it is, **every** lookup dies, including `github.com`, so\n`git pull` over HTTPS fails. This is host DNS, not GitHub auth."},"1767":{"id":"/troubleshooting-81","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"On Linux, `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --repair` writes\n`/etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/evo-t1-public-dns.conf` (`DNS=1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8`),\nruns `sudo tailscale set --accept-dns=false`, and keeps MagicDNS for `ts.net`\nvia `resolvectl` split-DNS. `--check` still writes nothing."},"1768":{"id":"/troubleshooting-82","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"A checkout that does not yet contain that `--repair` (the usual case: `git pull`\nis already failing) needs the same commands by hand, then pull:"},"1769":{"id":"/troubleshooting-83","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"`--check` prints which nameservers are in play and whether `github.com` resolves.\nOther ways to keep a public resolver: add `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` as a global\nnameserver in the Tailscale admin DNS page."},"1770":{"id":"/troubleshooting-84","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#could-not-resolve-host-githubcom---tailscale-magicdns-is-the-only-nameserver","content":"The same MagicDNS-only resolver is what makes Terraform inside the `coder`\ncontainer SERVFAIL `registry.terraform.io` (`127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`).\nThat spelling lives under \"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI\" below. Compose\npins public resolvers on `coder` so a template push does not wait on this host\nfix."},"1771":{"id":"/troubleshooting-85","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#env-line-188-qwen25-coder14b-command-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptspull-models---the-model-list-was-being-parsed-as-shell","content":"Cause: that target used to read `.env` with `. ./.env`, so the shell parsed the\noperator's file as code. An unquoted value that holds a space is a variable\nprefix followed by a COMMAND, and `set -e` ended the run on that line before a\nsingle pull:"},"1772":{"id":"/troubleshooting-86","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#env-line-188-qwen25-coder14b-command-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptspull-models---the-model-list-was-being-parsed-as-shell","content":"The word the shell says it cannot find is the second tag. The same read would\nalso have executed anything written on a line of its own in that file, and it\nexported every other key there (`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` included) into the\nenvironment of the `docker exec` calls."},"1773":{"id":"/troubleshooting-87","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#env-line-188-qwen25-coder14b-command-not-found-from-bazelisk-run-scriptspull-models---the-model-list-was-being-parsed-as-shell","content":"Fix: the target now reads the one key it needs through `env_get`\n(`scripts/lib/common.sh`), which takes the line as text. Quoted and unquoted\nvalues behave the same, and nothing else in `.env` reaches the pull. A value\nthat is not a model tag - a stray `;`, a leftover quote, a trailing comment - is\nrefused by name before the daemon is asked and before any of the ~35 GB\ndownload starts:"},"1774":{"id":"/troubleshooting-88","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"The `ollama` service is IPEX-LLM on the Arc 140T, not Ollama from a registry."},"1775":{"id":"/troubleshooting-89","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in its logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."},"1776":{"id":"/troubleshooting-90","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"Stable stack (`prod-stack` addresses the `evo-t1-prod` project by default; name\n`--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` to read a stack brought up by hand under the\ncheckout folder's own project name):"},"1777":{"id":"/troubleshooting-91","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"The same command aimed at the dev stack, so with `dev-stack` and its default\nproject name (`evo-t1-dev`):"},"1778":{"id":"/troubleshooting-92","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#how-do-i-know-the-ipex-llm-server-came-up---look-for-runnersipex_llm","content":"Flags are accepted before or after `logs`; `--project-name` and `--tail` move either\nway. Only `logs` takes trailing service names, and here that trailing word is the\nservice to read."},"1779":{"id":"/troubleshooting-93","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Cause: no Spark box was reachable, so the Arc answered, and the Arc path returns tool\ncalls as plain text."},"1780":{"id":"/troubleshooting-94","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Look at the dashboard's **Agent backend** card first: its per-box widgets go to error\nstate while `agent` still answers."},"1781":{"id":"/troubleshooting-95","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Probe that endpoint (the card reads the same URL, `AGENT_MODEL_URL` in `.env`):"},"1782":{"id":"/troubleshooting-96","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Or check the whole fleet at once, from the checkout. `spark-verify.sh` takes `--json`\nand `--no-tests`; `--no-tests` skips the recipe's own probes (a 195k-token needle plus\na 1/2/4/5-client sweep, minutes per box) and leaves the quick read-only check:"},"1783":{"id":"/troubleshooting-97","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Its host list comes from the `SPARK*_URL` keys in `.env` (`SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` and\n`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL`), which is why the default run needs no arguments. Export\n`SPARK_HOSTS=\"host1 host2 ...\"` in the process environment only to check a fleet that\n`.env` does not name. A host that does not answer is a skip, not a failure."},"1784":{"id":"/troubleshooting-98","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Fix, one of these:"},"1785":{"id":"/troubleshooting-99","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Bring the TensorFold server up on the box. `<host>` is what `.env` names, and `--apply`\nwrites the per-host override file that server runs:"},"1786":{"id":"/troubleshooting-100","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Or repoint `SPARK1_OPENAI_URL`, `SPARK2_OPENAI_URL` and `SPARK3_OPENAI_URL` in `.env`\nat a tool-capable OpenAI-compatible endpoint, then restart the proxy so it re-reads\nthem. The stack runners have no `restart` subcommand, so this one stays a direct\ncompose call:"},"1787":{"id":"/troubleshooting-101","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"Either way keep `SPARK_n_API_KEY` non-empty: `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY`,\n`SPARK_3_API_KEY`. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy before it ever\nreaches the box."},"1788":{"id":"/troubleshooting-102","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-agent-mode-makes-no-tool-calls-it-narrates-and-stops---an-arc-alias-served-it","content":"The `agent` -> `coder` fallback keeps chat answering while a Spark is down, which is\nexactly when tools disappear."},"1789":{"id":"/troubleshooting-103","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"The server on each Spark box logs four shapes, and three of them are this stack's own\ntraffic rather than anything wrong with the box. Read the shape before you reach for\nthe box."},"1790":{"id":"/troubleshooting-104","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"`[tensorfold] done chatcmpl-... prompt=58 ... effort=xhigh tokens=16 finish=length`\nfollowed by `warning: a reply reached max_tokens while still thinking, so its content\nis empty`: that is the LiteLLM proxy's health check, not a user. At the pinned tag the\nproxy sends the deployment's whole `litellm_params` through a real `acompletion`, so\nthe lane's baked `reasoning_effort` rides along, and it asks for the default\n`health_check_max_tokens` of 16. A thinking reply cannot finish its reasoning in 16\ntokens, so it returns empty content and the server warns about it. The reply is a\nhealthy 200 and the deployment stays in the pool."},"1791":{"id":"/troubleshooting-105","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"`info: prompt cache: 80693 tokens, resumed at 79747, kept 1; 12 states, 6289 MiB and\n0 MiB spare of 6465 MiB (hits ..., evicted ...)`: the box is out of prompt-cache room.\n`resumed at N` is how much of the prompt it did not have to recompute, so a healthy\nconversation logs a number just under `tokens`. `resumed at 0` means it recomputed the\nwhole prompt: something evicted that conversation's state. Watch `evicted` and the\nstate count rather than any one line."},"1792":{"id":"/troubleshooting-106","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"`[tensorfold] ended chatcmpl-... reason=client-left prompt=... after=Ns`: whoever asked\nhung up. A tiny prompt abandoned at around 81 s was a health check queued behind\nlonger work; a huge prompt abandoned at around 400 s is a client with its own timeout,\nand it re-prefills from zero if it retries."},"1793":{"id":"/troubleshooting-107","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"Two things on this stack cause the evictions, and both are tuned here rather than on the\nbox:"},"1794":{"id":"/troubleshooting-108","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"Probe cadence. `health_check_interval` and `health_check_concurrency` in\n`litellm/config.yaml` decide how often every Spark deployment gets a real generation.\nThey sit at LiteLLM's own default of 300 s and a concurrency of 2 on purpose: at 30 s\nacross five deployments a round was putting more probes on a box than user requests,\nand each one took a prompt-cache state from a conversation. Auto-recovery does not\nneed 30 s -- a box that is actually down is pulled by `allowed_fails` and\n`cooldown_time` under live traffic, and the re-probe is what brings it back. If the\nwarnings come back every few seconds, that is the first key to look at."},"1795":{"id":"/troubleshooting-109","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"The 195k needle. `//scripts:spark-verify` runs the recipe's `needle.py --length 195000`\nas the window proof, and a 195k prompt the server has never seen is the largest single\neviction on the box. It now skips itself while `/health` reports `requests_running`\nabove zero, so it does not land on a box that is serving:"},"1796":{"id":"/troubleshooting-110","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#the-tensorfold-log-is-full-of-warnings-or-resumed-at-0---read-the-line-shapes-first","content":"The window is still read passively on every run without the needle, from the server's\nown startup lines (`context_pairs` and `startup_estimate` in the report), so a box\nquietly rebuilt with a shorter window is still visible; the needle is the active\nconfirmation of it. `--no-tests` skips all four recipe probes for a quick read-only\npass, and that is what `//scripts:bootstrap` runs."},"1797":{"id":"/troubleshooting-111","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Cause: the auto-compaction summary hung before its first token and nothing abandoned it.\nThe CLI's 300 s wall-clock budget does not abort a call in flight - it is evaluated only\nafter the response returns - so at 300 s its entire effect was to reject a summary that\nhad already streamed in and make the session generate it again. Meanwhile the proxy\nre-dispatched every hung attempt to the same session-pinned Spark box, so one hang cost\nup to three `request_timeout` periods. Both halves are fixed here."},"1798":{"id":"/troubleshooting-112","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Prove recurrence before you touch anything. The events are in each session's\n`updates.jsonl`, so one pass over the store counts attempts against outcomes. The\nstore is the shared profile volume, so run it where the sessions are (inside a\nworkspace), and leave the `~/.grok/sessions` path %-escaped as the CLI wrote it:"},"1799":{"id":"/troubleshooting-113","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Read `auto_compact_failed` against `auto_compact_started`. Over the 11-day window this\nwas investigated, the store held 82 failures across 15 sessions in 3 workspaces,\nescalating from a 39 per cent failure rate to 85 per cent (45 attempts in one day, 19\nof them in the worst single session), and 3 of the 15 sessions ended on a dangling\n`auto_compact_started` and were abandoned."},"1800":{"id":"/troubleshooting-114","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Which sessions, worst first, with the same `head` cap so a busy store stays readable:"},"1801":{"id":"/troubleshooting-115","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Each link in the chain, measured:"},"1802":{"id":"/troubleshooting-116","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Auto-compaction always fired at about 75 per cent of the 262144-token window, the\nsetting in force when this was measured: all 124 `auto_compact_started` events carried\n`tokens_used` between 196,636 and 214,585. Both template twins now ship\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` at 70, so a future sweep measures the\nevents firing at a lower point than this."},"1803":{"id":"/troubleshooting-117","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"The summary call rides the **session model**, the `agent*` thinking family, and\ninherits its output ceiling: captured on the wire as `max_tokens: 32768`, alongside 25\ntool schemas and the workspace's `x-litellm-session-id` affinity header.\n`[models] session_summary` is **not** that lane - it serves titles and the session\nrecap."},"1804":{"id":"/troubleshooting-118","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"In every one of the 79 wall-clock failures no first token ever arrived. Successful\ncompactions do stream: their `elapsed_ms` spans 107 s to 1915 s, and 18 of the 36 ran\npast 300 s, which is the summary the budget was discarding."},"1805":{"id":"/troubleshooting-119","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Nothing on the client aborts the hang. `signals.json` reports `inferenceIdleTimeouts`\nat 0 or 1 across the failing sessions, and no hung call was ever cut off at the idle\ndeadline, so `inference_idle_timeout_secs` (then 1200 s) is not on this path. The key\nnow ships at 1800 s in both template twins, which does not put it on this path either:\nthe CLI still does not apply it to compaction. Reproduced directly: with an upstream\nheld open and no byte sent, the CLI was still waiting at 3000 s."},"1806":{"id":"/troubleshooting-120","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"The only live bound was the proxy's `request_timeout: 1200`, multiplied by every\nattempt. Observed failure durations were 1366 s, about 2400 s and 3465 s - the deadline\nhonoured once, twice and three times."},"1807":{"id":"/troubleshooting-121","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Those re-dispatches came from this repo's own config. At the pinned tag\n`ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:v1.102.1` a stream that dies before its first chunk raises\n`MidStreamFallbackError`, which subclasses `ServiceUnavailableError`\n(`litellm/exceptions.py:1114`), and `get_num_retries_from_retry_policy` walks the\nexception MRO most-specific first, so it lands on `ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` and\nnever reaches `DefaultRetries`. A hung request never cools the box either: cooldown\nhard-excludes any exception whose string contains `APIConnectionError`, and\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` only skips deployments that answered a non-retryable\nint status code. Under the workspace-wide `x-litellm-session-id` pin every attempt\ntherefore returned to the same saturated box."},"1808":{"id":"/troubleshooting-122","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"What the fix changed, and how to land it:"},"1809":{"id":"/troubleshooting-123","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is 0, not 2, on each of the four Spark lanes (`agent`,\n`agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`): one attempt for a dead stream, so a hung\nrequest is bounded at 1200 s instead of up to 3465 s. `DefaultRetries: 2` is untouched\non purpose - TensorFold's `NoRoom` refusal arrives as `APIConnectionError`, which is\nabsent from `_RETRIES_BY_EXCEPTION_TYPE`, so that walk falls through to\n`DefaultRetries` and keeps its two attempts. The proxy reads the file at boot:"},"1810":{"id":"/troubleshooting-124","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"Both `templates/*/main.tf` twins carry `GROK_COMPACTION_WALL_CLOCK_SECS = \"1200\"` in\nthe agent `env` merge, next to `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`. The CLI reads that budget\nfrom the environment only (resolver\n`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/util/config/resolve/compaction.rs`, remote-settings\nkey `compaction_wall_clock_budget_secs`); no TOML table accepts it, so a line pasted\ninto `grok-config.toml` is silently ignored, the same trap `[mcp] max_output_bytes`\nalready documents. It is honoured: setting it to `1` makes `/compact` fail with\n`exceeded wall-clock budget 1s`. Push, then stop and start the workspace so the new\nagent env lands:"},"1811":{"id":"/troubleshooting-125","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"What it deliberately does not do:"},"1812":{"id":"/troubleshooting-126","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"It does not stop the upstream from hanging. It stops the hang being bought three times,\nand stops a finished summary being thrown away."},"1813":{"id":"/troubleshooting-127","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"`TimeoutErrorRetries: 0` would not help: the hang never becomes a `litellm.Timeout`."},"1814":{"id":"/troubleshooting-128","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is the only one of the two that can be zeroed; zeroing\n`DefaultRetries` breaks the `NoRoom` contract above."},"1815":{"id":"/troubleshooting-129","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#sessions-log-compact-failed-exceeded-wall-clock-budget-300s-runaway-generation---a-compaction-stream-that-never-sent-a-token-bought-three-times","content":"The lever, now pulled: `[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` ships at 70 in both\ntemplate twins, so auto-compaction fires near 183,500 tokens rather than the 197k-214k\nthese events measured, and the ~200k-token summary input shrinks with it. The store\nshows no size threshold separating failure from success - the successes reach the same\n197k-214k range - so this is a load reduction, not a proven cure. Re-run the first\ncommand above to measure whether the ratio moved."},"1816":{"id":"/troubleshooting-130","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"Cause: that chat has no model config on this deployment, so there is nothing to route to."},"1817":{"id":"/troubleshooting-131","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"First run this to see the plan, then run it again without the flag to apply it:"},"1818":{"id":"/troubleshooting-132","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"After a `stack up`, the seeder that runs it in the background keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (the `note:` line names the path). A chat\nthat never got wired usually means that file holds a `skipped:` line: no coder\ncontainer yet, no db container, or nobody had registered when the stack came up.\nRe-run the command above once the stack is up and somebody has signed in."},"1819":{"id":"/troubleshooting-133","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"It skips while the coder or db container is down, or nobody has registered yet, and\nre-running it repairs a provider that drifted and moves a model pin whose alias has\nsince gone unhealthy."},"1820":{"id":"/troubleshooting-134","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"The closing summary counts the auxiliary lanes that carry a pin. A `warning:` naming a\nlane means that lane falls back to Coder's own pick, and `title_generation` and\n`compaction` are the two an operator notices first: conversations stop getting titles,\nthen a long one stops at the compaction threshold. Re-run `--apply` once the alias\nbehind that lane answers."},"1821":{"id":"/troubleshooting-135","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"A `warning:` that a stored model config is disabled, is not the default, or compacts\nagainst the wrong window names a row this script found already in place. It reads\nbefore it writes and this surface has no PATCH, so the correction is the one click in\nAdmin -> Experiments -> Agent Chat the line names."},"1822":{"id":"/troubleshooting-136","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-chat-refuses-every-prompt-or-its-model-dropdown-is-empty---no-model-config","content":"A chat that starts but narrates instead of calling tools is the no-Spark case above."},"1823":{"id":"/troubleshooting-137","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-offers-a-model-that-errors-or-a-chat-never-gets-a-title---an-alias-with-no-healthy-deployment","content":"Cause: the LiteLLM alias behind that model has no deployment the router will use. The\nArc aliases (`chat`, `coder`, `coder-fast`) go unhealthy the moment their weights are\nnot in the IPEX-LLM container, which answers `model \"...\" not found, try pulling it\nfirst`, and the Spark `agent` aliases go unhealthy when no box answers."},"1824":{"id":"/troubleshooting-138","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-offers-a-model-that-errors-or-a-chat-never-gets-a-title---an-alias-with-no-healthy-deployment","content":"`//scripts:coder-agents` reads that same health view before it registers anything, so\nonce the weights are back, re-running it registers the alias and takes the\n`compaction` / `title_generation` pins off it. A `warning:` line names the alias it\nrefused to register, and the closing `summary:` counts the warnings, so a run that\nquietly wired half the model list is visible."},"1825":{"id":"/troubleshooting-139","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-agents-offers-a-model-that-errors-or-a-chat-never-gets-a-title---an-alias-with-no-healthy-deployment","content":"Titles that never appear and a long chat that dies at its context budget are the\nhelper lanes pointing at a dead alias. Re-running the script moves them."},"1826":{"id":"/troubleshooting-140","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"One lane reaches that port: a process inside the workspace. The two reads that matter\nare the probe from inside the workspace and the liveliness probe from the stack host,\nand those share nothing but the destination."},"1827":{"id":"/troubleshooting-141","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The **model lane**: a process inside the workspace dials\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` directly. `host.docker.internal` is the docker\nbridge gateway (`172.17.0.1`), compose publishes the proxy's container port 4000 on\nhost port 4000, and nothing between the two authenticates. `LITELLM_BASE_URL` and the\ncoding CLIs use this lane."},"1828":{"id":"/troubleshooting-142","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The **agent bar** keeps `:4000` off its own path: the workspace page's `litellm` tile\nis `coder_metadata` text (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out rather than a\n`coder_app`, so `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is never consulted here. Health-check the\nproxy with `curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack\nhost. The app lane under `A workspace app link answers Bad Gateway` below is the\n**VS Code Web** editor link and stays on 13337 inside the workspace."},"1829":{"id":"/troubleshooting-143","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The checks that apply:"},"1830":{"id":"/troubleshooting-144","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` must be an `https://` URL: a LAN IP, or the public coder name in\npublic mode. Never `localhost` and never `127.0.0.1`."},"1831":{"id":"/troubleshooting-145","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"The workspace container must resolve `host.docker.internal`; the model lane reaches\nLiteLLM through that host gateway, and so does `LITELLM_BASE_URL`."},"1832":{"id":"/troubleshooting-146","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-cannot-reach-litellm---coder_access_url-or-hostdockerinternal","content":"Agent traffic ignores that URL and dials `host.docker.internal:3002`; see the\n`coder_agent_url` template variable."},"1833":{"id":"/troubleshooting-147","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"The self-hosted SearXNG MCP stdio server is the search lane, declared by default\n(`search_mcp` defaults to true). Grok's native `web_search` is parked: the render sets\n`disable_web_search = true`, because no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type - LiteLLM's `agent` group answers `tools of type 'web_search' are not\nsupported: this server runs function tools only` (HTTP 400) before anything is\nfetched, and `supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it. An agent that\nstill spends a turn on a native search call and comes back with that message behaved\nas configured; there is no native lane to fall back to. Two probes split a SearXNG\nfault in half:"},"1834":{"id":"/troubleshooting-148","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` (or `scripts/search-check.sh` from a shell that\ncan reach the host gateway) dials the service directly. It reads `$SEARXNG_URL`\nbefore its `http://127.0.0.1:8181` default, and every workspace's agent env carries\nthat key, so a workspace-side probe needs no `--url`. It separates engines that\nanswered, engines that were unresponsive, and an empty result page, and it names\neach engine the instance has parked together with the reason the backend gave\n(`parked by its backend: google: access denied`). A park expires after\n`search.suspended_times` in `searxng/settings.yml`, so re-run before writing an\nengine off. `--json` adds `engines_parked` for a dashboard to trend."},"1835":{"id":"/troubleshooting-149","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"An agent whose searches come back empty while the service answers 200 is nearly\nalways parked engines rather than a broken deployment: the general-purpose engines\nare the ones the public backends bot-wall first. The same query without an\n`engines` pin usually still returns results, which is why the guidance in\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md` tells an agent to leave the engine choice to the instance."},"1836":{"id":"/troubleshooting-150","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"`grok mcp doctor searxng` inside the workspace probes the MCP hop. A healthy\nsearch-check plus a failed handshake points at the server, the URL or the port."},"1837":{"id":"/troubleshooting-151","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` covers both hops plus the config side in one\npass: the declared lanes, whether each `command=` binary is there, whether\n`~/.grok/config.toml` is still stack-managed, whether the lane's credential is\nset, whether the search lane returns results for a plain lookup rather than just\nan HTTP 200, and each server's handshake. A missing binary prints the exact\n`npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command that puts it back."},"1838":{"id":"/troubleshooting-152","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#search-never-answers-or-answers-empty---prove-the-search-lane-before-blaming-the-mcp-hop","content":"There is no second lane behind this one, so run these probes before touching anything\nelse. A service that answers but a server that will not handshake is the MCP hop; a\nserver that cannot launch at all usually means no\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`, and that binary is baked into the golden\nimage - an image that predates it needs a rebuild and a workspace recreate, not a\nconfig edit. Park the lane with `search_mcp = false` on the next template push\n(`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` applies it to every new workspace) when a session should\nhave no search tool at all. To restore the native tool, flip `disable_web_search` to\nfalse in both template twins and re-push - only for an endpoint that really serves the\ntool, or the 400 comes straight back."},"1839":{"id":"/troubleshooting-153","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"The three MCP servers (`github`, `context7`, `searxng`) ship by default, so a lane\nmissing from a session has one of three shapes: the block absent from the rendered\nconfig, the `command=` binary absent from the golden image, or the credential absent.\nRun `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` first - it reports the declared lanes, their\nbinaries, their credentials and their handshake in one pass, and it names which shape\nyou are in (an undeclared lane is a failure, a missing binary a warning).\n`grok mcp doctor` inside the workspace lists the declared servers and their handshake.\nA *park* is deliberate and looks like this: `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` /\n`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var github_mcp=false` / `--var context7_mcp=false` / `--var search_mcp=false` on a\ntemplate push. With none of those set, a missing block is a defect, not a park."},"1840":{"id":"/troubleshooting-154","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A missing `[mcp_servers.github]` or `[mcp_servers.context7]` in\n`~/.grok/config.toml` means the last push passed `--var ...=false`, or the\nworkspace started from an older template, or the file carries no stack marker at all\n(next bullet). Clear the gate and re-push, then re-materialize the file and restart\nthe workspace so the managed `config.toml` re-renders. Two routes re-materialize it:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>` from the\nstack host, for a running workspace container, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` for a host home (a laptop, CI,\nor the contributor container)."},"1841":{"id":"/troubleshooting-155","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A declared server whose `command=` the box cannot find is image drift: the\ntemplates declare a binary the running image never got, because it predates the\nnpm bake in `images/dev/Dockerfile`. Rebuild `evo-t1-dev` and recreate the\nworkspace, or install the pinned package into the running box (`npm i -g --prefix\n/opt/npm-global searxng-mcp-server@<pin>`, the pins live in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."},"1842":{"id":"/troubleshooting-156","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A `~/.grok/config.toml` carrying neither the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` line\nnor the `__evoT1Stack = true` key is a file the stack stopped managing, so neither\nthe startup script nor the seed writes it again and its default lanes never arrive.\nThe usual cause is not a hand edit: `grok mcp add` persists its own settings by\nrewriting that file and deletes every comment line, which used to revoke stack\nownership by itself. The TOML key is what survives that write, so a config rendered\nby the current templates keeps its marker through `grok mcp add`. Take the file\nover deliberately by removing both lines. To give it back: delete the file and\nrestart the workspace, or re-materialize it with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --force`."},"1843":{"id":"/troubleshooting-157","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A declared github server that fails handshake: the owner has not connected\nGitHub External Auth, so `$GH_TOKEN` is empty. Use the **GitHub reauth** app,\nthen start a new Grok session."},"1844":{"id":"/troubleshooting-158","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"A declared context7 server: an empty `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is the shipped default and\ndoes *not* fail the handshake. With no value the server sends no `Authorization`\nheader, so it handshakes and answers at the public rate limit, and only private\nlibraries are out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env. When a\ncontext7 handshake really fails the key is not the cause: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` and it says which of the block, the binary, the\ncache config or the handshake is not OK."},"1845":{"id":"/troubleshooting-159","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"No `web_fetch` either, and that is not a misconfiguration: `features.web_fetch` is\non, but on a non-xAI model endpoint that tool runs only through\n`toolset.web_fetch.proxy_endpoint` and this stack sets none. Route a URL already in\nhand to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`, anything else on the web to the `searxng`\n`search` tool, and a library, framework, CLI or setup question to `context7`\n(`resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on that id)."},"1846":{"id":"/troubleshooting-160","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#context7-or-github-tools-missing-in-grok-build---parked-mcp-block-or-a-handshake-with-nothing-to-authenticate","content":"Discover live tool names with `search_tool`, then call `use_tool` with that\nschema. Do not hardcode MCP tool names from memory."},"1847":{"id":"/troubleshooting-161","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"The **VS Code Web** button is the one app this stack still proxies: its URL is\n`/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/code-server` on the coder hostname, because\n`coder_app.code_server` sets `subdomain = false`. So a failing app link is an app-lane\nfailure, and a 200 from `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is not evidence about it."},"1848":{"id":"/troubleshooting-162","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"Two hops can answer 502 on that path: this box's nginx, when `coder:3000` will not\nresolve or connect, and Coder's own Go proxy, whose `ReverseProxy` ErrorHandler\nfires when the app upstream answers nothing. Two reads tell them apart, both from\nthe stack host:"},"1849":{"id":"/troubleshooting-163","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"A hit in the first means Coder tried the upstream and gave up, so the fault sits on\nthis box: check the devcontainer or workspace container first, then `DOCKER_HOST`."},"1850":{"id":"/troubleshooting-164","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`X-Coder-Request-Id` on the response, beside `X-Coder-Build-Version`, means Coder's\nown Go proxy answered, so Traefik and Cloudflare let the request through and the\nfault sits behind them. Nothing on this box sets `Via` or `CF-RAY`."},"1851":{"id":"/troubleshooting-165","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`X-Evo-Proxy` names the `server_name` that matched here: `coder`, `litellm`, `kasm`,\n`homepage` or `hermes`. Its absence means the request never reached this box's nginx."},"1852":{"id":"/troubleshooting-166","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"Neither header, and a `Server: cloudflare` with a `cf-ray` instead: Traefik or\nCloudflare answered, and there is nothing on this box to read."},"1853":{"id":"/troubleshooting-167","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"To take the same reading from inside the workspace, replay the path on `:8080`, the\npublished `router` port. That reaches `coder:3000` through the sidecar while\nbypassing Traefik, Authelia and the WAN, so a reply there that differs from the\nbrowser's `https://` URL names the cloud hops."},"1854":{"id":"/troubleshooting-168","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`--noproxy '*'` matters: with a proxy set in the environment curl hands the name to the\nproxy instead of resolving it, and `000` or a 5xx then describes the proxy."},"1855":{"id":"/troubleshooting-169","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`000` on the first probe means nothing listened on 4000. A `200 application/json`\nmeans LiteLLM answered, so the model lane is healthy and the app lane failed\nsomewhere else."},"1856":{"id":"/troubleshooting-170","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"`000` on the second probe means the private lane is down as well, so the public\nfailure is not a cloud-side one. `421` means the Host header matched no\n`server_name` here, which is what a rewritten Host looks like. `STACK_ROUTER_CONF`\nin `.env` names the conf those `server_name` lines live in; the vhost side of it is\nin UI and logins."},"1857":{"id":"/troubleshooting-171","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"A `404` is what this stack answers for an unknown app slug or an agent that is not\nConnected; the agent is the text after the first `.` in `<ws>.<agent>`."},"1858":{"id":"/troubleshooting-172","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-workspace-app-link-answers-bad-gateway---find-who-answered","content":"Signed into Authelia with the link still failing puts the fault behind Authelia: the\napp path is gated by the same policy as the rest of the coder name."},"1859":{"id":"/troubleshooting-173","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#workspace-stuck-in-starting---startup-is-blocking","content":"`startup_script` is blocking by design, so a failure there surfaces as a **failed**\nbuild with the reason in the workspace startup log. The container is not marked\nhealthy until the toolchain is staged."},"1860":{"id":"/troubleshooting-174","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#image-missing-tools-or-the-build-aborts-mid-layer---curl-22","content":"Cause: a pinned download returned an error. The layer prints `curl: (22)` and stops\nrather than shipping a partial image."},"1861":{"id":"/troubleshooting-175","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#image-missing-tools-or-the-build-aborts-mid-layer---curl-22","content":"Fix: check that version in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` against the upstream\nrelease, then rebuild the image."},"1862":{"id":"/troubleshooting-176","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Cause: that container started before the CLI was baked into the image, or its\nstart-time download failed."},"1863":{"id":"/troubleshooting-177","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Both of these answer nothing in that case; the first is the one to type:"},"1864":{"id":"/troubleshooting-178","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"The same probe in the other spelling: `test -x ~/.grok/bin/grok`."},"1865":{"id":"/troubleshooting-179","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"`/tmp/coder-startup-script.log` holds exactly one `curl: (28)` line when the\nstart-time download was what failed."},"1866":{"id":"/troubleshooting-180","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"One-off fix, from the stack host:"},"1867":{"id":"/troubleshooting-181","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Then open a new terminal; `PATH` there already includes `~/.grok/bin`."},"1868":{"id":"/troubleshooting-182","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-cli-was-never-installed","content":"Permanent fix: rebuild the image and the workspace, which then starts from the\nimage's own copy. See the next two entries for the command."},"1869":{"id":"/troubleshooting-183","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"Covers all three stack-shipped skill commands: `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify` and\n`/ez-create-skill`. The diagnosis and the commands below are identical for each one; only\nthe name changes, so read `<name>` as whichever of the three you are chasing."},"1870":{"id":"/troubleshooting-184","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"Cause: no published source names an upstream `/learn`, so there is none to miss. The\n`xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of docs.x.ai, every\nfirst-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog all carry zero occurrences of it, so there is no\npublished copy to vendor in. The memory family\n`/ez-learn` copies (`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory`) is a different case:\nthose are upstream commands the CLI ships itself - `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` are\n*built-in* registry entries gated behind cross-session memory, which is off by default\nhere, and `/remember` is a pager command with no such gate. The CLI does download its\nbundled platform skills at runtime (`GROK_SKILLS_BASE_URL`, `cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`)\nand that route needs an x.ai sign-in: from a workspace `api.x.ai` answers 404 and\n`cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`\nanswers 401 anonymously, and a session started with `--sandbox off` still leaves\n`~/.grok/bundled/` uncreated. LiteLLM is the sole model transport here, there is no\nx.ai credential, and the fetch never runs. So each slash command this stack ships is its\nown seed, written at workspace start by `coder_script.stack_skills` into\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` with a twin copy at\n`~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, and a missing command means that seed is\nmissing, not that the image is stale. Each carries its own `ez-` name to stay clear of\nthe upstream names: a user-scope skill overrides a same-named bundled one, so a seed\nnamed `create-skill` would hide the CLI's bundled platform skill of that name on any\nCLI that does sign in."},"1871":{"id":"/troubleshooting-185","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"An entry for `<name>` with `userInvocable: true` in the `grok inspect --json` output is\nthe pass condition: the slash command exists on that CLI, and the **Grok Build**\nterminal profile shows it because that profile execs the same binary."},"1872":{"id":"/troubleshooting-186","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"No `<name>` entry and both `ls` paths missing: the seed never landed, which means the\nworkspace booted from templates pushed before the seed existed. Re-push the templates\nand restart the workspace, or reseed into the running container from the stack host:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>`."},"1873":{"id":"/troubleshooting-187","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"One of the three missing from both homes while the other two landed points at\n`coder_script.stack_skills`, not at the startup script: that script's own log is where\nits seed body ran or failed, and the body is silent on a healthy workspace, so an empty\nlog with a missing file means the body did not run at all (restart the workspace and\nread that script's log). The trio moved out of `startup.sh.tftpl` into that one body, so\na startup-script log that says nothing no longer covers it."},"1874":{"id":"/troubleshooting-188","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"Both `ls` paths present but no `<name>` entry: the loader dropped the file. Frontmatter\nmust start on line 1, so anything above the opening `---` silently kills the skill."},"1875":{"id":"/troubleshooting-189","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"A `learn` directory still listed by the last `ls` is a copy someone took over: the boot\nand the reseed delete that path only while it still carries the stack marker, and a\nmarker-free file is user-owned, so it stays. A marker-carrying leftover is retired on\nthe next boot or reseed, and that removal only finishes the rename: no published source\nnames an upstream `/learn` for the name to free."},"1876":{"id":"/troubleshooting-190","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"A file you deleted or edited on purpose is not a fault. To keep your own version,\nedit it and delete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a marker-free\nfile is user-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass `--force`. A\ndeletion is undone - the workspace boot writes the file again whenever it is absent,\nand `refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores the\nstack's copy. Copy the payload back from `templates/common/<name>.SKILL.md` to give\nthe file back to the stack."},"1877":{"id":"/troubleshooting-191","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"`ls ~/.grok/bundled/skills` printing nothing is the expected answer, not a symptom:\nwithout the sign-in there is no bundled cache at all, on any version. Because a\nuser-scope skill overrides a same-named bundled one, and the built-in memory names are\nthe CLI's, this stack's seeds deliberately carry `ez-` names; the boot and the reseed\nretire a marker-carrying `skills/learn/SKILL.md` that predates the rename rather\nthan relying on you to delete it, since deleting a seeded copy would not retire the\nold command name anyway - the boot and the reseed write it again. What works: keep a\nmarker-free local copy only if you also stop the stack from seeding it, or disable\nthe lane with `disabled = [\"<name>\"]` under `[skills]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`."},"1878":{"id":"/troubleshooting-192","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"`command -v grok` answering `~/.grok/bin/grok` is the normal answer, not a leftover\ninstaller copy: the workspace `PATH` lists `~/.grok/bin` before `/usr/local/bin`, and\nthat entry is a symlink into `~/.grok/downloads/`, which holds one file per downloaded\nrelease and is where `grok update` installs - so moving it aside does not outlast the\nnext self-update. Compare `command -v grok` and both `--version` outputs\n(`~/.grok/bin/grok --version`, `/usr/local/bin/grok --version`) against `GROK_VERSION`\nin `images/dev/tool-versions.env`: the profile copy follows `grok update`, the image\ncopy follows the pin. Neither copy gates the three seeded commands, and the two\ndisagreeing is never on\nits own a reason to rebuild the image."},"1879":{"id":"/troubleshooting-193","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"A golden image that predates a pin bump reports an older `/usr/local/bin/grok` than\n`GROK_VERSION` (1.0.77 at the time of writing), and rebuilding `images/dev`\n(`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`) is the CLI upgrade path - but the seeded skill trio\nworks on either CLI, so this symptom alone never justifies an image rebuild."},"1880":{"id":"/troubleshooting-194","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-stack-skill-command-missing-from-grok-build-on-code-server-web---the-stacks-own-seed-is-what-provides-it","content":"Pin notes and the seeding contract: grok-build-and-agents.md."},"1881":{"id":"/troubleshooting-195","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-doctor-flags-voiceno-input-device-or-terminallimited-color---a-headless-container-and-where-you-ran-the-command","content":"Cause: both are properties of the environment. Neither points at a workspace that\nneeds repair, and neither is fixable from inside the container."},"1882":{"id":"/troubleshooting-196","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-doctor-flags-voiceno-input-device-or-terminallimited-color---a-headless-container-and-where-you-ran-the-command","content":"`voice.no-input-device`: the container has no microphone and no recorder on\nPATH. The probe checks for a recorder binary, so installing `alsa-utils` makes\nDoctor answer `available` with nothing to record from; the image ships no\nrecorder and the finding stays. The PulseAudio route that would make capture\nreal: the voice bullet in grok-build-and-agents.md."},"1883":{"id":"/troubleshooting-197","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-doctor-flags-voiceno-input-device-or-terminallimited-color---a-headless-container-and-where-you-ran-the-command","content":"`terminal.limited-color`: a tool command spawned by a running Grok session\ninherits `NO_COLOR=1`. Rerun `grok doctor` from the terminal itself and the\nreport reads `color truecolor` with `themes all`."},"1884":{"id":"/troubleshooting-198","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-doctor-flags-voiceno-input-device-or-terminallimited-color---a-headless-container-and-where-you-ran-the-command","content":"A tmux finding that survives a reseed of `~/.tmux.conf` is a reload gap: a\nclient reads `terminal-features` when it attaches. Run `tmux source-file\n~/.tmux.conf`, detach, reattach, then `tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`\nand expect `RGB` and `extkeys` in the list."},"1885":{"id":"/troubleshooting-199","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-doctor-flags-voiceno-input-device-or-terminallimited-color---a-headless-container-and-where-you-ran-the-command","content":"`terminal.ssh-wrap` asks to be run on the workstation you type `ssh` on:\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there. Nothing a workspace installs satisfies it."},"1886":{"id":"/troubleshooting-200","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#docker---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---an-older-image-or-the-wrong-preset","content":"Cause: that container was built from an image predating the Docker client toolchain,\nor from the bare-Ubuntu *Minimal shell* preset."},"1887":{"id":"/troubleshooting-201","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#docker---command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---an-older-image-or-the-wrong-preset","content":"Rebuild the image, then rebuild the workspace; the image is fixed at create time."},"1888":{"id":"/troubleshooting-202","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#permission-denied--jq-error-could-not-open-file-on-sharedvscodesettingsjson---empty-named-volume-is-root-owned","content":"Cause: Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root` 755. The\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume is empty on first use (the image has no\nfiles there to copy in), so `coder` cannot write `settings.json`. `jq` and\n`python` then fail because the file was never created. `already cloned, skipping`\non the next line is informational: the clone already exists."},"1889":{"id":"/troubleshooting-203","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#permission-denied--jq-error-could-not-open-file-on-sharedvscodesettingsjson---empty-named-volume-is-root-owned","content":"Fix: the template chowns that volume (and the shared `~/.grok` profile volume)\nto the home owner before the seed, and skips `jq`/`python` when the write\nfails. Push the templates and restart the workspace; there is nothing to delete."},"1890":{"id":"/troubleshooting-204","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-profile-missing-in-a-fresh-workspace---startup-already-finished-so-read-the-log","content":"`startup_script` is blocking, so it finished before the agent reported ready and\nbefore code-server started. Check the workspace agent logs first."},"1891":{"id":"/troubleshooting-205","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#grok-profile-missing-in-a-fresh-workspace---startup-already-finished-so-read-the-log","content":"Then run `bash -lc 'command -v grok'`. A workspace on a current image answers\n`/usr/local/bin/grok`, so an empty answer means the CLI is missing, not the profile."},"1892":{"id":"/troubleshooting-206","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"Cause: the sidecar is unhealthy, its image was never pulled, or the workspace was\ncreated with DinD off."},"1893":{"id":"/troubleshooting-207","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"`DOCKER_HOST` is injected per template, not per toggle. `templates/workspace` sets\nit only while the toggle is on; `templates/devcontainer` sets it always, because\nthat template always provisions the sidecar and the `devcontainer` CLI has to\nreach its daemon either way. So a set `DOCKER_HOST` tells you the template; the\n*docker-in-docker* metadata tile tells you the state (the devcontainer template\nalways reads `on (tcp://dind:2375)`, the workspace one reads `off` when unset)."},"1894":{"id":"/troubleshooting-208","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"Three separate reads, from the stack host. The first prints `unhealthy` rather than\nan error when the daemon fails its own probe, and `no such container` when the\nworkspace has no sidecar at all:"},"1895":{"id":"/troubleshooting-209","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#cannot-connect-to-the-docker-daemon-at-tcpdind2375---the-sidecar-or-dind-off","content":"That probe only asks whether the daemon answers `docker version`. It says nothing\nabout the workspace that used to reach it."},"1896":{"id":"/troubleshooting-210","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#did-not-find-docker-image-docker2981-dind---plan-time-failure-not-runtime","content":"Cause: that image is not in the host's local image store, and Terraform never pulls\nit. The `docker_image` data source only inspects the store."},"1897":{"id":"/troubleshooting-211","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#did-not-find-docker-image-docker2981-dind---plan-time-failure-not-runtime","content":"So the workspace build fails at **plan** time, before anything is created, and it\nprints in the workspace's build log rather than anywhere inside the workspace."},"1898":{"id":"/troubleshooting-212","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#did-not-find-docker-image-docker2981-dind---plan-time-failure-not-runtime","content":"Fix with the `docker pull` above, or turn DinD off for that workspace."},"1899":{"id":"/troubleshooting-213","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` queues, then dies during\n\"Initializing Terraform Directory\". `workspace` cannot list versions for\n`coder/coder` and `kreuzwerker/docker` on `registry.terraform.io`;\n`devcontainer` cannot fetch `module \"devcontainers_cli\"` from\n`registry.coder.com`. Both lookups fail with\n`dial tcp: lookup … on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`. Coder itself\nstays up; `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs coder` repeats the same Terraform\nlines."},"1900":{"id":"/troubleshooting-214","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Cause: provisionerd runs `terraform init` inside the `coder` container.\nDocker's embedded DNS (`127.0.0.11`) forwards names it does not know to\nthe host resolver. When that resolver is Tailscale MagicDNS only, or\nDocker/Kasm iptables has made `100.100.100.100` unreachable from a\ncontainer, every public lookup SERVFAILs. The host CLI is fine; only the\nprovisioner job dies. The lockfile reuse lines (`Reusing previous version\nfrom the dependency lock file`) do not skip the registry: Terraform still\nfetches the discovery document."},"1901":{"id":"/troubleshooting-215","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Fix: `docker-compose.yml` sets `dns: [1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8]` on the `coder`\nservice (embedded DNS still answers `db` and other compose names). Both\ntemplates pin the same resolvers on the workspace and DinD containers so\na later `git clone` of `github.com` does not hit the same SERVFAIL. Recreate\n`coder` so the new upstreams apply, then push:"},"1902":{"id":"/troubleshooting-216","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#lookup-registryterraformio-on-127001153-server-misbehaving-from-push-template---coder-cannot-reach-the-terraform-registry","content":"Host `git pull` still needs a public resolver beside MagicDNS when the host\nitself is MagicDNS-only (heading above; `setup-host --repair` on Linux).\nExisting workspaces pick up the container DNS on the next start after this\ntemplate version is active."},"1903":{"id":"/troubleshooting-217","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#pq-value-too-long-for-type-character-varying256-from-push-template---editor-icon-data-url","content":"Symptom: `coder templates push` plans, then dies during \"Cleaning Up\" with\n`internal provisionerserver error: execute transaction: insert resource: insert\nagent app: upsert app: pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`.\nCoder logs `coder_app's id was unexpectedly empty` and then that Postgres error\nat `inserting template import job resource`."},"1904":{"id":"/troubleshooting-218","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#pq-value-too-long-for-type-character-varying256-from-push-template---editor-icon-data-url","content":"Cause: both templates inline `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` URL on `coder_app.code_server`. Coder v2.36 stores\n`workspace_apps.icon` as `varchar(256)`. A multi-kilobyte SVG produces a data\nURL thousands of characters long, and the insert fails. The provisioner\nreports it at `insert agent app: upsert app`."},"1905":{"id":"/troubleshooting-219","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#pq-value-too-long-for-type-character-varying256-from-push-template---editor-icon-data-url","content":"Fix: the SVG is a compact ribbon whose data URL fits the column, and\n`push-template` rejects a too-large copy locally before talking to the server.\nPull this commit onto the stack host and re-run:"},"1906":{"id":"/troubleshooting-220","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-no-configuration-files-from-push-template---runfiles-symlinks-in-the-tar","content":"Symptom: both templates fail during \"Detecting persistent resources\" with\n`Terraform initialized in an empty directory!` / `Error: No configuration files`.\nCoder logs `unpacking source archive  size_bytes=30720` (tens of KB, not the\n~120 KB of real `.tf` / `.tftpl` contents)."},"1907":{"id":"/troubleshooting-221","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-no-configuration-files-from-push-template---runfiles-symlinks-in-the-tar","content":"Cause: `bazelisk run` executes from the runfiles tree, where `templates/**` are\nsymlinks into the execroot. A tar that stores those links unpacks in the\nprovisioner as dangling paths, so Terraform sees no `.tf` files. The two\n`templates/common/` payloads still land (copied with `cp`, which follows\nlinks), which is why the archive is small rather than empty."},"1908":{"id":"/troubleshooting-222","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#error-no-configuration-files-from-push-template---runfiles-symlinks-in-the-tar","content":"Fix: `push-template` now prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the live\ncheckout) and stages with `tar -h` so the archive holds regular files. Pull\nthis commit onto the stack host and re-run:"},"1909":{"id":"/troubleshooting-223","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#repo_root-unbound-variable-from-push-template---the-size-guard-read-an-ambient-root","content":"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints `== Preflight ==` and\n`== Variables ==`, then aborts inside `== Templates ==` with\n`push-template.sh: line 740: REPO_ROOT: unbound variable` and never prints a\n`summary:` line. Nothing was pushed."},"1910":{"id":"/troubleshooting-224","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#repo_root-unbound-variable-from-push-template---the-size-guard-read-an-ambient-root","content":"Cause: `startup_size_guard` looks up a payload the twin does not keep beside its\nown `main.tf` under `templates/common/`, and it did that by reading an ambient\n`REPO_ROOT`. Nothing in `scripts/` ever sets that variable: the checkout is the\n`root` the script resolved and `cd`'d into at startup. Under the script's own\n`set -u` the read aborts the run. It only fires for a template that inlines a\ncanonical payload, which is why a twin carrying its own copy never showed it."},"1911":{"id":"/troubleshooting-225","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#repo_root-unbound-variable-from-push-template---the-size-guard-read-an-ambient-root","content":"Fix: the guard measures against the resolved `root`, and a parser it cannot open\nis now a hard stop rather than a silent \"everything fits\". Pull this commit onto\nthe stack host and re-run:"},"1912":{"id":"/troubleshooting-226","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#terraformlockhcl-is-missing---a-note-not-a-failure","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints it because no lockfile is committed\nunder `templates/`, and Coder pushes the directory without one."},"1913":{"id":"/troubleshooting-227","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#terraformlockhcl-is-missing---a-note-not-a-failure","content":"Pin provider versions locally only if you want to, per template directory. The push\ngoes ahead either way, and `workspace` and `devcontainer` are the two names:"},"1914":{"id":"/troubleshooting-228","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"The usual wrapper is `Failed to check server \"https://...\" for first user, is the URL\ncorrect and is coder accessible from your browser?`"},"1915":{"id":"/troubleshooting-229","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"Cause: the CLI is talking to a public subdomain, and Authelia answers its API calls\nwith the sign-in page."},"1916":{"id":"/troubleshooting-230","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"This is not a broken credential. It happens with a valid token too, because Authelia\ngates every route."},"1917":{"id":"/troubleshooting-231","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"Fix: point the CLI at the container's loopback listener, `http://127.0.0.1:3000`,\nwhich nothing can gate. See \"Coder workspace templates\" in README.md."},"1918":{"id":"/troubleshooting-232","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpected-non-json-response-texthtml-charsetutf-8---authelia-answered-the-cli","content":"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` itself stays the public name: browsers need it, and only CLI\ntraffic should not use it."},"1919":{"id":"/troubleshooting-233","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#installed-terraform-version-newer-than-expected---harmless","content":"Cause: an upstream pin. The Coder image ships its own provisioner binary at\n`/usr/local/bin/terraform` (1.15.5 in `coder:v2.36.6`) and Coder caps the version it\nwas tested against."},"1920":{"id":"/troubleshooting-234","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#installed-terraform-version-newer-than-expected---harmless","content":"`coder server` logs it; the host `coder` CLI does not."},"1921":{"id":"/troubleshooting-235","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#installed-terraform-version-newer-than-expected---harmless","content":"Builds still succeed, so verify with a workspace build before acting on it. Nothing\nin this repo's compose file sets the provisioner's Terraform."},"1922":{"id":"/troubleshooting-236","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpanded-count-argument-on-block-coder_appgithub_app_install---apps-were-gated-on-start_count","content":"Cause: `coder/preview` warns when a `coder_app` `count` is not expanded at\nworkspace-create preview. `start_count` is that value. The default is then\nassumed to be 1. The only `coder_app` carrying a `count` today is\n`coder_app.github_app_install`, and it counts on `github_auth_id`, which the\npreview does expand."},"1923":{"id":"/troubleshooting-237","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#unexpanded-count-argument-on-block-coder_appgithub_app_install---apps-were-gated-on-start_count","content":"Fix: `coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count` (scripts, metadata\nand the container still are). Push the templates; new creates stop showing the\nwarning. Existing workspaces pick it up on the next update to the new template\nversion."},"1924":{"id":"/troubleshooting-238","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#bash--c-line-32-syntax-error-near-unexpected-token--after-running-code-server-in-the-background---hcl--in-the-editor-wait-loop","content":"Cause: Coder runs every `coder_script` as `bash -c`. HCL heredocs in `main.tf` only\nescape `$${name}` to `${name}`; `$$((` is two literal dollars. The editor wait loop\nthen becomes `waited=<pid>((waited + 1))`, which is the syntax error. The echo\nbefore the loop still prints. `already cloned, skipping` on the next line is the\nconcurrent clone stage, not the failure."},"1925":{"id":"/troubleshooting-239","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#bash--c-line-32-syntax-error-near-unexpected-token--after-running-code-server-in-the-background---hcl--in-the-editor-wait-loop","content":"Fix: the increment is `waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar). Push the templates and\nrestart so the editor script relaunches."},"1926":{"id":"/troubleshooting-240","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-opens-homecoder-instead-of-the-cloned-repository---last-workspace-won","content":"Cause: path-based Coder apps drop the `?folder=` query before the browser sees\nit, and code-server restores the last opened workspace (often `/home/coder`).\nThe clone itself is fine (`already cloned, skipping` is not a failure)."},"1927":{"id":"/troubleshooting-241","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-opens-homecoder-instead-of-the-cloned-repository---last-workspace-won","content":"Fix: the inlined fork starts code-server with `--ignore-last-opened` and the\nclone folder as a positional argument (`/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` on the\n`workspace` template). Push the templates and restart so the editor process\nrelaunches."},"1928":{"id":"/troubleshooting-242","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"Check 1: the code-server install script's log is green in the workspace's Scripts/Log\ntab."},"1929":{"id":"/troubleshooting-243","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"Check 2: this answers from inside the workspace:"},"1930":{"id":"/troubleshooting-244","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"Check 3: the browser is on `https://` with a trusted cert. Plain `http://` is an\ninsecure context, which is what breaks `crypto.randomUUID()`; see \"Public subdomains\n(optional)\" in README.md."},"1931":{"id":"/troubleshooting-245","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"On `devcontainer`:"},"1932":{"id":"/troubleshooting-246","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"The dev container must build first, and that needs `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nin the cloned repository."},"1933":{"id":"/troubleshooting-247","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"A repo that ships none wants the **Plain clone** preset, or the dev-container toggle\nset to **No** at create time. Either one runs code-server on the plain workspace\ncontainer and opens the clone there."},"1934":{"id":"/troubleshooting-248","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#browser-editor-code-server-stays-unhealthy---check-these-three-in-order","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` decides that for you, per repo:"},"1935":{"id":"/troubleshooting-249","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-copy-from-an-app-inside-a-tmux-pane-never-reaches-the-laptop-clipboard-in-vs-code-web---the-browser-build-drops-osc-52","content":"Cause: an app inside the pane - vim's `+` register, anything that emits OSC 52 -\npushes its copy out through the pane, `set-clipboard on` with\n`allow-passthrough on` forwards it, and code-server's terminal hands the escape to\nnobody. There is no setting for it: the shipped 4.145.0 registers no OSC 52 key,\nand upstream\nmicrosoft/vscode#318254 is\nopen with two unmerged fixes. Over ssh from Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm or iTerm2 the\nsame copy lands, which puts the drop in the browser and not in tmux."},"1936":{"id":"/troubleshooting-250","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-copy-from-an-app-inside-a-tmux-pane-never-reaches-the-laptop-clipboard-in-vs-code-web---the-browser-build-drops-osc-52","content":"Fix: there is nothing to turn on in tmux. Copy the text the way the rest of the tab\ndoes - a plain drag is the client's own highlight, because tmux asks for no mouse\nreporting (measured on the shipped tmux 3.4: an attach with `mouse on` writes\n`ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the client, one with it off writes\nnone), and the settings seed's `terminal.integrated.copyOnSelection: true` puts\nthat highlight on the machine clipboard with no chord (the headless harness on\n4.145.0 recorded `navigator.clipboard.writeText` firing on a client selection,\nbefore this switch). Right-click pastes\nit back (`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` pastes when nothing\nis selected) and middle-click pastes it too (`middleClickBehavior` pinned to\n`\"paste\"`). tmux keeps no buffer of its own, so `Prefix-]` is unbound and there is\nno `tmux show-buffer` to read: the machine clipboard is the only clipboard here.\n`Prefix m` is the wheel switch, not a copy route: one press hands the wheel to tmux\nfor a long scroll, the next hands it back. Paste is `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click in\na bash terminal and a tmux pane alike, and `Ctrl+V` is not a paste on a secure\norigin (the pane gets the `\\x16` byte). Both directions, and what a pane app may\nstill do over ssh, are in\nCopy and paste between a tmux pane and the machine you type on."},"1937":{"id":"/troubleshooting-251","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-copy-from-an-app-inside-a-tmux-pane-never-reaches-the-laptop-clipboard-in-vs-code-web---the-browser-build-drops-osc-52","content":"Output already scrolled off the screen: `Prefix-[` is a viewer, not a copier -\nwalk up with `k`, `PPage` or `Ctrl-u` until the line is painted on the screen,\nselect it with a client drag, then `q`. A selection made *inside* copy-mode is\ninert: every copy binding in `copy-mode-vi` is unbound, so nothing ends it."},"1938":{"id":"/troubleshooting-252","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-copy-from-an-app-inside-a-tmux-pane-never-reaches-the-laptop-clipboard-in-vs-code-web---the-browser-build-drops-osc-52","content":"Copy and paste both dead, in both directions, a drag included: read the next\nentry. This one is a pane-app-only symptom, and a broken origin breaks the\neditor's own chords too."},"1939":{"id":"/troubleshooting-253","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#a-copy-from-an-app-inside-a-tmux-pane-never-reaches-the-laptop-clipboard-in-vs-code-web---the-browser-build-drops-osc-52","content":"`history-limit` reads 100000 but a pane still forgets old output: tmux applies\nthe option when the pane is created, so that pane keeps the limit it was born\nwith. Open a new pane, or restart the server with `tmux kill-server`."},"1940":{"id":"/troubleshooting-254","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#copy-and-paste-both-do-nothing-in-vs-code-web---the-page-is-not-a-secure-context","content":"Cause: the browser exposes `navigator.clipboard` to a secure context only, and\nCoder's LAN door is plain HTTP by default (`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is the LAN\naddress). Opened that way the editor has no clipboard API to call, so\nright-click paste stops with VS Code's own toast - \"This browser doesn't support\nthe clipboard.readText API needed to trigger a paste, try Ctrl+V instead.\" - and\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` only works at all through the browser's native paste event. A\nClipboard site permission that was once denied does the same damage on an https\norigin, and there VS Code answers `NotAllowedError: Read permission denied`."},"1941":{"id":"/troubleshooting-255","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#copy-and-paste-both-do-nothing-in-vs-code-web---the-page-is-not-a-secure-context","content":"Fix: open the editor on the TLS public name (the `coder-<host>.<domain>` door\nTraefik holds a real certificate for) rather than `http://<LAN-address>`, and\ngive the site Clipboard permission - Chrome: the lock icon in the address bar,\nSite settings, Clipboard, Allow. The templates are not the variable here: both\ndoors serve the same payload, so a workspace that pastes on one URL and does not\non the other is an origin problem. What still works on plain HTTP: copy with a\ndrag - the selection is the client's own, since tmux asks for no mouse reporting,\nand `copyOnSelection`'s `document.execCommand('copy')` fallback really lands - and\npaste with `Ctrl+V`, the browser's own paste event - which is exactly what VS Code's\ntoast tells you to do."},"1942":{"id":"/troubleshooting-256","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-tab-in-chrome-creeps-to-gigabytes-or-freezes-now-and-then---every-tab-was-another-mirror-of-one-pane","content":"Cause: each client attached to a tmux session is a full xterm.js in its own\nChrome tab re-parsing the same stream, each with a scrollback buffer\npreallocated from `terminal.integrated.scrollback` (VS Code preallocates the\nbuffer from the value, and xterm.js keeps 3 `Uint32` words per column per\nline), plus a per-client encode in the tmux server and a re-wrap repaint for\nevery client each time any of them activates or resizes. Count the clients\nagainst the panes:"},"1943":{"id":"/troubleshooting-257","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-tab-in-chrome-creeps-to-gigabytes-or-freezes-now-and-then---every-tab-was-another-mirror-of-one-pane","content":"More clients than you have visible terminals means the tabs are mirrors."},"1944":{"id":"/troubleshooting-258","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-tab-in-chrome-creeps-to-gigabytes-or-freezes-now-and-then---every-tab-was-another-mirror-of-one-pane","content":"Fix: the seed keeps one pane per visible terminal. `vscode-terminal-tmux`\nreattaches a folder's session only while nothing is attached to it, and opens a\nnumbered sibling (`vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ...) beside live ones, so an\nextra tab is a new pane rather than a mirror. Every session target in that\nhelper carries tmux's `=` exact-match prefix, because a bare name is also\nanswered by any session starting with it - once `foo` is closed, `foo-2` would\notherwise answer for it and put a second client on a live pane. And when tmux\n3.4 does take a session down under a tab that is reopening it, the helper raises\na fresh one rather than letting the tab exit with it. The settings seed drops\n`terminal.integrated.scrollback` to 2000 - the browser keeps a short viewport\nand tmux's `history-limit 100000` behind `Prefix-[` holds the deep history - and\nturns `cursorBlinking` off, and `templates/common/tmux.conf` sets\n`set -s escape-time 10` and `set -g focus-events on`. The first is the freeze:\n3.4 holds back any Escape it reads for 500 ms to decide whether a sequence\nfollows, so Alt and the arrow keys feel like dropped input and a focus-in can\nwait that long - twice, with `set-timing` - before tmux repaints. Upstream cut\nthe default to 10 ms in 3.5, and 0 - the value vim guides suggest - is the one\nto avoid: measured on this build, a throttled tab that delivers Esc and the next\ncharacter more than 10 ms apart loses its Alt chords, and 0 makes that far\nlikelier. `focus-events` is what gives tmux a focused client to work with at\nall. `aggressive-resize` stays at its default on purpose: its own entry in\n`options-table.c` scopes it to the `smallest` and `manual` `window-size` modes,\nthe shipped default is `latest`, and tmux(1) calls it \"poor for interactive\nprograms such as shells\". Both files reseed on the next start only while they\nstill carry the stack marker, so delete either one you edited by hand. Before\ndebugging a setting that will not take: tmux reads `~/.tmux.conf` and then\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/tmux.conf` and `~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf`, last file read\nwins, so either of those quietly outranks everything the seed writes.\n`focus-events` is read when a client attaches, so the tabs need to be gone for\nit to land: close them all after the restart and reopen one per pane."},"1945":{"id":"/troubleshooting-259","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-tab-in-chrome-creeps-to-gigabytes-or-freezes-now-and-then---every-tab-was-another-mirror-of-one-pane","content":"If a tab keeps dying on you and you want to know whether the pane's shell\nexited or tmux took the session down under the tab, run `tmux set -w\nremain-on-exit on` in it first: the pane then stays on screen with its exit\nstatus in `#{pane_dead_status}` instead of vanishing, which is what the\ntmux FAQ recommends for exactly this\nquestion. Turn it back off afterwards - a dead pane is not yours to keep."},"1946":{"id":"/troubleshooting-260","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#vs-code-web-tab-in-chrome-creeps-to-gigabytes-or-freezes-now-and-then---every-tab-was-another-mirror-of-one-pane","content":"Residual: a tab you hide rather than close keeps its xterm instance alive and\nstill buffers whatever the pane prints, and nothing in the stack caps that.\nNeither does tmux: 3.4 has no per-client output limit, and an ordinary\nattached client that stops draining its pty is never dropped (measured here:\none stayed attached past 15 minutes while the server kept serving commands).\nThe only \"too far behind\" disconnect is in control mode, behind a hardcoded\n300 s age in `control.c`, which a VS Code tab never uses.\nmicrosoft/vscode#327437\nis open (a heap snapshot showed ~38 live terminals holding ~80 MB of a 484 MB\nrenderer heap) and\ncoder/code-server#6413\nreports browser-tab RAM reaching several GB with a continuous-output terminal\nleft running after the tab is closed; neither has a maintainer mitigation.\nClosing the tab is the mitigation, and the numbered sessions are what make the\nlive set match the tabs you can see."},"1947":{"id":"/troubleshooting-261","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"What you see: `git push` answers `remote: Invalid username or token. Password\nauthentication is not supported for Git operations.`, or `gh auth status` calls the\ntoken invalid. An expired token and a never-connected account read the same from that\nmessage, so ask which one you have before you touch either."},"1948":{"id":"/troubleshooting-262","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"What its answers mean here: `REJECTED` beside `env token (GITHUB_TOKEN/GH_TOKEN)` is an\nexpired mint (401 refuses the credential itself, which is what an hour-old injected\ncopy is); `NO GitHub credential in the chain slot` means nothing is connected and\nnothing can be minted; `PUSH-READY` means the credential is fine and you want\nthe push-denied entry\ninstead."},"1949":{"id":"/troubleshooting-263","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"The one command, whichever of the two it was:"},"1950":{"id":"/troubleshooting-264","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"Inside a workspace it repairs that workspace; on the stack host `fix <container>\n[repo-url]` names the one workspace it repairs, and a host run with no container is\nrefused for exactly that reason. It mints a live token through Coder external-auth (the\ninjected pair is never trusted, because it is the frozen copy that expired), writes the\ngh auth store, points git's `github.com` and `gist.github.com` credential helper at that\nstore through a form that clears the env pair first, then re-runs the write probe and\nprints the verdict again, so it proves itself. It is idempotent and it never prints a\ntoken."},"1951":{"id":"/troubleshooting-265","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"When nothing can be minted AND the store is empty its line begins `HANDOFF` and names\nthe three clicks below. That line is the boundary: no command run in that workspace can\ndo them."},"1952":{"id":"/troubleshooting-266","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"On the workspace page, click **GitHub reauth**. Coder opens `/external-auth/<id>`\n(device flow for the injected App, OAuth for a custom app in `coder.env`). If the\npage says you are already authenticated, click **Reauthenticate**."},"1953":{"id":"/troubleshooting-267","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"If that page says \"You must install the GitHub App to clone private repositories\"\nand lists no accounts, the device-flow login already succeeded. Click **Install\nGitHub App** on the workspace (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant the private repos, then restart. Coder's injected provider omits\n`AppInstallURL`, so the warning on `/external-auth/github` is unlinked plaintext."},"1954":{"id":"/troubleshooting-268","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"Restart the workspace afterwards so `GH_TOKEN` and the gh auth store match the new\ngrant. `fix` makes git push work without the restart; the injected pair itself only\nchanges when the workspace restarts or `gh_token_refresh` next runs."},"1955":{"id":"/troubleshooting-269","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"Existing workspaces pick up the buttons after `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`\nand a workspace update to the new template version."},"1956":{"id":"/troubleshooting-270","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#github-token-expired-or-the-workspace-has-no-github-account---github-reauth","content":"How the credential is assembled, and what every slot in that report is, is one page:\nGitHub credentials for a workspace."},"1957":{"id":"/troubleshooting-271","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"What you see, in that `coder_script`'s log, is its own two lines:"},"1958":{"id":"/troubleshooting-272","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"Cause: the refresh script inherits `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` from the agent, and\n`gh auth login --with-token` refuses stdin while those names are set (`The value of the\nGH_TOKEN environment variable is being used for authentication`). Current templates\nunset the pair, log out, then log in with `--git-protocol https`, and print gh's\nstderr (token redacted) if that still fails - which is the line above."},"1959":{"id":"/troubleshooting-273","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"The one command does the same three steps and then proves them:"},"1960":{"id":"/troubleshooting-274","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"Then read the store with the pair cleared, because `gh auth status` on its own reports\nthe env pair rather than what is stored:"},"1961":{"id":"/troubleshooting-275","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"What each answer means: a store that now holds a token means git is repaired and the\nrefresh script keeps it fresh (start, then every 20 minutes); `gh refused the token, so the\nstore is unchanged` means the credential is revoked or the account disconnected, so run\n`check` to see what github.com says about it, then take the clicks from\nthe reauth entry;\n`cannot install the credential helper - gh is not installed` is\nthe gh-not-installed entry."},"1962":{"id":"/troubleshooting-276","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh_token_refresh-says-gh-would-not-store-the-minted-token","content":"`HANDOFF` means there is no credential anywhere in reach: click **GitHub reauth**,\nfinish device-code login, install the Coder GitHub App if the reauth page says to, and\nrestart. Those clicks are in\nthe reauth entry,\nand the script's own note on a workspace with no connected account points at the same\ntwo buttons."},"1963":{"id":"/troubleshooting-277","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"What you see is one of: `remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is\nnot supported for Git operations.`, `remote: Repository not found.`, `fatal:\nAuthentication failed for 'https://github.com/...'`, or `fatal: could not read Username\nfor 'https://github.com'`. None of them names the token or its age, and the first is\nwhat a refused token plus a silent askpass looks like, not anything to do with\npasswords."},"1964":{"id":"/troubleshooting-278","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"The one command, run where the push was attempted, naming the repository:"},"1965":{"id":"/troubleshooting-279","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"It does not ask \"is there a credential\" and it does not trust the environment: it runs\ngit's own configured helper the way git runs it, takes the password that would go on the\nwire, and probes THAT credential against the endpoint `git push` negotiates through -\none GET of `<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`. A read-only credential\nprints REFUSED-GRANT, never a pass; a healthy-but-shadowed one prints `push would FAIL`,\nnever `ok`. Inside a workspace (or the contributor devcontainer) it runs SELF mode with\nno docker and prints one `self:` line per credential; on the stack host it enumerates\nthe labeled workspace containers, or probes a named container alone. Flags may go before\nor after the positionals, and a path below `owner/repo` is refused rather than probed."},"1966":{"id":"/troubleshooting-280","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"The repair is also one command, and every `push would FAIL` line names it:"},"1967":{"id":"/troubleshooting-281","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Read the report by its last line, then by these. `git would send this credential\n(source: <slots>)` is the credential a real push uses, in git's consult order; the same\ntoken held by several slots is named once, as one credential with several names. A plain\n`credential present (source: <slots>)` is a credential that exists and that git never\nreaches."},"1968":{"id":"/troubleshooting-282","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`[shadowed: the credential above outranks this slot, ...]` is the most misread line in\nthe report: the credential under it is healthy and git will not use it while the env\npair stands. `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN <command>` makes git or gh use that slot\nfor one command; `fix` makes it permanent for git. See\nthe Bad credentials entry."},"1969":{"id":"/troubleshooting-283","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`NO GitHub credential in the <slot> slot (git will prompt and be refused)` means there\nis nothing to send, so the account comes first:\nthe reauth entry."},"1970":{"id":"/troubleshooting-284","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"The note line, `note: GIT_ASKPASS is set but answers no credential, which is what turns\na refused token into \"Password authentication is not supported for Git operations.\"; a\nworking credential helper makes it moot`, is the second half of the story. The agent env\nexports `GIT_ASKPASS` pointing at the `coder` binary the agent staged under\n`/tmp/coder.<rand>/`, and in a measured workspace that binary answered every prompt, and\n`coder gitaskpass` itself, with `unrecognized subcommand` and no output, so git got no\nanswer at all and fell through to prompting. Clearing `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` helps\nonly while the helper answers; with a silent helper it changes nothing."},"1971":{"id":"/troubleshooting-285","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`push would WORK (the credential git consults writes <repo>)` and `push would FAIL` are\nthe lines to gate on, keyed to the credential git would really send; `check` exits\nnon-zero on FAIL so a script or an agent can refuse to push on it."},"1972":{"id":"/troubleshooting-286","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"The verdicts, and what each one costs you:"},"1973":{"id":"/troubleshooting-287","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`PUSH-READY` (200 advertising refs): a push would negotiate. If the push still fails,\nthe credential is not the failing thing."},"1974":{"id":"/troubleshooting-288","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`REFUSED-GRANT` (403): that credential authenticates and every write to the repo is\ndenied, which is the push 403."},"1975":{"id":"/troubleshooting-289","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`NOT VISIBLE` (404): a private repo hidden from it answers 404, and so does an absent\none; clone and push would both fail."},"1976":{"id":"/troubleshooting-290","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`REJECTED` (401): github.com refused the credential itself, as it refuses an expired or\nrevoked token - the fate of the injected pair about an hour after start. `fix` re-mints."},"1977":{"id":"/troubleshooting-291","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`no useful answer`: github.com gave nothing useful (offline? probe again). A 200 that\ndoes not advertise refs is this verdict too: a push is not proven."},"1978":{"id":"/troubleshooting-292","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"`--source=chain` (the default) probes every credential that could answer for that\nrepository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or `--source=mint` answers one slot and says\nbeside the answer that git and gh consult the env pair first."},"1979":{"id":"/troubleshooting-293","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Reads prove nothing about push, so nothing here asks a read question: a public repo\nanswers a read for any authenticated caller, so a credential that READS a repo can still\nbe refused every WRITE to it. Go to the GitHub side only after the report says\nREFUSED-GRANT or NOT VISIBLE."},"1980":{"id":"/troubleshooting-294","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"The grant behind the credential - the GitHub App installation that the External Auth\nprovider (id `github` by default) minted its token from - has to include the repository\nthe workspace clones or pushes: GitHub -> Settings -> Applications -> `<app>` ->\n**Configure** -> Repository access."},"1981":{"id":"/troubleshooting-295","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"An empty github.com/settings/apps page is the usual\nsurprise here: it means no App is installed under your account, so there is no\n**Configure** link to click and nothing to grant, while the provider itself stays\nconfigured and keeps minting tokens that authenticate. Install Coder's App from the\nworkspace **Install GitHub App** button, or at\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new,\ngrant the repo, then restart. A private repository is never visible to a credential\nthat was never granted it."},"1982":{"id":"/troubleshooting-296","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Then check the URL scheme, which no probe can fix: that credential covers\n`https://github.com/...` remotes only, so a `git@github.com:` or `ssh://` URL gets\nneither helper nor askpass token and needs an SSH key you placed yourself, which this\nstack provisions nowhere."},"1983":{"id":"/troubleshooting-297","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Then check **how** the credential is sent, because the two GitHub endpoints disagree\nabout the scheme. `api.github.com` and `gh` accept `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The\ngit data endpoint refuses it: one token answered `/user` with `200` and\n`<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack` with `401` plus\n`remote: invalid credentials` while the token rode a bearer header. git has to send it\nas **Basic** auth with the token as the password. The username text is not checked\n(measured on Coder v2.36.6: `toxicoder`, `x-access-token`, `somebody-else` and `octocat`\neach returned `200`), but an empty username or an empty password is refused, and the\nempty password is what produces the misleading `Invalid username or token. Password\nauthentication is not supported for Git operations.`"},"1984":{"id":"/troubleshooting-298","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Do not hand-roll a credential helper. Measured across 222 past sessions in this\nworkspace: 29 hit a GitHub auth error (`Bad credentials` 19, `Password authentication is\nnot supported` 11, `could not read Username` 5, `unrecognized subcommand` 4), and agents\nhand-rolled their own askpass scripts in 5 sessions and hand-edited `credential.helper`\nin 4. Both were wrong the same way: they fought the credential-helper precedence that\n`fix` repairs, and they put a live token where `ps` could read it, while the line that\nnames the real cause was one `check` away. `fix` is idempotent, keeps every token off\nargv and out of any file it creates, and repairs the helper plus the store it reads."},"1985":{"id":"/troubleshooting-299","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Your own credential stays gated on the report: install one only after `check` has\nanswered `REFUSED-GRANT`, `NOT VISIBLE` or `REJECTED`. No verdict short of those\njustifies a pasted token, and the repo rule is that a workspace's GitHub credential\ncomes from the External Auth provider alone. From the stack host (it drives `docker\nexec`, so it has no SELF path), on stdin:"},"1986":{"id":"/troubleshooting-300","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"It installs that credential into that one workspace's gh auth store and then\nre-installs the env-proof helper that `gh auth setup-git` would otherwise leave behind,\nsince that helper goes straight back to echoing a stale env pair over the token just\npasted. A PAT is never typed as an argument: argv is readable through `ps` and the\ntoken lands in shell history. Pipe it in (`echo \"$TOKEN\" | bazelisk run\n//scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> -`) or run `auth <container>` alone and\nanswer the hidden prompt. In the line above, `-` is the only argument after the\ncontainer and `< PAT` is a shell redirect, not an argument. It REPLACES the per-start\nminted credential for that workspace, and while the account stays connected the next\n`gh_token_refresh` run rewrites the store, so treat a pasted PAT as a stand-in and\nrotate or remove it in GitHub -> Applications when the work ends."},"1987":{"id":"/troubleshooting-301","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#private-repo-clone-or-push-denied-while-a-credential-is-present---the-grant-not-the-format","content":"Full usage, verdict kinds and both argument orders:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- -h`."},"1988":{"id":"/troubleshooting-302","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-answers-bad-credentials---the-stale-env-pair-not-the-store","content":"What you see, inside a workspace: `gh api /user`, `gh pr create` or `gh repo view`\nanswers `Bad credentials` (HTTP 401) while the same command on the stack host works, and\n`gh auth status` still insists you are logged in. This is the most frequent GitHub\nfailure here: 19 of 222 past sessions in this workspace hit `Bad credentials`."},"1989":{"id":"/troubleshooting-303","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-answers-bad-credentials---the-stale-env-pair-not-the-store","content":"Cause: Coder injects `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` into the agent environment when the\nworkspace starts. That GitHub App user-to-server token expires about an hour later and\nthe injected copy is never refreshed for the life of the workspace. `gh` consults that\npair before it reads its own auth store, so it sends the dead token while the store\nbeside it, re-minted by the `gh_token_refresh` coder_script, is healthy and unreachable."},"1990":{"id":"/troubleshooting-304","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-answers-bad-credentials---the-stale-env-pair-not-the-store","content":"The answer for every `gh` call, for as long as that workspace lives:"},"1991":{"id":"/troubleshooting-305","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-answers-bad-credentials---the-stale-env-pair-not-the-store","content":"No process can rewrite another process's environment, so the stale pair stays in the\nenvironment until the workspace restarts; the prefix is the permanent form, not a\nworkaround you graduate from."},"1992":{"id":"/troubleshooting-306","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-answers-bad-credentials---the-stale-env-pair-not-the-store","content":"`git` needs no prefix once `fix` has run, because the helper it installs clears the pair\nitself:"},"1993":{"id":"/troubleshooting-307","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-answers-bad-credentials---the-stale-env-pair-not-the-store","content":"You read the right line when `check` marks the healthy stored credential\n`[shadowed: ...]`: that label is this entry. If the credential `check` says `git would\nsend` is the env token and it answers `REJECTED`, `fix` is the repair; if it answers\n`REFUSED-GRANT`, it is a grant and the clicks are in\nthe push-denied entry."},"1994":{"id":"/troubleshooting-308","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-helper-needs-gh","content":"What you see is `gh: command not found`, or `fix` refusing with `cannot install the\ncredential helper - gh is not installed in this environment; install gh (apt install gh,\nor the pinned copy the golden image ships) and run this again`. `check` reports the same\nhole honestly: with no gh there is no `gh stored login` slot to read, and a chain with\nnothing else in it prints `NO GitHub credential`."},"1995":{"id":"/troubleshooting-309","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-helper-needs-gh","content":"Why it blocks the repair rather than just the CLI: the helper `fix` installs is\n`gh auth git-credential` wrapped in `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN ...`, and the\ncredential it points git at is the gh auth store. With no `gh` binary there is no\nenv-proof helper to install and no store to write, so `fix` refuses and says so instead\nof leaving a half-repaired `~/.gitconfig` behind."},"1996":{"id":"/troubleshooting-310","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-helper-needs-gh","content":"Install gh, then run `fix` again. The golden image ships a pinned copy (`gh 2.101.0`,\nfrom the release tarball, at `/usr/local/bin/gh`); apt's `gh` is 2.45 and predates\nsubcommands the agent experience uses, so the durable fix is the image, not the distro\npackage. Rebuild the image, then rebuild the workspace: the image is fixed at create\ntime."},"1997":{"id":"/troubleshooting-311","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#gh-command-not-found-inside-a-workspace---the-helper-needs-gh","content":"Whether you are looking at a missing tool or a missing credential, `check` is what tells\nthem apart: a missing binary shows up as a slot that cannot answer, a missing grant\nshows up as `REFUSED-GRANT`."},"1998":{"id":"/troubleshooting-312","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"Cause: Hermes v0.22.5 refuses a non-loopback bind when no\n`DashboardAuthProvider` is registered. The bundled basic-auth plugin skips\nregistration when the password is empty. Compose interpolation\n`${HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD:-}` (empty default) did that after a pull that\nadded the Hermes keys: `.env` never gained them, so `prod-stack up` without\n`bootstrap` / `update-configs` crash-looped the gateway. The logs repeat\n\"no auth providers are registered\" and do not mention `plugins.disabled`."},"1999":{"id":"/troubleshooting-313","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"Fix: compose interpolation defaults now match `.env.sample`\n(`change-me-hermes` / `change-me-hermes-secret`). Recreate the service:"},"2000":{"id":"/troubleshooting-314","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"Sign-in is `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` from `.env`\n(default `admin` / `change-me-hermes` until bootstrap rotates the\nplaceholders). To fill missing keys and replace those placeholders:"},"2001":{"id":"/troubleshooting-315","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a no-op. Logs:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs hermes`."},"2002":{"id":"/troubleshooting-316","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"The Chat tab is a PTY over WebSocket on the same port. Approvals render as\ncards there; a mutating command that seems to hang is waiting for the card.\nSeeded `approvals.mode` is `manual` — never `off`. `hermes-init` must exit 0\nbefore `hermes` starts. If the gateway loops on a missing `config.yaml`, the\nnamed volume was not seeded: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap -- --help` and recreate. Full\naccess / mounts / socket-risk notes: hermes.md."},"2003":{"id":"/troubleshooting-317","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#refusing-to-bind-dashboard-to-0000---empty-hermes-basic-auth-env","content":"The assistant also opens in your own terminal with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-shell`. When you cannot tell whether the agent,\nits volume, or the model lane is the broken part, start with the readiness\nreport — it launches nothing and names each blocker (daemon, container state,\nseeded volume, model key set or still a placeholder, LiteLLM\n`/health/liveliness`):"},"2004":{"id":"/troubleshooting-318","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"Cause: this box's `router` answers `421` when the Host matches no `server_name`.\nThe hermes vhost is not typed by hand: `STACK_ROUTER_CONF` in `.env` names the conf\ncompose mounts (`proxy/router.gen.conf`, which bootstrap renders from\n`proxy/router.conf.template`, or the checked-in `proxy/router.conf` while that key is\nempty), and every `server_name` in it is composed from the naming parts. Under\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` a missing or mistyped part is what produces a name nothing\nanswers. Traefik pointed at `:9119` never hits that file. A rewritten Host also\nmisses every block."},"2005":{"id":"/troubleshooting-319","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"The public backend is the same hop as the other names. Replay it from a\nworkspace, which reaches the published router and skips Cloudflare, Traefik and\nAuthelia:"},"2006":{"id":"/troubleshooting-320","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"`421` means this box has no vhost for that Host. `STACK_ROUTER_CONF` in `.env` is\nwhat tells you which conf compose actually mounted, so read that file's\n`server_name` lines first, then recreate `router` so it picks up the one that\nlists `server_name hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`."},"2007":{"id":"/troubleshooting-321","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"Anything else means the router matched (Hermes basic auth is `401` until you\nsend credentials). A `421` only on the public URL, with this probe succeeding,\nis a rewritten Host or a Traefik backend that is not `:8080`."},"2008":{"id":"/troubleshooting-322","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"Under `auto` both sides come from the same parts, so a `421` on a name nobody\nrecognises is a part problem, not a router problem: an empty `STACK_DOMAIN_BASE`\ncomposes nothing (the stack stays LAN-only) and a mistyped `STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX`\nor `STACK_ENV_LABEL` composes a name no vhost, no DNS and no certificate serves.\nRead the composed list back from `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` in `.env`, fix the part, and\nre-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap`, then recreate. Editing the rendered\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` instead only survives until the next bootstrap, which\nre-renders it from the parts."},"2009":{"id":"/troubleshooting-323","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"A part carrying an extra dot is the other failure this table cannot catch: the\ncomposed name sits more than one label under the zone, the wildcard does not cover\nit, and the browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` before Traefik\nis ever reached, so no `421` is ever printed."},"2010":{"id":"/troubleshooting-324","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` does not create the vhost. It is what bootstrap writes\ninto `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` for the Chat tab.\n`bootstrap` now also warns when a name in that list is missing from the\nconf it mounts."},"2011":{"id":"/troubleshooting-325","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#public-hermes-gmktecbeast-answers-421---no-vhost-a-rewritten-host-or-traefik-aimed-past-the-router","content":"The paste-ready Traefik file (one backend at `:8080`, `passHostHeader: true`,\nAuthelia on the four interactive names, LiteLLM without Authelia) is in\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."},"2012":{"id":"/troubleshooting-326","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"Cause: the Chat tab is a WebSocket on `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`. Hermes sends a\nkeepalive every 20 seconds. Close code `4403` is the Host / Origin / peer guard."},"2013":{"id":"/troubleshooting-327","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` has to be the public origin. Bootstrap writes\n`STACK_HERMES_URL` from a `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry, and compose copies\nthat into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`. The variable is environment: recreate\nthe container after bootstrap."},"2014":{"id":"/troubleshooting-328","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"The seed sets `dashboard.trusted_proxies` to `[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]` so Hermes\nhonours `X-Forwarded-Proto` (Secure cookie and `wss`). A `hermes-data` volume\nfrom before that seed keeps the old file until you reseed. `<mount>` is the\nhost directory of that volume:"},"2015":{"id":"/troubleshooting-329","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"`hermes config set` on `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is the in-container\nalternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` does not need that reseed."},"2016":{"id":"/troubleshooting-330","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"Traefik and Authelia have to pass `Upgrade`. Cloudflare's orange-cloud proxy\nhas WebSockets on by default. A page that loads and a Chat tab that dies is\nthis hop, not the basic-auth provider."},"2017":{"id":"/troubleshooting-331","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#chat-tab-never-connects-or-the-socket-closes-4403---public-url-trusted-proxies-or-a-dropped-upgrade","content":"The `0.0.0.0` bind refusal above is a different failure: empty basic-auth env,\nnot this public path. Full notes: hermes.md."},"2018":{"id":"/troubleshooting-332","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#kasm-wizard-gone-after-install---expected","content":"The wizard only listens on `:3000` at first boot; after install, use `:4443`."},"2019":{"id":"/troubleshooting-333","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#kasm-wizard-gone-after-install---expected","content":"Reset Kasm by removing the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config. Stop\nthe stack first, and use the name your stack owns: `evo-t1-coder-stack_kasm-data` for\nthe stable stack, `evo-t1-dev_kasm-data` for the dev one."},"2020":{"id":"/troubleshooting-334","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-login-problems---the-admin-account-and-coder_access_url","content":"The first registered account is the site admin."},"2021":{"id":"/troubleshooting-335","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting#coder-login-problems---the-admin-account-and-coder_access_url","content":"If the UI is unreachable, check that `CODER_ACCESS_URL` matches the address you are\nbrowsing from: the LAN IP by default, or the public coder name in public mode."},"2022":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"page","content":"Trunk-based + flags refactor","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Workflows"],"tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor"},"2023":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-0","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","content":"The full procedure for moving a repository onto trunk-based development: environments become configuration instead of git branches, unfinished work ships behind a flag that defaults off, and every other branch is inventoried, ported, or listed for deletion. Worked examples for Compose, K3s, Ansible, Terraform/ArgoCD, Bazel and manage.sh stacks."},"2024":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-1","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"Trunk-based + flags refactor"},"2025":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-2","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"The model, in six statements"},"2026":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-3","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"Ideal-state checklist"},"2027":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-4","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"Inventory first"},"2028":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-5","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#environments-without-environment-branches","content":"Environments without environment branches"},"2029":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-6","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#docker-compose","content":"Docker Compose"},"2030":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-7","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#k3s--kubernetes","content":"K3s / Kubernetes"},"2031":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-8","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ansible","content":"Ansible"},"2032":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-9","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#terraform--opentofu-with-argocd","content":"Terraform / OpenTofu with ArgoCD"},"2033":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-10","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#coder-and-template-parameters","content":"Coder and template parameters"},"2034":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-11","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"The flag ladder"},"2035":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-12","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"Branch cleanup cookbook"},"2036":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-13","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#per-repository-notes","content":"Per-repository notes"},"2037":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-14","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#safety-lines","content":"Safety lines"},"2038":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-15","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","type":"heading","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#running-it","content":"Running it"},"2039":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-16","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"**What's on this page**"},"2040":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-17","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"The model in six statements, and what each one rules out"},"2041":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-18","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"The ideal-state checklist a refactor has to satisfy before it is called done"},"2042":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-19","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"How an environment is expressed without a branch, per stack"},"2043":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-20","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"The flag ladder, from one env var up to a checked-in `config/flags.yaml`"},"2044":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-21","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"The branch-cleanup cookbook: classify, port, preserve, list for deletion"},"2045":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-22","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"Per-repository notes for the four reference repos"},"2046":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-23","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"The safety lines that never move"},"2047":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-24","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"**What this enables**"},"2048":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-25","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"Deleting the `development` branch without losing a commit that was only ever on it"},"2049":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-26","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"Deploying dev, staging and production from one commit instead of three builds"},"2050":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-27","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"Shipping work that is not finished, on the trunk, without exposing it"},"2051":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-28","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"Running the same procedure in any of this fleet's repositories with `/trunk-refactor`"},"2052":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-29","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#trunk-based--flags-refactor","content":"This is the human-readable twin of the `trunk-refactor` skill\n(`skills/trunk-refactor/SKILL.md`). The skill is what an agent session follows; this page is\nwhat a person reads, with the examples the skill leaves out to stay short. The branch rules\nthemselves live in Branching - this page is about getting a repository *onto*\nthose rules."},"2053":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-30","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"One long-lived branch. It is normally `main`, it is always deployable, and it is what every\nenvironment is built from."},"2054":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-31","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"An environment is a set of values, a set of secrets, and a place to run. It is a GitHub\nEnvironment, a compose project, a namespace, an inventory group. It is not a git branch."},"2055":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-32","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"Work happens on short-lived branches cut from the tip of the trunk and merged by pull\nrequest within days: `feat/`, `fix/`, `refactor/`, `docs/`, `chore/`, `ci/`."},"2056":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-33","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"Incomplete or experimental work lands on the trunk behind a flag that defaults off."},"2057":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-34","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"Promotion is the same commit or tag applied to a different environment. Nothing is rebuilt\nper environment, because a per-environment build is a per-environment bug surface."},"2058":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-35","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"Branch cleanup is part of the work, not a chore someone does later."},"2059":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-36","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"What the model rules out: long-lived `development`/`staging`/`production` branches; \"merge to\ndevelopment first\"; a release branch that has to be kept forever; cherry-picking a fix from one\nenvironment branch to another; and a flag platform nobody asked for."},"2060":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-37","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-model-in-six-statements","content":"The failure that motivates all of this is the merge that goes the wrong way. A `development`\nbranch that is 134 commits ahead of `main` (a real number from a real repository in this fleet)\nis not a staging area, it is a second product whose differences nobody is reconciling, and the\nday someone finally merges it, the review is unreviewable."},"2061":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-38","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"The skill's twelve binary criteria, in prose. Each needs a command's output as proof."},"2062":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-39","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                          | How you prove it               |\n| --- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| 1   | One trunk, no environment branches | `git ls-remote --heads origin` |","table":"table-0"},"2063":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-40","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                               | How you prove it                                           |\n| --- | --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 2   | No branch holds commits the trunk lacks | `git rev-list --count origin/main..origin/<branch>` is `0` |","table":"table-0"},"2064":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-41","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion      | How you prove it                                                                     |\n| --- | -------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 3   | Trunk is gated | the CI file names a job that runs the full gate on pull request and on push to trunk |","table":"table-0"},"2065":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-42","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                           | How you prove it                              |\n| --- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| 4   | Branch names and ages obey the rule | `git for-each-ref` over `refs/remotes/origin` |","table":"table-0"},"2066":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-43","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                                     | How you prove it                                             |\n| --- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 5   | Every environment has one home for its values | the file or overlay exists and no branch is needed to deploy |","table":"table-0"},"2067":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-44","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                  | How you prove it                                |\n| --- | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| 6   | One artifact, many targets | the promotion command block, pasted and working |","table":"table-0"},"2068":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-45","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                         | How you prove it                                                     |\n| --- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 7   | Unfinished work is off by default | the flag's default reads as false in the sample dotenv or flags file |","table":"table-0"},"2069":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-46","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                                | How you prove it                                                                            |\n| --- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 8   | The flag inventory is complete both ways | `grep -rn \"<FLAG>\" .` finds a reader for every declaration and a declaration for every read |","table":"table-0"},"2070":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-47","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion            | How you prove it                              |\n| --- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| 9   | No committed secrets | `git ls-files` shows only sample dotenv names |","table":"table-0"},"2071":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-48","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                    | How you prove it                                                |\n| --- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 10  | The docs agree with the tree | branching doc, contributing, and agent rules say the same thing |","table":"table-0"},"2072":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-49","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion                      | How you prove it                       |\n| --- | ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |\n| 11  | The repo's own gates are green | the gate command with its summary line |","table":"table-0"},"2073":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-50","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ideal-state-checklist","content":"| #   | Criterion             | How you prove it                                                                         |\n| --- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 12  | Nothing was destroyed | no force-push, no unconfirmed remote delete, every proposed deletion carries its tip SHA |","table":"table-0"},"2074":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-51","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"Before any edit, answer five questions and keep the answers in a scratch file:"},"2075":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-52","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"**Which branches exist, how old are they, who owns them?**"},"2076":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-53","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"**What does each non-trunk branch hold that the trunk does not?**"},"2077":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-54","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"`git cherry` marks a commit `-` when an equivalent patch is already upstream. A branch that\nis `ahead=0` behind trunk is done, and its row reads \"delete\" without further argument."},"2078":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-55","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"**How is anything deployed here?** Look, do not assume: `compose*.yml`, `docker-compose*.yml`,\n`kustomization.yaml`, `values.yaml`, `inventories/`, `*.tf`, `argocd/`, `manage.sh`,\n`Makefile`, `MODULE.bazel`, `.github/workflows/`, `templates/*/main.tf`."},"2079":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-56","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"**What flags already exist?** `grep -rnE 'FEATURE_|_ENABLED|^\\s*profiles:'` over the compose\nfiles, dotenv samples, Terraform variables and scripts."},"2080":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-57","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#inventory-first","content":"**What is the gate you must not break?** `bazelisk test //:test-fast`, `npm test`,\n`make test`, `./manage.sh <check>`, `pytest`, `go test ./...`. Run it before you start so a\nlater red is attributable."},"2081":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-58","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#environments-without-environment-branches","content":"An environment may differ from its neighbour in values, secrets, and where it runs. If a branch\ndiffers in *code*, that code belongs on the trunk behind a flag."},"2082":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-59","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#docker-compose","content":"Name the deployment, not the branch:"},"2083":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-60","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#docker-compose","content":"The project name (`-p`) is what isolates networks, volumes and container names, which is how two\nenvironments share a host. Every published port has to be a value for that to work:\n`\"${APP_PORT:-3000}:3000\"`. Optional services go behind a profile:"},"2084":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-61","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#docker-compose","content":"so `docker compose up` stays clean and `--profile experimental` opts in."},"2085":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-62","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#docker-compose","content":"This fleet's own dev-versus-stable split is the reference implementation: separate project\nnames, separate dotenv files, and a subdomain composed from parts rather than from a branch\nname, driven by `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up`,\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up`, and `bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack` for the\npull-and-deploy pair. In that repository the environment branches were still sitting on the\nremote (`dev`, `development`, `staging`, `uat`, `prod`) while every one of them reported\n`ahead=0` against `main`: five branches, zero unique commits, waiting for someone to confirm the\ndelete."},"2086":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-63","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#k3s--kubernetes","content":"One namespace per environment, one overlay per environment, one image reference:"},"2087":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-64","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#k3s--kubernetes","content":"Promotion edits the tag, not a branch:"},"2088":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-65","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#k3s--kubernetes","content":"With Helm the same shape is `values/dev.yaml`, `values/staging.yaml`, and\n`helm upgrade --install app ./chart -n lab-staging -f values/staging.yaml --set image.tag=...`.\nSecrets come from the cluster's own store or an external secret, never from the overlay."},"2089":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-66","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ansible","content":"The playbook is the trunk; the environment is inventory:"},"2090":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-67","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#ansible","content":"Promotion is the same playbook revision and role versions against a different inventory. A\nbranch that carries playbook changes is a pull request; a branch that carries only variable\nchanges is a file under `group_vars/`."},"2091":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-68","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#terraform--opentofu-with-argocd","content":"Environment is a workspace or an `envs/<env>/` directory that instantiates one shared module;\n`terraform plan` against `envs/staging` and `envs/production` is the same module revision twice.\nArgoCD gets one `Application` per environment, all pointing at the same Kustomize or chart path,\nand promotion is a commit that bumps the image tag (by hand, or by the Image Updater). A\nper-environment ArgoCD branch is a sync-drift generator and a rollback trap."},"2092":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-69","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#coder-and-template-parameters","content":"Where the switch selects infrastructure rather than behaviour, use the platform's own parameter\nsurface: a `variable` in the workspace template plus the values each workspace supplies, which is\nhow this stack already separates a development workspace from a stable one. Bump the template\nrevision; do not fork a template per environment."},"2093":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-70","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"Climb as far as you need and stop."},"2094":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-71","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"**One env var, read at one seam**, documented in the dotenv sample with its off default. This\ncarries most needs: `FEATURE_AGENT_TUNNEL_V2=0`."},"2095":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-72","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"**Compose `profiles:`** when what you are hiding is a whole service."},"2096":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-73","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"**A checked-in `config/flags.yaml`** once there are enough switches that a reviewer cannot\nhold them, or when one tree runs in several environments with different settings. Copy\n`config/flags.yaml.example` from this repository. Keep the schema - `default`, `owner`,\n`description`, `expires`, `environments`, optional `stacks` overrides - and keep the real\nprocess environment winning, so CI and an operator can still force a value."},"2097":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-74","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"**The platform's parameters** (Terraform variables, Helm values, Ansible vars, Coder template\nvariables) when the flag selects infrastructure."},"2098":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-75","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"A flag service, an SDK, or a database-backed flag table is a decision an operator makes\nexplicitly. It is not the default for a home lab or a single-node host, and it is never what a\nfirst pass should add."},"2099":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-76","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"Rules that keep flags alive long enough to be removed:"},"2100":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-77","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"One naming convention. `FEATURE_<THING>` for behaviour, a plain value for a setting. The name\nin the inventory is exactly the name the process reads, so `grep` is a complete audit."},"2101":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-78","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"Off by default on the trunk. An on-by-default flag is an unfinished feature that became\nload-bearing on the day it shipped."},"2102":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-79","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"Owner and expiry on every flag, and one `chore:` pull request that deletes the flag, its\noff-path, and any branch still carrying it."},"2103":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-80","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"Read once, branch there. A flag checked in nine modules is nine edits when it retires."},"2104":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-81","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#the-flag-ladder","content":"The gate runs with it off; at least one test or documented smoke command runs with it on."},"2105":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-82","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"One verdict per branch, with the evidence beside it."},"2106":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-83","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Verdict | Test                                                            | What you do       |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| merged  | `rev-list --count` is 0, or `git cherry` marks every commit `-` | list for deletion |","table":"table-1"},"2107":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-84","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Verdict    | Test                                     | What you do                                  |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| superseded | its files exist on trunk in another form | list for deletion, citing what superseded it |","table":"table-1"},"2108":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-85","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Verdict | Test                          | What you do                                                 |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| port    | small, complete, still wanted | re-adapt onto a new short-lived branch, open a pull request |","table":"table-1"},"2109":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-86","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Verdict  | Test                    | What you do                          |\n| -------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| preserve | valuable and incomplete | port behind a flag that defaults off |","table":"table-1"},"2110":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-87","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Verdict | Test                              | What you do                       |\n| ------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| dead    | no value, or explicitly abandoned | list for deletion with the reason |","table":"table-1"},"2111":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-88","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Verdict         | Test                        | What you do                                     |\n| --------------- | --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| release-bearing | carries an untagged version | tag the tip, push the tag, then list the branch |","table":"table-1"},"2112":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-89","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"Porting is re-adaptation. Take the files, then fix them against today's trunk:"},"2113":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-90","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"Cherry-picking is for a coherent run of commits you want verbatim, and `-x` keeps the source SHA\nin the message so the provenance survives:"},"2114":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-91","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"A port that also reformats is two pull requests. A port of a whole branch at once is a review\nnobody does properly."},"2115":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-92","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"Then hand the deletions to a human:"},"2116":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-93","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Branch      | Age        | Author  | Unique commits | Verdict | Recovery SHA |\n| ----------- | ---------- | ------- | -------------- | ------- | ------------ |\n| development | 2026-11-02 | someone | 0              | delete  | `a1b2c3d`    |","table":"table-2"},"2117":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-94","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"| Branch  | Age        | Author  | Unique commits | Verdict | Recovery SHA |\n| ------- | ---------- | ------- | -------------- | ------- | ------------ |\n| staging | 2026-08-14 | someone | 0              | delete  | `e4f5g6h`    |","table":"table-2"},"2118":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-95","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"with the commands they will run:"},"2119":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-96","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#branch-cleanup-cookbook","content":"Record the tip SHA of every branch in that table before anything goes. A recorded SHA is\nrecoverable until garbage collection; an unrecorded one is not."},"2120":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-97","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#per-repository-notes","content":"Verified against the remotes on 2026-11-08. Repositories move: re-run the Phase 0 inventory\nrather than trusting this table."},"2121":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-98","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#per-repository-notes","content":"| Repository                     | Default | Environment-shaped branches                                    | Deployment mechanism | First move                                                                    |\n| ------------------------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack` | `main`  | `dev`, `development`, `staging`, `uat`, `prod` - all `ahead=0` |                      | Confirm deletion of the five merged branches; the model is already documented |","table":"table-3"},"2122":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-99","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#per-repository-notes","content":"| Repository                       | Default | Environment-shaped branches                                  | Deployment mechanism                                               | First move                                                                                          |\n| -------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/nvidia-dgx-spark-lab` | `main`  | `development` (+ `dev`), `development` 134 ahead / 27 behind | `k8s/` with a `k8s/dev/` overlay, Compose for dashboard and hermes | Port the 134 commits in slices onto `main`, flag-off the unfinished ones, then retire `development` |","table":"table-3"},"2123":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-100","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#per-repository-notes","content":"| Repository                 | Default | Environment-shaped branches                                                                                      | Deployment mechanism                                                                            | First move                                                                                                                                        |\n| -------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/ez-comfy-stack` | `main`  | `development` (+ `dev`), `development` 523 ahead / 19 behind, plus `feat/`, `fix/`, `feature/`, `docs/` families | Compose (`docker/docker-compose.yml`), `scripts/manage.sh`, Makefile, an image-publish workflow | Port in themed slices; the published image tag is the promotion lever, so land the tag story first and normalise the `feature/` prefix to `feat/` |","table":"table-3"},"2124":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-101","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#per-repository-notes","content":"| Repository               | Default | Environment-shaped branches                               | Deployment mechanism                           | First move                                                                          |\n| ------------------------ | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/inkorporated` | `main`  | `development` (+ `dev`), `development` 0 ahead / 2 behind | `infrastructure/`, CI plus presubmit workflows | `development` is already merged: list it for deletion, then fix the naming families |","table":"table-3"},"2125":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-102","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#per-repository-notes","content":"Two patterns worth reading across those rows. A branch family that is `ahead=0` (this repo,\n`inkorporated`) is a cleanup with no risk once a human confirms it. A branch family that is\nhundreds of commits ahead (`nvidia-dgx-spark-lab`, `ez-comfy-stack`) is a porting programme:\nslice by area, land each slice green, and let the flags hold what is not finished. Both begin\nwith the same inventory, and both end with a table a person signs off on."},"2126":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-103","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#safety-lines","content":"No force-push, no rewritten shared history, no merging your own pull request."},"2127":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-104","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#safety-lines","content":"No remote branch deleted without explicit confirmation, and never a trunk ref."},"2128":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-105","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#safety-lines","content":"No secret in a committed file, including the flags file and per-environment value files.\nSamples hold key names and inert defaults; secrets go to the GitHub Environment or the\ncluster's secret store."},"2129":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-106","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#safety-lines","content":"Keep the existing gates green and extend what exists. No new CI system, no Kubernetes, no\nflag platform, no new dependency."},"2130":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-107","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#safety-lines","content":"One logical change per branch and per pull request, with docs and tests in the same change."},"2131":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-108","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"type":"text","url":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor#safety-lines","content":"A default-branch rename is its own confirmed step, done after the content has converged,\nbecause GitHub rewrites links, protections and open pull requests when it happens."},"2132":{"id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor-109","page_id":"/workflows/trunk-feature-flags-refactor","tags":["git","branching","ci","feature-flags","environments","refactor"],"ty