81,"2063":479,"2064":488,"2065":480,"2066":477,"2067":485,"2068":482,"2069":478,"2070":487,"2071":484,"2072":483,"2073":486,"2074":823,"2075":151,"2076":100,"2077":301,"2078":54,"2079":101,"2080":102,"2081":783,"2082":1271,"2083":1784,"2084":1506,"2085":1917,"2086":1340,"2087":1397,"2088":2107,"2089":1782,"2090":1399,"2091":1024,"2092":2076,"2093":915,"2094":70,"2095":36,"2096":16,"2097":88,"2098":699,"2099":1456,"2100":1341,"2101":1312,"2102":1368,"2103":1420,"2104":1704,"2105":1349,"2106":654,"2107":652,"2108":656,"2109":653,"2110":655,"2111":651,"2112":1384,"2113":911,"2114":720,"2115":1900,"2116":511,"2117":512,"2118":2111,"2119":1434,"2120":1995,"2121":606,"2122":605,"2123":607,"2124":608,"2125":1967,"2126":1295,"2127":1299,"2128":1300,"2129":1224,"2130":1337,"2131":690,"2132":1840,"2133":1907,"2134":984},"orderedDocs":[[1148,"**`--data DIR`** points the two volume checks at a directory instead of asking the\ndaemon where the volume is mounted, so the audit can be run against a copy of one."],[1538,"**`--task`** calls `coder task create` once the repository exists. The Coder server here is v2.36.6\nand does ship `coder task`, but the Terraform provider is pinned to `~> 2.18`, so a task cannot be\ndeclared inside a template. The CLI therefore creates it at run time and, when it cannot reach the\nCoder API -- the usual case outside a logged-in host shell -- prints the exact command and continues\nwith exit 0. That follows `new-workspace.sh`, which treats \"cannot create right now\" as a not-yet\nrather than an error."],[1142,"**`--writable` matters more than it looks.** The official image sets\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT=/opt/data`, so a file-tool write anywhere else is refused\noutright and no approval card even appears. To have the assistant edit files on the\nhost, name the directory: `--writable \"${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}\"` or\nthe checkout path."],[1138,"**`check` is the first thing to run when an answer looks impossible.** It reports the\ndaemon, the compose CLI, the project, the container state, whether the service\nresolves, the image, whether the data volume is seeded, whether the model key is set\nor still a shipped placeholder, whether LiteLLM answers `/health/liveliness`, and the\nwork directory. It prints key names and never a value, and it launches nothing."],[143,"**`coder_env` is the only provably-earlier file write.** The agent's\n`writeSecretFiles` (`agent.go`) runs before any start script, `MkdirAll 0700` then\n`WriteFile 0600`. It takes a string value, so it can carry a payload, and it writes\ninto the agent's secret directory rather than anywhere you name."],[144,"**`CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` is the per-script scratch the agent already hands you.**\n`agent/agentscripts/agentscripts.go` appends `CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` and\n`CODER_SCRIPT_BIN_DIR` to every script's environment, which is a writable place to\nassemble something across scripts."],[988,"**`Ctrl+V` on a secure origin is not a paste.** VS Code binds terminal paste to\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` on Linux and `Cmd+V` on macOS, so `Ctrl+V` reaches the pane as\nthe `\\x16` byte (readline's quoted-insert, vim's visual-block). The seed\nre-binds nothing there on purpose: a TUI in the pane owns that chord."],[985,"**`Prefix m` is the wheel, not a copy route.** One press hands the pointer to\ntmux so the wheel walks pane history, the next hands it back. It is the escape\nhatch for a long scroll, because tmux paints in the alternate screen - it sends\n`ESC[?1049h` either way - so the client holds no scrollback of its own and a wheel\nover a pane has nothing to walk. Output that has scrolled away is copied by\nbringing it on screen first: `Prefix-[`, scroll it into view, select it with a\nclient drag, `q`. A drag while `Prefix m` has the pointer on selects nothing,\nsince the copy bindings stay unbound either way."],[856,"**`run` can call a helper, and a helper that `exit`s is safe.** `run` forks, so\n`run --separate-stderr usage_error '…'` followed by `[ \"${status}\" -eq 2 ]` works even\nthough `usage_error` ends in `exit 2`. Pass a real command (`bash \"${SCRIPT}\" …`) when\nthe script is what is under test."],[855,"**`run` keeps stdout in `$output`.** With `run --separate-stderr` there is no `$stdout`:\n`$output` is stdout and `$stderr` is stderr. An assertion written against `$stdout`\ncompares against an empty string and fails, which is how a whole suite of real\nassertions can look like a broken harness."],[992,"**`terminal.limited-color` during an agent turn.** A tool command launched by a\nrunning Grok session inherits `NO_COLOR=1`, `FORCE_COLOR=0`, `CLICOLOR=0` and\n`CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never` from that session, so a `grok doctor` run from inside\nan agent turn reads color as off. The `grok` process itself and the tmux server\ncarry none of those variables, so run it from a plain terminal for the real\ncapability: the line reads `color truecolor` and `themes all`."],[994,"**`terminal.ssh-wrap`** is a recommendation about the computer you type `ssh`\non, since the wrapper has to sit in front of that client: run\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there, or `grok wrap ssh <host>` per connection. It\nforwards copies to the local clipboard and restores terminal modes when the\nlink drops. `/settings` -> **Show contextual hints** -> **SSH wrap** silences\nthe tip."],[995,"**`unavailable` tmux and runtime probes.** `tmux.version`,\n`tmux.client-features` and the `runtime.*` lines come back `unavailable`\nwhenever Doctor has no interactive client of its own, which covers a piped run\nand a run from an agent tool call. Read them by hand (`tmux -V`,\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`) or from `/doctor` in a live\nsession."],[993,"**`voice.no-input-device`.** Expected in a workspace. The container has no\ncapture device at all (`/dev/snd` and `/proc/asound` are absent) and no\nrecorder on PATH. The probe only looks for a recorder binary, so installing\n`alsa-utils` moves the line to `available` while capture still has nowhere to\nread from, and `features.voice_mode = false` leaves the finding in place.\nNeither would be a fix, so the stack ships neither and the finding stands as an\naccurate report of a headless container. Dictation belongs on a laptop-side\n`grok`. To give a workspace dictation instead: run PulseAudio on the workstation\nwith its TCP module enabled, export `PULSE_SERVER=tcp=<workstation>:4713` into\nthe workspace, and install `pulseaudio-utils` there for `parec`."],[137,"**A `#!` line does not help.** The body still becomes\n`execve(env, [env, bash, -c, body])`; the interpreter line is content inside the\nargument."],[722,"**A Bats file is cheap; a script is not.** `tests-for tests/bats/common.bats` returns five targets.\n`tests-for scripts/lib/common.sh` returns 60 of the 67 test targets on this checkout, because every\nsuite declares `//scripts:doc_sources`, which is every `scripts/**.sh` (66 targets reach that\ngroup). That is the honest graph answer: editing a broadly sourced script genuinely re-runs nearly\nthe whole suite set, and knowing it before you start is what decides whether to run the derived\nlist or the aggregate."],[2096,"**A checked-in `config/flags.yaml`** once there are enough switches that a reviewer cannot\nhold them, or when one tree runs in several environments with different settings. Copy\n`config/flags.yaml.example` from this repository. Keep the schema - `default`, `owner`,\n`description`, `expires`, `environments`, optional `stacks` overrides - and keep the real\nprocess environment winning, so CI and an operator can still force a value."],[468,"**A defined-but-empty key crash-loops the server.** Any `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\ncounts as a configured provider even when its value is empty, and an empty key\nmakes `coder server` exit at boot with `external auth provider \"\" doesn't have a\nvalid id` - which the compose restart policy turns into a crash loop. Do not set\nonly `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_APP_INSTALL_URL` either: it suppresses the injected\nApp like any other key in the set. A custom GitHub App belongs in `coder.env`\nfully filled, including\n`APP_INSTALL_URL=https://github.com/apps/<slug>/installations/new`."],[1002,"**A leftover `/learn` seed retires itself.** This skill was seeded under the name\n`learn` until it moved, and a copy left standing keeps offering that old name beside\n`/ez-learn` for the life of the volume; there is no bundled `learn` for it to hide,\nbecause no published source names one. Both startup scripts delete\n`~/.coder/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and `~/.agents/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and prune the\ndirectory each leaves, but only while the file still carries the\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker; a marker-free `learn/SKILL.md` is user-owned\nand stays. `refresh-configs`, `storage-clean` and `seed-dev-env` retire the same two\npaths on reseed. That removal only finishes the rename: there is no upstream `/learn`\nwaiting on the name, because no published source names one."],[29,"**A local commit is free, a push is not.** Nothing leaves the machine until you push, so err\ntoward committing, including a `WIP:` checkpoint before a step that might break something green.\nHistory is local until then, so amend the message, fold the checkpoint into the commit it belongs\nto, and reorder there; that door closes at the push, after which a shared commit is never\nrewritten."],[1668,"**A published dev stack needs its own routers here.** The `dev-stack.env` behind\n`bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack` moves every published port by 10000 (the recipe is\nin the\nREADME),\nso that stack's `router` answers on `18080` (`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` default 8080) and\nits names carry the environment label\n(`coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`). A dev name is a different `Host` rule and a\ndifferent backend port: add a router per dev name to the file above aimed at\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:18080`, and the DNS records for those names have to exist\ntoo, or the browser fails before this box is ever asked."],[413,"**A token that authenticates still has to be granted.** A credential that was never\nGRANTED the repository fails clone and push, and reads prove nothing about write: a private\nrepository hidden from the credential answers 403 or 404 on both lanes, and a public one\nanswers a read probe with 200 for any authenticated caller, so a read-only credential\npasses a read test while every write to that repo is denied. `check` therefore asks the\nwrite question directly: one GET of `<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`, the\nendpoint `git push` itself negotiates through."],[656,"**Abbreviations.** A term listed in `docs/includes/abbreviations.md` as `*[TERM]: definition`\nis wrapped wherever it appears in prose. Hover or focus shows the definition; click or Enter\nopens it in a dialog. Text inside a link, a heading, inline code, or an existing term is left\nalone. Keep each definition tooltip-sized."],[536,"**Action cache.** An action's outputs are keyed on its inputs and command line, so an\nunchanged suite replays its cached PASS instead of re-running Bats."],[1462,"**Add an auto-reboot watchdog.** A box sitting in the initramfs with dropbear\nlistening is the recoverable state; rebooting on a timer destroys the\nrecovery window and can turn one bad boot into a loop. `-I` bounds the\nsession, and the listener stays."],[900,"**After install:** the UI is on `https://<host>:4443`. Both ports serve TLS with the\nself-signed certificate Kasm's own installer generates, and plain `http://` reaches\nneither port."],[986,"**An app inside the pane drives the machine clipboard over OSC 52; the browser\nbuild drops it.** `set -g set-clipboard on` with `set -g allow-passthrough on`\nstay in the seed for that one route: vim's `+` register, and anything else that\nemits the escape, reach the machine clipboard the way a native terminal hands them\nto it. Over ssh from Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm or iTerm2 that copy lands.\ncode-server's browser build drops the escape before any clipboard call: a\nhand-written `printf '\\033]52;c;<base64>\\a'` was just as silent with\n`set-clipboard off` as with it on, which puts the drop in the browser and not in\ntmux. Upstream\nmicrosoft/vscode#318254 is\nopen with two unmerged fixes, and the shipped 4.145.0 registers no OSC 52 setting\nat all, so there is nothing here to turn on. Nothing on the tmux side can spoil\nthat clipboard either: tmux has no copy left to send, so an accidental selection\nin the pane cannot overwrite what the client just put there."],[723,"**An empty result means run everything.** Nothing changed against HEAD, or the change touches no\nfile the graph tracks, and `scope` says so and points at `//:test-fast`."],[1140,"**An interactive session needs a real tty** (the TUI draws on a pty compose allocates\nfrom your terminal), so `chat` and `shell` refuse from a pipe or a cron line - that is\nwhat `ask` is for."],[1139,"**Approvals are the same gate, inline.** A dangerous command stops in your tty with\n`[o]nce | [s]ession | [a]lways | [d]eny`, deny by default. There is no browser card\nhere and nothing to approve elsewhere; the terminal door does not loosen\n`approvals.mode: manual`, and `--yolo` / `HERMES_YOLO_MODE` have no place in this\nstack. See Approvals."],[227,"**Autostop is disabled by default.**"],[1164,"**Both keyless switches stay off, and that is the part that keeps the promise.**\n`agent/web_search_registry.py` resolves a backend in five steps, and its last\none is an anonymous free-tier ring - `exa`, `parallel`, `firecrawl`,\n`keenable` - reached with no key at all. A seed that named no backend and left\n`web.keyless_fallback` and `web.keyless_rescue` at their shipped `true` would\nanswer a search from a vendor's public endpoint while looking local. With both\nfalse, a query either goes to your instance or fails on the box."],[26,"**Branch first, then edit.** The work branch is cut as the first action of the change, off the\ntip of `main`. `main` is not a place to work: an agent edits it only when the operator asked for\nthat in words. Cut late and the branch is cheap to add anyway - `git switch -c` carries the\nworking tree with it - which is the fix, where `stash`, `reset --hard`, or `checkout -- .` to\nclean the tree is how an unfinished change is destroyed."],[264,"**Build repo's dev container** (`use_devcontainer`, default `true`) is immutable:\nbuilding a dev container or not is a different topology. With Yes the editor lives in\nthe dev container; with No, in the plain container."],[306,"**Clones a non-empty Git repository field** into `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`\n(in `devcontainer`, onto the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>` bind). The\nscript creates that folder first (`mkdir -p` plus a chown to the workspace user) so\nthe editor's wait loop can see it, then clones into it. This is idempotent, runs as\nthe workspace user so their git credentials are consulted, and refuses to touch an\nexisting non-empty non-git folder there. An empty pre-created target still clones."],[27,"**Commit each unit as it lands.** A unit is what a reviewer reads as one step: a script with its\nBats file, a template with its twin, a flag with the docs that name it. Commit when that unit's\nown check is green, then start the next one. `git log --oneline main..HEAD` then shows the shape\nof the change, and a wrong turn is one `git revert` away instead of a lost afternoon."],[2095,"**Compose `profiles:`** when what you are hiding is a whole service."],[470,"**Create `coder.env` only for a *custom* GitHub OAuth app**, and fill EVERY key -\n`ID` names the provider and `TYPE` says what kind it is. Restart coder to apply\nthe file."],[475,"**Device-flow login is not the App install.** Private repos need the Coder GitHub\nApp installed from\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new\n(the workspace \"Install GitHub App\" button), then a restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints.\nThe grant must include the repos you clone, or a connected credential authenticates\nand is still 403."],[658,"**Diagrams.** A ` ```mermaid ` fence renders through `Mermaid`, follows the light/dark switch,\nand shows the chart text beside a parse error rather than leaving a blank box."],[200,"**Docker-in-docker** (`dind`, Yes/No radio, immutable)."],[136,"**Each body is its own `execve`, so each gets its own ceiling.** Splitting a body into\ntwo `coder_script` resources relocates the wall; it does not raise it. On this server\n(v2.36) the start scripts fan out concurrently in an errgroup and `ready` is set only\nafter all of them return, so splitting does not reduce a peak either.\n`coder_script_order` needs Coder `>= v2.39`. This is the escape the stack now uses: the\nthree skill payloads ride `coder_script.stack_skills` rather than the startup argument,\nbecause the devcontainer twin had 491 bytes of headroom left and three more inlined\npayloads could not fit there. `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` still rides the startup\nargument - it predates the split, it fits, and its own tests pin it there."],[1460,"**Edit `/etc/crypttab` or the LUKS headers.** The volumes are read to decide\nwhether the boot can open them. Key slots, passphrases and headers are never\nwritten, so this CLI cannot be the thing that destroys a key."],[1461,"**Enroll a TPM.** `systemd-cryptenroll` is a different design with a\ndifferent failure mode - a firmware update that changes the PCR policy seals\nthe box with nobody in the loop - and it belongs in its own change."],[659,"**Fenced code.** Every fence needs a language, checked by the same contract test, because the\nhighlighter and the copy button both key off it."],[899,"**First boot:** `https://<host>:3000` - run the install wizard once."],[202,"**Git repositories (additional)** (`additional_repos`, textarea, mutable, default\nempty)."],[201,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`, input, mutable). The Terraform default is empty\n(`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`); after a push that forwards\n`.env` `REPO_URL` (this stack's own repo in the sample) the form prefills that URL.\nAnything you type is cloned into the workspace on the next start."],[263,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`) is mutable: switching repository is a re-clone plus a\ndevcontainer build, not a new topology the way `workspace`'s DinD toggle is."],[975,"**Grok Build** keeps its own session, keyed off the repository: the helper walks up\nfrom the terminal's folder with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or uses the\nterminal's `$PWD` outside a clone), names the session `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`\nafter that root, and runs `grok --cwd <repo root>` inside it. A terminal opened\nanywhere inside the clone rejoins the one session the repository shares, and starts\nGrok Build at the repository root. That profile is deliberately the old\nalways-reattach shape: watching the same agent run from several tabs is the\nfeature, and the mirrors pay what mirrors pay. What the seed gives them is\n`focus-events on`, so a TUI in the pane can idle its animations while its tab\nis blurred, and `escape-time 10`, which keeps typing responsive on each of\nthem."],[205,"**Grok config extras** (`grok_config_extra`, textarea, mutable, default empty).\nWhatever you type is appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is only observably per-workspace while the\nprofile mode is `private`: one shared profile is one file for every workspace that\nowner owns, and the last boot to re-render it wins."],[204,"**Grok default model** (`grok_default_model`, dropdown, immutable, default `agent`)."],[203,"**Grok profile** (`grok_profile_mode`, dropdown, immutable, default `shared`).\n`private` gives that one workspace a private `~/.grok` on its own home volume, so one\nworkspace can sit outside the shared profile while its siblings stay inside it.\nSwitching needs a rebuild."],[535,"**Hermetic sandbox.** `test --spawn_strategy=local,sandboxed` and `build\n--spawn_strategy=sandboxed`: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not\nthe whole checkout. That is why a suite declares its stubs and payload files as `data` and\nwhy a script resolves its repo root with `resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`) instead of\ntrusting the cwd."],[2078,"**How is anything deployed here?** Look, do not assume: `compose*.yml`, `docker-compose*.yml`,\n`kustomization.yaml`, `values.yaml`, `inventories/`, `*.tf`, `argocd/`, `manage.sh`,\n`Makefile`, `MODULE.bazel`, `.github/workflows/`, `templates/*/main.tf`."],[476,"**How the token reaches a workspace.** It arrives as `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN`\nin the agent environment, and that pair is a snapshot: Coder mints it when the\nworkspace starts and never refreshes it, so it expires about an hour later while\nthe workspace keeps running. The template's `gh_token_refresh` coder_script\nre-mints on start and every 20 minutes (`0 */20 * * * *`) into the **gh auth store**,\nwhich is the copy that stays live. git asks its credential helper, and the\nstack's helper reads the store through a form that clears the stale pair first,\nso push follows the store and not the frozen environment. Check it with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check <repo-url>` - pass the repository,\nbecause without it the doctor lists credentials and never asks the write\nquestion. `bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix <repo-url>` repairs a\nworkspace that cannot push.\ncoder-templates.md owns\nthe template-side detail."],[987,"**Into the pane: `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click, on a secure context.** Both\narrive bracketed (`ESC[200~ ... ESC[201~`) in a bash terminal and in a tmux\npane alike, and a three-line paste arrives whole without a warning dialog. Both\nchords ask the browser's clipboard API, which the browser only exposes to a\nsecure context, so the URL the tab was opened on decides it. On the TLS public\nname they work; on `http://<LAN-address>` `navigator.clipboard` is absent,\nright-click stops with VS Code's own toast (\"This browser doesn't support the\nclipboard.readText API needed to trigger a paste, try Ctrl+V instead.\"), and\nthe browser's native paste event is what still delivers. A Clipboard site\npermission that was once denied breaks both API chords the same way, with\n`NotAllowedError: Read permission denied`. Neither is a tmux route: the prefix\npaste `]` and the buffer picker `=` are unbound, so the client's own clipboard is\nwhat arrives in the pane."],[1137,"**It enters the running container rather than starting a second one.** Hermes keeps\nits sessions and memory in a SQLite store under `/opt/data`, and two containers on one\ndata directory is the documented way to corrupt it - the WAL sidecars and the lock\nonly mean something inside one container. So while the gateway is up this is\n`docker compose exec`, and the terminal agent and the dashboard agent are literally\nthe same process on the same store. Only when nothing is running does it\n`docker compose run --rm --no-deps` a lone container. `--one-shot` forces the second\ncontainer anyway and says out loud what it risks."],[1146,"**It reports, it does not gate.** Exit 0 on a clean bill (warnings allowed), 1 when a\ncheck failed, 2 on a bad invocation, 5 when it cannot find a checkout.\n`hermes-shell -- check` stays the thing that decides whether to launch a session."],[1129,"**LAN:** `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` - plain HTTP, exactly like LiteLLM on `:4000`.\nThere is no self-signed cert for this port and no redirect to HTTPS. This URL stays\nwhen the public name is on. Tailscale reaches it the same way, on that port."],[469,"**Leave `coder.env` absent for the usual case.** Coder CE injects its own GitHub\nApp (provider id `github`, device flow) and both templates declare that id by\ndefault, so `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` works with no extra file.\nWorkspace owners connect GitHub under Settings -> Tokens / external auth."],[537,"**Local disk and repository cache.** `.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` at\n`~/.cache/bazel-disk` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache/bazel-repo`. A contributor\ncontainer and a Coder workspace reuse those directories across runs. In CI the gate\ncontainer is `--rm` and runs with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)`, so a plain run keeps\nneither cache between jobs, and Bazel can name a uid the image passwd file does not\nlist; the cache that survives there is the image's buildx GHA cache. No remote cache is\nconfigured — the same two flags would take a `--remote_cache=<uri>` endpoint later."],[1033,"**MCP servers: the default render declares three - `github`, `context7` and the\ngated `searxng` server, which is the search lane itself.** Each is a boolean gate in\nthe template, so one env flag parks exactly one block and a template re-push applies\nit fleet-wide: `GITHUB_MCP=false`, `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`, `SEARCH_MCP=false`.\nThe `searxng` block names the pre-installed `searxng-mcp-server` binary (pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image), is gated on\n`search_mcp` (default true) and dials the service above through `SEARXNG_URL`.\nThat URL is one of six keys the render sets in `[mcp_servers.searxng.env]`; the\nothers pin the bridge's own tuning rather than taking its compiled-in defaults:\n`SEARXNG_TIMEOUT_MS` (20s, above the instance's per-engine budget and equal to the\nprobe's `--timeout`), `SEARXNG_CACHE_TTL_MS` (15 minutes, which switches on the\nbounded 128-entry response cache that ships disabled, so a repeated query stops\nre-dialling backends that already refused this instance), `SEARXNG_MAX_RESULTS`\n(a ceiling that keeps one search inside the MCP output cap - on the pinned 1.0.77\nCLI that cap rides `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`, because `grok inspect` still answers\n`[mcp] — unrecognized config key` and never applies it),\n`SEARXNG_DEFAULT_SAFESEARCH` (mirrors `search.safe_search` in\n`searxng/settings.yml`) and `SEARXNG_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE` (`all`, so an image bump\ncannot quietly start geo-filtering every agent search).\nThe five offline stdio servers (`filesystem`, `memory`, `sequential-thinking`,\n`git` and `time`) stay pre-installed in the image (`npm -g` for the first\nthree, `uv tool` for the Python two), but the rendered `config.toml` names\nnone of them: the session traces collected on this machine called none of\ntheir tools, and a declared-but-unused server still costs every session its\nhandshake time and tool schemas. Declare one by hand and check a stubborn\nserver with `grok mcp doctor <name>` inside a workspace."],[141,"**Move the prose out.** Rationale belongs in this file, not in `startup.sh.tftpl`.\nComments in the template are bytes in everybody's argument; here they cost nothing."],[477,"**Never a static PAT in `.env`.** `.env.sample` used to carry a `GITHUB_TOKEN=`\nline holding a long-lived fine-grained PAT, which `push-template.sh` forwarded to\nboth templates as a template variable, into every workspace's environment. That\npath is gone: there is no such template variable and no such key in the twin. A\nworkspace's GitHub credential comes from the External Auth provider alone, so an\nowner who connected nothing has no GitHub credential anywhere - and the only way\nto add one by hand is per workspace, after `check` has answered `REFUSED-GRANT`,\n`NOT VISIBLE` or `REJECTED` (a passing read is not that answer):"],[1000,"**No published source names an upstream `/learn`, and the bundled skill set never\nlands here**: the `xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of\ndocs.x.ai, every first-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog carry zero occurrences of it. The bundled\nplatform skills are a separate set, enumerated by\n`FORMER_PLATFORM_SKILL_HASHES` in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/builtin.rs`\n(help, imagine, create-skill, check-work, check, xlsx, pptx, docx, code-review,\nbest-of-n, create-workflow), and neither `learn` nor `skillify` is among them. That\nset is downloaded at runtime through the CLI's proxy endpoints config\n(`cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`, gated by `GROK_SKILLS_BASE_URL`) and needs an x.ai\nsign-in: the upstream `product_skills*` fetch in\n`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/slash_commands.rs` is gated on\n`xai_grok_login::GrokAuth`. This stack has no x.ai credential:\nLiteLLM is the sole model transport, with `env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"` and\n`base_url` `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, so `~/.grok/bundled/` is never\ncreated and there is no bundled copy for `grok inspect` to label. The one surface\nnobody can read anonymously is the authenticated skills feed (`x.ai/skills/*` answers\n403), so the honest claim is that no published source names `/learn`, not that it\ncannot exist behind that feed. Nothing writes `~/.grok/skills/` either, but\nthat is a stack choice, not a CLI limit: the upstream user guide\n(`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/docs/user-guide/08-skills.md`) documents both tiers -\n`~/.grok/skills/` is the User scope and `<repo_root>/.grok/skills/` the Repo one, and\n`grok inspect` labels each definition by its actual source (`user`, `project`,\n`bundled`) - and startup discovery skips project skills and commands in untrusted\nfolders, so a project-tier folder has to be trusted first or its skill is withheld.\nWhat separates the two consumers is that the **Coder Agents** chat agent reads\n`~/.coder/skills/` only, so reaching both means shipping both homes."],[625,"**Not a credential store.** GitHub auth stays Coder External Auth; no static token and no\nPAT appears in any file this page describes."],[624,"**Not a second toolchain.** Nothing in the contributor path installs pins onto a laptop\nor a runner — that is what the image is for."],[623,"**Not a template change.** `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/startup.sh.tftpl` are untouched by any of this; the contributor\nseed reads them as payloads, it does not rewrite the twins."],[622,"**Not the stack host.** The compose host (gmktec) is bootstrapped with\n`bash scripts/setup-host.sh`, and it cannot be this container: it owns the daemon that\nruns Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes. See\n../project-conventions.md for the ingress rule that keeps\nthat script the single documented `bash scripts/…`."],[2094,"**One env var, read at one seam**, documented in the dotenv sample with its off default. This\ncarries most needs: `FEATURE_AGENT_TUNNEL_V2=0`."],[1136,"**Options** (before the subcommand): `-p/--project-name` (`evo-t1-prod`; the dev\nsandbox is `evo-t1-dev`), `-f/--file`, `-e/--env-file`, `-m/--model <alias>` for one\nsession, `-w/--writable <dir>`, `--one-shot`, `--dry-run`, `-h/--help`."],[982,"**Out of a pane: drag, and it is already on the clipboard.** tmux asks the outer\nterminal for mouse reporting only while `mouse` is on: measured on tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient, and an attach with it off writes none of them. With the seed's\n`mouse off` tmux never sees a button, so a plain drag is the client's own\nhighlight and the seed's `terminal.integrated.copyOnSelection: true` puts that\nhighlight on the machine clipboard with no chord (the harness recorded\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText` firing on a client selection, before this switch)."],[854,"**Prepend; never replace PATH.** `use_stubs` and `with_path` put the stub directory *in\nfront of* the host PATH. `PATH=\"${BIN}\"` takes `bash`, `grep`, `cut`, `sort` and `wc` away\nfrom the script under test as well, and a script that pipes its answer through\n`grep | wc | tr` then fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the contract the\nsuite means to check."],[1130,"**Public name, opt-in:** `hermes-<suffix>.<base>`, so\n`https://hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` under the shipped defaults, when\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` composes it or an explicit list names it. Bootstrap\nclassifies `hermes-*` and `hermes.*` and writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`. The path is the\nsame as the other public names: Cloudflare\n(orange cloud, WebSockets on by default), Traefik on the other LAN server (TLS\n`*.overeazy.io`, Authelia), the `router` on this box `:8080`, then\n`http://hermes:9119`, with the original Host preserved. Traefik's backend is\n`:8080`, not `:9119`. See public-access.md."],[30,"**Push once, when the pull request opens**, and pre-flight it: the log above reads as one commit\nper unit with imperative prefixed subjects, `git diff --stat main...HEAD` matches what was meant,\nand no `.env`, token, key, or workspace-local state is in any commit."],[1459,"**Reboot anything.** The reboot is the operator's decision, made after a\ngreen `check`."],[412,"**Refresh, and reconnect.** `coder external-auth access-token` is the live path when the\nstore goes stale: it re-mints on demand and answers with a live token, or with the\nre-authentication URL and exit 1; with no connected account the refresh script prints its\none note (pointing at **GitHub reauth**, and at **Install GitHub App** when the injected\nApp is in use) and exits 0, and mints nothing. After a reconnect, restart the workspace (or\nwait for the next `gh_token_refresh`) so the injected pair and the store match the new\ngrant. The refresh script unsets `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before\n`gh auth login --with-token`, because gh refuses stdin while those names are set."],[983,"**Right-click and middle-click paste that clipboard.** The seed sets\n`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` - copy while there is a\nselection, paste when there is none - and pins\n`terminal.integrated.middleClickBehavior: \"paste\"`, because the config registry in\nthe shipped 4.145.0 gives that setting the default `\"default\"`, which pastes only\non the Linux build. tmux has no middle-click paste of its own to answer first:\n`MouseDown2Pane` is unbound in the root table."],[1149,"**Run it before a `--force` re-seed and after a merge.** The drift check names the\nfile that differs and prints the `hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force` line that\nwould overwrite it, which is the pair an operator wants side by side."],[28,"**Stage by path.** `git add <path> <path>`, and read the index back with\n`git diff --cached --name-only`. `git add -A`, `git add .`, and `git commit -a` are how a scratch\nfile, a build artifact, or someone else's edit walks into a commit - and the secret hygiene rule\nabove depends on the agent being able to say what is in each one."],[142,"**Stage the payload as a file, not an argument.** The obvious resource does not\nexist: the OSS provider's `ResourcesMap` (`provider/provider.go`) registers\n`coder_agent`, `coder_agent_instance`, `coder_ai_task`, `coder_app`,\n`coder_metadata`, `coder_script`, `coder_env`, `coder_devcontainer` and\n`coder_external_agent`, and nothing else. `coder_file` was requested in\n`coder/coder#3055` and closed `not_planned` on 2022-09-23; a maintainer was still\nasking for it in 2025. Do not plan around it."],[1135,"**Subcommands:** `chat` (the default), `ask TEXT` for one question and exit, `shell`\nfor a shell with the container's mounts and socket, `check` for the readiness report,\n`commands` for the assistant's own help. Every word after the subcommand is handed to\nthe assistant verbatim, so a flag this script has never heard of still works."],[657,"**Tables.** A content table gets its head pinned under the navbar while the table is on\nscreen, and a mirrored scroll bar at the bottom of the viewport when its columns are wider\nthan the page. Both come from `docs-site/components/table-chrome.tsx`; a table that already\ncarries a class is left as authored."],[1040,"**The binary is missing from the golden image.** A warning: the running\n`evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake. Remedy: rebuild the image and recreate the\nworkspace, or run the `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command the check prints\nto install the pinned package into the running box (pins in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."],[1039,"**The block is missing from the rendered config.** That is a failure, not a\nwarning: the default render declares the lane, so something took it away - a push\npassed its gate `false`, the workspace runs an older template, or the file lost\nits stack marker and nothing re-renders it. Remedy: clear the gate and re-push,\nthen re-materialize the file with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>` from\nthe stack host, or `bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` for a\nhost home."],[1041,"**The credential is missing.** Also a warning, and per lane it means something\ndifferent. `github` with `$GH_TOKEN` empty is a real gap: connect GitHub External\nAuth (the **GitHub reauth** app) and start a new session. An empty\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is not a gap - see the key's paragraph above, the lane answers\nkeyless at the public rate limit."],[444,"**The env files.** `docker-compose.yml` builds its variable references in two\nsteps: `${VAR}` inside `expose` expands during the shell phase, so compose\nnever sees those variables and a changed value needs a rebuild, and an unset\nvariable collapses to `127.0.0.1:57620:57620/tcp`, which fails to bind. So the\nhost ports and addresses live in a key=value env file - `.env` for the stable\nstack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and each has a checked-in twin beside\nit that carries the keys and explains them in the comment lines above. A pull\nthat renames, adds or retires a key silently changes what the stack runs on."],[2097,"**The platform's parameters** (Terraform variables, Helm values, Ansible vars, Coder template\nvariables) when the flag selects infrastructure."],[1001,"**The stack ships its own three skill commands as `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify` and\n`/ez-create-skill`**, so they are stack payloads like the delegation skill, not something\nto install, and the `ez-` prefix keeps them clear of the upstream names they sit\nbeside: `/create-skill` is one of the CLI's bundled platform skills and\n`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory` are its built-in memory commands, while no\npublished source names a `/learn` or a `/skillify` at all. A user-scope skill overrides\na same-named bundled one, so a seed named `create-skill` would have hidden the bundled\ncopy.\n`/ez-learn` writes memory files - it reproduces the upstream `/flush`, `/dream`,\n`/remember` and `/memory`; the other two write skill files.\nThe three payloads - `templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md` - are seeded at workspace start by\n`coder_script.stack_skills`: each is written into `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and\ntwin-copied to `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, each only while that file is absent.\nTo take a\ncopy over, edit it and delete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a\nmarker-free file is user-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass\n`--force`. A deletion is undone - the boot writes the file again whenever it is\nabsent, and `refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores\nthe stack's copy. To hide the slash command, set `disabled = [\"ez-learn\"]` under\n`[skills]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`. A seeded `SKILL.md` shows up in\n`grok inspect --json` as `userInvocable: true`, and a live session advertises it in\nboth `slash_commands` and `skills`, so it works on whichever CLI the image baked at\n`/usr/local/bin/grok` and this needs no image rebuild; the **Grok Build** terminal\nprofile gets it because that profile execs the same binary."],[1145,"**The twelve checks:** `approvals` (manual, with cron and unattended both deny),\n`terminal-cwd`, `write-root`, `web-lane`, `keyless-egress`, `mcp-servers`,\n`seed-drift`, `mount-identity`, `image-pin`, `model-key`, `dashboard`, `memories`."],[445,"**The workspace config.** `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.grok/config.toml`,\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`, `~/.tmux.conf` and the `~/.coder` and\n`~/.agents` seeds are materialised inside each workspace when it first boots and\nare never revisited after that. A re-pushed template only reaches workspaces\nthat have been **start**ed or **restart**ed since the push - **stop** and\n**start** is what re-runs the provisioning script and therefore re-runs\n`before_config`."],[1147,"**Three findings it exists to catch**, each one measured on a live box:\n`terminal.cwd` still carrying the `${STACK_HOST_PATH}` token (Hermes validates that\nvalue with `expanduser()` alone, so the token reads as a missing directory while\n`hermes config get` prints it looking healthy), `STACK_HOST_PATH` naming a directory\nwith no checkout (compose mounts an empty directory over the repo and every\ncontainer-level check still reads healthy), and a `web` block that stopped pinning\n`searxng` or flipped a keyless switch back on, which routes a search to a vendor's\nfree tier while the config still looks local."],[1672,"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder and hermes 300s, LiteLLM 600s, Kasm 3600s - so\nTraefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all five at `3600s` is the\nsimple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway. To cap each tier separately,\ndeclare one `loadBalancer` service per tier, each pointing at the same\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with `passHostHeader: true` and its own transport\n(`evo-t1-coder` / `evo-t1-hermes` at `300s`, `evo-t1-litellm` at `600s`,\n`evo-t1-kasm` at `3600s`), and point each router at its own service. If a session\ndies at about a minute with the page still loaded, check the client-facing side\ntoo: an entryPoint's `transport.respondingTimeouts.readTimeout` (default 60s) and\n`idleTimeout` (default 180s) apply to the browser-to-Traefik hop."],[984,"**tmux keeps no buffer, so there is nothing to paste out of one.** `Prefix-[`\nstill enters vi copy-mode and it is a viewer: the cursor keys, `PPage`/`NPage`,\n`Ctrl-u`/`Ctrl-d` and `k`/`j` walk history, `q` leaves, and `Escape` clears a\nselection. Every copy that used to end a selection is unbound - Enter, `C-j`, `D`,\n`A`, the word and line copies a double- or triple-click fires, the copy a drag\nrelease ran, and the `y` the earlier seed bound itself - along with the prefix\npaste `]` and the buffer picker `=`. Selecting inside copy-mode with `Space`, `V`\nor the cursor keys stays bound and is inert, with no copy command left for it to\nend on. Proof: sourcing the payload on a scratch server leaves `list-keys` with no\ncopy command in `copy-mode-vi`, no `paste-buffer` or `choose-buffer` in `prefix`,\nand no `MouseDown2Pane` in `root`. The pointer's other jobs keep their prefix\nkeys: `Prefix-o` or `Prefix-q` to select a pane, and `Prefix-Alt-arrow` to resize\n(plain `Prefix-arrow` selects a pane in the shipped 3.4's own default table)."],[991,"**tmux options.** `templates/common/tmux.conf` seeds the four options Doctor's\nown fixes write, `set -g set-clipboard on`, `set -g allow-passthrough on`,\n`set -g extended-keys on` and the `*:RGB` `terminal-features` entry, plus the\n`xterm*:extkeys` entry Doctor leaves to the operator. Those last two decide\nwhat survives the multiplexer: tmux rewrites 24-bit color down to the palette\nan attached client advertises, which is what makes a theme look washed out in\nthe browser, and extkeys is what makes Shift+Enter and Ctrl+. arrive distinct\nfrom Enter. A client picks up `terminal-features` when it attaches, so after a\nreseed run `tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf` and then detach and reattach\n(a `refresh-client` keeps the client on its old features). Proof:\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'` lists `RGB` and `extkeys`.\n`tests/bats/tmux-fidelity.bats` boots a scratch server on the seed and reads\nall of it back."],[275,"**Treat the repository as untrusted code.** Everything it builds runs on a privileged\ndaemon on this box, so a Dockerfile in that repo is root-equivalent on the stack host.\nThe create form's own description says so, and only repositories you trust belong in\nthe field."],[989,"**Two scroll-back stores, two different budgets.** `terminal.integrated.scrollback`\nin the settings seed is the xterm.js buffer, which is what a plain `bash`\nprofile scrolls through; tmux's `history-limit` is what `Prefix-[` walks\ninside a pane. They read 2000 and 100000 lines here, and the two numbers\ndiffer on purpose because the two stores are billed differently. VS Code\npreallocates the xterm.js buffer from the setting's value - its own\ndescription warns that \"as the value increases, so will the amount of\nmemory\", against a VS Code default of 1000\n(terminalConfiguration.ts) -\nand xterm.js keeps 3 `Uint32` words per column per line\n(BufferLine.ts),\nso that cost is paid per terminal in the tab, and again per view of a pane. tmux\nholds its history once per pane, in the server, and every client of that pane\nshares the one buffer. Hence the split: the deep store is tmux's and the\nbrowser keeps a short viewport. Inside a tmux pane the client's wheel has nothing\nto walk - tmux paints in the alternate screen, so that buffer holds no pane\nhistory - and the 100000 lines live behind `Prefix-[`, paid for in the server and\nnot in the tab. Measured on tmux 3.4 with one pane pushed to its limit, 100000\nlines of 120-column output holds the tmux server at 381 MB RSS; the same 100000\nlines of plain 80-column output costs 60 MB. tmux reads `history-limit` when a\npane is created, so an existing pane keeps the number it was born with."],[707,"**Use `--json` when you want to act on the answer.** Prose (`== section ==`, `ok:`, `note:`,\n`summary:`) rides stderr and stdout carries only labels, one per line, which is the shape\n`bazelisk test` consumes:"],[1671,"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."],[2076,"**What does each non-trunk branch hold that the trunk does not?**"],[2079,"**What flags already exist?** `grep -rnE 'FEATURE_|_ENABLED|^\\s*profiles:'` over the compose\nfiles, dotenv samples, Terraform variables and scripts."],[2080,"**What is the gate you must not break?** `bazelisk test //:test-fast`, `npm test`,\n`make test`, `./manage.sh <check>`, `pytest`, `go test ./...`. Run it before you start so a\nlater red is attributable."],[1141,"**What it can see:** the checkout at `$STACK_HOST_PATH`, the deploy workspace at\n`${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}`, `/opt/stack-skills`, the data volume, and\nthe docker socket. **What it cannot:** `/home` is not mounted, so no dotfiles, SSH\nkeys or shell history - by design. The image carries git and the Docker CLI but not\nbazelisk, so inside the container the same operations go through\n`docker compose -p evo-t1-prod -f \"$STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml\"`."],[1144,"**What it reads:** the config the container actually loads, the environment it was\ncreated with, the mounts it was given, and the two seed files beside the checkout.\nThe created environment is the fact and the `.env` beside the compose file is the\nintention: compose interpolated the former at create time, so a key can be right in\nthe file and stale in the container until the next recreate. Every container-level\nfact comes from `docker inspect` or `docker compose ps`; nothing writes, restarts,\nrecreates, or execs."],[14,"**What this enables**"],[90,"**What this enables**"],[438,"**What this enables**"],[514,"**What this enables**"],[568,"**What this enables**"],[642,"**What this enables**"],[688,"**What this enables**"],[764,"**What this enables**"],[817,"**What this enables**"],[883,"**What this enables**"],[917,"**What this enables**"],[966,"**What this enables**"],[1121,"**What this enables**"],[1217,"**What this enables**"],[1247,"**What this enables**"],[1294,"**What this enables**"],[1425,"**What this enables**"],[1484,"**What this enables**"],[1554,"**What this enables**"],[1583,"**What this enables**"],[1626,"**What this enables**"],[1750,"**What this enables**"],[2047,"**What this enables**"],[9,"**What's on this page**"],[84,"**What's on this page**"],[432,"**What's on this page**"],[508,"**What's on this page**"],[561,"**What's on this page**"],[636,"**What's on this page**"],[680,"**What's on this page**"],[757,"**What's on this page**"],[810,"**What's on this page**"],[878,"**What's on this page**"],[912,"**What's on this page**"],[958,"**What's on this page**"],[1113,"**What's on this page**"],[1213,"**What's on this page**"],[1243,"**What's on this page**"],[1287,"**What's on this page**"],[1419,"**What's on this page**"],[1476,"**What's on this page**"],[1551,"**What's on this page**"],[1578,"**What's on this page**"],[1620,"**What's on this page**"],[1744,"**What's on this page**"],[2039,"**What's on this page**"],[2075,"**Which branches exist, how old are they, who owns them?**"],[411,"**Why the env pair goes stale.** Coder injects `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` into the agent\nenvironment when the workspace starts. That is a GitHub App user-to-server token, it\nexpires about an hour later, and the injected copy is never refreshed for the life of the\nworkspace: `gh_token_refresh` re-mints on start and every 20 minutes into the gh auth store\nand the gitconfig helper, never into the environment, and no process can rewrite another\nprocess's environment anyway. So for most of a workspace's life the helper echoes a dead\ntoken, github.com answers `remote: Invalid username or token. Password authentication is\nnot supported for Git operations.`, and the healthy store beside it is never asked. `gh`\nconsults the same pair before its own store, so inside that workspace `gh` needs\n`env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh <args>` for the whole of its life; `git` needs no\nprefix once the helper is the env-proof form above, which is what `fix` installs."],[199,"**Workspace image** (`workspace_image`, dropdown, immutable). `--var image=` seeds its\ndefault and is also injected as its own option, so a custom tag still reaches the\ncontainer while the form stays honest about what it is building."],[230,"`--activity-bump` goes to `0` with it: a bump only extends a deadline, and with no\ndeadline there is nothing to extend."],[389,"`--additional-repos` is the opt-in escape hatch for the service-and-the-library-it-uses\ncase. It takes a comma-separated list (`\"url1,url2\"`), one URL per line works too, and\nthe spaces around each entry are trimmed. It joins the list into one `additional_repos`\nparameter, so `\"a , b\"` and `\"a\\nb\"` both arrive in the template as `a,b`."],[1606,"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough). `bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."],[1769,"`--check` prints which nameservers are in play and whether `github.com` resolves.\nOther ways to keep a public resolver: add `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` as a global\nnameserver in the Tailscale admin DNS page."],[289,"`--devcontainer` forces the devcontainer template and `--template <name>` overrides the\ntemplate choice outright."],[395,"`--dry-run` prints the list under `also:` before it creates anything, so the decision\nis readable first."],[1053,"`--help` prints the same two commands with every flag and every `CODER_AGENTS_*`\noverride, which is the list to reach for from a shell."],[461,"`--home` takes a host directory shaped like a workspace `HOME`, which is also how\nyou try a change on a scratch tree before pointing a live stack at it. See\ntroubleshooting.md for the docker-vantage rules."],[1457,"`--iface ens3` with `--ip dhcp` produces the same pinned-device shape with\nDHCP kept. A name the kernel never assigned is a blocker, not a typo you\ndiscover after the reboot: `check` compares it against `/sys/class/net`."],[1854,"`--noproxy '*'` matters: with a proxy set in the environment curl hands the name to the\nproxy instead of resolving it, and `000` or a 5xx then describes the proxy."],[1605,"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name). The env file follows the runner: the dev project takes `dev-stack.env`\nbeside the compose file when one exists, and any other project gets none, so a run aimed at\nthe stable stack never picks up the dev host ports; `prod-stack` names no env file unless\n`-e` does, and compose then reads the `.env` beside the compose file itself."],[524,"`--repair` is the recovery when `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` dies creating\nthe runfiles tree (`FileAccessException ... Permission denied` on\n`homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`). Bazel never reaches the stack script, so the\nrepair has to live in this shell entry point. On Linux it also installs public\nDNS so `github.com` still resolves when Tailscale MagicDNS is the only\nnameserver (`Could not resolve host: github.com` after a compose `up`). `--check`\nreports that lookup and writes nothing."],[1978,"`--source=chain` (the default) probes every credential that could answer for that\nrepository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or `--source=mint` answers one slot and says\nbeside the answer that git and gh consult the env pair first."],[1056,"`--wait` (or `--wait=SECONDS`) polls for that first owner instead of skip-0. The\nwindow is counted from the database's first \"no owner\" answer, so a slow buildinfo\nprobe or a slow first query cannot spend it. `SKIP_CODER_AGENTS=1` skips bootstrap\nand stack-up seeding. `CODER_AGENTS_WAIT_SECS`\nis what stack `up` forwards as `--wait=`."],[1694,"`./.env: line 188: qwen2.5-coder:14b: command not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:pull-models` -> the model list was being parsed as shell"],[618,"`.devcontainer/contributor-env` is a sentinel, not a config file. It is tracked in this\nrepository, and `probe_repo` (in `scripts/lib/workspace.sh`) treats a clone that tracks it\nas a **miss** — exit `3` — even when the same clone also tracks\n`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`."],[579,"`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` is what that reads:"],[467,"`.env.sample` deliberately carries no GitHub credential of any kind, so the env\npass has nothing to reconcile here: the twin declares no `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\nkey and `update-configs` will never add one. Those keys belong in `coder.env`\nbeside `docker-compose.yml` (gitignored), which is not a file this tool touches."],[48,"`.env` keys it reads"],[613,"`.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds `images/dev` with `docker buildx build` and the GitHub\nActions layer cache (`type=gha`), then `docker run`s that image and runs the checks inside\nit: `//:test-fast`, `//:lint`, `//:validate`, and the `docs-site/` Node checks."],[1722,"`.terraform.lock.hcl is missing` -> a note, not a failure"],[1005,"`[models] default` points at the `grok_default_model` alias - `agent` unless the\ntemplate variable or the create form's **Grok default model** dropdown names\nanother. `[models] default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`. The Spark lane answers\n`low` / `medium` / `xhigh`; `high` is never advertised."],[1007,"`[models]` also carries `extra_headers` with\n`x-litellm-session-id: ws-<workspace-id>` - the key LiteLLM uses to pin a\nworkspace's traffic to one healthy Spark box. Under a shared profile the\nlast-booted workspace's key wins for the whole fleet."],[1011,"`[permission]` holds the guardrails the stack owns; the approval mode itself stays\na per-user call (`[ui] permission_mode`). `Bash(rm -rf *)` blocks the destructive\nform, and the dotenv family is split by what a file actually holds: the live\n`.env` and the populated suffixed shapes (`.env.local`, `.env.*.local`,\n`.env.production` / `.env.prod`, `.env.staging` / `.env.stage`,\n`.env.development` / `.env.dev`, `.env.test` / `.env.testing`, `.env.ci`,\n`.env.override`) are denied for `Read` and `Edit`, while the secret-free\ntemplates a repo ships (`.env.example`, `.env.sample`, `.env.template`,\n`.env.dist`, `.env.defaults`, plus the `.env.*.example` / `.env.*.sample`\nspellings) are named in `allow`, so an agent maintains the env key list without a\nprompt in any mode. Neither list is the old blanket `**/.env.*`: deny outranks\nallow, so the blanket form blocked the templates along with the live files and\nleft the key list -- the file that keeps a real `.env` honest -- to drift.\n`.gitignore` carries the matching half on the git side: the whole family is\nignored except the names `allow` lists."],[1968,"`[shadowed: the credential above outranks this slot, ...]` is the most misread line in\nthe report: the credential under it is healthy and git will not use it while the env\npair stands. `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN <command>` makes git or gh use that slot\nfor one command; `fix` makes it permanent for git. See\nthe Bad credentials entry."],[1790,"`[tensorfold] done chatcmpl-... prompt=58 ... effort=xhigh tokens=16 finish=length`\nfollowed by `warning: a reply reached max_tokens while still thinking, so its content\nis empty`: that is the LiteLLM proxy's health check, not a user. At the pinned tag the\nproxy sends the deployment's whole `litellm_params` through a real `acompletion`, so\nthe lane's baked `reasoning_effort` rides along, and it asks for the default\n`health_check_max_tokens` of 16. A thinking reply cannot finish its reasoning in 16\ntokens, so it returns empty content and the server warns about it. The reply is a\nhealthy 200 and the deployment stays in the pool."],[1792,"`[tensorfold] ended chatcmpl-... reason=client-left prompt=... after=Ns`: whoever asked\nhung up. A tiny prompt abandoned at around 81 s was a health check queued behind\nlonger work; a huge prompt abandoned at around 400 s is a client with its own timeout,\nand it re-prefills from zero if it retries."],[727,"`//:test-fast` runs in a sandbox: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not the\ncheckout. So \"what does this target read\" is a real question with a printed answer, and the answer has\ntwo depths worth keeping separate."],[773,"`//:validate` adds the Terraform half, and `//tests:bats_mypy_lint_test` and\n`//tests:bats_tf_types_test` are the asserting sides of the two newest gates."],[663,"`//docs-site:nav_test` and the contract test together refuse a page that exists but is not\ndeclared, and a declaration whose file is gone, so an unlinked page cannot be merged."],[788,"`//lints:shell` runs ShellCheck at `--severity=warning` over every first-party\n`.sh`, and the tree is clean at that level today."],[1824,"`//scripts:coder-agents` reads that same health view before it registers anything, so\nonce the weights are back, re-running it registers the alias and takes the\n`compaction` / `title_generation` pins off it. A `warning:` line names the alias it\nrefused to register, and the closing `summary:` counts the warnings, so a run that\nquietly wired half the model list is visible."],[1430,"`//scripts:luks-unlock` writes that config and then proves the generated\ninitramfs really contains the unlock path. The proof is the point: every one of\nthe six settings involved fails by staying silent until the boot that needs it."],[1544,"`//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` keeps `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD[\"new-repo\"]` honest about what the CLI\nactually reads."],[1543,"`//tests:bats_new_repo_test` owns the renderer: token substitution in file contents and in file\nand directory names, the markdown `**NAME**` alias, the lane overlay and its `gitignore`\nfragments, the generator-owned paths stripped from every render, the argv of the scaffold clone,\nthe credential ladder that retries once with `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` cleared, the GitHub ladder\nstaying advisory (a refused step warns and never fails the run), and the Coder Task being a\nconvenience that cannot fail a render."],[842,"`//tests:py_coverage` discovers `tests/python/test_*.py` and\n`docs-site/test_gen_nav.py` under coverage.py 7.17, pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image; without it the\ntarget fails with a pointer to `//scripts:test-in-image` rather than passing while\nmeasuring nothing. `//docs-site:unit_coverage` is `npm run unit:coverage`\n(vitest v8 provider, thresholds in `docs-site/vitest.config.ts`)."],[839,"`//tests:shell_coverage` is the one to understand before you write a shell test.\nIt builds a mirror of the checkout with `tests/coverage/instrument.py`: every\nproduction `.sh` is copied with one extra statement at the top of each function\nbody that appends `<path>\\t<function>` to `$EVO_COV_LEDGER`, and every other path\nstays a symlink into the real tree. `tests/bats_runner.sh` points `REPO_ROOT` at\nthat mirror when `EVO_COV_MIRROR` is set, so the suites execute the instrumented\ncopies through whichever access pattern they already use — run the CLI, source the\nlib, or `load_shell_function`. Then `tests/coverage/aggregate.py` names every\nfunction whose body never ran."],[339,"`/ez-learn` is seeded on the same absent-only contract because nothing here supplies the\ncommand: no published source ships an upstream `/learn` at all, and the built-in memory\nfamily it copies is gated behind a memory store that is off by default, so those names\nstay hidden. The bundle is a separate thing, and a real one: the CLI extracts\nplatform skills such as `create-skill` into `~/.grok/bundled/`, read back with\n`SkillScope::Bundled`, and that catalog is fetched per identity/team behind an x.ai\nsign-in this stack deliberately does not have - LiteLLM is the only transport - so the\ndirectory is never created and none of those skills ever lands. Writing the file is what\nmakes each command exist, and it is seeded under the `ez-` name, which exists to stay\nclear of the upstream ones. The same stage deletes a leftover `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from\nbefore the rename, but only while it still carries the stack marker; a marker-free one is\nuser-owned and stays. Deleting a seeded copy never keeps your own - the boot writes it\nback - so only dropping that marker does."],[1180,"`/home` is **not** mounted in v1. Hermes cannot see your dotfiles, your SSH keys or\nyour shell history, and it should not need to."],[1173,"`/opt/data` is a **named volume on purpose**. Hermes keeps a SQLite database there in\nWAL mode, and WAL on a virtiofs bind mount is not safe. Bind-mounting that path is how\nyou corrupt the assistant's own memory."],[1865,"`/tmp/coder-startup-script.log` holds exactly one `curl: (28)` line when the\nstart-time download was what failed."],[779,"`# type: ignore` needs a specific error code and a reason that names the real cause.\n`warn_unused_ignores` makes a stale one an error, so an ignore cannot quietly\noutlive the problem it papered over. Where the value is unstructured but an\n`isinstance` guard already decides what it is, a `cast` is the honest instrument:\nnarrowing there would change what happens to the malformed input."],[1189,"`<mount>` is the host directory of the Hermes data volume (what compose mounts at\n`/opt/data`). `--force` is how an existing volume picks up the seeded\n`dashboard.trusted_proxies`, and `--cwd /absolute/path/to/checkout` is how a host re-seed\nnames the working directory when the shell running it carries no `STACK_HOST_PATH`.\nSetting that key with `hermes config set` is the\nin-container alternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is not in the volume;\nrecreate the container and the env applies."],[1075,"`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`"],[1082,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"],[1078,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"],[1080,"`~/.agents/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"],[1076,"`~/.agents/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"],[1073,"`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`"],[1081,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"],[1077,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"],[1079,"`~/.coder/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"],[1074,"`~/.coder/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"],[344,"`~/.grok/config.toml` is emitted from a quoted heredoc for the same reason: a\n`Bearer $${GH_TOKEN}` line the config template emits travels through as literal text\ninstead of being expanded there, and the CLI expands it from the agent environment. The\nsame holds for the `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` reference."],[321,"`~/.grok/config.toml` is re-rendered when it is absent, when\n`GROK_FRESH_INSTALL` is set, or while *either* marker is present: the `managed-by`\ncomment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key."],[326,"`~/.grok/notes-setup.sh` is the exception to the absent-only rule: it is stack-owned code\nand the copy on the shared profile volume has to be the one this template shipped, so it\nis rewritten every start. It is written beside its target and `mv`'d in - and `chmod\n0644`'d first, because `mktemp` makes 0600 and `mv` keeps that mode - so the rename is the\nfirst moment the file exists for anyone to source, and a sibling workspace booting at the\nsame time against the one shared volume never sources a half-written library."],[327,"`~/.grok` is not necessarily a workspace's own directory, and that decides several of the\nrules above."],[465,"`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` is the one rendered target it does reseed, and\nit re-*renders* rather than copies: `${litellm_url}` and the `$${...}`\ndollar-brace unfoldings are rewritten, and that workspace's own `cline.apiKey`\nand `roo.openaiApiKey` are carried forward."],[320,"`~/.tmux.conf` and `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` are rewritten while the\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` line is present; editing the file away from the marker\nhands it to the user for good, and each prints one warning line when it is marker-free."],[309,"`~/.tmux.conf` is seeded *before* the two terminal helpers. `tmux` reads that file once\nat server start and every terminal (plus an SSH client that reattaches) shares that one\nserver, so seeding after the first `tmux new-session -A` could run would leave that\nsession unconfigured for the whole boot. `mouse off` is what keeps every click with\nthe terminal client: tmux requests no mouse reporting, so a drag is the client's own\nhighlight, the settings seed's `copyOnSelection` puts it on the machine clipboard,\nand right-click and middle-click paste it back. Measured on the shipped tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient and an attach with it off writes none of them. tmux keeps no clipboard of its\nown next to that: the copy-mode copies, the prefix paste `]`, the buffer picker `=`\nand the root-table middle-click paste are unbound, and `Prefix m` is only the switch\nthat hands the wheel to tmux for a long scroll and hands it back.\n`set-clipboard on` and `allow-passthrough on` stay for the one copy route that is\nnot tmux's own - an app inside a pane (vim's `+` register, anything that emits\nOSC 52) drives the machine clipboard through them, which is what a native terminal\nhands that app - and a client that blocks OSC 52 (iTerm2 by default) gets nothing at\nall, because there is no tmux buffer left to fall back to. The browser build of VS\nCode drops that escape as well\n(microsoft/vscode#318254), so a\npane app's OSC 52 copy is silent in the tab and lands over ssh from a real terminal\napp, while the operator's own copy in the tab is the plain drag."],[1855,"`000` on the first probe means nothing listened on 4000. A `200 application/json`\nmeans LiteLLM answered, so the model lane is healthy and the app lane failed\nsomewhere else."],[1856,"`000` on the second probe means the private lane is down as well, so the public\nfailure is not a cloud-side one. `421` means the Host header matched no\n`server_name` here, which is what a rewritten Host looks like. `STACK_ROUTER_CONF`\nin `.env` names the conf those `server_name` lines live in; the vhost side of it is\nin UI and logins."],[2006,"`421` means this box has no vhost for that Host. `STACK_ROUTER_CONF` in `.env` is\nwhat tells you which conf compose actually mounted, so read that file's\n`server_name` lines first, then recreate `router` so it picks up the one that\nlists `server_name hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`."],[1604,"`additional_repos` — the `--additional-repos` flag on the new-workspace CLI, and the\n`coder_parameter` of the same name that receives its value in both templates\n(`mutable = true`, default empty): extra git URLs for the workspace's clone folders,\ngiven as `url1,url2` or one URL per line. The flag exists on\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` only - the stack runners (`dev-stack`,\n`prod-stack`) have no `--additional-repos` (their flags are `-p`, `-f`, `-e`,\n`--tail`, `--follow`, `--json`, `-y`, `-h`)."],[497,"`AGENT_MODEL_URL` is the single `/v1/models` URL the \"Agent backend\" card uses\nfor both its browser link and its status probe. All three boxes serve the same\nmodel id, so point it at any reachable one; the per-box health keys are what tell\nyou which box answered."],[1155,"`agent` also thinks hard by default: it bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`.\n**`agent-low`**, **`agent-medium`** and **`agent-xhigh`** are the thinking variants of\nit - the same Qwen3.8-Flash-Next on the same three Spark boxes, with real\n`tool_calls` and that level baked into the alias instead. Use one when a run needs a\ndifferent level; Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and `xhigh` and nothing else."],[1335,"`agent` is not the unthinking default. It bakes `reasoning_effort: xhigh`, so a\nCoder Agents chat on the default alias thinks hard, and `agent-xhigh` asks the\nbox for the same level by name. A `reasoning_effort` the client sends in the\nrequest still wins over the baked one."],[448,"`all` runs `env` and then `workspace`."],[1310,"`allowed_fails: 40` and `cooldown_time: 5` decide when a box stops being a candidate."],[782,"`allowJs` and `checkJs` are on, which is how the two operator CLIs\n`docs-site/scripts/serve_static.mjs` and `verify_site.mjs` are type-checked: each\nopens with `// @ts-check` and carries JSDoc `@param`/`@returns` on every function,\nwith `@typedef`s for the shapes it reads out of `lib/nav.json`. `zod` already\nvalidates the frontmatter at the content boundary in `docs-site/lib/content.ts`, so\nparsed pages arrive as typed `PageNode`s rather than as `unknown`."],[783,"`any`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error`, and `@ts-nocheck` are absent from every\ntracked file. The one exclusion is `docs-site/.source/`, the code fumadocs-mdx\ngenerates, which is gitignored. `docs-site/tests/unit/helpers/fumadocs-stub.ts` is\nwhere a dependency's shape gets stated once when a test needs one."],[1669,"`authelia@file` is a placeholder for the Authelia middleware that box already\ndeclares. Name it exactly as that box names it - a middleware created by labels is\n`authelia@docker`, and a name Traefik cannot resolve fails the router rather than\nserving it unauthenticated. The four interactive names carry it; LiteLLM must not."],[1654,"`auto` composes the five names from the parts above;"],[1311,"`background_health_checks: true`, with `health_check_interval: 300` and\n`health_check_concurrency: 2`, re-probe a box on their own, so recovery does not\nwait for user traffic to rediscover it. Those two keys have to sit in\n`general_settings:`; under `router_settings:` the proxy raises `ValueError` at boot.\nThe interval is LiteLLM's own default and the concurrency is deliberately small:\na probe is a real generation that carries the lane's baked `reasoning_effort`, and\non a box whose prompt cache sits full it evicts a warm conversation to fit itself.\nSee the TensorFold log section of troubleshooting.md."],[1727,"`bash: -c: line 32: syntax error near unexpected token '('` after \"Running code-server in the background\" -> HCL `$$((` in the editor wait loop"],[1012,"`Bash(rm -rf *)` is a prefix glob, so it catches a benign per-path scratch cleanup\nalong with the destruction it is there to prevent. Agents run into that constantly:\nacross 271 workspace sessions, 86 recorded at least one denied tool call and 56\nattempted an `rm -rf`, and 55 sessions did both. The shapes that are never denied\nare per-path deletions - `find <dir> -type f -delete`, or `rm -f` naming each path -\nso clean scratch that way. An `allow` entry cannot rescue `rm -rf`, because deny\noutranks allow. Narrowing the pattern is an operator call and a deliberate one: it\nis the only thing between an agent and a recursive delete on a path it got wrong."],[850,"`bats_source_repo_root` — the checkout whose **bytes** are what the stack ships, which\nunder the coverage mirror is the one the mirror was built from (`EVO_COV_SOURCE_ROOT`)\nwhile `REPO_ROOT` is the mirror. A gate that counts the size of a shipped payload reads\nit there; the production functions it loads still come from `REPO_ROOT`, so measuring\nthe shipping tree costs no coverage mark."],[849,"`BATS_TEST_TMPDIR` — per-test scratch, removed after each test. `make_scratch` prints a\nfresh directory under it; `fake_root`-style helpers build a throwaway checkout there."],[705,"`bazel-map` is a thin shell over `bazelisk query`. It reads nothing and writes nothing; each verb\nis one query, run from the real checkout, with Bazel's stderr kept so a miss is reported instead of\nswallowed. Run it through Bazel like every other CLI:"],[1657,"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"],[575,"`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` refuses to build when a pin has no matching\n`ARG`, or when an `ARG` default drifted from the pin. Add both sides of a new pin in the\nsame change."],[418,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- -h` - full usage: the verdict kinds, both argument\norders, both modes and the fix contract."],[417,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> [-]` - STACK HOST ONLY (it\ndrives `docker exec`). Installs a pasted PAT on stdin into that one workspace, then\nre-installs the env-proof helper that `gh auth setup-git` would otherwise overwrite.\nLegitimate only once `check` has already answered REFUSED-GRANT, NOT VISIBLE or REJECTED:\nthe repo rule is that a workspace's credential comes from the External Auth provider, so\nno verdict short of those justifies a pasted token. It REPLACES the per-start minted\ncredential, the next `gh_token_refresh` rewrites the store while an account is connected,\nand `gh auth logout` or a rebuild clears it - treat it as a stand-in and rotate or remove\nit in GitHub -> Settings -> Applications when the work ends. The PAT arrives on stdin only\n(the `-` form, or the hidden prompt): argv is readable through `ps` and keeps the token in\nshell history."],[415,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check [container] [repo-url] [--source=<slot>]` -\nread-only. Inside a workspace or the contributor devcontainer it runs SELF mode with no\ndocker and prints `self:` lines for this environment's own chain; on the stack host it\nenumerates the labeled workspace containers, or probes a named container alone.\n`--source=chain|env|gh|mint` names one slot (chain is the default), and flags may go\nbefore or after the positionals. The last line is `push would WORK` or\n`push would FAIL`, keyed to the credential git would really send, and check exits\nnon-zero on FAIL. The verdicts are PUSH-READY (200 advertising refs), REFUSED-GRANT\n(403, it authenticates and every write is denied), NOT VISIBLE (404, hidden or absent),\nREJECTED (401, the credential itself is refused, as an expired mint is), or no useful\nanswer (offline)."],[416,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix [container] [repo-url]` - THE ONE COMMAND,\nand the answer to every `push would FAIL` line. Inside a workspace it repairs that\nworkspace; on the host a named container repairs that one workspace, and no container is\nrefused there because it names the blast radius. It mints a live token through Coder\nexternal-auth, writes the gh auth store, points git's `github.com` and `gist.github.com`\ncredential helper at that store through a form that clears the env pair first, then\nre-runs the write probe and prints the verdict again, so it proves itself. Idempotent.\nWhen nothing can be minted AND the store is empty its line begins `HANDOFF` and names the\nthree clicks the human owes it."],[1837,"`bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` covers both hops plus the config side in one\npass: the declared lanes, whether each `command=` binary is there, whether\n`~/.grok/config.toml` is still stack-managed, whether the lane's credential is\nset, whether the search lane returns results for a plain lookup rather than just\nan HTTP 200, and each server's handshake. A missing binary prints the exact\n`npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command that puts it back."],[276,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url> [name]` answers the two questions\nabove for you: which template, and whether to build the dev container."],[1934,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` decides that for you, per repo:"],[1762,"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` is the command that pair recovers with; the\n`make prod-stack-up` (and `make stack-up`) shims run `--repair` before calling that same\ntarget. Bare `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` repairs leftovers and then installs any missing\nhost tools. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --check` lists leftover paths without writing."],[1912,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints it because no lockfile is committed\nunder `templates/`, and Coder pushes the directory without one."],[117,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` runs `scripts/push-template.sh`."],[1834,"`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` (or `scripts/search-check.sh` from a shell that\ncan reach the host gateway) dials the service directly. It reads `$SEARXNG_URL`\nbefore its `http://127.0.0.1:8181` default, and every workspace's agent env carries\nthat key, so a workspace-side probe needs no `--url`. It separates engines that\nanswered, engines that were unresponsive, and an empty result page, and it names\neach engine the instance has parked together with the reason the backend gave\n(`parked by its backend: google: access denied`). A park expires after\n`search.suspended_times` in `searxng/settings.yml`, so re-run before writing an\nengine off. `--json` adds `engines_parked` for a dashboard to trend."],[488,"`bootstrap.sh` derives `STACK_DASHBOARD_URL` / `STACK_CODER_URL` /\n`STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` from the prefix on\neach entry (`coder-` / `kasm-` / `litellm-` / `hermes-`, dotted form still\nrecognised) and upgrades `CODER_ACCESS_URL` to the coder name, so Coder builds\nworkspace links on a secure context with a real certificate. Compose sets\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the Chat tab's Host and\nOrigin)."],[1715,"`Cannot connect to the Docker daemon at tcp://dind:2375` -> the sidecar, or DinD off"],[249,"`cgroupns_mode = \"private\"` and `shm_size = 1024` (MB to the provider, so 1 GiB) are\nboth load-bearing; the sidecar is torn down with the workspace because it is gated on\n`start_count` as well as on the toggle."],[1158,"`chat` - `qwen2.5:7b` on the Arc, and it returns real `tool_calls`. The reliable\ndegrade path when every Spark box is down."],[346,"`clone_owner` is whichever user the workspace really belongs to: the owner of `$HOME`\nwhen the agent runs as root, otherwise whoever is running the script. It decides both the\nchown target and whose git credentials the clone consults."],[152,"`coder login` on the host must reach the API *before* it has a credential, and in\npublic mode the only URL it will use is `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, which Authelia gates."],[1920,"`coder server` logs it; the host `coder` CLI does not."],[192,"`coder templates` is the canonical name; `template` is its alias."],[1918,"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` itself stays the public name: browsers need it, and only CLI\ntraffic should not use it."],[1830,"`CODER_ACCESS_URL` must be an `https://` URL: a LAN IP, or the public coder name in\npublic mode. Never `localhost` and never `127.0.0.1`."],[181,"`coder_agent_url` exists so agents keep dialing the local plain listener when\n`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is a public name."],[370,"`coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count`. `coder/preview` warns\n`Unexpanded count argument on block coder_app.*` when they are: that value is\nnot expanded at workspace-create preview. Scripts, metadata and the container\nstay gated."],[182,"`CODER_CONTAINER_SERVICE` (default `coder`), `CODER_DB_SERVICE` (default `db`),\n`CODER_CONTAINER_API_URL` (default `http://127.0.0.1:3000`), `CODER_PUSH_USER`,\n`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, `DOCKER_BIN`, `PROBE_TIMEOUT` (20s) and `VERIFY_TIMEOUT`\n(60s) are the overrides."],[1048,"`CODER_EXPERIMENTS` in `.env` (empty by default since 2026-10-08) has never gated\nanything about it: the old `agents` default was not a recognized experiment name,\nand the server logged `ignoring unknown experiment` and ignored it on every boot."],[471,"`CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_REGEX` is only for telling providers or hosts apart;\nomitted, it matches every `github.com` remote."],[364,"`coder_metadata.workspace_info` shows the image, the cloned repository (in `workspace`,\nor `none` when the Git repository field is empty), the LiteLLM URL, the default Grok\nmodel, whether DinD is `on (tcp://dind:2375)` or `off`, and - redacted - which key is\nwired in. A misconfigured workspace is therefore visible in the UI without shell\naccess. `devcontainer` adds a `devcontainer folder` tile and always reads\n`on (tcp://dind:2375)` for Docker-in-docker."],[272,"`coder_script.dind_bootstrap` is an ordering guard, not a starter: it waits up to 120s\n(180s cap) for the sidecar to answer `docker version`, because the agent runs its\n`run_on_start` scripts before it creates the dev containers."],[1159,"`coder` / `coder-fast` - the Qwen2.5-Coder weights on the Arc. Chat is good, but\nthose weights write the tool call into the message body as plain text, so agent\nturns silently lose their tools. See\nlitellm-routing.md."],[1878,"`command -v grok` answering `~/.grok/bin/grok` is the normal answer, not a leftover\ninstaller copy: the workspace `PATH` lists `~/.grok/bin` before `/usr/local/bin`, and\nthat entry is a symlink into `~/.grok/downloads/`, which holds one file per downloaded\nrelease and is where `grok update` installs - so moving it aside does not outlast the\nnext self-update. Compare `command -v grok` and both `--version` outputs\n(`~/.grok/bin/grok --version`, `/usr/local/bin/grok --version`) against `GROK_VERSION`\nin `images/dev/tool-versions.env`: the profile copy follows `grok update`, the image\ncopy follows the pin. Neither copy gates the three seeded commands, and the two\ndisagreeing is never on\nits own a reason to rebuild the image."],[299,"`connection_timeout = 300`."],[1009,"`context7_mcp` (default `true`, parked the same way with `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`) adds\nthe cached `context7` stdio MCP server. Grok launches\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/mcp-cache-proxy` with\n`--config /home/coder/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json`, and that proxy fronts the\nofficial `/opt/npm-global/bin/context7-mcp`; both are pre-installed in the golden\nimage, so nothing is fetched with `npx` at launch. The proxy config names\n`$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` and nothing else: the key reaches the workspace environment from\nthe sensitive template variable `context7_api_key`, filled by the push from\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in the host `.env` - not from Coder External Auth, which is what\nfills `GH_TOKEN` above."],[1692,"`Could not resolve host: github.com` -> Tailscale MagicDNS is the only nameserver"],[893,"`custom.css` lives in `/app/config` and is served by the allow-listed\n`/api/config/custom.css` route. It styles the sign-in page too, so keep internal\nhostnames out of it."],[649,"`description` is printed as the lead paragraph under the title and indexed for search. Quote\nit when it contains a colon, or YAML reads the line as a mapping."],[1603,"`devcontainer.json` — a file the *application repository* carries, and the one thing the\n`devcontainer` template builds. `coder_devcontainer.repo` in\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf` sets no `config_path`, so `@devcontainers/cli` on the\nDinD sidecar searches the clone for `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` (or a\n`devcontainer.json` anywhere inside it). This repo ships a `.devcontainer/` of its own —\nthe **contributor** one for a laptop and CI, built from `images/dev/Dockerfile` — and it\nis not that nested kind: `.devcontainer/contributor-env` alongside it makes\n`probe_repo` answer a miss, so a Coder workspace of this repo stays on the `workspace`\ntemplate instead of building its own container inside its own DinD sidecar. Other\nrepositories keep carrying their own nested dev-container files, and those are still a\nprobe hit. See contribute/devcontainer.md."],[1602,"`devcontainer` — the dev-container template (directory `templates/devcontainer`, Coder\ntemplate name `devcontainer`), always backed by its own DinD sidecar. Its sibling is\n`workspace`: directory `templates/workspace`, template name `workspace`, the plain\ndev container with the optional `dind` parameter."],[98,"`devcontainer` (`templates/devcontainer/`) is the DinD-backed one."],[1088,"`devcontainer` and `workspace` both seed it, so the seed is *not* fetched from\nGitHub any more: no `web_search`/`web_fetch` call is needed to inspect this repo,\nand the instruction text tells the agent so."],[206,"`devcontainer` asks two questions instead: **Git repository** and **Build repo's dev\ncontainer** (`use_devcontainer`, Yes/No, immutable), plus the same three Grok fields."],[100,"`devcontainer` reuses `workspace`'s variables. `repo_url` exists in both; `dind` does\nnot exist in `devcontainer`."],[115,"`devcontainer`: **Devcontainer default** (the template default) and **Plain clone**."],[1716,"`did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` -> plan-time failure, not runtime"],[254,"`dind` is not a variable here. A devcontainer workspace without a daemon is nothing,\nand the plain-clone fallback still wants a daemon its `docker` commands can reach."],[775,"`disallow_any_explicit` refuses `Any` typed into a signature by a person.\n`disallow_any_generics` refuses `list` and `dict` written without their element\ntype, which are the same hole reached by a different spelling. The binary is the\n`MYPY_VERSION` pin from `images/dev/tool-versions.env`, baked into the golden image\nby `images/dev/Dockerfile`, so the gate needs no network and no `pip install`."],[977,"`display_apps.web_terminal` stays **off**: it would open a shell on the agent's\nworking dir (`/home/coder`). A custom command-type `coder_app` named **Web\nTerminal** (`order = 6`, after **VS Code Web** at `order = 5`, before **GitHub\nreauth** at `order = 7` and **Install GitHub App** at `order = 8`) takes that slot,\n`cd`s to the clone folder - an empty `repo_url` keeps it on `/home/coder` - and\nexecs a login shell. Like the built-in, it reads no `terminal.integrated.*`\nsettings."],[357,"`display_apps` gives: the **VS Code Desktop** helper (a locally installed VS Code /\nCursor / Windsurf plus the `coder.coder-remote` extension), port forwarding, and the\nSSH helper."],[240,"`docker build`, `docker run` and `docker compose` then work inside the workspace. The\n*AI workspace + DinD* preset is the usual way to get this shape, since the client\nbinaries ship only in the golden image."],[250,"`DOCKER_CLI_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and the `docker:29.8.1-dind` tag\nin `templates/workspace/main.tf` and `templates/devcontainer/main.tf` are the same\nengine line (29.8.1). Bump all three together."],[1893,"`DOCKER_HOST` is injected per template, not per toggle. `templates/workspace` sets\nit only while the toggle is on; `templates/devcontainer` sets it always, because\nthat template always provisions the sidecar and the `devcontainer` CLI has to\nreach its daemon either way. So a set `DOCKER_HOST` tells you the template; the\n*docker-in-docker* metadata tile tells you the state (the devcontainer template\nalways reads `on (tcp://dind:2375)`, the workspace one reads `off` when unset)."],[239,"`DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` is injected into the agent environment - absent when the\ntoggle is off."],[245,"`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` is deliberately **empty**, so the daemon serves plain\n`tcp://0.0.0.0:2375` with no TLS and no auth. (`DOCKER_TLS_CERTSIZE=0` does not do\nthis: the daemon would listen on 2376 with TLS and the client fails against 2375.)"],[1711,"`docker` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> an older image, or the wrong preset"],[654,"`docs-site/lib/remark-page-brief.ts` recognises that four-node run and rewrites it into one\n`<PageBrief>` — the two-column card with the list and tick icons. The match is exact: the label\nhas to be the whole paragraph, bold, and spelled `What's on this page` and `What this enables`;\nthe lists have to be lists; and no heading may sit between the title and the pair. Get any of\nthat wrong and the page renders the lists as plain bullets, which is the only symptom."],[780,"`docs-site/tsconfig.json` starts at `strict: true` and adds the switches that find\nreal defects at no churn cost: `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, `noImplicitOverride`,\n`noImplicitReturns`, `noFallthroughCasesInSwitch`, `noUnusedLocals`, and\n`noUnusedParameters`. Each carries a comment naming the class of bug it catches."],[664,"`docs/manage-docs.sh` is the same set as a single operator CLI (`serve`, `build`, `preview`,\n`verify`, `typecheck`, `test`, `nav`, `status`, `clean`) for a host that has Node but not Bazel.\nThe gates run through Bazel; see Testing with Bazel for how the npm-backed\ntargets are wired and why they stay out of `//:test-fast`."],[446,"`env` reconciles one env file with its twin. It keeps every value you have set,\nfills a key the twin adds with the twin's default (or a reusable value already\nset in a sibling live file - `.env` filling `dev-stack.env`), and moves a key\nthe twin no longer declares into a commented trailer at the end of the file -\nparked, with its value and its explanation still readable, rather than deleted.\nComment lines always come from the twin, even when every value stays as you\nset it, so a pull that only rewrote an explanation still lands in `.env`. The\nrewrite lands in a staging file and replaces the live file only at the end,\nwith mode `600` kept. On a TTY the pass then walks every key that is new this\npull or still a placeholder (`change-me…`, `NOTSET`, `YOUR_LAN_IP`). An empty\nvalue whose example default is also empty is the intended value (LAN-only\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`, a parked MCP flag) and is not asked again. Enter keeps a\nsibling's value or the example default. A missing live file is created from\nthe twin only after a `Y/n` confirm. `--non-interactive` (and `--dry-run`)\nlist those keys and write no answers. A `summary:` line closes the pass."],[1691,"`error: .../.env not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` -> the runfiles copy has no working `.env`"],[1720,"`Error: No configuration files` from `push-template` -> runfiles symlinks in the tar"],[108,"`ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and `ez-create-skill.SKILL.md` are the\nstack's three skill commands: `/ez-learn` writes memory files, reproducing the upstream\nmemory family `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`; `/ez-skillify` captures the\nsession that just worked into a skill; `/ez-create-skill` scaffolds one from what the\noperator describes. Skill authoring is those last two - `/ez-learn` stores facts, not\nprocedures. They ride `coder_script.stack_skills` rather than the startup argument (see\n\"The one-argument ceiling\"), which writes each absent skill into\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and twin-copies it to `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`,\neach only while absent. Upstream there is no `/learn` and no `/skillify` to shadow: no\npublished source names either one. `/create-skill` is real, but only as a *bundled*\nplatform skill whose catalog is fetched per identity/team behind an x.ai sign-in this\nstack does not have; and the memory family `/ez-learn` copies is no skill at all but the\nCLI's own `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` registry entries plus the pager's `/remember`,\nwith the registry three gated on a memory store, which is off by default and never\nconfigured here. So these payloads are what make the three commands exist, and the `ez-`\nprefix is what keeps them clear of those upstream names."],[1309,"`fallbacks:` carries the degrade path `agent: [\"coder\"]`, repeated for each thinking\nalias, because those groups go down with exactly the same boxes."],[1690,"`FileAccessException` / `Permission denied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` -> leftover runfiles bind-mount"],[22,"`fix/…` means a defect that is already on `main`. **A hotfix branches from `main` and lands\non `main` first** — every environment deploys *from* `main` (or from a tag cut on `main`),\nso there is no `staging` or `production` branch to patch and then remember to merge back."],[111,"`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl` is the shared GitHub-token refresh `coder_script`,\nrendered with `templatefile()` so `${github_auth_id}` and\n`${coder_github_app_install_url}` interpolate."],[1737,"`gh: command not found` inside a workspace -> the helper needs gh"],[1736,"`gh` answers `Bad credentials` -> the stale env pair, not the store"],[2077,"`git cherry` marks a commit `-` when an equivalent patch is already upstream. A branch that\nis `ahead=0` behind trunk is done, and its row reads \"delete\" without further argument."],[407,"`GIT_ASKPASS`, when no helper answers. Where the staged binary is alive it mints on\ndemand; where it answers `unrecognized subcommand` git gets no answer at all and falls\nthrough to prompting, which is where `could not read Username` comes from."],[1992,"`git` needs no prefix once `fix` has run, because the helper it installs clears the pair\nitself:"],[1008,"`github_mcp` (default `true`, so the block renders unless you park it with\n`GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push) adds the `github` HTTP MCP server\n(`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`), whose `Authorization` header references\n`${GH_TOKEN}` **by name**, so no token value is ever part of a template variable.\nThe agent environment fills it from the Coder External Auth provider (id `github` by default); a\nworkspace whose owner connected no GitHub account renders the block anyway and pays\none failed handshake per session."],[176,"`GITHUB_MCP`, `CONTEXT7_MCP` and `SEARCH_MCP` share one polarity: all three MCP blocks\nship from the template default (`true`), so the only value worth setting is `false`,\nand `true`/unset/empty pushes nothing at all."],[990,"`grok doctor` checks terminal, clipboard and microphone support without starting a\nsession, and `/doctor` inside a session adds the checks that need a live one. Each\nitem in the **Findings** list carries its own fix, and `--json` prints the same\nreport for a script. What the report should read here:"],[942,"`grok doctor` findings in a workspace"],[1710,"`grok doctor` flags `voice.no-input-device` or `terminal.limited-color` -> a headless container, and where you ran the command"],[1836,"`grok mcp doctor searxng` inside the workspace probes the MCP hop. A healthy\nsearch-check plus a failed handshake points at the server, the URL or the port."],[103,"`GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` is the orchestration-protocol seed text the startup scripts\ninline into every workspace's `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` (read with `file()`, chomped)."],[1010,"`grok_config_extra` appends whatever the create form's **Grok config extras** field\nholds, verbatim under its own comment header."],[345,"`grok_config` arrives already rendered from `grok-config.toml.tftpl`. Its boolean keys\n`github_mcp`, `search_mcp` and `context7_mcp` only decide whether their\n`[mcp_servers.*]` block appears, and no credential byte is part of that decision;\n`search_url` supplies the URL the searxng block dials, and the workspace affinity key is\na per-workspace routing hint (a workspace UUID, not a secret)."],[318,"`GROK_FRESH_INSTALL` records that an install *completed* this boot - the second\n`command -v` probe inside the install branch is what sets it, so a failed download leaves\nthe flag at `0`. The installer writes its own `~/.grok/config.toml` (a\n`[cli] installer = ...` stub) that is indistinguishable from a user's config, so the\nconfig stage may treat that stub as stack-owned only on a completed install: a download\nthat failed must never wipe a user-owned config in its place. Without that flag a\nbrand-new workspace would be left with an unconfigured CLI, because the stub carries\nneither stack marker and the file already exists."],[479,"`GROK_PROFILE_MODE` in the twin is the default for the template variable\n`grok_profile_mode`, which the create form also shows per workspace as the **Grok\nprofile** dropdown. Read once per push, so changing the default here needs\n`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`. Overriding it for one workspace happens on\nthat dropdown and needs a rebuild, like `dind`."],[107,"`grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md` and `coder-agents.md` are the Coder Agents\nskill and instruction seeds (chomped `file()`)."],[384,"`grok-build-terminal` keeps its own session, `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`, keyed off\nthe repository root found with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or the terminal's own\n`$PWD` outside a clone) and runs `grok --cwd <that root>` inside it. It is the\nalways-reattach case on purpose: every tab of the clone watches the one agent run."],[1708,"`grok` -> `command not found` inside a workspace -> the CLI was never installed"],[1178,"`group_add: ${DOCKER_GID}` plus the socket is the same credential the `coder` service\nalready holds: root-equivalent on the host. Nothing new is exposed here that the Coder\ncontainer does not already have - which is precisely why approvals, below, are the\ncontrol rather than the mount list. A public Hermes name has that same standing as\npublic Coder."],[1962,"`HANDOFF` means there is no credential anywhere in reach: click **GitHub reauth**,\nfinish device-code login, install the Coder GitHub App if the reauth page says to, and\nrestart. Those clicks are in\nthe reauth entry,\nand the script's own note on a workspace with no connected account points at the same\ntwo buttons."],[2015,"`hermes config set` on `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is the in-container\nalternative. `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` does not need that reseed."],[1196,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` does not remove that requirement. Leave\n`approvals.mode` on `manual`."],[1151,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a deprecated no-op. Setting it changes nothing and buys\nnothing, and it is not how the public name is opened."],[2001,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_INSECURE` is a no-op. Logs:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs hermes`."],[2013,"`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` has to be the public origin. Bootstrap writes\n`STACK_HERMES_URL` from a `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry, and compose copies\nthat into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`. The variable is environment: recreate\nthe container after bootstrap."],[1179,"`HERMES_UID` / `HERMES_GID` are set to the host operator, so a file Hermes writes into\n`$STACK_HOST_PATH` is yours to commit and not owned by uid 10000."],[1143,"`hermes-shell -- check` answers \"can I open the assistant\". It cannot answer \"is this\nstill the assistant this repo describes\", and the two come apart on a long-lived box:\nan operator edits the config inside the volume, a recreate reads a stale `.env`, a\nmerge changes the seed and a seed-only-when-absent copy never takes it. `hermes-doctor`\nanswers the second question. It is read-only and prints key names with no value."],[1176,"`hermes/skills/` is tracked in git. Add a skill by editing the repo, never by editing\nthe volume; the volume copy is not the record."],[1939,"`history-limit` reads 100000 but a pane still forgets old output: tmux applies\nthe option when the pane is created, so that pane keeps the limit it was born\nwith. Open a new pane, or restart the server with `tmux kill-server`."],[892,"`homepage/config/*.yaml` plus `custom.css` are bind-mounted read-only, so `git diff`\nstays the record of dashboard changes, and the directory itself has to stay writable\nbecause the app creates `logs/` inside it. A new config file (`kubernetes.yaml`, ...)\nneeds its own mount line in `docker-compose.yml` before the app sees it."],[297,"`host.docker.internal` mapped to `host-gateway`, so workspaces reach the Coder server\nand LiteLLM."],[889,"`https://<host>/` serves the dashboard behind a self-signed certificate that\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh` issues, so expect a one-time browser warning. Port 80 redirects\nto HTTPS."],[1680,"`https://gmktecbeast.overeazy.io/`, `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` 302 to\n`https://authelia.overeazy.io/`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and Hermes are interactive\nsessions, and the Hermes policy is already in place. Covering a newly named host\nis an Authelia-side config choice; nothing here adds or requires a policy. Hermes\nis not an API bypass: it stays behind that interactive policy, and after Authelia\nit asks for its own HTTP basic auth. Both prompts on the public URL are expected."],[573,"`images/dev/tool-versions.env` is the single source of truth for every tool version:\nBazelisk, buildifier, shfmt, shellcheck (apt), bats, Terraform/tflint/terragrunt, the\nCoder CLI, the Docker *client* trio, Node, Python, Go, protoc and the pinned Grok CLI."],[1791,"`info: prompt cache: 80693 tokens, resumed at 79747, kept 1; 12 states, 6289 MiB and\n0 MiB spare of 6465 MiB (hits ..., evicted ...)`: the box is out of prompt-cache room.\n`resumed at N` is how much of the prompt it did not have to recompute, so a healthy\nconversation logs a number just under `tokens`. `resumed at 0` means it recomputed the\nwhole prompt: something evicted that conversation's state. Watch `evicted` and the\nstate count rather than any one line."],[1433,"`install` installs the two packages, writes the config, regenerates the\ninitramfs, and then runs the same check over what it produced. Add `--dry-run`\nfirst and every write and command is printed as `would: ...` instead of\nexecuted. Nothing reboots."],[1724,"`installed terraform version newer than expected` -> harmless"],[901,"`KASM_UI_PORT` has to match on **both** sides of the port mapping. The daemon-side\nlistener listens on that number, so the mapping is `4443:4443`."],[1354,"`least-busy` picks the healthy deployment carrying the fewest in-flight requests."],[1718,"`lookup registry.terraform.io on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving` from `push-template` -> coder cannot reach the Terraform registry"],[1877,"`ls ~/.grok/bundled/skills` printing nothing is the expected answer, not a symptom:\nwithout the sign-in there is no bundled cache at all, on any version. Because a\nuser-scope skill overrides a same-named bundled one, and the built-in memory names are\nthe CLI's, this stack's seeds deliberately carry `ez-` names; the boot and the reseed\nretire a marker-carrying `skills/learn/SKILL.md` that predates the rename rather\nthan relying on you to delete it, since deleting a seeded copy would not retire the\nold command name anyway - the boot and the reseed write it again. What works: keep a\nmarker-free local copy only if you also stop the stack from seeding it, or disable\nthe lane with `disabled = [\"<name>\"]` under `[skills]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`."],[261,"`main.tf` therefore spells the editor's `folder` per hosting environment\n(`local.editor_folder`): `/workspaces/<repo-name>` inside the dev container,\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>` on the plain container, and\n`/home/coder` when no repository was named."],[19,"`main` is the only long-lived branch. It stays green and deployable: `main` is what a\nmachine is built from, so a merge to `main` is by definition deployable to the stack host."],[533,"`make test`/`bats`/`lint`/`fix`/`validate` and the `stack-*`/`prod-stack-*` shims are the\nsame commands;\n`make help` lists them. `//:test-fast` does not start Compose, pull models, or SSH to a Spark\nbox: the suites exercise those scripts against `tests/bats/tool_stubs`. `bazelisk run\n//scripts:<stem> -- <args>` is how a human runs one of them for real."],[1593,"`make` only delegates. An operator CLI runs as `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`\n(`<stem>` = the file name under `scripts/`); `make help` lists the shims."],[110,"`mcp-cache-proxy.json` is the context7 MCP cache-proxy blob the startup script\nwrites, into `~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json` and only while that file is absent\n(so a shared profile volume reuses one SQLite cache across that owner's workspaces). It\nnames no secret: the empty `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` value is what tells `mcp-cache-proxy` to\ninherit `process.env`."],[1313,"`model_group_retry_policy:` gives the four agent lanes their own `RetryPolicy`. The\nreason it exists is narrow enough to be worth its own section below."],[1307,"`model_list:` is the alias list; `router_settings:` is the routing."],[1339,"`model:` is sent to the backend verbatim, so `Qwen3.8-Flash-Next` has to match the\nserved id byte-for-byte, case and hyphens included."],[774,"`mypy.ini` at the repo root is the whole policy. It is `--strict` written out option\nby option, plus the two that make this a rule rather than a preference:"],[1969,"`NO GitHub credential in the <slot> slot (git will prompt and be refused)` means there\nis nothing to send, so the account comes first:\nthe reauth entry."],[1977,"`no useful answer`: github.com gave nothing useful (offline? probe again). A 200 that\ndoes not advertise refs is this verdict too: a push is not proven."],[1689,"`not a directory: Are you trying to mount a directory onto a file` -> missing compose bind-mount source"],[1975,"`NOT VISIBLE` (404): a private repo hidden from it answers 404, and so does an absent\none; clone and push would both fail."],[311,"`notes_materialize` runs *before* the recursive chown of a fresh clone: the notes file\nand the `.git/info/exclude` write are the stack's to make, and the chown afterwards\nsettles their ownership."],[105,"`notes-setup.sh` is the library behind each clone's private `notes.md`. The\nstartup script inlines it with `file()` the same way, writes it into\n`~/.grok/`, sources it, and calls `notes_materialize` per clone. That call also\nseeds the ignore entries that keep the file uncommitted; see \"What is shared\" in\n`docs/grok-build-and-agents.md` for what it writes into git config.\nOnce every clone has been through it the same script calls `run_notes_watch`, which\nreaches `notes_sync_start` on the home owner's uid and leaves the container with one\nbackground watcher (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL` seconds, 30 by default; one instance per\ncontainer behind a `flock` on `$HOME/.notes-sync.lock`) copying each clone's notes back\nto the owner's profile volume between boots. The workspace twin watches\n`$HOME/workspace`; the devcontainer twin passes its `$dc_dir`, because those clones sit\non the `/srv/coder-devcontainers` bind."],[1020,"`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/notes.md` is keyed per git remote, so every clone a\nworkspace holds — the primary and each additional repository — has its own file. What you\nedit is the regular file at the clone root: that is what the editor, the terminal and a\nnested dev container all see, since the inner container mounts the clone bind and has\nits own `~/.grok`. The copy runs both ways, and not only at boot. Startup copies the live\nfile here and restores it when a recreate leaves the clone with nothing or with only the\nseeded stub line, and then leaves **one background watcher per container** that repeats\nthe back-copy about every 30 seconds (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`, in seconds) over every clone\nunder `$HOME/workspace`; the devcontainer template watches its `/srv/coder-devcontainers`\nbind root instead, since those clones sit there rather than under the workspace home. The\nwatcher is single-instance behind a non-blocking `flock` on `$HOME/.notes-sync.lock`, in\nthe container's own home rather than the shared volume, because each of the owner's\nworkspaces needs its own."],[1312,"`num_retries: 2` covers a transient blip; moving off a busy box is routing's job, not\nthe retry chain's. `request_timeout: 1200` is now the tightest deadline on the chain -\nthe CLI's streaming idle allowance (`inference_idle_timeout_secs`) sits at 1800 s - so\nthe proxy is the end that gives up first, at a full twenty minutes per attempt."],[1401,"`OLLAMA_NUM_GPU=999` offloads every layer to the 140T, and the iGPU pulls its weights\nstraight out of the 96 GB DDR5."],[1667,"`passHostHeader: true` is the line that makes this work at all. Without it Traefik\nsends the upstream URL's host, no `server_name` matches, and this box answers `421`."],[1712,"`Permission denied` / `jq: error: Could not open file` on `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` -> empty named volume is root-owned"],[1719,"`pq: value too long for type character varying(256)` from `push-template` -> editor icon data URL"],[330,"`private` mounts nothing extra: the tree then lives on this workspace's own home volume,\nso its `config.toml`, defaults and extras included, is its own and dies with it."],[481,"`private`: no nested mount. Each workspace gets a private `~/.grok` on its own\nhome volume, so its `config.toml` - plus memory, skills and logs - differs per\nworkspace and dies with the workspace."],[1016,"`private`: that workspace's `~/.grok` - `config.toml` included - lives on its own\nhome volume, differs from its siblings, and dies with the workspace (or survives a\nrebuild on the same volume)."],[1558,"`prod-stack` addresses the stable project by name from any directory; `dev-stack` does the\nsame for the dev one. `update-configs` is what a `git pull` cannot do for you, because the\nfiles that go stale are not tracked — see\nconfig-updates.md."],[608,"`push would FAIL` from the check above means one command:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git`.\nIt mints a live token, writes the store, installs that helper, and re-proves the verdict.\nFor `gh` itself, prefix `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN`: no process can rewrite another\nprocess's environment."],[1971,"`push would WORK (the credential git consults writes <repo>)` and `push would FAIL` are\nthe lines to gate on, keyed to the credential git would really send; `check` exits\nnon-zero on FAIL so a script or an agent can refuse to push on it."],[1973,"`PUSH-READY` (200 advertising refs): a push would negotiate. If the push still fails,\nthe credential is not the failing thing."],[231,"`push-template.sh` re-sends both after every push, and `new-workspace.sh` names no\n`--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline either."],[472,"`push-template.sh` reads `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` from `coder.env` and\nforwards it as `--var github_auth_id=` so the templates declare the same id.\nChanging the id orphans existing GitHub account links."],[1404,"`qwen2.5-coder:32b` is treated here as a Mixture-of-Experts model: 32B-class quality\nwith far fewer active parameters per token."],[1346,"`qwen2.5:7b` on the same runtime does return a real `tool_calls` array."],[858,"`real_bin <tool>` resolves a tool with the stub directory removed from PATH. That is the\nintegration-tier gate: `if [ -z \"$(real_bin terraform)\" ]; then skip \"terraform not\ninstalled\"; fi` — a test that could talk to a real daemon skips when the tool is absent\ninstead of failing."],[1974,"`REFUSED-GRANT` (403): that credential authenticates and every write to the repo is\ndenied, which is the push 403."],[1739,"`Refusing to bind dashboard to 0.0.0.0` -> empty Hermes basic-auth env"],[1976,"`REJECTED` (401): github.com refused the credential itself, as it refuses an expired or\nrevoked token - the fate of the injected pair about an hour after start. `fix` re-mints."],[1721,"`REPO_ROOT: unbound variable` from `push-template` -> the size guard read an ambient root"],[848,"`REPO_ROOT` — where the checkout is *for this run*. `tests/bats_runner.sh` exports it;\noutside Bazel it is whatever `bats_canonical_repo_root` resolves. Derive it, never\nhardcode a machine path, and treat \"cannot resolve\" as a `skip`, not a failure."],[179,"`repo_url` prefills the Git repository field on both create forms. The Terraform\ndefault is still no clone (`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`).\n`.env.sample` sets `REPO_URL` to this stack's GitHub URL, so a normal push prefills\n`https://github.com/toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack.git` and **VS Code Web** opens\n`~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. A blank `REPO_URL=` line stays unpassed."],[1315,"`RetryPolicy` in that tag has no `APIConnectionErrorRetries` field. `DefaultRetries` is\nthe only field that can reach the error, which is why each agent lane declares one:"],[1308,"`routing_strategy: least-busy` is the picker;\n`optional_pre_call_checks: [\"session_affinity\"]` is the pinning layer above it."],[540,"`rules_shell` only symlinks an `sh_test` or `sh_binary` source when that file is executable.\n`//tools:exec_shell.bzl` writes a generated launcher and runs the real `.sh` from runfiles\nwith bash, so a checkout whose scripts are mode `100644` still builds."],[313,"`run_notes` and `run_notes_watch` follow the clone's identity rule rather than the\nagent's uid, because that stage writes into the workspace home and runs\n`git config --global`: run as root it would rename a root-owned `~/.gitconfig` over the\nworkspace user's and leave `notes.md` unreadable on an already-cloned folder."],[1775,"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in its logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."],[1403,"`runners=[ipex_llm]` in the ollama logs is that GPU backend confirming itself."],[719,"`scope` derives the test list from what this checkout changes, which is the loop that replaces\nre-running the whole fast gate after every edit:"],[539,"`scripts/BUILD.bazel` declares one `exec_shell` launcher plus one `sh_binary` wrapper per\n`scripts/*.sh`, generated from the directory, so a new CLI is a Bazel target without a second\nedit there. `//:validate` is an alias for `//scripts:validate`; `//:fix` keeps the same\nlauncher pattern at the repo root."],[1350,"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, for the same reason: an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or\nedit, so registering `coder` or `coder-fast` would only add a visibly broken model to\nthe pickers. The four Spark aliases all drive tools, so all four are registerable - the\nthinking aliases are there to make the level selectable from that picker."],[1398,"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the Spark lane is dark),\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, and `chat` as the Coder Agents chat model\nconfigs, through `CODER_AGENTS_PROVIDER_URL`\n(default `http://litellm:4000/v1`)."],[1647,"`scripts/lib/domain.sh` owns the grammar and every part is normalized before it is\njoined, so a value copied out of a browser bar cannot leak into an origin or a\n`server_name`:"],[785,"`scripts/lib/tf_types.awk` is run by `//:validate` over both template twins and\nreports three findings: `missing-type`, `broad-any`, and `bare-container` (a `list`,\n`set`, or `map` with no element type, which is `any` wearing a different hat). Only\nthe `type` attribute of a top-level `variable` block is read, so a `type` key inside\na `default` body is never mistaken for the constraint, and `any` is matched as a word\nso `company_mcp` is not a finding."],[588,"`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh` materializes, into a laptop or CI home, the same stack-owned\nfiles a Coder workspace writes at startup — from the same template payloads, with no\nTerraform and no docker:"],[523,"`scripts/setup-host.sh` is the only script run straight from a shell, and it is the\n**stack host's** bootstrap: the box that runs Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and\nHermes, where Compose and Bazel have to live on the host itself. It installs what Bazel\nneeds (Bazelisk, shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier, Terraform), so it necessarily runs before\nBazel exists — that is its whole exemption, not a convenience:"],[1028,"`searxng` (SearXNG metasearch, host port 8181) is the search backend the stack runs\nfor itself: keyless engines only, no key, no account, no request logging. A\nworkspace reaches it through the host gateway, so the workspace networks stay\nuntouched."],[1809,"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is 0, not 2, on each of the four Spark lanes (`agent`,\n`agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`): one attempt for a dead stream, so a hung\nrequest is bounded at 1200 s instead of up to 3465 s. `DefaultRetries: 2` is untouched\non purpose - TensorFold's `NoRoom` refusal arrives as `APIConnectionError`, which is\nabsent from `_RETRIES_BY_EXCEPTION_TYPE`, so that walk falls through to\n`DefaultRetries` and keeps its two attempts. The proxy reads the file at boot:"],[1814,"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is the only one of the two that can be zeroed; zeroing\n`DefaultRetries` breaks the `NoRoom` contract above."],[1359,"`session_affinity` sits above the picker, in `router_settings.optional_pre_call_checks`,\nand it arms the router's `DeploymentAffinityCheck`. Checked on LiteLLM 1.102.1."],[791,"`set -euo pipefail` is the floor, so an unset parameter is a failure rather than\nan empty string."],[373,"`settings.json.tftpl` is copied once, the first time `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`\nis missing, onto the per-owner volume `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`. Startup chowns\nthat volume (and the shared `~/.grok` profile volume) to the home owner before\nthe write: an empty Docker named volume is `root:root` 755, and the agent runs\nas `coder`. A failed write logs a warning instead of running `jq`/`python`\nagainst a file that was never created."],[1015,"`shared` (the default here, so a workspace gets the profile unless you turn the\nmode off): configure the CLI once and the rest of that account's workspaces\ninherit it."],[480,"`shared` (the default): every workspace of a user mounts that user's shared\ngrok-profile volume nested inside `/home/coder`, so `config.toml`, the MCP warm\ncaches, `memory-v2`, skills and a self-installed CLI are one profile shared by\nthat account's workspaces. It is re-rendered on every start and the\n**last-started workspace wins** for all of them."],[184,"`SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` skips the step."],[1193,"`SOUL.md` is the identity - voice and stance, a short persona. It is not a fork of\nAGENTS.md: the repo's shell conventions stay in that file, where the\nwriters and the linters read them."],[495,"`SPARK_n_API_KEY` is the bearer token for each endpoint. TensorFold is\nvLLM-lineage, so treat it like vLLM/SGLang: any non-empty string is accepted\nunless the server was started with `--api-key`, in which case set the REAL key\nper box. Empty is not a working value - LiteLLM v1.102.1 fails every request at\nclient construction (`AuthenticationError`, before reaching the box) and `agent`\nfalls back to `coder`. The placeholder `local-spark` is not a secret and is\nignored by a keyless backend."],[496,"`SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` (kept commented in the twin) feed the dashboard's per-box\nSpark cards - one widget per box, so no card assumes one \"usual\" box."],[493,"`SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` are the Ollama endpoints behind the `spark-<n>` aliases. The\nplaceholder hostnames are on purpose: replace them with real Ollama URLs or\nleave them as-is, and those aliases simply fail when used."],[494,"`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` are the OpenAI-compatible `/v1` endpoints. A workspace pins\nits session to one healthy box; fresh session-id-less traffic fans out by\n`least-busy`, shortest in-flight queue first."],[1649,"`STACK_CODER_NAME` / `STACK_KASM_NAME` / `STACK_LITELLM_NAME` / `STACK_HERMES_NAME`\n(defaults `coder` / `kasm` / `litellm` / `hermes`) are the service prefixes.\n`STACK_DASHBOARD_NAME` is empty by default, which puts the dashboard on the zone apex."],[1659,"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL`,\nthe card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills `STACK_HERMES_URL`.\nCompose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the Host and Origin\nthe Chat tab checks;"],[1658,"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"],[1177,"`STACK_DEPLOY_PATH` (default `/opt/stack-deploy`) is the **deploy workspace**: the one\ndirectory meant for clones, builds, and the compose project of a third-party project\nthe assistant brings up. It is mounted at its own absolute path for the same reason\n`STACK_HOST_PATH` is - a bind source inside a project file the assistant writes has to\nresolve on the host daemon - and bootstrap fills it in without overwriting a path you\nset yourself. It is deliberately outside `/home`, so giving the assistant somewhere to\nwork did not mean giving it your dotfiles. An existing container gains the mount on\nrecreate, since `restart` keeps the old mounts."],[1648,"`STACK_DOMAIN_BASE` (default `overeazy.io`) is the zone the edge terminates TLS for;\n`STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX` (default `gmktecbeast`) is this box's label, shared by every\nname."],[1650,"`STACK_ENV_LABEL` is empty for the stable stack and sits **after** the service prefix.\nTwo reasons: the one-label rule below rules the dotted form out anyway, and\n`classify_public_entry` in `scripts/lib/bootstrap.sh` routes an entry by its service\nprefix, so `coder-dev-box.example.com` is Coder while `dev-coder-box.example.com`\nfalls through to the dashboard."],[1174,"`STACK_HOST_PATH` is the checkout's `pwd -P`, or, from a subdirectory or a linked\nworktree, its git top-level. Compose bind-mounts that host path at\nthe same path inside the container, so `docker compose -f $STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml`\ntalking through the socket names bind sources the daemon can find. Empty (CI\n`compose config`) falls back to `.:/opt/stack`. `bootstrap` refreshes the key only\nafter checking the path holds this `docker-compose.yml`: compose mounts whatever the\nkey names, so a stale value leaves the assistant working an empty directory while\nevery container-level check still reads healthy."],[1663,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, which records that the names are derived. Under `auto`\nthe parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output only, so a list\nedited by hand there is ignored and bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear. It clears itself on the LAN-only and explicit-list paths;"],[2010,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` does not create the vhost. It is what bootstrap writes\ninto `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` for the Chat tab.\n`bootstrap` now also warns when a name in that list is missing from the\nconf it mounts."],[1652,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` has three states, and only the middle one composes:"],[484,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is the one switch, and it has three states. Empty keeps everything\nbelow LAN-only and every derived `STACK_*_URL` stays empty. `auto` composes the five\nnames from the naming parts - `STACK_DOMAIN_BASE`, `STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX`,\n`STACK_ENV_LABEL` and the per-service `STACK_*_NAME` prefixes, all documented in\n`.env.sample`. A comma-separated list is the hand-pin: those names are used verbatim."],[109,"`stack-skills.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_skills\"` body that seeds that trio,\nrendered with `templatefile()` from the three `chomp(file())` entries `main.tf` passes it.\nIt is absent-only and silent on a healthy workspace, because its log is what an operator\nreads when a skill is missing."],[298,"`startup_script_behavior = \"blocking\"`, so a workspace only reports *ready* once the\ntoolchain is genuinely staged - no opening an editor mid-install."],[1859,"`startup_script` is blocking by design, so a failure there surfaces as a **failed**\nbuild with the reason in the workspace startup log. The container is not marked\nhealthy until the toolchain is staged."],[1890,"`startup_script` is blocking, so it finished before the agent reported ready and\nbefore code-server started. Check the workspace agent logs first."],[138,"`startup_size_guard` in `scripts/push-template.sh` enforces this before the CLI runs,\nbeside the icon and preset-description guards. It sums each body's composed bytes and\nrefuses the push past `STARTUP_SIZE_BOUND` (122880, which leaves 8 KiB, about two\ndocumentation-sized commits, under the kernel's own limit). Its reader is\n`scripts/lib/startup_size.awk`; `//tests:bats_startup_script_size_test` re-derives the\nsame totals with an independent parser and pins the two together, so a guard that starts\nmeasuring something else goes red in review."],[266,"`startup.sh.tftpl` clones the repository onto that bind in **both** toggle modes."],[340,"`startup.sh.tftpl` is rendered by `templatefile()`, so every braced shell expansion has\nto be written `$${name}`; the one-dollar braced form is swallowed as an interpolation.\nThe unbraced form (`$name`) needs no escape."],[1567,"`storage-clean` is the janitor for the persistent layer and asks before it deletes; the\ndev-stack `reset` and a Kasm reset both destroy state that has no backup. The cost of each\nis spelled out on the entry in troubleshooting.md — read that line\nfirst."],[852,"`stub_bin <tool…>` copies each stub out of `STUB_BIN_DIR` into `${BATS_TEST_TMPDIR}/bin`\nand `chmod 755`s it; `use_stubs` = `stub_bin` + a fresh `STUB_LOG` + the PATH shadow.\nThe copy-plus-chmod is load-bearing: the Bazel sandbox and some tar extractions strip the\nexec bit, and a per-test copy keeps one test's argv log out of the next test."],[857,"`stub_log <tool>`, `stub_log_last`, `stub_log_count` read back what the stub recorded from\n`$STUB_LOG/<tool>.log`; assert on the argv, not on prose."],[379,"`subdomain = false` everywhere: there is no `CODER_WILDCARD_ACCESS_URL` on this box, so\nthe editor is proxied on the path-based access URL."],[650,"`tags` are indexed, so a reader who searches for the word you filed the page under finds it.\n`pageSchema` strips unknown keys, which is why `tags` is declared explicitly in\n`docs-site/lib/content.ts` — a new field needs the same treatment or it silently never\nreaches the index."],[129,"`templates push` carries no description (`push` has no `--description` flag), so\n`templates edit --description` is the write. That text is the routing hint the Coder\nAgents chat reads when it picks a template to provision."],[102,"`templates/common/` exists once instead of twice, and both templates consume it.\n`template_push_one` stages every file in this list beside the staged `main.tf`; a\nmissing one fails the push before the CLI runs."],[1600,"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload. `templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and\nholds the single-copy payloads both templates consume: `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`,\n`vscode-alt.svg`, `notes-setup.sh`, `tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`,\n`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`, `grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md`, `ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, `stack-skills.sh.tftpl`,\n`coder-agents.md`, `mcp-cache-proxy.json`, and `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`. Markdown\nand JSON are read with `file()` (chomp the markdown); shell helpers that Terraform must\nnot re-interpolate are also `file()` (so `$folder` stays `$folder`); `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`\nis `templatefile()` because it interpolates `${github_auth_id}`. `coder_script`\nbodies are sibling `.tftpl` files next to each `main.tf` (`code-server.sh.tftpl`,\nand on `devcontainer` also `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`) rendered with `templatefile()`;\n`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl` and `stack-skills.sh.tftpl` (the body that seeds the\n`ez-learn` / `ez-skillify` / `ez-create-skill` trio) are the two that live in\n`templates/common/` instead, so both twins share one copy of them.\nThe SVG stays small enough that the resulting data URL fits Coder v2.36's 256-character\n`workspace_apps.icon` column. The push\n(`scripts/push-template.sh` through `template_push_one` in `scripts/lib/template-push.sh`)\nstages every common payload beside each template's staged `main.tf` copy, which is the only place the\nserver-side Terraform can read them from, so editing the common copy changes both\ntemplates; a staged copy that lacks any one of them fails the push early by design."],[711,"`templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` keep independent payload copies that must move\ntogether, and `templates/common/` holds the files both consume as one shared copy. The verb matches\nbasenames over the union of the three payload groups, so the twin set is the graph's answer rather\nthan a convention you have to remember:"],[1183,"`terminal.backend: local` keeps the dangerous-command checker active. The `docker`\nbackend skips that checker, because it treats the container as the safety boundary -\nand here the container is mounted onto the host, so that boundary is not real."],[381,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `\"tmux\"`. The automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build/tasks commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[972,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `tmux`; the automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build and task commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[1883,"`terminal.limited-color`: a tool command spawned by a running Grok session\ninherits `NO_COLOR=1`. Rerun `grok doctor` from the terminal itself and the\nreport reads `color truecolor` with `themes all`."],[1885,"`terminal.ssh-wrap` asks to be run on the workstation you type `ssh` on:\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there. Nothing a workspace installs satisfies it."],[128,"`terraform init` on the server still talks to `registry.terraform.io` (and\n`registry.coder.com` for `devcontainer`'s `devcontainers_cli` module), even\nwhen a previous provider version is cached. The `coder` compose service\nforwards those lookups to `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` so a host whose only\nnameserver is Tailscale MagicDNS does not SERVFAIL the push. Recreate\n`coder` after pulling that compose change (`prod-stack up`) before pushing."],[826,"`tests/bats_runner.sh` — what Bazel executes: it locates bats-core and this checkout in\nrunfiles, exports `REPO_ROOT`, and execs bats."],[822,"`tests/bats/*.bats` — one suite per module. `tests/bats.bzl` turns each file into one\n`bats_<stem>_test` sh_test (dashes become underscores: `bats_spark_configure_test` for\n`spark-configure.bats`) and adds it to the `bats` suite that `//:test-fast` runs."],[823,"`tests/bats/test_helper.bash` — the shared helpers, pulled in with `load 'test_helper'`."],[825,"`tests/bats/tool_stubs/` — the fake external tools (`docker`, `ssh`, `curl`, `git`,\n`coder`, `psql`, `timeout`, …); `tests/bats/fixtures/` holds a fixture that has to answer\nlike the real client, such as the `psql-token` copies that echo `returning id`."],[547,"`tests/bazel_deps.py` re-derives all of them by scanning the shell and Bats sources and\nreports the drift both ways: a script that starts reading a payload its target does not\ndeclare, and a target that still ships a payload nothing opens. `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test`\nruns it, so the BUILD files stay something an automated reader can trust, and a\n`bazelisk query` of any target names the files a change to which should be reviewed with it."],[1589,"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."],[827,"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a function under `scripts/` is not named under\n`tests/`; `tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails when a `# @function` block (or the file header) is\nmissing or incomplete. So every new function needs, in the same change as the function:"],[824,"`tests/test_helpers.sh` — the assertions, shared with the bash gates."],[1813,"`TimeoutErrorRetries: 0` would not help: the hang never becomes a `litellm.Timeout`."],[648,"`title` is the sidebar label, the search result title, and the string your `# H1` has to\nmatch character for character."],[1670,"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."],[106,"`tmux.conf`, `grok-build-terminal.sh`, and `vscode-terminal-tmux.sh` are the\nterminal payloads the startup script writes. Helpers use `$folder` (not\n`$${folder}`) because `file()` contents are not re-interpolated."],[1726,"`Unexpanded count argument on block \"coder_app.github_app_install\"` -> apps were gated on start_count"],[1723,"`unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"` -> Authelia answered the CLI"],[1338,"`unverified` on the `spark-*` rows means exactly that: this box never ran them\nagainst a live Spark, so the column records the absence of a measurement, not a\n\"no\"."],[587,"`updateRemoteUserUID` is on, so the container's `coder` uid matches the host user and the\nbind-mounted checkout stays writable without a `chmod` dance."],[1882,"`voice.no-input-device`: the container has no microphone and no recorder on\nPATH. The probe checks for a recorder binary, so installing `alsa-utils` makes\nDoctor answer `available` with nothing to record from; the image ships no\nrecorder and the finding stays. The PulseAudio route that would make capture\nreal: the voice bullet in grok-build-and-agents.md."],[104,"`vscode-alt.svg` is the editor icon (read with `filebase64()`). It stays small\nenough that the resulting `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` URL fits Coder\nv2.36's `workspace_apps.icon` column (`varchar(256)`)."],[383,"`vscode-terminal-tmux` keeps `vscode-<folder>-<hash>` as its base session name. It\nre-joins one of that folder's sessions while no client is attached to it, oldest\nfirst (the closed-tab rejoin: an unattached session is reused even while sibling\nterminals are live); it takes the next numbered sibling, `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`,\n`-3`, ... only when every session of the folder already has a client. With no\n`tmux` on PATH it falls back to a plain login shell.\nBecause the profile runs inside a session rather than in the tab's own process,\nclosing a tab detaches the client and leaves whatever was running alive."],[1165,"`web_extract` is deliberately unconfigured. SearXNG aggregates engine results\nand does not fetch pages, so the seed leaves `web.backend` and\n`web.extract_backend` empty and page extraction fails with \"no extract provider\nconfigured\". Set `web.extract_backend` yourself when you decide a fetch is\nworth a third-party extractor; nothing reaches one by default."],[1163,"`web_search` goes to the stack's SearXNG container: the seeded config sets\n`web.search_backend: searxng`, and compose gives the container\n`SEARXNG_URL=${STACK_HERMES_SEARCH_URL:-http://searxng:8181}`. The variable's\npresence is what registers the provider (`plugins/web/searxng` reads\n`SEARXNG_URL` as its key env), and it is the only bundled web provider that\nneeds no API key. The name resolves on the compose network, so this adds no\npublished port."],[853,"`with_path <dir…>` prepends directories to PATH when a test needs a hand-written fake\ninstead of a shared stub."],[1049,"`workspace_external_agent`, the entitlement the docs name, gates *external* agents\n(Claude Code, Codex, ...), not this one, and nothing here needs it."],[97,"`workspace` (`templates/workspace/`) is the plain dev container."],[460,"`workspace` addresses docker, so run it from a **host** shell. A terminal inside a\nworkspace is not a host shell: the `docker` CLI there is only installed so you can\ndrive that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so `docker ps` answers empty and an empty\n`docker ps` means \"wrong vantage\", not \"stack down\". The pass now prints which\ndaemon it addressed and tells you to use a host shell, or to name the target:"],[99,"`workspace` goes first, `devcontainer` last: a dependent template pushes last so a\npartial failure leaves the base template usable."],[392,"`workspace` keeps the clone tree under `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`;\n`devcontainer` keeps it under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>`."],[447,"`workspace` re-materialises the stack-owned config inside the targets you name.\nThe reseed set is the thirteen home-relative targets the templates seed -\n`~/.tmux.conf`, `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.coder/AGENTS.md`,\n`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`, the two `grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md` copies, the six\n`ez-learn`, `ez-skillify` and `ez-create-skill` `SKILL.md` copies (two homes each) and\n`~/.shared/vscode/settings.json` - and the same rule\napplies as in `refresh-configs`: a file is replaced only while it still carries its\nstack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment, the\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` JSON key (settings files) or the `__evoT1Stack = true` TOML\nkey (`~/.grok/config.toml`) - so a config you hand-edit in a terminal is your\nfile and this tool will not move it. `--force` overrides that, per run, and says\nso. The six skill copies are the stack's three skill commands, each seeded at\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and twin-copied to its `~/.agents` twin:\n`/ez-learn`, which turns a session into durable memory (this stack's copy of the\nupstream `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory` - the CLI's own commands, not\nskills: flush the conversation into a session log, dream those logs into organized\nmemory, save one note, browse or manage the store. Three of the four are gated on a\nmemory store that is off by default, so they stay hidden until one is configured);\n`/ez-skillify`, which captures the session that just worked; and\n`/ez-create-skill`, which scaffolds one from a description. To take a seeded copy\nover, edit it and\ndelete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a marker-free file is\nuser-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass `--force`. A deletion is\nundone - the workspace boot writes the file again whenever it is absent, and\n`refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores the stack's\ncopy. To hide one of the slash commands, set `disabled = [\"ez-learn\"]` (or\n`ez-skillify`, or `ez-create-skill`) under `[skills]` in\n`~/.grok/config.toml`. An edited copy which keeps its marker is still rewritten\nback to its `templates/common/ez-<name>.SKILL.md` by this tool. The pass also retires the two\n`skills/learn/SKILL.md` paths this skill occupied before the rename, and only while\neach still carries the stack marker, so a leftover copy cannot keep offering a `/learn` the\nstack no longer maintains, and a file someone took over stays."],[114,"`workspace`: **AI workspace** (golden image, the selected default), **Minimal shell**\n(bare `ubuntu:24.04`, for when the golden image is mid-rebuild), **AI workspace + DinD**\n(golden image plus a privileged Docker daemon)."],[1850,"`X-Coder-Request-Id` on the response, beside `X-Coder-Build-Version`, means Coder's\nown Go proxy answered, so Traefik and Cloudflare let the request through and the\nfault sits behind them. Nothing on this box sets `Via` or `CF-RAY`."],[1851,"`X-Evo-Proxy` names the `server_name` that matched here: `coder`, `litellm`, `kasm`,\n`homepage` or `hermes`. Its absence means the request never reached this box's nginx."],[1225,"<Card title=\"Contribute\" href=\"/contribute/devcontainer/\" description=\"The golden image, the contributor container, and the Bazel gates a change runs through.\" />"],[1222,"<Card title=\"Getting started\" href=\"/getting-started/\" description=\"First boot, the order the services come up in, and what to check once Coder answers.\" />"],[1223,"<Card title=\"Learn the stack\" href=\"/learn/\" description=\"Templates, model routing, the workspace agent, Hermes, conventions, branching.\" />"],[1224,"<Card title=\"Operate the box\" href=\"/operate/\" description=\"Dashboard and desktops, config refresh, public access, troubleshooting.\" />"],[1250,"> **Not the same as the `/ez-learn` command.** This tab is the concepts index for people\n> reading the docs. `/ez-learn` typed at the Grok Build prompt turns the session you are in\n> into durable memory - flush it into a session log, dream those logs into organized memory,\n> remember one note, or browse and clear the store; it is this stack's copy of the upstream\n> `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`. Skill authoring is its siblings:\n> `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked and `/ez-create-skill` scaffolds one\n> from a description. `grok inspect` lists the three once their seed has landed. A missing\n> one is the skill-seed entry in troubleshooting.md."],[2066,"| #   | Criterion                                     | How you prove it                                             |\n| --- | --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| 5   | Every environment has one home for its values | the file or overlay exists and no branch is needed to deploy |"],[2069,"| #   | Criterion                                | How you prove it                                                                            |\n| --- | ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 8   | The flag inventory is complete both ways | `grep -rn \"<FLAG>\" .` finds a reader for every declaration and a declaration for every read |"],[2063,"| #   | Criterion                               | How you prove it                                           |\n| --- | --------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 2   | No branch holds commits the trunk lacks | `git rev-list --count origin/main..origin/<branch>` is `0` |"],[2065,"| #   | Criterion                           | How you prove it                              |\n| --- | ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| 4   | Branch names and ages obey the rule | `git for-each-ref` over `refs/remotes/origin` |"],[2062,"| #   | Criterion                          | How you prove it               |\n| --- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------ |\n| 1   | One trunk, no environment branches | `git ls-remote --heads origin` |"],[2068,"| #   | Criterion                         | How you prove it                                                     |\n| --- | --------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 7   | Unfinished work is off by default | the flag's default reads as false in the sample dotenv or flags file |"],[2072,"| #   | Criterion                      | How you prove it                       |\n| --- | ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------- |\n| 11  | The repo's own gates are green | the gate command with its summary line |"],[2071,"| #   | Criterion                    | How you prove it                                                |\n| --- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| 10  | The docs agree with the tree | branching doc, contributing, and agent rules say the same thing |"],[2067,"| #   | Criterion                  | How you prove it                                |\n| --- | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| 6   | One artifact, many targets | the promotion command block, pasted and working |"],[2073,"| #   | Criterion             | How you prove it                                                                         |\n| --- | --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\