---------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `the initramfs listens on 2222, the same port the installed sshd uses` | the two daemons share a port with different host keys | pick another `--port`, and keep using the separate `UserKnownHostsFile` |"],[1451,"| Report                                                          | Cause                                                          | Fix                                                                         |\n| --------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `key file /etc/keys/root.key is not covered by KEYFILE_PATTERN` | the crypttab key lives on disk and never reaches the initramfs | add a `KEYFILE_PATTERN` to `conf-hook`, or move that volume to a passphrase |"],[1450,"| Report                                              | Cause                         | Fix                                          |\n| --------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `the key body holds characters that are not base64` | a pasted key wrapped mid-body | re-paste on one line; `check` names the line |"],[1449,"| Report                                             | Cause                                                                              | Fix                                                                                        |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `no authorized key was packed into the boot image` | the key was written after the image, or in a path the installed hook does not read | re-run `install`; the CLI asks the installed hook which path it reads rather than assuming |"],[1454,"| Report                                             | Cause                                  | Fix                                              |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n| `an encrypted /boot needs GRUB to read the initrd` | `/boot` is inside the encrypted volume | verify GRUB can read it before trusting a reboot |"],[1446,"| Report                                             | Cause                  | Fix                                                                                               |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `no initramfs address (IP is unset, none, or off)` | nothing ever set `IP=` | `install --ip dhcp`, or `--iface ens3` to pin one NIC, or a raw netboot spec for a static network |"],[1452,"| Report                                            | Cause                                                              | Fix                                                                             |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `MODULES=dep packs only drivers loaded right now` | the NIC driver is not loaded today, so it will be missing tomorrow | name the driver in `/etc/initramfs-tools/modules`, or go back to `MODULES=most` |"],[1448,"| Report                                 | Cause                                                       | Fix                                                            |\n| -------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `the boot image holds no bin/dropbear` | the package was installed after the last `update-initramfs` | re-run `install` so the image is regenerated after the install |"],[1447,"| Report                          | Cause                                                   | Fix                                                                     |\n| ------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HOOKS leaves out net dropbear` | a hand-written `HOOKS=` list that predates the packages | `install` merges the four names it needs into whatever list is in force |"],[2122,"| Repository                       | Default | Environment-shaped branches                                  | Deployment mechanism                                               | First move                                                                                          |\n| -------------------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/nvidia-dgx-spark-lab` | `main`  | `development` (+ `dev`), `development` 134 ahead / 27 behind | `k8s/` with a `k8s/dev/` overlay, Compose for dashboard and hermes | Port the 134 commits in slices onto `main`, flag-off the unfinished ones, then retire `development` |"],[2121,"| Repository                     | Default | Environment-shaped branches                                    | Deployment mechanism | First move                                                                    |\n| ------------------------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/evo-t1-coder-stack` | `main`  | `dev`, `development`, `staging`, `uat`, `prod` - all `ahead=0` |                      | Confirm deletion of the five merged branches; the model is already documented |"],[2123,"| Repository                 | Default | Environment-shaped branches                                                                                      | Deployment mechanism                                                                            | First move                                                                                                                                        |\n| -------------------------- | ------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/ez-comfy-stack` | `main`  | `development` (+ `dev`), `development` 523 ahead / 19 behind, plus `feat/`, `fix/`, `feature/`, `docs/` families | Compose (`docker/docker-compose.yml`), `scripts/manage.sh`, Makefile, an image-publish workflow | Port in themed slices; the published image tag is the promotion lever, so land the tag story first and normalise the `feature/` prefix to `feat/` |"],[2124,"| Repository               | Default | Environment-shaped branches                               | Deployment mechanism                           | First move                                                                          |\n| ------------------------ | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `toxicoder/inkorporated` | `main`  | `development` (+ `dev`), `development` 0 ahead / 2 behind | `infrastructure/`, CI plus presubmit workflows | `development` is already merged: list it for deletion, then fix the naming families |"],[1231,"| Service | Host port   | What it is for                                   |\n| ------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------ |\n|         | 3000 → 4443 | Streamed desktops; the wizard only on first boot |"],[1232,"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                                                                 |\n| ------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 9119      | The host assistant. LAN `:9119` stays; the public name is opt-in via `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` |"],[1228,"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                                        |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 3002      | The plain listener a workspace agent dials via `host.docker.internal` |"],[1229,"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                            |\n| ------- | --------- | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 4000      | The single OpenAI-compatible door in front of every model |"],[1230,"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                          |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | —         | The Arc 140T model runtime, never published to the host |"],[1226,"| Service | Host port | What it is for                                     |\n| ------- | --------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 80 / 443  | The landing page, and the only thing publishing it |"],[1233,"| Service | Host port | What it is for                              |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------- |\n|         | 8181      | Keyless metasearch, the agent's search lane |"],[1227,"| Service | Host port | What it is for                             |\n| ------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n|         | 3001      | Control plane: one container per workspace |"],[1530,"| Step     | Endpoint               | App permission          |\n| -------- | ---------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Settings | `PATCH /repos/{o}/{r}` | `Administration: write` |"],[1532,"| Step   | Endpoint                                  | App permission          |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Alerts | `PUT /repos/{o}/{r}/vulnerability-alerts` | `Administration: write` |"],[1531,"| Step   | Endpoint                    | App permission          |\n| ------ | --------------------------- | ----------------------- |\n| Topics | `PUT /repos/{o}/{r}/topics` | `Administration: write` |"],[1529,"| Step   | Endpoint           | App permission                                            |\n| ------ | ------------------ | --------------------------------------------------------- |\n| Create | `POST /user/repos` | `Administration: write` (or `Repository creation: write`) |"],[1533,"| Step | Endpoint       | App permission       |\n| ---- | -------------- | -------------------- |\n| Push | git over HTTPS | the credential above |"],[1565,"| Symptom or task                                              | Page           |\n| ------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------- |\n| The box will not finish booting and nobody is at the console | luks-unlock.md |"],[1563,"| Symptom or task                                    | Page               |\n| -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A workspace is stuck, or its editor will not start | coder-templates.md |"],[1564,"| Symptom or task                                   | Page             |\n| ------------------------------------------------- | ---------------- |\n| A public name does not resolve or redirects wrong | public-access.md |"],[1560,"| Symptom or task                               | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| Something fails, and you have an error string | troubleshooting.md |"],[1559,"| Symptom or task                               | Page         |\n| --------------------------------------------- | ------------ |\n| A card is red, or the dashboard will not load | dashboard.md |"],[1562,"| Symptom or task                         | Page               |\n| --------------------------------------- | ------------------ |\n| A model answered from the wrong backend | litellm-routing.md |"],[1561,"| Symptom or task                         | Page              |\n| --------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| A `git pull` landed and the ports moved | config-updates.md |"],[1566,"| Symptom or task                | Page      |\n| ------------------------------ | --------- |\n| The assistant will not sign in | hermes.md |"],[531,"| Target                    | What it runs                                                                                   |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:test-in-image` | The merge gate inside `evo-t1-dev:latest`, for a machine that is not the contributor container |"],[532,"| Target                   | What it runs                                                                                                     |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:seed-dev-env` | Materialize a contributor home (Grok config, editor settings, notes, tmux, helpers) from the workspace templates |"],[530,"| Target             | What it runs                                                                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//scripts:<stem>` | One operator CLI: `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`, e.g. `//scripts:dev-stack -- up`, `//scripts:new-workspace -- --dry-run <url>` |"],[526,"| Target         | What it runs                                                                             |\n| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:test-fast` | One `sh_test` per `tests/bats/*.bats` file, plus the shell inventory and docstring gates |"],[528,"| Target        | What it runs                                                                                                            |\n| ------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:validate` | test-fast, lint, `docker compose config -q`, `.env.sample` vs `.env.example`, terraform fmt when terraform is installed |"],[527,"| Target    | What it runs                                       |\n| --------- | -------------------------------------------------- |\n| `//:lint` | ShellCheck, shfmt, buildifier (manual: host tools) |"],[529,"| Target   | What it runs                    |\n| -------- | ------------------------------- |\n| `//:fix` | buildifier and shfmt write mode |"],[211,"| Variable             | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| -------------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_default_model` | `agent` | `agent` is the tool-capable Spark lane (thinking `xhigh` by default, picker `low` / `medium` / `xhigh`); `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` bake the level into the alias. |"],[217,"| Variable            | Default  | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |\n| ------------------- | -------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `grok_profile_mode` | `shared` | `shared` mounts the owner's shared `~/.grok` profile volume; `private` gives that workspace a private `~/.grok` on its own home volume. Also the default behind the per-workspace **Grok profile** dropdown; switching a live workspace between the two modes needs a rebuild. |"],[221,"| Variable            | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `grok_config_extra` | `\"\"`    | The **Grok config extras** textarea: free-form TOML appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. |"],[220,"| Variable           | Default           | Notes                                                                                |\n| ------------------ | ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `context7_api_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `CONTEXT7_API_KEY`. The rendered file names the variable. |"],[216,"| Variable           | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                 |\n| ------------------ | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `additional_repos` | `\"\"`    | The **Git repositories (additional)** textarea under the repository field. Extra URLs cloned into their own folder beside `repo_url`. |"],[213,"| Variable          | Default                            | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                            |\n| ----------------- | ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `coder_agent_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:3002` | The URL the agent dials. Kept on the local plain listener so a public `CODER_ACCESS_URL` does not hairpin agent traffic through the reverse proxy. Empty keeps the provider-rendered access URL. |"],[212,"| Variable        | Default | Notes                                                         |\n| --------------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `docker_socket` | `\"\"`    | Optional Docker socket URI; empty means the provider default. |"],[219,"| Variable       | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| -------------- | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `context7_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the cached `context7` MCP stdio server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. The key is optional, so it ships without one. Set `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |"],[209,"| Variable      | Default                               | Notes                                    |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_url` | `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` | Reached through the Docker host gateway. |"],[210,"| Variable      | Default           | Notes                                        |\n| ------------- | ----------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `litellm_key` | `\"\"`, `sensitive` | Handed to the workspace as `LITELLM_API_KEY` |"],[218,"| Variable     | Default | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                              |\n| ------------ | ------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `github_mcp` | `true`  | Wires the `github` MCP HTTP server into Grok's rendered `config.toml`. Its `Authorization` header names `$GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, so no token value is ever part of a template variable. Set `GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push to drop the block. |"],[215,"| Variable   | Default                                                                                                                                                 | Notes                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `repo_url` | `\"\"` in `workspace`, `https://github.com/coder/coder` in `devcontainer` (Terraform); `.env.sample` `REPO_URL` overrides both to this stack's GitHub URL | Prefills the **Git repository** field. Both templates clone that URL on the first start; a later start leaves a folder that already holds a `.git` alone. `REPO_URL` in `.env` overrides the default. The sample value is this stack's own repo so **VS Code Web** opens `~/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`. |"],[208,"| Variable | Default             | Notes                                                                                                                       |\n| -------- | ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `image`  | `evo-t1-dev:latest` | Seeds the **Workspace image** dropdown default. In `devcontainer` it decides nothing about the toolchain inside the editor. |"],[214,"| Variable | Default | Notes                                                                                           |\n| -------- | ------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `dind`   | `false` | Turns on the per-workspace privileged daemon described below. Does not exist in `devcontainer`. |"],[2111,"| Verdict         | Test                        | What you do                                     |\n| --------------- | --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n| release-bearing | carries an untagged version | tag the tip, push the tag, then list the branch |"],[2107,"| Verdict    | Test                                     | What you do                                  |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |\n| superseded | its files exist on trunk in another form | list for deletion, citing what superseded it |"],[2109,"| Verdict  | Test                    | What you do                          |\n| -------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------ |\n| preserve | valuable and incomplete | port behind a flag that defaults off |"],[2106,"| Verdict | Test                                                            | What you do       |\n| ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| merged  | `rev-list --count` is 0, or `git cherry` marks every commit `-` | list for deletion |"],[2110,"| Verdict | Test                              | What you do                       |\n| ------- | --------------------------------- | --------------------------------- |\n| dead    | no value, or explicitly abandoned | list for deletion with the reason |"],[2108,"| Verdict | Test                          | What you do                                                 |\n| ------- | ----------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |\n| port    | small, complete, still wanted | re-adapt onto a new short-lived branch, open a pull request |"],[1083,"1 and 2 are the Coder agent's *default* discovery paths."],[1085,"1-4 are written by `startup.sh.tftpl`. 5-10 are the stack's three skill commands and are\nwritten by `coder_script.stack_skills`, whose own `execve` carries the three payloads;\nthey ride their own script body rather than the startup argument because that argument\nhas a single-argument ceiling of its own\n(coder-templates.md). Each `.agents`\ncopy is a `cp` of the managed `.coder` copy, never a second inline body, so the pair\ncannot drift."],[1084,"3 and 4 are the same text under the Grok Build CLI's convention, and the CLI is\nwhat `execute`-lane delegation drives. The chat agent dedupes skills by name,\nfirst match wins, home scope before a working-dir path, so one agent never loads a\nskill twice: the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."],[1086,"5 and 6 are `/ez-learn`: this stack's copy of the upstream memory commands `/flush`,\n`/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`. It turns the current session into durable facts -\nflush the conversation into a session log under the workspace's `~/.grok/memory/`\ndirectory, dream those logs into an organized `MEMORY.md`, save one note, or browse and\nclear what was stored. Reaching past those logs into the raw transcripts under\n`~/.grok/sessions/` is opt-in depth, done only when the operator asks for it. Its\ndefault mode is report-only, and it writes memory files, never a skill. 7 and 8 are\n`/ez-skillify`, which turns the session that just worked into a skill from its own\ntranscript, and 9 and 10 are `/ez-create-skill`, which scaffolds one from what the\noperator describes. The three payloads are\n`templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, on the same absent-only contract. `/ez-create-skill` stands\nin for the bundled platform skill `create-skill`, which arrives only in the skills\nbundle behind an x.ai sign-in this stack does not have; `/ez-skillify` has no published\nupstream counterpart at all; and the memory family `/ez-learn` copies is upstream\n*built-ins*: `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` are gated behind cross-session memory,\nwhich is off by default, while `/remember` is a pager command with no such gate. Either\nway the seed is what makes the three commands exist. Each ships under its own `ez-`\nname to stay clear of those upstream names, since a user-scope skill overrides a\nsame-named bundled one and a seed named `create-skill` would hide the bundled copy on a\nworkspace that ever does sign in. The boot and the reseed delete a leftover\nmarker-carrying `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from before the rename. See the CLI section\nabove."],[360,"A **GitHub reauth** external app (`order = 7`, `/icon/github.svg`) opens Coder's\n`/external-auth/<id>` page in a slim window on the user's Coder session: connect, or\n**Reauthenticate** when the account is already linked. It stays on the main agent so\na failed clone (or a `devcontainer` subagent that never starts) still leaves the\nbutton on the workspace page. Device-flow login is not the App install: with the\ninjected App (id `github`) that page prints \"You must install the GitHub App to\nclone private repositories\" as unlinked plaintext, because Coder omits\n`AppInstallURL` on the default provider."],[378,"A *regular* file at the User settings path is the old per-workspace copy: the startup\nscript keeps it beside the link once, as `<path>.pre-shared`, then points the editor at\nthe shared file on every start so a workspace cannot drift off it. GNU `ln -sfn` replaces\na symlink and does not follow it."],[1842,"A `~/.grok/config.toml` carrying neither the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` line\nnor the `__evoT1Stack = true` key is a file the stack stopped managing, so neither\nthe startup script nor the seed writes it again and its default lanes never arrive.\nThe usual cause is not a hand edit: `grok mcp add` persists its own settings by\nrewriting that file and deletes every comment line, which used to revoke stack\nownership by itself. The TOML key is what survives that write, so a config rendered\nby the current templates keeps its marker through `grok mcp add`. Take the file\nover deliberately by removing both lines. To give it back: delete the file and\nrestart the workspace, or re-materialize it with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --force`."],[1857,"A `404` is what this stack answers for an unknown app slug or an agent that is not\nConnected; the agent is the text after the first `.` in `<ws>.<agent>`."],[185,"A `DEV_IMAGE` that is not in the local image store is a note, not a stop: build it\nfirst with `bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`."],[35,"A `fix/…` branch that also has to rename a template directory, retarget a `.bats` suite, and\nupdate `docs/` is one change: the rename is not green until the last reference follows.\nWhen a rename cannot be proven complete, the reference stays (a rename is only \"done\" when\nthe old name is gone outside historical docs) and the CI file says so in a comment."],[225,"A `github_mcp` of `true` with no GitHub account connected gives that workspace a server\nthat fails its handshake once per session, because `$GH_TOKEN` is empty. A\n`context7_mcp` of `true` with an empty `context7_api_key` is *not* the same shape:\nthe key is optional, the block renders either way, and the server handshakes and\nanswers without one - no `Authorization` header goes out, so the lane runs at the\npublic rate limit and private libraries are the only thing out of reach. That keyless\nblock is the pushed default and what a fresh deploy gets, since nothing in git\ncarries a key, and `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` only warns about it. Park a\nlane deliberately with `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed\nby `push-template.sh`) or `--var github_mcp=false` / `--var context7_mcp=false` on\nthe push; a block missing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a\npark. Fill the context7 key with `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in `.env` plus a re-push when a\nsession needs private libraries or a higher rate limit."],[1875,"A `learn` directory still listed by the last `ls` is a copy someone took over: the boot\nand the reseed delete that path only while it still carries the stack marker, and a\nmarker-free file is user-owned, so it stays. A marker-carrying leftover is retired on\nthe next boot or reseed, and that removal only finishes the rename: no published source\nnames an upstream `/learn` for the name to free."],[784,"A `variable` block is the only place a Coder template can declare what an input is,\nand an undeclared one takes whatever HCL guessed. `search_mcp = \"flase\"` then reaches\na `== \"true\"` comparison and reads as false, so the operator gets a workspace that\ncame up wrong and a plan that said nothing. A declared constraint turns that into a\nplan-time error."],[1821,"A `warning:` that a stored model config is disabled, is not the default, or compacts\nagainst the wrong window names a row this script found already in place. It reads\nbefore it writes and this surface has no PATCH, so the correction is the one click in\nAdmin -> Experiments -> Agent Chat the line names."],[229,"A 0 default TTL leaves the checkbox unchecked and leaves `coder create` with nothing to\ninherit, so a workspace runs until its owner stops it."],[1192,"A bind-mounted skill needs no recreate when the agent reloads skills from disk; start a\n**new session** - a fresh Chat tab, or a new `hermes-shell` run - and the new skill is\noffered. Recreate only if it still does not show up, since `restart` keeps the old\nmounts."],[1456,"A box with no DHCP gets the raw initramfs form, which is passed through\nuntouched because `initramfs-tools` parses it, not this CLI:"],[1044,"A built dev container mounts neither the volume nor the symlink, so it is the one\nplace the seed is **merged** instead of linked: the inlined\n`coder_script.code_server` fork bakes a base64 copy of it and writes it over the\ndev container's private `User/settings.json` (or merges over a copy that survived a\nrebuild, when `jq` is on PATH; without `jq` the existing file is kept). Edits\ninside a built dev container therefore survive neither a rebuild nor a look at the\nshared file. Everywhere else no settings map is passed at all, because a merge\nwould replace the symlink."],[375,"A built dev container sees neither the volume nor that symlink, so it is the one place\nthe seed is merged instead of linked."],[1363,"A busy pinned box sheds its own overflow in its own scheduler instead of queueing\ninside the proxy."],[887,"A card per service, with status pulled from each service's own API over the compose\nnetwork - `http://coder:3000`, `http://litellm:4000`, `http://ollama:11434`,\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status` - so nothing here needs a daemon credential. The\nheader carries boxed tiles for host CPU, memory, CPU temperature, disk and `eth0`\nthroughput, beside the clock, local weather (open-meteo, keyless) and a DuckDuckGo\nsearch."],[1822,"A chat that starts but narrates instead of calling tools is the no-Spark case above."],[1058,"A chat turn needs an enabled chat model config - the chat's own pick, else the\ndeployment default, else it errors before the first tool call - and that wiring is\nwhat the script writes. It mints a short-lived admin API token from the stack's own\nPostgres (`token_name 'stack-coder-agents'`, deleted by the trap, never printed) and\ndrives the server's experimental chat API:"],[1768,"A checkout that does not yet contain that `--repair` (the usual case: `git pull`\nis already failing) needs the same commands by hand, then pull:"],[1662,"a Coder certificate whose DNS SANs now carry the public names alongside the existing\nones. The LAN cert keeps working; in public mode the browser never sees it, since\nTraefik presents real ones;"],[1655,"a comma-separated list is used verbatim, which is how you pin a name by hand (leave\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE` empty, or that key keeps re-deriving)."],[1362,"A conversation that goes quiet for longer than the TTL may move."],[1358,"A cooled box therefore stays visibly out of `healthy_deployments` instead of blending\ninto the group's aggregate stats."],[1730,"A copy from an app inside a tmux pane never reaches the laptop clipboard in VS Code Web -> the browser build drops OSC 52"],[359,"A custom **Web Terminal** command-app at `order = 6` takes the terminal slot. Custom\napps are ordered among themselves; static helper buttons have no `order` or `icon` knob\nin this Coder release and are not reorderable."],[1844,"A declared context7 server: an empty `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is the shipped default and\ndoes *not* fail the handshake. With no value the server sends no `Authorization`\nheader, so it handshakes and answers at the public rate limit, and only private\nlibraries are out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env. When a\ncontext7 handshake really fails the key is not the cause: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` and it says which of the block, the binary, the\ncache config or the handshake is not OK."],[1843,"A declared github server that fails handshake: the owner has not connected\nGitHub External Auth, so `$GH_TOKEN` is empty. Use the **GitHub reauth** app,\nthen start a new Grok session."],[1841,"A declared server whose `command=` the box cannot find is image drift: the\ntemplates declare a binary the running image never got, because it predates the\nnpm bake in `images/dev/Dockerfile`. Rebuild `evo-t1-dev` and recreate the\nworkspace, or install the pinned package into the running box (`npm i -g --prefix\n/opt/npm-global searxng-mcp-server@<pin>`, the pins live in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."],[2131,"A default-branch rename is its own confirmed step, done after the content has converged,\nbecause GitHub rewrites links, protections and open pull requests when it happens."],[178,"A dev stack that moved the search port needs its own template push: `searxng_url`\nis derived from `STACK_SEARCH_PORT` as read from `.env`, so a push made while\n`dev-stack.env` carries `18181` still names `8181` unless the key is set in `.env`\ntoo. A workspace dials the host gateway, where the compose network does not help,\nso the port in the rendered `SEARXNG_URL` has to be the one actually published."],[1759,"A failed bind from an older runfiles compose file can also leave a **directory**\n(or a root-owned file) at `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg` inside\n`bazel-out/.../prod-stack.runfiles`. The next `bazelisk run` then dies before\nthe stack script starts; that spelling is the next heading."],[350,"A failed clone is loud and non-fatal. The workspace still boots, an empty editor folder\nis fixable from a terminal, git removes its own partial clone on failure, and nothing in\nthe script deletes a folder that may hold someone's work."],[131,"A failed description edit and a failed autostop pin only add a note; neither blocks the\ntemplate."],[600,"A file carrying the stack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment, the\n`\"__evoT1Stack\": true` JSON key, or the `__evoT1Stack = true` TOML key - is\nstack-owned and is reseeded when the seed in this repo drifted. Taking\n`~/.grok/config.toml` over means deleting both its marker lines, since the Grok CLI\ndeletes comments when it rewrites that file:\n../grok-build-and-agents.md."],[713,"A file the graph does not know is a warning naming that path, and the usual cause is a file you just\ncreated that no filegroup claims yet. `owners` answers \"is this visible to the harness at all\":"],[1022,"a file written in the last two seconds is left for the next tick, because copying it\nwhole could archive a half-saved file. Going the other way, two guards stop a recreate from\nerasing notes: a live file that is empty or still holds only the seeded stub never replaces\na canonical copy carrying real notes, and a real canonical copy restores over such a stub\nin a clone. Before any substantive canonical copy is replaced it is archived as\n`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/backups/notes-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS.md`, keeping the newest\n`NOTES_BACKUP_KEEP` (10 by default), and that directory is where an operator recovers notes\nafter an accident. `grok_profile_mode=private` mounts no shared volume, so those notes die\nwith that workspace; `shared` is the default. Like `config.toml`, the last workspace\nstarted for a given user and repository wins."],[1876,"A file you deleted or edited on purpose is not a fault. To keep your own version,\nedit it and delete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker line: a marker-free\nfile is user-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass `--force`. A\ndeletion is undone - the workspace boot writes the file again whenever it is absent,\nand `refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores the\nstack's copy. Copy the payload back from `templates/common/<name>.SKILL.md` to give\nthe file back to the stack."],[2098,"A flag service, an SDK, or a database-backed flag table is a decision an operator makes\nexplicitly. It is not the default for a home lab or a single-node host, and it is never what a\nfirst pass should add."],[349,"A folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone: re-fetching is the user's call,\nbecause a pull there could conflict mid-edit or fail on a dirty tree, and either way the\nworkspace would start on code nobody chose."],[921,"A GMKtec EVO-T1 (or any box with the Arc 140T and enough RAM for the model in\n`OLLAMA_MODELS`) with Docker and Compose v2 installed."],[1879,"A golden image that predates a pin bump reports an older `/usr/local/bin/grok` than\n`GROK_VERSION` (1.0.77 at the time of writing), and rebuilding `images/dev`\n(`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`) is the CLI upgrade path - but the seeded skill trio\nworks on either CLI, so this symptom alone never justifies an image rebuild."],[1068,"A Grok Build session logging `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s\n(runaway generation)` is the session-model summary hanging before its first token on\nthe lane that also serves the whole agent loop. Its only live bounds are\n`GROK_COMPACTION_WALL_CLOCK_SECS`, rendered into the agent `env` because no TOML table\ntakes it, and the proxy's `request_timeout`; troubleshooting.md\nowns the measurement and the retry split that stops one hang being bought three times."],[1849,"A hit in the first means Coder tried the upstream and gave up, so the fault sits on\nthis box: check the devcontainer or workspace container first, then `DOCKER_HOST`."],[1038,"A lane an agent needs fails in exactly three shapes, and that check tells them\napart, so run it before editing anything:"],[410,"A live `coder external-auth access-token <id>` mint, on demand. Not a helper: it is\nwhat `github-auth fix` calls, and what a workspace shell can call, whenever a token has\nto be certainly fresh."],[601,"A marker-free file is user-owned and stays untouched unless `--force` says otherwise.\nThe terminal helpers and `notes-setup.sh` are the exception: startup rewrites those on\nevery boot, so this script does too."],[596,"A missing `[mcp_servers.context7]` block in that file means the seed ran keyless and\nwithout `--context7-mcp`: re-run the seeder with the flag, adding `--force` when the\nexisting file carries no stack marker and the seeder left it alone. On a Coder\nworkspace the same repair runs from the stack host:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>`."],[1840,"A missing `[mcp_servers.github]` or `[mcp_servers.context7]` in\n`~/.grok/config.toml` means the last push passed `--var ...=false`, or the\nworkspace started from an older template, or the file carries no stack marker at all\n(next bullet). Clear the gate and re-push, then re-materialize the file and restart\nthe workspace so the managed `config.toml` re-renders. Two routes re-materialize it:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>` from the\nstack host, for a running workspace container, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` for a host home (a laptop, CI,\nor the contributor container)."],[183,"A missing `docker`, `timeout`, `tar` or `openssl`, a stack that is not running, or no\nactive Coder account is a *skip*: a one-line reason and exit 0. `bootstrap.sh` calls\nthis script, and a fresh clone has none of those prerequisites yet."],[248,"A missing image fails the workspace build at **plan** time, before anything is\ncreated, with `did not find docker image 'docker:29.8.1-dind'` - see\ntroubleshooting.md."],[836,"A native `bazelisk test` on macOS is convenience only: the BSD userland differs from the\nGNU one in the image (`sed`, `date`, `find`, `sort`), so a red run there is not proof of a\ndefect and a green one is not the run that unlocks the merge button. CI and branch\nprotection gate on the image result either way."],[716,"A new `scripts/*.sh` is a Bazel target automatically (the package is generated from the directory),\nbut it is not wired automatically: it needs a `_SCRIPT_LIBS` key (the `scripts/lib` closure it\nsources) and a `_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD` key (the payload groups it opens), both in `scripts/BUILD.bazel`.\nUntil those keys land the graph does not know the file at all, which is worth saying out loud:\n`rdeps` reads the graph, not the filesystem."],[715,"A new top-level payload directory belongs in its own package with its own `filegroup` targets, joins\n`//:stack_data` by label, and is named in the map of every target that reads it, as\nbuilding-with-bazel.md spells out. `unlisted` is how you find out you have\nnot done that, rather than finding out that the gate never noticed."],[2009,"A part carrying an extra dot is the other failure this table cannot catch: the\ncomposed name sits more than one label under the zone, the wildcard does not cover\nit, and the browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` before Traefik\nis ever reached, so no `421` is ever printed."],[1656,"A part that cannot be a DNS label composes nothing: that service is left out of the list\nrather than sent as an empty entry, and a missing zone keeps the whole stack LAN-only."],[712,"A per-tree file whose sibling tree has no counterpart is a `warning:` naming the group that claims\nit: that asymmetry is the drift worth knowing about before you edit, not after."],[296,"A persistent per-workspace home volume (`coder-<wsid>-home`)."],[895,"A plain `restart` keeps the generated page, so day-to-day YAML edits need nothing.\nNew asset files do need a recreate (`up -d`), since `restart` keeps the old mounts."],[2114,"A port that also reformats is two pull requests. A port of a whole branch at once is a review\nnobody does properly."],[394,"A private extra repository still has to be GRANTED to the connected GitHub account, or\nits clone is refused, just like the primary one."],[1599,"A probe-style CLI takes its target as a flag (`--url`) rather than a hard-coded host, and reports\n\"nothing answered\" as a result rather than a crash, so one run works from the host and from inside\na workspace and still answers the question the operator asked. Its suite stubs the transport."],[616,"A pull request that does not touch `images/dev/**` replays the cached layers instead of\nrebuilding them, which is what keeps the gate's wall time flat."],[162,"A push is also the only thing that *registers* a template: Coder stores the source as a\nFilestore row, not as files it scans on disk."],[798,"A Python file that lands outside the runner's list is unchecked code that looks\ngated, which is the failure `//tests:bats_mypy_lint_test` exists to catch: it\nenumerates the tree and fails on any `.py` the target list does not cover, with\n`tests/bats/fixtures/` the one sanctioned exclusion because that tree is scaffold\npayload typed in the repository it is copied into."],[742,"A reachability proof, for when you must know that a gate really does sit on top of a file:"],[1062,"a read-only drift check on the stored configs: a config that is disabled, is not\nthe deployment default, or carries a `context_limit` that does not match the alias's\nserving window is reported with a `warning:` and the screen to fix it on. The\nexperimental chat API has no PATCH for model configs, so these are named rather than\nwritten."],[1306,"A rename or deletion is the edit that can reach workspaces: both\n`templates/*/grok-config.toml.tftpl` twins render the alias list into\n`~/.grok/config.toml`, a running workspace keeps the copy it rendered at start, and\nthe next start re-renders it."],[148,"A repeated name is one push, not two, and the push order stays `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT`\norder whatever the argument order is. Naming `devcontainer` first cannot orphan it."],[1933,"A repo that ships none wants the **Plain clone** preset, or the dev-container toggle\nset to **No** at create time. Either one runs code-server on the plain workspace\ncontainer and opens the clone there."],[265,"A repository that ships no `devcontainer.json` gets no dev container and hence no\neditor with the toggle on. The toggle - or\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url>`, which reads the repository and\npicks for you - is how that case is meant to be handled."],[1343,"A request carrying `tools` for `qwen2.5-coder:32b` or `:14b` comes back as plain text,\n`finish_reason: \"stop\"`, and no `tool_calls` field at all."],[1675,"A router per name pointing at `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080`, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`. That backend is `:8080` with the original Host,\nthe same as the other four - not `:9119`. A router aimed at `:9119` skips this\nbox's vhost. The file in Traefik configuration is that\nconfig; diff what the box runs against it."],[718,"A script missing its key outright fails `//scripts` analysis before any test runs, and a script\nwhose declared map disagrees with its sources is what that checker reports, naming the pairing and\nthe direction. Once the keys are in place the same verb resolves the file, and\n`tests-for scripts/bazel-map.sh` lists `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` among its consumers. Every new function additionally owes one `# @function` block and one asserting test in exactly\none suite, which test-with-bazel.md covers; `//tests:shell_inventory` and\n`//tests:doc_coverage` are the two gates, and both are test targets the graph can point you at\nbefore the gate does."],[861,"A script must work when the cwd is **not** the checkout. Under `bazel test` the working\ndirectory is an exec root or a runfiles tree that holds *copies* of whatever the target\ndeclares as `data`; under `bazel run` it is wherever the operator typed `bazelisk`, and\n`bazel run` alone sets `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`. Neither guarantees a working Docker\ndaemon, an `ssh`, or a reachable Spark box — a sandboxed action usually has none of them on\nPATH."],[1597,"A script never runs another repo script by relative path, and never assumes the cwd is the\ncheckout: it resolves the repo root with `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` and then\n`cd`s there (see the harness doc), because a Bazel sandbox may only hold a copy. That helper tries\n`${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` / `${PWD}` /\n`${SELF_DIR}/..` in order, and a candidate counts only when it holds the marker file\n(`docker-compose.yml` unless the caller names another) and its `pwd -P` is not under a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree — an explicit `REPO_ROOT` is\nthe one candidate trusted as given, which is how the Bats runner names the copy under test.\nNothing proving out is one\n`error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line on stderr and exit `5`, never a guessed cwd."],[549,"A script that needs the checkout reaches it the same way: source its libs by absolute path from\n`SELF_DIR`, resolve the root with `resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`), then `cd` into\nwhat it prints. Never `cd \"$(dirname \"$0\")/..\"` - under `bazelisk run` the launcher execs the\nrunfiles copy of the script, so that is the runfiles tree, which holds only `//:stack_data` and is\nrecreated on the next build."],[661,"A section holds an ordered mix of leaves and collapsible folders. A leaf is `(label, path)` and a\nfolder is `(label, [child, ...])`. Folders stay ordinary directories: a section whose first entry\nis an index page uses that page as its clickable label."],[999,"A self-installed CLI under `~/.grok/bin` stays first on the login-shell PATH, so it\nwins over the baked copy."],[358,"A separate in-browser **code-server** editor is Coder's VS Code fork (no local VS\nCode), labelled **VS Code Web** with `order = 5` so it sorts before GitHub reauth (7)\nand Install GitHub App (8) and reads apart from the **VS Code Desktop** helper. It is\nthe only app in the bar the agent still proxies."],[1392,"A single conversation pinned to one box now keeps its streams on that box while it\nstays healthy - that is the behaviour the burst predates."],[1190,"A skill is one directory: `hermes/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, with\nagentskills.io frontmatter for the name and description.\nCommit it, and the next `up` has it."],[1709,"A stack skill command missing from Grok Build on code-server web -> the stack's own seed is what provides it"],[832,"A suite is one cached action. Its `.bats` file, `test_helper.bash`, `test_helpers.sh`,\n`bats_runner.sh` and the `scripts/**.sh` plus stub/fixture files it declares as `data` are\nits inputs, so editing one script re-runs only the suites that read it, and an unchanged\nsuite replays its cached PASS. Beyond that common set each suite names only the payload\ngroups it opens, in `_SUITE_PAYLOAD` in `tests/BUILD.bazel`; a suite whose target does not\ndeclare a payload it reads finds that payload missing under its own target, and\n`//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test` is the gate that keeps the map honest."],[696,"A sweep of this repo's own agent session logs measured the habits the graph is meant to\nreplace. Corpus: the 199 parent sessions recorded for this checkout under\n`~/.grok/sessions/` before 2026-11-01, counted with `jq` and `grep -c` over each session's\n`chat_history.jsonl`. Every count is a floor, because a truncated tool result hides a call."],[457,"A target named without a flag is a container, the same reading `refresh-configs`\ngives a bare word, so these two lines address the same container:"],[132,"A template whose push or verification failed is counted in the\n`N of M template(s) did not reach an active version` line and makes the run exit\nnon-zero, while every other template still gets its push attempt."],[1063,"a three-line system-prompt addendum, appended after Coder's default prompt text\n(`include_default_system_prompt: true`), that keeps the delegation and\nsecret-hygiene rules which must hold even when a chat has no workspace attached."],[1884,"A tmux finding that survives a reseed of `~/.tmux.conf` is a reload gap: a\nclient reads `terminal-features` when it attaches. Run `tmux source-file\n~/.tmux.conf`, detach, reattach, then `tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`\nand expect `RGB` and `extkeys` in the list."],[283,"A tracked `.devcontainer/contributor-env` outranks every other signal: the probe is a\n**miss** even when the same clone also tracks `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`. That is\nhow this repo keeps its own Coder workspaces on `workspace` — the `.devcontainer/` it\nships is the contributor one for a laptop and CI, not a nested toolchain to rebuild\ninside the workspace's own DinD sidecar (see\ncontribute/devcontainer.md). Application repositories do\nnot carry the sentinel, so their dev containers stay a hit."],[336,"A twin is copied only while absent, and only from the managed `~/.coder` copy, so a\nhand-placed file in `~/.agents` is never overwritten and the dedupe keeps exactly one\nlive source. The CLI-side spelling of the instruction file keeps the `INSTRUCTIONS.md`\nname because that is the name the `~/.agents` convention was seeded under."],[1365,"A value has to look like an id to count: alphanumerics, hyphens or underscores, 8\ncharacters or more. The sources, in order, are `x-litellm-trace-id`, then\n`x-litellm-session-id`, then any `x-<vendor>-session-id`, then\n`session-id`/`session_id`/`thread-id`/`conversation_id` (Codex style), then W3C\ntraceparent and baggage, plus `metadata.session_id` in the body."],[918,"A working stack on a fresh box without reading the whole README first"],[1636,"A workspace **app** link (the **VS Code Web** button is the only app this stack still\nproxies) rides the coder row of that table. `coder_app.code_server` sets\n`subdomain = false`, so its URL keeps the `/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` path\nform and goes to `coder:3000` on its way to the editor on `localhost:13337` inside the\nworkspace. A 502 on that link is an app-lane failure and `litellm-gmktecbeast.` took\nno part in it."],[1704,"A workspace app link answers `Bad Gateway` -> find who answered"],[482,"A workspace moving `shared` -> `private` starts on an empty profile: nothing is\ncopied and the shared volume is left alone."],[290,"A workspace name can follow the URL. Coder names start with a letter (40 characters\nmax); the derived default is the repository leaf."],[232,"A workspace owner can still tick the box for one workspace: `allow_user_autostop`\nstays on, because CE cannot turn it off -- `--allow-user-autostop=false` is marked\n\"can only be disabled in enterprise\"."],[1387,"A wrong model id does not crash the proxy: 1.102.1 builds the Router with\n`ignore_invalid_deployments` forced to True, so a bad id drops that deployment at boot\nwith one log line and later surfaces as \"No deployments available\"."],[1102,"Access"],[767,"Adding a `.py`, `.ts`, or `.tf` file without guessing which list it belongs on"],[756,"Adding a file"],[763,"Adding a new language, or a new file to an existing one"],[643,"Adding a page that shows up in the menu, the search index and the prev/next footer"],[516,"Adding a script without editing a BUILD file"],[818,"Adding a test that passes in the Bazel sandbox and on a laptop"],[1818,"After a `stack up`, the seeder that runs it in the background keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (the `note:` line names the path). A chat\nthat never got wired usually means that file holds a `skipped:` line: no coder\ncontainer yet, no db container, or nobody had registered when the stack came up.\nRe-run the command above once the stack is up and somebody has signed in."],[874,"After a recreate: revalidate once"],[1096,"After creating or editing anything, check discovery and provenance with `grok inspect`,\nand `grok inspect --json` for each skill's `source` path and `userInvocable`. `/skills`\nopens the same set as a menu, where a toggle takes a skill out of the slash menu\nwithout deleting the file. Discovery reloads from disk, so an edit lands next turn\nwith no restart."],[1348,"Agent mode therefore needs either the Spark `agent` lane or the small `chat` model."],[1832,"Agent traffic ignores that URL and dials `host.docker.internal:3002`; see the\n`coder_agent_url` template variable."],[361,"An **Install GitHub App** external app (`order = 8`, same icon, opens in a tab)\nis the clickable install path for that default App:\n`https://github.com/apps/coder/installations/new`. It is declared only while\n`github_auth_id` is `github`. After installing, grant the private repos the\nworkspace clones, then restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints."],[1059,"an `openai-compat` AI provider named **`litellm`** (not `litellm-auto`), base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1` - LiteLLM over the compose network, *server-side*,\nauthenticated with `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`. The agent dials the in-process AI Gateway\n(aibridge), which forwards to the provider's `base_url` + `/chat/completions`, so\n`/v1` has to be part of the base URL and the key has to match the live proxy: with\n`.env` leaving the key empty, compose still boots the proxy on its\n`change-me-litellm` interpolation default, and that default is what gets sent.\nCreated when absent, drift-patched when an existing provider carries a drifted\n`base_url` or key."],[397,"An `ssh://` or `git@` URL reaches nothing here - this stack provisions no SSH keys."],[1835,"An agent whose searches come back empty while the service answers 200 is nearly\nalways parked engines rather than a broken deployment: the general-purpose engines\nare the ones the public backends bot-wall first. The same query without an\n`engines` pin usually still returns results, which is why the guidance in\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md` tells an agent to leave the engine choice to the instance."],[1304,"An alias is a `model_name:` entry in `litellm/config.yaml`, not a model."],[253,"An always-on privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on the per-workspace bridge\nnetwork, with `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375` injected **unconditionally** and\n`DOCKER_TLS_CERTDIR=` empty for the reasons above. `internal` stays false for the same\none reason."],[1981,"An empty github.com/settings/apps page is the usual\nsurprise here: it means no App is installed under your account, so there is no\n**Configure** link to click and nothing to grant, while the provider itself stays\nconfigured and keeps minting tokens that authenticate. Install Coder's App from the\nworkspace **Install GitHub App** button, or at\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new,\ngrant the repo, then restart. A private repository is never visible to a credential\nthat was never granted it."],[714,"An empty list is the finding: no filegroup carries the file, so `test-fast` and `lint` never open it.\nThe pair verb, `unlisted`, asks it per directory by diffing `git ls-files` against the graph's own\nsource list. It skips Bazel's own declaration files (`BUILD.bazel`, `*.bzl`, `MODULE.bazel`), which\nBazel reads implicitly and which only appear in the graph when a package deliberately exports them,\nso a directory holding nothing but a BUILD file reads as clean."],[1031,"An empty result page and \"no answer\" are different answers, which is why the probe\nreports which one it got. It also names every engine the instance has parked and\nthe reason its backend gave (`parked by its backend: google: access denied`),\nread out of the reply body whether or not that reply found results - an instance\nrunning on half its engines is visible there before it starts answering empty. A\npark is temporary: SearXNG holds an engine out for `search.suspended_times`\nseconds after it refuses, so re-run before writing one off. `--json` adds\n`engines_parked`. It checks the service hop; `grok mcp doctor searxng`\ninside the workspace checks the MCP hop. The binary it launches\n(`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`) is baked into the golden image, so a\nprobe that answers with results plus a failed handshake is a server, URL or port\nproblem, not a missing package."],[1871,"An entry for `<name>` with `userInvocable: true` in the `grok inspect --json` output is\nthe pass condition: the slash command exists on that CLI, and the **Grok Build**\nterminal profile shows it because that profile execs the same binary."],[2054,"An environment is a set of values, a set of secrets, and a place to run. It is a GitHub\nEnvironment, a compose project, a namespace, an inventory group. It is not a git branch."],[2081,"An environment may differ from its neighbour in values, secrets, and where it runs. If a branch\ndiffers in *code*, that code belongs on the trunk behind a flag."],[1661,"an extended `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` - homepage validates the incoming Host against\nthat exact-match list, so an unlisted name fails its host check;"],[393,"An extra folder never becomes the folder the tools open on: the editor, the Web\nTerminal button and the tmux helpers all stay on the primary clone."],[1429,"An Ubuntu server with a LUKS root stops in the initramfs on every reboot and\nwaits for a passphrase. With no keyboard attached, that wait is the outage: the\nmachine is up, the disks are unreadable, and the only fix is a person at the\nconsole. The distro answer is to move an SSH server and cryptsetup's unlock\nprompt into the initramfs, so the passphrase is typed over SSH from anywhere."],[1660,"an upgraded `CODER_ACCESS_URL` (the public coder name);"],[1036,"and `search_tool` lists the live tool names rather than a hardcoded list. Prove it\n  with `grok mcp doctor context7` inside a workspace."],[745,"And set algebra, for \"is this payload covered by the suite I trust with it\":"],[2031,"Ansible"],[666,"Answer what-depends-on-this with one bazelisk query instead of a grep sweep: the bazel-map CLI, the raw query predicates behind it, and the empty-result trap."],[1677,"Any authentication as the Traefik router's middleware. Authelia already fronts\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` (the name 302s), the same interactive policy as\nthe homepage and Coder names. The sidecar forwards what it receives and\nauthenticates nothing, so a name whose router skips the auth middleware reaches\nits container the same way the LAN does. The app path is behind that policy\nlike the rest of its name, so an expired session surfaces there too."],[2007,"Anything else means the router matched (Hermes basic auth is `401` until you\nsend credentials). A `421` only on the public URL, with this probe succeeding,\nis a rewritten Host or a Traefik backend that is not `:8080`."],[458,"Anything that carries a prefix other than `c:` (container) or `h:` (host\ndirectory) is reported as unrecognized and counted in the closing summary's\nskipped tally. A target never leaves the pass unheard."],[1052,"Apply it (or re-run after a skip):"],[1120,"Approval prompts, bootstrap seeding, and the skills directory"],[1109,"Approvals"],[45,"Argument handling"],[147,"Arguments are template *names*, and they select which names to push. A name that is not\none of the known names is an error that lists the known names, so a typo cannot quietly\ndo nothing."],[1124,"Asking from a terminal you already have - the right Linux command, a triage, a\nGitHub project deployed into the deploy workspace - with the same skills, memory\nand approval gate behind the answer"],[860,"Assertions come from `tests/test_helpers.sh`: `fail`, `assert_eq`, `assert_match`,\n`assert_not_match`, `assert_file_exists`, `assert_file_not_exists`, `load_shell_function`.\n`load_shell_function` is the one harness place that expands a function body (it is how a\nsingle entry-CLI function gets unit-tested); production scripts still never use `eval`."],[1004,"At top level the render sets `disable_web_search = true`, which parks Grok's native\n`web_search` tool: no transport this box ships serves that tool type, so the\nself-hosted `searxng` MCP server is the search lane. The search lane section below\ncarries the measurement and how to flip the key back."],[1104,"Audit and drift"],[1105,"Auth"],[1618,"Authelia"],[1802,"Auto-compaction always fired at about 75 per cent of the 262144-token window, the\nsetting in force when this was measured: all 124 `auto_compact_started` events carried\n`tokens_used` between 196,636 and 214,585. Both template twins now ship\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` at 70, so a future sweep measures the\nevents firing at a lower point than this."],[226,"Autostart, autostop and TTLs are template *metadata*, so they stay out of the Terraform\n(where they would fight the UI) and travel on the CLI after pushing."],[54,"Autostop and TTLs"],[194,"Autostop is metadata, not Terraform. Re-pin it with `templates edit` after a push made\nfrom the checkout root, or on the dev stack swap `-p evo-t1-coder-stack` for\n`-p evo-t1-dev --env-file dev-stack.env`."],[88,"Autostop, TTLs, Docker-in-Docker, and the DinD sidecar failure modes"],[792,"awk is POSIX-subset and single-purpose: a program in `scripts/lib/*.awk` takes its\ninput in `ARGV`, its configuration in `ENVIRON`, and prints tab-separated fields the\ncaller reads line by line. The types are that line shape, and they are documented in\nthe program's header block — see `scripts/lib/tf_types.awk` for the shape to copy.\nBats suites are bash and follow the same rules as their shell."],[1071,"Backgrounded and polled because `execute` waits only a short window per tool call\n(about a minute), so a synchronous foreground `grok -p` would die mid-run on any big\ntask. Coder Agents then reviews the delegated diff, runs the project's tests, and\ncommits. Subagents spawned by Coder Agents cannot create workspaces: provisioning\ndecisions stop at the top-level chat."],[996,"Baked in at `/usr/local/bin/grok`, with an `agent` link beside it, **outside** the\n`/home/coder` home volume, so a rebuilt volume cannot lose it."],[787,"Bash has no type system, and no annotation comment buys one. What the repo does\ninstead is put the checking where the language can actually be checked:"],[755,"Bash, awk, and Bats"],[762,"Bash, awk, and Bats: what an untyped language owes instead of annotations"],[500,"Bazelisk as the ingress for tests, lint and the operator CLIs, and what the runfiles layout means for a script you just added."],[518,"Bazelisk is how this repo runs tests, lint, and the operator CLIs. The Makefile targets call\nthe same commands. test-with-bazel.md covers the Bats harness;\n../branching.md the branch rules that CI guards;\ndevcontainer.md the container that carries the toolchain."],[1591,"Bazelisk is the only ingress, with one exception: `scripts/setup-host.sh`. That script is\nthe **stack host's** bootstrap — it installs Bazelisk, the shell/Starlark lint toolchain\n(shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier) and Terraform — so it has to run before Bazel exists, and\nstays the single documented `bash scripts/setup-host.sh`. On a laptop you do not run it:\nthe contributor container or `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image` brings the same pinned\ntoolchain in from `images/dev/` instead\n(contribute/devcontainer.md). `--repair` is the other half of\nthat exemption: leftover Docker bind-mounts in Bazel's runfiles tree make\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` die creating `prod-stack.runfiles` (`Permission\ndenied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`) before any stack script can run.\nOn Linux it also writes a systemd-resolved drop-in (`1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8`) and\nruns `tailscale set --accept-dns=false` so host `git pull` still resolves\n`github.com` when MagicDNS is the only nameserver."],[1535,"Because git and `gh` consult `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` before the stored login, an expired token\npair from an old refresh can shadow a working credential. Every call tries the environment first\nand, on refusal, retries once with the pair cleared and says so in a `warning:`. Re-mint with\n`coder external-auth access-token github`."],[683,"Before a large edit: the twin copies, the filegroup, the map key, the test a new function owes"],[670,"Before a large edit: what moves with this file?"],[2074,"Before any edit, answer five questions and keep the answers in a scratch file:"],[1550,"Before you reach for a hammer"],[908,"Before you start"],[24,"Below the subject leave one empty line, then the bullet points, and leave an empty line\nbetween every two bullets, so each bullet is its own paragraph. The same rule is in\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, which is the text every workspace seeds into\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, so a commit written by an agent and one written by hand read the\nsame way in `git log`."],[1110,"Bootstrap and skills"],[1205,"Bootstrap options, including `--data` and `--force`:"],[924,"Bootstrap the toolchain — the only step that runs before Bazel exists, because it is what\ninstalls Bazelisk:"],[1874,"Both `ls` paths present but no `<name>` entry: the loader dropped the file. Frontmatter\nmust start on line 1, so anything above the opening `---` silently kills the skill."],[1089,"Both `main.tf` files deliberately set **no** `CODER_AGENT_EXP_*` env keys on\n`coder_agent.main`: those keys *replace* the agent's discovery defaults, and the\ndefaults already cover exactly the seeded paths - the `~/.coder` roots plus a\nworking-dir-relative `.agents/skills`, which resolves inside the agent's working\ndir (`/home/coder` in `workspace`; in `devcontainer` with the toggle on, the\ncloned folder under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, where a dev container built on\nthe sidecar carries its own home and no seeded path is visible from inside it\nanyway)."],[1810,"Both `templates/*/main.tf` twins carry `GROK_COMPACTION_WALL_CLOCK_SECS = \"1200\"` in\nthe agent `env` merge, next to `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`. The CLI reads that budget\nfrom the environment only (resolver\n`crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/util/config/resolve/compaction.rs`, remote-settings\nkey `compaction_wall_clock_budget_secs`); no TOML table accepts it, so a line pasted\ninto `grok-config.toml` is silently ignored, the same trap `[mcp] max_output_bytes`\nalready documents. It is honoured: setting it to `1` makes `/compact` fail with\n`exceeded wall-clock budget 1s`. Push, then stop and start the workspace so the new\nagent env lands:"],[1302,"Both doors authenticate with the same `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` value."],[1371,"Both float across the healthy boxes by load."],[522,"Both give you the GNU userland the gate runs on, which a macOS laptop cannot promise\nnatively. Neither replaces the bootstrap below — that one is for the stack host."],[1863,"Both of these answer nothing in that case; the first is the one to type:"],[130,"Both the preset descriptions and the two routing hints fit the same 128-byte ceiling.\n`coder_parameter` descriptions are **not** capped (some run past 450 characters) and are\nnot checked."],[786,"Both twins conform today: every `variable` carries a `type` and a `description`, and\nneither uses `any`, `list(any)`, or a bare container. `//tests:bats_tf_types_test`\nholds that, with fixtures that prove the gate still reports each finding rather than\nhaving quietly stopped looking."],[366,"Both twins pin the editor release in one `code_server_version` local in their own\n`main.tf`, and the script hands that value to the upstream installer as\n`--version`. Without the flag `https://code-server.dev/install.sh` follows\n`latest`, so a release nobody reviewed would reach every new workspace with no\ncommit in this repo - the same reason the Registry module was forked out. The two\nlocals carry the same value and `//tests:bats_coder_apps_test` fails a drift. To\nbump: read `https://github.com/coder/code-server/releases`, edit both locals,\n`bazelisk test //tests:bats_coder_apps_test`, `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`,\nthen restart the workspaces. The pin is a template value and deliberately stays out\nof `images/dev/tool-versions.env`, where every key has to be an `images/dev/Dockerfile`\n`ARG` and this tool ships in no image at all."],[369,"Both twins' `coder_app` opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`. The\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL, in the Web Terminal's `command`, and as a positional\nargument to the code-server binary with `--ignore-last-opened` (path-based apps\ndrop the query string, and without that flag the last restored workspace wins)."],[1367,"Both workspace templates render `x-litellm-session-id = \"ws-<workspace-uuid>\"` under\n`[models]` in `~/.grok/config.toml`, so one key covers every conversation of a\nworkspace, subagent calls included."],[2035,"Branch cleanup cookbook"],[2058,"Branch cleanup is part of the work, not a chore someone does later."],[33,"Branch protection on `main` should require a pull request before pushing, and the two\nBazel checks before merging — `//:test-fast` and `//:lint`, run inside the golden image\nthe workflow builds, so a green check means the suite passed on the Linux toolchain the\nstack actually ships rather than on whatever the runner image carried that day."],[1,"Branching"],[3,"Branching"],[1785,"Bring the TensorFold server up on the box. `<host>` is what `.env` names, and `--apply`\nwrites the per-host override file that server runs:"],[439,"Bringing an old checkout back to a runnable state with one command"],[914,"Bringing the stable stack up and pulling the models"],[1729,"Browser editor (code-server) stays unhealthy -> check these three, in order"],[577,"Build it locally when you want the tag in the local store (the Coder docker provider\nresolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from there and never pulls):"],[499,"Building with Bazel"],[501,"Building with Bazel"],[695,"building-with-bazel.md explains the maps, runfiles, and the daily\ncommands; test-with-bazel.md explains the suites those queries name and the\ntwo inventory gates; ../project-conventions.md the CLI vocabulary this\ntool prints in."],[1921,"Builds still succeed, so verify with a workspace build before acting on it. Nothing\nin this repo's compose file sets the provisioner's Terraform."],[888,"Card `href`s come from `STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` /\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`, which default to LAN URLs built from `STACK_LAN_HOST` and the\nport variables. Change a port or re-detect the LAN IP and the links follow; set `auto`\nin `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` and the cards follow the names bootstrap composes from the\nnaming parts (public-access.md). The Hermes card\nfollows `STACK_HERMES_URL` when bootstrap sets it, and stays on\n`http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` when that key is empty. See hermes.md."],[1427,"Catching a config that looks right and boots dead, before the reboot"],[691,"Catching a payload directory no filegroup claims before the gates stay green by accident"],[766,"Catching a wrong-shaped input at plan time or type-check time rather than in a container"],[1766,"Cause: `/etc/resolv.conf` names only Tailscale MagicDNS (`100.100.100.100`).\nDocker's iptables, and Kasm's privileged nested dockerd, can make that stub\nunreachable. When it is, **every** lookup dies, including `github.com`, so\n`git pull` over HTTPS fails. This is host DNS, not GitHub auth."],[1907,"Cause: `bazelisk run` executes from the runfiles tree, where `templates/**` are\nsymlinks into the execroot. A tar that stores those links unpacks in the\nprovisioner as dangling paths, so Terraform sees no `.tf` files. The two\n`templates/common/` payloads still land (copied with `cp`, which follows\nlinks), which is why the archive is small rather than empty."],[1922,"Cause: `coder/preview` warns when a `coder_app` `count` is not expanded at\nworkspace-create preview. `start_count` is that value. The default is then\nassumed to be 1. The only `coder_app` carrying a `count` today is\n`coder_app.github_app_install`, and it counts on `github_auth_id`, which the\npreview does expand."],[1756,"Cause: `docker compose` resolved `./proxy/homepage.conf` (or another file bind\nmount) next to a compose file that did not have that file. Under `bazelisk run`\nthat file used to be the runfiles copy, which only holds `//:stack_data`. Docker\nthen created a directory at the source path and failed mounting it onto nginx's\n`/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf`, which is a file in the image."],[1910,"Cause: `startup_size_guard` looks up a payload the twin does not keep beside its\nown `main.tf` under `templates/common/`, and it did that by reading an ambient\n`REPO_ROOT`. Nothing in `scripts/` ever sets that variable: the checkout is the\n`root` the script resolved and `cd`'d into at startup. Under the script's own\n`set -u` the read aborts the run. It only fires for a template that inlines a\ncanonical payload, which is why a twin carrying its own copy never showed it."],[1860,"Cause: a pinned download returned an error. The layer prints `curl: (22)` and stops\nrather than shipping a partial image."],[1935,"Cause: an app inside the pane - vim's `+` register, anything that emits OSC 52 -\npushes its copy out through the pane, `set-clipboard on` with\n`allow-passthrough on` forwards it, and code-server's terminal hands the escape to\nnobody. There is no setting for it: the shipped 4.145.0 registers no OSC 52 key,\nand upstream\nmicrosoft/vscode#318254 is\nopen with two unmerged fixes. Over ssh from Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm or iTerm2 the\nsame copy lands, which puts the drop in the browser and not in tmux."],[1760,"Cause: an older `bazelisk run` used the runfiles copy of `docker-compose.yml`, so\ncompose mounted `./homepage/assets/icons` from `bazel-out`. Docker then created a\ndirectory where `evo-t1.svg` belongs, or the homepage container chowned those\nfiles to root. Bazel cannot recreate `prod-stack.runfiles` over that leftover,\nand the stack script never starts, so a repair inside `prod-stack` cannot help."],[1763,"Cause: an older target ran the runfiles copy of the script, so `$(dirname \"$0\")/..`\nwas the runfiles tree, and that tree ships only `//:stack_data`. The working `.env`\nis untracked, so it exists only in the checkout, and the rotation reported a missing\nfile on a host that had one. The path in the message tells the two apart: a\n`bazel-out/.../dashboard-password.runfiles/...` path is this entry. The same bug had\n`pull-models` pulling its default list instead of the checkout's `OLLAMA_MODELS`,\n`coder-agents` provisioning the compose placeholder `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` instead of the\ncheckout's, and `spark-configure` / `spark-verify` writing `sparks/` output into a tree\nBazel recreates."],[1919,"Cause: an upstream pin. The Coder image ships its own provisioner binary at\n`/usr/local/bin/terraform` (1.15.5 in `coder:v2.36.6`) and Coder caps the version it\nwas tested against."],[1881,"Cause: both are properties of the environment. Neither points at a workspace that\nneeds repair, and neither is fixable from inside the container."],[1904,"Cause: both templates inline `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` URL on `coder_app.code_server`. Coder v2.36 stores\n`workspace_apps.icon` as `varchar(256)`. A multi-kilobyte SVG produces a data\nURL thousands of characters long, and the insert fails. The provisioner\nreports it at `insert agent app: upsert app`."],[1989,"Cause: Coder injects `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` into the agent environment when the\nworkspace starts. That GitHub App user-to-server token expires about an hour later and\nthe injected copy is never refreshed for the life of the workspace. `gh` consults that\npair before it reads its own auth store, so it sends the dead token while the store\nbeside it, re-minted by the `gh_token_refresh` coder_script, is healthy and unreachable."],[1924,"Cause: Coder runs every `coder_script` as `bash -c`. HCL heredocs in `main.tf` only\nescape `$${name}` to `${name}`; `$$((` is two literal dollars. The editor wait loop\nthen becomes `waited=<pid>((waited + 1))`, which is the syntax error. The echo\nbefore the loop still prints. `already cloned, skipping` on the next line is the\nconcurrent clone stage, not the failure."],[1888,"Cause: Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root` 755. The\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume is empty on first use (the image has no\nfiles there to copy in), so `coder` cannot write `settings.json`. `jq` and\n`python` then fail because the file was never created. `already cloned, skipping`\non the next line is informational: the clone already exists."],[1942,"Cause: each client attached to a tmux session is a full xterm.js in its own\nChrome tab re-parsing the same stream, each with a scrollback buffer\npreallocated from `terminal.integrated.scrollback` (VS Code preallocates the\nbuffer from the value, and xterm.js keeps 3 `Uint32` words per column per\nline), plus a per-client encode in the tmux server and a re-wrap repaint for\nevery client each time any of them activates or resizes. Count the clients\nagainst the panes:"],[1998,"Cause: Hermes v0.22.5 refuses a non-loopback bind when no\n`DashboardAuthProvider` is registered. The bundled basic-auth plugin skips\nregistration when the password is empty. Compose interpolation\n`${HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD:-}` (empty default) did that after a pull that\nadded the Hermes keys: `.env` never gained them, so `prod-stack up` without\n`bootstrap` / `update-configs` crash-looped the gateway. The logs repeat\n\"no auth providers are registered\" and do not mention `plugins.disabled`."],[1870,"Cause: no published source names an upstream `/learn`, so there is none to miss. The\n`xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of docs.x.ai, every\nfirst-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog all carry zero occurrences of it, so there is no\npublished copy to vendor in. The memory family\n`/ez-learn` copies (`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory`) is a different case:\nthose are upstream commands the CLI ships itself - `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` are\n*built-in* registry entries gated behind cross-session memory, which is off by default\nhere, and `/remember` is a pager command with no such gate. The CLI does download its\nbundled platform skills at runtime (`GROK_SKILLS_BASE_URL`, `cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`)\nand that route needs an x.ai sign-in: from a workspace `api.x.ai` answers 404 and\n`cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`\nanswers 401 anonymously, and a session started with `--sandbox off` still leaves\n`~/.grok/bundled/` uncreated. LiteLLM is the sole model transport here, there is no\nx.ai credential, and the fetch never runs. So each slash command this stack ships is its\nown seed, written at workspace start by `coder_script.stack_skills` into\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` with a twin copy at\n`~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, and a missing command means that seed is\nmissing, not that the image is stale. Each carries its own `ez-` name to stay clear of\nthe upstream names: a user-scope skill overrides a same-named bundled one, so a seed\nnamed `create-skill` would hide the CLI's bundled platform skill of that name on any\nCLI that does sign in."],[1779,"Cause: no Spark box was reachable, so the Arc answered, and the Arc path returns tool\ncalls as plain text."],[1926,"Cause: path-based Coder apps drop the `?folder=` query before the browser sees\nit, and code-server restores the last opened workspace (often `/home/coder`).\nThe clone itself is fine (`already cloned, skipping` is not a failure)."],[1900,"Cause: provisionerd runs `terraform init` inside the `coder` container.\nDocker's embedded DNS (`127.0.0.11`) forwards names it does not know to\nthe host resolver. When that resolver is Tailscale MagicDNS only, or\nDocker/Kasm iptables has made `100.100.100.100` unreachable from a\ncontainer, every public lookup SERVFAILs. The host CLI is fine; only the\nprovisioner job dies. The lockfile reuse lines (`Reusing previous version\nfrom the dependency lock file`) do not skip the registry: Terraform still\nfetches the discovery document."],[1816,"Cause: that chat has no model config on this deployment, so there is nothing to route to."],[1862,"Cause: that container started before the CLI was baked into the image, or its\nstart-time download failed."],[1886,"Cause: that container was built from an image predating the Docker client toolchain,\nor from the bare-Ubuntu *Minimal shell* preset."],[1896,"Cause: that image is not in the host's local image store, and Terraform never pulls\nit. The `docker_image` data source only inspects the store."],[1771,"Cause: that target used to read `.env` with `. ./.env`, so the shell parsed the\noperator's file as code. An unquoted value that holds a space is a variable\nprefix followed by a COMMAND, and `set -e` ended the run on that line before a\nsingle pull:"],[1797,"Cause: the auto-compaction summary hung before its first token and nothing abandoned it.\nThe CLI's 300 s wall-clock budget does not abort a call in flight - it is evaluated only\nafter the response returns - so at 300 s its entire effect was to reject a summary that\nhad already streamed in and make the session generate it again. Meanwhile the proxy\nre-dispatched every hung attempt to the same session-pinned Spark box, so one hang cost\nup to three `request_timeout` periods. Both halves are fixed here."],[1940,"Cause: the browser exposes `navigator.clipboard` to a secure context only, and\nCoder's LAN door is plain HTTP by default (`CODER_ACCESS_URL` is the LAN\naddress). Opened that way the editor has no clipboard API to call, so\nright-click paste stops with VS Code's own toast - \"This browser doesn't support\nthe clipboard.readText API needed to trigger a paste, try Ctrl+V instead.\" - and\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` only works at all through the browser's native paste event. A\nClipboard site permission that was once denied does the same damage on an https\norigin, and there VS Code answers `NotAllowedError: Read permission denied`."],[2012,"Cause: the Chat tab is a WebSocket on `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`. Hermes sends a\nkeepalive every 20 seconds. Close code `4403` is the Host / Origin / peer guard."],[1915,"Cause: the CLI is talking to a public subdomain, and Authelia answers its API calls\nwith the sign-in page."],[1823,"Cause: the LiteLLM alias behind that model has no deployment the router will use. The\nArc aliases (`chat`, `coder`, `coder-fast`) go unhealthy the moment their weights are\nnot in the IPEX-LLM container, which answers `model \"...\" not found, try pulling it\nfirst`, and the Spark `agent` aliases go unhealthy when no box answers."],[1958,"Cause: the refresh script inherits `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` from the agent, and\n`gh auth login --with-token` refuses stdin while those names are set (`The value of the\nGH_TOKEN environment variable is being used for authentication`). Current templates\nunset the pair, log out, then log in with `--git-protocol https`, and print gh's\nstderr (token redacted) if that still fails - which is the line above."],[1892,"Cause: the sidecar is unhealthy, its image was never pulled, or the workspace was\ncreated with DinD off."],[2004,"Cause: this box's `router` answers `421` when the Host matches no `server_name`.\nThe hermes vhost is not typed by hand: `STACK_ROUTER_CONF` in `.env` names the conf\ncompose mounts (`proxy/router.gen.conf`, which bootstrap renders from\n`proxy/router.conf.template`, or the checked-in `proxy/router.conf` while that key is\nempty), and every `server_name` in it is composed from the naming parts. Under\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` a missing or mistyped part is what produces a name nothing\nanswers. Traefik pointed at `:9119` never hits that file. A rewritten Host also\nmisses every block."],[948,"Caveats, honestly"],[967,"Changing a model alias in one place and knowing which workspaces see it"],[93,"Changing a template variable without guessing which file owns it"],[1018,"Changing the mode needs a **rebuild** - the nested mount either exists or it does\nnot. A workspace that goes from `shared` to `private` starts on an empty profile:\nnothing is copied, and the shared volume is left alone."],[1060,"chat model configs `agent` (context limit 262144, set as the default; LiteLLM bakes\n`reasoning_effort` `xhigh`), `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` (same\nlane with a baked thinking level, not default), and `chat` (32768), for models not\nalready registered. `coder` and `coder-fast` stay out on purpose: on this stack's\nIPEX-LLM runtime those weights return tool calls as plain text with no\n`tool_calls`, and an agent turn without callable tools cannot browse or edit, so\nregistering them would only add a silently broken model to the pickers.\n`context_limit` is the agent's own compaction budget (a 70 per cent default\ncompaction threshold applies), which is why it is pinned per alias.\nBefore that sweep the script asks the proxy `GET /v1/model/info` and `GET /health`\nand joins them on `model_id`, so it knows which aliases still have a healthy\ndeployment. An alias with none is not registered: `chat` goes unhealthy the moment\n`qwen2.5:7b` is not pulled on the Arc, and a dead entry in the model picker is a\nchat that errors on the first send. `/health` is read without\n`?check_deployments=true` because that form fires a live completion per deployment\nand evicts the Spark prompt caches the health-check notes in `litellm/config.yaml`\nwarn about. The gate fails open: a proxy that will not answer leaves every alias\nregisterable, and `CODER_AGENTS_LIVE_ALIASES=\"agent chat\"` overrides it outright."],[1070,"Chat on the Agents page -> if the ask needs more tooling than chat has, Coder Agents\nprovisions or uses a workspace through `execute` -> big multi-file tasks are\n*delegated inside* that workspace: a headless Grok Build CLI run, started backgrounded\nand polled:"],[1376,"Chat stays usable while agent mode quietly loses its tools. That is the point of the\nhop, not an accident."],[1741,"Chat tab never connects, or the socket closes `4403` -> public URL, trusted proxies, or a dropped Upgrade"],[1928,"Check 1: the code-server install script's log is green in the workspace's Scripts/Log\ntab."],[1929,"Check 2: this answers from inside the workspace:"],[1930,"Check 3: the browser is on `https://` with a trusted cert. Plain `http://` is an\ninsecure context, which is what breaks `crypto.randomUUID()`; see \"Public subdomains\n(optional)\" in README.md."],[197,"Check it in the Coder UI afterwards: **Default autostop** must read *Disabled* (0 h)."],[234,"Check it in the Coder UI: the template editor's **Default autostop** reads *Disabled*\n(0 h), and a workspace's **Schedule** tab shows **Enable Autostop** unchecked."],[1395,"Check that `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY` and `SPARK_3_API_KEY` are still\nnon-empty. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy, before it ever reaches\nthe box."],[1617,"Checking the other machine"],[1625,"Checking the other machine (including the Hermes WebSocket), Authelia, and the two follow-ups left by hand"],[635,"Checks"],[2113,"Cherry-picking is for a coherent run of commits you want verbatim, and `-x` keeps the source SHA\nin the message so the provenance survives:"],[91,"Choosing between the plain container and the devcontainer before you click create"],[633,"Chrome that arrives on its own"],[7,"CI and protection"],[2093,"Climb as far as you need and stop."],[1369,"Cline and Roo editor traffic carries no session id: the extension settings have no\ncustom-header field."],[1043,"Cline and Roo/Kilo are pre-wired to the LiteLLM proxy (endpoint from `litellm_url`,\ndefault `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`; model alias `agent`, with\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`/`coder`/`coder-fast`/`chat` listed in\n`__evoT1StackModels`). The seed is\n`settings.json.tftpl`, copied onto the per-owner volume\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` (mounted at `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`) only\nwhile that file is absent, and symlinked from\n`~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json`. Startup chowns that volume to\nthe home owner before the first write (empty named volumes are `root:root`\n755). Edits stick, and every workspace\nthat owner has shares the file; delete it to copy the template in again. From the\nhost it is the volume's `settings.json`:"],[60,"Clone and build"],[72,"Cloning as the workspace user"],[79,"Cloning more than one repository"],[1632,"Cloudflare proxies the name (orange cloud) in front of Traefik. WebSockets are on\nthere by default, which is what the Hermes Chat tab needs."],[374,"code-server's User settings file is a symlink to that path, so every workspace that\nowner has opens the same file, and an edit - in any workspace, or from the host by\nmounting the volume - is not overwritten on the next start. Delete the file to reseed\nfrom the template."],[950,"Coder Agents (control-plane)"],[1699,"Coder Agents chat refuses every prompt, or its model dropdown is empty -> no model config"],[1370,"Coder Agents chats carry none either. Coder's aibridge forwards model calls with the\nprovider key only and adds no per-conversation session id."],[1098,"Coder Agents is control-plane-native. This stack does not wrap Grok Build: the wiring\nabove *teaches* (skill + instructions) and *equips* (the workspace-side CLI with its\nLiteLLM alias) the native Coder agent to drive Grok Build when work is too big to\nhand-drive. Delegation is a taught habit, not the product."],[1700,"Coder Agents offers a model that errors, or a chat never gets a title -> an alias with no healthy deployment"],[965,"Coder Agents on the control plane and the model config it needs"],[2033,"Coder and template parameters"],[1743,"Coder login problems -> the admin account, and `CODER_ACCESS_URL`"],[228,"Coder reads the template's **Default autostop** (its default TTL) when it seeds a\nworkspace. That field also decides whether the create form's **Enable Autostop**\narrives ticked, and it ships at 24 h - which is how a workspace would otherwise start\nwith a shutdown deadline nobody chose."],[1474,"Coder side"],[1046,"Coder v2.36.6 ships *Coder Agents*: a coding agent that lives in the Coder **server** -\na chat UI and an API beside your workspaces, not inside one. A chat runs its tools\n(`read_file`, `edit_files`, `execute`, `spawn_agent`) against a workspace over that\nworkspace's agent connection, and consults two pieces of per-workspace context first:\nan **instructions** file and any **skills** it discovers. The agent itself is upstream\nCoder, not a home-grown agent."],[36,"Coder workspace templates"],[38,"Coder workspace templates"],[933,"Coder workspace templates — what the two templates build and which\nvariable does what."],[1252,"Coder workspace templates owns the templates, the create form and\nthe variables; Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal\nowns what runs inside the container once it is up."],[1067,"Coder's chat dies at its context budget when `compaction` and `title_generation` both\nsit on a dead `chat` alias. `bazelisk run //scripts:coder-agents -- --apply` moves the\nstale pin, as above."],[1064,"Coder's optional AI Gateway proxy stays off - LiteLLM remains the model transport,\nwhich is why no AI-Gateway keys exist in `docker-compose.yml`."],[1112,"Commands"],[6,"Commits, and when they happen"],[1152,"Compose supplies the provider: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_USERNAME`,\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_PASSWORD` and `HERMES_DASHBOARD_BASIC_AUTH_SECRET`,\ninterpolated from `.env` with the same non-empty fallbacks `.env.sample`\ncarries (`admin` / `change-me-hermes` / `change-me-hermes-secret`). An empty\ninterpolation default is a boot error on `0.0.0.0`: the plugin skips\nregistration and the gateway refuses to bind."],[873,"Config files"],[1262,"Config keys that gate all of this"],[1409,"Configure the stack host so a reboot with an encrypted root can be answered from a laptop, and prove the boot image can do it before you reboot."],[1703,"Context7 or GitHub tools missing in Grok Build -> parked MCP block, or a handshake with nothing to authenticate"],[550,"Contributor environment"],[552,"Contributor environment"],[941,"Copy and paste between a tmux pane and the machine you type on"],[1938,"Copy and paste both dead, in both directions, a drag included: read the next\nentry. This one is a pane-app-only symptom, and a broken origin breaks the\neditor's own chords too."],[1731,"Copy and paste both do nothing in VS Code Web -> the page is not a secure context"],[851,"Copy this shape; it is the convention every suite already uses (`use_stubs` prints nothing\nand shadows PATH for the current test only, and fails when a stub is missing):"],[845,"coverage.py resolves the symlink and records the **checkout** path, while the\nprocess cwd is the runfiles tree. A relative `--include=scripts/lib/*.py` is\nmatched against the cwd, matches nothing, prints `No data to report`, and exits\n`0`. The gate therefore decides from `tests/coverage/py_report.py`, which lists\nthe `.py` files the tree ships and requires each one to appear in the machine-\nreadable report above the floor. A module nobody imported is a named failure."],[1869,"Covers all three stack-shipped skill commands: `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify` and\n`/ez-create-skill`. The diagnosis and the commands below are identical for each one; only\nthe name changes, so read `<name>` as whichever of the three you are chasing."],[896,"CPU, memory, CPU temperature and disk are host-wide through `/proc`. A network widget\nonly sees the container's own netns (`lo`/`eth0`) - `/sys/class/net` is namespaced, so\nthe host Wi-Fi NIC stays invisible even with `/sys` mounted. That tile reads `eth0`."],[931,"Create the first workspace. One repository per workspace is the default, and the template\npicks itself from the URL:"],[923,"Create the one file that holds real secrets, and lock it down before anything else reads\nit:"],[279,"Create the workspace it picks:"],[52,"Create-form fields"],[916,"Creating the first workspace from a git URL"],[47,"Credentials"],[607,"Credentials still arrive by the one supported path: Coder External Auth mints them, and\nthe gh auth store is what git actually reads. That store is the target because\n`GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` are the copy injected when the container started and expire\nabout an hour later, and the plain `!gh auth git-credential` helper echoes that pair, so\ngit would keep sending a dead token. The helper this stack installs clears the pair first,\nwhich is why `gh auth setup-git` alone is not enough. There is no static token and no PAT\nin this repo's tooling."],[21,"Cut `feat/…`, `fix/…`, `refactor/…` (and `docs/…`, `test/…`, `chore/…`, `ci/…`) from the\ncurrent tip of `main`, one branch per change, and delete it the moment it merges. A branch\nthat outlives its change is a branch nobody rebases any more."],[504,"Daily commands"],[867,"Dashboard and desktops"],[869,"Dashboard and desktops"],[1128,"Dashboard on host port **9119**, from `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PORT`. The dev sandbox shifts\nit to **19119** the same way it shifts every other published port."],[1546,"Day-to-day operation — the dashboard and desktops, what to refresh after a git pull, the public shape, and the symptom-first fix list."],[94,"Deep reference for `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/`: what they build,\nhow they get pushed, what each template variable means, and how the create form and the\nin-editor terminal and editor behave."],[37,"Deep reference for `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/`: what they build, how they get pushed, and what every variable means."],[1297,"Deep reference for the model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request\npicks a backend, what a pinned conversation does, and what happens when a Spark box goes\ndown."],[970,"Deep reference for what runs *inside* a workspace and in the Coder control plane: the\nterminal profiles, the per-user `~/.grok` profile, where the CLI and the chat agent get\ntheir models, and what the stack seeds for the agent."],[1753,"Deep, symptom-first list. The operational `dev-stack`/`prod-stack`, `search-check` and\n`spark-verify` targets are described in README.md and\nproject-conventions.md."],[720,"Default scope reads `git diff HEAD`; add `--staged` for the index, `--untracked` to count files git\ndoes not track yet (a file you just created is exactly the case worth covering), or `--ref <ref>` for\nwhat this branch adds over a base. `--all` also lists the aggregate suites that reach everything\n(`//:test`, `//:test-fast`, `//:test-all` and `//tests:bats`), which is useful when you want the full\nset named explicitly rather than the leaf suites you can invoke. The default already includes the\nlint targets: every test rule here, `//lints:shell` and `//lints:shfmt` among them, is an `sh_test`."],[2048,"Deleting the `development` branch without losing a commit that was only ever on it"],[1534,"Dependabot **secrets** are a different permission that this App does not currently hold, which is\nwhy the CLI does not attempt them. When a step is refused the CLI prints the endpoint, GitHub's\nmessage, and the permission to ask for, then re-mints nothing and continues."],[2049,"Deploying dev, staging and production from one commit instead of three builds"],[241,"Details that matter:"],[1206,"Dev sandbox, which puts the dashboard on 19119:"],[74,"Dev-container preflight"],[936,"Develop and\nBranching — the gates a change passes on its way back to `main`."],[1296,"Diagnosing a wrong-backend answer from the routing rather than from the client"],[1846,"Discover live tool names with `search_tool`, then call `use_tool` with that\nschema. Do not hardcode MCP tool names from memory."],[2134,"Discovery reloads from disk, so the copy above needs a new turn before the slash command\nappears. The run ends with the report the skill specifies: summary, criteria table, branch\ndecisions, environment mapping, flags introduced, promotion commands, deletions pending\nconfirmation, and next manual steps."],[1678,"DNS records for all five names, each one label deep and Cloudflare-proxied (orange\ncloud). WebSockets are on there by default. A missing record shows up as a DNS\nerror, a rewritten Host as `421`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and LiteLLM resolve\nthat way and complete the TLS handshake, checked from this box; the Hermes\nname is the same zone and the same proxy."],[621,"Do not copy the sentinel into another repository. An application repo's\n`.devcontainer/` is exactly what `new-workspace` is supposed to find, and the sentinel in\nfront of it silently downgrades that workspace to the plain template."],[1984,"Do not hand-roll a credential helper. Measured across 222 past sessions in this\nworkspace: 29 hit a GitHub auth error (`Bad credentials` 19, `Password authentication is\nnot supported` 11, `could not read Username` 5, `unrecognized subcommand` 4), and agents\nhand-rolled their own askpass scripts in 5 sessions and hand-edited `credential.helper`\nin 4. Both were wrong the same way: they fought the credential-helper precedence that\n`fix` repairs, and they put a live token where `ps` could read it, while the line that\nnames the real cause was one `check` away. `fix` is idempotent, keeps every token off\nargv and out of any file it creates, and repairs the helper plus the store it reads."],[2029,"Docker Compose"],[259,"Docker creates the missing host directory itself (root-owned) and `startup.sh.tftpl`\nchowns it."],[55,"Docker-in-docker"],[1714,"Docker-in-docker"],[1748,"Docker-in-Docker, Terraform, and the host `coder` CLI"],[1601,"Dollar escaping differs across those two layers. A `templatefile()` `.tftpl` treats `$$`\nas an escaped `$` everywhere (`$name` or `$${name}`), and leaves `$((` arithmetic\nalone. An HCL heredoc in `main.tf` (`<<-EOT`) only escapes `$${name}` to `${name}`;\na lone `$` is already literal, so shell arithmetic is `$((...))` with one dollar.\n`$$((` is two dollars, and Coder runs `coder_script` bodies as `bash -c`, which\nexpands `$$` to the PID. Do not wrap `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."],[278,"Dry run (decision only, creates nothing):"],[574,"Each `KEY` there is also an `ARG` in `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and the `ARG` default\nmirrors the pin, so a bare `docker build` matches a scripted one."],[541,"Each CLI target also declares the repo files that script reads by path: the libs it\nsources (`_SCRIPT_LIBS`) and the payload groups whose files it opens (`_SCRIPT_PAYLOAD`),\nboth keyed by script stem in `scripts/BUILD.bazel`. Those payload groups live one per\npayload directory (`//litellm:config`, `//searxng:settings`, `//homepage:config`,\n`//homepage:icons`, `//hermes:seed`, `//hermes:skills`, `//proxy:confs`,\n`//images/dev:dockerfile`, `//images/dev:tool_versions`, and a `:payload` in each of\n`templates/common`, `templates/workspace`, `templates/devcontainer`), with the root\nBUILD.bazel holding the groups that have no directory of their own (`//:compose_file`,\n`//:env_sample`, `//:env_example`, `//:license`, `//:devcontainer`, `//:githooks`, and the\n`//:ci_*` groups). `//:stack_data` is the explicit aggregate of all of them, and\n`//scripts:doc_sources` (every `scripts/**.sh`, so a sourced `scripts/lib/*.sh` resolves\ntoo) rides along on every CLI. `proxy/certs/` is `.bazelignored` (bootstrap issues those on the host), so a\n`bazelisk run` of a stack runner, of `push-template`, or of the six CLIs that read a file the\ncheckout alone holds (`dashboard-password`, `pull-models`, `coder-agents`, `spark-verify`,\n`spark-configure`, `build-dev-image`) prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout)\nover the runfiles copy as the compose project directory, the live `.env`, and the template tree (runfiles\n`templates/**` are symlinks that Coder cannot follow after unpack). Without the committed payloads a\nsandboxed run would read a partial copy of the repo and fail for the wrong reason. A new\ntop-level payload directory gets its own package with its own `filegroup` targets, joins\n`//:stack_data` by label, and is named in the map of every target that reads it: `test-fast`\nand `lint` only see repo files that travel in runfiles, so an unlisted directory is invisible\nto the harness rather than checked loosely."],[1754,"Each fence below is one copy-paste action. A heading quotes the error string where there\nis one, so start with `grep -n` on the text you saw."],[335,"Each file also gets a twin in the `~/.agents` tree, because the `execute`-lane delegation\nthis stack teaches starts `grok -p` inside the workspace and that CLI reads the\n`~/.agents` convention. The chat agent would only ever take the `~/.coder` copies - skill\ndedupe is first-match-wins, home scope before working-dir paths - so one agent never\nloads a skill twice; the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."],[1507,"Each lane lives at `<scaffold>/.template-lanes/<lane>/`. A file there is copied over the rendered\ntree at the same path, so a lane may add files or replace a core one. `LANE.md` and\n`gitignore.fragment` are lane metadata: the renderer appends the fragment to `.gitignore` and drops\nboth from the output. Ask for a lane the fetched tree does not have and the run dies naming the\nlanes it does offer."],[1801,"Each link in the chain, measured:"],[651,"Each page writes its own `# H1`, once, as the first heading of the body, spelled exactly like\nthe frontmatter `title`. That heading is the one the table of contents and every `#anchor`\npoint at, so `DocsH1` prints it and then the description beneath it as the lead paragraph. A\npage whose first heading is not its title keeps a plain heading and gets no lead paragraph,\nwhich is the visible sign that the two strings drifted apart."],[1090,"Each seeded file carries the stack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack`\ncomment, or the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` / `__evoT1Stack = true` key in a JSON or TOML\nfile - in `SKILL.md` directly under the `---` frontmatter, since the agent's skill\nparser requires frontmatter on line 1 and silently drops anything else. Delete a file\nto take it over; edit it and the stack leaves it alone. `~/.grok/config.toml` is the\none seed where deleting a file is not the way: it needs both marker lines gone, see\nThe rendered `~/.grok` config. Either way the marker\nnever reaches the model: the agent strips HTML comments from injected instruction\ntext."],[486,"Each service name is **one label** under the zone and carries its prefix with a\n**HYPHEN**: a wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so the dotted\ntwo-label form has no certificate at the public edge."],[292,"Each template creates one Docker container per workspace. Extra named volumes\n(`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` at `~/.shared/vscode`, and `grok-profile-<owner-id>`\nat `~/.grok` in shared mode) are chowned to the home owner at the start of\n`startup.sh.tftpl`, because Docker creates an empty named volume as `root:root`\n755 and the agent runs as `coder`."],[101,"Each template dir keeps its own independent copy of the `.tftpl` payloads\n(`startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). Change one twin,\nchange the other."],[998,"Each template's startup script probes `command -v grok` on every start and\nre-downloads from `https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` - bounded, with retries - only\nwhile `grok` is missing from PATH. That installer follows the `stable` pointer, not\nthis pin. It deliberately does **not** fetch on every boot: the bake-in under\n`/usr/local/bin` is the upgrade path, so shipping a new CLI means bumping the pin,\nrebuilding the image (`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`) and re-pushing the templates."],[1377,"Each thinking alias carries the same hop of its own - `agent-low: [\"coder\"]`, and the\nsame for `agent-medium` and `agent-xhigh`. Groups are independent, so none of them\ninherits `agent`'s entry, and all four go down with exactly the same boxes."],[401,"Each workspace's agent injects a `GIT_ASKPASS` helper pointing at the `coder` binary it\nstages, alongside the `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair in the agent environment. Some\nbuilds of that binary answer every prompt with `unrecognized subcommand`, so the stack\ndepends on the helper below rather than on askpass, and `check` prints a note line when\nthat askpass answers nothing."],[1393,"Edit `litellm/config.yaml`."],[1281,"Editing an alias"],[112,"Editing the common copy changes both templates; the staged copy overwrites any\nsame-named file the template dir still carries."],[77,"Editor settings seed"],[1725,"Editor, credentials, the clone"],[1749,"Editor, credentials, the clone, UI logins, and the public names (`421`, Chat `4403`, Traefik)"],[75,"Editor, terminal, and the metadata tiles"],[891,"Either form rewrites `.env`, recreates the container and verifies the new value landed.\nThe script's job is keeping the secret out of shell history, `ps` and git, and leaving\n`.env` at mode 600."],[270,"Either way `folder` is the clone, so code-server and its terminal profiles land on the\nrepository, and the **Web Terminal** button `cd`s there too."],[1787,"Either way keep `SPARK_n_API_KEY` non-empty: `SPARK_1_API_KEY`, `SPARK_2_API_KEY`,\n`SPARK_3_API_KEY`. An empty key fails every request inside the proxy before it ever\nreaches the box."],[1653,"empty is LAN-only;"],[301,"Ensures the **Grok Build CLI** is on PATH. The golden image ships it, so the\n`https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` download runs only where `grok` is missing from PATH -\nthe `ubuntu:24.04` image option, or a home volume that never received the CLI."],[485,"Entries are DNS names, not URLs: no scheme, no port, no path. The entry with no\nprefix is the dashboard."],[676,"Enumerate targets instead of guessing them"],[685,"Enumerating real target names instead of guessing them"],[2091,"Environment is a workspace or an `envs/<env>/` directory that instantiates one shared module;\n`terraform plan` against `envs/staging` and `envs/production` is the same module revision twice.\nArgoCD gets one `Application` per environment, all pointing at the same Kustomize or chart path,\nand promotion is a commit that bumps the image tag (by hand, or by the Image Updater). A\nper-environment ArgoCD branch is a sync-drift generator and a rollback trap."],[34,"Environments are where future protection rules and secrets go, per environment:\n`development`, `staging`, `production`. `production` stays restricted to `main` — or to\ntags — with required reviewers. See the header comment in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`."],[2028,"Environments without environment branches"],[351,"Every `[mcp_servers.*]` block the render writes names its binary by the absolute path the\ngolden image bakes (`/opt/npm-global/bin/...`), so the config and the image are two\nartifacts that have to agree - and they do not always. A workspace running an image built\nbefore that binary was added boots with a lane that can never start, and the CLI only\nsays so in its own MCP log."],[1637,"Every `location` adds `X-Evo-Proxy` with the upstream name, and `always` is what\nmakes it survive a 502. Read it beside `X-Coder-Request-Id`, which Coder's own proxy\nsets on an answered request: neither header means the request stopped at Traefik or\nCloudflare, and `421` means the Host header matched no server block here."],[1587,"Every `scripts/**/*.sh` file starts with a `# ## ` header that names the file and says whether it is an entry point or a sourced library."],[1305,"Every alias is a stable name. A workspace setting never has to change when a backend\nmoves behind one."],[87,"Every create-form field and template variable, with its default"],[456,"Every flag goes **after** the subcommand, so `update-configs --dry-run env` is a\nusage error and prints the usage rather than guessing."],[1588,"Every function carries this block immediately above the definition:"],[790,"Every function in `scripts/` carries a `# @function` block with `Globals:`,\n`Arguments:`, `Outputs:`, and `Returns:`, and each `Arguments:` line states what\nthe parameter is. `//tests:doc_coverage` enforces the block and\n`//tests:shell_inventory` enforces that a test names the function."],[1590,"Every function under `scripts/` is named by an asserting test under `tests/`: one suite per\nmodule, plus one test per entry-level function that drives the whole script (`tests/bats/\nentry-fns.bats` is where those live). `tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a name is\nmissing, and `tests/doc_coverage.sh` when a `# @function` block is missing or incomplete.\nTests are hermetic: docker, ssh, curl, git and the other external tools resolve through\n`tests/bats/tool_stubs`."],[414,"Every one prints slot names, credential kinds and verdicts, and none of them prints a\ntoken. Repo convention: `bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- <subcommand> ...`."],[647,"Every page declares three fields, and `docs-site/tests/unit/docs-content.test.ts` refuses a\npage that is missing any of them:"],[1445,"Every row below is a `check` line you can get instead of an `ok`, and each one\nhas booted dead in the field."],[315,"Every stage from the Coder Agents seeds onward is best-effort: an offline first boot has\nto leave a working terminal, and a repository that will not clone has to leave a\nworkspace someone can fix, so nothing there may abort the script. In `devcontainer` a\nmissing or half-cloned folder is not silent either: `coder_devcontainer` fails on its own\nwith a visible error naming the folder it looked in, and the clone stage warns first when\na clone arrives with no devcontainer configuration in it."],[821,"Every suite under `tests/` is hermetic Bats: it runs against stubs and a throwaway tree,\nnever against a daemon, a network, or the operator's real `.env`."],[769,"Every value has a declared shape at the boundary where it enters a module, and a\nbroad type is a design smell rather than a shortcut. Where a language has a type\nsystem, the strictest setting that compiles is the setting the repo uses. Where it\nhas none, the convention is the gate."],[732,"Every verb is one of these, so a question outside the verb set needs one predicate, not a grep plan.\nAll of these run warm in about 50 to 70 ms."],[1014,"Every workspace gets its own `/home/coder` volume, and `~/.grok` is a **second,\nnested** mount inside it: a Docker volume named `grok-profile-<owner-id>`,\nreferenced by name rather than declared as a Terraform resource, which every\nworkspace of that owner mounts read-write at the same path **while that workspace is\nin `shared` mode**."],[150,"Everything Coder-facing runs **inside the coder container**, against\n`http://127.0.0.1:3000`."],[525,"Everything else is a Bazel target, including every `scripts/*.sh`. CI repeats the gate from\na clean runner inside the same golden image: `.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds\n`images/dev` with buildx and the Actions layer cache and docker-runs the two suites below in\nit, so the bazel job does not install the pins onto the runner at all — the image already\ncarries them. The persistent cache on that path is the buildx GHA layer cache, so a pull\nrequest that leaves `images/dev/**` alone replays the image layers."],[1592,"Everything else is a Bazel target:"],[1557,"Everything else is a target those two feed:"],[1208,"evo-t1-coder-stack"],[1210,"evo-t1-coder-stack"],[1955,"Existing workspaces pick up the buttons after `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`\nand a workspace update to the new template version."],[866,"Exit `5` with one `unresolvable repo root` line beats guessing a cwd and half-running a\nfleet check against a copy of the repo."],[968,"Explaining why one workspace's `~/.grok` disagrees with another's"],[1627,"Exposing the stack without breaking the LAN routes anyone at home uses"],[1598,"External tools are probed with `command -v` and reported as \"not on PATH\", not assumed.\n`require_tool` skips locally and fails under `CI=true`."],[1276,"Fallback: `agent` to `coder`"],[1157,"Fallbacks, in the order worth trying them:"],[1571,"File header"],[399,"Fill every `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*` key or omit them all. A defined-but-empty key\ncrash-loops the server, which is why these keys live in `coder.env` and not in `.env`."],[909,"First boot"],[906,"First boot on a GMKtec EVO-T1, top to bottom — bootstrap the host, bring the stable stack up, register Coder, push the templates, open a workspace."],[1817,"First run this to see the plan, then run it again without the flag to apply it:"],[1898,"Fix with the `docker pull` above, or turn DinD off for that workspace."],[1784,"Fix, one of these:"],[1923,"Fix: `coder_app` resources are not gated on `start_count` (scripts, metadata\nand the container still are). Push the templates; new creates stop showing the\nwarning. Existing workspaces pick it up on the next update to the new template\nversion."],[1901,"Fix: `docker-compose.yml` sets `dns: [1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8]` on the `coder`\nservice (embedded DNS still answers `db` and other compose names). Both\ntemplates pin the same resolvers on the workspace and DinD containers so\na later `git clone` of `github.com` does not hit the same SERVFAIL. Recreate\n`coder` so the new upstreams apply, then push:"],[1757,"Fix: `homepage/**` and `proxy/*.conf` belong in `//:stack_data`, and the stack\nrunners prefer `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout) so relative\nmounts and bootstrap-issued `proxy/certs/` resolve there. Re-run from the stack\nhost:"],[1908,"Fix: `push-template` now prefers `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the live\ncheckout) and stages with `tar -h` so the archive holds regular files. Pull\nthis commit onto the stack host and re-run:"],[1861,"Fix: check that version in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` against the upstream\nrelease, then rebuild the image."],[1999,"Fix: compose interpolation defaults now match `.env.sample`\n(`change-me-hermes` / `change-me-hermes-secret`). Recreate the service:"],[1941,"Fix: open the editor on the TLS public name (the `coder-<host>.<domain>` door\nTraefik holds a real certificate for) rather than `http://<LAN-address>`, and\ngive the site Clipboard permission - Chrome: the lock icon in the address bar,\nSite settings, Clipboard, Allow. The templates are not the variable here: both\ndoors serve the same payload, so a workspace that pastes on one URL and does not\non the other is an origin problem. What still works on plain HTTP: copy with a\ndrag - the selection is the client's own, since tmux asks for no mouse reporting,\nand `copyOnSelection`'s `document.execCommand('copy')` fallback really lands - and\npaste with `Ctrl+V`, the browser's own paste event - which is exactly what VS Code's\ntoast tells you to do."],[1917,"Fix: point the CLI at the container's loopback listener, `http://127.0.0.1:3000`,\nwhich nothing can gate. See \"Coder workspace templates\" in README.md."],[1761,"Fix: recover from a shell, then re-run. `--repair` uses sudo when chmod is not\nenough:"],[1911,"Fix: the guard measures against the resolved `root`, and a parser it cannot open\nis now a hard stop rather than a silent \"everything fits\". Pull this commit onto\nthe stack host and re-run:"],[1925,"Fix: the increment is `waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar). Push the templates and\nrestart so the editor script relaunches."],[1927,"Fix: the inlined fork starts code-server with `--ignore-last-opened` and the\nclone folder as a positional argument (`/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` on the\n`workspace` template). Push the templates and restart so the editor process\nrelaunches."],[1944,"Fix: the seed keeps one pane per visible terminal. `vscode-terminal-tmux`\nreattaches a folder's session only while nothing is attached to it, and opens a\nnumbered sibling (`vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ...) beside live ones, so an\nextra tab is a new pane rather than a mirror. Every session target in that\nhelper carries tmux's `=` exact-match prefix, because a bare name is also\nanswered by any session starting with it - once `foo` is closed, `foo-2` would\notherwise answer for it and put a second client on a live pane. And when tmux\n3.4 does take a session down under a tab that is reopening it, the helper raises\na fresh one rather than letting the tab exit with it. The settings seed drops\n`terminal.integrated.scrollback` to 2000 - the browser keeps a short viewport\nand tmux's `history-limit 100000` behind `Prefix-[` holds the deep history - and\nturns `cursorBlinking` off, and `templates/common/tmux.conf` sets\n`set -s escape-time 10` and `set -g focus-events on`. The first is the freeze:\n3.4 holds back any Escape it reads for 500 ms to decide whether a sequence\nfollows, so Alt and the arrow keys feel like dropped input and a focus-in can\nwait that long - twice, with `set-timing` - before tmux repaints. Upstream cut\nthe default to 10 ms in 3.5, and 0 - the value vim guides suggest - is the one\nto avoid: measured on this build, a throttled tab that delivers Esc and the next\ncharacter more than 10 ms apart loses its Alt chords, and 0 makes that far\nlikelier. `focus-events` is what gives tmux a focused client to work with at\nall. `aggressive-resize` stays at its default on purpose: its own entry in\n`options-table.c` scopes it to the `smallest` and `manual` `window-size` modes,\nthe shipped default is `latest`, and tmux(1) calls it \"poor for interactive\nprograms such as shells\". Both files reseed on the next start only while they\nstill carry the stack marker, so delete either one you edited by hand. Before\ndebugging a setting that will not take: tmux reads `~/.tmux.conf` and then\n`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tmux/tmux.conf` and `~/.config/tmux/tmux.conf`, last file read\nwins, so either of those quietly outranks everything the seed writes.\n`focus-events` is read when a client attaches, so the tabs need to be gone for\nit to land: close them all after the restart and reopen one per pane."],[1905,"Fix: the SVG is a compact ribbon whose data URL fits the column, and\n`push-template` rejects a too-large copy locally before talking to the server.\nPull this commit onto the stack host and re-run:"],[1773,"Fix: the target now reads the one key it needs through `env_get`\n(`scripts/lib/common.sh`), which takes the line as text. Quoted and unquoted\nvalues behave the same, and nothing else in `.env` reaches the pull. A value\nthat is not a model tag - a stray `;`, a leftover quote, a trailing comment - is\nrefused by name before the daemon is asked and before any of the ~35 GB\ndownload starts:"],[1889,"Fix: the template chowns that volume (and the shared `~/.grok` profile volume)\nto the home owner before the seed, and skips `jq`/`python` when the write\nfails. Push the templates and restart the workspace; there is nothing to delete."],[1936,"Fix: there is nothing to turn on in tmux. Copy the text the way the rest of the tab\ndoes - a plain drag is the client's own highlight, because tmux asks for no mouse\nreporting (measured on the shipped tmux 3.4: an attach with `mouse on` writes\n`ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the client, one with it off writes\nnone), and the settings seed's `terminal.integrated.copyOnSelection: true` puts\nthat highlight on the machine clipboard with no chord (the headless harness on\n4.145.0 recorded `navigator.clipboard.writeText` firing on a client selection,\nbefore this switch). Right-click pastes\nit back (`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` pastes when nothing\nis selected) and middle-click pastes it too (`middleClickBehavior` pinned to\n`\"paste\"`). tmux keeps no buffer of its own, so `Prefix-]` is unbound and there is\nno `tmux show-buffer` to read: the machine clipboard is the only clipboard here.\n`Prefix m` is the wheel switch, not a copy route: one press hands the wheel to tmux\nfor a long scroll, the next hands it back. Paste is `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click in\na bash terminal and a tmux pane alike, and `Ctrl+V` is not a paste on a secure\norigin (the pane gets the `\\x16` byte). Both directions, and what a pane app may\nstill do over ssh, are in\nCopy and paste between a tmux pane and the machine you type on."],[1764,"Fix: those six CLIs (`dashboard-password`, `pull-models`, `coder-agents`,\n`spark-verify`, `spark-configure`, `build-dev-image`) resolve the checkout with\n`resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`) before they read or write anything, so\nthey honour `REPO_ROOT` and the `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` that `bazel run` names.\nWhen nothing proves out - no candidate holds `docker-compose.yml` outside a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree - the run exits `5` with one\n`error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line instead of half-running against a copy. Re-run\nfrom the stack host:"],[885,"Fixing a card that reports down when the service itself is up"],[645,"Fixing a docs failure from the assertion that named it"],[425,"Flags"],[1778,"Flags are accepted before or after `logs`; `--project-name` and `--tail` move either\nway. Only `logs` takes trailing service names, and here that trailing word is the\nservice to read."],[706,"Flags, accepted anywhere in argv: `--staged`, `--untracked`, `--ref <ref>`, `--all`,\n`--label-kind`, `--json`, `-h|--help`. Exit codes: 0 when every named thing resolved, 1 on a query\nfailure, 2 on a usage error (an unknown verb or flag, or a verb invoked without the argument it\nneeds)."],[280,"Force the `workspace` template:"],[284,"Found means `devcontainer`; not found means `workspace`. A probe that fails **entirely**\nis also a `workspace` answer, with a warning."],[62,"From a git URL: `bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace`"],[610,"From a machine that is **not** inside the container, ask the image to run them instead of\ninstalling the toolchain onto that machine:"],[1103,"From the host terminal"],[1185,"From the terminal door the same gate is inline in your tty: a dangerous command stops\nwith `[o]nce | [s]ession | [a]lways | [d]eny` and does not proceed until you answer,\nwith deny the default. An approval is scoped to the session you answer in, so a yes\ngiven over there does not cover a fresh session opened at this door.\n`--yolo` and `HERMES_YOLO_MODE` bypass the gate entirely; they have no place here."],[630,"Frontmatter"],[1987,"Full usage, verdict kinds and both argument orders:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- -h`."],[1572,"Functions"],[1218,"Getting oriented in one screen before opening a terminal"],[905,"Getting started"],[907,"Getting started"],[1614,"Getting the naming right"],[517,"Getting the same Bazel, Bats, shellcheck and Terraform that CI uses without installing\nany of them"],[1486,"Getting the same scaffold on a laptop, in CI, and from a workspace"],[1555,"Getting to the right runbook in one click instead of searching the README"],[1734,"gh_token_refresh says gh would not store the minted token"],[404,"git never reads `GH_TOKEN` itself: it asks its credential helpers in order and sends the\n**first** answer. That order is what decides what github.com sees, and the first slot that\nanswers wins outright - everything under it exists but a real push never reaches it, which\nis the whole difference between a credential that works and a push that works."],[348,"git never runs as root, so the credentials it consults - a helper, `~/.git-credentials`,\nssh keys - are the workspace user's. `HOME` is passed explicitly through `env` because\n`runuser` keeps the invoker's environment; a root agent would otherwise hand git `/root`\nand miss those files entirely. An empty prefix means \"run git directly\", which is correct\nfor a non-root agent and for a root agent with no derivable workspace user."],[556,"GitHub auth in the container"],[565,"GitHub auth inside the container, and why `github-auth check` runs SELF there"],[1607,"GitHub auth is Coder External Auth, id `github` by default (Coder's injected\nGitHub App when coder.env is absent). A custom OAuth app in coder.env keeps\nits `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` (commonly `primary-github`); push-template.sh\nforwards that id. There is no GitHub-token template variable and no PAT in\nany agent env map."],[396,"GitHub credentials arrive by one path only: opt-in, and HTTPS-only. This is the canonical\npage for that path: where the token comes from, the order git consults it in, and the four\ncommands that read or repair it. The troubleshooting entries link here rather than\nrepeating it."],[80,"GitHub credentials for a workspace"],[428,"GitHub external auth and coder.env"],[1733,"GitHub token expired, or the workspace has no GitHub account -> GitHub reauth"],[1696,"Grok agent mode makes no tool calls (it narrates and stops) -> an Arc alias served it"],[945,"Grok Build state: one shared profile per user"],[1349,"Grok Build, Cline, Roo and Kilo all default to `agent`."],[937,"Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal"],[939,"Grok Build, Coder Agents, and the workspace terminal"],[1713,"Grok profile missing in a fresh workspace -> startup already finished, so read the log"],[429,"Grok profile mode"],[1357,"Health checks, cooldowns and those counters all attribute per deployment through\n`model_info.id`, which is why each box carries a unique id: `agent-spark-1`,\n`agent-spark-2`, `agent-spark-3`. Each thinking group has its own triple -\n`agent-low-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-medium-spark-1/2/3`, `agent-xhigh-spark-1/2/3` - so the\nsame physical box is a separate deployment, with its own health state and its own\nin-flight counter, inside every group it belongs to."],[1099,"Hermes host assistant"],[1101,"Hermes host assistant"],[1254,"Hermes is the interactive assistant with the checkout, the compose project and\nthe docker socket in reach, gated by approval prompts. Its public name is opt-in with\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto`, which composes it from the naming parts; with that key empty\nthe dashboard stays on the LAN.\npublic-access.md is the page that decides what the box exposes."],[1125,"Hermes is the Nous Research Hermes Agent running as the interactive assistant **on the\nstack host itself**: not inside a workspace, not in a streamed desktop, but with the\ncheckout, the compose project and the docker socket in reach. It adds an official Web\nDashboard with a Chat tab that carries approval prompts, so the assistant can plan a\nchange, show the plan, and wait for you to approve it before it touches anything."],[1646,"Hermes is the same additive hop as the other four. Traefik forwards\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with the original Host, and the router sends that\nHost to `http://hermes:9119`. The LAN URL `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` stays.\nHTTP basic auth on the dashboard stays too; Authelia is the extra public gate.\nSee hermes.md."],[1150,"Hermes since the June 2026 hardening refuses a non-loopback bind without a\n`DashboardAuthProvider`. Binding `0.0.0.0` with no provider is a boot error, not a\nwarning."],[1154,"Hermes talks to the LiteLLM proxy the stack already runs: base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1`, model alias **`agent`**. That is the tool-capable Spark lane,\nwhich is what an agent loop needs, and it stays the default."],[800,"Hermetic Bats: how a suite is discovered, what the stub PATH gives you, and the two inventory gates a new function has to satisfy."],[870,"Homepage dashboard"],[1902,"Host `git pull` still needs a public resolver beside MagicDNS when the host\nitself is MagicDNS-only (heading above; `setup-host --repair` on Linux).\nExisting workspaces pick up the container DNS on the next start after this\ntemplate version is active."],[875,"Host stats, and the docker socket"],[86,"How `push-template.sh` ships the source, and the same push by hand"],[42,"How `push-template` works"],[640,"How abbreviations, tables and diagrams pick up their chrome without a component call"],[2042,"How an environment is expressed without a branch, per stack"],[1695,"How do I know the IPEX-LLM server came up? -> look for `runners=[ipex_llm]`"],[1467,"How scripts/new-repo.sh fetches the ezrepo scaffold from GitHub, renders it into a new repository, and what the Coder side adds."],[1956,"How the credential is assembled, and what every slot in that report is, is one page:\nGitHub credentials for a workspace."],[1239,"How the pieces fit"],[1216,"How the sidebar tabs divide the subjects"],[1246,"How this tab differs from the `/ez-learn` slash command in Grok Build"],[11,"How to cut, name and delete a short-lived work branch, and when the branch and its commits happen"],[1114,"How to reach the dashboard, on the LAN and on the public name"],[1195,"HTTP basic auth stays, including on the public URL. Authelia is the gate in front of\nthat name only. LAN `:9119` and a matching Host on `:8080` bypass Authelia, so the\nbasic-auth provider is load-bearing. It is a provider, not a toggle."],[2026,"Ideal-state checklist"],[1379,"If a concurrency cap is ever reintroduced, its pairing is `fallbacks` plus\n`context_window_fallbacks`. Both, not one."],[1945,"If a tab keeps dying on you and you want to know whether the pane's shell\nexited or tmux took the session down under the tab, run `tmux set -w\nremain-on-exit on` in it first: the pane then stays on screen with its exit\nstatus in `#{pane_dead_status}` instead of vanishing, which is what the\ntmux FAQ recommends for exactly this\nquestion. Turn it back off afterwards - a dead pane is not yours to keep."],[1953,"If that page says \"You must install the GitHub App to clone private repositories\"\nand lists no accounts, the device-flow login already succeeded. Click **Install\nGitHub App** on the workspace (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant the private repos, then restart. Coder's injected provider omits\n`AppInstallURL`, so the warning on `/external-auth/github` is unlinked plaintext."],[2021,"If the UI is unreachable, check that `CODER_ACCESS_URL` matches the address you are\nbrowsing from: the LAN IP by default, or the public coder name in public mode."],[1050,"If you know a real experiment you want (`chat-advisor`, `chat-virtual-desktop`,\n...), list it in `.env` and restart the coder service. Empty means no experiments\non."],[293,"Image from `images/dev/Dockerfile` (overridable with the `image` variable) carrying:\nNode 22 + 24, Python 3.12 + 3.11, Go 1.24, Bazelisk/buildifier,\nTerraform/tflint/terragrunt, protoc and its Go plugins, kubectl/helm/kubeconform,\nshfmt/shellcheck/bats/ruff/mypy/prettier, the Coder CLI, and the pinned Grok Build CLI\nat `/usr/local/bin/grok`."],[1707,"Image missing tools, or the build aborts mid-layer -> `curl: (22)`"],[653,"Immediately after the title, two bold labels each followed by a bullet list:"],[331,"In `devcontainer` the mount is on the workspace container only. A dev container the repo\nbuilds on the sidecar keeps its own home and its own `~/.grok`, which is why the live\n`notes-setup.sh` there is a regular file reached through the clone bind rather than a\nlink into `~/.grok`, and why a built dev container sees neither the settings volume nor\nthe editor symlink."],[337,"In `devcontainer`, working-dir resolution has a second case: with the dev-container\ntoggle off the agent's working dir is `/home/coder`, exactly as in `workspace`; with it\non, the dev container's own working dir is the cloned folder under\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, and that folder ships no skill tree, so the `~/.coder`\ncopies remain the live source either way."],[328,"In `shared` mode (the default `grok_profile_mode`) the container mounts the owner's\nshared per-user Docker volume read-write at `/home/coder/.grok`, nested inside that\nworkspace's own `/home/coder` volume. The terminal helpers, `config.toml`,\n`notes-setup.sh` and the `memory-v2/` tree the CLI keeps there are then the same files\nevery other workspace this owner has reads and writes, and they survive a rebuild."],[1019,"In `shared` mode: `config.toml`, `skills/`, `memory-v2/`, `sessions/`, `notes/` (the\ncanonical copy of each repository's private scratch `notes.md`), and a CLI you\ninstalled yourself under `~/.grok/bin`. The CLI's own skill directories, `skills/` and\n`bundled/`, are never created here without an x.ai sign-in; the stack's skill seeds\nlive under `~/.coder/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`, outside this volume."],[1368,"In `shared` profile mode that is one file per user, re-rendered by the last-booted\nworkspace, which then shares that key across that user's workspaces."],[338,"In a `SKILL.md` the marker sits *after* the `---` frontmatter: the skill parser needs\n`---` on line 1 and silently drops a skill whose first line is anything else. In an\ninjected instruction file the marker is pure provenance, because injected content has its\nHTML comments stripped anyway. The stage is best-effort: a read-only or missing `$HOME`\nchanges nothing observable."],[1804,"In every one of the 79 wall-clock failures no first token ever arrived. Successful\ncompactions do stream: their `elapsed_ms` spans 107 s to 1915 s, and 18 of the 36 ran\npast 300 s, which is the summary the budget was discarding."],[2056,"Incomplete or experimental work lands on the trunk behind a flag that defaults off."],[1574,"Ingress"],[1580,"Ingress rules: what runs from a shell and what is a Bazel target"],[260,"Inside a built dev container the same folder appears under `/workspaces/<repo-name>`,\nthe devcontainer CLI's default mount target and where the dev-container editor opens."],[466,"Inside a running container the same thirteen targets are `refresh-configs`'s job\ntoo: same reseed set, same marker rule, `--json` for a machine-readable\nanswer, and `--litellm-url <url>` for a stack environment that publishes\nLiteLLM on a port other than 4000 (the reseeded `settings.json` then still\nnames that stack's own model transport). grok-build-and-agents.md covers the profile\nvolume and what seeds on first boot."],[1950,"Inside a workspace it repairs that workspace; on the stack host `fix <container>\n[repo-url]` names the one workspace it repairs, and a host run with no container is\nrefused for exactly that reason. It mints a live token through Coder external-auth (the\ninjected pair is never trusted, because it is the frozen copy that expired), writes the\ngh auth store, points git's `github.com` and `gist.github.com` credential helper at that\nstore through a form that clears the env pair first, then re-runs the write probe and\nprints the verdict again, so it proves itself. It is idempotent and it never prints a\ntoken."],[1303,"Inside a workspace the key travels as the `LITELLM_API_KEY` environment variable, which\n`~/.grok/config.toml` references by `env_key`, so no key byte lands in a file."],[834,"Inside the contributor dev container (devcontainer.md) the commands\nabove are exactly what CI runs."],[64,"Inside the workspace container"],[1996,"Install gh, then run `fix` again. The golden image ships a pinned copy (`gh 2.101.0`,\nfrom the release tarball, at `/usr/local/bin/gh`); apt's `gh` is 2.45 and predates\nsubcommands the agent experience uses, so the durable fix is the image, not the distro\npackage. Rebuild the image, then rebuild the workspace: the image is fixed at create\ntime."],[1045,"Installing Cline, Roo, the Red Hat YAML extension and Material Icon Theme is a\nmanual step in the Extensions panel (Open VSX by default). If an extension build\nnames its settings differently, set the OpenAI-compatible endpoint and key in its\nUI."],[300,"Installs `tmux` if it is missing, then seeds `~/.tmux.conf` (only while that file is\nabsent or still carries the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker)."],[2027,"Inventory first"],[1284,"IPEX-LLM over vanilla Ollama"],[1344,"It behaves that way on both `/api/chat` and `/v1/chat/completions`."],[274,"It carries its own copies of `startup.sh.tftpl`, `settings.json.tftpl` and\n`grok-config.toml.tftpl`, so the tmux-default terminal and LiteLLM wiring above apply\nto it too."],[124,"It confirms an **active** version."],[1356,"It decides only for unpinned traffic: the affinity pin below resolves first, and\n`least-busy` runs when nothing is pinned, when the pinned deployment is unhealthy or in\ncooldown, and for any request with no session id."],[1965,"It does not ask \"is there a credential\" and it does not trust the environment: it runs\ngit's own configured helper the way git runs it, takes the password that would go on the\nwire, and probes THAT credential against the endpoint `git push` negotiates through -\none GET of `<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack`. A read-only credential\nprints REFUSED-GRANT, never a pass; a healthy-but-shadowed one prints `push would FAIL`,\nnever `ok`. Inside a workspace (or the contributor devcontainer) it runs SELF mode with\nno docker and prints one `self:` line per credential; on the stack host it enumerates\nthe labeled workspace containers, or probes a named container alone. Flags may go before\nor after the positionals, and a path below `owner/repo` is refused rather than probed."],[1812,"It does not stop the upstream from hanging. It stops the hang being bought three times,\nand stops a finished summary being thrown away."],[464,"It does not touch `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is rendered *inside* the\nworkspace, and `~/.grok` is one profile volume per owner, so a copy from the\nhost would move the model list, the session-affinity key and the editor keys\nof every workspace sharing the profile. A **start/restart** re-renders it from\nthis checkout, which is the only way a per-workspace value gets in, and the\npass says so after it reseeds."],[288,"It either runs the command (`--no-wait`; watch with `coder ls` / `coder logs <name>`)\nor, with no usable `coder` login, prints the same command to run by hand and exits 0."],[1986,"It installs that credential into that one workspace's gh auth store and then\nre-installs the env-proof helper that `gh auth setup-git` would otherwise leave behind,\nsince that helper goes straight back to echoing a stale env pair over the token just\npasted. A PAT is never typed as an argument: argv is readable through `ps` and the\ntoken lands in shell history. Pipe it in (`echo \"$TOKEN\" | bazelisk run\n//scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> -`) or run `auth <container>` alone and\nanswer the hidden prompt. In the line above, `-` is the only argument after the\ncontainer and `< PAT` is a shell redirect, not an argument. It REPLACES the per-start\nminted credential for that workspace, and while the account stays connected the next\n`gh_token_refresh` run rewrites the store, so treat a pasted PAT as a stand-in and\nrotate or remove it in GitHub -> Applications when the work ends."],[1029,"It is **not** a model transport, and the native tool is parked: both twins render\n`disable_web_search = true` at top level, so a session starts with no `web_search`\ntool at all. Measured 2026-10-25: no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type. The `agent` group is LiteLLM in front of the Spark boxes\n(`openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next`), and it refuses the request before anything is\nfetched - `tools of type 'web_search' are not supported: this server runs function\ntools only`, HTTP 400. `supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it,\nbecause the client-side fallback still hands the model alias a `web_search`-typed\ntool, which is the exact shape that endpoint rejects. The fallback lane fails too:\nit is the `coder` alias, Ollama `qwen2.5-coder:32b`, and that model is not pulled on\nthe host. So `web_search = \"agent\"` and `supports_backend_search = false` stay as\nthey are - the role stays filled so re-enabling is a one-key flip - but while the\nkey reads `true` nothing dials it."],[353,"It is advisory only: one warning line per missing path, never a failed start, so the call\nswallows whatever status the function returns."],[149,"It is also the manual path after you register an account, and the retry path for one\ntemplate."],[951,"It is always on"],[317,"It is bounded and retried on purpose: one unbounded attempt burned a whole start on a\nsingle stalled connection to x.ai (`curl: (28) SSL connection timeout`) and still booted\nthe workspace without a CLI."],[282,"It looks for a dev container in `.devcontainer/`, `.github/devcontainers/`, or a\n`devcontainer.json`/`devcontainer.yml` anywhere in the tree."],[287,"It names no `--stop-after`, so a script-made workspace gets no autostop deadline."],[463,"It never drops a key your env file still sets; retired keys are commented out\nin place."],[291,"It never mints, reads, or stores a token - unlike `push-template.sh`, which mints its\nown because a template push has no other credential path. With `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN`\nset but `CODER_URL` unset it refuses to guess, and an empty token is a *not yet* state\nrather than an error."],[462,"It never prints an env value that looks like a credential."],[602,"It never writes a secret, never writes a GitHub token or a PAT, and never installs the\nGrok CLI — the golden image already carries `/usr/local/bin/grok`. Exit `5` means no\nproven checkout was found (it wants `docker-compose.yml` where it is looking), which is the\nsame repo-root probe every other script in the repo makes."],[126,"It re-pins **Autostop** to *Disabled*."],[125,"It re-sends the template's own description."],[121,"It rejects a `coder_workspace_preset` description over Coder's 128-**byte** ceiling."],[122,"It rejects a `vscode-alt.svg` whose inlined data URL would exceed Coder's\n256-character `workspace_apps.icon` column."],[118,"It resolves the repo root from `BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY` (the real checkout\n`bazelisk run` names) ahead of the runfiles copy, so `.env` and the template\nfiles are regular files rather than execroot symlinks."],[119,"It resolves the running stack on the Docker **daemon** by container label, never\nthrough a cwd-resolved compose project."],[354,"It runs after the clone because it is about what the clone holds, and it also runs on a\nre-start that cloned nothing, because the devcontainer build re-runs then too."],[155,"It runs the image's own `coder` binary, so client and server are the same build."],[281,"It shallow-clones (`--depth 1 --no-checkout --single-branch`) into a temporary\ndirectory, from the host, and lists the tracked files."],[1819,"It skips while the coder or db container is down, or nobody has registered yet, and\nre-running it repairs a provider that drifted and moves a model pin whose alias has\nsince gone unhealthy."],[1390,"It spread 12/10/8 across the three deployments and returned 30x 200."],[123,"It streams the source as a tar on stdin, with symlinks dereferenced into regular\nfiles. Coder's provisioner unpacks that archive into an empty workdir and does\nnot follow host paths, so a stored runfiles symlink plans as \"No configuration\nfiles\"."],[286,"It then assembles `coder create` with the template's parameters: `repo_url=<url>`\nalways, `use_devcontainer=false` whenever `devcontainer` was chosen but no dev\ncontainer was found (forced with `--devcontainer` onto a repo that has none, or a probe\nthat could not check the repo - the cases where leaving the toggle at its default would\nbuild nothing anyway), `dind=true` on `--dind`, and `additional_repos=<list>` whenever\n`--additional-repos` named at least one extra URL."],[863,"It tries `${REPO_ROOT:-}`, then `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}`, then `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}`,\nthen `${PWD}`, then `${SELF_DIR}/..`, and prints the first candidate that proves out."],[1378,"It used to be a chain, `agent -> agent-fast -> coder`. `agent-fast` was a second alias\nfor the same model on a second box with a lower output ceiling; one group of three equal\ndeployments already covers every reachable box, so the chain collapsed to one acyclic\nhop."],[1355,"Its counters are per deployment and expire after a hardcoded 3600 s; there is no\n`routing_strategy_args` block to set, and `ttl` there would be a paste from newer docs."],[153,"Its first unauthenticated call (the first-user check) gets the HTML sign-in page back,\nand the CLI reports that as `unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"`."],[1783,"Its host list comes from the `SPARK*_URL` keys in `.env` (`SPARKn_OLLAMA_URL` and\n`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL`), which is why the default run needs no arguments. Export\n`SPARK_HOSTS=\"host1 host2 ...\"` in the process environment only to check a fleet that\n`.env` does not name. A host that does not answer is a skip, not a failure."],[1382,"Its per-box widgets go to error state while `agent` still answers, because `agent`\nanswers from the Arc while every Spark is down."],[160,"Its scope has to be `coder:all` with `allow_list` `{*:*}`: a narrowed token fails the\nCLI's opening organization lookups, so least privilege is not available on v2.36."],[2030,"K3s / Kubernetes"],[903,"Kasm is a privileged Docker-in-Docker container and the only **always-on** privileged\nservice in this stack - the other privileged container is the opt-in workspace DinD\nsidecar. Keep Kasm LAN-only unless you have decided who may reach the public name\n(see SECURITY.md)."],[1634,"Kasm is the only hop that speaks TLS to its origin. `proxy_ssl_verify` is off there\nbecause the certificate is the one Kasm's installer generates and reissues. Hermes,\nlike Homepage, Coder and LiteLLM, is plain HTTP at the origin (`http://hermes:9119`)."],[1684,"Kasm stores its connection endpoints in its own setup database (first-boot wizard or\nadmin UI), so the endpoint has to change there too or streams keep dialing the LAN\naddress."],[1742,"Kasm wizard gone after install -> expected"],[877,"Kasm Workspaces"],[882,"Kasm's wizard-and-UI port pair, and what resetting it destroys"],[1322,"Keep in-flight fan-out at or under a box's `PARALLEL` (5) per workspace. The\norchestrator cap of 4 is inside that budget."],[388,"Keep it that way unless a workspace genuinely needs two trees open at once."],[2129,"Keep the existing gates green and extend what exists. No new CI system, no Kubernetes, no\nflag platform, no new dependency."],[233,"Keep the other licensed flags out of the CE stack too. `--failure-ttl` (Failure\ncleanup), `--dormancy-threshold` and `--dormancy-auto-deletion` are all marked \"This\nlicensed feature's default is 0h (off)\" in `templates edit --help` on v2.36.6, and\n`--allow-user-autostart` works the same way. Older notes also list `--inactivityTTL`\namong the rejected names; v2.36.6 has no flag of that name, and `--default-ttl` and\n`--activity-bump` are the closest."],[420,"Keeping a checkout's config current"],[422,"Keeping a checkout's config current"],[1024,"Keyed by **owner id**, never host-wide and never cross-user: another user's\nworkspace mounts a differently named volume, and a stop or start of a *different\nuser's* workspace never sees the volume at all."],[49,"Knobs and skips"],[1752,"Knowing the cost of a fix before running it, including what it destroys"],[17,"Knowing which green check actually gates a merge into `main`"],[1249,"Knowing which page owns a concept when two of them seem to"],[919,"Knowing which step to re-run when a later one says a prerequisite is missing"],[969,"Knowing why the native `web_search` tool is parked and the SearXNG lane answers"],[1502,"Lane ids are not declared here. `scripts/new-repo.sh` discovers them from the fetched tree's\n`.template-lanes/` directory at run time, so a lane the scaffold repository adds needs no change\nin this repo. The four below are what it ships today:"],[1471,"Lanes"],[802,"Layout"],[1236,"Learn the stack"],[1238,"Learn the stack"],[1182,"Leave the mode on `manual`. `off` is `--yolo`: every mutating command executes on the\nhost with no card."],[498,"Leaving all three `SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` keys empty keeps `agent` defined but\nunusable: LiteLLM then falls back to `coder`, the Arc alias. That fallback is what\nthe \"Agent backend\" card exists to catch - Grok Build, Cline and Roo need\nstructured tool calls, which the Arc IPEX-LLM Ollama returns as plain text for the\nQwen2.5-Coder weights. litellm-routing.md owns the alias\ntable, the picker, the pin, and the measured tool-call limitation (including which\naliases do emit real calls)."],[1256,"LiteLLM model routing"],[1258,"LiteLLM model routing"],[934,"LiteLLM model routing — why the answer you got came from the box it\ncame from."],[872,"Login"],[876,"Look"],[1780,"Look at the dashboard's **Agent backend** card first: its per-box widgets go to error\nstate while `agent` still answers."],[154,"Loopback inside the container cannot be gated, so the push cannot fail that way."],[904,"Losing the wizard after install is expected: use `:4443`. Resetting Kasm means\nremoving the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config."],[4,"main"],[819,"Making a new function satisfy `//tests:shell_inventory` without guessing"],[765,"Making an annotation that a reviewer cannot argue about, because a tool decides it"],[1526,"Markdown carries a token in a second spelling. Prettier reads a bare `__PROJECT_NAME__` as strong\nemphasis and rewrites it to `**PROJECT_NAME**`, so a markdown formatter run over the template turns\nevery prose placeholder into that form; the renderer substitutes both spellings. Which of the seven\nthe scaffold actually uses, in either spelling, is the scaffold's own invariant and its own\n`.self-check/` suite checks it."],[68,"Marker ownership of the seeded files"],[1585,"Matching the house style instead of inventing a local one"],[73,"MCP command reachability advisory"],[1342,"Measured against this box's IPEX-LLM Ollama, whose bundled `ollama` is 0.9.3."],[806,"Measured coverage"],[1388,"Measured on a harness boot of this config against the live Spark boxes, before\n`session_affinity` existed."],[362,"Memory (90 percent) and disk (85 percent, `/home/coder`) usage gauges, six resource\nmetadata tiles sampled every 30s - four `coder stat` calls (`cpu --host`,\n`mem --host`, `disk --path /home/coder`, `cpu --host`) and two awk one-liners for swap\nand load average."],[1693,"Model lane: Spark, LiteLLM, the agent backends"],[952,"Model wiring"],[1106,"Models"],[1285,"MoE over dense"],[1943,"More clients than you have visible terminals means the tabs are mirrors."],[319,"Most seeded files are written only while absent, so a hand-edited copy is user-owned and\nthe stage re-runs harmlessly. Three of them are re-written on every start, and ownership is\ndecided by a marker - never by mtime and never by first boot:"],[1108,"Mounts and \"be the host\""],[1295,"Moving a workload between the Arc iGPU and a Spark box without touching a workspace"],[830,"Name it in exactly one suite. A chain test that happens to mention the name also satisfies\nthe inventory, but the owning suite is the one that asserts its behaviour."],[23,"Name the branch after the change, not the author, and keep the commit subject imperative\nwith one of `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `chore:`, `ci:`, `refactor:`."],[2082,"Name the deployment, not the branch:"],[258,"Named volumes cannot substitute: a volume is a daemon-local object and does not cross\nto the sidecar's daemon."],[1577,"Names and flags"],[1582,"Names, flags, and the style guide shell follows"],[15,"Naming a branch and a commit so CI and `git log` read the way the rest of the repo does"],[689,"Naming every suite that must move with a payload file in one query, in about 60 ms"],[612,"Native `bazelisk test` on macOS is a convenience, not a merge path. The suites run there,\nbut the BSD userland differs from the GNU one the image ships (`sed`, `date`, `find`,\n`sort`), so a green Darwin run is not the run that gates the merge and a red one is not\nproof of a defect. The container, or `test-in-image`, is the answer\ntest-with-bazel.md and ../branching.md gate on."],[665,"Navigating the repo with the Bazel graph"],[667,"Navigating the repo with the Bazel graph"],[634,"Navigation"],[334,"Neither `main.tf` sets `CODER_AGENT_EXP_SKILLS_DIRS` or the\n`CODER_AGENT_EXP_INSTRUCTIONS_*` keys: those *replace* the default lists rather than\nappending, and the seeded copies already sit where the unmodified defaults point. Chat\nitself is on by default in 2.36.6 - no experiment name and no entitlement gates it."],[1852,"Neither header, and a `Server: cloudflare` with a `cf-ray` instead: Traefik or\nCloudflare answered, and there is nothing on this box to read."],[1396,"Neither stack runner has a `restart` subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`\nare the whole set), so the proxy restart stays a direct compose call. Stable stack:"],[978,"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries\nan inlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`), because the\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. Both twins pin the\nrelease in one `code_server_version` local and pass it to the upstream installer as\n`--version`: without that flag `https://code-server.dev/install.sh` follows `latest`,\nand the editor would move under every workspace with no commit in this repo.\n`workspace` installs code-server only while that pinned release is missing from\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server` (the guard names\n`lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`, the directory the installer unpacks, not\nthe `bin/code-server` symlink, which points at the last release installed and would\nhold a warm volume on its first one) and starts it with `--auth none --port 13337\n--app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`, passing no settings payload.\n`devcontainer` has no persistent prefix to reuse, so it installs the pinned release\nfresh under `/tmp/code-server` on every start."],[365,"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries an\ninlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`) because that\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. The script body\nlives in a sibling `code-server.sh.tftpl` and is rendered with `templatefile()`\n(workspace: `{ editor_folder, code_server_version }`; devcontainer: that plus\n`settings_b64`). Do not wrap that `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."],[1466,"New projects from the template"],[1468,"New projects from the template"],[127,"No `.terraform.lock.hcl` is committed under `templates/`. Each `main.tf` pins\n`coder/coder` `~> 2.18` and `kreuzwerker/docker` `~> 4.6` in `required_providers`; Coder\npushes with a warning when no lockfile sits beside them. `coder_devcontainer` needs a\nserver `>= 2.21`, which this stack runs (v2.36.6)."],[1872,"No `<name>` entry and both `ls` paths missing: the seed never landed, which means the\nworkspace booted from templates pushed before the seed existed. Re-push the templates\nand restart the workspace, or reseed into the running container from the stack host:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>`."],[271,"No `config_path` is set, so the CLI discovers the file the way VS Code does, and a repo\nusing the other legal locations behaves the same."],[1594,"No `eval`. Prefer `\"${var}\"`, `[[ ]]`, and `$(...)`."],[1845,"No `web_fetch` either, and that is not a misconfiguration: `features.web_fetch` is\non, but on a non-xAI model endpoint that tool runs only through\n`toolset.web_fetch.proxy_endpoint` and this stack sets none. Route a URL already in\nhand to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`, anything else on the web to the `searxng`\n`search` tool, and a library, framework, CLI or setup question to `context7`\n(`resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on that id)."],[614,"No Actions `container:` key is involved. The job runs on a plain runner and reaches the\ntoolchain through `docker run --user \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\"` with `HOME=/tmp` and\n`USER=$(id -un)`, so what gates the merge is an image tag, the bind-mounted checkout\nstays writable, permission tests see a non-root identity, and Bazel can name a uid the\nimage passwd file does not list."],[146,"No arguments pushes every template in `TEMPLATES_DEFAULT` order."],[2126,"No force-push, no rewritten shared history, no merging your own pull request."],[1199,"No GitHub PAT in Hermes. GitHub auth in this stack is Coder External Auth only, and a\ntoken here would sit on a container that can both write to the checkout and drive the\nhost daemon. See SECURITY.md."],[156,"No host `coder` CLI and no `coder login` are involved, and a stale host-side session\ncannot aim the push at some other deployment."],[1991,"No process can rewrite another process's environment, so the stale pair stays in the\nenvironment until the workspace restarts; the prefix is the permanent form, not a\nworkaround you graduate from."],[2127,"No remote branch deleted without explicit confirmation, and never a trunk ref."],[2128,"No secret in a committed file, including the flags file and per-environment value files.\nSamples hold key names and inert defaults; secrets go to the GitHub Environment or the\ncluster's secret store."],[222,"Notes on the two dropdowns that take a custom value:"],[655,"Nothing below needs a component call in the page."],[165,"Nothing else about GitHub travels in a `--var`."],[356,"Nothing here changes what the build does. With the toggle on, a clone carrying no\ndevcontainer configuration fails the build either way, and the editor hangs off the dev\ncontainer, so no dev container also means no editor and no in-container terminal; the\nwarning is only early notice in the same log the clone warnings use. With the toggle off\nthe same miss is expected and the editor opens the clone directly."],[1635,"Nothing in this path replaces the LAN routes: `https://<lan-ip>/` still serves the\ndashboard on the self-signed cert, `https://<lan-ip>:3001` still serves Coder's\nown TLS listener, and `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` still serves Hermes."],[1666,"Nothing in this repo configures Traefik. This is the file the other box needs: drop it\nin the directory that box's `providers.file.directory` watches and Traefik picks it up\nwithout a restart. The shape below is the one Traefik v2 and v3 both read; the\nentryPoint name, the certificate and the middleware name have to match what that box\nalready defines, so treat those three as the values to align, not as new requirements."],[20,"Nothing is committed straight to `main`. Integration into `main` is by pull request, and a\nPR is only mergeable once CI is green:\n`bazelisk test //:test-fast`, `bazelisk test //:lint --test_tag_filters=manual` and the\nfull gate `bazelisk test //:test-all`, all\nexecuted inside the golden image (`images/dev/Dockerfile`) that CI builds with buildx and\ndocker-runs — the toolchain comes from that image, not from anything installed onto the\nrunner. Locally, run them the same way: inside the contributor container\n(contribute/devcontainer.md) or with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image --`. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` is the bootstrap\nfor the stack host, where the suites have to run on the host itself.\nTwo things run those checks for you so the gate is not a thing to remember:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:pre-commit` on demand, and, once\n`bazelisk run //scripts:install-hooks` has pointed `core.hooksPath` at `.githooks`,\n`.githooks/pre-commit` on every commit and `.githooks/pre-push` before anything is\npushed. `bazelisk run //:validate` runs the fast pair plus\n`docker compose config -q` and the `.env.sample`/`.env.example` comparison; the\npre-PR gate is `//:test-all`, which `//:validate` and the pre-push hook both reach."],[246,"Nothing is published to the host, and 2375 is reachable only from that workspace's own\nnetwork - which is also why the network is not `internal`. An internal network gets no\ngateway route or NAT, so `host.docker.internal` would resolve and never answer, and the\nagent would strand. See SECURITY.md."],[402,"Nothing long-lived lives in any file, env or template: the rendered `config.toml`\nnames `GH_TOKEN` and nothing else, and the pair stops at the workspace container's edge\n(a dev container built on the sidecar is not handed either key)."],[1805,"Nothing on the client aborts the hang. `signals.json` reports `inferenceIdleTimeouts`\nat 0 or 1 across the failing sessions, and no hung call was ever cut off at the idle\ndeadline, so `inference_idle_timeout_secs` (then 1200 s) is not on this path. The key\nnow ships at 1800 s in both template twins, which does not put it on this path either:\nthe CLI still does not apply it to compaction. Reproduced directly: with an upstream\nheld open and no byte sent, the CLI was still waiting at 3000 s."],[1161,"Nous Portal is an optional upstream, not a default, and this page does not document it.\nNothing here needs an account."],[2101,"Off by default on the trunk. An on-by-default flag is an unfinished feature that became\nload-bearing on the day it shipped."],[1234,"Ollama is reachable only from inside the compose network: LiteLLM is the gateway, and it\nis the thing that carries the master key."],[1931,"On `devcontainer`:"],[835,"On a host that is not that container, `//scripts:test-in-image` docker-runs\n`evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid, with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER` set so\nBazel can name that uid, and runs them there. `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE=<dir>` keeps the\narchives Bazel downloads between runs; both CI jobs set it and wrap it in\n`actions/cache` (building-with-bazel.md)."],[1405,"On a memory-rich but bandwidth- and latency-constrained iGPU that is the sweet spot,\nbecause an equivalent dense model would move every parameter per token and run much\nslower at the same footprint."],[323,"On a shared profile volume the config file usually already exists, because another of the\nowner's workspaces wrote it, so the marker rather than first boot is what decides."],[459,"On a TTY the env pass asks by default (`-i`/`--interactive` is then a no-op;\n`--non-interactive` turns it off). For every key that is new this pull or still\n`change-me…`/`NOTSET`/`YOUR_LAN_IP` it prints the explanation the twin carries\nabove that key and prompts for a value. Enter keeps a reusable value already\nset in a sibling env file, or the example default. A key whose\nname carries `KEY`, `SECRET`, `TOKEN` or `PASSWORD` is echoed nowhere - the\nreport says `set` or `empty` and the prompt does not echo what you type - so a\nscreenful of this output is safe to paste into an issue. With no terminal to\nprompt through the pass warns and leaves those keys alone, which is why\n`--dry-run` / `--non-interactive` is the shape that belongs in a script or CI."],[1767,"On Linux, `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --repair` writes\n`/etc/systemd/resolved.conf.d/evo-t1-public-dns.conf` (`DNS=1.1.1.1 8.8.8.8`),\nruns `sudo tailscale set --accept-dns=false`, and keeps MagicDNS for `ts.net`\nvia `resolvectl` split-DNS. `--check` still writes nothing."],[1952,"On the workspace page, click **GitHub reauth**. Coder opens `/external-auth/<id>`\n(device flow for the injected App, OAuth for a custom app in `coder.env`). If the\npage says you are already authenticated, click **Reauthenticate**."],[910,"Once Coder answers"],[828,"one asserting test in the **one** suite for its module — a `scripts/lib/<mod>.sh` helper\nbelongs to `tests/bats/<mod>.bats`, and a helper that only an entry CLI defines belongs to\n`tests/bats/entry-fns.bats`, which loads single functions with `load_shell_function`;"],[981,"One clipboard is in play: the one on the computer you are typing on. The seed runs\n`set -g mouse off` and unbinds every key and every click that could move text into\nor out of tmux's own buffer, so the terminal client keeps each button and does the\ncopying itself, the way it does in a native terminal on that machine. OSC 52 is the\none route that still depends on what sits at the other end of the terminal, and it\nbelongs to an app inside the pane rather than to the operator. Where each claim\nbelow comes from: the tmux ones were read off the shipped tmux 3.4 on this box (what\nan attach writes to the client, and what `list-keys` still holds once the payload is\nsourced), and the setting names, values and defaults were read out of the shipped\ncode-server 4.145.0's own config registry (VS Code base 1.145.0). The two\nClipboard-API observations -- that a client selection fires\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText`, and that `document.execCommand('copy')` is the\nfallback that still lands on an insecure origin -- come from the earlier\nheadless-Chromium pass over that build with the rendered `settings.json` twin and the\ntmux payload loaded, when `mouse` was still on and the copy out was a Shift+drag.\nThat path is the client's own selection, which is what a plain drag drives now; the\nplain drag is due the same pass before it is restated as measured under `mouse off`."],[1209,"One compose stack that turns a GMKtec EVO-T1 into a self-hosted dev-workspace server — Coder, LiteLLM, IPEX-LLM Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes."],[403,"One connection covers every workspace, and `gh auth status` inside a workspace is how\nyou check it landed. Ask it with the pair cleared - `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh\nauth status` - because gh reports the env pair in preference to the store."],[1360,"One conversation from one client sticks to the box that answered its first turn, for as\nlong as that box stays healthy."],[1353,"One endpoint per box, one box per endpoint. Never point two deployments, or two\naliases, at the same physical machine: the proxy counts deployments, not machines."],[1259,"One front door"],[1270,"One group, three deployments"],[255,"One host bind, `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>`, mounted at that **identical**\npath in both containers."],[262,"One host-side path for all three would open the editor on a folder that exists nowhere\nthe editor runs, and VS Code answers that with\n`Workspace does not exist. Please select another workspace to open.`"],[572,"One image — `images/dev/Dockerfile`, built as `evo-t1-dev:latest` — is the contributor and\ntest runtime on macOS, on Linux, and on GitHub. The same Dockerfile serves the Coder\nworkspaces the stack provisions, the contributor dev container on a laptop, and the\ncontainer CI docker-runs its checks inside. Every version in it comes from\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`; ../coder-templates.md covers the\nworkspace side, and CONTRIBUTING.md\nis the checklist the PR follows."],[1826,"One lane reaches that port: a process inside the workspace. The two reads that matter\nare the probe from inside the workspace and the liveliness probe from the stack host,\nand those share nothing but the destination."],[1595,"One language per file where reasonable. A bash, python, or awk program that lives\ninside another file is its own tested file, imported with `source`, `cat` + `sh -c`\n/ `ssh`, `python3 file.py`, `awk -f`, or Terraform `file()` / `templatefile()`. Keep\n1–3 line awk, usage heredocs, Dockerfiles, YAML data, docs fences, and\n`python3 -m webbrowser`. Remote probes are POSIX and self-contained under\n`scripts/lib/probes/`; the host `cat`s them and tests run the file with `sh`."],[1581,"One language per file, the shared log contract, and the payload copies the templates share"],[1092,"One line each: `/ez-learn` turns a session into durable memory - flush it into a session\nlog, dream the logs into organized memory, remember one note, or browse and clear the\nstore; `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked, working from its own\ntranscript; `/ez-create-skill` builds a skill from what the operator describes. The last\ntwo write skill files; `/ez-learn` writes memory files."],[2130,"One logical change per branch and per pull request, with docs and tests in the same change."],[2053,"One long-lived branch. It is normally `main`, it is always deployable, and it is what every\nenvironment is built from."],[747,"One mistyped character, an empty stdout, and a shell that tests only `-z \"$out\"` reports a clean\nresult. So before you believe an empty list: check the exit status (Bazel exits non-zero here), read\nstderr, and confirm the operand resolved at all with `bazelisk query '\"litellm/config.yaml\"'`, which\nprints its label back. A miss you can see is a finding; a miss you cannot see is a green gate over a\nfile nobody tests."],[2086,"One namespace per environment, one overlay per environment, one image reference:"],[2100,"One naming convention. `FEATURE_<THING>` for behaviour, a plain value for a setting. The name\nin the inventory is exactly the name the process reads, so `grep` is a complete audit."],[1873,"One of the three missing from both homes while the other two landed points at\n`coder_script.stack_skills`, not at the startup script: that script's own log is where\nits seed body ran or failed, and the body is silent on a healthy workspace, so an empty\nlog with a missing file means the body did not run at all (restart the workspace and\nread that script's log). The trio moved out of `startup.sh.tftpl` into that one body, so\na startup-script log that says nothing no longer covers it."],[1253,"One OpenAI-compatible door, several backends behind it: the Arc 140T through IPEX-LLM, and\noptional Spark boxes. Which one answers is a routing decision made in\nlitellm-routing.md, not in the client — and that page also states the\none thing the local model cannot do."],[974,"One pane per visible terminal is what that rule buys. Clients attached to one\ntmux session share that session's current window, so a design that always\nreattaches makes every extra browser tab a mirror of the same pane: another\nfull xterm.js in the Chrome tab re-parsing the same stream (each with a\nscrollback buffer preallocated from `terminal.integrated.scrollback`), another\nper-client encode in the tmux server, and a re-wrap repaint for every client\neach time any client activates or resizes. Measured on this workspace: 14\nattached clients against 2 real panes. Two terminals opening in the same\ninstant can still land on one session - that same mirror, and rarer than a\nkeystroke."],[237,"One privileged `docker:29.8.1-dind` sidecar on a bridge network private to that\nworkspace (`coder-<wsid>-dind`, network alias `dind`)."],[386,"One repository per workspace is the default, and that is what `repo_url` and the\n**Git repository** field mean: one URL, cloned into one folder."],[1027,"One shared profile is one `config.toml` for every workspace that owner owns, and\nthe last boot to re-render it wins - for the default model, the affinity key and\nthe config extras alike."],[963,"One shared profile volume per user, and what last-booted rewrites"],[2105,"One verdict per branch, with the evidence beside it."],[252,"One workspace container from the golden image: launcher and clone target only. It still\nhas to be the golden image, because that is what ships the Docker client the\ndevcontainer flow talks through. The editor's real environment is whatever the repo\nbuilds, so `image` decides nothing about the toolchain inside it."],[1866,"One-off fix, from the stack host:"],[243,"Only the literal string `\"true\"` enables it; anything else reads as off."],[341,"Only the variables `main.tf` passes interpolate. The `workspace` startup script gets\ntwelve: `grok_config`, `grok_build_agents`, `notes_setup`, `tmux_conf`,\n`grok_build_terminal`, `vscode_terminal_tmux`, `grok_build_delegation_skill`,\n`coder_agents`, `mcp_cache_proxy`, `repo_url`, `additional_repos`, `vscode_settings`.\nThe `devcontainer` twin adds `dc_dir` and `build_devcontainer`. The three skill payloads\nare not among them: `ez_learn_skill`, `ez_skillify_skill` and `ez_create_skill_skill`\nbelong to the `coder_script.stack_skills` map, and their `chomp(file())` calls sit inside\nthat block rather than behind a `local.`, because both size readers credit a body by the\n`path.module}` names in its own block."],[554,"Open it on a laptop"],[1202,"Open the assistant in your own terminal (subcommands `chat`, `ask`, `shell`, `check`,\n`commands`; `--help` for the flags):"],[520,"Open the checkout in the contributor dev container (`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nbuilds it) and run the commands below unchanged."],[563,"Opening the repo as a contributor container in VS Code or a compatible editor"],[1115,"Opening the same assistant in your own terminal with `hermes-shell`"],[1545,"Operate the box"],[1547,"Operate the box"],[1465,"operate/index.md for the rest of the day-to-day runbooks."],[1596,"Operator CLIs source `scripts/lib/common.sh` and do not redefine `log` / `die` /\n`skip`. Grouping: `section` prints `== name ==`, `phase` prints `-> text`, `ok`\nprints `ok: text`, `summary` prints `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed` plus\n`  failed: …` and optional `next: cmd`. `JSON_MODE` set sends prose to stderr.\nCLIs without `--json` unset `JSON_MODE` after source. Keep prefixes `warning:` /\n`note:` / `error:`."],[235,"Opt-in in `workspace` (the **Docker-in-docker** Yes/No question, preset *AI workspace +\nDinD*, template variable `dind`). With the toggle off, `docker` in that workspace fails\nthe normal way rather than against a daemon that does not exist."],[1782,"Or check the whole fleet at once, from the checkout. `spark-verify.sh` takes `--json`\nand `--no-tests`; `--no-tests` skips the recipe's own probes (a 195k-token needle plus\na 1/2/4/5-client sweep, minutes per box) and leaves the quick read-only check:"],[1786,"Or repoint `SPARK1_OPENAI_URL`, `SPARK2_OPENAI_URL` and `SPARK3_OPENAI_URL` in `.env`\nat a tool-capable OpenAI-compatible endpoint, then restart the proxy so it re-reads\nthem. The stack runners have no `restart` subcommand, so this one stays a direct\ncompose call:"],[521,"Or, from a machine that is not in that container, `bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image --`\ndocker-runs `evo-t1-dev:latest` and runs the gate inside it."],[1937,"Output already scrolled off the screen: `Prefix-[` is a viewer, not a copier -\nwalk up with `k`, `PPage` or `Ctrl-u` until the line is painted on the screen,\nselect it with a client drag, then `q`. A selection made *inside* copy-mode is\ninert: every copy binding in `copy-mode-vi` is unbound, so nothing ends it."],[2102,"Owner and expiry on every flag, and one `chore:` pull request that deletes the flag, its\noff-path, and any branch still carrying it."],[606,"Pass only the repository URL. A named container argument is refused with a pointer to the\nhost shell, because that container lives on a daemon this environment cannot enumerate."],[120,"Per template it checks `main.tf` exists (otherwise \"run this from the repo root\") and\nnotes a missing `.terraform.lock.hcl`."],[2036,"Per-repository notes"],[2045,"Per-repository notes for the four reference repos"],[1868,"Permanent fix: rebuild the image and the workspace, which then starts from the\nimage's own copy. See the next two entries for the command."],[1880,"Pin notes and the seeding contract: grok-build-and-agents.md."],[1913,"Pin provider versions locally only if you want to, per template directory. The push\ngoes ahead either way, and `workspace` and `devcontainer` are the two names:"],[1440,"Pin the identity while the box is still up, so a stranger's key cannot be\nmistaken for it later:"],[997,"Pinned as `GROK_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` (1.0.77 at the time of\nwriting, mirrored as the Dockerfile `ARG` default). The image build unpacks that\npinned `.zst` and runs `grok --version` in two PATH contexts as a build-time smoke\ncheck."],[1487,"Pinning a scaffold revision, or rendering a local tree with no network at all"],[51,"Pinning autostop by hand"],[1061,"pins for the four auxiliary lanes - `general` and `explore` (the two subagent\ntypes) prefer `agent`, `compaction` and `title_generation` (the helper calls)\nprefer `chat` - chosen only from the aliases the health gate left standing, and\nwritten when a lane carries no pin or is pinned to one of these configs whose alias\nhas since gone unhealthy. A pin aimed at a config the script did not create is the\nadmin's and is left alone. Both helper lanes landing on a dead `chat` is what made\nconversations lose their titles and die at the compaction threshold, which is why a\nstale pin now moves instead of being kept forever."],[1525,"Placeholders are substituted in file contents *and* in file and directory names:\n`__PROJECT_NAME__`, `__PROJECT_SLUG__`, `__PROJECT_MODULE__`, `__PROJECT_DESCRIPTION__`,\n`__PROJECT_OWNER__`, `__PROJECT_LICENSE_HOLDER__`, `__PROJECT_YEAR__`. Those seven are named once,\nin `NEW_REPO_TOKENS`, and they are the whole contract between this script and the scaffold -- a\nceiling rather than an equality, so the template may use a subset of them. A token that survives is\na hard failure, not a warning -- a repository that ships with its placeholders showing is worse\nthan no template at all."],[1755,"Placeholders: `<wsid>` is a workspace id and `<ws>` a workspace name. The workspace\ncontainer is `coder-<owner>-<ws>` (so `coder-alx-self-coder`); the `<wsid>` form names\nthe DinD pieces around it - the sidecar `coder-<wsid>-dind-sidecar`, on the network\n`coder-<wsid>-dind`, with its own `coder-<wsid>-dind-lib` volume. What runs \"from the\nstack host\" needs a shell there: inside a workspace `docker` addresses that workspace's\nown private daemon through `DOCKER_HOST=tcp://dind:2375`, so a `docker inspect` aimed at\nthe stack's `coder` or `router` container and run from inside a workspace queries the\nwrong daemon and answers `no such container`. `bazelisk run //scripts:...` also runs\nfrom the checkout."],[195,"Point the CLI at the in-container plain listener, with a token by name:"],[2112,"Porting is re-adaptation. Take the files, then fix them against today's trunk:"],[644,"Predicting what a reader sees from what you wrote, without opening a browser to find out"],[1198,"Prefer structured commands - the existing Bazel scripts and `docker compose` - over\nad-hoc shell. A script the repo owns is reviewable and test-covered; a one-liner the\nmodel invented is neither. See AGENTS.md."],[926,"Prepare the box: rotate the secrets, set `CODER_ACCESS_URL`, issue the self-signed\ncertificates, build `evo-t1-dev:latest`, pull the DinD image. It is idempotent, so re-run\nit after changing `docker-compose.yml`, `litellm/config.yaml`, `searxng/settings.yml` or\n`proxy/*.conf`."],[116,"Preset keys are parameter *names*; `display_name` is UI-only and would be ignored."],[41,"Presets"],[113,"Presets collapse the create form to one click."],[1735,"Private-repo clone or push denied while a credential is present -> the grant, not the format"],[1794,"Probe cadence. `health_check_interval` and `health_check_concurrency` in\n`litellm/config.yaml` decide how often every Spark deployment gets a real generation.\nThey sit at LiteLLM's own default of 300 s and a concurrency of 2 on purpose: at 30 s\nacross five deployments a round was putting more probes on a box than user requests,\nand each one took a prompt-cache state from a conversation. Auto-recovery does not\nneed 30 s -- a box that is actually down is pulled by `allowed_fails` and\n`cooldown_time` under live traffic, and the re-probe is what brings it back. If the\nwarnings come back every few seconds, that is the first key to look at."],[1781,"Probe that endpoint (the card reads the same URL, `AGENT_MODEL_URL` in `.env`):"],[1568,"Project conventions"],[1570,"Project conventions"],[1255,"project-conventions.md is what a script has to look like to be\nmergeable, and branching.md is how it gets to `main`. The Bazel harness they\nboth assume is in the Contribute tab."],[2087,"Promotion edits the tag, not a branch:"],[2057,"Promotion is the same commit or tag applied to a different environment. Nothing is rebuilt\nper environment, because a per-environment build is a per-environment bug surface."],[2090,"Promotion is the same playbook revision and role versions against a different inventory. A\nbranch that carries playbook changes is a pull request; a branch that carries only variable\nchanges is a file under `group_vars/`."],[1037,"Prove all three at once with `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check`: it reads the\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, reports whether each lane is declared, whether its\n`command=` binary is present, whether the lane's credential and cache config are\nthere, and what each server's handshake says. A binary the box cannot find is image\ndrift (the running `evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake), and the check prints the\npinned `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command that restores it."],[1798,"Prove recurrence before you touch anything. The events are in each session's\n`updates.jsonl`, so one pass over the store counts attempts against outcomes. The\nstore is the shared profile volume, so run it where the sessions are (inside a\nworkspace), and leave the `~/.grok/sessions` path %-escaped as the CLI wrote it:"],[1740,"Public `hermes-gmktecbeast` answers `421` -> no vhost, a rewritten Host, or Traefik aimed past the router"],[430,"Public hosts and the dashboard"],[1609,"Public subdomains"],[1611,"Public subdomains"],[1464,"public-access.md for what the box exposes on the network\nafter it boots - the initramfs listener is gone by then."],[929,"Push both templates (`workspace` first, `devcontainer` last). The source travels as a tar\non stdin and the credential is minted, used and deleted in the same run, so there is no\nhost `coder` CLI and no login here:"],[1681,"Put LiteLLM behind it and every client breaks: LiteLLM authenticates from the\n`Authorization` header, and header-carried API traffic cannot pass an interactive\nsign-in. Either leave `litellm-gmktecbeast` outside any policy or add an explicit\nbypass - and then `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` is the only credential in front of an\nendpoint that went from LAN-only to reachable WAN-wide (see\nSECURITY.md)."],[302,"Puts `~/.grok/bin` on PATH (login shells and that process)."],[1629,"Putting the five routers on the Traefik box without guessing its timeouts"],[752,"Python"],[759,"Python: the mypy profile, the aliases that replace `Any`, and how to add a file"],[1166,"Query the lane the same way the agent does:\n`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` from a host shell counts results per\nengine, which is how an empty answer stays distinguishable from no answer.\nInside the container,\n`curl -s -H 'Accept: application/json' \"$SEARXNG_URL/search?q=hermes&format=json\"`\nis the exact request the provider makes. The instance only answers JSON\nbecause `searxng/settings.yml` adds `json` to `search.formats`; without it the\nrequest is refused with 403 before the query is parsed."],[678,"Raw query recipes when a verb does not fit"],[1167,"Re-point the lane with `STACK_HERMES_SEARCH_URL` in `.env` (a sibling box, say)\nand recreate. An operator who wants a keyed provider sets\n`web.search_backend` plus that vendor's key env in the container themselves -\nthe seed carries neither, and adding one is a decision with a privacy cost."],[324,"Re-rendering carries over the tables the CLI owns instead of wiping them: `[cli]` holds\n`cli.installer`, which picks the CLI's update path; `[ui]` holds `permission_mode`, so\nre-adopting a file must never flip an operator back to prompting; and `[marketplace]` is\nCLI settings the stack never owns."],[1123,"Reaching the assistant on the LAN, and on the public name `auto` composes from the parts"],[1219,"Reaching the page that owns a subject from one card, instead of reading four that touch it"],[1799,"Read `auto_compact_failed` against `auto_compact_started`. Over the 11-day window this\nwas investigated, the store held 82 failures across 15 sessions in 3 workspaces,\nescalating from a 39 per cent failure rate to 85 per cent (45 attempts in one day, 19\nof them in the worst single session), and 3 of the 15 sessions ended on a dangling\n`auto_compact_started` and were abandoned."],[2103,"Read once, branch there. A flag checked in nine modules is nine edits when it retires."],[1967,"Read the report by its last line, then by these. `git would send this credential\n(source: <slots>)` is the credential a real push uses, in git's consult order; the same\ntoken held by several slots is named once, as one credential with several names. A plain\n`credential present (source: <slots>)` is a credential that exists and that git never\nreaches."],[1316,"Read these three facts before changing the block:"],[1203,"Read-only audit of what the assistant was created with, and how far it has drifted from\nthis checkout (`report`, `posture`, `web`, `diff`; `--check` narrows to one):"],[92,"Reading a failed push or a stuck workspace against what the template actually does"],[884,"Reading the box's health from one page without a shell"],[1428,"Reading the exact ssh command and host key fingerprint to expect"],[1277,"Reading the routing back"],[1293,"Reading the routing back, and editing an alias safely"],[1265,"Reading the table"],[1979,"Reads prove nothing about push, so nothing here asks a read question: a public repo\nanswers a read for any authenticated caller, so a credential that READS a repo can still\nbe refused every WRITE to it. Go to the GitHub side only after the report says\nREFUSED-GRANT or NOT VISIBLE."],[1441,"Reboot the box."],[1426,"Rebooting a server nobody is standing next to"],[1887,"Rebuild the image, then rebuild the workspace; the image is fixed at create time."],[2119,"Record the tip SHA of every branch in that table before anything goes. A recorded SHA is\nrecoverable until garbage collection; an unrecorded one is not."],[16,"Recovering a half-finished change with one revert, because each landed step was committed as it landed"],[1556,"Refreshing a stale checkout before diagnosing what it looks like"],[662,"Regenerate after you change either the tree or the file layout:"],[928,"Register the first account — that account is the site admin. Browsers only expose\n`crypto.randomUUID()` in a secure context, so this points at the TLS listener:"],[915,"Registering the first Coder account, pushing both templates, wiring the chat agent"],[971,"Related: coder-templates.md (the template files themselves),\nlitellm-routing.md (what the aliases route to), and\ntroubleshooting.md."],[1299,"Related: coder-templates.md (the templates that render\n`~/.grok/config.toml`), grok-build-and-agents.md (the CLI and\nthe control-plane chat), project-conventions.md."],[1127,"Related: dashboard.md (the card that links here),\nlitellm-routing.md (the aliases Hermes calls),\npublic-access.md (the public name, which `auto` composes),\nSECURITY.md."],[95,"Related: project-conventions.md (shell and payload conventions),\nSECURITY.md, and\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md\n(the long prose on the tmux profiles and the Grok Build session)."],[1003,"Rendered per workspace from `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` (and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy of the same payload), and re-rendered idempotently\nwhile the managed block is intact."],[1472,"Rendering a repository"],[1480,"Rendering a repository with `new-repo`, its flags, and its lanes"],[305,"Renders `~/.grok/config.toml` (the three gated MCP servers ship by default; the five\noffline stdio ones are not declared)."],[820,"Reproducing a CI failure locally by running the suites in the image CI runs them in"],[569,"Reproducing CI's failure on your own machine with the same image CI runs"],[2019,"Reset Kasm by removing the `kasm-data` volume, which destroys all Kasm config. Stop\nthe stack first, and use the name your stack owns: `evo-t1-coder-stack_kasm-data` for\nthe stable stack, `evo-t1-dev_kasm-data` for the dev one."],[1946,"Residual: a tab you hide rather than close keeps its xterm instance alive and\nstill buffers whatever the pane prints, and nothing in the stack caps that.\nNeither does tmux: 3.4 has no per-client output limit, and an ordinary\nattached client that stops draining its pty is never dropped (measured here:\none stayed attached past 15 minutes while the server kept serving commands).\nThe only \"too far behind\" disconnect is in control mode, behind a hardcoded\n300 s age in `control.c`, which a VS Code tab never uses.\nmicrosoft/vscode#327437\nis open (a heap snapshot showed ~38 live terminals holding ~80 MB of a 484 MB\nrenderer heap) and\ncoder/code-server#6413\nreports browser-tab RAM reaching several GB with a continuous-output terminal\nleft running after the tab is closed; neither has a maintainer mitigation.\nClosing the tab is the mitigation, and the numbered sessions are what make the\nlive set match the tabs you can see."],[1394,"Restart the proxy on the stack you changed, so it re-reads that file."],[1954,"Restart the workspace afterwards so `GH_TOKEN` and the gh auth store match the new\ngrant. `fix` makes git push work without the restart; the injected pair itself only\nchanges when the workspace restarts or `gh_token_refresh` next runs."],[1269,"Routing inside the `agent` group"],[1292,"Routing inside the `agent` group, and the `agent` to `coder` fallback"],[2099,"Rules that keep flags alive long enough to be removed:"],[443,"Run it after you pull, not before: a pull is what changes the templates these\ntools copy from. `bazelisk run` feeds the real checkout (`BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY`),\nnot the runfiles copy, so the env pass rewrites the live `.env` beside\n`docker-compose.yml`."],[1432,"Run it before every reboot of an encrypted box, and after any kernel or\n`initramfs-tools` update. It exits `1` when something blocks the unlock, so it\nfits in front of a maintenance script as a gate."],[776,"Run it directly when you want the error text without Bazel in the way:"],[804,"Running a suite"],[515,"Running any operator CLI from a foreign working directory with pinned inputs"],[2038,"Running it"],[813,"Running one suite, from any directory"],[1122,"Running stack operations by conversation with a plan and an approval first"],[570,"Running the Bazel gate on macOS without maintaining a second, BSD-flavoured toolchain"],[557,"Running the gate here"],[2051,"Running the same procedure in any of this fleet's repositories with `/trunk-refactor`"],[690,"Running the suites that cover the files you touched instead of the whole fast gate"],[1608,"Runtime stays `docker compose`: Bazel runs the CLIs that drive Compose and never talks to a\ndaemon, a Spark box, or a model registry by itself. No Kubernetes layer."],[2037,"Safety lines"],[158,"Same trust model as `coder reset-password`, which also bypasses the API and talks to\nthe database directly."],[816,"Sandbox-safe repo-root probing"],[809,"Sandbox-safe: probe for the repo root, then say so"],[8,"Scope"],[506,"Script targets and runfiles"],[513,"Script targets, runfiles, and why a new `scripts/*.sh` is a target automatically"],[1051,"Scripted, idempotent, re-runnable, and already started from `prod-stack` /\n`dev-stack` `up` (`--apply --wait`, default 1800 s). Registering the first account\nis the only operator step: the seeder continues once an owner exists, and the Agents\npage is usable with no extra Coder UI setup. Print the plan:"],[1575,"Scripts"],[949,"Search lane and MCP servers"],[1702,"Search never answers (or answers empty) -> prove the search lane before blaming the MCP hop"],[1673,"SearXNG is deliberately absent. It stays LAN-only on `:8181`, which is where the\nworkspaces reach it through `host.docker.internal`; putting it behind Traefik would\nexpose a search box that has no authentication of its own."],[1197,"Secrets live in gitignored `.env` and in the `hermes-data` volume. Never commit them,\nand never paste `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` into a chat to prove a route works."],[1111,"Security notes"],[555,"Seeding the contributor home"],[304,"Seeds the Coder Agents context files, then the shared code-server User settings, only\nfor files that are absent."],[605,"SELF means no docker at all and no `DOCKER_HOST`: one probe of this environment's own\ncredential chain, printed as one `self:` line per credential. `docker` in here addresses\nthe workstation daemon mounted in, never the stack host's workspace fleet."],[604,"SELF mode applies when `CODER_WORKSPACE_ID` is set **or** `EVO_T1_DEVCONTAINER=1` is. The\ncontainer sets the latter; a Coder workspace sets the former."],[1610,"Serving the same five services on public names over real TLS, additively, with the LAN shape left intact."],[1698,"Sessions log `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s (runaway generation)` -> a compaction stream that never sent a token, bought three times"],[1095,"Set `disable-model-invocation: true` when only an explicit `/name` should run it.\nAnything that deploys, pushes or resets belongs behind that key."],[189,"Set a token first. `CODER_SESSION_TOKEN` is any token from the Coder **Tokens** page or\nfrom `coder tokens create`."],[1017,"Set in two places: the template variable `grok_profile_mode`\n(`GROK_PROFILE_MODE=shared|private` in `.env`, forwarded by `push-template.sh` as\n`--var grok_profile_mode`), and the create form's per-workspace **Grok profile**\nfield, which defaults to the variable. One workspace can therefore sit outside the\nshared profile while its siblings stay inside it."],[1665,"Set the naming parts in `.env` and put `auto` in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` (`.env.sample`\ndocuments every part and shows the explicit-list shape beside it), and in\n`dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside the stable one. Then re-run `bootstrap`\nand recreate the stack you switched. The commands are in the README."],[491,"Set the parts in `.env`, and in `dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside\nthe stable one, then re-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` and recreate the\nstack you switched. That one command is the whole rename: it re-renders the router's\nvhost table and rewrites the derived URLs, so a hostname is no longer an edit split\nacross `proxy/router.conf`, `scripts/bootstrap.sh` and `docker-compose.yml`. A\n`hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is part of that list:\nbootstrap writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`, and the LAN `:9119` listener stays either\nway. Leave the hermes name out and the Homepage card keeps the LAN URL.\npublic-access.md owns the naming rule, the measured\n`ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` on the dotted form, the request path and the\nchecks to run on the Traefik box."],[725,"Seven logged sessions guessed a target name and got `ERROR: no such target`. A target is never\nguessed, it is listed:"],[710,"Seven suites, printed in about 60 ms. Change that file and those are the suites that go red; edit one\nof them and the same verb in the other direction tells you what it covers."],[57,"Shape"],[1586,"Shell follows the Google Shell Style Guide.\nBranch rules live in branching.md; the Bazel harness lives in\ncontribute/building-with-bazel.md and\ncontribute/test-with-bazel.md."],[2050,"Shipping work that is not finished, on the trunk, without exposing it"],[1298,"Short version lives in README.md (\"LiteLLM model aliases\"); this file is\nthe long version behind it. Symptom-first checks live in\ntroubleshooting.md."],[2000,"Sign-in is `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` from `.env`\n(default `admin` / `change-me-hermes` until bootstrap rotates the\nplaceholders). To fill missing keys and replace those placeholders:"],[1131,"Sign-in is HTTP basic auth from `.env`: `HERMES_DASHBOARD_USER` (default `admin`) and\n`HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD`. Bootstrap generates the password; it is **not** the\nHomepage password, and `bazelisk run //scripts:dashboard-password` does not rotate it.\nThat basic auth stays on every door. Hermes v0.22.5 refuses to bind `0.0.0.0` with no\nprovider. On the public URL Authelia challenges first and basic auth challenges\nsecond; two logins are expected. LAN `:9119`, and LAN `:8080` with this name's Host\nheader, never see Authelia, so basic auth is the credential on those paths."],[1116,"Sign-in: HTTP basic auth, with Authelia in front of the public name"],[1858,"Signed into Authelia with the link still failing puts the fault behind Authelia: the\napp path is gated by the same policy as the rest of the coder name."],[31,"Small commits cost nothing at the gate: `.githooks/pre-commit` runs the hermetic half on every\ncommit and `.githooks/pre-push` runs the full set before anything leaves the machine, so a stream of\nfocused commits is held to the same bar as one large one. See `main` for installing them."],[2084,"so `docker compose up` stays clean and `--profile experimental` opts in."],[1399,"So an alias edited or deleted in `litellm/config.yaml` moves in the Agents model\ndropdowns exactly as it does in the CLI."],[862,"So resolve the root from the environment first, then prove it, and fail loudly when the\nproof fails. `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` is that resolver and every CLI that\nreads a file the checkout alone holds calls it, so do not write a second one. It takes the calling\nscript's own directory as its last candidate and the marker to probe for (`docker-compose.yml` by\ndefault); the call site in every one of those CLIs is this:"],[257,"So the clone must exist at the same path inside the sidecar too, or it dies with\n`bind source path does not exist`."],[1347,"So the limitation tracks the model, not the runtime."],[1066,"So the two compactions fail for unrelated reasons, and the fix for one is not the fix\nfor the other:"],[1897,"So the workspace build fails at **plan** time, before anything is created, and it\nprints in the workspace's build log rather than anywhere inside the workspace."],[1407,"So you can flip `coder` from the local Arc to a Spark box, or add a new alias, and no\nworkspace setting has to change."],[431,"Spark agent model group"],[1352,"Spreading across every reachable box is the point of running three of them, five full\n262144-token streams each. Pinning only narrows the candidate list first."],[1204,"Stable stack - `hermes` comes up with the rest:"],[1776,"Stable stack (`prod-stack` addresses the `evo-t1-prod` project by default; name\n`--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` to read a stack brought up by hand under the\ncheckout folder's own project name):"],[1057,"Stack `up` starts that seeder in the background and keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (`CODER_AGENTS_SEED_LOG` moves it, and the\n`note:` line stack `up` prints names the path it chose). Read that file first when\nthe Agents chat is dead after a boot: the seeder's most common outcome is a `skipped:`\nline that exits 0 - no coder container yet, no db container, nobody registered - and\nan unwritable log path degrades to the old discard with a `warning:` instead of\nfailing the boot."],[1688,"Stack bring-up"],[1745,"Stack bring-up: bootstrap, ports, containers that refuse to start"],[1013,"Stack ownership of the file rests on either marker spelling: the line-1\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key.\nBoth twins emit both, because the comment alone does not last - `grok mcp add`\npersists its own settings by rewriting the file and deletes every comment line, so a\ncomment-only marker handed the config to the user with no human in the loop, and the\ndefault lanes silently stopped arriving. A hand-edited config is left alone only\nwhile it carries one of them, so takeover means deleting **both** lines; delete the\nfile and restart to hand it back. When the guard does re-render, the CLI-owned tables\n`[cli]`, `[ui]` and `[marketplace]` are carried over rather than wiped, because the\nstack owns no approval mode - `[ui] permission_mode` / yolo stays a per-user call."],[503,"Stack-host bootstrap"],[509,"Stack-host bootstrap: the one script run straight from a shell"],[1211,"Start here"],[927,"Start the stable stack, and pull the models `OLLAMA_MODELS` names (a large download):"],[1485,"Starting a new repository with the quality gate already in place, in one command"],[1751,"Starting from the error text you actually saw and landing on one command"],[71,"Startup script templatefile rules"],[1415,"Static networks"],[693,"Stopping the late-gate discovery of a coupling rule the graph already recorded"],[748,"Strong types"],[750,"Strong types"],[1160,"Switch it inside the container with `hermes config set model.model chat` (or\n`agent-medium` for a thinking level), which edits the live config under `/opt/data`."],[483,"Switching either way is a **rebuild**, not a stop/start, because the nested mount\nis decided when the workspace is built.\ngrok-build-and-agents.md covers what the shared\nprofile holds and what seeds on first boot; the `workspace` pass above never\nreseeds `~/.grok/config.toml` for exactly that reason."],[1686,"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."],[1909,"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints `== Preflight ==` and\n`== Variables ==`, then aborts inside `== Templates ==` with\n`push-template.sh: line 740: REPO_ROOT: unbound variable` and never prints a\n`summary:` line. Nothing was pushed."],[1899,"Symptom: `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` queues, then dies during\n\"Initializing Terraform Directory\". `workspace` cannot list versions for\n`coder/coder` and `kreuzwerker/docker` on `registry.terraform.io`;\n`devcontainer` cannot fetch `module \"devcontainers_cli\"` from\n`registry.coder.com`. Both lookups fail with\n`dial tcp: lookup … on 127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`. Coder itself\nstays up; `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs coder` repeats the same Terraform\nlines."],[1903,"Symptom: `coder templates push` plans, then dies during \"Cleaning Up\" with\n`internal provisionerserver error: execute transaction: insert resource: insert\nagent app: upsert app: pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`.\nCoder logs `coder_app's id was unexpectedly empty` and then that Postgres error\nat `inserting template import job resource`."],[1906,"Symptom: both templates fail during \"Detecting persistent resources\" with\n`Terraform initialized in an empty directory!` / `Error: No configuration files`.\nCoder logs `unpacking source archive  size_bytes=30720` (tens of KB, not the\n~120 KB of real `.tf` / `.tftpl` contents)."],[768,"Telling a contributor why `Any` came back from CI"],[440,"Telling a ported env key apart from a value you set yourself and must keep"],[1488,"Telling a refused GitHub permission apart from a broken render"],[1628,"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"],[692,"Telling a rule class from a target name without opening a BUILD file"],[571,"Telling the contributor container, the Coder workspace and the stack host apart in one look"],[161,"Template source travels as a tar on stdin (`coder templates push --directory -`), so\nnothing is copied into the container or its volumes."],[53,"Template variables"],[207,"Template variables are pushed defaults; the create form can still override them per\nworkspace."],[380,"Template-side facts only; the long prose lives in\ndocs/grok-build-and-agents.md."],[1576,"Templates"],[1072,"Ten files seed into every new workspace, on first boot and only while the file is\nabsent:"],[1314,"TensorFold on the Spark boxes refuses an over-admitted stream with the Zig error value\n`NoRoom`, delivered as an HTTP 500 body. The OpenAI SDK hands that to LiteLLM with no\nint `status_code`, which at the pinned `v1.102.1` puts it on the status-less branch of\n`exception_mapping_utils`, so the router sees `APIConnectionError`."],[78,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[940,"Terminal and editor defaults"],[89,"Terminal and editor defaults inside the container, and workspace GitHub credentials"],[959,"Terminal and editor defaults, and the profile the templates seed"],[754,"Terraform"],[2032,"Terraform / OpenTofu with ArgoCD"],[1717,"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI"],[761,"Terraform: why a `variable` without a `type` is a runtime bug"],[799,"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)"],[801,"Testing with Bazel (the Bats harness)"],[1573,"Tests"],[598,"That `notes.md` is `notes_materialize` from the same library: it restores the canonical copy\nunder `~/.grok/notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/` over a live file that is empty or still the\nseeded stub, and copies a real live file back there, so a stub never walks over written\nnotes. Here that runs once, at seed time - the background watcher that repeats the back-copy\nabout every 30 seconds is a workspace-startup thing (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`; see\n../grok-build-and-agents.md) - so re-run the\nseeder to push a fresh edit to the canonical copy in this container."],[1389,"That 30-request burst was multi-session, fan-out-style traffic."],[620,"That container is the *laptop and CI* environment: it mounts the host docker socket and\nassumes a workstation daemon, neither of which belongs inside a workspace's nested dev\ncontainer."],[435,"That env comments always refresh from the twin, even when values stay"],[268,"That exposes the dev container as a sub-agent with its own terminal and apps, and the\nin-browser editor attaches to *that* sub-agent, so the editor runs inside the dev\ncontainer where the cloned repo's toolchain lives."],[387,"That folder is what code-server, the Web Terminal button and the Grok Build session\nopen on. Inside a dev container, code-server sees that same folder under its mount\ntarget, `/workspaces/<repo-name>`."],[187,"That form addresses the stack through `docker compose` instead, so it names the\nproject with `-p`. From the checkout root the folder name would have said the same thing."],[1431,"That is `check`, the default verb, and it writes nothing. It answers one\nquestion - if this box rebooted right now, could you decrypt it from a laptop -\nand closes with either `ok: this box can be unlocked from a laptop after a\nreboot` or `error: do not reboot yet - N blocker(s)`."],[329,"That is also why `config.toml` cannot be per-workspace in that mode: it is one file, and\nthe last workspace to boot re-renders it for all of them, so this workspace's default\nmodel (or, in `devcontainer`, its model alias) and config extras overwrite whatever the\nprevious boot wrote - for every sibling."],[251,"That is the *AI workspace + DinD* shape, always on, plus a repository that brings its own\ntoolchain."],[611,"That is the host ingress: it `docker run`s `evo-t1-dev:latest` as this process's uid:gid\n(`$(id -u):$(id -g)`), with `HOME=/tmp` and `USER=$(id -un)` so Bazel has a writable home\nand a name for a uid the image passwd file does not list, and runs the same suites CI\nruns. A macOS laptop gets the Linux result without a Linux box. Build the tag first with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` if the daemon does not have it."],[151,"That is the server's own plain listener (`CODER_HTTP_ADDRESS` binds `0.0.0.0:3000`\ninside the container), reached without crossing the reverse proxy, Authelia, or the WAN."],[1126,"That is the whole point of the service: stack ops by conversation. \"Recreate the dev\nstack and tell me which container restarts\", \"why is the coder agent offline\", \"run the\ngate and fix what it reports\" - each one runs with a plan first and an approval card\nbefore the mutating command."],[1021,"That is what makes \"survives workspace recreate\" true rather than a hope: an edit is on the\nvolume within roughly one interval, so a recreate no longer waits on someone restarting the\nworkspace to pick up what was typed since the last boot. What is not promised is the tail"],[342,"That is why the `.git` suffix is stripped with `sed` rather than the shorter\n`${leaf%.git}`: a braced expansion would add an interpolation `main.tf` does not pass."],[478,"That pipes a PAT straight into that container's `gh` credential store - no `.env`\nedit, no template push - and `gh auth logout` inside the container, or a rebuild,\nremoves it. The repositories a token can reach are whatever that GitHub app\ninstallation was granted."],[1540,"That preset/CLI pair is the whole hand-off. It is where this repo hands a new project over, and the\nonly coupling between the two repositories: this repo names the scaffold it clones, the scaffold\nnever names this one."],[1895,"That probe only asks whether the daemon answers `docker version`. It says nothing\nabout the workspace that used to reach it."],[1372,"That request routes elsewhere, to whichever box `least-busy` picks."],[730,"That target resolves to 198 source files through 25 declarations: the gap is the `filegroup` and\n`scripts/lib` expansion under each label. `labels(\"data\", ...)` is the narrower question (\"what does\nthe BUILD file say\"), `files` is the wider one (\"what is in the sandbox\"), and the two disagreeing is\nusually the bug."],[717,"That warning is the honest version of \"nothing depends on this\", and it is the reason to keep\nstderr with a query. Go to the gate that owns the map instead of guessing its label:"],[1537,"The **`New project (scaffold ready)` preset** in both templates is a form shortcut. It pins the\ngolden image, turns on the DinD sidecar in the workspace template, and passes this stack as\n`additional_repos`, which is what puts `scripts/new-repo.sh` inside a fresh workspace beside the\nproject being started. The scaffold does not ride along: the CLI fetches it from GitHub when it\nruns. It is a preset, so it only sets parameter values."],[1828,"The **agent bar** keeps `:4000` off its own path: the workspace page's `litellm` tile\nis `coder_metadata` text (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out rather than a\n`coder_app`, so `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is never consulted here. Health-check the\nproxy with `curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack\nhost. The app lane under `A workspace app link answers Bad Gateway` below is the\n**VS Code Web** editor link and stays on 13337 inside the workspace."],[1132,"The **Chat tab** is the primary UX. It embeds the Hermes TUI - a PTY over WebSocket\non `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` - so the terminal agent and the browser are the same\nprocess, and approval prompts render as cards in the chat rather than vanishing into\na detached tty. Hermes sends a keepalive every 20 seconds. Traefik and Authelia have\nto pass the `Upgrade` hop. A close code **4403** is the Host / Origin / peer guard.\nCompose sets `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` from `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the seed file\ndoes not carry `public_url`). The seeded `dashboard.trusted_proxies` is\n`[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]`, which is what makes Hermes honour `X-Forwarded-Proto` from the\nrouter (the Secure cookie and `wss`). `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` is environment\nand applies when the container is recreated. An existing `hermes-data` volume does\nnot pick up `trusted_proxies` until a reseed or `hermes config set` - the volume\nseed is copy-once. The reseed command is below."],[1827,"The **model lane**: a process inside the workspace dials\n`http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1` directly. `host.docker.internal` is the docker\nbridge gateway (`172.17.0.1`), compose publishes the proxy's container port 4000 on\nhost port 4000, and nothing between the two authenticates. `LITELLM_BASE_URL` and the\ncoding CLIs use this lane."],[1847,"The **VS Code Web** button is the one app this stack still proxies: its URL is\n`/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/code-server` on the coder hostname, because\n`coder_app.code_server` sets `subdomain = false`. So a failing app link is an app-lane\nfailure, and a 200 from `litellm-gmktecbeast.` is not evidence about it."],[277,"The `--` ends Bazel's own flags, so a URL that begins with a dash still reaches the\nscript. Every fence below is one complete command."],[744,"The `//` half is the honest measure of what the gates can see, and it moves as files are added, so\ntreat any absolute total in a document as a snapshot rather than a fact to assert."],[829,"the `# @function` block above the definition, with Globals/Arguments/Outputs/Returns."],[864,"The `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` default is not decoration: these scripts run\nunder `set -euo pipefail`, and a bare `${REPO_ROOT?}` aborts with `REPO_ROOT: parameter not\nset` the moment a sandbox or CI runner leaves the variable unset."],[2017,"The `0.0.0.0` bind refusal above is a different failure: empty basic-auth env,\nnot this public path. Full notes: hermes.md."],[1788,"The `agent` -> `coder` fallback keeps chat answering while a Spark is down, which is\nexactly when tools disappear."],[1340,"The `agent` lane deliberately carries the `openai/` provider prefix. `ollama_chat/`\nwould route those boxes through the Ollama-specific code path, and `api_base` has to\ninclude `/v1`."],[492,"The `agent` model group is three deployments behind one LiteLLM alias, one per\nSpark box, and these are the keys that fill it:"],[1333,"The `agent` row is ONE model group with three deployments, one per Spark box,\nall equal."],[682,"The `bazel-map` CLI, its verbs, and the `--json` split that feeds `bazelisk test`"],[32,"The `ci` workflow (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs for `push` to `main` and for a\n`pull_request` whose **base** is `main` or one of the sanctioned work-branch prefixes\n(`feat/**`, `fix/**`, `refactor/**`, `docs/**`, `test/**`, `chore/**`, `ci/**`).\n`pull_request.branches` filters on the base rather than the head, so naming only\n`main` left a stacked pull request - a dependent branch that names its parent as its\nbase, the shape the agent guidance in `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` tells\nyou to open - with no gate at all: it collected\njust the docs deploy its branch push earned from `deploy-docs`. Gate a stack the same\nway as any other pull request. The other shipped workflow, `deploy-docs`, also runs\non a `push` to any branch (`branches: [\"**\"]`) whose diff touches its `paths:` list -\n`docs/**`, `docs-site/**`, the two `scripts/ci/` helpers it publishes through\n(`publish-pages-tree.sh`, `docs-alias.sh`), and its own workflow file - and by\n`workflow_dispatch`."],[1065,"The `compaction` pin above belongs to Coder's chat agent and to nothing else. It is one\nof the four auxiliary lanes the seeder writes into Coder's own model-config store, so it\ndecides which alias serves *that* agent's summariser and title call. It never reaches a\nGrok Build CLI: the CLI reads no Coder table, and its compaction summary is an\n**in-session call on the session model** - the `[models] default` alias, `agent` here -\nwhich is why the summary inherits that lane's output ceiling (measured on the wire at\n`max_tokens: 32768`) instead of a cheap helper lane's. `[models] session_summary` is not\nthat call either; the key serves titles and the session recap."],[1035,"The `context7` server (library and API docs) also renders by default, and is the\nonly cached one. `context7_mcp` (default true) is its gate, and the block it\nrenders runs `mcp-cache-proxy` in front of the official `context7-mcp` - two packages\npinned in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image, so parking\nor restoring it is a flag plus a template push, while changing the packages\nthemselves takes an image rebuild. The proxy keeps its SQLite cache under\n`~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/`, which rides the owner's shared\nprofile volume in `shared` mode, and the cache TTL is 12 h: the first lookup of a\nlibrary reaches the Context7 API and needs network, while an identical lookup inside\nthe TTL is answered locally. `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is optional and the lane does not\ndepend on it: an empty value sends no `Authorization` header at all, so the server\nhandshakes and answers at the public rate limit, with private libraries the only\nthing out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env. What *parks* the\nlane on purpose is `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var context7_mcp=false` on a template push; a `[mcp_servers.context7]` block\nmissing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a park. The lookup\norder is fixed - `resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on the id it returns"],[371,"The `devcontainer` editor cannot set `agent_id` with\n`local.use_dc ? coder_devcontainer.repo[0].subagent_id : coder_agent.main.id`.\n`coder_devcontainer.repo` is also gated on `start_count`, so the stop /\ntemplate-import ephemeral plan has an empty tuple even with the toggle on\n(the default), and that ternary then fails with `Invalid index` / `empty\ntuple`. `local.code_server_agent_id` is\n`concat(coder_devcontainer.repo[*].subagent_id, [coder_agent.main.id])[0]`:\nthe sub-agent when that resource exists, the parent agent on the stopped\npage. The start-gated `coder_script.code_server` uses the same local."],[368,"The `devcontainer` fork has no persistent prefix to reuse - both toggle modes rebuild\nthe container that hosts the editor - so it installs the pinned release fresh under\n`/tmp/code-server` on every start, and bakes the settings seed as base64 into `settings_b64` (empty when the\ntoggle is off, so the shell skips the merge). The DinD wait lives in\n`templates/devcontainer/dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`."],[56,"The `devcontainer` template"],[859,"The `docker` stub passes `exec`/`compose exec` through by default (`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=1`),\nso a binary the script execs inside a container lands on its own stub; set\n`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RUN=0` with `STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_RC`/`STUB_DOCKER_EXEC_OUT` for the\n\"the daemon answered, and it said No\" case."],[1765,"The `first boot? run bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap to create it` hint alongside it\nmeans the checkout has no `.env` at all: that is the bootstrap path. `DASHBOARD_ENV_FILE`\npoints the rotation at another file and exists for test harnesses; there is deliberately\nno flag for it, because a flag that names a file this CLI overwrites is one mistype away."],[408,"The `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` pair - reached through the helper, because\n`gh auth git-credential` echoes either name whenever one is set. A helper that does not\nclear the pair therefore answers with the injected copy and never asks the store: that\nis the shadow, and it is why a hand-edited helper or a hand-rolled askpass script is the\nwrong instrument (see\nthe push-denied entry)."],[1034,"The `github` server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) renders by default even\nthough it needs a token and outbound network: with GitHub tools on, the agent can\ndrive the repository it is working in, and the cheaper failure is the block that\nrenders with no credential and pays one failed handshake per session. `github_mcp`\n(default true) is the gate; `$GH_TOKEN` comes from Coder External Auth, never from a\nPAT."],[223,"The `grok_default_model` aliases are `agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`,\n`agent-xhigh`, `coder`, `coder-fast` and `chat`, plus a **Custom** entry when the\nvariable names another one."],[740,"The `kind` filter is what makes an `rdeps` usable. A bare `rdeps(//..., \"litellm/config.yaml\")`\nincludes `//:test-fast`, which reaches every file and therefore tells you nothing about this one:"],[1482,"The `New project` workspace preset and the optional Coder Task"],[1774,"The `ollama` service is IPEX-LLM on the Arc 140T, not Ollama from a registry."],[1030,"The `searxng` MCP stdio server is what answers instead, and it is the search lane\nrather than a fallback: both templates render that block gated on `search_mcp`,\nwhich defaults to true. It dials `$SEARXNG_URL` (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:8181`), the same key `main.tf` puts in the agent\nenvironment. It carries nine tools - `search`, `image_search`, `news_search`,\n`video_search`, `music_search`, `paper_search`, `fetch_content`, `autocomplete`,\n`list_engines` - and an agent's two workhorses are the first and the seventh:\n`search` for anything that is not a library question, `fetch_content` for a URL\nalready in hand. Measure the self-hosted lane with:"],[1337,"The `spark-*` rows are a different thing: the same Qwen2.5-Coder and Qwen2.5\nweights, served over Ollama on the Spark boxes instead of on the Arc."],[40,"The `templates/common/` payloads"],[973,"The `tmux` profile gives each terminal of a folder its own pane. The base session\nname is unchanged: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>`. A new terminal walks into one of\nthat folder's sessions only while no client is attached to it, oldest first -\nthat is the closed-tab rejoin, so a tab you closed or reloaded leaves its\nsession unattached and the next terminal finds it with its history intact, and\n`ssh` + `tmux attach -t <session>` lands there too. A terminal opened when\nevery session of the folder already has a client takes the next numbered\nsibling instead: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ... Sessions persist\nunattached until the container restarts, so a reattached terminal still\nreaches the output a closed tab produced. `tmux ls` lists the names and\n`tmux list-clients` says who is looking at each. Every target the helper\ngives tmux carries the `=` exact-match prefix (a bare name is answered by any\nsession starting with it, so a closed `foo` could be answered by a live\n`foo-2` and re-create the mirror), and a reopen whose session vanished\nmid-attach raises a fresh session rather than exiting along with the tab.\nTwo tmux behaviors shape that path: a session raised by a tab's own\nforeground `new-session` has its first pane closed when that client exits\n(superuser#575909),\nwhich is why the helper creates detached and attaches separately; and with\nthe default `exit-empty` the server itself leaves when its last session does\n(tmux#2081), which is why a\nreopen probes the server before raising anything - a tab whose server is gone\nhas ended normally, and re-raising there would boot a server for a terminal\nnobody is looking at (tmux has no server probe: `has-session` answers the same\nfor a missing session and a missing server, which is why upstream declined to\nadd `check-server`, tmux#2523).\nNo upstream issue accounts for the session loss: `destroy-unattached` is off,\nso a detached session is meant to survive, and tmux gives a session no grace\nperiod after it loses its last client\n(tmux#5821 is an open request for\none)."],[367,"The `workspace` fork installs code-server **only when the pinned release is\nmissing** from `/home/coder/.local/share/code-server`, and starts it with\n`--auth none --port 13337 --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`,\npassing no settings payload. The guard tests\n`/home/coder/.local/share/code-server/lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`,\nthe directory the installer unpacks, because `bin/code-server` beside it is a\nsymlink to whatever release was installed last: a guard on that symlink would keep\na warm volume running its first release after a bump. A bump therefore re-downloads\non the next start and leaves the previous release under `lib/`; removing that old\ndirectory by hand is what reclaims the space. Before that launch it `mkdir -p`s the\nfolder and, when the folder is not `/home/coder`, waits up to 60 s for\n`<folder>/.git` so a first-start\nclone and the editor no longer race. That wait loop increments with\n`waited=$((waited + 1))` (one dollar) inside the tftpl: `templatefile()` interpolates\n`${…}` and leaves `$((` alone. `$$((` would expand `$$` to the PID. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the agent would\nchdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail the agent. The\neditor's settings live on the per-owner `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` volume seed, not\nin a per-start merge."],[1384,"The `x-litellm-model-id` response header names the deployment that answered."],[1795,"The 195k needle. `//scripts:spark-verify` runs the recipe's `needle.py --length 195000`\nas the window proof, and a 195k prompt the server has never seen is the largest single\neviction on the box. It now skips itself while `/health` reports `requests_running`\nabove zero, so it does not land on a box that is serving:"],[1278,"The Agent backend card"],[133,"The agent runs `coder_agent.startup_script`, and every `coder_script.script`, as\n`bash -c <body>`. The whole body therefore travels as **one** argument to `execve(2)`,\nand Linux caps a single argument at `MAX_ARG_STRLEN`, 32 pages = 131072 bytes. Measured\non this host: an argument of 131071 bytes execs and one of 131072 fails with `E2BIG`.\nPast the wall the agent logs `fork/exec /bin/bash: argument list too long` with\n`exit_code=255` a few milliseconds in, the home directory never seeds, and the workspace\nbanner reads \"A startup script has failed\". Coder puts no limit there and the plan\nreports no error, so the wall only announces itself on a live recreate."],[1264,"The alias table"],[1289,"The alias table, and which backend each alias resolves to"],[1990,"The answer for every `gh` call, for as long as that workspace lives:"],[2003,"The assistant also opens in your own terminal with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-shell`. When you cannot tell whether the agent,\nits volume, or the model lane is the broken part, start with the readiness\nreport — it launches nothing and names each blocker (daemon, container state,\nseeded volume, model key set or still a placeholder, LiteLLM\n`/health/liveliness`):"],[1162,"The assistant can look things up, and the query leaves through your own SearXNG."],[1241,"The assistant on the host"],[615,"The Bazel job no longer installs the pins onto the runner: the image already has them at\nthe pinned versions."],[406,"The blank first value is git's reset marker: it drops any helper inherited from a\nlower-precedence config file, and git takes the **last** value it finds for that host.\nThe startup seed rewrites both keys every start (idempotent), and `github-auth fix`\ninstalls the same form, leaving a helper that already ignores the pair alone. This\nhelper is the slot that wins, and the clearing is the subject of the paragraph below."],[1444,"The boot continues on its own once the mapping appears - no second reboot. If\nthe SSH connection is refused, the box is still in the initramfs or before it:\nthe boot does not proceed past an unmapped root, so waiting is safe and the\nconsole (out-of-band console, IPMI, or the provider's serial console) still\nshows where it stopped."],[1221,"The box deliberately runs **two isolated copies** of the stack: a stable one you work in\nand a dev one you break. Two stacks on one box\nin the README is the short version; docs/config-updates.md covers\nwhat a `git pull` leaves behind."],[1321,"The box-side reclaim switch. The recipe measured the same refusal going from\n\"2 of 5 streams refused\" to \"5 of 5 admitted\" once patch 0029 is live with\n`TF_FLASHNEXT_ADMIT_RECLAIM=1`. `//scripts:spark-configure --apply` owns the per-host\noverride file those settings belong in, and `//scripts:spark-verify` already fails a\nbox whose `streams.max` drifts from the fleet's `PARALLEL`. Asserting the\nadmit-reclaim setting itself is still open work: neither script names that key today."],[25,"The branch and the commits are in place while the work happens, and the push waits. This is the\norder the agent seed teaches and the order a human contributor gets the most out of:"],[2044,"The branch-cleanup cookbook: classify, port, preserve, list for deletion"],[474,"The callback URL on the GitHub side is\n`<CODER_ACCESS_URL>/external-auth/<id>/callback` - `github` for the injected\nApp, `primary-github` for the sample app above."],[1383,"The card and its per-box widgets read `AGENT_MODEL_URL` and `SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` from\n`.env`, so they follow the LAN, and they answer without a key."],[333,"The chat agent discovers its context through its *default* paths (verified against the\nv2.36.6 agent binary and its `agentcontextconfig` source): the instruction file\n`AGENTS.md` under `~/.coder`, skills under `~/.coder/skills`, and a working-dir-relative\n`.agents/skills`, which here resolves to `/home/coder/.agents/skills` - the same folder\nthe Grok Build CLI looks in."],[1047,"The chat agent is compiled into coderd unconditionally."],[2002,"The Chat tab is a PTY over WebSocket on the same port. Approvals render as\ncards there; a mutating command that seems to hang is waiting for the card.\nSeeded `approvals.mode` is `manual` — never `off`. `hermes-init` must exit 0\nbefore `hermes` starts. If the gateway loops on a missing `config.yaml`, the\nnamed volume was not seeded: run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap -- --help` and recreate. Full\naccess / mounts / socket-risk notes: hermes.md."],[1134,"The Chat tab is the door for someone at a browser. The terminal door is for someone\nalready on the box, and it opens the **same** assistant - same `SOUL.md`, same seeded\n`config.yaml`, same git-tracked skills, same LiteLLM lane, same memory, same manual\napproval gate:"],[1829,"The checks that apply:"],[312,"The chown of `~/.grok` and `~/.shared` runs a second time at the end of the script,\nbecause the CLI installer and the seeds above may have created files as root when the\nagent ran as root."],[1539,"The CLI never creates the workspace itself. It prints\n`next: bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <url>` and stops, because `new-workspace.sh` owns the\ndev-container probe and the template choice, and a second copy of that decision would eventually\ndisagree with the first."],[669,"The CLI: bazel-map"],[310,"The clone folder is created before git runs, and before the `coder_script.code_server`\nscript's own `mkdir`: the editor starts in parallel with the startup script, and an\nempty tree is a folder VS Code can open while the clone is still writing. An empty\ndirectory is also what `git clone` accepts, so pre-creating it does not change what gets\ncloned - the emptiness guard still refuses a folder holding anything."],[285,"The clone is a plain `git clone` from the host, so it consults the host's own git\ncredentials. A private repository therefore needs a grant covering it **on the machine\nyou run this from**."],[1055,"The closing summary counts how many of the four auxiliary lanes carry a pin, and a lane\nleft without one prints a `warning:` that names the lane."],[1820,"The closing summary counts the auxiliary lanes that carry a pin. A `warning:` naming a\nlane means that lane falls back to Coder's own pick, and `title_generation` and\n`compaction` are the two an operator notices first: conversations stop getting titles,\nthen a long one stops at the compaction threshold. Re-run `--apply` once the alias\nbehind that lane answers."],[376,"The code-server module is deliberately given no settings map in plain mode: its merge\nruns on every start and `mv`s over that symlink, which would fork a private copy and\ndiscard the sharing. (`devcontainer` passes the seed to the module only in the mode where\nthe merge is wanted, and bakes it as `settings_b64` for the built container.)"],[70,"The Coder Agents seeds and their agents-tree twins"],[332,"The Coder Agents seeds under `~/.coder` and `~/.agents` are not on that mount: they live\non the workspace's own home volume, and that stage neither needs nor disturbs it."],[1411,"The command that matters"],[920,"The commands are the README's spin-up section\nwith the reasoning attached. Run them on the stack host, not inside a workspace: a\nworkspace's `docker` addresses its own DinD sidecar, and the whole-stack targets reach the\ndaemon that runs Coder."],[1613,"The composed names"],[1237,"The concepts behind the box — what the templates build, how a model request is routed, what the agents file seeds, and the rules a change follows."],[841,"The consequence for a test author: a function counts when something calls it. A\nname in a comment, a stub, a fixture or a quoted grep pattern satisfies\n`//tests:shell_inventory` and satisfies nothing here."],[628,"The contract every page in docs/ has to satisfy before the site will render it: frontmatter, the authored H1, the opening scan pair, and the navigation file that decides whether a page is reachable at all."],[603,"The contributor container is its own credential chain, so the doctor runs in SELF mode\nthere — exactly as it does inside a Coder workspace:"],[502,"The contributor path"],[510,"The contributor path — the dev container, or `test-in-image` — and where it fits"],[1301,"The control-plane chat agent dials the same proxy at `http://litellm:4000/v1`, over the\ncompose network, from inside the `coder` container. That is the server-side door."],[1374,"The conversation goes back to the box once it is healthy again."],[703,"The cost of not querying shows up late. In the sessions that hit a coupling rule, the first\n`FAIL: //` line appeared only after a hundred or more messages of editing: the rule was\ndiscoverable in the graph on message 1, and was instead discovered by running the gate. One agent\naudited `//:stack_data` by listing the repo's top-level directories rather than diffing the graph\nagainst `git ls-files`, which is the same question asked in a way that cannot answer it."],[1491,"The coupling runs one way: this repo may name the scaffold repository; the scaffold repository\nnever names this one. And the scaffold's quality gate is its own -- the `.self-check/` suite and\nthe CI that runs it in that repository -- so a scaffold change is tested where it is made, and a\nlane or a file the scaffold adds needs no change here."],[405,"The credential helper configured for `https://github.com`. Both template twins seed\n`~/.gitconfig` at startup, for `github.com` and `gist.github.com` alike:"],[1528,"The credential here is the Coder External Auth GitHub App **user-to-server token**; this stack\nnever introduces a static token or PAT. An App can be granted one endpoint and refused another, so\nthe CLI runs the publish side as independent steps, each reporting `ok:` or `warning:` on its own.\nNo step is fatal, because aborting on the first refusal would abandon the steps that were allowed."],[82,"The credential ladder, in git's consult order"],[372,"The custom icon `templates/common/vscode-alt.svg` travels as a\n`data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from `templates/common/`. Coder serves icons\nonly from its bundled `/icon/*` set or from a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is\nnot bundled. `workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a\ncompact ribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`. `push-template` rejects\nthat locally, before the CLI runs."],[247,"The daemon image is resolved with `data \"docker_image\"`, which inspects the host store\nand **never pulls**, so `docker:29.8.1-dind` must already be there. `bootstrap.sh`\npre-arms it; to do it manually:"],[244,"The daemon's `/var/lib/docker` lives on its own volume (`coder-<wsid>-dind-lib`), so a\nstop/start does not re-pull every image the builds used. It is not gated on\n`start_count`, so it survives a stop and is reused by the next start."],[511,"The daily commands, and the Makefile shim over them"],[1381,"The dashboard's **Agent backend** card exists to surface exactly that failure."],[1630,"The default shape is LAN-only. `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` additionally serves the same\nfive services on public names over real TLS, each name composed from the parts you set\nonce. It is purely additive: every LAN route keeps working."],[954,"The delegation sketch"],[507,"The dependency maps and their gate"],[1201,"The deploy workspace is writable by the assistant's file tools only when you say so\nwith `--writable`, and the file tools refuse anything outside the image's\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT` (the official default is `/opt/data`) with no prompt at all.\nName the narrowest directory that finishes the job."],[1054,"The deployment default follows the health gate. `agent` is registered as the default,\nand when LiteLLM reports no healthy deployment behind the Spark alias the Arc `chat`\nconfig takes the default in its place. That matters because a chat opened without an\nexplicit model pick has only the default to resolve to, so a stack that comes up while\nthe Spark lane is dark still gets an Agents chat that answers. An existing config is\nleft as it stands, since this API surface has no PATCH: the script names the Admin\nscreen to correct it when a stored row drifted."],[1932,"The dev container must build first, and that needs `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json`\nin the cloned repository."],[1645,"The dev row is what `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up` composes for the\n`evo-t1-dev` project: that command applies `STACK_ENV_LABEL=dev` when neither the shell\nnor the env file already sets a label, and only for that project, so\n`--project-name evo-t1-review` gets none. A label goes on every name, so that stack's\ndashboard is `dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` too, and all five need their own Traefik\nrouters (see below). `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` never invents a label."],[1397,"The dev sandbox takes the same restart with its own project name and env file:"],[256,"The devcontainer CLI always bind-mounts the workspace folder into the dev container it\ncreates, and a bind source is resolved by the daemon that *runs* that container - here\nthe sidecar, not the workspace's."],[728,"The direct inputs are the `data` the dependency maps write, which is what you edit when a target\nneeds one more payload group:"],[294,"The Docker **client** toolchain only (`docker`, `docker buildx`, `docker compose` as\nCLI plugins under `/usr/local/lib/docker/cli-plugins`). No daemon: the build asserts\n`dockerd`, `containerd`, `runc` and `docker-proxy` are absent."],[193,"The docker provider resolves `evo-t1-dev:latest` from the local image store and never\nattempts a registry pull, which is what keeps workspace creation working with no\ninternet."],[897,"The docker socket stays unmounted: service status comes from each service's API, which\navoids handing the dashboard a root-equivalent host credential. The Hermes card in the\n**Host** group is the same pattern (`siteMonitor` of `http://hermes:9119/api/status`);\nthe Hermes container itself *does* mount the socket — that credential lives there, not\nhere. See hermes.md."],[979,"The editor opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`: the\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL and as a positional argument to the code-server binary\nwith `--ignore-last-opened`. `workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and\n`/home/coder` with no repository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a\nbuilt dev container and `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the\nplain container. On first start the inlined `coder_script.code_server` creates that\nfolder and waits up to 60 s for `.git` (skipped when the folder is `/home/coder`) so\nthe clone can land before code-server launches. `coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the\nagent would chdir there before `startup.sh`, and a missing clone folder would fail\nthe agent. With the sample `REPO_URL` that folder is\n`/home/coder/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`."],[679,"The empty result is not an answer"],[617,"The equivalent run on your own machine is the host ingress above, so a CI-only failure is\nreproducible without a Linux box or a workflow rerun."],[391,"The extras are cloned **beside** the primary repository, into sibling folders, by the\nsame clone-once rule (a folder that already holds a `.git` is left alone on later\nstarts, never pulled on)."],[2060,"The failure that motivates all of this is the merge that goes the wrong way. A `development`\nbranch that is 134 commits ahead of `main` (a real number from a real repository in this fleet)\nis not a staging area, it is a second product whose differences nobody is reconciling, and the\nday someone finally merges it, the review is unreviewable."],[1579,"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"],[865,"The file probe (`-f \"${candidate}/${marker}\"`) says \"this is a repo root\"; refusing a derived\ncandidate whose `pwd -P` lands under `bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or a\n`*.runfiles` tree says \"and it is the real checkout, not a copy\", because runfiles carries its\nown copy of the marker. Two probes, because either one alone passes in the wrong case: writing a\nrotated `.env` or a `sparks/<host>.env` through the runfiles copy reports success while the\ncheckout keeps the old bytes. An explicit `${REPO_ROOT}` is exempt from the second probe — it is\na statement about where to work, and this harness points it at the copy of the repo under test."],[673,"The filegroup that must claim a new payload file"],[729,"The files it can actually open are the transitive closure of those groups, which is what the verb\nprints:"],[1186,"The first `compose up` runs `hermes-init`, which is the in-container equivalent of\n`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-bootstrap`: it runs\n`scripts/hermes-bootstrap.sh --in-container` and copies `hermes/config.yaml` and\n`hermes/SOUL.md` into `/opt/data` **only when those files are missing**. Editing the\ncopy in the volume is how an operator ends up with a config no one can reproduce."],[2020,"The first registered account is the site admin."],[295,"The five offline MCP servers ship in the image, but the rendered config declares none\nof them by default, so a workspace needs no network to start a session."],[2034,"The flag ladder"],[2043,"The flag ladder, from one env var up to a checked-in `config/flags.yaml`"],[83,"The four commands"],[1317,"The four groups are independent and none inherits `agent`, so each needs its own six\nkeys. The zeros pin the exceptions `DefaultRetries` would otherwise reach; at this tag\n`NotFoundError` has no field of its own, so without a policy a typo'd model id is\nretried on `DefaultRetries`."],[2023,"The full procedure for moving a repository onto trunk-based development: environments become configuration instead of git branches, unfinished work ships behind a flag that defaults off, and every other branch is inventoried, ported, or listed for deletion. Worked examples for Compose, K3s, Ansible, Terraform/ArgoCD, Bazel and manage.sh stacks."],[2104,"The gate runs with it off; at least one test or documented smoke command runs with it on."],[409,"The gh auth store (`~/.config/gh`, 0600): the one copy on disk, maintained by\n`gh_token_refresh`. Reachable only past a helper that clears the pair."],[1980,"The grant behind the credential - the GitHub App installation that the External Auth\nprovider (id `github` by default) minted its token from - has to include the repository\nthe workspace clones or pushes: GitHub -> Settings -> Applications -> `<app>` ->\n**Configure** -> Repository access."],[943,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image"],[961,"The Grok Build CLI in the golden image, and how it is pinned"],[67,"The Grok Build CLI probe, the download, and the installer stub"],[1366,"The Grok CLI forwards `extra_headers` verbatim."],[139,"The guard also says the number while it is still a warning: a body within\n`STARTUP_SIZE_WARN` (4096) of the bound prints its composed total and the bytes left,\nbecause the distance to the budget is what a reviewer needs and a hard failure only\never reports the crossing. Measured on 2026-11-06 with the byte algorithm in\n`tests/bats/startup-script-size.bats`: the widest body is now the devcontainer startup\nscript at 115967 bytes, 6913 clear of the bound, with the workspace twin at 113767 and\n9113 clear. Before the trio moved out that same devcontainer body measured 122389 with\nonly 491 clear, and `ez-learn.SKILL.md` plus its heredoc accounted for roughly 7 KB of\nit. The three payloads now travel in their own body, `coder_script.stack_skills`, which\ncomposes to 36847 bytes."],[880,"The header tiles: CPU, memory, temperature, disk, interface throughput"],[1133,"The Homepage card in the **Host** group links to `STACK_HERMES_URL` (the public\norigin when set, otherwise the LAN URL). Its `siteMonitor` is\n`http://hermes:9119/api/status`, which resolves over compose DNS, so the card stays\ncorrect whatever the browser link is. See dashboard.md."],[868,"The Homepage dashboard and Kasm Workspaces: what each card reads, how the password is issued, and how a desktop gets streamed."],[980,"The icon travels as a `data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from\n`templates/common/vscode-alt.svg`: Coder serves icons only from its bundled\n`/icon/*` set or a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is not bundled.\n`workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a compact\nribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`."],[2041,"The ideal-state checklist a refactor has to satisfy before it is called done"],[902,"The image ships default users (`admin@kasm.local` / `user@kasm.local`); change them\nduring the wizard."],[576,"The image ships the Docker client only. The build ends with `test ! -x\n/usr/local/bin/dockerd` (plus `containerd`, `runc`, `docker-proxy`), so the merge-gate\ncontainer drives a daemon it is given rather than owning one."],[76,"The inlined code-server fork"],[316,"The install check is `command -v grok`, not `[ -x ~/.grok/bin/grok ]`: the golden image\nbakes the CLI into `/usr/local/bin`, so on a golden-image boot the probe answers yes and\nnothing downloads. The download exists for the **Minimal shell** (`ubuntu:24.04`) preset\nand for a home volume that never received a CLI."],[831,"The inventory strips comment lines before it looks for a name, so a name that only ever\nappears in a `# @function` block or in prose is still untested: it has to sit in a code line\nsomewhere under `tests/`. The usual way to get one there is a unit case that calls the\nhelper directly:"],[1400,"The IPEX-LLM runtime is Intel's XPU path (oneAPI / Level Zero) for the Arc stack."],[1156,"The key is `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`, substituted from the environment into the seeded\n`config.yaml`. Do not copy the key into a Hermes file - one secret, one owner."],[322,"The key is what makes the rule hold. The CLI's own settings writer (`grok mcp add` and\nfriends) deletes every comment line, so a comment-only marker can vanish with nobody\ntouching the file."],[922,"The LAN address you will browse to. `CODER_ACCESS_URL` has to be an `https://` URL that\nis not `localhost`, or a workspace cannot reach LiteLLM."],[1455,"The last one is worth dwelling on: `check` can read every config file and still\nbe looking at an image GRUB cannot load, so an encrypted `/boot` is reported as\na warning to verify by hand rather than cleared."],[898,"The layout follows the upstream README example - logo plus greeting top-left, boxed\nresource tiles, clock, weather and search top-right, cards on a wallpaper. The\nwallpaper (`background.webp`) and the logo (`evo-t1.svg`) live in\n`homepage/assets/icons/`, mounted at `/app/public/icons` and served as `/icons/...`.\nHomepage's auth middleware exempts only that path from the sign-in redirect, which is\nwhat lets the same wallpaper render on the login page."],[811,"The layout: suites, helpers, stubs and fixtures"],[1815,"The lever, now pulled: `[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` ships at 70 in both\ntemplate twins, so auto-compaction fires near 183,500 tokens rather than the 197k-214k\nthese events measured, and the ~200k-token summary input shrinks with it. The store\nshows no size threshold separating failure from success - the successes reach the same\n197k-214k range - so this is a load reduction, not a proven cure. Re-run the first\ncommand above to measure whether the ratio moved."],[419,"The lines `check` prints are documented with the failures they explain, in\ntroubleshooting.md:\n`git would send this credential (source: ...)`, `NO GitHub credential ...`,\n`[shadowed: ...]`, `push would WORK` / `push would FAIL`, and the dead-`GIT_ASKPASS` note.\n`gh` refusing a healthy store is its own entry there, at\n`gh` answers `Bad credentials`,\nand a workspace with no `gh` binary at\n`gh: command not found`."],[437,"The long form of the `.env.sample` comments: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, the Spark `agent` group"],[674,"The map key a new `scripts/*.sh` needs, and the test a new function needs"],[1280,"The measured burst"],[1025,"The memory and index files are SQLite, opened live by every workspace holding the\nmount, so two workspaces writing memory at once can race. *Roughly in sync* is the\nbar, not transactional sharing."],[566,"The merge gate (Linux image) versus the convenience path (native Darwin)"],[833,"The merge gate is the Linux golden image — the same `images/dev/Dockerfile` the Coder\nworkspaces boot and CI builds:"],[609,"The merge gate is the Linux image, so run it in the contributor container:"],[846,"The mirror pads every `.sh` it instruments, so a suite that measures a payload's\n**byte size** through `REPO_ROOT` prices the harness instead of the product. This is\nhow `//tests:shell_coverage` failed an execve budget the checkout had already passed:\n`templates/common/notes-setup.sh` weighed 1182 bytes more in the mirror than it\nships. A size gate resolves its tree through `bats_source_repo_root`, and\n`startup-script-size.bats` builds a real instrumented mirror to prove it still\nmeasures what ships."],[2040,"The model in six statements, and what each one rules out"],[1240,"The model lane"],[1746,"The model lane: Spark boxes, LiteLLM, and the agent backends"],[1257,"The model lane: the aliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, how a request picks a backend, and what happens when a Spark box goes down."],[1345,"The model writes the JSON call into the message body, which no agent driver can consume."],[2025,"The model, in six statements"],[1117,"The models it calls through LiteLLM"],[273,"The mount covers the workspace container only; a dev container built on the sidecar\nkeeps its own home either way."],[489,"The names also go into `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` (compose appends this raw value)\nand into the Coder certificate's subjectAltName."],[1622,"The naming rule, and what `auto` composes from the parts you set"],[1970,"The note line, `note: GIT_ASKPASS is set but answers no credential, which is what turns\na refused token into \"Password authentication is not supported for Git operations.\"; a\nworking credential helper makes it moot`, is the second half of the story. The agent env\nexports `GIT_ASKPASS` pointing at the `coder` binary the agent staged under\n`/tmp/coder.<rand>/`, and in a measured workspace that binary answered every prompt, and\n`coder gitaskpass` itself, with `unrecognized subcommand` and no output, so git got no\nanswer at all and fell through to prompting. Clearing `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` helps\nonly while the helper answers; with a silent helper it changes nothing."],[837,"The npm-backed gates (`//docs-site:typecheck`, `:unit`, `:unit_coverage`, `:verify`) are\n`manual` for the same reason, and they all run out of `docs-site/node_modules` in the\ncheckout: `run_npm.sh` resolves its own runfiles copy back into it, so a cold checkout\ninstalls once for the whole family, under `docs-site/.npm-ci.lock`. Without that lock\nevery test action ran its own `npm ci`, and a gate that started while another was still\nwriting the tree died on a SIGBUS reading a short esbuild binary. A box with no `flock`\n(a macOS laptop) installs unlocked, since nothing else is running there."],[1100,"The on-host assistant with two doors - the Web Dashboard on :9119 and the host terminal via hermes-shell: how to reach it, what it may touch, and how approvals gate a mutating command from either."],[1959,"The one command does the same three steps and then proves them:"],[1421,"The one command that answers \"can I unlock this from a laptop\""],[1964,"The one command, run where the push was attempted, naming the repository:"],[1949,"The one command, whichever of the two it was:"],[1288,"The one front door every client dials, and why nothing else is published"],[553,"The one image, the one pin file"],[551,"The one Linux golden image behind laptop work, the Coder workspaces and CI: what the contributor dev container builds, how it is seeded, and which path is the merge gate."],[44,"The one-argument ceiling"],[1286,"The one-front-door rule"],[913,"The one-time host bootstrap, and why it is the only step run straight from a shell"],[1806,"The only live bound was the proxy's `request_timeout: 1200`, multiplied by every\nattempt. Observed failure durations were 1366 s, about 2400 s and 3465 s - the deadline\nhonoured once, twice and three times."],[632,"The opening scan pair"],[308,"The order above is not incidental; each line is a constraint the script has to keep."],[1439,"The order is deliberate: the hook packs the authorized key at generation time,\nso a key written after `update-initramfs` is not in the image. That is why\n`install` ends by reading the image back."],[1245,"The order that makes the pieces click, if you are reading them all"],[1676,"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."],[1490,"The other *template* is a **project scaffold**: a tree of files that `scripts/new-repo.sh` copies,\nsubstitutes, and hands to `git init` as the first commit of a brand new repository. It is not\nvendored here. It lives in its own repository -- by default `https://github.com/toxicoder/ezrepo`\n-- and this repo's whole job is to fetch that tree and render it. The scaffold is a product with\nits own CI and its own release rhythm, and a vendored copy drifts from the day it is committed, so\nnothing here is kept in step with it."],[631,"The page title"],[347,"The parent folder (`/home/coder/workspace`, or the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<wsid>` bind\nin `devcontainer`) is chowned before git runs, and *non-recursively*: other clones live\nalongside this one and are not ours to take over. The clone folder itself is chowned too,\nor a root agent leaves it root-owned and the clone dies writing `.git` inside it."],[890,"The password gate is homepage v2's built-in one, and it is worth keeping: the page\nlists every service on the box. `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` holds the plaintext, because\nhomepage hashes that env var itself but still needs the value."],[881,"The password gate, the proxy in front of it, and how to rotate the password"],[2011,"The paste-ready Traefik file (one backend at `:8080`, `passHostHeader: true`,\nAuthelia on the four interactive names, LiteLLM without Authelia) is in\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[1612,"The path a public request takes"],[1621,"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"],[398,"The path is Coder's external-auth broker. With no `coder.env`, Coder CE injects\na GitHub App whose id is `github`, and both templates declare that id by\ndefault. A custom OAuth app lives in `coder.env` (beside `docker-compose.yml`);\n`push-template.sh` forwards `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` so the templates declare\nthe same id. Link that provider to **both** templates."],[777,"The pattern the profile pushes you toward is a named alias for anything unstructured.\n`scripts/lib/mcp_lane_report.py` reads TOML and JSON, so rather than widen a\nsignature it declares the two document shapes and narrows with `isinstance`:"],[1271,"The picker: least-busy"],[1361,"The pin idles out after 1 h (3600 s) without a request, and every request refreshes it.\nIt bounds idle time between requests, not conversation length."],[1373,"The pin itself is kept."],[1272,"The pin: session_affinity"],[2089,"The playbook is the trunk; the environment is inventory:"],[1318,"The policy is scoped to the Spark lanes. `coder` (Arc) and `chat` keep the plain\n`num_retries` path."],[2083,"The project name (`-p`) is what isolates networks, volumes and container names, which is how two\nenvironments share a host. Every published port has to be a value for that to work:\n`\"${APP_PORT:-3000}:3000\"`. Optional services go behind a profile:"],[224,"The provider requires a parameter default to be one of its options, which is why a\ncustom `--var` value is injected as its own option instead of being dropped."],[1386,"The proxy's own `/health` lists every deployment, so trust it over the absence of\nboot errors."],[2005,"The public backend is the same hop as the other names. Replay it from a\nworkspace, which reaches the published router and skips Cloudflare, Traefik and\nAuthelia:"],[1194,"The public name is opt-in (`hermes-*`, which `auto` composes from the naming parts or\nan explicit list pins). It does not change what the container can do: local terminal,\nhost mounts and the docker socket still mean this agent can affect the real host.\nTreat its approvals the way you treat a root shell. That is the same standing as\npublic Coder."],[1341,"The Qwen2.5-Coder weights do not emit structured tool calls on this runtime."],[687,"The raw `bazelisk query` predicates for when a verb does not fit"],[652,"The reason the authored heading is the only title: the page header used to print the frontmatter\ntitle and the body printed its own, so every page opened with the same string twice."],[962,"The rendered `~/.grok/config.toml`: model aliases and role fallbacks"],[944,"The rendered `~/.grok` config"],[1966,"The repair is also one command, and every `push would FAIL` line names it:"],[538,"The repository cache is the one worth persisting in CI, and `EVO_T1_BAZEL_CACHE=<host dir>`\nis the seam: `//scripts:test-in-image` bind-mounts that directory into the gate container and\npoints `--repository_cache` at it, so each external archive downloads once per key. Each CI\ngate step exports it as `${RUNNER_TEMP}/bazel-repo-cache` and is wrapped in an `actions/cache`\npair keyed on `MODULE.bazel`, `MODULE.bazel.lock` and `.bazelrc`, with the save under\n`if: always()` — the archives fetched\nbefore a red gate are exactly what the next run would otherwise pay for again. That endpoint\nmatters: `github.com/.../releases/download/...` answers 504 for a slice of requests, one such\n504 aborts Bazel's analysis, and a refetch-on-every-job job decides its colour by luck. The\nin-container path is passed as an explicit option because the `~` in `.bazelrc` expands from\nthe Bazel server's own `user.home`, which this container's uid does not appear in: a mount\nplaced from that `~` could sit where Bazel never writes and look like a working cache for\nexactly one run. With the variable unset the script mounts nothing, which is what a\ncontributor laptop gets."],[307,"The repository leaf is derived the same way in `main.tf` and in `startup.sh.tftpl`:\nsplit on `/`, drop empty segments, take the last, drop a trailing `.git`, map every\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` to `-`, and fall back to `repo` when that leaves no\nleading alphanumeric. Both derivations must agree, or the editor and the terminal\nhelpers point at a folder that was never cloned."],[343,"The repository URLs arrive as rendered literals and are read through a *quoted* heredoc,\nso no shell quoting has to survive and the value is only ever expanded as data. An empty\nGit repository field means a home-directory workspace, so the whole clone stage is\nskipped rather than attempted with an empty URL."],[1153,"The result fails closed: no credentials, no dashboard. There is no unauthenticated\nread-only mode to fall back to."],[1664,"the router's vhost table: `proxy/router.conf.template` is rendered into\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` (gitignored) and `STACK_ROUTER_CONF=router.gen.conf` is\nwritten, because compose mounts `./proxy/${STACK_ROUTER_CONF:-router.conf}`. The\n`warn_missing_router_hosts` check runs against that rendered file, so a name the\ntable does not answer is reported before the recreate rather than as a `421` after\nit."],[1633,"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."],[751,"The rule"],[726,"The rule class is the part that saves the round trip. `//docs-site:app` looks like the site's test\ntarget and is a `filegroup`, so `bazelisk test` on it fails to start; the real test targets are the\n`sh_test` rows. The same listing filtered by a name fragment is how you find a Bats suite's target\nwithout knowing the stem convention:"],[758,"The rule in one sentence, and the two gates that hold it"],[1501,"The rule that ties it together: **a check that silently skips is a bug**. Two existing\nrepositories print `tool not found ... Skipping` and then exit zero, and a pre-commit config in\nanother wraps every hook in `command -v ... || exit 0`. Nothing in the scaffold may do that, and\nthe scaffold's own `.self-check/` suite is what holds the line."],[1242,"The rules a change follows"],[1524,"The run exits 0 when the render succeeded -- a refused GitHub step is a `warning:`, not a failure --\nand 1 on a render failure, a bad argument, or a scaffold that cannot be fetched."],[2046,"The safety lines that never move"],[847,"The same \"empty looks like green\" rule applies to the vitest gate: it must measure\nfiles no test imported. Vitest 4 made that the default and removed the `all` key\nfrom `CoverageOptions`. To prove the default still holds after a vitest bump, drop\na `lib/__cov_probe.ts` exporting one uncalled function and confirm the run reports\nit at 0% and fails; then delete the file."],[1777,"The same command aimed at the dev stack, so with `dev-stack` and its default\nproject name (`evo-t1-dev`):"],[1758,"The same command with `dev-stack` brings the dev project up. A leftover\n`homepage-proxy` container from the failed create is recreated on that `up`."],[778,"The same file writes its two fixed vocabularies as `Literal`s, which is what makes a\ntypo in a check name a gate failure rather than a line an operator never sees. A\nrecord that travels between functions gets a `NamedTuple`, a `@dataclass`, or a\n`TypedDict` — `tests/bazel_deps.py` names its Starlark map `LabelMap` and its\n`drift()` result `Drift`, and `tests/coverage/py_report.py` types the slice of\n`coverage report --json` it reads with three `total=False` TypedDicts."],[1770,"The same MagicDNS-only resolver is what makes Terraform inside the `coder`\ncontainer SERVFAIL `registry.terraform.io` (`127.0.0.11:53: server misbehaving`).\nThat spelling lives under \"Terraform and the host `coder` CLI\" below. Compose\npins public resolvers on `coder` so a template push does not wait on this host\nfix."],[1864,"The same probe in the other spelling: `test -x ~/.grok/bin/grok`."],[50,"The same push, by hand"],[1508,"The scaffold is fetched before anything is rendered: a shallow (`--depth 1`), single-branch clone\nof `--template-ref` into a scratch directory an EXIT trap removes. `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0` and an\nempty credential helper keep a scaffold that turned private from hanging on a prompt nobody can\nanswer inside a workspace. A fetched tree with no `template.yml` at its root is a hard failure --\nthat manifest belongs to the scaffold repository, so pick a ref that has it. The new repository's\nfirst commit records what it was built from: `- Scaffold: <url>@<sha>.`, or\n`- Scaffold: local tree <dir>.` for a `--template-dir` render."],[1542,"The scaffold's `.self-check/` suite, in that repository and run by its own CI, owns the template's\ninternal invariants: every token the template uses is one this script declares, every lane ships\nits `LANE.md` plus `gitignore.fragment` pair, every tool is pinned rather than `@latest`, the\nworkflows declare `permissions`, the docs links resolve, and the pins cover the lanes present."],[548,"The scan treats a comment, a here-doc body, a log message, and a scratch file a suite writes\nas prose rather than a dependency, so the maps record reads and not mentions. When the scan\ngenuinely cannot see a pairing (a lib that builds a path and returns it as a string, a\n`case` glob over `git diff` output, help text), the entry goes in the `_ALLOW` map with the\nreason as a comment above the key; the Bats gate fails an `_ALLOW` key that has no reason."],[157,"The script mints a short-lived token by inserting a row into the stack's own Postgres."],[964,"The search lane and the MCP servers behind it"],[385,"The seed also pins `terminal.integrated.cwd` and `terminal.integrated.splitCwd` to the\nopened folder, adds a `terminal.integrated.env.linux` block, and carries a\n`commandsToSkipShell` list - all described in the sibling doc."],[377,"The seed in git carries no API key. Cline and Roo read their key out of `settings.json`\nand have no `env_key` field, so when `LITELLM_API_KEY` is set the startup script injects\nit into the seed once (`jq`, falling back to `python3`) and nowhere else."],[1187,"The seed is a template for one key: `${STACK_HOST_PATH}` in `terminal.cwd` is rendered\nto a literal path on the way into the volume, from `--cwd`, then `STACK_HOST_PATH`,\nthen `/opt/stack` (announced when it falls back). `${LITELLM_MASTER_KEY}` stays a token\non purpose - Hermes expands it at boot, and a byte of the key in a volume copy is what\nMounts and \"be the host\" argues against."],[2014,"The seed sets `dashboard.trusted_proxies` to `[\"172.16.0.0/12\"]` so Hermes\nhonours `X-Forwarded-Proto` (Secure cookie and `wss`). A `hermes-data` volume\nfrom before that seed keeps the old file until you reseed. `<mount>` is the\nhost directory of that volume:"],[1181,"The seeded config sets `approvals.mode: manual`. `cron_mode` and `unattended_mode` are\nboth `deny`, so a scheduled or unattended run cannot approve anything either."],[1091,"The seeds live per workspace, on that workspace's own home volume. They are *not*\non the shared `~/.grok` profile volume above, and seeding does not care whether\nthat mount exists."],[1833,"The self-hosted SearXNG MCP stdio server is the search lane, declared by default\n(`search_mcp` defaults to true). Grok's native `web_search` is parked: the render sets\n`disable_web_search = true`, because no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type - LiteLLM's `agent` group answers `tools of type 'web_search' are not\nsupported: this server runs function tools only` (HTTP 400) before anything is\nfetched, and `supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it. An agent that\nstill spends a turn on a native search call and comes back with that message behaved\nas configured; there is no native lane to fall back to. Two probes split a SearXNG\nfault in half:"],[619,"The sentinel outranks every other signal, so `new-workspace` answers `workspace` for this\nrepo and a Coder workspace of it never builds the contributor container on its DinD\nsidecar."],[1789,"The server on each Spark box logs four shapes, and three of them are this stack's own\ntraffic rather than anything wrong with the box. Read the shape before you reach for\nthe box."],[188,"The service appears twice by necessity: the first `coder` selects the compose service, the\nsecond is the CLI binary inside it."],[1006,"The seven aliases (`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast`, `chat`) are defined with `base_url` set to `litellm_url` and\n`env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"`. The key is injected as an environment variable, so\nit never appears in a file. `agent` carries three\n`[[model.\"agent\".reasoning_efforts]]` tables (`xhigh` is the default). The\n`agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` aliases set\n`supports_reasoning_effort = false` because LiteLLM bakes the level for callers\nthat have no reasoning-effort field."],[1651,"The shape is load-bearing: each name is **one label under the zone**, and each service\nprefix joins it with a **hyphen**. A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so\nthe dotted two-label form (`coder.gmktecbeast.`) has no certificate at the public edge:\nthe browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` and Traefik never sees the\nrequest. Measured on this box - `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` completes the handshake\nwith the zone's `*.overeazy.io` cert, while the dotted form fails with a TLS alert 40\nand presents no certificate at all."],[69,"The shared grok profile volume"],[1569,"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."],[660,"The sidebar is not read from the filesystem: most pages sit flat in `docs/` while belonging to\ndifferent sections, and the reading order a prev/next footer needs is an editorial decision. The\ntree is declared once in `NAV` inside `docs-site/scripts/gen_nav.py` and generated into\n`docs-site/lib/nav.json`, which the page-tree builder imports and a Zod schema validates."],[1235,"The sidebar tabs divide the rest: **Learn** covers how the pieces work, **Operate** covers\nrunning the box day to day, and **Contribute** covers the gates a change runs through.\nEach subject has exactly one page, and the README links to that page rather than\nrepeating it."],[562,"The single golden image, and the file that pins it"],[646,"The site is a Fumadocs app that reads `docs/` straight out of the repository, so the markdown\nhere is the source of record: nothing is copied into `docs-site/`, and the README's links keep\npointing at these files. Most of the chrome is derived from the prose rather than authored in\nit, which is what keeps 20-odd pages consistent."],[355,"The six paths it probes are the ones `@devcontainers/cli` looks in: `devcontainer.json`\nand `devcontainer.yml` at the root, under `.devcontainer/`, and under\n`.github/devcontainers/`."],[134,"The size that matters is not `startup.sh.tftpl`. `main.tf` composes the body from that\ntemplate plus every `templates/common/` payload it inlines, so any edit to the rulebook,\nthe skills, the tmux config, the notes seeder, or the Grok config moves the number. That\nis how six commits crept from 104 KB to 130 KB and one documentation commit crossed the\nwall at 135 KB."],[2132,"The skill is `skills/trunk-refactor/SKILL.md`. A session in this repository reaches it through\n`AGENTS.md`; any other repository gets it by installing it into the two skill homes the agents\nread:"],[2061,"The skill's twelve binary criteria, in prose. Each needs a command's output as proof."],[1541,"The split follows the ownership: the scaffold's own invariants are checked in the scaffold\nrepository, and this repo checks only the renderer."],[1207,"The stack runners take `up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`; `status` and `logs` are\nwhere a Hermes that will not bind its port is diagnosed first. Status across the whole\nbox, including this card, is in dashboard.md; the LAN and public\nrouting rules are in public-access.md, and the service inventory is\nin README.md."],[352,"The stage reads the *rendered* file, pulls every `command = \"...\"` value out of it, and\nnames the ones that are not executable files, in the same startup log that wrote the\nconfig. `sed` keeps only a double-quoted value and a TOML path carries no spaces, so an\nempty entry there is an odd line rather than a missing binary."],[808,"The stub-PATH recipe"],[815,"The stub-PATH recipe, and the two paths a suite may take"],[671,"The suites that read a payload"],[1803,"The summary call rides the **session model**, the `agent*` thinking family, and\ninherits its output ceiling: captured on the wire as `max_tokens: 32768`, alongside 25\ntool schemas and the workspace's `x-litellm-session-id` affinity header.\n`[models] session_summary` is **not** that lane - it serves titles and the session\nrecap."],[186,"The supported push is `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: no arguments pushes every\ntemplate, a template name retries just that one, and the image tag comes from `DEV_IMAGE`\nin `.env`. The raw equivalent below is what that script does internally — it streams the\nsame tar and makes the same CLI call the script makes for each template, execing into the\ndaemon's `coder` container by name."],[163,"The tar is built from a throwaway staging copy, not from the template dir itself.\n`.terraform/` is excluded (pushing it would put the plan's provider binaries into the\nversion's Filestore row), symlinks are dumped as their targets (`tar -h`), and the\n`templates/common/` payloads are copied in beside `main.tf`. A staged copy that still\nholds a symlink is refused before the CLI runs."],[1697,"The TensorFold log is full of warnings, or `resumed at 0` -> read the line shapes first"],[1200,"The terminal door adds a way in for someone who already has a shell on this host, and\nopens no new listener, no new port and no new credential. `hermes-shell` authenticates\nnothing of its own: `docker` on that host is the credential, and it was always\nroot-equivalent here. Its one guard is that the assistant's approvals stay `manual` on\nboth doors."],[1087,"The text of 1-4 is the orchestration protocol (fan-out / fan-in, the edit-conflict gate,\nprobe-one-worker-first, verify-what-a-worker-actually-produced). It travels as\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, staged beside the staged `main.tf` and\nread back with `file()` / `filebase64()`; `templates/common/` keeps one copy for\nboth templates, and the push stages it into each template's pushed copy."],[1334,"The three `agent-*` rows are that same lane with a thinking level baked into\n`litellm_params.reasoning_effort`. Qwen3.8-Flash-Next takes `low`, `medium` and\n`xhigh` and nothing else - there is no `high` - so the level is a name to pick\nrather than a knob to turn, and `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` are\nhow the level reaches Coder Agents: its model dropdown chooses an alias, not a\nrequest parameter."],[1839,"The three MCP servers (`github`, `context7`, `searxng`) ship by default, so a lane\nmissing from a session has one of three shapes: the block absent from the rendered\nconfig, the `command=` binary absent from the golden image, or the credential absent.\nRun `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` first - it reports the declared lanes, their\nbinaries, their credentials and their handshake in one pass, and it names which shape\nyou are in (an undeclared lane is a failure, a missing binary a warning).\n`grok mcp doctor` inside the workspace lists the declared servers and their handshake.\nA *park* is deliberate and looks like this: `GITHUB_MCP=false` / `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` /\n`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n`--var github_mcp=false` / `--var context7_mcp=false` / `--var search_mcp=false` on a\ntemplate push. With none of those set, a missing block is a defect, not a park."],[807,"The three paths a suite may use"],[1523,"The three scaffold flags exist so the source can be pointed elsewhere without editing anything:\nthe env pair covers a mirror or a fork for a whole deployment, and `--template-dir` renders a tree\nyou already hold, which is the offline route and the one the hermetic tests use."],[956,"The three stack-seeded skill commands"],[1069,"The thresholds are separate as well. `context_limit` above is Coder's own compaction\nbudget with its 70 per cent default, while the CLI fires at\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` (70) of its 262144-token window."],[242,"The toggle is `mutable = false`: turning it on or off means a workspace **rebuild**,\nbecause a network, a volume and a second container appear or vanish. The preset makes\nthat one click."],[159,"The token is scoped to minutes (`CODER_PUSH_TOKEN_MINUTES`, default 10), deleted on the\nway out including on a failed or interrupted run, never printed, and minted **once** for\nthe whole run however many templates it covers."],[519,"The toolchain Bazel drives comes from one Linux golden image, `images/dev/Dockerfile`, and\nthe contributor path is that image rather than a set of tools installed on your machine:"],[1364,"The trade is deliberate: stickiness buys prefix-cache locality and one predictable box\nfor the whole conversation, so one workspace's agent loop can sit on a busy box."],[1624,"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"],[490,"The Traefik on the other box owns TLS for those names and must forward plain\nHTTP to this box's router container **with the original Host header intact** -\n`STACK_ROUTER_CONF` names the conf that container mounts (`proxy/router.gen.conf`,\nrendered by bootstrap from `proxy/router.conf.template`, or the checked-in\n`proxy/router.conf` while the key is empty), and it picks the service from\n`$http_host` on `STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` (default 8080). A rewritten Host comes back\nas `421`.\nThat box needs one file, and the paste-ready version of it - one `loadBalancer`\nat `:8080`, a router per name, Authelia on the four interactive names, the\nlong-lived timeouts - is\npublic-access.md#traefik-configuration."],[672,"The twin template copies"],[1548,"The two commands"],[1553,"The two commands an operator runs most"],[812,"The two inventory gates that tie a production function to a test"],[1320,"The two levers that actually move load off a shedding box:"],[542,"The two maps in `scripts/BUILD.bazel` and the one in `tests/BUILD.bazel` are the\nmachine-readable record of which repo file each target reads:"],[946,"The two modes"],[639,"The two opening lists that become the scan card, and the order they have to sit in"],[424,"The two passes"],[434,"The two passes of `update-configs`, and the flags that select one"],[59,"The two questions"],[177,"The two research lanes split by question type: a library, framework, CLI or setup\nquestion goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then query docs on that\nid), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool, and a URL already\nin hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`. Grok's native `web_search` is parked\nhere, and native `web_fetch` is unavailable because that tool needs an egress proxy\nendpoint this stack does not run, so `SEARCH_MCP=false` leaves a session with library\ndocs and no way to reach the web."],[595,"The two research lanes that config can render are split by question type: a library,\nframework, CLI or setup question goes to `context7` (resolve the library id first, then\nquery docs on that id), anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool,\nand a URL already in hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content` - Grok's native\n`web_search` is parked here and native `web_fetch` needs an egress proxy endpoint this\nstack does not run, so neither is a lane. Prove what this home declares with:"],[749,"The typing rule for every language in this repo: Python under mypy with no Any, TypeScript under the strictest flags it compiles, Terraform variables with a declared constraint, and what bash and awk owe instead."],[724,"The uncached pass is not replaced. `bazelisk test` replays a cached PASS for an action whose inputs\ndid not change, so the derived list proves your change against whatever actually rebuilds, and one\n`bazelisk test //:test-fast --nocache_test_results` remains the pass you owe before the PR, per\nbuilding-with-bazel.md. Use the derived list for the loop and keep the full\nuncached pass for the end, instead of paying for sixty suites to learn one thing per edit."],[1423,"The unlock itself, step by step, from the operator's own machine"],[1458,"The unlock key authorizes a root shell on a machine whose disks are still\nsealed, and the initramfs image holding it sits on an unencrypted `/boot`.\nAnyone who can read `/boot` can read that public key, so treat it as a\nseparate credential with its own purpose: a dedicated key whose private half\nlives on the laptop you bring to a reboot, not the key you push to every\nrepository. `UMASK=0077` keeps the image itself out of unprivileged hands, and\nthe initramfs host keys stay separate from `/etc/ssh` on purpose - they are\ngenerated per boot image, and sharing them would leak which key the sealed\nmachine answers to."],[325,"The unmanaged case is announced rather than silent, because the render is the only place\na workspace's model catalog and MCP lanes are ever written."],[1914,"The usual wrapper is `Failed to check server \"https://...\" for first user, is the URL\ncorrect and is coder accessible from your browser?`"],[704,"The verbs below are the shapes of those questions."],[1972,"The verdicts, and what each one costs you:"],[314,"The watcher left at the end is what makes notes survive a recreate that lands *between*\nedits. The boot-time sync runs once, so anything written later lived solely inside that\ncontainer, which is how a whole workspace's notes went missing on a recreate."],[743,"The whole-graph enumeration `unlisted` is built from, which is also the answer to \"how much of the\nrepo do the gates actually see\":"],[1796,"The window is still read passively on every run without the needle, from the server's\nown startup lines (`context_pairs` and `startup_estimate` in the report), so a box\nquietly rebuilt with a shorter window is still visible; the needle is the active\nconfirmation of it. `--no-tests` skips all four recipe probes for a quick read-only\npass, and that is what `//scripts:bootstrap` runs."],[2018,"The wizard only listens on `:3000` at first boot; after install, use `:4443`."],[1772,"The word the shell says it cannot find is the second tag. The same read would\nalso have executed anything written on a line of its own in that file, and it\nexported every other key there (`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` included) into the\nenvironment of the `docker exec` calls."],[1175,"The working directory the agent runs in is `terminal.cwd`, and it has to be a literal\npath. Hermes validates it in `agent/runtime_cwd.py` with `expanduser()` alone, so a\n`${VAR}` left in the file is reported as `does not exist` and the agent works somewhere\nelse, while `hermes config get terminal.cwd` still prints the token because the config\n*loader* does expand `${VAR}` - the two paths disagree, and only the runtime one runs\ncommands. `hermes-bootstrap` renders the placeholder in the seeded `config.yaml` at\nseed time (`--cwd DIR` overrides what it renders), which is the part that fixes a\nlong-lived box. compose also sets `TERMINAL_CWD` from the same key, as the backstop for\na config that names no `terminal.cwd`: `apply_terminal_config_to_env` lets an explicit\n`terminal.cwd` in the file own that variable, so the file decides wherever it carries\none. Re-seed a volume that predates the render with\n`hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force`."],[238,"The workspace container attaches to **both** `bridge` and that private network; once a\n`networks_advanced` block appears the provider stops letting `network_mode` do the\nattaching, so `bridge` has to be listed explicitly."],[1831,"The workspace container must resolve `host.docker.internal`; the model lane reaches\nLiteLLM through that host gateway, and so does `LITELLM_BASE_URL`."],[1983,"Then check **how** the credential is sent, because the two GitHub endpoints disagree\nabout the scheme. `api.github.com` and `gh` accept `Authorization: Bearer <token>`. The\ngit data endpoint refuses it: one token answered `/user` with `200` and\n`<repo>.git/info/refs?service=git-receive-pack` with `401` plus\n`remote: invalid credentials` while the token rode a bearer header. git has to send it\nas **Basic** auth with the token as the password. The username text is not checked\n(measured on Coder v2.36.6: `toxicoder`, `x-access-token`, `somebody-else` and `octocat`\neach returned `200`), but an empty username or an empty password is refused, and the\nempty password is what produces the misleading `Invalid username or token. Password\nauthentication is not supported for Git operations.`"],[1982,"Then check the URL scheme, which no probe can fix: that credential covers\n`https://github.com/...` remotes only, so a `git@github.com:` or `ssh://` URL gets\nneither helper nor askpass token and needs an SSH key you placed yourself, which this\nstack provisions nowhere."],[473,"Then connect your GitHub account in Coder (Settings -> Tokens / external auth)\nand link the same provider to **both** workspace templates (Templates -> edit\n-> External Auth)."],[400,"Then connect your GitHub account. On a running workspace, click **GitHub reauth** on\nthe agent bar (Coder v2.36's `/external-auth/<id>` page; use **Reauthenticate** if the\npage says you are already authenticated). First-time connect also works from the\nworkspace create/update form and from Settings -> Tokens / external auth. Device-flow\nlogin is not enough for private repos: install Coder's GitHub App with **Install\nGitHub App** on the same bar (or open\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new),\ngrant those repositories, then restart. An empty\ngithub.com/settings/apps page is not a misconfiguration:\nwith `auth_type=client_secret` Coder has no App to install, and Coder's built-in GitHub\nApp only earns a page there once it is installed on the account. Until something is\ninstalled and granted, that page simply has nothing to **Configure**, and the credential\nthe provider mints still authenticates - it just cannot write to a private repository."],[932,"Then finish the two browser-only steps: run the Kasm wizard once at `https://<host>:3000`\nand use `https://<host>:4443` after it, and open the dashboard at `https://<host>/` with\nthe `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` that `bootstrap` printed."],[2115,"Then hand the deletions to a human:"],[925,"Then look before you change anything. `--check` is read-only: missing tools, placeholder\nsecrets, certificates, images."],[1867,"Then open a new terminal; `PATH` there already includes `~/.grok/bin`."],[190,"Then push `workspace` (the repo is not mounted into the container, so the directory has to\ntravel as a tar):"],[196,"Then re-pin both values:"],[1960,"Then read the store with the pair cleared, because `gh auth status` on its own reports\nthe env pair rather than what is stored:"],[1891,"Then run `bash -lc 'command -v grok'`. A workspace on a current image answers\n`/usr/local/bin/grok`, so an empty answer means the CLI is missing, not the profile."],[2133,"Then, in a new session in the repository you want to convert:"],[164,"Then, per template, it confirms an **active** template version. A push can exit 0 and\nstill leave the template inactive if its Terraform run failed, which shows up as the\ntemplate missing from the create dropdown."],[1380,"There are no rpm, tpm or `max_parallel_requests` caps anywhere, on purpose. A cap parks\nrequests behind an in-memory semaphore inside the proxy; without one, a fully offered\nbox sheds load in its own scheduler."],[180,"There is no GitHub-token template variable, so no push ever carries a personal access\ntoken. See GitHub credentials for a workspace."],[1838,"There is no second lane behind this one, so run these probes before touching anything\nelse. A service that answers but a server that will not handshake is the MCP hop; a\nserver that cannot launch at all usually means no\n`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`, and that binary is baked into the golden\nimage - an image that predates it needs a rebuild and a workspace recreate, not a\nconfig edit. Park the lane with `search_mcp = false` on the next template push\n(`SEARCH_MCP=false` in `.env` applies it to every new workspace) when a session should\nhave no search tool at all. To restore the native tool, flip `disable_web_search` to\nfalse in both template twins and re-push - only for an endpoint that really serves the\ntool, or the 400 comes straight back."],[1638,"These are what the shipped defaults compose. The operator sets the parts, not these\nstrings: the grammar is `<service>-<environment>-<suffix>.<base>`, always ONE label\nunder the zone."],[1492,"These files belong to the scaffold repository. This repo does not ship them, lint them, or keep a\ncopy: it fetches them at run time and renders them. The scaffold is language-neutral at the core,\nwith optional per-language lanes. Everything below was chosen from what is measurably missing\nacross the account, not from taste: across a sample of eleven ordinary projects, `SECURITY.md`,\n`CONTRIBUTING.md`, issue templates, a pull-request template, `dependabot.yml`, `CODEOWNERS` and\n`.editorconfig` were each present in none of them, and no repository in the surveyed set had a\nsingle release or tag."],[442,"These four sections are also the long form of the `.env.sample` comments that\npoint here: GitHub external auth, Grok profile mode, public hosts, and the\nSpark `agent` model group. Where a topic page already owns a subject, this page\npoints at it instead of restating it."],[1319,"These retries are instant and stay on the same box. A status-less exception means\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` excludes nothing and `_time_to_sleep_before_retry`\nreturns 0 while any deployment is healthy, and the workspace-wide\n`x-litellm-session-id` pin sends every subagent of a workspace to one box anyway. The\nblock makes a refusal less likely to surface. Relocation comes from the box and the\nfan-out."],[1291,"Thinking levels: what `agent` bakes, and what the baked aliases change"],[2085,"This fleet's own dev-versus-stable split is the reference implementation: separate project\nnames, separate dotenv files, and a subdomain composed from parts rather than from a branch\nname, driven by `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up`,\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up`, and `bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack` for the\npull-and-deploy pair. In that repository the environment branches were still sitting on the\nremote (`dev`, `development`, `staging`, `uat`, `prod`) while every one of them reported\n`ahead=0` against `main`: five branches, zero unique commits, waiting for someone to confirm the\ndelete."],[1916,"This is not a broken credential. It happens with a valid token too, because Authelia\ngates every route."],[2052,"This is the human-readable twin of the `trunk-refactor` skill\n(`skills/trunk-refactor/SKILL.md`). The skill is what an agent session follows; this page is\nwhat a person reads, with the examples the skill leaves out to stay short. The branch rules\nthemselves live in Branching - this page is about getting a repository *onto*\nthose rules."],[709,"This is the question that costs the most sessions, because the answer is spread over four different\nmechanisms and each one is normally discovered by a failing gate."],[746,"This is the trap that makes a hand-check unreliable, and it is the reason the CLI keeps Bazel's\nstderr instead of discarding it. A query can print `ERROR: no such target` and leave stdout empty,\nwhich looks exactly like \"nothing depends on this\":"],[731,"This is why an unlisted payload directory stays invisible rather than being checked loosely: a file\nthat travels in no runfiles tree is a file no sandboxed action can open, so `test-fast` and `lint`\nnever read it and never fail. Nothing prints a warning about that. `owners <path>` and\n`unlisted <dir>` are the two ways to ask, and they are the difference between a payload the harness\nguards and a payload nobody has ever executed."],[1097,"This is workflow curation, separate from the memory system, which stores durable facts\n(upstream `/remember`, `/memory`, `/dream`, shipped here as `/ez-learn`), and from\n`AGENTS.md`, which is the always-loaded rules file. A skill loads when its `description`\nmatches, and holds a procedure."],[1674,"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."],[694,"This repo keeps its coupling in BUILD files: `scripts/BUILD.bazel` names the libs and payload\ngroups each CLI reads, `tests/BUILD.bazel` names what each Bats suite opens, and the root\n`BUILD.bazel` aggregates every payload group into `//:stack_data`. `//tests:bats_bazel_deps_test`\nfails when those maps drift from the sources, so the graph is a record an automated reader can\ntrust, and \"which suites cover this file\" has a ground truth sitting in it. Answering that from\n`grep` re-derives what one `bazelisk query` prints."],[1489,"This repository already uses the word *template* for a **Coder template**: the Terraform tree\nunder `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` that `push-template.sh` registers with\nthe Coder server."],[1220,"This repository brings up one Compose project on a GMKtec EVO-T1 (Intel Core Ultra 9\n285H, Arc 140T iGPU, 96 GB DDR5) and keeps it runnable by conversation, by dashboard, or\nby `bazelisk`. Everything is a Bazel target addressed as\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`. On the stack host, `scripts/setup-host.sh` is\nthe one script meant to run straight from a shell. On a laptop, reopen the repo in the\ncontributor environment or run\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image`."],[363,"Those four are the whole bar. The proxy address reaches the operator as the `litellm`\nitem on the `workspace_info` tile below (`value = var.litellm_url`), a read-out\nrather than a proxied app, so health-checking the proxy is\n`curl http://host.docker.internal:4000/health/liveliness` on the stack host."],[534,"Those operator runs are bounded: a script's non-interactive `docker exec` closes stdin\n(`</dev/null`), and the docker wrappers go through `timeout(1)` with `--kill-after`, so a\n`bazelisk run //scripts:<stem>` cannot sit forever the way `docker exec -i` did under\n`bazelisk run`, waiting for an EOF that never came. `logs --follow` is the intentional\nlong-running exception, and you stop it with Ctrl-C."],[1807,"Those re-dispatches came from this repo's own config. At the pinned tag\n`ghcr.io/berriai/litellm:v1.102.1` a stream that dies before its first chunk raises\n`MidStreamFallbackError`, which subclasses `ServiceUnavailableError`\n(`litellm/exceptions.py:1114`), and `get_num_retries_from_retry_policy` walks the\nexception MRO most-specific first, so it lands on `ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` and\nnever reaches `DefaultRetries`. A hung request never cools the box either: cooldown\nhard-excludes any exception whose string contains `APIConnectionError`, and\n`_deployment_ids_to_skip_on_retry` only skips deployments that answered a non-retryable\nint status code. Under the workspace-wide `x-litellm-session-id` pin every attempt\ntherefore returned to the same saturated box."],[1336,"Those three groups reuse the same `SPARK1/2/3_OPENAI_URL` / `SPARK_n_API_KEY`\npairs as `agent`. They are separate model groups on the same hardware, not three\nmore boxes: routing state is per group, so least-busy's counters and the session\npin are computed inside a group and two chats on different aliases can land on the\nsame box at once. What does not change is the box - 5 full 262144-token streams in\ntotal, whichever aliases arrive on it. This is not the \"two deployments on one\nbox\" mistake below, which is about a single group counting two URLs on one machine\nas two machines."],[1351,"Three deployments, one per Spark box, and no `order:` tier. A tier would make the\nlower boxes unreachable while any tier-1 box stayed healthy."],[843,"Three runfiles traps bit this gate, and all fail *silently*, so they are worth\nnaming:"],[1894,"Three separate reads, from the stack host. The first prints `unhealthy` rather than\nan error when the daemon fails its own probe, and `no such container` when the\nworkspace has no sidecar at all:"],[838,"Three targets answer \"is this code actually executed by a test\" instead of\nassuming it. They are `manual` like the npm gates, so `//:test-fast` stays fast, and\nthey are members of `//:test-all`, which pre-push and CI run:"],[1093,"Three things about skills matter in practice here:"],[1825,"Titles that never appear and a long chat that dies at its context budget are the\nhelper lanes pointing at a dead alias. Re-running the script moves them."],[1631,"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."],[1032,"To put native search back on a transport that really serves the tool: flip the key\nto `false` in the seed - both `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy - and re-push, which applies it fleet-wide. For one\nworkspace without a push, the create form's **Grok config extras** field appends\nfree-form TOML verbatim to that workspace's file. Either way start a new session,\nand expect the same 400 back if the endpoint still answers function tools only."],[1188,"To reseed deliberately:"],[1853,"To take the same reading from inside the workspace, replay the path on `:8080`, the\npublished `router` port. That reaches `coder:3000` through the sidecar while\nbypassing Traefik, Authelia and the WAN, so a reply there that differs from the\nbrowser's `https://` URL names the cloud hops."],[1266,"Tool calls: the measured limitation"],[1290,"Tool calls: the measured limitation and the path that supports them"],[2016,"Traefik and Authelia have to pass `Upgrade`. Cloudflare's orange-cloud proxy\nhas WebSockets on by default. A page that loads and a Chat tab that dies is\nthis hop, not the basic-auth provider."],[1616,"Traefik configuration"],[1685,"Troubleshooting"],[1687,"Troubleshooting"],[935,"Troubleshooting — start here when a step above says no."],[1463,"troubleshooting.md for the symptom-first list once the\nbox is back up."],[2022,"Trunk-based + flags refactor"],[2024,"Trunk-based + flags refactor"],[2,"Trunk-based branch rules: main is the only long-lived branch, work branches are short-lived, and a pull request is the only way back in."],[18,"Trunk-based, GitHub-Flow shaped: one long-lived branch, short-lived work branches, and a pull\nrequest as the only way back into `main`."],[1477,"Two different things called \"template\", and which one this page is about"],[1260,"Two doors, one key"],[741,"Two exclusions earn their place. `except kind(\"test_suite\", //...)` drops the aggregates, which is\nwhat the CLI filters on so `//:test-fast` cannot answer a per-file question, and\n`except kind(\"sh_test\", //lints:*)` drops the two manual lint targets, `//lints:shell` and\n`//lints:shfmt`, that ride on every script:"],[1619,"Two follow-ups, both by hand"],[770,"Two gates carry most of it, and both are members of `//lints:all`, so `//:lint`,\n`//:test-all`, and the `bazel` job of `.github/workflows/ci.yml` run them:"],[803,"Two gates, one rule"],[382,"Two helpers are seeded into `~/.grok/bin` on every start, both `chmod +x`:\n`vscode-terminal-tmux` (the default profile) and `grok-build-terminal`."],[1848,"Two hops can answer 502 on that path: this box's nginx, when `coder:3000` will not\nresolve or connect, and Coder's own Go proxy, whose `ReverseProxy` ErrorHandler\nfires when the app upstream answers nothing. Two reads tell them apart, both from\nthe stack host:"],[1416,"Two keys, two purposes"],[441,"Two kinds of file go stale in this repo, and because neither of them is\nchecked in, `git pull` cannot fix either one for you. `update-configs` is the\none command that refreshes both, and it is written to be safe to run when you\nare not looking closely at the diff."],[1527,"Two kinds of path are removed on the way out: a lane's reserved metadata (`LANE.md`,\n`gitignore.fragment`, whose contents are consumed rather than copied) and the generator-owned paths\nin `NEW_REPO_TEMPLATE_META`, stripped from every render: `.template-lanes/` (the overlay source this\nscript reads), `.self-check/` (the scaffold's conformance suite, which goes stale the moment a real\nproject diverges from the template it was checked against), and `.git` (which would hand the new\nrepository the template's history and origin remote)."],[1469,"Two kinds of template"],[1323,"Two knobs that look right and do nothing here: `retry_after` is only a `min_timeout` and\nis never consulted while the group has healthy deployments, and `fallbacks_by_model`\ndoes not exist in this tag (the proxy warns and ignores an unknown `router_settings`\nkey, so a config that looks correct silently does nothing, which is why\n`//tests:bats_litellm_config_test` rejects it by name)."],[2125,"Two patterns worth reading across those rows. A branch family that is `ahead=0` (this repo,\n`inkorporated`) is a cleanup with no risk once a human confirms it. A branch family that is\nhundreds of commits ahead (`nvidia-dgx-spark-lab`, `ez-comfy-stack`) is a porting programme:\nslice by area, land each slice green, and let the flags hold what is not finished. Both begin\nwith the same inventory, and both end with a table a person signs off on."],[599,"Two rules decide every write, and they are the same rules the workspace startup applies:"],[886,"Two services that read the box rather than serve code: the Homepage dashboard (the\nlanding page) and Kasm Workspaces (streamed desktops)."],[96,"Two templates live under `templates/`, and a push covers both."],[1251,"Two templates turn a git URL into a running container: the plain `workspace` template, and\n`devcontainer`, which builds the clone's own `.devcontainer` on a DinD sidecar. Both are\npushed from this checkout, and both seed the agent config the workspace starts with."],[1023,"Two things about that start are worth knowing before you go looking for a vanished\nsetting. Boot **writes `git config --global core.excludesFile`** — but only when you\nhave not set it yourself. Left unset it points at `~/.grok/notes/exclude`, which lists\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, so from then on *every* git repository in that\nworkspace ignores both names at any depth; if you already set the option, those two\nlines are appended to the file it names and nothing else about it is touched. Boot also\ndrops the same two lines into each clone's own `.git/info/exclude`, which is the layer\nthat works even with no global config at all. And when a repository tracks a `notes.md`\nof its own, the scratch file is written as `.scratch-notes.md` beside it instead — your\nclone is never asked to delete somebody else's committed file."],[135,"Two things follow from the mechanism:"],[1793,"Two things on this stack cause the evictions, and both are tuned here rather than on the\nbox:"],[721,"Two things scope tells you that a habit of `bazelisk test //:test-fast` hides:"],[1536,"Two things, and neither replaces the CLI."],[781,"Two well-known switches are deliberately off. `exactOptionalPropertyTypes` collides\nwith React's optional DOM props and with fumadocs' `StaticOptions`;\n`noPropertyAccessFromIndexSignature` collides with how the unit tests read the\nfumadocs source config. Both would be paid for in casts, which is the wrong\ndirection."],[1026,"Two workspaces of one owner that both hold a clone of the *same* repository each run a\nnotes watcher over the same canonical path, so there too the last write wins, tick by\ntick. The stub and empty guards and the `backups/` archive are what bound the damage a\nlosing tick can do; they do not merge two diverging files."],[1443,"Type the passphrase:"],[753,"TypeScript"],[760,"TypeScript: the compiler switches, and how the `.mjs` scripts get checked"],[1738,"UI and logins"],[2008,"Under `auto` both sides come from the same parts, so a `421` on a name nobody\nrecognises is a part problem, not a router problem: an empty `STACK_DOMAIN_BASE`\ncomposes nothing (the stack stays LAN-only) and a mistyped `STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX`\nor `STACK_ENV_LABEL` composes a name no vhost, no DNS and no certificate serves.\nRead the composed list back from `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` in `.env`, fix the part, and\nre-run `bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap`, then recreate. Editing the rendered\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` instead only survives until the next bootstrap, which\nre-renders it from the parts."],[487,"Under `auto` the parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output\nonly: bootstrap writes the resolved list back and records the intent in\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, so a later run re-composes and a list edited by hand\nin `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is ignored. Bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear when that happens."],[844,"Under `bazelisk test` every source arrives as a **symlink**. A `find -type f`\nover the tree then sees no regular file and reports an empty suite, which reads\nas \"nothing to test\". `py_cov.sh` uses `find -L`."],[1248,"Understanding a behaviour before trying to change it"],[1408,"Unlock an encrypted host remotely"],[1410,"Unlock an encrypted host remotely"],[1413,"Unlocking from a laptop"],[191,"Use this shape for reference, or for pushing a tag `.env` does not carry."],[578,"Use VS Code, Cursor, or any editor with the Dev Containers extension, and reopen this\nrepository **in a container**."],[894,"v2.4.0 serves a prerendered page with the settings baked in, so a freshly created\ncontainer shows an empty default layout (`initialSettings {}`) until the config is\nre-read. Click the refresh button in the header, or:"],[1402,"Vanilla Ollama on an Intel iGPU is effectively CPU-bound, and several times slower on a\n32B coder model."],[789,"Variables are declared before they are used and carry their shape in the\ndeclaration: `local -a` for an indexed array, `local -A` fo